      "summary": "Spain's harbour fortress became Mexico's most feared prison. Its dungeons were called las tinajas (\"the jars\") for their damp, and named Gloria, Purgatorio, Infierno and Limbo. Benito Juárez was held there ten days incommunicado in 1853, in flooded cells, before Santa Anna deported him. Under Díaz the political prisoners of the 1906 risings (Acayucan) went there: César E. Canales (May 1909) described cells \"narrow, unventilated, dark, damp, pestilent and full of vermin\"; Enrique Novoa, after three years in El Infierno, told of asphyxiating heat, rats against his body at night and a liver swollen from hunger; Cipriano Medina described floods to the knee and inmates like \"walking skeletons,\" 20–30 to a cell, fed on scraps from the city's restaurants. Legend puts the bandit Chucho el Roto there (his death date is disputed — 1885 or 1894 — UNVERIFIED). Carranza closed the prison by decree on 2 July 1915 (search summary) and used the fortress as a presidential residence. The odd detail: the cells were named for the stations of the Catholic afterlife — a prisoner was sent to Purgatory or Hell by address.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the cell names, Juárez and the testimonies; Medium on the 2 July 1915 closure date; Low on Chucho el Roto's death there. • his death date is disputed — 1885 or 1894 — UNVERIFIED",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the Marines reopen El Infierno as a stockade in the 1920s. The Mexican guards who knew the old prison won't go below the waterline, and the jars still flood with the tide.",
