      "summary": "Fray Bernardino de Sahagún's Historia general de las cosas de Nueva España (compiled c. 1540–1585; the Nahuatl–Spanish Florentine Codex) lists ten omens said to have foretold the Conquest. The sixth: \"many times a woman would be heard going along weeping and shouting\" at night — crying for her children, asking where she could take them. Sahagún also describes the goddess Cihuacóatl (\"Snake Woman\") appearing in white, walking at night weeping and wailing — an evil omen. Scholars read this as the ancestor of La Llorona; the pre-Hispanic Chocacíhuatl (\"the crying woman\", a spirit of women who died in childbirth) is the other root. Many popular sources give \"1550, Mexico City\" as La Llorona's first sighting — that date is Sahagún's era, not a dated police-blotter event (treat \"1550\" as folklore's own date). The odd detail: the first attestation is not a ghost story but a prophecy of conquest — the weeping woman is heard before the invader comes. In a Mexico that lost its north in 1917, she has a second invasion to weep for.",
