      "summary": "Augustinian friars Nicolás de Perea and Sebastián de Tolentino came to the region about 1537 to preach against the cult of a cave god (Oztoteotl, \"god of the cave\"); by the shrine's tradition, returning to destroy the idol, they found a life-sized crucified Christ standing where it had been. The sanctuary built around the image draws over two million a year (up to 50,000 on great feasts). Its customs are older than the friars' reading of them: pilgrims dance before the image \"to cleanse sins,\" bathe at the spring, are crowned with flowers, and first-timers circle the ancient ahuehuete cypress three times, some hanging a newborn's umbilical cord in a bag on it. Folk belief: skip the tree-and-spring ritual and you will be turned to stone — the boulders along the Malinalco road are said to be such pilgrims. The proverb for an impossible request: you won't get it \"without going to dance at Chalma.\" The odd detail: the stones on the road are the pilgrims who skipped the rite (legend).",
