      "summary": "Martín Ocelotl (\"Jaguar\"), born c. 1496 to a merchant father and a well-known priestess mother, baptised in 1525, was a tlaciuhqui and ticitl (diviner-healer) who predicted rain and famine. In autumn 1536 he was tried before Bishop Juan de Zumárraga's Apostolic Inquisition; witnesses said he could turn himself into a jaguar (\"tiger\") and a cat — and scholars say he identified the Spaniards with the tzitzimime, the star-demons expected to descend at the world's end (unverified — V_M02: not on the Wikipedia page read). His house was searched and ritual objects seized (jade, gold disks, codices, per a secondary source (unverified — V_M02: not on the Wikipedia page read)). Sentenced 10 February 1537 to public humiliation and imprisonment in Seville, he was put on a ship for Spain — and the ship disappeared. No one knows what became of him. The odd detail: the first documented nahualli in the Inquisition's files is lost at sea on the way to prison — the record simply stops.",
