      "summary": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón (brother of the playwright Juan Ruiz de Alarcón), a parish priest acting with inquisitorial commission, wrote a manual in 1629 to help churchmen root out surviving Nahua religion. He recorded the incantations in Nahuatl with Spanish glosses — for curing, love, hunting, travel, sleep — and offered an etymology of nahualli: likely \"a person wrapped up or disguised under the appearance of such an animal, as they commonly believe.\" The only surviving manuscript copy is in the national museum. (English translation: Andrews & Hassig, University of Oklahoma Press, 1984.) The odd detail: a book written to destroy the spells is the reason the spells survive — the only copy sits in a Mexico City museum case.",
