      "summary": "Prospectors were in the Parral hills from the 1560s and the great silver strike of 1631 made it the \"Silver Capital of the World\"; the Palmilla mine's owner Pedro Alvarado Torres, the richest miner in Chihuahua, built the Alvarado Palace (1899–1904) and his family was said to be rich enough to offer to pay off Mexico's national external debt. The American Smelting and Refining Company (ASARCO), founded on El Paso's Chihuahua mining trade from 1881, bought a lead and zinc mine at Santa Eulalia; a smelter finished there in 1907 became the largest lead smelter in the world in the 1920s, and during the Revolution ASARCO ringed its Chihuahua smelter with forts, walls and pillboxes against raids. From 1917 to 1920 it expanded its holdings at Parral. The odd detail: an American company built pillboxes around a smelter in its own private siege — a fortified industrial town in a country at war, and the palace of a miner who could have paid the national debt.",
