      "summary": "The windigo is a malevolent cannibal spirit of winter, cold, famine and starvation, and a person can be possessed by or become one. In 1661 the Jesuit Relations recorded men \"seized with an ailment unknown to us,\" with \"more than canine hunger\" for human flesh, and that such men were put to death before they killed. In 1878–79 the Plains Cree trapper Swift Runner killed and ate his wife and five children only 25 miles from Hudson's Bay Company food supplies; he was hanged at Fort Saskatchewan. In 1907 the North-West Mounted Police arrested the Oji-Cree healer Jack Fiddler (Zhauwuno-geezhigo-gaubow, \"He who stands in the southern sky\") of the Sandy Lake–Deer Lake country, who said he had defeated fourteen wendigos — some sent by enemy shamans, some community members who had asked to be put down before they turned; he was charged over the killing of Wahsakapeequay, his brother Joseph's daughter-in-law. Jack slipped away on a walk and hanged himself (30 Sept 1907); Joseph was sentenced to death, and the order releasing him arrived three days after he died in 1909. Algernon Blackwood's \"The Wendigo\" (1910) brought the word into English horror; the 1930s were the decade of the ethnographers (John M. Cooper's \"The Cree Witiko Psychosis,\" 1933, and Ruth Landes's Ojibwa fieldwork, 1932–36 — see next entry — both not read this session). The odd detail: Jack Fiddler was a windigo-killer — the person his own people sent for — and Canada tried him for murder. The monster-hunter as defendant.",
