      "summary": "In 1673 Father Jacques Marquette saw two painted monsters on the limestone bluff, \"as large as a calf,\" with \"horns on their heads like those of a deer, a horrible look, red eyes, a beard like a tiger's, a face somewhat like a man's, a body covered with scales,\" and a tail winding round the body \"ending in a fish's tail,\" painted in green, red and black. The original was quarried away (c. 1846–47; nothing left by 1867). In August 1836 John Russell published \"The Piasa: An Indian Tradition of Illinois,\" with a man-eating bird slain by chief Ouatoga, who offered himself as bait under an invisible shield while warriors shot poisoned arrows — and later admitted to his son that he invented it; his gloss \"the bird that devours men\" is disputed. In 1896 Skinner guessed the carving might remember the last pterodactyls. In 1924 the 18-year-old Boy Scout Herbert Forcade painted a new Piasa on the cliff (dedicated 4 Oct 1924); Irvin H. Mawdsley repainted it in 1935 (in yellows) and again in 1938 (in reds and greens). The odd detail: The Piasa was repainted twice in the 1930s — the monster on the bluff was literally being refreshed in the setting's decade, in two different colour schemes.",
