      "summary": "Mitla is \"Lyobaa\" — \"place of rest\" — in Zapotec; the Nahuatl name means \"place of the dead.\" The Hall of the Columns (about 120 × 21 ft, six volcanic-stone columns) stands beside the Church of San Pablo, which the Spaniards built directly on pre-Hispanic foundations \"to keep the 'devil' from escaping\" (Wikipedia's account of the tradition). In 1674 the Dominican chronicler Francisco de Burgoa described a great underground temple beneath the site, of four connected chambers, the last closed by a stone door that led to a deep cavern; further chambers held a chapel and the burial place of Zapotec high priests, and a last \"dark and gruesome room\" where the bodies of those killed in battle by great lords were thrown. The Spanish missionaries who went down resolved to come out and had the \"infernal gate\" walled up with masonry. A local legend says that embracing the \"Column of Life\" tells you how long you have left. Alfonso Caso worked at Mitla in the 1920s–30s. (A 2022 INAH–UNAM geophysical survey found large cavities under the church group, where tradition puts Burgoa's entrance — outside the period.) The odd detail: a Dominican priest's own record of walling the door shut is the real-world version of the setting's line about something \"the Vatican sealed for three centuries\" (ALTERNATE_HISTORY §29).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High that Burgoa wrote it (1674); the \"to keep the devil from escaping\" rationale is a modern retelling, LABELLED tradition.",
