      "summary": "A silver vein found in 1683 near La Aduana produced a town of once ~30,000; the price fell in the 1890s and the mines played out by 1915. John Hays Hammond ran the Promontorio and Minas Nuevas mines. In the Revolution an Orozquista column reached Álamos on 21 August 1912 (650 defenders; \"over 100 dead men on the ground\" at La Aurora) and on 12 May 1915 500 Villistas attacked it — Major Mendoza, with 30 troops and 50 citizens, lost a quarter of them and then, ordering surrender, killed himself. The railway from Navojoa reached Álamos in 1908 and was disbanded in 1931; the town fell to about 1,000 people by 1933 and its mansions turned to ruins — but \"Álamos does not become a ghost town.\" The actress María Félix was born there on 8 April 1914. Álamos lies in Mayo country. The odd detail: the famous Mexican film star was born in a silver town that had already died of the Revolution.",
