      "summary": "In Hamlin's \"The Nun of Ste. Claire,\" the coureur de bois Jacques Morand loves the devout Geneviève Parent, who means to enter the Church. Refused, he goes to \"an old Indian witch\" who gives him power \"to change himself into the form of a wolf or Loup Garou (wehr-wolf).\" He hunts her down the beach; she throws herself at the feet of the Virgin's statue in a rocky grotto, and as the beast leaps onto the rocks it is turned to stone. Hamlin says the stone remained at Tonnancour and the habitants pointed at it and said reverently, \"Voilà le Garou!\" The odd detail: A werewolf you can still visit — a petrified wolf-shaped rock on a Grosse Pointe beach, by the 1930s the lakefront of Detroit's richest families (the Fords and Dodges built there).",
