      "summary": "Francisco Núñez de la Vega, Dominican Bishop of Chiapas and Soconusco from 1687, devoted his ninth Pastoral Letter to nagualismo and printed it in his Constituciones Diœcesanas (Rome, 1702). He found at Tlazoaloyan a hidden \"dark house\" said to have been built by the culture-hero Votan \"by blowing with his breath\", kept by a noble lady and guardians (tlapiane); he seized its lidded jars, a stone carved with ancient heroes, green chalchihuites and other objects and had them \"publicly burned in the plaza of Huehuetan\" (1691). He described the nagualists' calendar books in \"a secret cypher\" used to fix each newborn's fate, and an initiation in which the disciple lies on an ant-hill while the master calls a black, white and red snake that \"enters the body with a leap\". The odd detail: the ant-hill initiation — ants entering at the finger-joints, the snake passing through the body. A bishop printed it in Rome.",
