      "summary": "Legend says a fortune-teller warned Antoine de la Mothe Cadillac, Detroit's founder, to appease a red imp; he struck it with his cane (\"Get out of my way, you red imp!\") and lost everything — his post, his freedom, his fortune. The creature — very red in the face, a bright glistening eye, a grinning mouth of sharp teeth — is said to appear before disasters: the 1763 Battle of Bloody Run, Hull's surrender in 1812, and later calamities. The earliest written version is Marie Caroline Watson Hamlin's Legends of Le Détroit (1883), probably French lutin lore blended with Native tradition. The odd detail: It is a harbinger, not a killer: it shows up before the city's catastrophes.",
