      "summary": "Skinner's \"Were-Wolves of Detroit\" (from Hamlin's \"Le Loup Garou,\" set 1770): a werewolf carried off the bride Archange Simonet from her own wedding. Her bridegroom hunted it relentlessly; a neighbour shot off its tail with a silver bullet; the beast escaped into the river, and the habitants held that it \"jumped down the throat of a catfish\" — which, Skinner says, explained the French-Canadian prejudice against eating catfish. Hamlin's own chapter names the bridegroom Pierre La Fontaine (Lafontaine), a young farmer near Grosse Pointe, and Archange's father as the trapper Simonet — that much is CONFIRMED by independent secondary retellings of Hamlin's text. But the \"silver bullet … severed his tail … secured, dried and stuffed … wonder of the whole country\" resolution is WRONG as an account of Hamlin's chapter — that stuffed-tail/bullet ending belongs only to Skinner's short paraphrase (rightly credited to Skinner in the sentence above). Hamlin's own version has Pierre melt down his silver wedding gifts into multiple silver axe-heads and fight a running battle with the werewolf on horseback; no tail-severing or taxidermy resolution is reported in the sources read this session. (unverified — V_R22: exact Hamlin wording, not independently re-read this session; secondary agreement from two unrelated retellings) The odd detail: A werewolf hunted with silver axe-heads melted from wedding gifts, in a running battle on horseback — not the \"stuffed tail\" version this entry originally gave — with a fish-market taboo attached (Skinner's ending only).",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High as printed legend. • no tail-severing or taxidermy resolution is reported in the sources read this session. (unverified — V_R22: exact Hamlin wording, not independently re-read this session; secondary agreement from two unrelated retellings)",
