      "summary": "Named for James Kingsbury, \"the first inhabitant of Newburgh\" (1797). Railroads ran down it from 1857 (the Cleveland & Mahoning Valley); in the 1860s John D. Rockefeller's first crude-oil refinery stood in it, beside William Halsey Doan's oil and naphtha works. In 1930 the city hung the Sidaway Avenue Footbridge across it — Cleveland's only suspension bridge — linking the Polish St. Hyacinth (Jackowo) parish district to the Garden Valley neighbourhood on the Kinsman side — majority-Black by the 1960s, when the bridge was burned in the 1966 Hough riots, not a description of 1930. By the 1930s the floor of the Run held rail lines (and the Van Sweringens' Shaker Rapid) and hobo camps (R06: the Torso victims, the shantytown burning). The odd detail: Standard Oil — the Rockefeller fortune buried at Lake View — started in the same ravine where the headless bodies turned up seventy years later.",
