      "summary": "German religious dissenters fleeing Lutheran church persecution founded Zoar in 1817, organised as a commune — all property owned in common, farms, shops and factories managed by elected trustees. Joseph Bimeler (1778–1853), a pipemaker and teacher from Ulm, led it; his death on 31 August 1853 began its slow decline. In the 1850s it held more than 10,000 acres and about $1 million in assets, and had earned $21,000 building sections of the Ohio and Erie Canal. The residents voted to dissolve the society in 1898 and divide the property. Ten buildings are now a living museum run by the Ohio History Connection; it is a National Historic Landmark District (2016); levee repairs finished in 2023. The odd detail: A German communal village survived in Ohio for 81 years; the National Historic Landmark designation of 2016 still calls it \"an island of Old-World charm in east-central Ohio.\"",
