      "summary": "Born 2 Feb 1827 at Derbach in Prussia, Schoenhofen founded his brewery with Matheus Gottfried in 1861, bought him out in 1867, and grew it from ~600 barrels a year to over 10,000 (1868) and over a million barrels a year (1910) on the flagship Edelweiss; he gave $300 to the Turngemeinde (1879), beer to St. Paul's church fair, presided over the saloonkeepers (1884), and died of diabetes at 2500 Prairie Avenue on 7 Jan 1893 leaving $4,000,000 with \"liberal provisions for many charitable institutions\" (the Altenheim among them); his sons-in-law Joseph Theurer and Carl Buehl were executors on a $4,000,000 bond and ran the company. Schoenhofen's Hall at Milwaukee and Ashland was a North-West Side institution (Turner exhibitions 1900; the German war veterans' new organisation met there Oct 1900). In 1919 the company bought the Green River soft-drink recipe and lived through Prohibition on it and on grain storage; Peter Schoenhofen Theurer, president of the Schoenhofen Beverage Co., 1928 W. 18th St., died in April 1931; National Brewing bought the plant after repeal; Edelweiss ended in 1951. The wartime seizure of the company by the Alien Property Custodian, because part of the Schoenhofen inheritance sat with heirs resident in Germany, is general knowledge and UNVERIFIED this session (Google Books rate-limited). The odd detail: the Chicago brewery whose office and powerhouse are city landmarks is the one the federal government ran as enemy property — if the record holds — for the crime of having grandchildren in Germany.",
