      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Chihuahua city and the whole state — the Hacienda Encinillas",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(1.3 million acres) and the Banco Minero",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Chihuahua%20city%20and%20the%20whole%20state%20%E2%80%94%20the%20Hacienda%20Encinillas%20%20and%20the%20Banco%20Minero%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Luis Terrazas (born 20 July 1829, governor 1860–73, 1879–84, 1903–04) and his son-in-law Enrique Creel (1854–1931; son of the American consul; governor 1904–06 and 1907–11; ambassador to Washington; interpreter at the 1909 Díaz–Taft meeting) held together an estate of 50 haciendas and ranches, 8 million acres or more (some say 15 million), with over a million cattle, 225,000 sheep, 250,000 horses and 50,000 mules; Enrique's own haciendas came to 1.7 million acres. In December 1913, as provisional governor, Pancho Villa decreed the confiscation of the Chihuahua oligarchy's assets; Terrazas had fled to the United States. He returned in 1920 and in 1922 the federal government paid him 13 million pesos. He died in 1923. The odd detail: the largest landowner in the hemisphere was compensated by the same government that lost his state — because Villa's decree of 1913 proved, in the end, revocable.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the estate; the 13-million-peso compensation and the confiscation decree are from a search summary (Medium).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"who governs the ceded north\" answered by \"the family that already did\" — the American-blooded Creel and the Terrazas cattle barons, the Golden Circle's natural local partners. Their ranch archives are where the occupation's real title deeds sit.",
