      "summary": "Laid out in 1829 as Cross Street and called Canal Street by 1854, the street was the end of the Erie Canal: by the 1880s it held about 400 working women, 93 saloons and 15 concert-hall dives, and \"a murder every day\" is local lore. Dug's Dive, run by William Douglas, a former slave, is the supposed first \"dive bar\" (patrons went down steps from the towpath). The 1895 deepening of the canal and the railroads killed the trade; immigrant families — mainly Italian — moved in; the harbour end of the canal was mostly filled in by the 1930s, and later the Thruway, the Skyway and Memorial Auditorium were built over the district, leaving the Commercial Slip \"buried and forgotten\" until archaeologists dug it up. The odd detail: The tale told of the saloonkeepers is a poisoned drink, robbed clothes, and a body pushed into the canal — and in 1901 the neighbourhood's women attacked visiting sex workers from New York City and police had to escort them back north.",
