      "summary": "Haiti's criminal code defined poisoning as attempted murder. Under President Fabre Nicolas Geffrard (in power from 1859), who set out to eradicate \"old superstitions\", an 1864 amendment added the famous paragraphs: using against a person substances that, without causing death, produce a more or less prolonged lethargic state, is attempted murder by poisoning — and if the person was buried as a result, it is murder. Seabrook printed it in 1929 (as \"Article 249\", in English, and in French in his appendix) as evidence that zombies were real; the modern numbering is 246. The odd detail: the clause never uses the word zombi. The \"zombie law\" is a poisoning statute that the whole American zombie myth was made to stand on — including by the author who got its number wrong.",
