      "summary": "Joseph Smith renamed Commerce \"Nauvoo\" in 1840; by 1844 it had about 12,000 people, \"rivaling the size of Chicago\"; after Smith's killing (1844) the Mormons were driven west. French utopian socialists — the Icarians of Étienne Cabet — took over 1849–1856 (peak ~500). Then Germans: Nauvoo was the largest German-speaking community in Illinois for fifty years, with wine and fruit; Prohibition ended the wine, and in the 1930s the Nauvoo Blue Cheese company began aging cheese in the old Icarian wine cellars. Baxter's Vineyards (1857) is Illinois's oldest winery. The odd detail: Blue cheese maturing in the cellars dug by French socialists under a prophet's abandoned city.",
