      "summary": "According to traditions told to fishermen in the 1840s, a Potawatomi war party was caught by the Ho-Chunk (Winnebago) at a bluff in the mid-17th century, and a north wind and surf smashed their canoes on the rocks — hence the French Porte des Morts, door of the dead. A government engineer, Captain Brink, recorded the tradition in an 1834 survey; Hjalmar R. Holand printed it in his History of Door County, Wisconsin (1917). Shipwrecks in the strait included the Fleetwing (1888) and, in 1892, the A. P. Nichols, Forest and J. E. Gilmore, though the passage took relatively few lives. The odd detail: A whole Wisconsin county is named after a drowning.",
