      "summary": "193 emigrants from the Canton of Glarus founded the village; scouts Nicolas Duerst and Fridolin Streiff bought 1,200 acres on 17 July 1845 at $1.25 an acre; the party arrived 15 August 1845 after a four-month journey in which three died. New Glarus became a \"Cheese Capital of the World\" — 22 cheese factories at the 1905 peak — and Nickolaus Gerber started America's first Swiss cheese factory outside the village; today one Limburger plant, the last in North America, remains in the region. The 1930 census counted 1,010 people; the farm and dairy crisis before the war pushed the village toward tourism. (The Wilhelm Tell festival — Schiller's drama of a Swiss archer who defies an imperial governor — is a current event; the year it began, commonly given as 1938, is not on the page I read.) The odd detail: The village's signature festival is a play about a Swiss archer defying an imperial governor.",
