      "summary": "The Deutsche Evangelisch-Lutherische Synode von Missouri, Ohio und andern Staaten — the Missouri Synod — held its founding convention at St. Paul's in Chicago on 26 April 1847 and for thirty years served German-speaking Lutherans only; by 1897 it had 687,000 members and the largest Protestant parochial-school system in the country. Its teachers' seminary opened at Addison on 1 Sept 1864 with 43 male students aged 14–33 and moved to River Forest in 1913 as Concordia Teachers College, dedicated before an estimated 30,000–45,000 people. English crept in a generation before 1917 (\"the German-American school was a bilingual one … earlier than 1917\"), but the war \"hastened the Americanization of the church,\" and congregations cut German services altogether; by its 1947 centennial \"German\" was already gone from the synod's name, and that year it shortened \"The Evangelical Lutheran Synod of Missouri, Ohio, and other States\" to \"The Lutheran Church – Missouri Synod\". The odd detail: the most German church in America was founded in Chicago, and its 1913 River Forest dedication drew a crowd the size of a World Series.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a pastor's library in German; a parochial school whose primer teaches children to read the language the occult texts are in. The IHS regards the Missouri Synod's Chicago as the one Protestant ground where its own Latin is understood.",
