      "summary": "The Caste War of Yucatán had begun in 1847 (the Tepich uprising is dated 30 July 1847 in most accounts; one source says 30 June). In October or November 1850, in the fourth year of the war, a small cross appeared on a mahogany tree beside a cenote. It \"spoke.\" The rebel leader José María Barrera said it had been sent to help the Maya against the whites; the voice was that of Manuel Nahuat, an Indian ventriloquist who, in the version told by Heyden, had been instructed by Barrera, \"supposedly the inventor of the device.\" Juan de la Cruz preached its sermons. Nahuat was discovered by government troops and killed (1851 in one account, after a failed attack on Kampocolché on 4 January 1851), and the cross taken or destroyed — but other crosses appeared, some without voice (written messages attached to them, or a \"voice\" interpreted by patrons or priests), and three \"daughter\" crosses replaced the original at Chan Santa Cruz, with others at Tulum, Chancah, Chumpón and Muyil. Later leaders worked the oracle: after Barrera's death (31 December 1852) Venancio Puc took control and, per the HistoricalMX account, used trickery to make it speak; a man named José Nah was exposed as its \"speaker\" and executed in December 1863. The faith organised a Church: a Nohoch Tata (supreme pontiff), cardinals, archbishops and eighty priests, with a parallel military command. Because the Catholic Church had barred the Maya from ordination, the maestros cantores — lay choir-masters — became the priests. The crosses are dressed in huipiles and skirts, fed twice weekly with suckling pigs, and honoured with fiddle-and-drum mayapax, culminating each 3 May (the Day of the Holy Cross). Heyden and Villa Rojas note that speaking idols already belonged to the Maya religious tradition (the priests spoke for them at Cozumel), which is why the Cross was so quickly and fervently accepted. The odd detail: Britain recognised Chan Santa Cruz as a de facto independent country from the 1850s to 1893 and traded with it (arms and gunpowder among the trade); in 1887 the Cruzob petitioned London to make them a British protectorate.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the outline; the accounts differ on who first inspired the cross (Barrera vs Juan de la Cruz), whether the original was destroyed in 1851, and whether the \"ventriloquist\" explanation is the rebels' own belief or the government's charge — the entry reports the government-side charge as such and does not treat it as settled.",
