      "summary": "Gov. Nelson Dewey chose Waupun for the state prison on 4 July 1851; a temporary building held 40 men; the permanent South Cell Hall was finished in 1854 and is still in use. By 1918, under Warden Henry Town, there were 853 inmates; by 30 June 1934, under Warden Oscar Lee, the male population was 1,664, working ten outdoor camps including three logging operations, with 148 men in classroom instruction (40 hours a week, directed by a UW employee). The 1934 report says \"corporal punishment is a thing of the past, stripes and red uniforms following suit\"; guards worked 11–12 hour shifts. The 1934 legislative inquiry (Oscar Schrader entry, above) reached Waupun. John Schrank (Roosevelt's shooter) died in 1943 in the state's Central State Hospital for the Criminally Insane, which I believe stood at Waupun (not confirmed on a page read). The odd detail: In 1934 three of the state prison's ten outdoor camps were logging operations — Wisconsin's convicts were cutting timber outside the walls.",
