      "summary": "The Forty-Eighters' gymnastic-political clubs reached Chicago in 1852 (the Chicago Turngemeinde). In March 1861 its members formed the \"Union Cadets\" militia; in Oct 1861 they raised money for Hecker's regiment; in 1863–64 they built the Turnhalle on North Clark; in 1867 they sat in the anti-temperance delegate meetings; by 1871 they ran a Sunday school of \"almost a hundred.\" Nationally the Nordamerikanischer Turnerbund peaked in 1894 at 317 societies, ~40,000 men, 25,000 children, 3,000 women; 3,000 Turners performed at the 1893 Bundesturnfest in Milwaukee. Chicago's halls were the community's meeting rooms (the Alliance met at the Northside Turner Hall; the 1916 Kaiser's birthday overflowed it; the Austro-Hungarian bazaar was at the La Salle Turner Hall). In 1917–18 the societies went English-only to survive; many halls ended as dance halls, bars and bowling alleys; the American Turners' camp at Cary is where the Wooden Knight ended. The odd detail: the Turner halls were the first place a Chicago German went to drill, vote, sing, drink and — in 1861 — enlist; their 1917 answer to suspicion was to stop speaking.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a gymnasium where men have drilled with staves and rifles since 1861, under the same hall's roof — the Hunter Guild's Chicago chapter was a Turner squad first.",
