      "summary": "France transported more than 80,000 convicts to Guiana between 1852 and the last departures; mortality reached as high as 75% in the worst years. Under the doublage rule a convict who finished a sentence of under eight years had to stay in the colony as a libéré for as long again (life, for eight years or more) — free, penniless and unable to leave. Captain Alfred Dreyfus, falsely convicted of spying for Germany, was held on Devil's Island from 1895 to 1899. The journalist Albert Londres went in 1923 and his series in Le Petit Parisien (from 8 August 1923) made the bagne a national scandal; the Salvation Army's Charles Péan worked there from 1928. In 1938 the Daladier government, pushed by Guiana's deputy Gaston Monnerville, ended transportation; the last convicts went home in 1953. The odd detail: the most famous prisoner of the bagne was sent there for betraying France to Germany — and was innocent.",
