      "summary": "Born in Moscow on 16 November 1853 to a Cossack father and German mother, he deserted the Russian navy at Venezuela in 1871, made his way to Sonora and joined the Mexican army. He spoke nine languages (Russian, Polish, Spanish, French, Italian, English, German, Danish, Swedish), commanded the Gendarmería Fiscal (1885) and the Sonoran Rurales, was nicknamed the \"Mexican Cossack\" by American troops in the Apache wars, put down the 1896 \"Teresitas\" rising at Nogales, was at Cananea in 1906, and in 1913, with 400 men, surrendered to the American garrison at Nogales, Arizona rather than yield to Obregón's 2,000. He went to Los Angeles, became a postal translator, was recruited into the US Bureau of Investigation (1917), and was appointed Special Agent on 1 May 1922, handling border cases and Mexican informants until he resigned on 4 September 1926. He died in Los Angeles on 2 March 1928. The odd detail: a Russian-born Mexican colonel who once made 275 Arizona volunteers give up their weapons at Cananea ends as a federal agent liaising with the Mexican informants he used to command.",
