      "summary": "Founded \"34 years\" before 1887 (i.e. 1853 — the usual date is 1854), the German Society met arriving Germans, found them work and kept them from the runners. President William (Wilhelm) Vocke reported in Dec 1887 that in the year to 30 Nov 1886 111,696 immigrants had reached Chicago, 41,635 of them Germans; the society had aided 246 families and 221 individuals with $1,595.95, held a $13,000 fund and $4,000 in real estate, had just received its \"first large gift\" — a $500 Luise Hesing bequest — and \"never felt the need for the erection of a home for immigrants more than it does now.\" Vocke added that \"anarchistic disturbances of recent years\" (Haymarket) had \"prejudiced the American employer against German labor.\" The odd detail: 41,635 Germans in one year through one city — and the society's whole relief budget was $1,595.95.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the society's arrival registers since 1854 — every German who came through Chicago, by ship and name. The vampire who has been \"arriving\" every thirty years is in them four times.",
