      "summary": "August Willich, the exiled 1848 revolutionary, founded the Sociale Turnverein in 1853 (Turnverein Milwaukee from 1854 to 1940). At the peak one Milwaukeean in twelve had family in it; the Milwaukee gymnasts took the most prizes at the 1880 Turnfest at Frankfurt; Debs spoke at the hall in 1898; Berger led the South Side Turners from 1889. The Uihlein brothers, Frederick Pabst and Republican mayor Emil Wallber were members who objected to the socialists. When America entered the war the society made U.S. citizenship a condition of membership; it kept its minutes in German until the mid-1920s; by 1940 German was heard \"on rare occasion\" and only one of the eight city chapters was left. The odd detail: A gymnastic society whose two factions were the brewers and the socialists, meeting under an owl in the glass.",
