      "summary": "George Washington Lafayette Bickley, a Virginia doctor and newspaper editor, founded the Knights of the Golden Circle on July 4, 1854. The \"circle\" was literal: a ring centred on Havana with a radius of 16 degrees (about 1,200 miles — roughly 2,400 miles across; Handbook of Texas: \"some 2,400 miles in diameter\", https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/knights-of-the-golden-circle), taking in the American South, Mexico, Central America, Cuba, Haiti, the Dominican Republic and the Caribbean — a slave empire that could have yielded some 25 new slave states. The order had three degrees: the Knights of the Columbian Star (political), the True Faith (financial) and the Knights of the Iron Hand (military). Local lodges were called \"castles.\" During the war it spread into Kentucky, Tennessee, and the southern parts of Indiana, Ohio, Illinois and Missouri, then reorganised as the Order of American Knights (late 1863) and the Order of the Sons of Liberty (February 1864). The odd detail: the organisation was drawn as a compass circle on a map with a Caribbean capital at its centre — the Golden Circle's geography is a radius, not a country.",
