      "collection": "Chicago & Great Lakes",
      "synopsis": "From 1855 Chicago raised its street grade — five feet and more — to lay sewers above the swamp and fight cholera. Property owners either jacked their buildings up or moved the main entrance to the second floor to meet the new street; the gap between the old ground and the new pavement was left as vaulted, hollow sidewalks — rooms under the pavement that owners used for storage.",
