      "summary": "Pabst (Frederick Pabst, a lake-steamer captain who married into the Best family and bought half the brewery in 1863) was \"the nation's largest brewer\" by 1874 and in 1893 the first American brewer to sell over a million barrels in a year (unverified — V_R23); in Prohibition it made 8 million pounds of Pabst-ett cheese. Schlitz (Joseph Schlitz took August Krug's brewery in 1856, married his widow, drowned in the Schiller in 1875 — 335 dead; the Uihleins took over) was America's largest brewer in 1902, pioneered brown bottles in 1912, built the Schlitz Palm Garden and Schlitz Park in Milwaukee and tied houses across Chicago. Blatz (Valentin Blatz, 1850–52, merged with Johann Braun's City Brewery; 16,000 barrels by 1868) was the first Milwaukee brewer to bottle and ship nationally (1875), sold part to an English syndicate in 1891, and was the city's third brewer in the 1900s. Miller — Frederick Miller came from Hohenzollern in 1854 with \"a unique brewer's yeast\" and bought the little Plank Road Brewery in 1855 for $2,300; his family held it until 1966 — was the fourth. The political weight is in §3 above: the USBA, the Alliance, the anti-Prohibition fight. The odd detail: Milwaukee's second brewer died on a ship named Schiller; Chicago's German consulate sat in a building named Schiller; the Schwabenverein's first statue was Schiller. The name is a thread through the whole German Midwest.",
