      "summary": "The Gadsden Purchase of 1854 included provisions for American transport rights across the isthmus by \"plank road and railroad\"; the unratified McLane–Ocampo Treaty of 1859 would have granted extensive US transit rights. In 1857 Judah P. Benjamin, then a US senator, and a committee of New Orleans businessmen joined with the financier Peter A. Hargous and secured a Louisiana charter for the Louisiana Tehuantepec Company, which sold stock and sent survey teams; the papers run until Benjamin resigned from the Senate in February 1861 on Louisiana's secession. In the 1880s the engineer James B. Eads proposed a \"quadruple-track ship-railway\" across the isthmus. The Knights of the Golden Circle were founded in 1854 by George W. L. Bickley to extend the United States into Latin America; I found no documented link between the Tehuantepec company and the Knights — the two simply grew from the same soil. The odd detail: a man who would become a Confederate cabinet minister helped charter a company to build a railway over Mexico's narrow waist.",
