      "summary": "Founded in 1857 by the Dutch Jesuit Arnold Damen at Bishop O'Regan's request, dedicated 26 Aug 1860 (architect John Van Osdel); its 1874 tower (236 ft) was the tallest structure in Chicago until 1890, and by the 1890s it was the largest English-speaking Catholic congregation in the US, over 25,000 people, with a parish once reaching seven miles west to Austin. The O'Learys of 137 De Koven St. — whose barn was blamed for the fire — were parishioners (three children baptised there 1860–66). The fire started just across the parish line and the wind drove it east and north; the church and, the parish history claims, every parishioner's wooden cottage survived. Damen was preaching a mission at St. Patrick's in Brooklyn; the telegram reached him in the confessional; he prayed at the altar most of the night and vowed \"for all time\" to keep seven lights burning before the statue of Our Lady of Perpetual Help. Back in Chicago he charged the parish: \"My vow must be kept … so long as this church stands.\" The candles proved too hard to keep lit at night and were replaced by gas jets shaped like candlesticks (later electric); they burn in the east transept today. The odd detail: The 1923 parish history adds that Damen also held a novena \"for the banishment of the disturbing echoes in the church\" and set up a statue of the Virgin \"for the protection of the east wall\" — a Jesuit who prayed against sounds in his own building and posted a guardian on one wall.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the church's history; the vow story rests on the 1921 New World article and on living parishioners' testimony printed in 1923 (\"verified by many men and women still living\").",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Three layers: the period's reading (a pastor's vow answered), the folklore (the wind turned at the parish line), what is there (the seven lights are a ward, and Damen knew it — the echoes he prayed out of the nave and the wall he posted the Virgin on are the places something tried to come in). In the 1930s the lights are a Jesuit trust; an IHS agent would know which one has gone out.",
