      "summary": "In 1857 Michael von Koziczkowski arrived at Stevens Point with his wife, nine children and $50, seeking cheap farmland near the river; within a year three more families followed, and chain migration made a rural Polish settlement — by a search summary, more than a third of Portage County by 1900, the state's largest rural Polish population. The Stevens Point farmers' market began in 1870 when Polish farmers sold crops, animals and eggs at the square that is now Mathias Mitchell Public Square, and is billed as Wisconsin's longest-running. In 1871 Fr. Joseph Dabrowski moved his parish out of the rowdy saloon town of Ellis to a new site he called Polonia — later Sacred Heart, said to be the largest rural Catholic parish in Wisconsin (search summary; UNVERIFIED). Pulaski, in Brown County, is named for Casimir Pulaski and is now \"Polka Capital of the World\" with the annual Pulaski Polka Days; its diocese's bishop from 1915 was the Kashub Paul Rhode (above). The odd detail: A saloon town pushed the priest out; he named the new parish \"Polonia\" and the market it fed is still on the same square.",
