      "summary": "The vejigante is the horned, masked figure of Puerto Rican carnival, whose name comes from vejiga, the inflated bladder once used to strike onlookers. It stands either for the devil in the battle of good and evil or for the Moors St. James (Santiago) fought in Spain's Reconquista; in Loíza the masks are made from carved coconut and the festival — in July — fuses Spanish and African cultures; in Ponce the masks are papier-mâché with many horns. Craft skills were passed within families in both towns through the late 19th and early 20th centuries. The odd detail: the Ponce and Loíza masks are two different traditions — coconut in the Black coastal town, papier-mâché in the city — and both are called vejigante.",
