      "summary": "Chicago's City Cemetery lay where the south end of Lincoln Park is now; from the 1860s the city moved the graves (to Graceland, Rosehill, Calvary) and turned the ground into park — but an unknown number of bodies were never found, and construction in the park has turned up remains ever since. One mausoleum stayed: hotelier Ira Couch (Tremont House; third-richest man in Chicago) died in Cuba in 1857 and was entombed in a John M. Van Osdel mausoleum in 1858; it is the only marked grave still standing on the old cemetery ground. A plaque at the tomb states that six Couch family members and one family friend are inside, and the door has not been opened in living memory (unverified — V_R08). The odd detail: In 1932 the Chicago Historical Society opened its new museum on top of a graveyard, with a sealed tomb in its front yard (seven inside, by its plaque).",
