      "summary": "The Michigan Asylum for the Insane opened on 29 August 1859 under Dr. Edwin Van Deusen, built on the Kirkbride plan with a working \"Colony Farm\" whose cottage-like buildings were meant to be therapeutic; overcrowding forced the legislature to appropriate $400,000 in 1873 for a second hospital at Pontiac. It became the Kalamazoo State Hospital in 1911 and at its peak held about 3,500 patients, the largest mental hospital in Michigan (renamed Kalamazoo Regional Psychiatric Hospital 1 January 1978). [1930s conditions and deaths: UNVERIFIED — the pages I could reach did not detail them; the Kalamazoo Public Library and WMU pages returned 403.] The odd detail: It was built as a farm-colony — patients worked the land — and was so overcrowded by 1873 that the legislature paid $400,000 for a second hospital rather than enlarge it. The same city is the Yellow Kid Weil's Kalamazoo (R33).",
