      "summary": "Roque Rojas Esparza, a former seminarian and civil-registry judge in Mexico City, entered trance in the 1860s identifying himself with the prophet Elías (Elijah) and founded the Mexican Patriarchal Church of Elías, with seven churches after the seven seals of Revelation; the movement's own history dates his appointments of guides to 1863 and 1869, and his death to 18 May 1879. A woman, Damiana Oviedo, broke the rule against women leading churches and led the \"Sixth Seal\" oratory; by the movement's account \"by 1930 the Trinitarian Marian Spiritualism was more widespread than the church that gave it origin.\" Its temples, with an all-seeing eye and candles, hold cátedras in which mediums called \"pedestals\" receive the spirits of Jehovah, Jesus, Mary or Elías, with convulsions, and consult the spirits of dead doctors to heal the sick (healing on Tuesdays and Fridays). The odd detail: dead physicians prescribe through entranced \"pedestals\" in back-street temples of Mexico City — the Spiritism of the poor, beside Madero's Spiritism of the rich.",
