      "summary": "Founded in 1861 by German Jews as Chicago's first Reform congregation, Sinai moved its Sabbath service to Sunday in the nineteenth century. Its rabbi for forty-two years (1880–1923), Emil Gustav Hirsch — born in Luxembourg in 1851, son of the philosopher-rabbi Samuel Hirsch, trained at Berlin and Leipzig 1872–76, professor of rabbinical literature at the University of Chicago from 1892, editor of the Reform Advocate, a presidential elector in 1896 and a public-library trustee — preached social justice to Jews and gentiles alike, put Hannah G. Solomon in his pulpit in 1897, advised Julius Rosenwald to build the Southern Black schools, and stood on the Medinah Temple stage with Dernburg in Dec 1914 to say that Deutschtum \"is rather a matter of mind and spirit\" than of race and that Germany's armaments were self-preservation. He died 7 Jan 1923; his grandson was Attorney General Edward H. Levi. The odd detail: the intellectual head of Chicago Jewry defended the Kaiser's Germany in public in 1914 — the German-Jewish loyalty the setting takes as given.",
