      "summary": "The United States Brewers' Association was founded on 21 Aug 1862 at Pythagoras Hall, New York, by 37 (German) breweries against the Civil War beer tax; it won a refund of the 1863 lager tax and held the malt-beverage tax flat from 1864 to 1898 while the tax on distilled liquors was raised three times. At its 1867 Chicago convention it resolved to oppose any candidate \"disposed towards the total abstinence cause.\" Chicago's brewers protested taxation as a body in Sept 1875 (Schoenhofen among them); in March 1884 Peter Schoenhofen presided over the saloonkeepers' convention; in 1889 the Staats-Zeitung counted ten German-American millionaires in Chicago (fortunes of $1–5 million): E. J. Lehmann (The Fair store), Conrad Seipp (brewer), Jacob Rosenberg, Otto Young, George Schneider (banker, ex-editor), Jacob Rahm, Conrad Fuerst, Henry A. Cohn, Michael Brand (brewer) and Peter Schoenhofen (brewer) — three brewers in ten, and \"at least one thousand\" Chicagoans worth $500,000–$1,000,000. In Dec 1903 the Alliance's Hexamer petitioned against increased prohibition enforcement; in 1918 the Senate examined how brewers' money had flowed into the Alliance (general knowledge — the Washington Times purchase and the Wheeler charges are UNVERIFIED here). The odd detail: the German brewers wrote Chicago's anti-temperance politics into a national trade resolution in 1867; three of the ten richest Germans in the city in 1889 made beer.",
