      "summary": "Born 12 Aug 1862 in Springfield, Illinois, to Samuel Rosenwald, a German-Jewish clothier who arrived nearly penniless, and Augusta Hammerslough, Rosenwald and Aaron Nusbaum bought Alvah Roebuck's half of Sears for $75,000 in 1895; vice-president and treasurer 1895–1907, president from 1908 (sales from $750,000 to over $50 million), chairman after 1924; Goldman Sachs took the firm public in 1906 with $40 million of stock. The Rosenwald Fund (1917) gave over $70 million in his lifetime — nearly 5,000 schools for Black children in the South with Booker T. Washington, plus universities, museums and Jewish charities; he sat on the Tuskegee board from 1912 and the Chicago Commission on Race Relations in 1919. He paid for the Museum of Science and Industry after seeing the Deutsches Museum with his son (R15 has Oskar von Miller's 1925 visit). He died 6 Jan 1932 at Highland Park, aged 69. The odd detail: the richest German Jew in Chicago copied Munich to give the city its science museum, and the museum's godfather was hosted by the brother of the setting's President Dawes.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
