      "summary": "Arrested May 5, 1863 under Burnside's General Order No. 38 after a May 1 speech at Mount Vernon, Vallandigham was tried by military court (May 6–7) and deported through Confederate lines on Lincoln's order (May 19, 1863). He went to Canada via Bermuda, settled in Windsor, won the Ohio Democratic nomination for governor in absentia 411–11 and ran his campaign from a Windsor hotel, losing 288,374 to 187,492. Around March 1864 he led the Sons of Liberty; he slipped back into Ohio \"under heavy disguise\" on June 14, 1864, and wrote the peace plank at the Chicago convention. On June 17, 1871, defending an accused murderer in Lebanon, Ohio, he demonstrated how the victim could have shot himself — with a pistol he thought was unloaded. It fired into his abdomen; he died the next day. His client was acquitted. The odd detail: the Copperhead chief killed himself proving a man could kill himself by accident — and won the case.",
