      "summary": "Born 2 February 1863 in Livingston Parish, Louisiana, Christmas worked as a Lake Pontchartrain tugboat pilot, then an Illinois Central brakeman (McComb, 1879), fireman and, by 22, a locomotive engineer on the Memphis–New Orleans run. In 1897 he fell asleep after 54 hours on duty, caused a collision, was cleared, but failed a colour-blindness test, which ended his career. He had already moved to Puerto Cortés in 1894 as a railroad engineer. On 14 April 1897 rebels captured his train at the Laguna trestle; he joined Manuel Bonilla's rebels, was commissioned on the spot, and earned the name \"the Incredible Yanqui\". He then changed sides three times: 1899 defected to the government as a colonel, and police chief of Tegucigalpa from May 1902, under President Terencio Sierra; 1903 back to Bonilla, a general when Bonilla became president; 1907 wounded in a failed Nicaraguan invasion and exiled to Guatemala; 1910–11 the coup in Part A-3. He lost influence when Bonilla died in 1913, was turned down as too old to volunteer in the Great War, patented a baited rattrap and a railroad device meant to prevent engineer-fatigue accidents, caught tropical sprue, married Ida Culotta in 1914, and died in New Orleans on 21 January 1924 of acute anemia, aged 60. The odd detail: the most famous mercenary of the Caribbean began as a railroad man who fell asleep at the throttle — and patented a device against exactly that.",
