      "summary": "Camp Douglas held ~26,060 Confederate prisoners 1862–65; deaths are counted at 4,454 (official), 4,243 names on the monument, 5,000–6,000 by modern estimates, muddied by an undertaker who sold bodies to medical schools. The dead were moved from City Cemetery and a smallpox ground to Oak Woods, buried in concentric trenches, and the Confederate Mound monument was dedicated in 1895. Beginning on the night of November 6–7, 1864 — two days before the November 8 election — Col. Benjamin Sweet arrested about 106 suspected conspirators, including leaders of the Sons of Liberty (the Copperhead successor of the Knights of the Golden Circle), for a plot to free the prisoners and burn Chicago; over half were released, and historians still argue whether the plot was real. Oak Woods also holds Ida B. Wells (d. 1931), Cap Anson, Jesse Owens, Enrico Fermi, Harold Washington and Big Bill Thompson (d. 1944); it desegregated only in 1963. The odd detail: The largest Confederate burial in the North is on Chicago's South Side, and the Golden Circle's own men were arrested in the city seventy years before the setting's decade.",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
