      "summary": "Teobert (Teoberto) Maler (b. Rome, 12 January 1842; usually described as German-born-in-Italy and later an Austrian subject — not on the page read, UNVERIFIED) came to Mexico in 1864 as a cadet in Emperor Maximilian's expedition, rose to captain, and stayed after the Empire fell in 1867. From 1876 he photographed Maya ruins — Palenque (1877), Chichén Itzá, and remote Usumacinta and Petén sites — until 1905; Harvard's Peabody Museum published his reports from 1898, but they quarrelled over how much he could print and parted in 1909 (last volumes 1912). Ruined by bad investments and the 1907 crash, he lived in Mérida selling prints of his photographs to tourists and young archaeologists, and died there on 22 November 1917. Much of his work was published only in the 1930s, 1970s and 1990s. The odd detail: the man whose glass plates are still the record of half the Maya cities was a soldier of a failed European emperor of Mexico, who died poor in Mérida in the middle of the Great War.",
