      "summary": "William Boyington's 1864 castellated Lemont-limestone gate is the twin of his Water Tower (1869); the name is either pioneer Hiram Roe's hill misspelled or the wild white roses that grew there. Sidney Lovell's 1914 community mausoleum — Chicago's largest — has 30-plus stained-glass windows, several by Tiffany, and holds both Aaron Montgomery Ward and Richard Warren Sears, the rival mail-order kings. Also here: 16 Union generals, Julius Rosenwald (1932), Charles Hull (Hull House's landlord), Oscar Mayer, victims of the Iroquois fire and the Eastland, and Vice President Charles G. Dawes (1951). Legends of Lulu Fellows and Frances Pearce are folklore (not on the page). The odd detail: Sears and Ward, who fought for forty years, spend eternity in the same marble corridor; the gate to it is the Water Tower's sister.",
