      "summary": "Confederate Acting Master John Yates Beall and his men boarded the Detroit–Sandusky steamer Philo Parsons as passengers on September 19, 1864, seized her, and also took the steamer Island Queen. The plan was to capture the USS Michigan, the only U.S. warship on the Great Lakes, and free the Confederate officers held on Johnson's Island. Their inside man, Capt. Charles H. Cole, had posed in Sandusky as secretary of the \"Mount Hope Oil Company of Titusville, Pennsylvania\", befriended the Michigan's officers, and meant to drug their wine at dinner. A tip reached the Union; an officer boarded the Michigan shortly before midnight and told Cole \"Captain Cole, you are my prisoner.\" Beall's crew refused to attack without the signal, and Beall ran for Canada and scuttled the Parsons off Sandwich, Ontario. The odd detail: the plan to seize a warship hinged on a fake oil-company secretary and a dinner party with drugged wine.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
