      "summary": "Lambdin P. Milligan was arrested at home on October 6, 1864; a military commission sat from October 21. On December 10, 1864, Milligan, William A. Bowles and Stephen Horsey were convicted and sentenced to hang on May 19, 1865; President Johnson commuted the sentences on May 30, 1865. On April 3, 1866, the Supreme Court ruled in Ex parte Milligan that military tribunals could not try civilians where civil courts were open; Milligan was freed April 12, 1866. He later sued and won — damages capped by statute at five dollars plus costs (May 30, 1871). The odd detail: a man sentenced to hang for treason won his lawsuit against his jailers and was awarded five dollars.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the legal ceiling on both federal badges — Milligan is why the Secret Service and Section 13 alike need a civilian court or a quiet disappearance; the Golden Circle's lawyers know the case by heart.",
