      "summary": "The plan was to strike on Election Day, November 8, 1864: free the roughly 8,000 prisoners, sack the city, then move on the prisoners at Rock Island, Springfield and Alton. An informant told Col. Benjamin Sweet, who had only about 900 guards, and after midnight on November 6–7 his men made simultaneous arrests. At the Richmond House they took the English soldier of fortune George St. Leger Grenfell and J. T. Shanks; at 70 Adams Street, Col. Vincent Marmaduke; at Walsh's, Walsh himself, Capt. Cantrill of Morgan's command and Charles Traverse. Walsh's house was searched without a warrant (unverified — V_R10) and yielded shotguns \"all loaded with cartridges, composed of from nine to twelve largest-sized buckshot, and capped\", plus 10-inch Joslyn revolvers, also loaded. 106 men were arrested; over half were released, and the rest went before a military commission in Cincinnati. The odd detail: the loaded, capped shotguns were stored in a house within a long rifle-shot of the camp fence — the arsenal was next door to its target.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on arrests, date, and arms; historians still debate how real the plot was versus Sweet's exaggeration (Wikipedia notes \"real or a hoax\" debate). • Walsh's house was searched without a warrant (unverified — V_R10) and yielded shotguns \"all loaded with cartridges, composed of from nine to twelve largest-sized buckshot, and capped\", plus 10-inch Joslyn revolvers, also loaded. 106 men were arrested;",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the Secret Service's 1865 founding (Col. William P. Wood) lands months after this plot — the Secret Service could date its Golden Circle file to November 1864, and the unresolved \"was it a hoax?\" gives the three-layers-of-truth structure for free.",
