      "id": "R13-035",
      "title": "Chicago's drinking water: cribs, keepers and no filters",
      "date_start": "1865",
      "date_end": "1935",
      "date_label": "1865–1935 (the Dever Crib, 1935)",
      "category": "Science, forensics, medicine, radio & media, weather & disasters: Chicago and the Great Lakes, 1929–1939",
      "tags": [
        "science",
        "media",
        "Chicago's",
        "drinking",
        "keepers"
      ],
      "people": [
        "—"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Lake Michigan off Chicago: Two-Mile Crib",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(1865), Four-Mile (1891), 68th Street (1892), Lake View (1896), Carter H. Harrison (1900), Edward F. Dunne (1909), Lawrence Avenue (1915), Wilson Avenue (1918),",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Lake%20Michigan%20off%20Chicago%3A%20Two-Mile%20Crib%20%2C%20Four-Mile%20%2C%2068th%20Street%20%2C%20Lake%20View%20%2C%20Carter%20H.%20Harrison%20%2C%20Edward%20F.%20Dunne%20%2C%20Lawrence%20Avenue%20%2C%20Wilson%20Avenue%20%2C%20Willia"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Chicago drank raw lake water drawn through offshore intake cribs and tunnels 10–20 feet wide up to 200 feet beneath the lake bed. Keepers lived on the cribs (the Four-Mile had a heating plant from 1898 so men could winter there at 70°F; the 68th Street and Dunne cribs were joined by a footbridge so one crew served both). During the 1909 construction of the Dunne crib tunnel, fire broke out in the wooden barracks on the Intermediate Crib and about 70 tunnel workers burned or drowned; survivors reached rescue boats on floating ice. The Dever Crib, the last, went up in 1935. The water was chlorinated but not filtered until the South District (Sawyer) plant of 1947 (unverified — V_R13) — everything a 1930s Chicagoan drank came straight from the lake with a dose of chlorine. The odd detail: men living alone in stone towers two and three miles out on the lake, all winter, tending the city's water — and no filter between the lake and the tap.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on cribs and 1909 fire; Medium on the \"unfiltered until 1947\" statement (the page mentions only the 1947 plant; chlorination dates not in it). • The water was chlorinated but not filtered until the South District (Sawyer) plant of 1947 (unverified — V_R13) — everything a 1930s Chicagoan drank came straight from the lake with a dose of chlorine.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Owner's wish list — loss of water. The crib keeper's log, three miles out, on the night the intake ran dark for six hours and the city never knew.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Water cribs in Chicago — Wikipedia",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_cribs_in_Chicago",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "kind": "encyclopedia article (follow its citations for strong claims)",
          "supports": ""
        }
      ],
