      "summary": "Born 22 August 1865 in Knoxville, Iowa, Cherrie went to work in sawmills at 12, trained as a mechanical engineer at Iowa State, then took up taxidermy and natural history. He worked at the National Museum of Costa Rica (1889–97) and was assistant curator of ornithology at Chicago's Field Museum (1894–97) — that is, on the Chicago staff in the years right after the 1893 fair (Wikipedia's Costa Rica and Chicago dates overlap, so he was probably a collector on a Chicago appointment — UNVERIFIED). His career spanned about forty expeditions in Central and South America. On the Roosevelt expedition he was the AMNH's bird collector and one of the three Americans on the river. In 1925 he was zoological collector on the Simpson–Roosevelts Asiatic Expedition with Kermit and Theodore Jr. His memoir is Dark Trails: Adventures of a Naturalist (1930). He was made an Honorary Scout in 1927 and died on 20 January 1948 in Newfane, Vermont. The odd detail: the Guild-founding expedition's bird man had lived in Chicago (unverified — V_M19: Wikipedia gives an assistant-curatorship at the Field Museum 1894–97, overlapping his Costa Rica post, but does not say he lived in Chicago) — as an employee of the Field Museum — during the very years of the World's Columbian Exposition's aftermath.",
