      "summary": "Born 5 May 1865 in Mato Grosso of Portuguese, Spanish, Guaná, Terena and Bororo ancestry; entered the army in 1881, second lieutenant 1888. From 1890 he led the Rondon Commission, building over 4,000 miles of telegraph lines through the interior; between 1900 and 1906 he extended lines to Bolivia and Peru, made peaceful contact with the Nambikwara and \"pacified\" the Bororo. His 1909 expedition discovered the River of Doubt. As first director of the Indian Protection Service (1910) he set its motto: \"die if need be, never kill.\" After the Roosevelt expedition he mediated the Colombia–Peru dispute over Leticia (1934–38), backed the Villas-Bôas brothers' campaign for the Xingu National Park (1961), was made a Marshal on his 90th birthday (1955) and nominated for the Nobel Peace Prize (1957). The state of Rondônia bears his name. He died on 19 January 1958, aged 92. The odd detail: the man who guided the Guild's founder was a positivist who forbade his men to fire — the philosophical opposite of the mercenary and the Marine.",
