      "summary": "The ciguapa is a small, naked woman of the mountain forests with knee-length hair and, in later versions, feet that point backwards — so her tracks lead the wrong way. Nocturnal and impossibly agile, she lures men who wander the woods at night. The first known written account is Francisco Javier Angulo Guridi's \"La Ciguapa\" (1866), where she is a beautiful Ciguayo (Taíno-region) woman about three feet tall with golden skin; the backwards feet and blue or dark skin came later. Scholars debate whether it preserves Taíno or maroon tradition or is 19th-century \"fakelore\"; the Trujillo regime used it for its mestizo (anti-African) ideology. The odd detail: the thing that makes the ciguapa uncatchable — backwards feet — is not in her first attestation.",
