      "summary": "For nearly a century Elgin's works were \"the world's largest site dedicated to watchmaking\", making about half of all American pocket watches. In 1910 the company built its own astronomical observatory to set precise time for its watches — railway time for much of the country ran, in effect, off an Elgin star-sighting. The Elgin National Road Races (1910–1920) ran on 8.5 miles of oil-soaked dirt and gravel with \"no crossroads, railroad tracks, high hills or towns\"; five drivers died (three in 1911, two in 1914); farmers' objections ended them after 1920. They were revived for the World's Fair on 26 August 1933: Phil Shafer won the National Trophy in a Buick, and one 1933 race is counted among the first organised stock-car races in the US. The odd detail: A factory with a telescope — the watch company checked the stars nightly so that a whole region's clocks would agree.",
