      "summary": "The first Polish parish in Chicago (1867), run by the Resurrectionist order since 1869. Its first great pastor, Fr. Wincenty Barzynski, founded 23 Polish parishes, schools, a college, orphanages, newspapers, St. Mary of Nazareth Hospital and the Polish Roman Catholic Union's headquarters. By 1908 it had more than 35,000 parishioners, among the largest parishes in the country. The Keely church (1877–81) seats 1,500 with an altar painting by Żukotyński and Tiffany chandeliers. The odd detail: One parish with a population the size of a small city — a Polish state-within-the-city run by a religious order.",
