      "summary": "After Naperville won an 1857 vote for county seat, Wheaton won an 1867 re-vote 1,686 to 1,635; when Naperville would not hand over the records, Wheaton men made the \"Midnight Raid\" on the Naperville courthouse and carried them off — some were then kept in Chicago and burned in the 1871 fire. In 1887 Wheaton banned the sale of alcohol (it stayed dry until 1985). Wheaton College made it an evangelical town; in 1926 the Theosophical Society in America built its national headquarters here (R05, R10); and Col. McCormick's estate Cantigny is on the edge of town (R08, R19). The CA&E served it 1902–1959. The odd detail: One small dry Protestant college town held both the Theosophists' American seat and a Tribune colonel's estate named for a battle — and it got its courthouse by night raid.",
