      "summary": "The reservation was created on 19 March 1867 when ten Ojibwe chiefs met President Andrew Johnson; it covered about 1,300 square miles. The Nelson Act of 1889 aimed to move all Minnesota Ojibwe except Red Lake here and to open their timber. What followed is documented as fraud: by 1920 non-Indians had acquired roughly 93 per cent of the original ~836,000 acres (unverified — V_R39: not on the Wikipedia page cited as fetched, which says only that under ten per cent was tribal-owned by 1989); a 1909 investigation by the federal Board of Indian Commissioners documented over 1,200 fraud cases (unverified — V_R39) — forged deeds, sales coerced by alcohol or threat, collusion with local officials — and lumber companies used mixed-descent men to buy timbered allotments cheaply. \"The White Earth Tragedy\" is the name for what followed: landless families with no income. By the 1930s Indians owned under 10 per cent of White Earth, about 4 per cent of Leech Lake and about 7 per cent of Mille Lacs (search summary), and a large share of tribal members had left the reservation. Winona LaDuke's White Earth Land Recovery Project (1989) began the slow buying back. The odd detail: A reservation created by treaty in 1867 had lost about 93 per cent of its land by 1920 — and a federal investigation had documented over 1,200 frauds eleven years earlier, in 1909.",
