      "summary": "St. Stanislaus Kostka (1867) was Chicago's first Polish parish; its pastor Rev. Vincent Barzynski founded 23 Polish parishes, schools, orphanages, newspapers and St. Mary of Nazareth Hospital. With Holy Trinity it formed \"the largest parish in the world,\" over 60,000 members in the early 1900s; by 1898 eleven contiguous precincts were 86.3% Polish and one precinct had exactly one non-Pole among 2,500 people. Nearly every major Polish-American organization sat within a few blocks of the Triangle — the Polish National Alliance (1514–1520 W. Division), the Polish Roman Catholic Union and its Polish Museum, the daily Dziennik Związkowy. Holy Trinity (founded 1872) fought St. Stanislaus for twenty years over whether it could be a parish at all, until an Apostolic Delegate from the Vatican settled it and gave it to the Congregation of Holy Cross under Fr. Casimir Sztuczko; its present church (1905–06, $200,000) is a \"Polish Cathedral.\" The odd detail: a precinct with one outsider in 2,500; and a church war so bitter Rome had to send its own envoy to Chicago.",
