      "summary": "Wausau grew from 12,354 (1900) to 27,268 (1940). By 1906 the lumber was gone, and the city was rescued by diversification, led by Employers Insurance of Wausau (now part of Liberty Mutual) and the group of businessmen called the Wausau Group; the 1929 crash hit hard — layoffs and closures. In the mid-1920s Ben and Judd Alexander built an airport on the southeast side, on the understanding that the city would take it over after five years; by the early 1920s Marathon County had 2,200 miles of hard-surfaced roads. The Wausau Ski Club (formed 1927) built a big ski slide off Stark Street in late 1929. (See Fromm's ginseng and Rib Mountain above.) The odd detail: A lumber town whose forests were gone in 1906 became the home of an insurance company.",
