      "summary": "The liberal general Refugio I. González (1814–1892) founded La Ilustración Espírita in Guadalajara in 1868, moved it to Guanajuato in 1869, and in 1872 translated Kardec's Gospel According to Spiritism and co-founded the Sociedad Espírita Central in Mexico City with Manuel Plowes and Santiago Sierra — the national centre, recognising circles in Guadalajara, Guanajuato, San Luis Potosí, Monterrey and Tampico. Porfirian insiders — the positivist Porfirio Parra, Foreign Minister Ignacio Mariscal — sat in séances. González's journal printed Teresa Urrea's cures (above). Francisco Madero wrote a Manual espírita (1911) — see M01 \"Madero the spiritist\" for his dictation from his dead brother and Juárez. A Theosophical Logia Aura was founded in Mexico City in 1906; Krishnamurti's Order of the Star of the East appears in the early 1920s. The odd detail: a Mexican Spiritist network had circles in Monterrey and Tampico — both on the American side of the setting's treaty line.",
