      "summary": "Benjamin Johannes Viljoen, a Boer general (birth year given as 1868 and 1869), wrote My Reminiscences of the Anglo-Boer War (1902). Boer refugees were given land near Ortiz, Chihuahua, and Viljoen was among those who tried to colonise it; he later organised Boer settlements in Doña Ana County, New Mexico (Berino, Chamberino and La Mesa) and took American citizenship in 1909. In 1911 he served as chief of staff and military adviser to Madero, and afterwards Madero named him peace commissioner to the Yaqui of Sonora. According to the Holmdahl article, Viljoen met the Yaquis at Guaymas with Holmdahl as an escort; the Yaquis refused to disarm; he resigned under heavy criticism and left Mexico. He died on 14 January 1917 on his farm at La Mesa, New Mexico, and is buried in the Masonic Cemetery there. A Stellenbosch University thesis title calls his life \"restless\" (Aweregs). The odd detail: a general of the Boer Republic ended up negotiating peace with the Yaqui for a Mexican president, and is buried in New Mexico among Boer neighbours.",
