      "summary": "James Duggan (b. 22 May 1825) was Bishop of Chicago from 1859; he consecrated Calvary Cemetery himself on All Souls' Day, 2 Nov 1859. After the Second Plenary Council of Baltimore (Oct 1866) he began showing \"mood swings, erratic behavior and signs of psychological stress\"; in 1868 he dismissed four priests who had been his close advisers, and clergy wrote to Rome questioning his competence. On 14 April 1869 Pius IX ruled him no longer mentally capable and sent him to a sanatorium run by the Sisters of Charity in St. Louis. In Sept 1880, \"sufficiently rational,\" he signed his resignation; he died in the same sanatorium on 27 March 1899 and was buried at Calvary. Meanwhile Archbishop James Quigley built the Bishops' Mausoleum at Mount Carmel (1905–12; exterior William J. Brinkmann, interior Aristide Leonori — marble, a domed tiled ceiling, crypts either side of the altar, mosaics of Celtic, Nordic and Slavic saints, the roof a stepped pyramid crowned by the Archangel Gabriel) and moved his predecessors' bodies into it; Quigley himself was laid there in July 1915. Duggan was not moved until 29 March 2001. The odd detail: Through the whole of the 1930s Chicago's bishops lay together in Quigley's chapel on the hill at Hillside — except the one Rome had declared mad, who stayed alone in the lakeside cemetery he had consecrated on the Day of the Dead. (And Holy Name's ceiling was still bare: a cardinal's red galero is hung above the cathedral apse only after his death, \"and stays there until it disintegrates\" — Mundelein's, the first and now the centre of six, went up after October 1939.)",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
