      "summary": "Founded in 1869 by the German-Jewish families of the South Side (the Staats-Zeitung listed it among the South Side's societies in 1872), the Standard Club voted $1,000 for Russian-Jewish relief in April 1892, hosted charity balls and Sinai concerts, and in June 1922 \"decided to move into the Loop\": a $2,000,000 building at 307–25 S. Plymouth Court on a 99-year lease at $60,000 a year ($5,940,000 over the term). Through the 1920s it was where Jewish Chicago's money met: the Chicago Hebrew Institute banquet (May 1922, \"100,000 Jews in the Lawndale District\"), the United Jewish Charities' $250,000 campaign opened with a fish dinner (March 1923), B'nai B'rith's annual meeting (1924), the $250,000 United Jewish Campaign conference (Oct 1927), and a dinner for Judah L. Magnes of the Hebrew University (April 1931). Its presidents ran the Jewish Aid Society, the Federated Charities, and K.A.M. and Sinai. The odd detail: the club's own newspaper listings are a directory of the German-Jewish establishment — every obituary of the 1920s–30s ends \"member of the Standard Club.\"",
