      "summary": "Congress created the National Asylum on 3 March 1865; George H. Walker won the Northwestern Branch for Milwaukee (1866–67) after a month-long Soldiers' Aid Fair on Main Street raised the money. E. Townsend Mix's Victorian Second Empire Old Main (1869), the Governor's Residence (c. 1867), the hospital (1879), Ward Memorial Hall (1882 — a theatre, meeting room, store, restaurant and railroad ticket office under one roof), the chapel (1889), the Wadsworth Library (1892) and the headquarters (1894) made a self-contained veterans' town with its own lake. In 1930 it passed to the new Veterans Administration and became the \"Wood, Wisconsin\" station; a paint shop went up in 1938. It is one of three surviving soldiers' homes in the country and a National Historic Landmark (2011). The odd detail: A theatre that sold railway tickets, in a walled town of old soldiers on the edge of the German city.",
