      "summary": "The Union Coal, Iron and Transportation Company started it in 1869; reorganised in 1873 as the Joliet Iron and Steel Company, it began operations in 1879, with two 72-foot blast furnaces (two more added in 1904 and 1906); it was the ninth US plant to use the Bessemer converter and made over 300 tons of steel a day. By the early 1900s it employed more than 2,000 men on a payroll above $2 million, 12 hours a day, six days a week, at $2–$9 a day. The work force was drawn from northern and western Europe first, then from eastern and southern Europe (Poles, Slovenes, Swedes named in a summary). After the crash the U.S. Steel-owned works shut down in 1933; the machinery was shipped to other U.S. Steel plants by the end of the decade and only foundations remain. U.S. Steel gave 45 acres to CorLands in the early 1990s; the site opened as a historic site in 1998. The odd detail: The town lost its steel works and its canal in the same year — 1933 — and was left with a prison, a limestone quarry and a theatre.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the outline; the 1933 closure is confirmed on the Reconnect with Nature page.",
