      "summary": "From 696 families (3,252 people) in 1870 to about 10,000 by 1890, with the biggest wave 1900–14. By the late 1880s \"more than half\" of Czech-Americans had left the Church; each settlement had a church and a freethinkers' hall facing it — St. Wenceslas (founded 1867, rebuilt 1892 at Broadway and E. 37th), St. Procop (1874, West Side) and Our Lady of Lourdes on one side; Bohemian National Hall and Česká Síň Sokol Hall (freethinker) on the other, plus the Catholic Karlin Hall. Sokol gymnastic units from 1879; a socialist weekly (Americké Dělnické Listy, 1909); a Catholic paper (American); ten of the Cleveland Orchestra's 57 charter members were Czech. Monsignor Oldřich Zlámal, pastor of Our Lady of Lourdes, pushed Cleveland Czechs into politics. The odd detail: The schism \"forced every Czech-American to make a choice\" — church or hall — and the neighbourhood was laid out that way, building facing building.",
