      "summary": "After 1870 a permanent Polish settlement grew around St. Stanislaus, \"Mother Church of Polonia in Buffalo\"; the East Side became the second-largest Polish-American community in the country, with over 75,000 people of Polish descent in the city by 1940, mostly labourers in the rail yards, foundries and tanneries. Corpus Christi (architects Schmill and Gould, dark sandstone, copper domes; now Pauline Fathers, on the National Register since 2007) was the seventh of fourteen Polish Catholic parishes. A search-summary of the parish history says that in 1929 about 300 homes within the parish were demolished to make way for the New York Central Terminal. Dom Polski (\"Polish Home\") opened in 1889 as \"The Polish Literary and Assembly Rooms Association, Inc.\" (same source). A Dyngus Day parade (Easter Monday) is anecdotally the country's largest. The odd detail: The Terminal (R07) was built for 200 trains a day on land that was, until 1929, a neighbourhood — the 300 houses are the price of the \"Babylonian\" city's grand entrance.",
