      "summary": "From the 1870s Kashubian Poles and Pomeranian Germans built a fishing village on the island without deeds or the city's leave — up to 1,600 people in \"a picturesque jumble of homes, saloons, fish sheds, and net reels,\" on marsh they filled themselves. The Illinois Steel Company sued them as squatters; the city, wanting the ground for the harbour and the 1910s–20s sewage plant (whose product is R20's Milorganite), evicted them family by family through the 1910s and 1920s. The last resident left in 1943. The island had held the cholera \"pest house\" in the mid-1800s and the garbage incinerator from 1902. The odd detail: A Baltic fishing village with its own saloons, inside the city limits, that the city spent twenty years removing — and its last man outlasted the Depression.",
