      "summary": "The Cook County Recorder of Deeds keeps land records, indexed in tract books by legal description (the parcel), not by the buyer's or seller's name — to find what someone owns you need the address or the legal description first. The pre-fire records that survive exist because private abstract and title companies had saved their own books; they are still held privately and consulted by appointment. The odd detail: Chicago's surviving pre-1871 title history belongs to private title companies, not the county — the city's memory of who owned what is a commercial product.",
