      "places": [
        {
          "name": "The South Side Gas Works",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Adams Street near the Armory, by the river (West Loop edge)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=The%20South%20Side%20Gas%20Works%2C%20Adams%20Street%20near%20the%20Armory%2C%20by%20the%20river%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "On the night of the Great Fire the Chicago Journal reported that the flames reached \"the South Side Gas Works, the immense gasometer exploding with a fearful detonation, heard all over the city\". A second story, \"often repeated at the time\", held that gas from the Adams Street holder was turned into the sewers and rose into the buildings ahead of the fire, which is why buildings seemed to burn from the inside. In its 1925 anniversary history the Peoples Gas company rebutted both: workers opened the holder's manholes as the plant was doomed, letting the gas out; a holder full of gas cannot detonate without air; and the Adams Street holder stood, unexploded, on its site until February 1879, when it was sold for $3,000 (OCR reads \"33,000\"). The odd detail: The city's most-believed Fire legend — gas in the sewers making houses burn from within — was still being denied by the gas company fifty-four years later.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High that both legends were current and that the company denied them; what happened that night is the company's own account.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The three layers, ready-made: the period's reading (gas exploded), the folklore (gas ran through the sewers and lit the houses from inside), and what was actually there — the company says nothing. Something that moves through the sewers and lights buildings from within is a creature, not a gas.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Internet Archive: 75yearsofgasserv00rice",
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/75yearsofgasserv00rice",
          "publisher": "Internet Archive",
          "kind": "book or scanned text",
          "supports": "Wallace Rice, 75 Years of Gas Service in Chicago, Peoples Gas Light and Coke Co., 1925, pp. 15–16"
        }
      ],
