      "summary": "After Sedan (2 Sept 1870) and the peace of Frankfurt (May 1871), Chicago's Germans organised a Friedensfest — a peace festival — for Monday 29 May 1871. A meeting at Concordia Hall on 1 May chose A. Schoninger to lead it; the School Board was asked to close the schools for it (17 May); Dr. Chronik moved that the clergy \"preach a sermon\" for it (25 May); the Swiss Grütli Society voted to march. The Illinois Staats-Zeitung had greeted Sedan as \"the most glorious feat of arms recorded in modern history\" (8 Sept 1870), and in January 1871 the German community held a mass meeting against the \"dirty arms trade\" — American arms sales to France. Five months after the festival the city burned (8–10 Oct 1871). The festival was remembered: a 25-year memorial in May 1896, and a historical tableau \"from the wonderful peace festival in Chicago in the Spring of 1871\" at German Day, October 1907. The odd detail: Chicago's Germans lobbied to shut the public schools for a foreign victory parade — and the Chicago Germans' first mass protest against American arms sales to a belligerent was 1871, not 1914.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the items as printed (details of the parade itself not read — see Leads).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — two victory festivals fifty years apart, same club banners. The Kriegerverein's oldest members marched in both. What the Germans carried at the head of the 1871 parade (a lead to chase) is carried again in the 1930s.",
