      "summary": "(1) Drought and wind. 1871: about one inch of rain from 4 July to 9 October and a strong south-west wind; 1934: under four inches since 1 January, 92°F, gusts to 60 mph. (2) A small wooden hay-and-straw structure. 1871: a cow barn full of hay; 1934: straw-lined wooden cattle pens. (3) The unproven spark. 1871: the cow and lantern (officially exonerated 1997), \"Peg Leg\" Sullivan's pipe, Louis Cohn's craps game; 1934: a cigarette from the viaduct, a match, or spontaneous combustion — the marshal never settled it. (4) The evening and the weekend. 1871 began about 8:30 p.m. on a Sunday after the \"Saturday Night Fire\" of 7 October had exhausted the department; 1934 began at 4:14 on a Saturday afternoon. (5) The stone survivor. 1871 left the Water Tower; 1934 left the Stock Yard Gate. (6) The comparison was made at the time: Mayor Kelly and Marshal Corrigan both called it the worst since 1871 within hours, the Evening Star's front page the next morning said \"MOST DISASTROUS SINCE 1871 HOLOCAUST,\" and its inside story drew the drought parallel itself — \"prolonged drought creating tinder-dry wooden structures,\" with only \"modern fire-fighting apparatus and methods\" making the difference. (7) The lakeside fires and the comet — folklore layer. The same night as the 1871 fire, Peshtigo, Wisconsin (1,200–2,500 dead), Holland and Manistee, Michigan, and Port Huron burned; the fringe theory that fragments of Biela's Comet fell that night is still repeated and has no scientific support — legend says. (8) The yards' own first fire — the one the owner's source means. The owner's \"Chicago's second major fire\" comes from Chicago in the 1930s: \"the second major fire in its history after the 1910 fire\" — \"its\" is the Stock Yards, so in his source \"the first fire\" is 1910, not 1871. Between 1871 and 1934 lies 22 December 1910 — the Nelson Morris warehouse collapse that killed Chief Horan and twenty of his men, the deadliest building collapse for American firefighters until 2001. Scale, for honesty: 1871 burned 2,112 acres, 17,500 buildings, ~300 dead, 100,000 homeless, $222M; 1934 burned about 80 acres, one dead, $8M. The odd detail: Chicago's three great fires — 1871, 1910, 1934 — are 39 and 24 years apart, each in a structure of straw, hay or meat, each with the cause never proved.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on each fact; the parallels are this file's arrangement, not a source's claim",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the owner asked for the link — the honest one is the pattern: drought, straw, an unproved spark, a stone survivor, and the comet story the city told itself in 1871. Three layers of truth: the period reading (carelessness), the folklore (the comet, the cow), and what is actually there (something in the yards that burns its way out every generation and leaves the gate standing).",
