      "summary": "José María Leyva \"Cajemé\" (\"he who does not drink water\"; born Hermosillo 1835 or 1837 — sources differ) was schooled at Guaymas in Spanish and English, served the Mexican army, and from 1872 turned his office as alcalde mayor into a unified Yaqui republic of eight pueblos with taxes, river tolls, schools and an army; he was beaten at El Añil (May 1886), betrayed and taken near Guaymas in April 1887 and shot at Cócorit / Chumampaco in April 1887 (dates 12 and 23 April both appear) \"attempting escape.\" Juan Maldonado \"Tetabiate\" (born Hermosillo 28 August 1857) led a fourteen-year guerrilla war until he was killed in the Bacatete on 9 July 1901. After the battle of Mazocoba (18 January 1900) — about 397 Yaqui confirmed dead, some 1,000 captured — Díaz's \"extermination and deportation\" policy shipped an estimated 8,000–15,000 Yaquis (of ~30,000) between 1902 and 1908 to Yucatán henequen fields and Oaxaca cane; the port of Guaymas was the embarkation point, and one report (Indigenous Mexico, via search) claims 200 prisoners were thrown from a gunboat off Guaymas in 1892 (Low-Medium confidence). A Yaqui rising ended in defeat in 1929; in 1937 Cárdenas allotted about 500,000 hectares of ejidos to the Yaquis. The NPS notes the Yoeme are \"the only Native American tribe that has never officially surrendered.\" The odd detail: Cajemé, the great Yaqui leader, was mission-educated and spoke English — he had gone to the California gold fields as a boy of fourteen.",
