      "summary": "Born 24 September 1824 at Dumbarton, Scotland, into a Scotch-Presbyterian family that moved to Nova Scotia and then Pennsylvania, Gilmour converted to Catholicism in 1842, was ordained 30 August 1852, and ran a model school at St. Patrick's in Cincinnati, where he translated a Bible history (papal commendation) and wrote the readers used across Catholic schools. Consecrated bishop of Cleveland 14 April 1872, he founded and subsidised the Catholic Universe newspaper in 1874 \"to defend Catholic rights,\" and fought a six-year court battle (1877–1883) against the Cuyahoga County auditor's attempt to tax parochial school buildings. St. Ann's Asylum & Maternity Home, St. Alexis Hospital (erected 1884 at 5163 Broadway Ave., the city's second Catholic hospital) and St. John Hospital were founded with his support, and (per the St. Ignatius history) he invited the Buffalo Jesuits who opened St. Ignatius College in 1886. He died at St. Augustine, Florida, on 13 April 1891. The odd detail: St. Alexis Hospital (1884) was founded with Gilmour's support — the Broadway-district hospital where, forty years later, Dr. Francis Sweeney did his residency on the edge of Kingsbury Run (R06). The bishop who brought the Jesuits also backed the Torso suspect's training ground.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on Gilmour and on the St. Alexis–Sweeney link (re-read this session).",
