      "summary": "Born 15 October 1873 to a 14-year-old Tehueco (Cahita) mother, Cayetana Chávez, and the hacendado Don Tomás Urrea. In 1889 she fell into convulsions, ate only earth mixed with her saliva, then a trance of \"three months and eighteen days\" — and woke a healer, saying the Virgin had given her the don. Thousands of pilgrims came to Cabora; she charged nothing and healed with touch, saliva-and-earth poultices and herbs. Rebels invoked her: the Mayo shouted \"¡Viva la Santa de Cabora!\" attacking Navojoa (May 1892), and the Tarahumara/mestizo village of Tomóchic (Chihuahua) rose in her name and was razed in October 1892 (~300 dead). Porfirio Díaz deported her and her father in May 1892 (19 May by her own statement; the WRSP page says June). From El Paso (1896) the Spiritist Lauro Aguirre published her as \"Juana de Arco Mexicana\"; in August–September 1896 raiders called Teresistas hit the customs houses at Nogales (12 Aug), Ojinaga (17 Aug) and Palomas in her name. She toured US cities 1900–02 (St. Louis, San Francisco, New York), was advertised as a Spiritualist medium, and died of tuberculosis in Clifton, Arizona, 11 January 1906, aged 32. The odd detail: the rebels who attacked the customs houses in her name — in the setting, the customs house at Veracruz is exactly what America holds. The Teresistas already had the target.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — respectfully — she is a healer and a symbol, not a monster. The hook is the people who used her name: a 1930s guerrilla band in the occupied Sonoran foothills calls itself Teresistas again, and the occupation's files do not know whether they are Mayo farmers, Spiritists, or something wearing her memory. IHS would read her as a possible true mystic; the Bureau would read her as a flag.",
