      "summary": "The Polish Roman Catholic Union (1873) was clergy-aligned and saw Polish-Americans as a parish people; the Polish National Alliance (founded 15 Feb 1880, Philadelphia) was lay-led and existed \"to mobilize support … for the liberation of Poland.\" For most of the century they were \"locked in battle.\" On 1 Jan 1931 the PNA's Weekly Zgoda claimed 300,000 members — \"the largest Polish organization … in the entire world, Poland included\" — with $19,750,706.85 paid in life insurance and $3,740,233.34 given to \"educational, national and philanthropic purposes in America and in Poland\" since 1880, and $18.6 million in reserve at 31 Dec 1929. The PNA ran Alliance College in Cambridge Springs, Pa. (1912–91) and a Polish Library in Chicago; Charles Rozmarek became its president in 1939. The odd detail: a Chicago insurance society with a larger membership than any organization in Poland, and a standing budget line for Poland itself.",
