      "summary": "From 77 Poles in 1870 to 35,024 in 1920 and a peak of 36,668 foreign-born in 1930. St. Stanislaus (parish 1873, church completed 1881) is the mother parish; daughter parishes include Sacred Heart (1889), St. Hyacinth (1907), St. John Cantius (1897, Kantowo), St. Casimir (1893), St. Josaphat (1908), and Corpus Christi (1936). In 1892 Fr. Anton Kolaszewski was removed from St. Stanislaus and in 1894 set up his own Immaculate Heart of Mary parish; its members were excommunicated until he resigned in 1908; the breakaway Polish National Catholic Church opened its first Cleveland parish in Kantowo in 1914. By 1919 Poles were over half the workforce at U.S. Steel's American Steel & Wire. The Polish Falcons' Nest 141 recruited volunteers \"to fight with the Allies in World War I.\" (unverified — V_R25) By 1930 the two dailies split over Piłsudski (Wiadomości Codzienne for, Monitor Clevelandski against). The odd detail: 67 Polish saloons and 32 Polish grocers by 1900 — and a parish whose people were excommunicated for fourteen years for following their own priest.",
