      "summary": "Authorised after the Lady Elgin disaster of 1860 (more than 300 dead), built 1873 and lit March 1874, Grosse Point carries a second-order Fresnel lens still working today (one of about 70 operational Fresnels in the United States; two white flashes every 15 seconds). Edwin James \"E. J.\" Moore was assistant from 9 September 1883, principal keeper from 27 August 1888, and died in post on 2 March 1924. The light was automated in 1935; the Coast Guard decommissioned it in December 1941 as an air-raid precaution after Pearl Harbor and it was relit in 1946 as a private aid. The Wikipedia page also carries an uncited claim that a keeper named George Sheridan, who arrived in March 1915, \"disappeared\" and was later found hanged in the boathouse, and that tours report \"spirit sightings\" — treat as folklore/UNVERIFIED until a keeper list confirms it. The odd detail: A lighthouse built because of the worst shipwreck on the lake, whose keeper died in the house after 41 years, and which was switched to a machine in 1935 — the decade's keepers were the last.",
