      "summary": "On Sunday 4 May 1873 some 150–200 people crowded the iron Truesdell Bridge to watch the Baptist pastor Rev. J. H. Pratt baptise six converts in the river; the west sidewalk tipped and all five spans fell 18 ft into 15–20 ft of water: 46 dead (37 women, 9 men; 19 under 21), 56+ injured. Half a century later the Reagans settled in Dixon (1920); young Ronald Reagan took his first job as lifeguard at Lowell Park on the Rock River, went to Eureka College (1928–32), then re-created Cubs games from telegraph wire on WHO Des Moines before Hollywood (1937). (Also: Lincoln joined the militia at Fort Dixon in 1832; the 1885 \"Bloody Gulch Road\" murder of a Bible salesman.) The odd detail: The river that swallowed a baptism crowd in 1873 is the river where the future president pulled swimmers out.",
