      "id": "M15-007",
      "title": "7. Felipe Carrillo Puerto — from Motul to the firing squad",
      "date_start": "1874",
      "date_end": "1924-01-03",
      "date_label": "1874–3 January 1924",
      "category": "Mexico's unceded south, 1900–1940: Yucatán, Quintana Roo, Chiapas, Oaxaca, Tabasco, Campeche",
      "tags": [
        "Mexico",
        "unceded",
        "Felipe Carrillo Puerto",
        "Motul"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Felipe Carrillo Puerto",
        "Manuel Berzunza",
        "Felipe G. Cantón",
        "Howard S. Phillips"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Motul → Mérida → flight to the coast near Holbox → Mérida",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(UNCEDED)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Motul%20%E2%86%92%20M%C3%A9rida%20%E2%86%92%20flight%20to%20the%20coast%20near%20Holbox%20%E2%86%92%20M%C3%A9rida%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Born 8 November 1874 in Motul, Maya-speaking, a small rancher who had spent time with Zapata, he became Alvarado's rural organizer (agente de propaganda) and in March 1917 centralized the Ligas de Resistencia of the Socialist Party of Yucatán. Governor from 1 February 1922, he gave his inaugural address in Maya, opened 417 schools in the first year, founded the Universidad Nacional del Sureste (25 February 1922), backed birth control and women's rights, and tied the state's future to its ruins (roads to Chichén and Uxmal). His last, most radical move: agrarian laws of 28 November–4 December 1923 (expropriation of whole haciendas, collective operation by workers). When the De la Huerta rebellion began (4 December), the Mérida garrison joined it. He fled, was captured near Holbox on 21 December 1923, and was shot in the early hours of 3 January 1924 with three brothers (Wilfrido, Benjamín, Edesio) and eight companions. One reading (Howard S. Phillips of the Chicago Daily News, quoted in Alma Reed's memoir) says the rebellion's arms came via British Honduras, backed by Royal Dutch Shell's Mexican subsidiary, and that the hacendados' land interests made Carrillo Puerto's removal necessary (how far they fomented it, Phillips leaves unclear); the historian James Carey concludes \"some members of the old Casta Divina took financial roles.\" His enemies called him the \"red dragon with the green (or jade) eyes.\" The odd detail: the Chicago Daily News man had the story of who armed the rebels and never printed it (Hulme: apparently to avoid involving the country of his birth — he was English-born); it survives because Reed quotes it in Peregrina (published 2007), and Hulme thinks she would have known him socially after 1952 — when and how he told her is not stated.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the dates and the fact of execution; the who paid story is Low-Medium (memoir-recorded, contested — Hulme calls the outline \"undisputed\" but the causes \"myth-making on a grand scale\").",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — an unsolved political killing with a paper-trail through a Chicago newsman, a Havana-linked caste, Royal Dutch Shell and a colony; the Circle's fingerprints are exactly what a Chicago investigator finds in a Daily News file.",
