      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Jackson County",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(Black River Falls), Shawano County (Wittenberg), the Wisconsin Dells region, and the government boarding school at Tomah",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Jackson%20County%20%2C%20Shawano%20County%20%2C%20the%20Wisconsin%20Dells%20region%2C%20and%20the%20government%20boarding%20school%20at%20Tomah"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "After the Homestead Act was extended to Indians in 1874, the Ho-Chunk who had returned to their homeland could claim homesteads of up to 80 acres and were given separate tribal status with their own roll. Missions followed: an Evangelical and Reform Church school and mission about seven miles from Black River Falls (1875), a Lutheran mission and school at Wittenberg (1884); children were sent to the government boarding school at Tomah. The Wisconsin Ho-Chunk \"developed an itinerant economy\" — harvesting crops for white growers and returning to their homesteads seasonally — sold baskets, and by about 1913 many took summer jobs as dancers at Wisconsin Dells. The peyote religion (Native American Church) reached the Ho-Chunk through John Rave in 1893 and was brought to Wisconsin in 1908, taking hold at Wittenberg, Black River Falls and the Dells region; it kept tobacco offerings, song and vision, and younger members added Christian elements from their own visions. The Indian Reorganization Act \"seemed irrelevant\" to the self-sufficient Wisconsin Ho-Chunk, and the nation voted a constitution only in 1963. Sam Blowsnake (b. 1875, Black River Falls; called Crashing Thunder) told his life to the anthropologist Paul Radin, who printed it in 1913 and as Crashing Thunder (D. Appleton, 1926) — Radin \"silently rephrased, re-arranged, and in some cases added,\" and scholars still argue how much of the book is Blowsnake's. The odd detail: A Wisconsin Ho-Chunk man's life story reached print in 1926 through a New York publisher, and its editor had rewritten it.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the homestead and mission facts (MPM page read); Medium on the peyote dates (search summary).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Cult versus population and the period's own social categories: a Ho-Chunk elder in 1936 is not a \"source of folklore\" but a citizen of a nation with its own reasons for silence. The investigator whose case turns on a mound or a Dells legend has to be a guest — and learns the answer because she stops asking for it. (No Ho-Chunk person was consulted for this entry.)",
