      "summary": "The Athens Lunatic Asylum opened in 1874 on the Kirkbride plan (architect Levi T. Scofield of Cleveland), with a main building 853 feet long designed for 572 patients — almost twice Kirkbride's own figure — on a 1,019-acre campus with 78 buildings. Early admission records show women committed for menopause, postpartum depression and \"hysteria,\" men for alcohol use and masturbation. Treatments recorded include hydrotherapy, electroshock and lobotomy. Three cemeteries hold about 1,930 graves; 700 women and 959 men lay under numbers, not names, until 1943. Legend (modern, and outside our window) says a patient, Margaret Schilling, vanished on 1 December 1978, was found on 12 January 1979 in a sealed ward, and left a stain on the floor that never cleaned. The odd detail: In the 1930s the people who died at the Ridges were buried by number; the graves got their names back in 1943.",
