      "summary": "Chabad Hasidim founded Congregation Anshei Lubavitch in 1875. Under Rabbi Mordechai Zevin, who served about 25 years, it built \"a tan brick edifice\" at 1500 S. Drake, dedicated on 4 September 1922 with a parade carrying the sacred articles, led by a band from the Marks Nathan Orphan Home; the day raised $5,000. Zevin died on Friday, 27 July 1923, aged 75, and \"more than 10,000 Jews attended his funeral.\" Chabad's own history remembers the other Hasidic congregations of the period: Bnei Ruven, \"a mile away\"; Tzemach Tzedek in Wicker Park; Nusach Ari in Albany Park; and later Agudas Chabad in Uptown. In 1930 the \"Lubavitcher shul\" held the farewell gathering for the visiting Rebbe (next entry). The odd detail: The Torah scrolls went to their new home behind an orphanage brass band.",
