      "summary": "The club dates from 1875; the first Race to Mackinac was 1898 (five boats), not held again until 1904, with a trophy bought in 1906, skipped 1917–20 and run every year since 1921 — \"the oldest annual freshwater distance race in the world\". Its worst weather came in \"the blows of 1911, 1937 and 1970.\" In July 1937 the fleet met \"the fiercest gale in the history of the event\": the Chicago yacht Rubaiyat, skippered by Nathaniel Rubinkam, commodore of the Columbia Yacht Club, finished shortly after dawn on July 27 after 62 hours on a storm-swept lake, having also won in 1936; Bob Benedict's Southern Cross was second, 1 hour 50 minutes back (unverified — V_R19); the race organiser records winds up to 75 mph and \"only 8 out of 42 boats finished,\" the Coast Guard rescuing the crew of Reverie after they were swept overboard (a 1937 wire report is quoted as 35 of 43 failing \"because of heavy seas, broken masts and other damage\"). The odd detail: In the summer of 1937, 34 of 42 rich men's yachts were driven out of the race by 75-mph winds, heavy seas and broken masts, and only eight reached the island — a whole society weekend wrecked on a freshwater sea.",
