      "summary": "From the pages read: H. Clausenius, consul 1876 (the Emperor's flag). Dr. Walther Wever, Consul General for eight years, c. 1901–April 1909 (Prince Henry's host; the schools row; Crown Order 3rd class), posted to Haiti. Dr. A. Geissler, from Seattle, from April 1909; on leave when war came. Baron Kurt von Reiswitz, vice-consul then consul, 1914–17 (the reservists; the Hindu conspiracy). Gap 1917–21 (relations broken). Dr. Rudolph Steinbach, 1925–26. Dr. Hugo Ferdinand Simon, 1927 to his August 1933 recall over the fair's swastika (R04) — still in Chicago in 1936. Dr. H. A. Kroll, deputy consul general 1928. On the Austrian side: Hugo Sylvestin (1914) and, from 1928 to 1930, Michael F. Girten — the same Judge Girten who spoke for the arms embargo at the Bush Temple in December 1914. On 13 April 1929 the community gathered at the Morrison Hotel for the first anniversary of the Bremen flyers' east-to-west Atlantic crossing: Simon accepted two bronze busts of Köhl and the late Baron von Hünefeld for Berlin's aviation hall, Girten was toastmaster, Ernest Kruetgen gave the dedication, and M. E. Meigs of the Herald and Examiner wanted Chicago to be \"as great an airway center as it is a railroad center.\" Simon: a \"praiseworthy spirit of co-operation\" had prevented \"animosity or hatred\" after the war. The odd detail: the anti-war judge of 1914 ends as Austria's consul general in 1928; the German consul's 1929 line about no \"animosity or hatred\" after the war is, in the setting, literally true — there was no war between them.",
