      "summary": "Born 1 June 1878 in Poltava; escaped the Tsarist Empire twice, then worked as a Liverpool docker; reached New York in January 1899 and enlisted in the 14th Infantry on 27 June 1899; fought in the Philippines and in the Peking relief of 1900 (\"Fighting Jew\" began there). Discharged in 1902, he tried to join the Japanese Army against Russia (1904), then re-enlisted at Fort Bliss and became a machine-gun specialist. With Richardson and Holmdahl he did security work in the Panama Canal Zone and served in Guatemala (his only wound, \"in the rear\"), Honduras (with Christmas, in the Hornet force) and Nicaragua. In Mexico he fought for Madero at Juárez (1911), was a machine gunner on the Orozco side in 1912 (per Taylor; see the pay-scale entry), then worked in El Paso for Sommerfeld, smuggling arms to Villa and doing sabotage. After Columbus (9 March 1916) he joined Pershing as a scout. In early 1917 he (then 39) married 19-year-old Helen Spence; their infant daughter died before his second war. Enlisting in the 141st Infantry, 36th Division, he led about thirty men at Saint-Étienne in October 1918, capturing four machine guns, killing over forty and capturing two with no losses; Pershing called him \"the finest soldier and one of the bravest men I ever knew.\" Awards: Distinguished Service Cross, Croix de Guerre with palm, Médaille Militaire. The odd detail: the man who armed Villa in 1915 was scouting against Villa's men in 1916 — and Pershing later said he was the best soldier he had known.",
