      "summary": "Born Thord Ivar Hallström on 17 April 1878 in Södermalm, Stockholm; a merchant seaman, a Robben Island guard (1896), a Cape Mounted Rifleman in the Boer War, a captain in Royston's Horse in the Bambatha Rising (1906), an officer in Nairobi's mounted police (1907), and Philippine Constabulary captain (1908–09). According to the Wikipedia infobox he \"joined the Mexican Revolution as captain and commander of Pancho Villa's artillery in 1913\", ending as colonel and chief of staff, 1st Mexican Army (1914). In the Great War he was a British Army major, then lieutenant colonel (Northumberland Fusiliers, Royal Fusiliers), then with the Canadian Siberian Expeditionary Force, and by November 1919 a major general in Russia's White Army. He published a Swedish book on Mexican archaeology (1923), founded an investment house I. T. Gray & Co. (Fifth Avenue, 1925), became a U.S. citizen in 1934, was made major general and chief of staff to Florida Governor David Sholtz in August 1935, and published a Tarahumara–English Dictionary (1955) and the memoir Gringo Rebel (1961). He died on 18 August 1964. The odd detail: the soldier of fortune ended as the chief of staff to the Governor of Florida and the author of a dictionary of the Tarahumara language of Chihuahua's canyons.",
