      "summary": "Slavery had ended in the Danish West Indies in 1848 (the enslaved took freedom into their own hands; Gen. Buddhoe's role is in standard accounts, not confirmed on the Danish museum page). The Labor Act of 1849 then bound freed workers to annual contracts, restricted their movement and pushed them into debt. On Contract Day (1 October 1878) a peaceful gathering at Frederiksted turned violent after a rumour that a labourer had died in custody; over the next days rioters burned about 879 acres (roughly 50 estates and much of Frederiksted; the estate count is from memory, UNVERIFIED) and the damage ran to hundreds of thousands of dollars (Danish rigsdaler). Wikipedia's summary reports about 60 Black labourers and two soldiers killed and twelve labourers hanged on 5 October 1878 (one summary line also says 14 women died in an explosion — unverified). The four leaders, called \"queens,\" were Mary Thomas (\"Queen Mary\"), Axeline Elizabeth Salomon (\"Queen Agnes\"), Mathilda McBean (\"Queen Mathilda\") and Susanna Abrahamsen (\"Bottom Belly\") — Wayne James found documents in 2004 revealing Abrahamsen's role. All four were imprisoned. New labour contracts followed in 1879, \"weighted in favor of the plantation owners.\" The folk song \"Queen Mary\" is still sung in Virgin Islands schools; a seven-metre statue of Queen Mary, \"I Am Queen Mary,\" stands in Copenhagen (unveiled 2018). The odd detail: Denmark's first public monument to a Black woman is of the woman who burned its plantations.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High for the narrative; Medium for casualty counts (the summaries disagree in detail; the executions and the four queens are consistent). • roughly 50 estates and much of Frederiksted; the estate count is from memory, UNVERIFIED",
