      "summary": "The Chicago Schützenverein's Bundesschützenfest of 25–29 June 1879 expected ~300 competing marksmen; the Schuetzenpark clubhouse offered room and board at $1.75 a day, cots in the hall $1.50; the programme had target shooting, foot races, rope-walking, concerts and dancing, a parade down Michigan Avenue with \"the mayor's welcoming address\" on Clark Street, all Chicago marksmen in matching hats — and \"on Sunday evening, at twilight, the 'Battle of Sedan' is to be performed\" under \"General\" R. Hendricks, an item afterwards struck from the programme. Sedan Day (2 Sept) itself was the Reich's national day, and in 1895 the Chicago Kriegerverein organised a cut-price veterans' excursion to march in Berlin's parade. William Schmidt's private shooting range at Western & Belmont became Riverview Park in 1904, Chicago's great amusement park, run by the Schmidt family until it closed in 1967. The odd detail: the Battle of Sedan re-enacted at twilight in a Chicago picnic ground was too much even for 1879; Riverview's roller coasters ran on a German rifle range.",
