      "summary": "Born 29 July 1879 in Melbourne to Ricciotti Garibaldi and Harriet Constance Hopcraft, grandson of the Italian liberator. He fought in the Greco-Turkish War of 1897, against Cipriano Castro in Venezuela, and in the Boer War on the British side (carrying a sword given to his grandfather by Tyneside workingmen in 1854). In 1911 he was a lieutenant colonel in Madero's army; after the first battle of Ciudad Juárez Pancho Villa dismissed him over credit disputes. Plaza Garibaldi in Mexico City is named for him (see M12). He fought at Drisko in the Balkan War (1912), commanded the 4th march regiment of the French Foreign Legion in the Great War, reached Col di Lana (Italy) in November 1915, became a brigadier general in June 1918, and retired in 1919. He opposed Mussolini; he married an American and left for America; arrested by the Germans in 1943, imprisoned at Regina Coeli in Rome, he is recorded as dying on 19 May 1950 (the article's wording leaves it unclear whether in the prison). The odd detail: the man for whom Mexico City's mariachi plaza is named was fired by Pancho Villa within a month of the victory.",
