      "summary": "Dr. Leslie Keeley founded his institute in 1879 to cure drunkards and addicts with injections of \"bichloride of gold\" four times a day for four weeks, plus tonics whose secret ingredients were said to include strychnine, alcohol, apomorphine and atropine; by 1900 some 300,000 had taken it, 200+ branches ran in the US and Europe, and 800 passengers a week arrived in Dwight at the peak. Nellie Bly attacked it in 1894; by the late 1930s most doctors called it useless; it closed in 1965. Outside town, the Oakdale Reformatory for Women opened in 1930 (dedicated 19 Nov 1931 by Gov. Louis Emmerson): eight French-Norman yellow-brick cottages of 15–28 women, each with its own kitchen, compared to \"a swank country club\"; an easily climbed wire fence marked the line; trusties wore white stockings, the rest black; guards timed the walks between buildings. The odd detail: Illinois sent its drunks to Dwight to be injected with gold, and its women convicts to cottages behind a fence anyone could climb — the \"Blonde Tigress\" Eleanor Jarman walked out of it in 1940 and was never found (R06).",
