      "summary": "Founded in Philadelphia on February 15, 1880, by Agaton Giller's circle, the Polish National Alliance became the largest Polish fraternal organisation in America, headquartered in Chicago. It ran fraternal insurance from 1886, organised Chicago's first Polish Constitution Day parade in 1891, built the Kościuszko monument in Washington (1910), owned Alliance College (1912–91), and published Zgoda (from 1881) and the daily Dziennik Związkowy (from 1908). It stood for Polish nationalism and often clashed with the parish-centred Polish Roman Catholic Union. The odd detail: Chicago's largest Polish organisation was an insurance company that was also a national movement — and it quarrelled with the Church-based union across town.",
