      "summary": "Fred Usinger, a sausage-maker's apprentice from Wehen in Hesse, bought Julia Gaertner's butcher shop on Third Street in 1880 and married her niece Louise; the firm has made sausage there to nineteenth-century recipes ever since. Charles Mader opened \"The Comfort\" in 1902, renamed it Mader's in 1912 and moved to Third Street in 1913; through Prohibition it survived on German food rather than beer, and when beer came back in 1933 it \"served Milwaukee's first legal beer at midnight\"; his son Gustave took over in 1937 and later filled it with suits of armour, stained glass and steins. Three presidents ate there (Kennedy and Reagan before office, Ford in it). The odd detail: The street's spine — sausage, beer hall, brewery — runs north to the corner where the Socialist congressman was killed by the streetcar.",
