      "summary": "Posada (2 February 1852 – 20 January 1913) cut relief prints for cheap one-sheet broadsides sold to the working class by Vanegas Arroyo — murders, disasters, miracles, executions and, every November, calaveras: skeletons dressed as every class of Porfirian society, with satirical verse. His zinc etching of the Calavera Garbancera (c. 1910–13) — a skeleton in a fine lady's hat, mocking Indigenous women who aped European fashion — became La Catrina. He died penniless and was buried in an unmarked common grave at the Panteón de Dolores in Mexico City. His work was rediscovered in the 1920s by the French-born artist Jean Charlot and championed by the muralists; Diego Rivera would later put the Catrina at the centre of a 1947 mural (after the period). The odd detail: the man who drew Mexico's dead for thirty years has no known grave — he lies somewhere in a common pit at Dolores.",
