      "id": "R15-036",
      "title": "Maxwell Street and \"Bloody Maxwell\"",
      "date_start": "1880",
      "date_end": "1939",
      "date_label": "1880s–1930s",
      "category": "Immigrant and Ethnic Chicago in the 1930s",
      "tags": [
        "ethnic Chicago",
        "immigrants",
        "Maxwell Street",
        "Bloody Maxwell"
      ],
      "people": [
        "\"King\" Levinsky",
        "Barney Ross",
        "Benny Goodman",
        "Jacob Arvey"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Maxwell & Halsted Sts.",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Roosevelt Rd. to 16th St. (Near West Side)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Maxwell%20%26%20Halsted%20Sts.%2C%20Roosevelt%20Rd.%20to%2016th%20St%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "the Maxwell Street police station",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(opened 1888)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20Maxwell%20Street%20police%20station%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Eastern European Jews built an improvised ghetto market here from the 1880s; the city recognized it in 1912, and people called it \"Jew Town\" — perhaps \"the largest open-air market in the country.\" After 1920 Black migrants moved in while Jewish merchants kept many stalls; Southern bluesmen later electrified their guitars to be heard on the street. The district police station earned the names \"Bloody Maxwell\" and \"the Wickedest Police District in the World\"; the Tribune (1906) called it \"the crime center of the country.\" Its sons included Benny Goodman, boxer Barney Ross (\"the Pride of the Ghetto\") and politician Jacob Arvey. On 18 Feb 1932 \"King\" Levinsky, \"the famous Jewish fighter of Maxwell Street\" (196½ lb), took a four-round decision from ex-champion Jack Dempsey (190 lb) before 23,000 at the Stadium — $75,000 gate. The odd detail: a Maxwell Street fish-peddler's son beating Jack Dempsey in 1932.",
