      "summary": "In the 1880s–90s \"the most expensive street west of Fifth Avenue,\" with over fifty mansions by the 1893 fair. Bertha Palmer's move to the Drive in 1882 began the drift north; railroad soot, industry and the encroaching Levee did the rest — the Tribune wrote in 1898 that it had become \"a place that was undesirable to those for whom it was affordable, and unaffordable to those for whom it was desirable.\" By 1911 warehouses and factories crowded the district; through the 1930s the surviving houses stood among printing plants and loft buildings, the Glessner house still a family home (Frances Glessner Lee, R08, grew up in it) and the Field house the address of the 1905 shooting (section 13). The odd detail: In the 1930s a GM can put a great house of the 1880s, still furnished, on a street of freight docks — the old society street as a place the new society will not visit.",
