      "summary": "Italian-born Clevelanders passed 32,000 by the late 1920s, mostly from the Mezzogiorno; Little Italy's families came largely from Ripalimosani, Matrice and San Giovanni in Galdo (Campobasso). Little Italy grew around the marble works that served Lake View Cemetery — Joseph Carabelli, a stonecutter who arrived in 1880, founded the Lakeview Marble Works — and in 1911 it was 96% Italian-born. Churches: St. Anthony (1887, Ohio's first Italian parish), Holy Rosary (1892, Little Italy), St. Marian (1905), St. Rocco (1922), Holy Redeemer (1924), Mt. Carmel West (1926). By 1920 Italians were 80% of Cleveland's barbers and 70% of its cooks. In 1935 the Italian ambassador, Augusto Rosso, dedicated a Sons of Italy temple. The Mayfield Road Mob took its name from Little Italy's main street (formed c. 1913). The odd detail: Little Italy exists because of the cemetery — its men carved Cleveland's gravestones, and the neighbourhood sits pressed against the graveyard wall.",
