      "summary": "Senator George Hearst bought the land in the late 19th century for as little as 20 cents an acre (Time repeats the story that he used advance word of Geronimo's 1886 surrender to buy hundreds of thousands of acres); his widow Phoebe Apperson Hearst added to it, and their son William Randolph inherited it. Time (1953) calls it 1,625,000 acres (2,500 square miles) (unverified — V_M08: Time does say 1,625,000, but other figures conflict — the 1954 land grant distributed 272,886 hectares (674,000 acres) of \"the best lands\"; a search snippet gives 350,000 ha (about 865,000 acres); PEOPLE and M01 say \"close to a million\". Treat the acreage as unsettled) — a Shorthorn and Hereford cattle empire whose beef went to El Paso — larger than some U.S. states and twice the King Ranch. Villa's forces occupied Babícora \"for a couple of years\" and took cattle and horses; armed vaqueros fought them. When Cárdenas's land reform targeted American estates, Hearst's lawyers and newspapers kept the ranch from being expropriated; after his 1951 death his heirs sold it to the Mexican government in 1953 for $2.5 million, to be subdivided into tracts of up to 375 acres. The odd detail: the same press lord whose papers printed the 1927 forged \"Mexican documents\" (M01) owned an estate of some 2,500 square miles — about the size of Delaware; the Geronimo tip is the family legend.",
