      "summary": "The National Register fixes the addresses: Otto Young, the State Street merchant, built Younglands, an Italian Renaissance pile by Henry Lord Gay, 1899–1901 (locally \"Stone Manor\"); Judge Thomas Withrow's Bonnie Brae (C. A. Alexander, 1881) and Seipp's Black Point (1888) stand on the Linn shore; Frank Lloyd Wright built the Fred B. Jones compound Penwern (1900–03) and the A. P. Johnson house (1905) on neighbouring Delavan Lake; Emily Baker, widow of a J. I. Case partner, had Redwood Cottage (1885). In 1932 — the Depression's floor — the town built the Riviera on its Wrigley Drive lakefront, a ballroom and marina \"hosting major musical acts\" — the dance hall the estates' children and the town's went to; the street itself is named for the gum family whose summer place was up the shore. Horticultural Hall (330 Broad Street, 1912) was the estates' garden society's own meeting hall. The odd detail: A town whose main lakefront street is named for a chewing-gum king, with a merchant's stone castle on its shore and Frank Lloyd Wright's summer houses one lake over, on Delavan — and a municipal ballroom built in 1932.",
