      "summary": "Born to Tarascan (Purépecha) parents near Guadalajara, Pedro Jaramillo, a poor labourer, cured a nose affliction by burying his face in the mud at a pool for three days; a voice woke him and told him he had received the gift of healing from God. He came to South Texas in 1881, settled at Los Olmos, and for 26 years prescribed \"the first thing that he thought of\" — Catholic faith and simple remedies — charging nothing, walking or riding a donkey to patients between the Nueces and the Rio Grande. Letters came by mail with fifty-cent and dollar donations; more than $5,000 in fifty-cent pieces was found in his house at his death on 3 July 1907. His grave became a shrine visited by hundreds a year. Ruth Dodson's Don Pedrito Jaramillo: Curandero was published (in Spanish) in 1934. The odd detail: $5,000 in half-dollars under a healer's roof who never charged — and he gave the remedies away as well.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — his shrine near Falfurrias is on the road between the King Ranch country and the border; in the 1930s pilgrims still leave notes. A Section 13 agent reading Dodson's 1934 book finds one prescription that is not a remedy at all — a warning, dated.",
