      "summary": "Built 1877–83 for $305,000 (first lit 4 July 1882), Stannard Rock was a \"stag station\" — men only — with keepers landed in March to chip one to two feet of ice off the doors with sledgehammers and taken off in early December. Illness or fire could leave them alone for days or weeks; in the first three seasons four keepers resigned and three transferred. (In 1961 the gasoline and propane store exploded during automation, killing one keeper and stranding three on the pier for three days.) The odd detail: The keepers' own name for the post — \"the loneliest place in the world\" — on a reef in the lake that keeps its dead.",
