      "summary": "In 1918 St. Charles (a State institution, \"for custody and training of delinquent boys between ages of 10 and 17 years committed by Courts\") had a capacity of 800; Geneva (established 1882; managing officer Dr. Clara Hayes) held delinquent girls 10 to 18, who \"may be kept in school until they are 21\", capacity 500. Both were cottage campuses, not walled prisons — which is why the Forty-Two Gang could threaten to storm St. Charles in 1928 (see Part 3) and why escapes were chronic: the Tribune of 2 Nov 1939 headlined boys who \"ATTACK GUARD, FLEE UNWALLED\" school — \"the most dangerous of the young criminals in the institution\" overpowered \"a husky guard\" — noting how many had escaped that year under superintendent William T. Harmon. Harmon later ran the school for years; St. Charles went on to hold the founders of several Chicago street gangs. The odd detail: \"Unwalled\" was the reformers' point of pride — and the newspapers' headline word every time a boy walked away.",
