      "summary": "Founded in 1883 as St. Mary's Training School for Boys, the vision of Archbishop Patrick A. Feehan; after a massive fire in 1899 its director Rev. James Doran rebuilt it and opened it to girls so that orphaned brothers and sisters could live near each other (the girls' side was the Chicago Industrial School for Girls). By 1918 it took boys 7–14 (capacity 600) and girls 7–14 (capacity 600), the county paying $10 or $15 a month for every child the court committed; Father Doran was still superintendent, with Sister M. Geraldine as manager. It was the archdiocese's great orphan city through the Depression; it was renamed Maryville Academy in 1950, when its huge dormitories were still standing (razed under Msgr. George Halpin, 1954–70). The odd detail: A priest who ran the same children's city for decades — Doran was already its director after the 1899 fire and still in charge in 1918.",
