      "summary": "Disgruntled Catholic members of the (non-sectarian, Masonic-flavoured) Independent Order of Foresters split off to form two separate \"Catholic Order of Foresters\" — one in Massachusetts in 1879 and one in Chicago in 1883. The Chicago order became the national fraternal benefit society: life insurance for Catholic families, organised into local \"courts,\" with \"Forester\" ritual and regalia in place of the lodges the Church forbade Catholics to join. (The Massachusetts sister order alone had 60,000 members in 1930.) The odd detail: A Catholic \"secret society\" invented precisely so that Catholics would not join the real ones — the Church's answer to Freemasonry was an insurance company with a ritual.",
