      "summary": "Lumbermen, drunk on New Year's Eve and a hundred leagues from their sweethearts, make a bargain with the devil so their canoe will fly; the rules are never to say God's name and never to touch the cross of a church steeple, on pain of their souls. They paddle over the night sky, seeing Montreal and the frozen rivers below, and a drunken steersman blasphemes and nearly wrecks them. Honoré Beaugrand's telling appeared in English in The Century Magazine, August 1892; its root is the French legend of a nobleman called Gallery who hunted instead of going to Sunday Mass and was condemned to fly forever through the night skies chased by horses and wolves — a Wild Hunt. Hamlin's Detroit version (1883/84) is the older Wild Hunt, not the lumberjacks' joyride: \"A Legend of the Canadian Shore,\" which she says she took down from an old habitante of ninety who had never left the lake, calls it \"The Spectral Aerial Hunt\" and says \"many honest, upright people still living will testify to having seen this phenomenon.\" It takes three forms: a canoe manned by twelve men with a dog barking in its prow, \"always to the north flies the phantom boat\"; shaggy black dogs with drooping ears running on the water as if on a scent; and \"once in seven years a solitary horseman, with gaunt, bronzed face, rifle in hand, followed by his pack of dogs, is seen in the sky after sunset.\" Whoever sees it \"knows that it betokens death either to himself or to others dear to him.\" Her tale: the hunter Sebastien Lacelle of Askin Pointe, on the Canadian shore, leaves his bride Zoé de Mersac on the eve of their wedding to shoot ducks — \"To-morrow at dawn, dead or alive, sure\" — and never returns; she sees him in a boat in the clouds with his dog Chasseur, promising to come for her \"in a year and a day\"; a year and a day later, dressed as a bride on the beach, she dies crying \"there is Sebastien in the boat,\" and the onlookers see a phantom boat and hear a dog barking in the sky. The odd detail: In the Detroit version it is a death omen with a timetable — the horseman comes once in seven years, and the dead lover keeps \"a year and a day.\"",
