      "summary": "\"A small group of German citizens\" incorporated the German Hospital on 17 Dec 1883 so that the sick could be nursed in their own language — \"every German at the sound of his mother language is put in mind of home.\" A temporary hospital opened at 242 Lincoln Ave. in late summer 1884; by May 1887 it had treated 223 patients and the board (George Schneider, Charles Emmerich, John König and eight others) appealed for a first wing, 36 × 80 ft, 85–90 beds, $14,000–15,000, on land at Grand Place and Larrabee. In Jan 1889 it held eighty patients and did \"difficult operations\"; in Feb 1890 it took $10,000 of a $135,000 bequest; in Sept 1890 it opened a two-year nursing school. In 1917–18 it became Grant Hospital — Wikipedia's Anti-German sentiment names it with New York's German Hospital among the renamed. Edward Uihlein's children funded its research laboratory after 1921. The odd detail: a hospital founded so that Chicagoans could be sick in German was renamed for a Union general to prove it was not.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for founding and site; the renaming year is \"WWI\" (Wikipedia), not pinned to a month.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a German-language hospital whose records are in German is a wall to an English-speaking investigator — and a door to a German-reading one. What its morgue has been quietly sending to Berlin since 1884 is a question for Section 13.",
