      "summary": "Born 26 August 1883 in the Swedetown district of Fort Dodge, Iowa; his father left the family. At 15 he enlisted in the 51st Iowa Volunteer Infantry (a hired actor posed as his father). The regiment sailed for Manila on 3 November 1898 (SS Pennsylvania), fought at San Roque (9 Feb 1899), Guadalupe Church (5 Mar), Quingua and Pulilan (24 Apr), Calumpit (25 Apr 1899 — the same river crossing that won Funston his Medal of Honor) and San Fernando (May); in September 1899 he chose to stay in the Philippines. In 1900 he took an ensign's commission in \"General\" Edmund F. English's mercenary foreign legion for the Qing — the \"Royal Imperial Guards, Sinim Order of Dragoons\" — which dissolved when the Boxer crisis cut its funds. He re-enlisted in the regular 20th Infantry, Co. I, saw the Sulu campaigns (Bud Dajo, March 1905), made sergeant 15 December 1905, guarded the U.S. Sub-Treasury in San Francisco after the April 1906 quake (125 hours, by his account) and took an honourable discharge on 31 January 1907. Then: a two-week job as a steamfitter, a banana boat from New Orleans, a walk through Nicaragua into Honduras and a place in Lee Christmas's band — where, by his own account, he met Dreben, Richardson, O'Reilly and Molony and was made an officer. His Mexican career is in the next two entries; his 1926 Villa-head arrest is in M01/M14. The odd detail: he was fifteen when his first war started, and by twenty-three he had been a mercenary ensign in an Imperial Chinese guard, a Philippine sergeant, a San Francisco quake sentry, and a Christmas lieutenant.",
