      "summary": "Industrial coal began in 1884 at the Álamo mine, San Felipe (Coahuila). C. P. Huntington's Mexican International Railroad (1882, from Piedras Negras) bought coal from landowners including Evaristo Madero and then took control through the Sabinas Coal Mines Company, Coahuila Coal Mine Company, Alamo Coal Company and Fuente Coal Company; by 1905 William Ludlow's Mexican Coal & Coke Company was the largest producer (Las Esperanzas); the British industrialist William Broderick Cloete owned land, rails and mines, and the town of Cloete is named for him after he died in the sinking of the Lusitania (1915). Between 1901 and 1907 about 11,000 Japanese and Okinawan contract workers arrived; Japanese surnames still appear in Palaú, where Anastacio Simabuco, believed from Okinawa, is recorded around 1900. The revolution \"virtually destroyed\" the coal industry (per a search summary of Calderón's study); ASARCO later ran the Nueva Rosita mines and coke works. The odd detail: the Mexican coalfield's founding labour was Japanese, on contract, in a town named for a man who died on a torpedoed liner.",
