      "summary": "Built in 1884 (one Sheldon history says 1888) as an office building by Brooklyn-born Dr. Lucius Sheldon, remodelled into a hotel in 1899 (two Sheldon histories agree), the Sheldon became the \"unofficial headquarters\" of participants in the Mexican Revolution from both sides of the border (Wikipedia's Plaza Hotel page). Its lobby and bar were favourite meeting places for rebels, foreigners, correspondents and Secret Service agents as Juárez was planned; two Sheldon histories (plazahotelelpaso.com and henrytrost.org) say President Taft was a guest there for the 1909 summit — Medium; the Texas State Historical Association's Taft–Díaz page does not mention the hotel and no contemporary source was seen. The Sheldon burned down on 9 April 1929; Conrad Hilton's new hotel opened on the site on 30 November 1930 as the Hilton, and became the Plaza Hotel after Hilton sold it in the 1960s. The odd detail: the hotel where a decade of revolutions were staffed burned to the ground on 9 April 1929 and was replaced by Conrad Hilton's hotel (later called the Plaza) — the registers went with it (unverified — V_M19: no source says the registers were lost).",
