      "summary": "The first colonists left Snowflake, Arizona, on 9 February 1885 — polygamists escaping the Edmunds Act, settling in Mexico on land bought for them. Colonia Díaz and Juárez (1885), Pacheco (1887), Dublán (1888, on 73,000 acres bought by George M. Brown from Lewis Huller; six weeks after the first plough there were 350 people; laid out with wide streets like Salt Lake City), Oaxaca (Sonora, 1892), García and Chuichupa (1894), Morelos (1900), San José (1905). In 1912, with Pascual Orozco's rebels camped near and extorting, the Mexican government said it would no longer protect them; on 28 July 1912 residents of Dublán received expulsion orders and \"within two days almost all had fled\" — women and children rode the train from Pearson (now Mata Ortiz) to Douglas and El Paso, where Fort Bliss lent tents and a lumber shed on Magoffin Avenue held families. Fewer than half returned; only five of ten colonies were resettled; the mountain colonies were abandoned. George W. Romney (father of Mitt Romney) and Marion G. Romney were born in the colonies. The odd detail: Colonia Dublán became the Punitive Expedition's headquarters in 1916 — a Mormon village of wide streets, abandoned and looted in 1912, became the supply depot of the first American army in Mexico.",
