      "summary": "The Indiana Association of Spiritualists founded the camp in 1886; by the 1930s it was a 35-acre village of cottages around a common, with a 1914 hotel, seminary training, mediumship classes, healing, and trumpet and materialisation séances for paying summer visitors. In August 1925 fourteen of its mediums were arrested for fraud. Later exposures (infrared film of fake materialisations in 1960; Allen Spraggett in 1965 calling it \"a fraud so crude that it was an insult to the intelligence\"; ex-medium M. Lamar Keene's The Psychic Mafia, 1976; Joe Nickell, 2002) describe the trade as it was already practised in the 1930s. The grounds hold stone bridges, a sweathouse, a \"Native American memorial\" and a totem pole. It is on the National Register (17 Jul 2002). The odd detail: fourteen mediums arrested in one raid in 1925 and the camp simply reopened next summer; the cathedral (1954) came later, but the hotel a 1930s investigator would sleep in was the 1914 one.",
