      "summary": "St. Ignatius College opened 6 September 1886 with 76 students, founded at Bishop Richard Gilmour's request by Jesuits of the Buffalo Mission of the German Province of the Society of Jesus. It separated from St. Ignatius High School in 1902 and in 1923 renamed itself Cleveland University (May) and then John Carroll University (September), after America's first Catholic bishop. Land in Idlewood (a 45-acre site) was bought in 1923 as part of the Van Sweringen brothers' plan for schools on boulevards radiating from Fairmount Circle. The cornerstone was laid 5 July 1931; the Depression stopped work in 1932; building resumed in May 1935 and classes began on the new campus on 7 October 1935. The graduate school admitted women from 1934. The odd detail: In April 1932, when construction halted, the aged seismologist Fr. Frederick L. Odenbach, SJ moved into the unfinished Grasselli Tower with his dog Hector and a canary to guard his instruments — he cooked there and slept in an Arctic sleeping bag, and stayed until his superiors called him back to the West Side shortly before his death on 15 March 1933. He had founded America's first Jesuit seismic observatory (1900) (unverified — V_R07), invented the \"ceraunograph\" to record thunder and lightning (1899), and in 1909 proposed a cooperative seismograph network across Jesuit colleges in the US and Canada (the Jesuit Seismological Association followed in 1925).",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (the Hector/canary/sleeping-bag detail is from JCU's own magazine). • He had founded America's first Jesuit seismic observatory (1900) (unverified — V_R07), invented the \"ceraunograph\" to record thunder and lightning (1899), and in 1909 proposed a cooperative seismograph network … • Grasselli and three sisters — donated the tower's bells (cast in Baltimore) (unverified — V_R07);",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — IHS's American seat was literally unfinished and guarded by one old priest, a dog and a songbird for a year — and the priest ran a continent-wide Jesuit instrument network recording tremors and lightning. A network of seismographs in every Jesuit college is exactly how an order of monster-hunters would listen for things moving underground.",
