      "summary": "Born 25 January 1886 in Cherry Flats, Tioga County, Pennsylvania; worked on the Panama Canal (1908); learned to fly at the Curtiss school (1912). Royal Flying Corps records show Sgt. Dean Lamb (no. 8054) enlisted 30 August 1915 and was discharged 5 October 1917; he claimed five or eight victories, which historians Norman Franks and Christopher Shores could not confirm. He flew airmail in New York, New Jersey and Maryland (9 December 1918 – 6 February 1919), helped found the Honduran Air Force (1921), commanded the government air squadron in Paraguay's civil war (1922–23) (with a claim of 48 victories that historians treat as staged or unverifiable), was arrested on 1 December 1944 for a jewel theft of $19,000–$38,000 and cleared in May 1945, and appears in a 1949 FBI memo where he is interviewed about alleged 1933 espionage for Alger Hiss. He died in Tucson in November 1955 (Wikipedia says suicide; another page says heart failure from barbiturate poisoning). His memoir was ghostwritten by John Eoghan Kelly and published by Farrar & Rinehart in 1934. He belonged to the Quiet Birdmen and Early Birds of Aviation. The odd detail: the pilot of the \"first dogfight\" turns up, twenty years later, in an FBI memo about Alger Hiss.",
