      "summary": "R01 has Guzik's role and the Treasury file. What R01 does not have: he was born on 20 March 1886 in Kraków, in Austrian Galicia (not Russia), to Max and Mamie (Zeitlin) Guzik; the family came to Chicago in 1892, where Max kept a cigar store. By 1910 his older brother Harry owned a brothel in which Jake and a brother, Sam, worked; Jake married Rose Lipschultz in Lake County, Indiana, on 13 April 1907. Convicted of tax fraud in 1930 (five years; released after three years and eight months). At his 1956 funeral Rabbi Noah Ganze gave a twenty-minute eulogy calling him a man who \"never lost faith in God\"; he is buried at Oak Woods. A popular history — Michael Benson's Gangsters vs. Nazis — claims that a New York judge, Nathan Perlman, asked Guzik to do in Chicago what Meyer Lansky was doing in New York: break up German-American Bund meetings with Jewish muscle, boxers among them (Barney Ross is named) — a claim the article read gives no Chicago dates, places or numbers for. The odd detail: The Outfit's bagman went to his grave with a rabbi praising his faith.",
