      "summary": "In the eight-hour-day strikes of May 1886 — the same week as Haymarket in Chicago — about 7,000 building-trades workers and 5,000 Polish labourers marched on the rolling mill, 14,000 by 3 May. Governor Jeremiah Rusk sent 250 National Guardsmen with orders to \"shoot to kill\" if the strikers entered the mills; on the morning of 5 May the Guard fired on the approaching crowd. Seven died: Frank Kunkel, Frank Nowarczyk, John Marsh, Robert Erdman, Johann Zazka, Martin Jankowiak, Michael Ruchalski — one of them thirteen years old, one a bystander. The Polish South Side dates its politics from it; the Socialists' rise to City Hall a generation later is its long echo. The odd detail: Milwaukee's Haymarket happened the day after Chicago's, and its dead have names.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
