      "summary": "Theodore Roosevelt and George Bird Grinnell founded the Boone and Crockett Club on December 21, 1887, capped at 100 regular members, to promote hunting skill, exploration and the preservation of big game. Early officers were Roosevelt (president) and Archibald Rogers (secretary); members included the painter Albert Bierstadt, Gen. William T. Sherman, Gifford Pinchot, the scout Frederick Russell Burnham, Aldo Leopold — and the eugenicist Madison Grant. The club was central to ending market hunting and to founding the national park, forest and refuge systems. The odd detail: a closed club of a hundred men who had to prove themselves as hunters shaped federal land policy — and one of its members wrote America's most influential racial-science book.",
