      "summary": "Conrad Seipp (1825–1890), brewing in Chicago since 1854 and one of the country's largest brewers, had Adolph Cudell (also architect of his 1887 Chicago mansion with its turreted tower) build a 20-room Queen Anne summer house in 1888 — 13 bedrooms, one bathroom, a four-storey tower, no road and no utilities, everyone arriving by water. Seipp had two summers in it before he died in 1890; his descendants — the Seipps, the Madleners (Elsa Seipp married Albert Madlener; their Gold Coast house is in section 3) and the Petersens — summered there through the decade, and in 1938 \"descendants of Conrad Seipp comprise an orchestra\" for the house's fiftieth anniversary. It is now a Wisconsin Historical Society house museum, \"a rare time capsule.\" The odd detail: A family so large it could field its own orchestra, in a house with one bathroom, on a lake you could only reach by boat.",
