      "summary": "Patrick and John Cudahy bought John Plankinton's packing business for $600,000 in 1888 and opened their plant at Buckhorn in 1893 — up to 7,000 hogs a day. A Sept 1906 fire took a third of it; they rebuilt in 49 days. Patrick died in 1919 and his son Michael took over. Patrick's other son John Cudahy (b. 10 Dec 1887) went to Harvard and the Wisconsin law school, led a counter-attack against 1,000 Bolsheviks at Tulgas on 14 Nov 1918 as a lieutenant of the 339th Infantry in the Archangel expedition (his Archangel: The American War with Russia, 1924, under a pseudonym), built the Cudahy Tower, and served as Ambassador to Poland (13 June 1933–23 Apr 1937), Minister to the Irish Free State (28 May 1937–15 Jan 1940) and Ambassador to Belgium (1940). He was killed on 6 Sept 1943, thrown from a horse on his Brown Deer estate. The odd detail: The Milwaukee packer's son who fought Bolsheviks in the Arctic and then represented Roosevelt in Warsaw and Dublin was killed by a horse at home.",
