      "summary": "Karl Theodor Sapper (6 February 1866 – 29 March 1945) went to Guatemala in 1888, where his brother ran a coffee plantation; he managed it for two years, learned three local languages, and spent twelve years making the geological and topographical survey of Guatemala and Central America — geology, volcanoes, ethnography, archaeology — that made him the authority on the region. Back in Germany in 1900 he became professor at Tübingen (1902), then Strasbourg (1910), then Würzburg (1919), where he founded a geography institute; retired 1932. The odd detail: Germany's leading scholar of Central America learned his Maya languages on a coffee finca, as the planter's brother.",
