      "collection": "Chicago & Great Lakes",
      "synopsis": "In 1889 Illinois (the Edwards Law) and Wisconsin (the Bennett Law) required that reading, writing, arithmetic and American history be taught in English in every school, public or parochial. The Missouri and Wisconsin Synods and the German Catholic clergy rose against both; in Wisconsin the Democrats swept the state in 1890 (Peck over Governor Hoard) and repealed the law in 1891.",
