      "summary": "Teresa Urrea, born 15 October 1873 at Ocoroni, Sinaloa, had visions in fall 1889 after a grave illness and came to believe the Virgin Mary had given her healing power; around 1,200 people gathered at her father's ranch at Cabora, and she became \"the Saint of Cabora.\" When drought-hit villagers of Tomóchic, Chihuahua, sought her guidance, fighting broke out on 7–26 December 1891; the government held her responsible, exiled her and her father to Nogales, Arizona, in May 1892, and troops destroyed Tomóchic (\"300 villagers had died in the struggle by the end of that year\"). By 1895 she was at Solomonville, Arizona, where she met Díaz's opponents; insurgents carried her photograph over their hearts in the August 1896 raid on the Nogales customs house. She moved to California about 1900 and died of tuberculosis in 1906 in Clifton, Arizona. The odd detail: insurgents attacked a customs house wearing a photograph of a living saint on their chests as armour.",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a healer whose photograph is a talisman; the Tomóchic dead as unquiet ground under the American flag. Respect note: she was a real person and a living Catholic-folk tradition; the occult layer must belong to the thing that used her name, not to her.",
