      "summary": "When Jane Addams and Ellen Gates Starr moved into the old Hull mansion in 1889 it had been a second-hand furniture store, a factory store-room, and a Little Sisters of the Poor home for the aged. In Twenty Years at Hull-House (1910) Addams wrote that the house \"had a half-skeptical reputation for a haunted attic, so far respected by the tenants living on the second floor that they always kept a large pitcher full of water on the attic stairs.\" She guessed it was \"a survival of the belief that a ghost could not cross running water\" — but added, disarmingly, \"perhaps that interpretation was only my eagerness for finding folklore.\" (R15 has the 1913 Devil Baby at the same house; that is a separate legend.) The odd detail: The tenants' defence is a pitcher of water on the stairs — a barrier between the living floor and the dead one — and the most famous social worker in America recorded it.",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (Addams's own words). The later \"Mrs. Hull's ghost\" stories told on modern ghost tours are LATER and were not checked.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The water-on-the-stairs rule is a period-attested mechanic the setting can use for its ghosts: running (or poured) water as a threshold. After Addams dies in May 1935, someone forgets to keep the pitcher filled.",
