      "summary": "The coroner decided which bodies were opened; the cutting was done by a coroner's physician, a post created in 1889 under Coroner Henry L. Hertz — \"a pathologist appointed with the rank of deputy coroner\" (before 1889 the county physician did it). By 1919 there were six. Autopsies at County Hospital averaged 60 to 80 a month. Under Coroner Oscar Wolff (1922–28) Dr. E. R. LeCount, \"a pathologist of international reputation\", was discharged after thirteen years; by June 1928 \"no member of the staff of coroner's physicians holds a position as hospital pathologist.\" Dr. Hatton's review of their reports found external and internal findings run together so \"it is difficult to tell whether or not the body was opened.\" The odd detail: the Survey's pathologists could not tell from Cook County autopsy reports whether the body had actually been cut open.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the quoted findings. Who could demand an autopsy besides the coroner (family, State's Attorney) is not in what I read — UNVERIFIED.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A report that does not say whether the body was opened is a gift: the party can find out it wasn't, and ask why the coroner's physician signed for organs he never weighed.",
