      "summary": "Teresa Urrea (born 15 October 1873 at Ocoroni, Sinaloa; her mother a fourteen-year-old Indigenous ranch worker, her father Tomás Urrea) fell gravely ill in autumn 1889, had visions, and on recovery claimed the Virgin had given her the power to heal. About 1,200 people camped near Cabora for cures; the Mayo and Yaqui, pressed to give up their lands, venerated her as \"La Santa de Cabora.\" The Chihuahua mountain village of Tomóchic — in drought, and under an army that wanted its lands — declared that God alone was its authority under leader Cruz Chávez, and fought the federal troops from 7 December 1891; the army razed the village in October 1892. About 300 villagers were dead by year's end; six survivors, including Chávez and his brother, were lined up and shot on the morning of 29 October 1892. In May 1892 the Mayo sacked Navojoa crying \"Viva la Santa de Cabora\" and the government exiled Teresa and her father to Nogales, Arizona. She died of tuberculosis on 11 January 1906 in Clifton, Arizona. The odd detail: Tomóchic's defenders invoked her name in the sierra — but \"there is no direct evidence\" she took part, and the village never met her; a healer's name was enough to hang a rebellion on.",
