      "summary": "Bedward (born 1848) led the Jamaica Native Baptist Free Church from 1889, baptising and healing in the Hope River; at his height perhaps 30,000 followed him. In 1895 he was tried for sedition for preaching that \"there is a white wall and a black wall … the black wall has become bigger than the white,\" and was sectioned to the asylum, then freed by a lawyer. On 31 December 1920 he announced the Lord had called him to fly up to Heaven, with his followers to follow — and set the hour at 10 a.m., then 3 p.m., then 10 p.m. He stayed on the ground. In 1921 he marched on Kingston with some 800 followers, was arrested and committed to the asylum again; he died there on 8 November 1930. The odd detail: three appointed hours for the ascension, and a crowd that waited through all three.",
