      "summary": "The prison opened in 1889 in Richardsonian Romanesque style, in local sandstone with red trim, built for $135,817. As the Wikipedia summary tells it, in 1921 three inmates attacked Warden Theodore Burr Catlin and three staff with knives in the prison theatre and all four victims died; the attackers were flogged but not prosecuted further, \"due to double jeopardy concerns\" (not cross-checked). In 1931 three Detroit convicts (27 August) fired on hospital staff, killing Dr. A. W. Hornbogen; they then barricaded themselves and took their own lives (Wikipedia names only Hornbogen as killed) — a story that made national headlines, including the New York Times. (On 2 February 1954 the Detroit Red Wings played an exhibition game against inmates outdoors at 21°F.) The odd detail: A fairy-tale castle on the lakeshore whose two worst crimes were a warden knifed in the theatre and a doctor shot in the hospital.",
