      "summary": "When the 1889 Nelson Act set up allotment across Minnesota's Ojibwe lands, Red Lake \"successfully resisted\" it — the Nation's own account — and remains \"untouched Indian land, as it has never left tribal control.\" In 1889 Red Lake was forced to cede 2,905,000 acres of its original 3,260,000 as \"surplus\"; further agreements in 1902 and 1904 fixed the present boundaries (\"the 1904 Land Act\"). When the 1934 Indian Reorganization Act invited Red Lake to join six other Chippewa bands, it resisted joining and kept its own government; those six bands became the Minnesota Chippewa Tribe (constitution approved 1936). (A search-result summary adds that Red Lake sued the Office of Indian Affairs in the mid-1930s over trust funds, that the outcome affirmed its exclusive land rights in 1935, and that its constitution blended hereditary chiefs with elections in 1936 — UNVERIFIED; the Nation's own history says a democratic constitution came in the 1950s and the first elections were in 1959 with Roger Jourdain as chairman.) The anthropologist Ruth Landes worked among the Ojibwe in 1932–36 (see R22). The odd detail: While neighbouring White Earth was losing 93 per cent of its land, Red Lake kept its remaining reservation intact by refusing allotment — and refused the 1934 reorganization machinery too.",
