      "summary": "Born 10 October 1889 at Sagamore Hill, Kermit went with his father on the 1909 African expedition as a Harvard freshman. On the River of Doubt his rope- and canoe-handling skill \"was instrumental in saving his father's life\"; he downplayed his own sickness to save quinine for his father, nearly dying himself. He married Belle Wyatt Willard in 1914, founded the Roosevelt Steamship Company and United States Lines, served the British Army in Mesopotamia (Military Cross, 1918), was Audubon Society president (1935–37), and served with British and U.S. forces in the Second World War until discharged on health grounds in 1941. He died by suicide at Fort Richardson, Alaska, on 4 June 1943, after years of depression and alcoholism. The odd detail: the son who kept his father alive on the River of Doubt died at 53 by his own hand at a post in the Alaskan cold.",
