      "summary": "A soldier of fortune \"when he was little more than 21\", Richardson was advertised as \"the world's greatest machine gunner\": in one Nicaraguan revolution he and a partner are said to have \"wiped out a Nicaraguan federal regiment with two machine guns\" (Wikipedia's Holmdahl page says he \"single-handedly captured Managua\"). With Sam Dreben he served the Orozco rebels in 1912 at $500 in gold a month (Taylor's figure). A Frontier Partisans page adds a saloon story: Richardson allegedly held Pancho Villa at gunpoint in an El Paso saloon and made him revoke a death order. In September 1914 he joined the Princess Patricia's Canadian Light Infantry as a private and rose to lieutenant, then captain, in the 97th Canadian Infantry Battalion (the \"American Legion\" — see M14). The Canadian Great War Project lists him with fourteen wounds (some at Veracruz, others in Flanders); he was invalided with a pension, gave up the pension to rejoin a machine-gun battery, and was known there as \"the Human Sieve\". After the war he prospected, worked as a detective and wrote for magazines; a Frontier Partisans page says he transferred to the U.S. Air Service and became a lieutenant colonel in the Second World War. He died 20 April 1949. His biography: Dale L. Walker's \"World's Greatest Machine Gunner?\": Tracy Richardson, Soldier of Fortune (title as it appears in a Google Books listing). The odd detail: the Canadian attestation record lists his birthplace as \"Bleiz\" (Belize), British Honduras — a man sold as the archetypal American gunslinger may have been born in the very colony where the mercenary trade lived.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium on the military record (a Canadian archive page); Low on the Managua and Villa-saloon stories. The Canadian record's birthplace and the \"Veracruz\" wounds are UNVERIFIED (possible transcription errors). His WWII rank is Frontier Partisans only.",
