      "summary": "Founded 1889, the Abendpost (with its Sunday Sonntagpost) was Chicago's German evening daily for sixty-one years, to 1950 — outliving the Staats-Zeitung by three decades; among its editors was Paul Bechtner, later a Wisconsin assemblyman. It is the source of most of the German items in this file and in R15, and the paper that in 1930 counted the city \"by blood\" (R15). Wikipedia's list gives Chicago's German press as a crowd: Die Fackel 1879–1919, Abend Presse 1896–1913, Eintracht 1922–2017, and the socialist Chicagoer Arbeiter-Zeitung (Haymarket's paper). The odd detail: in 1918 the paper that had printed the Kaiser's birthday speeches printed \"Widows and Orphans Are Robbed\" against the Kaiser's ambassador — and survived by it.",
