      "summary": "Dr. Patrick Henry Cronin, Irish-born physician and Clan na Gael activist, accused faction leader Alexander Sullivan (\"the Triangle,\" with Michael Boland and Denis Feely — backers of the \"dynamite policy\" of bombing Britain, incl. the 1885 Tower of London and Commons bombs) of embezzling $100,000; Sullivan's side named Cronin a British spy. On 4 May 1889 a man summoned Cronin to treat an \"injured\" iceman; he left in a buggy with a white horse and never returned. At 2 a.m. on 5 May police saw a wagon carrying a large trunk north on Clark St.; the trunk turned up bloody, with a lock of hair. On 22 May his body was found wedged in a sewer catch basin — five scalp wounds \"possibly an ice pick.\" Police detective Daniel Coughlin had hired the buggy; iceman Patrick O'Sullivan arranged the call; Martin Burke rented the cottage as \"Frank Williams.\" Jury selection took 1,115 interviews (26 Aug–22 Oct 1889); O'Sullivan and Coughlin got life. 12,000 viewed the coffin; 8,000 marched, the largest procession since Lincoln's body. In 1929 the Tribune ran a $500 contest inviting readers to \"solve the mystery.\" The odd detail: a Chicago police detective hired the murder buggy; Patrick \"The Fox\" Cooney, who rented the furniture as \"J. B. Simmonds,\" was never caught — a severed finger was found in the bloody cotton.",
