      "summary": "A beautiful woman met on a lonely road at dusk — finely dressed, a wide hat or tall turban hiding the face, a long dress hiding that one foot is a cow's hoof (she walks with one foot on the road and the hoof in the grass); she smells of perfume and decay. She leads a man off into the forest or to a precipice and disappears; he is found mad, lost, or dead. Her origin, in later tellings: a woman who sold her soul to the Devil for beauty. Lafcadio Hearn gave her a chapter, \"La Guiablesse\", in Two Years in the French West Indies (Harper, New York, 1890), from his 1887–89 residence in St Pierre; in his telling, asked where she lives, she answers with a Creole proverb — \"Zaffai cabritt pa zaffai lapin\" (the goat's business is not the rabbit's). The odd detail: Hearn's St Pierre, where he heard her story, was obliterated twelve years after he printed it (Part F).",
