      "summary": "Policy came to Black Chicago in the 1890s with a man remembered only as \"Policy Sam\" (Sam Young — from memory), and by the late 1890s Patsy King was the premier purveyor of the racket, running the city's largest wheels — the Frankfort and the Kentucky, among others — the names the 1930s wheels still echoed. Harlem's numbers, by contrast, grew from the 1920s (thirty Black policy banks by 1925: Casper Holstein, Stephanie St. Clair, Bumpy Johnson and the rest) and drew their winning number from the Clearing House totals, at 600-to-1 against true odds of 999-to-1; Chicago's game kept the drawn wheel — numbered balls pulled by hand, twice a day, in a room somebody controlled. The odd detail: Harlem's number came from a bank clearing-house figure nobody could fix; Chicago's came out of a drum in a back room — which is why Chicago's game was always about who turned the wheel.",
