      "summary": "Charles Van Schaick, a German-American immigrant who was justice of the peace and town photographer, made about 30,000 glass-plate negatives between 1890 and 1900 (unverified — V_R42: a search result says ~30,000 negatives in all, ~8,000 salvaged; 1890–1900 dates the plates Lesy used). After his death in 1940 they sat in his studio for thirty years; the Wisconsin Historical Society acquired the collection in 1970 and salvaged about 8,000 negatives. Michael Lesy, a UW graduate student who found the plates in the late 1960s, chose about 200 for Wisconsin Death Trip (Pantheon, June 1973), set against the Badger State Banner (edited by Frank Cooper and his son George), Mendota State Hospital records, and passages from Hamlin Garland, Sinclair Lewis and Glenway Wescott. The book documents the 1890s panic in Jackson County (1885–1900): diphtheria among children, economic collapse after mine closures, violent crime, suicide, mental illness. A 1999 film by James Marsh (narrated by Ian Holm; music by DJ Shadow and John Cale) followed. The odd detail: In 1936 the man who photographed the dead children of the 1890s was still alive and working in the same town.",
