      "summary": "From 1890 James O. Scotford, a sign painter of Edmore, \"found\" clay cups and carved tablets with cuneiform-like symbols and scenes of Moses with the Ten Commandments and of Noah; by 1907 he and Daniel E. Soper — a former Michigan Secretary of State who had resigned over embezzlement charges — were selling \"copper crowns\" and copies of \"Noah's diary.\" In 1911 Scotford's stepdaughter swore she had seen him making them and that he threatened her. Father James Savage, pastor of Most Holy Trinity in Detroit, acquired some forty objects, believed they proved the Lost Tribes of Israel had reached Michigan, and held to it until his death in 1927; he left his relics to Notre Dame; the LDS Church later held 797 pieces, given to the Michigan History Museum in Lansing in 2003. The odd detail: A Detroit Catholic pastor spent his last decades defending buried \"Hebrew\" tablets from a Michigan field — dying in 1927, within living memory of any 1930s Detroit priest.",
