      "summary": "Lithuanians made Bridgeport's 33rd & Halsted their \"downtown\" from the 1890s to the 1950s — the same Bridgeport that produced the Irish mayors. Their press split along the community's great fault line: Draugas (\"Friend,\" Catholic, from 1903), Naujienos (socialist, 1914–1980s), Vilnis (communist, 1920–89), plus Lietuva and Sandara. The WPA survey found Lithuanian building-and-loan and benefit clubs everywhere — including, by 1936, a Darius-Girėnas Savings and Loan Association and an American Legion Darius-Girėnas Post No. 271. The odd detail: a Catholic daily, a socialist daily and a communist daily, all in Lithuanian, all within a streetcar ride of each other.",
