      "summary": "The reaper family held a ring of estates round the city. Villa Turicum, Charles A. Platt's Italian villa of 1912 on the Lake Forest bluff, was built for Harold F. McCormick and Edith Rockefeller McCormick and barely lived in after their 1921 divorce (Platt's list marks it simply \"1912, demolished\"). Cyrus Hall McCormick Jr. (1859–1936), head of International Harvester, kept Walden and the town house, gave Princeton McCormick Hall in 1923 and \"brought Sergei Prokofiev to the United States\"; he died in 1936. Chauncey Brooks McCormick (1884–1954), later Harvester's president, married Marion Deering — the Deering fortune's Villa Vizcaya in Miami was hers — and farmed St. James Farm outside Wheaton, a few miles from his cousin the Colonel's Cantigny (see R08). Medill McCormick, senator, had died in 1925; his widow Ruth Hanna McCormick ran for the Senate in 1930 (section 5). The odd detail: Two branches of one family owned, within twenty miles of the Loop, a demolished Roman villa, a farm named for a saint and an estate named for a battle — and the head of the house died the year the alpha is set.",
