      "summary": "9,558 Hungarians in 1900; 43,134 in 1920 (18% of the foreign-born). The city boasted North America's first Hungarian churches: First Hungarian Reformed (1891), St. Elizabeth of Hungary Roman Catholic (1892) and St. John's Greek Catholic (1892); by the early 1920s there were eleven Hungarian churches of six denominations plus three Hungarian Jewish congregations. Six Hungarian-language papers by 1920; Szabadság reached a daily circulation of 40,612 in 1940. A statue of Louis Kossuth, leader of the 1848 revolution against the Habsburgs, has stood at University Circle since 1902. The United Hungarian Societies date from 1902. The odd detail: The nickname \"American Debrecen\" (after Hungary's Calvinist capital) — which, ECH notes drily, \"did not hold up statistically.\"",
