      "summary": "In 1890 the National Malleable Castings Company moved about 200 Hungarian workers from its Cleveland plant to its new East Toledo foundry on Front Street; the neighbourhood took the name of the English steel town. Most settlers came from the \"Paloc\" counties of north-central Hungary (Heves, Abaúj, Gömör). They got their own parish in 1898 — St. Stephen, King of Hungary — with about a hundred families registered the next year, having first worshipped at Sacred Heart. In 1932, in the worst of the Depression, Tony and Rose Packo opened a sandwich shop at 1902 Front Street on a $100 loan; Packo trademarked his Hungarian sausage on rye with chili sauce in 1935. Library photos show a Birmingham branch library on Paine Avenue (about 1930) and Birmingham School at Paine and Valentine (about 1935). The odd detail: Both men the Guard killed in May 1934 carry what look like Hungarian surnames (Hubay; Cyigon ≈ Csigány) — but no source read this session says they were from Birmingham; treat any link as UNVERIFIED and worth one afternoon in the Blade's 1934 files.",
