      "summary": "The Cervecería Cuauhtémoc was founded on 8 November 1890 by Isaac Garza, José A. Muguerza, Francisco Sada, Joseph M. Schnaider and others, with 150,000 pesos and the Carta Blanca label; its first barrel came in 1893 and it reportedly won first prize at \"the Chicago and Paris world fairs.\" A cardboard works (c. 1900) and a glass-bottle company, Vidrios y Cristales de Monterrey (1909), made the group self-supplying; the working day was cut from twelve to nine hours in 1907 and the Sociedad Cuauhtémoc (1918) gave workers' families medical care and schooling. In the Revolution the founders backed Huerta; Carranza seized the brewery, the families fled to Texas, and they got it back \"through the intervention of U.S. and Russian diplomats\" (Wikipedia's wording). The odd detail: the Monterrey magnates' route back to their own brewery ran through a Russian diplomat — in a world where, in the setting, Russia never went red and Germany won, whichever embassy that was is a live question. (Source's phrase is unverified; Medium.)",
