      "summary": "The parish was organised by Rev. F. S. Sustersic, who arrived 12 May 1891; the cornerstone was blessed 19 July 1891 by a Benedictine abbot from Lisle and the church, a stone building 90 × 52 ft, was blessed on 18 October the same year. There were 73 families at the start and more than 200 by 1897; a parish school opened 2 October 1895 with 64 children and a 12-room parish house was dedicated in October 1898. Slovenes came from the northern Austro-Hungarian province that became Slovenia; Slovaks donated statues, and four societies (St. Joseph's, St. George's Knights, SS. Cyril and Methodius, St. John the Baptist) raised money for it. A second, larger church (1905) and the Croats and Poles who wanted to join are in other page summaries; the Krakar stone donation and the quarries are in a search-summary. The odd detail: The parish's founding priest and 73 families were Austro-Hungarian subjects — Joliet's Slovenes came out of the very empire that in the setting survives as Germany's junior partner.",
