      "summary": "Anton C. Hesing and other German investors formed the German Opera House Company and hired Adler & Sullivan to build a seventeen-storey tower — among the city's tallest — over a 1,300-seat theatre for German-language opera and drama; it opened in 1891–92 as the Schiller Theater Building. The German theatre did not pay: it became the Dearborn Theater (1898–1903) and then the Garrick, a movie house in the 1930s, a TV studio in 1950, and was demolished in 1961 over the first great Chicago preservation fight (Richard Nickel salvaged its ornament). In 1902 the Imperial German Consulate had its offices in the Schiller Building (the Prince Henry decorations were presented there). The odd detail: the German consulate sat in a skyscraper named for Schiller, above a German opera house, in the Loop — the Kaiser's man had Sullivan's terra-cotta for a doorway.",
