      "summary": "Holmes built the block in 1887–92, adding a third floor in 1892 which he told suppliers would be a hotel for the 1893 Fair. Nine murders are confirmed: Julia and Pearl Conner (Christmas Eve 1891), Emeline Cigrand (Dec 1892), Minnie and Nannie Williams (1893), Benjamin Pitezel (Sept 1894) and three Pitezel children, Alice, Nellie and Howard (Oct 1894). He confessed to 27; he lied about most things. The \"Murder Castle\" of trapdoors, gas chambers and a basement crematorium was invented by the press after his 1894 arrest; the building had hidden rooms, used to hide furniture bought on credit. It burned suspiciously in Aug 1895, survived, and was torn down in 1938. Hanged at Moyamensing, Philadelphia, 7 May 1896. The odd detail: The Castle was still standing, in use, on 63rd St through the whole of the 1930s — a Chicagoan of 1936 could walk past it — and came down the year the Torso killings stopped.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The owner's three layers in one building: what the papers said (torture castle), what the folklore says (the Fair's missing girls), what was really there (nine dead and a credit fraud). The 1938 demolition is a scene: what did the wreckers find?",
