      "summary": "The chickcharney is a feathered, red-eyed creature with three toes, a long prehensile tail and a head that turns all the way round; it nests by binding the tops of two pines together, and blesses or curses a traveller by how he treats it. Zoologists tie the tale to the extinct Andros giant barn owl, Tyto pollens. The record: the young Neville Chamberlain spent the 1890s running his father Joseph's sisal plantation on Andros, which failed; the island's joke-legend says he cut down the chickcharnies' trees and they cursed him — down to Munich. Elsie Clews Parsons printed 115 tales from this one island in Folk-Tales of Andros Island, Bahamas (American Folk-Lore Society Memoirs 13, 1918). The odd detail: a British prime minister's first failure was a haunted forest in the Bahamas.",
