      "summary": "The Chicago Tribune (16 Sept 1891) announced \"Chicago's First Spanish Newspaper,\" El Eco de Chicago, sixteen pages. Robert E. Park's 1922 count (cited by Arias & Durand; scope not stated in the passage) was 417 Spanish-language publications between 1884 and 1920, 334 already vanished. Redfield (1924) found two: the weekly México and El Heraldo de las Américas (which urged \"American patriotism\"). México was celebrating its sixth anniversary in April–May 1930, so began 1924; its editor Miguel Ángel Peral was passing through in June 1930. Others in the FLPS: El Nacional (semi-weekly, Dec 1930–33); El Liberal (started 1 July 1933; anticlerical; its fourth issue was 22 July, its ninth 19 Aug); La Lucha (1932–34); La Raza (editor Francisco F. Bentacourt); El Tringuete (satire; entry 23); El Indicador (\"educational paper,\" April 1933); El Buen Samaritano (evangelical, 1924); El Ideal Mexicano and La Defensa (R15). The assignment named El Correo Mexicano; it does not appear in what I read. The odd detail: every one of these papers exists for us because the WPA hired translators to type its best articles in the 1930s.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (names/dates); El Correo Mexicano unconfirmed.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a small editorial staff, a printing plant, a WPA translator — the classic setup for an investigator to find the one paper that printed something before it was true.",
