      "summary": "Born (29 June 1893, though official papers say 1891) to a domestic worker of African descent, Juliana Campos, and a Basque merchant who never acknowledged him. He studied at the University of Vermont (1912) and Harvard (1913), volunteered in the First World War, and served as a lieutenant in the 375th Infantry — a segregated regiment for \"Puerto Ricans of recognizably African descent.\" Harvard Law 1921. He joined the Puerto Rican Nationalist Party in 1924 and was elected its president on 11 May 1930, a post he held until death. In 1932 he published the accusation against Dr. Cornelius Rhoads (see below). He was convicted of seditious conspiracy in 1936 and sent to the Atlanta penitentiary (released 1943–47); after the 1950 revolts he was jailed again at La Princesa in San Juan and alleged radiation experiments on himself — a claim later physicians reportedly supported (a Cuban radiologist, Orlando Daumy, judged his sores radiation burns). He died 21 April 1965; some 75,000 attended the funeral. He was a Catholic and spoke six modern and two classical languages. The odd detail: he became \"El Rey de las Toallas\" — the King of the Towels — for the wet towels he wrapped round his head against the burning he blamed on radiation.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for dates and career; the radiation claim is Medium (alleged; post-1940, outside the window).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a Black Puerto Rican Harvard man who speaks eight languages and preaches to a crowd is exactly what an occupying empire and a Golden Circle recruiter would both want to watch. IHS angle: he was devoutly Catholic. Handle with respect — he is a real national hero to many Puerto Ricans, not a villain.",
