      "summary": "Louis Sullivan's rare residential commission, with the young Frank Lloyd Wright drawing for him, built 1891–92 for the Philadelphia-born lumber magnate James Charnley, who lived in it only a decade (he retired with kidney disease in 1902). After a run of prominent tenants the real-estate investor James B. Waller Jr. bought it in 1918 and his family held it fifty years — the whole of the 1930s. Restored by Skidmore, Owings & Merrill in 1986 (about $1.7 million) and bought for the Society of Architectural Historians with Seymour Persky's $1.65 million in 1995. The odd detail: The one house in the city where Sullivan and Wright both worked was, through the whole decade, simply a rich family's home with a famous front and no plaque.",
