      "summary": "Archbishop Patrick Feehan founded the paper with a Catholic Press Company capitalised at $20,000 from himself and about 40 pastors; he took the name from the coming World's Columbian Exposition's theme of America as the New World. The first issue (the archdiocese's own articles give both 8 and 10 Sept 1892) cost 5 cents, ran eight pages, and carried a pastoral letter, Fair stories and a report on Protestantism's spread in Scandinavia. Early editors were laymen: John Hyde, then William J. Dillon (1893–1902), who sided with labour. The 1938 register: \"Official Catholic Newspaper of the Archdiocese of Chicago and Province of Illinois; Circulation, 53,134. Founded in 1892. Managing Editor, Rev. Timothy Rowan. Rev. Edward V. Dailey, Editorial Writer\" — Dailey lived in the cathedral rectory (Part 1). The same register gives the Diocesan Catholic Cemeteries at 109 N. Dearborn too, under a priest superintendent (the name reads \"William P. Casny\" in the OCR — probably Casey; UNVERIFIED) and his assistant Rev. Joseph B. Heldmann. It was The New World that printed John Kelly's 1921 account of Damen's seven lights (Part 2). The odd detail: The Church's newspaper and the office that sold its graves shared one Loop address.",
