      "id": "R27-018",
      "title": "The Third Ward fire",
      "date_start": "1892-10-28",
      "date_end": "1892-10-28",
      "date_label": "Friday 28 October 1892, 5:40 p.m. (living memory)",
      "category": "Milwaukee 1925–1940: the most German city in America, in a world where Germany won",
      "tags": [
        "Milwaukee",
        "German",
        "Third Ward",
        "living memory"
      ],
      "people": [],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "From the Union Oil and Paint Company on the river at Water Street south of St. Paul Avenue",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "east to the lake and south to Erie Street — twenty blocks of the Third Ward",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=From%20the%20Union%20Oil%20and%20Paint%20Company%20on%20the%20river%20at%20Water%20Street%20south%20of%20St.%20Paul%20Avenue%2C%20east%20to%20the%20lake%20and%20south%20to%20Erie%20Street%20%E2%80%94%20twenty%20blocks%20of%20the%20Thi"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Spontaneous combustion in an oil-and-paint warehouse at 5:40 p.m.; a west and north-west wind drove the fire through twenty blocks by midnight, destroying more than 440 buildings, mostly small wooden houses. Five died — two firemen and an unidentified man crushed by a wall, and two women with weak hearts. More than 350 families (about 2,500 people), overwhelmingly Irish — the \"Bloody Third\" — were made homeless; only a tenth had insurance. The Irish moved west to Tory Hill and Merrill Park and out to West Allis, South Milwaukee and Cudahy; over the next ten years Sicilians took the rebuilt ward, and by 1920 about 12,000 Milwaukeeans had Italian-born fathers. The glow was seen from Waukegan, Illinois, to Sheboygan. The relief committees had to help policyholders fight their own insurers' fraud claims. The odd detail: The fire is why Milwaukee's Little Italy exists — the Italians moved into the ashes of the Irish, and forty years later the two communities' children were the city's police and its bombers respectively (see the 1917 bomb).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A ward that burned in a single evening from an oil warehouse with no known cause, then changed its whole population: the Third Ward is the ring's test case for three layers of truth — spontaneous combustion; the Irish said the Italians; underneath, something that wanted the ground cleared.",
