      "summary": "Germany's 1893 industrial showing included a porcelain display \"one of the greatest attractions to art lovers\"; Sonneberg toymakers' dolls in a carriage topped by a Christmas tree (Section F, 77–79); the Germania Fountain north of the German Building — Germania on a globe borne by four seated women, a lamp held aloft; and an 18 × 12-foot ceramic tableau of 1,057 tiles, \"The Glory of Germania.\" After the fair the tableau hung 93 years in the Germania Club — the German-American establishment's clubhouse, founded 1865 as the Germania Männerchor to sing at Lincoln's funeral, its August Fiedler building of 1889 with ballroom, banquet hall and bar — then 32 years elsewhere, and was exhibited at DANK Haus in July–September 2018. The odd detail: A piece of the 1893 German pavilion has hung in a Chicago German clubhouse for every decade the setting cares about.",
