      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Indianapolis",
          "address": "401 E. Michigan Street",
          "geography": "401 E. Michigan Street (near downtown)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Indianapolis%2C%20401%20E.%20Michigan%20Street"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The Socialer Turnverein's stock company built Das Deutsche Haus in two phases, the east wing in 1893–94 and the west wing in 1897–98, to a design by Bernard Vonnegut of Vonnegut & Bohn — both partners American-born of German parents and trained in Germany. It held a gymnasium, locker rooms, a restaurant, an auditorium, a billiard room, bowling alleys, a ball-and-concert hall and a walled beer garden with a concert pavilion. The Normal College of the North American Gymnastic Union was housed in it from 1907 to 1970. On 22 February 1918, under wartime nativism, the club renamed itself \"the Athenaeum.\" Clemens Vonnegut Sr. co-founded the city's first Turnverein in 1851; the family is still commemorated in the Rathskeller's \"Vonnegut Room.\" The odd detail: Legend says the building is haunted — by \"Jolly Verner,\" a regular who \"took a lethal fall in the Rathskeller\"; ghostly children in the Rathskeller and a \"Woman in Blue\" on the stairwell looking for them; a Woman in White by the old YMCA rooms; and Dr. Helene Knabe in the Wilkie Room. Knabe was real: a German-born bacteriologist and physician (b. 1875, Medical College of Indiana 1904), found dead with a slashed throat in her bed on 24–25 October 1911 — police called it suicide, the coroner called it murder, women physicians funded a private detective, and over a year later two men were indicted: Dr. William B. Craig, the Indiana Veterinary College's dean of students (whom accusers said killed her to end a relationship), and the undertaker Alonzo Ragsdale, her estate's executor, accused of hiding and laundering her bloodstained kimono. The ghost stories are legend.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the building history; ghosts are legend; Knabe details from a search summary.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A German house in a Klan-scarred capital, its Rathskeller a natural meeting place for Germany's \"patron of other people's revolutions.\" The Knabe murder — a woman doctor killed in her bed, an undertaker accused of washing her kimono — is a cold case a 1930s Section 13 researcher could reopen (the 1911 acquittal outcome is not in the summaries I read).",
