      "summary": "Burnham was Chief of Scouts in the Second Matabele War and the Boer War (twice captured, twice escaped; DSO from Edward VII) and taught Robert Baden-Powell woodcraft in the Matopos in 1896, which the movement's founders credit for the Boy Scouts. In Mexico he partnered with mining engineer John Hays Hammond on an irrigation scheme for the Yaqui River valley (Hammond bought some 900,000 acres; Burnham did archaeological work too — the Esperanza Stone, 1909 (unverified — V_M19: year not given on Wikipedia)). At the Taft–Díaz summit on 16 October 1909 he led 250 private security men hired by Hammond; with Texas Ranger C. R. Moore he seized an assassin (identified in a search summary as Julius Bergerson, 52 (unverified — V_M19: name, age and caliber not found on any page read; Wikipedia says only a concealed palm pistol)) carrying a .32-calibre, seven-shot \"Protector Palm Pistol\" disguised like a pencil. The mines and irrigation collapsed after 1912, Mexico barred foreign land sales in 1917, and Burnham and Hammond sold to the Mexican government in 1930. In 1917 Roosevelt named Burnham one of 18 officers for a volunteer division that Wilson refused. He co-founded the Wild Life Protective League (1914), sat on California's first parks commission (1927–34), and in 1936 enlisted Arizona Boy Scouts to save desert bighorn sheep (Kofa and Cabeza Prieta refuges, 1939). The odd detail: the man who saved two presidents from a pencil gun in 1909 was a friend of Roosevelt's and one of the men who wanted hippopotamuses in Louisiana (next entry).",
