      "summary": "The only whaleback passenger ship ever built — launched at Superior, Wisconsin on 3 December 1892, commissioned 13 May 1893, rated for 5,000 passengers and said to have carried 7,000 on her fair-year maiden trip — ran from 1909 into the early 1930s a daily Chicago–Milwaukee round for the Goodrich line: out mid-morning, two hours in Milwaukee, home by night. On 30 June 1917, being turned by tugs in the Milwaukee River, she struck a water tower on the bank which fell across her decks and killed 16 passengers; she was out for the rest of the year. She was taken out of service in 1933, the year Goodrich — Chicago's lake-passenger line since the 1850s — went bankrupt and ceased permanently; she changed hands twice and was cut up at Manitowoc Shipbuilding in 1936. The odd detail: A cigar-shaped steel ship with a bow like a pig's snout, the fastest and strangest thing on the Chicago waterfront for forty years, killed by the Depression rather than the lake.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
