      "summary": "The earliest known mention is a brief \"Paul Bunion\" item in the Gladwin County Record (Michigan), 17 March 1893; the first recorded story ran in the Duluth News Tribune, 4 Aug 1904 (\"the year of the blue snow\"); James MacGillivray's \"Round River\" appeared in the Oscoda Press in 1906. Between 1916 and 1922 William B. Laughead's advertising pamphlets for the Red River Lumber Company made him a giant and named the blue ox Babe; Esther Shephard (1924) and James Stevens (1925) published book collections; Bemidji put up his statue in 1937. Folklorist Richard Dorson later called him \"fakelore,\" though K. Bernice Stewart documented genuine loggers' oral tales predating the ads. The odd detail: A 1930s Midwesterner's \"old lumberjack legend\" was, largely, a lumber company's advertising mascot from the 1910s — and towns were building statues to it in 1937.",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A lesson the owner's \"three layers\" can use: the period often manufactured folklore. An investigator who learns the Hodag (R07) and Paul Bunyan were promotions should still ask what the real lumberjacks were afraid of.",
