      "summary": "Opened 1893 as the Auditorium Annex, joined to the Auditorium by a marble-lined underground passage called Peacock Alley; 871 rooms by 1907; in the 1930s run by hotel man Ralph Hitz and home to a Benny Goodman NBC broadcast. The Capone connection is legend (he is said to have used the Florentine Room; not on the page). In 1939 Adele Langer, a Jewish refugee, leapt from a thirteenth-floor window with her two sons, fearing deportation after fleeing occupied Czechoslovakia. Folklore adds \"Peg Leg Johnny\", the sealed room, and Room 441. The odd detail: An underground marble hall connecting two grand buildings, and a family who chose the window over being sent back.",
