      "id": "R04-008",
      "title": "The Krupp Gun Pavilion and \"The Thunderer\"",
      "date_start": "1893-05",
      "date_end": "1893-10",
      "date_label": "May–October 1893",
      "category": "A Century of Progress International Exposition (Chicago, 1933–34) and the fairs around it",
      "tags": [
        "World's Fair",
        "exposition",
        "Krupp Gun Pavilion",
        "Thunderer"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Friedrich Alfred \"Fritz\" Krupp",
        "Dr. William Raydt"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Jackson Park lakefront",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "between the La Rabida convent replica and the Leather exhibit, south of the Casino (the site is now La Rabida Children's Hospital)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Jackson%20Park%20lakefront%2C%20between%20the%20La%20Rabida%20convent%20replica%20and%20the%20Leather%20exhibit%2C%20south%20of%20the%20Casino%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Fried. Krupp of Essen built its own fortress-villa pavilion (entrance hall 138 × 25 × 30 ft; main hall 197 × 82 × 43 ft), modelled as a cross between a fort and the family's Villa Hügel (unverified — V2_R04), at a cost put at about $1.5 million to build and as much again to ship. Its centrepiece was the largest gun in the world: 42 cm (16.54 in) bore, 46 ft long, breech 6.5 ft across, barrel weight given as \"just over 240,000 pounds\" (drloih, about 109 metric tons) or 122.4 metric tons, about 270,000 lb (Wikipedia) — the sources disagree, costing $200,000 to make and $80,000 to bring across; the shell weighed 2,000–2,500 lb driven by 900 lb of brown powder, with an advertised range of 13–15 miles, and a shrapnel round throwing some 3,400 quarter-pound steel balls. Krupp offered it to the U.S. Army for $223,000 with turret and mounts; the Army declined it as too dangerous and too dear (about $1,500 a shot). Two other \"world's largest\" steel cannon in the hall \"worked poorly.\" Krupp also installed the fair's first indoor air-conditioning — two \"Glacier Fountains\" (Dr. William Raydt of Hanover, 1884–86 design) spraying water over brine-chilled copper coils, so ice formed in the hall and visitors \"plac[ed] their hands close to the fountain to cool themselves.\" By late March 1894 the dismantled pavilion was on a steamer home. The odd detail: The biggest gun on earth was displayed in the only air-conditioned room in Chicago (unverified — V_R04), and the ice came from a carbonic-acid refrigerator.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the gun's existence, size and the pavilion; Medium on cost figures (sources disagree between $1M and $1.5M) and range claims (Krupp's own marketing). • main hall 197 × 82 × 43 ft), modelled as a cross between a fort and the family's Villa Hügel (unverified — V2_R04), at a cost put at about $1.5 million to build and as much again to ship. • The biggest gun on earth was displayed in the only air-conditioned room in Chicago (unverified — V_R04), and the ice came from a carbonic-acid refrigerator.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — In 1933 the old men who saw the Thunderer as boys run the city. A Krupp return to the 1933 fair (see proposal above) reopens the 1893 site — and whatever was buried under a $1.5-million fortress that stood for six months and then vanished.",
