      "summary": "Sculptor Albert Weinert's monument — a 16-foot granite shaft with a bronze Justice standing over a fallen worker — was dedicated June 25, 1893. It carries August Spies's words, \"The day will come when our silence will be more powerful than the voices you are throttling today,\" the date 1887, and Governor Altgeld's pardon text. Spies, Fischer, Engel, Lingg and Albert Parsons are buried there, with Schwab (1898) and Neebe (1916); only Fielden is not. Through the 1930s it was a pilgrimage site; Lucy Parsons was buried near it in 1942. A time capsule laid November 6, 1892 has never been found (a 2016 dig found a stone urn and a concrete cube instead). The odd detail: a time capsule buried at the monument in 1892 has never been found.",
