      "summary": "Born Elias Glickman in Zhitomir, Russia, in April 1870, he debuted with Jacob Adler in London in 1887, made his American debut at Troy, N.Y., in Goldfaden's Kuni-Lemels, and was naturalised in Chicago on 8 December 1893. In 1894 he formed Chicago's first Yiddish stock company at Metropolitan Hall, moved to the Lyceum by 1896, opened the International Theater on Wabash in 1907 (closed 1909 for fire-code violations — four years after the Iroquois fire), leased the Bijou in 1912, and ran the Palace at 12th and Blue Island. In 1920 he lived at 3234 Douglas Blvd. He died of heart disease at Columbus Hospital on 3 October 1931, \"about 62\"; his wife Ray died 13 February 1935. He lies at Jewish Waldheim, Gate 9 — Anshe Maariv cemetery, Row 32, Lot 2. A search summary (Chicago Reader, not opened) calls the Douglas Park Theatre at Kedzie and Ogden Chicago's last Yiddish stage, \"a people's theater,\" where Luther and Stella Adler \"cut their teeth\" — UNVERIFIED. The odd detail: A theatre closed for fire violations in the years right after the Iroquois disaster — Glickman's International on Wabash (1907–09).",
