      "summary": "Born to a banking fortune, Bowen joined Hull House in 1894 and was its treasurer, chief donor and principal fundraiser for 53 years; she was the first president (1907, for 35 years) of the Juvenile Protective Association, which picked probation officers, investigated the courts and ran detention. Her memoir Growing Up with a City (1926) records her going to Cyrus H. McCormick, president of International Harvester, over women working nights in the twine mills. When Jane Addams died in May 1935 (see R08) Bowen took the Hull House board presidency for nine years — through the worst of the Depression. The Bowen Country Club at Waukegan is Bowen Park today. The odd detail: The Gold Coast's grandest matron spent the decade's charity money through a settlement house on Halsted Street and a \"country club\" that was a camp for slum children.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The JPA's files on every wayward girl in Chicago, kept by a society woman, are the best missing-persons index a Section 13 agent could hope for — and a vampire's best hunting list.",
