      "summary": "Born in Cincinnati 30 November 1854, McDowell headed the University of Chicago Settlement from its founding in January 1894, running classes, a kindergarten and vocational placement in the packing-house district. She stood with the strikers in the 1904 stockyard strike, fought the open garbage dumps and clay pits of \"Bubbly Creek\" country, sat on the city's 1913 Commission on Waste, and in 1923 Mayor Dever made her Commissioner of Public Welfare, where she set up employment and social-survey bureaus. She died of a paralytic stroke in Chicago on 14 October 1936. The odd detail: The city renamed her street after her, so the settlement's own address moved from Gross Avenue to McDowell Avenue.",
