      "summary": "Daniel Garrison Brinton, the University of Pennsylvania's Americanist, read Nagualism: A Study in Native American Folk-lore and History to the American Philosophical Society on 5 January 1894. He argued nagualism was \"a cult … which united many and diverse tribes of Mexico and Central America into organized opposition against the government and the religion … introduced from Europe\" — a secret pan-Indian society \"bound together by strange faculties and an occult learning.\" He tied it to the Zapotec rising of 1550 (a priest claiming to be Quetzalcoatl), the 1661 Tehuantepec revolt (Don Pascual's summons to \"the thirteen governors\"), and the 1692 Mexico City riot, after which the authorities banned the piochtli scalp-lock. From Herrera he took the rule that \"when such an animal dies, so does the Indian to whom it was assigned.\" The odd detail: the period's leading American scholar framed Mexican Indigenous religion as an occult secret society plotting against the state — the same shape the setting gives the Golden Circle.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on what Brinton wrote; his \"organised secret society\" thesis is the period's reading, not established history (modern scholars treat nagualism as a belief complex, not an organisation).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the period's own category. A Section 13 or Secret Service memo on the occupied north quotes Brinton to argue the Sonoran resistance is a nagual conspiracy — so the occupier's intelligence is wrong in the most useful way: it hunts shapeshifters among farmers while the thing that actually walks the Sierra is not Mexican at all.",
