      "summary": "Established 1892 with a temporary light and lit permanently in 1895, the 77-ft Italianate brick tower (designed by Orlando M. Poe) with a third-order Henry-LePaute lens was manned through the 1930s and automated only in 1972. The Wikipedia page repeats — without citation — that the station \"is said to be haunted by the old lightkeeper,\" Joseph William Townshend/Townsend; the fuller local legend (that he died in the house in 1910, was laid out in the basement until the ground thawed, and that his cigar smoke and rearranged silverware still trouble the house) is popular lore and is not verified in the page read. Folklore. The odd detail: The name means \"only choice\" — the one harbour of refuge on that shore — and the keeper whose body waited in the cellar for spring.",
