      "summary": "Feargus B. Squire — a Standard Oil executive and former mayor of Wickliffe — bought about 525 acres around 1890 and built a Romanesque Revival gatekeeper's house of Euclid bluestone (c. 1895–97) for an estate he never finished. The Cleveland Park Board bought most of the land in 1925 and started calling it \"Squire's Castle\"; it later removed the upper floors and filled the basement. Legend says Louisa Squire fell down the stairs during a storm and died there, and haunts it; in fact \"Louisa Squire did not die in the building.\" The odd detail: The \"castle\" was only ever the gatehouse to a mansion that was never built — a door with no house behind it.",
