      "summary": "The Union's own bulletin (Mar 1927) says it was founded in 1895 as the Unione Siciliana, \"composed exclusively of Sicilians,\" and \"twelve years ago\" (c.1915) renamed itself the Italo-American National Union to end regional divisions — \"In Italy … everybody from the Alps to Ethiopia and from Sardinia to Fiume is bound by loyalty to the Duce.\" In May 1933 the bulletin celebrated its Supreme President Joseph Imburgio, elected president of the Melrose Park board of trustees on 18 Apr 1933 by \"the greatest majority … in the history of the village\" — \"the first election in Cook County of an Italo-American to the presidency of any sizable community.\" The odd detail: the name gangland histories still use for the Capone-era \"Unione Siciliana\" was, officially, retired around 1915; its 1930s president was the mayor of Melrose Park.",
