      "summary": "Skinner: the demigod Sleeping Bear hid his daughter in a covered boat on the river; the Winds, coveting her, broke its cable; the boat became Belle Isle, and Manitou set \"a girdle of vicious snakes around the shore to guard her.\" Later priests smashed an idol there and its fragments became a serpent that hampered La Salle's Griffon. (Historically the French called the island Île aux Cochons, Hog Island, where they let livestock run free; it was renamed Belle Isle on 4 July 1845 for Isabelle Cass. The common story that the hogs were put there to eat the snakes is UNVERIFIED.) The odd detail: The same legend ties Belle Isle's serpents to the loss of the Griffon, the lakes' first ghost ship (see section D).",
