      "summary": "Porfirio Díaz's government built Tampico's customs house between 1896 and 16 October 1902 on an initial budget of 1,850,000 gold pesos, designed through the Mexican Central Railroad; the story is that Díaz picked the design from an English catalogue. It is a kit of the industrial age: prefabricated cast iron from Europe, English brick, French cast-iron columns and grilles, Louisiana timber, San Luis Potosí stone, green gabled roofs. Trotsky stepped ashore here on 9 January 1937 (M01). Since 2004 its ground floor houses the museum of the 1829 victory over Spain's last attempt at reconquest (Barradas's landing). The odd detail: the building that collected Mexico's oil duties was itself imported, piece by numbered piece — a customs house that would have had to pass through customs.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (dates, cost); the catalogue story is local tradition (\"it is said\").",
