      "summary": "After hearing Gen. Daniel Sickles speak at Madison Square Garden in 1896, Funston joined the Cuban Liberation Army as an insurgent artilleryman until malaria dropped him to 95 pounds. On 13 May 1898 he was commissioned colonel of the 20th Kansas Infantry; in April 1899 he swam the Bagbag River and crossed the Pampanga under fire at Calumpit (Medal of Honor, awarded 14 February 1900). On 23 March 1901 he captured Emilio Aguinaldo at Palanan by a ruse: Macabebe Scouts posed as prisoners to get into the camp; at 35 he was made a brigadier general in the Regular Army. In April 1906, as commander at the Presidio, he declared martial law on his own authority in San Francisco after the earthquake, ordered dynamiting for firebreaks (which \"often resulted in more fires\") and authorised shoot-on-sight for looters (with innocent deaths). Mark Twain published a sarcastic essay on his Philippine methods; Theodore Roosevelt reprimanded him for insulting Senator George Frisbie Hoar and forced silence. The odd detail: the general who would command the Southern Department in 1916 began as a foreign volunteer in someone else's revolution — a filibuster with a commission.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (Wikipedia).",
