      "summary": "A single woman without family in Chicago needed a respectable address, and the city built institutions to supply one. Elizabeth Lindsay Davis's Phillis Wheatley Club (1896) bought its first house in 1906 for $3,400 and opened it in 1908 as a home for young Black women arriving without lodging — a settlement house of the Great Migration — moving to a larger building in 1913 and to South Michigan Avenue in the mid-1920s, where it ran until the 1970s. Ina Law Robertson, an Oregon schoolteacher studying at the University of Chicago, founded the first Eleanor Club in 1898 as inexpensive, supervised residence for white working women; the clubs multiplied. Settlement houses took women residents in exchange for hours of service. All three enforced hours, visitors' rules and references — and kept registers. The odd detail: The registers: every women's residence in Chicago recorded who came in, when, and who called for her — a paper trail a GM can hand a PC.",
