      "summary": "Davis, the most famous American war correspondent of his day, became fascinated with Lee Christmas at Puerto Cortés and used him as the model for his 1897 novel Soldiers of Fortune (Robert Clay, a mining engineer and mercenary who organises a counter-coup in the fictional republic of Olancho) and for Captain Macklin. In 1898, aboard a U.S. Navy ship, he witnessed the bombardment of Matanzas, Cuba; his story made headlines, and the Navy afterwards banned reporters from its vessels for the rest of the war. He also published a book of profiles titled Real Soldiers of Fortune (on Project Gutenberg, no. 3029; not read). The odd detail: the phrase \"soldier of fortune\" as a category — the one the setting's 1930s people would use — comes from a novel written about a real engineer from Louisiana.",
