      "summary": "From 1897 to 1903 German staff officers drafted war plans against the United States as staff exercises. Lt. Eberhard von Mantey (aged 28) drew the first in winter 1897–98: a fleet crosses the Atlantic, beats the US Atlantic Fleet in one battle and strikes Hampton Roads, \"the most sensitive point\". After America's 1898 gains the Kaiser ordered a redesign (March 1899): landings at Cape Cod and Sandy Hook with 100,000 soldiers, 60 warships and 40–60 transports, an advance on Boston and the bombardment of Manhattan to panic civilians. Schlieffen judged 100,000 men \"would probably be enough\" for Boston but far too few for New York. Wilhelm von Büchsel's Operationsplan III (November 1903) refined the landings (Manomet Point and Rockport, a pincer on Boston) and added a naval base on Culebra, Puerto Rico to threaten the future Panama Canal. Shelved 1906. Largely unknown until Holger Herwig found the full plans in the Freiburg military archive in 1970; the New York Times ran the story in April 1971. The odd detail: the island the Kaiser's planners wanted — Culebra — is the one the US Navy then used for its own fleet exercises for most of the century.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the plans' existence, authors and dates; Medium on the exact troop/ship numbers (Wikipedia summary of Herwig).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a copy of Operationsplan III, Culebra annotated in a later hand, surfaces in a San Juan pawnshop. The Secret Service thinks it is a forgery; FBI Section 13 thinks the annotation's date matters.",
