      "summary": "In 1910 some 200 Germans controlled about 80 per cent of Haiti's international commerce, ran utilities in Port-au-Prince and Cap-Haïtien and the capital's main wharf. Since Haiti's constitutions barred foreigners from owning land, German merchants married into prominent Haitian families. In the Lüders affair (6 Dec 1897) two German warships, SMS Charlotte and SMS Stein, gave President Tirésias Simon Sam four hours to pay a $20,000 indemnity, apologise in writing, fire a 21-gun salute to the German flag and raise a white flag over the palace; he yielded. The State Department's own history lists fear of German control among the 1915 motives. In the real record the Germans left in Haiti were imprisoned at Fort National under American guard after Haiti entered the war (1918); most left for good; their property was never returned. The odd detail: a white flag over Haiti's palace, ordered by a German captain, eighteen years before the Marines.",
