      "summary": "Joseph Leiter (1868–1932) tried to corner the wheat market in 1897–98 and was \"briefly the largest individual holder of wheat in the history of the grain trade\"; when the corner broke, his father Levi (Field's co-founder) paid debts reputed at $10 million. Levi died at the Vanderbilt cottage at Bar Harbor June 9, 1904, and his estate went through eight years of litigation. Joseph built the Zeigler Coal Company town — laid out as a wagon wheel, streets radiating from a circular park, named for his father Levi Zeigler Leiter, incorporated 1914 — where fifty men died in a 1905 mine explosion. His sister Nancy Leiter Campbell died in 1930, he in 1932 leaving a will over \"an estate that may exceed $10,000,000\" to his wife Juliette and children (his son Thomas was 20); the youngest sister Margaret \"Daisy\" was Countess of Suffolk (d. 1968), and the eldest, Mary, had been Lady Curzon, Vicereine of India (d. 1906). The odd detail: The decade's Chicago society still contained the family that had once owned all the wheat — and a coal town shaped like a wheel where fifty men died.",
