      "summary": "William Bell Riley (born 22 March 1861 in Indiana; died 5 December 1947 in Golden Valley) was pastor of Minneapolis's First Baptist Church from 1897 (he resigned after forty-five years, i.e. 1942, and stayed as pastor emeritus until his death — Wikipedia and the MPR piece agree). He founded the Northwestern Bible Training School in 1902 (about 1,200 students at his death), the World Christian Fundamentals Association in 1919, and the Anti-Evolution League of Minnesota in 1923; his organization recruited William Jennings Bryan as lead prosecutor at the 1925 Scopes trial. In the 1930s he had drifted from a youthful leftism to bitter anti-communism and opposition to the New Deal, promoted the Protocols of the Elders of Zion, and (per Wikipedia) supported the Silver Legion and preached sermons endorsing the Nazis. Billy Graham conducted his funeral. The odd detail: The man who founded a training school for ministers and started the anti-evolution fight that produced the Scopes trial also drew on the Protocols in his sermons, in the same city as the Silver Legion chapter.",
