      "summary": "The 8th Illinois Infantry, a Black militia regiment founded in the 1870s and reformed in 1898, was the only regiment in the country commanded by Black officers. In the real record it was mustered on 25 July 1917 as the 370th U.S. Infantry, fought under French command in 1918 — 21 Distinguished Service Crosses, 68 Croix de Guerre, the Germans' \"Schwarze Teufel\" in the Argonne — came home on the SS France, demobilised at Camp Grant on 11 March 1919, and was reorganised as the 8th Infantry (federally recognised 25 August 1921; converted to the 184th Field Artillery on 6 October 1940). The Victory Monument (architect John A. Nyden, sculptor Leonard Crunelle) went up in 1927 with three bronze panels — a draped Black mother with a victory branch, a helmeted woman with the battle names, a bare-chested soldier of the 370th — and 137 names of the dead; the doughboy on top was added in 1936. A Memorial Day ceremony is still held there. The odd detail: The doughboy was added to the monument in 1936, nine years after the shaft — the Black Belt kept building its war memorial through the Depression.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the real record. • from memory — the regiment was called up for the Punitive Expedition period; UNVERIFIED this session",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — What the Eighth brought back from Veracruz — a relic, a cult, a debt to the Golden Circle's Havana map. The armory's basement is where it is kept.",
