      "summary": "In spring 1898 Sheboygan elected Fred C. Haack and August L. Mohr as aldermen — \"the first two Social Democratic Party candidates to be elected to public office in the United States\"; Haack later became a Socialist alderman in Milwaukee and in 1932 was recognised as \"the first Socialist officeholder in America.\" Sheboygan's early-20th-century newcomers included Orthodox Greeks, Catholic Slavs and Lithuanians on top of the Germans (post-1848), Dutch Protestants and Irish. The city's two industrial families were linked by marriage: Walter J. Kohler Sr. (governor 1929–31) was the son of John Michael Kohler II and Lillie Vollrath, and a grandson of Jacob Vollrath; a Vollrath became president of the Vollrath Company in 1932 after his father's death with Kohler as board chairman, and the firm began replacing enamelware with stainless steel by the late 1930s (19 salesmen in 1938). The odd detail: The town that elected America's first Socialists (1898) is the same town that, thirty-six years later, sent the Guard against strikers at its richest family's factory.",
