      "summary": "The Danville Branch, established in 1898 as the eighth of thirteen branches, was championed by Speaker Joseph Gurney Cannon — \"Father of the Home\" — who had lived in Danville since 1876. In its first decade it housed as many as 4,000 veterans; it was a planned community with wards, a chapel, library, theatre and recreational buildings. After the First World War the Home was folded into the Veterans Administration hospital system; the campus went on the National Register in 1992. Danville had a coal history from the 1850s to the 1940s (some of the first open-pit mining), and Cannon — the powerful Speaker who ran the House — died in 1926. The odd detail: The Speaker of the House credited as \"Father of the Home\" had lived in Danville since 1876, and the home he brought there housed up to 4,000 old soldiers within its first decade.",
