      "summary": "Overton (born 21 March 1865, or 18 March 1864 by another source) founded the Hygienic Manufacturing Company in 1898; its \"High Brown Face Powder\" was the first commercial success in cosmetics for Black women. He built a conglomerate — the Overton Hygienic Company, the Douglass National Bank (1923, the second nationally chartered Black-owned bank in the United States), Victory Life Insurance, the Great Northern Realty Company, Half-Century Magazine (1916) and the Chicago Bee newspaper (1925), which had a woman managing editor, Olive M. Diggs. In 1927 he received both the NAACP's Spingarn Medal and the first Harmon gold medal for business. The Depression broke the bank and cost him control of Victory Life (from memory: the Douglass National Bank closed in 1932 — UNVERIFIED this session). He died 2 July 1946, aged 81. The odd detail: The Bee is the paper whose theatre editor, James J. Gentry, coined the name \"Bronzeville\" in 1930 — a cosmetics fortune named the neighbourhood.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High; the bank's failure date and the loss of Victory Life are UNVERIFIED this session. • from memory: the Douglass National Bank closed in 1932 — UNVERIFIED this session",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Two Black banks on State Street failed within two years; the \"Old Settler\" elite who ran them lost everything to the newcomers' crash. The bitterness is real and specific — a good root for a cult versus population story where the cult is a handful of ruined men.",
