      "summary": "The remains believed to be Christopher Columbus's had been brought from Santo Domingo in December 1795 when Spain ceded that half of Hispaniola to France, and lay in Havana's coral-stone Baroque cathedral (built by the Jesuits 1748–77, consecrated 1782) from 1796. When Spain lost Cuba, the box was taken out of its niche on 26 September 1898, loaded aboard the cruiser Conde de Venadito on 13 December 1898 [both 1898 dates UNVERIFIED — V_M03: not on the Wikipedia pages read, which give the 20 December 1795 exhumation at Santo Domingo, the 1795–96 move to Havana and \"1899\" for Seville], and reached Seville in January 1899; the Seville tomb (from 1902) says, in effect, that when Cuba \"emancipated from Mother Spain\" Seville received the deposit of Columbus's remains. Santo Domingo, for its part, had announced in 1877 the discovery of a lead box inscribed with Columbus's name and has always claimed the real bones never left. In October 2024 University of Granada researchers said DNA confirms the Seville bones are his. The odd detail: the empire left Havana the way a family leaves a house it has sold — taking its dead with it — three months before the Americans formally took over on 1 January 1899.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the dates of the transfer (26 Sep / 13 Dec 1898 via search summary of the latinamericanstudies.org page); the 1877 Santo Domingo claim is standard but I did not read its detail this session — Medium. • When Spain lost Cuba, the box was taken out of its niche on 26 September 1898, loaded aboard the cruiser Conde de Venadito on 13 December 1898 [both 1898 dates UNVERIFIED — V_M03: not on the Wikipedia pages read, which give the 20 December 1795 exhumation at Santo Domingo, the 1795–96 move to …",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — two sets of Columbus's bones in the Caribbean, and an empty niche in Havana's cathedral since 1898 — the IHS would want to know what the Spanish took so carefully out of the centre of the Golden Circle's map on the eve of the American flag, and what, if anything, they left in its place.",
