      "summary": "Founded 1892 by Carpatho-Rusyn and Galician immigrants (first called St. Nicholas, then St. Vladimir, worshipping at 20 N. Peoria). Louis Sullivan — one of only two churches he designed — built it in a Russian village style: an octagon-within-a-square, Louis Millet stencils, an iconostasis imported from Russia. Tsar Nicholas II gave $4,000 personally and $15,000 more was raised in Russia (total ~$30,000); Sullivan halved his fee, counting the rest as a gift, because of his friendship with Charles Crane and the Imperial consul Baron Schlippenbach. Cornerstone 31 March 1902; consecrated 29 March 1903, the Fourth Sunday of Great Lent, by Bishop Tikhon (later Patriarch of Moscow, 1917). The rector Fr. John Kochurov (1895–1907) went home and was shot in 1917, the first priest-martyr of the revolution. It became a cathedral in 1923 when its rector Theodore Pashkovsky was made bishop. The odd detail: From 1949 to 2004 the rectory held the Tikhvin icon of the Virgin — said to be painted by St. Luke — brought out only for feasts. (Post-period; useful as a precedent.)",
