      "summary": "The Californian Edward L. Doheny (with his partner Charles Canfield) bought into the jungle country west of Tampico in 1900 and drilled Mexico's first commercial oil wells at El Ébano in 1901 under his Mexican Petroleum Company. The historian Jonathan C. Brown counts three thousand Mexican labourers clearing jungle at El Ébano and a 400-foot railroad siding in place by 1902; Doheny fired an American foreman for \"impatience\" toward the Mexican crews. In 1905 he bought land near Tuxpan and formed the Huasteca Petroleum Company to hold it; in September 1910 his men brought in Juan Casiano No. 7 in the Golden Lane, the Huasteca company's mainstay for nine years. Everything later — Cerro Azul No. 4 (M01), Pan American Petroleum (sold to Standard Oil of Indiana, April 1925), Teapot Dome — grows from this siding. The odd detail: the Mexican oil age began not at the port but thirty-five miles inland in the bush, where Doheny needed a small army of machete men before he could drill a single hole.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (1901, El Ébano, Huasteca 1905, Casiano Sept 1910); the 3,000 labourers are Brown's figure, read this session.",
