      "summary": "\"Thousands of Yaquis walked from Sonora to Arizona\" as refugees from Díaz's deportations and the Revolution, founding Pascua (Tucson), Barrio Libre, Marana and Guadalupe; Old Pascua was set on land a benefactor gave. The Lenten cycle (Waehma) runs from Ash Wednesday to Easter Sunday: the Fariseos / Chapayekas (leather-helmet masks with painted faces), an all-night Deer Dance by the maso (little-brother deer) with pascolas and matachines, and on Holy Saturday a battle in which flowers are thrown to drive off the masked Fariseos. Dancers make their promise (a manda) in dreams. Five societies carry out the 40 days. The NPS notes photography, video, audio recording and alcohol are strictly prohibited at Yoeme ceremonies — respect that in any table use. The odd detail: the Yaqui reached Arizona by walking north in a war and took their Lent with them; the same ceremony is held on both sides of what was the line.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the outline; ceremonial detail from search summaries (Medium). Report, don't reproduce: the ceremonies are living religion.",
