      "places": [
        {
          "name": "2131–2133 S. Dearborn Street",
          "address": "2131–2133 S. Dearborn Street",
          "geography": "in the Levee (roughly 18th to 22nd, State to Clark (unverified — V2_R16)), South Loop",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=2131%E2%80%932133%20S.%20Dearborn%20Street%2C%20in%20the%20Levee%20%29%2C%20South%20Loop"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Ada and Minna Everleigh put a $35,000 inheritance into the most expensive brothel in America (February 1900): a $15,000 gold-leafed piano in the Music Room, silk curtains, mirrored ceilings, 15 to 25 servants. On March 3, 1902 Prince Henry of Prussia was entertained there — legend says the champagne-from-a-slipper custom began when a girl's shoe came off. Mayor Carter Harrison Jr. closed the Club on October 24, 1911 after the Vice Commission counted nearly 600 brothels in the city; the sisters left with about a million dollars and lived out their lives in New York under another name. (The papers called Marshall Field Jr.'s fatal gunshot of November 22, 1905 an accident at home; Wikipedia says there is \"some evidence\" he was shot by a prostitute at the Club, and a rival madam accused the sisters.) The Levee's aldermen, \"Bathhouse\" John Coughlin and \"Hinky Dink\" Kenna, outlived the district; their successors Colosimo, Torrio and Capone inherited its cafés (the Four Deuces at 2222 S. Wabash (unverified — V2_R16) is the next generation's address — R01's lane). The odd detail: A Prussian prince in a Chicago brothel in 1902; in the setting, the same prince's country won the war.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. • roughly 18th to 22nd, State to Clark (unverified — V2_R16) • the Four Deuces at 2222 S. Wabash (unverified — V2_R16) is the next generation's address — R01's lane",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Everleigh guest book is the best list of the city's ruling men ever compiled — where is it in 1936, and who wants it?",
