      "summary": "Francisco Madero, the wealthy Coahuila landowner whose Plan of San Luis Potosí called the Revolution for 6 p.m. on 20 November 1910, was a devoted Spiritist. As a student in Paris he made a pilgrimage to the tomb of Allan Kardec, Spiritism's founder. From 1901 he believed the spirit of his brother Raúl, dead at age four (Mexico News Daily says three), was communicating with him, urging charity and self-discipline — and, after political violence in 1903, urging him to \"aspire to do good for your fellow citizens.\" He spent 1908 writing The Presidential Succession of 1910 (published January 1909) at the direction, he believed, of spirits — \"now including that of Benito Juárez himself.\" The odd detail: the man who toppled a 34-year dictatorship wrote the book that launched him by automatic dictation from a dead child and a dead president.",
