      "summary": "In 1900 Clan na Gael men John Walsh, John Nolan and \"Dynamite\" Luke Dillon set off a dynamite charge that damaged Lock No. 24 of the Welland Canal, the link between Lake Erie and Lake Ontario; they were caught and given life sentences. In 1914 Clan leader John Devoy met the German ambassador Count Bernstorff and his military attaché Franz von Papen; the Clan became \"the largest single financier\" of the 1916 Easter Rising, and German arms were sent aboard the Aud (scuttled after the Royal Navy intercepted her). Von Papen ran sabotage from the embassy — hiring detective Paul Koenig on August 22, 1914, organising the February 1915 Vanceboro bridge bombing — and was expelled December 3, 1915. In April 1916 a U.S. federal grand jury indicted von Papen, Hans Tauscher, Karl Boy-Ed, Constantine Covani and Franz von Rintelen for a plot to blow up the Welland Canal; the charges stood until 1932. The odd detail: the same Great Lakes lock system was targeted twice in sixteen years — once by Irish dynamiters, once by German diplomats who worked with the Irish.",
