      "summary": "Under Díaz, Sonoran governors ran manhunts and sold Yaquis into near-slavery on Yucatán henequen estates — 15,000–60,000 died in the deportations of 1900–1911 (estimates vary); at Mazocoba (1900) several hundred were killed, some choosing suicide over surrender. Yaquis fought in the Revolution, largely for Obregón, and were not paid in land; Obregón built his own estates on Yaqui land (1916–20). On 1 September 1926 some 2,000 Yaquis (TIME's dispatch says over 3,000) stopped former president Obregón's train at Vícam at 4 a.m. and held it 17 hours, to press for prisoners' release and their lands. Calles answered with a campaign of ~12,000–15,000 troops and eight Douglas aircraft bought in Santa Monica, California, bombing the Bacatete; a 2024 scholarly article quotes period sources on \"asphyxiating gases dropped by bombs from airplanes.\" At Cerro del Gallo (April 1927) 415 Yaquis were captured — 26 men, 214 women, 175 children. The odd detail: TIME (27 Sept 1926, \"Yaqui Rampage\") gave the rising's cause as the government's suppression of the Yaquis' Catholic pilgrimage — tying the last Yaqui war to Calles's war on the Church.",
