      "summary": "From 1905 she ran marriage advertisements in Chicago's Norwegian (unverified — V2_R06) papers asking suitors to bring their money and tell no one. Henry Gurholt of Wisconsin, John Moe of Minnesota, Andrew Helgelien of South Dakota (unverified — V2_R06) and others came and did not leave; their trunks — \"more than a dozen\" — stayed in the house. On 28 Apr 1908 the farmhouse burned; in it a headless woman's body and three children. Digging turned up at least eleven more bodies, \"decapitated, the arms removed at the shoulders, the legs severed at the knees.\" Dr. Harry Long said the charred woman weighed about 160 lb to Belle's 325 and was five inches shorter, with manicured nails — \"It is not that of Mrs. Gunness\"; a dentist matched a gold crown to her. Her hired man Ray Lamphere, convicted of arson, said she had staged it with a planted body and walked away. In 1931 a woman named Esther Carlson, arrested in Los Angeles for poisoning, was thought by some to be Gunness; she died before trial (unverified — V_R06). Sightings continued for decades. The odd detail: The dismemberment pattern — head, shoulders, knees — matches, joint for joint, what turned up in Cleveland thirty years later; and no one knows if she died.",
