      "summary": "A French-Canadian parish of the 1880s (the parish's own history says 1880; first pastor Fr. Joseph LeSage); the first stone was blessed on 7 June 1891 and the church dedicated in May 1892. In January 1900 the second pastor, Fr. Cyril A. Poissant, \"brought the first relic of the Saint from Monseigneur Marquis received from the Bishop of the Diocese of Nicolet, Quebec\"; the first novena to St. Anne was held that July, and pilgrims came every year after. In April 1916 a bone relic was obtained from Apt, France, through Bishop Lenfant — \"the largest after the one found in Beaupre Basilica.\" In 1937 Fr. Louis F. DeCelle built a new St. Anne sanctuary, and in May 1938 he added a novena to Our Lady of Sorrows. The 1938 register lists the parish as \"St. Joseph's (French), St. Anne's Shrine,\" with DeCelle as pastor, two assistants and 331 pupils taught by Sisters of St. Joseph from Concordia, Kansas. The odd detail: A saint's bone that left France in April 1916 — in the middle of the war that, in the setting, France went on to lose. (A 1900 Tribune report of a paralysed young woman, Matilda Cunnea, rising from her invalid chair after venerating the relic, with her wheelchair still displayed in the church, appears in a search summary of a Chicago Ancestors page I could not open — UNVERIFIED.)",
