      "summary": "In 1900 there were 68,969 German-born Milwaukeeans, 24.2% of the city; 64,816 (17.3%) in 1910; 39,771 (8.7%) in 1920. When America entered the war, armed \"patriots\" stopped a William Tell at the Pabst Theater (April 1918); the Milwaukee Journal campaigned German out of the public elementary schools, and enrollment in high-school German classes fell from 30,000 in 1916 to 200 in 1919; the Deutscher Club renamed itself the Wisconsin Club. By 1913 there was only one German-language daily, though weeklies abounded; the Germania-Herold urged German Americans in April 1917 to \"accept new duties.\" The Pabst Theater ended German-language productions completely in 1931. In the 1930s a small branch of the pro-Nazi Volksbund operated in the city and built a short-lived summer camp in Grafton in 1937. The odd detail: The single statistic that the setting overturns: 30,000 pupils enrolled in high-school German classes in Milwaukee in 1916.",
