      "summary": "Sonora produced four Mexican presidents — Adolfo de la Huerta, Álvaro Obregón, Plutarco Elías Calles and Abelardo L. Rodríguez — plus Maytorena, Benjamín G. Hill and others. Wikipedia points at what shaped them: Sonora's isolation from central Mexico (\"no direct railway connection to Mexico City\") but close ties north to the United States, and large-scale export agriculture. Guaymas had ~10,000 people by 1890, was where the Chinese arrived first, and was where Yaqui deportees were embarked. Hermosillo's state penitentiary held Yaqui prisoners. Ciudad Obregón got its name on 28 July 1928 (previously Cajeme). The odd detail: the state that made the Revolution's winners had no rail line to the capital but two to Arizona, and all four of its presidents were shaped by a border they had crossed.",
