      "summary": "Helen Morgan (born Helen Riggin/Riggins, 2 August 1900, Danville) became the archetypal torch singer, first singing in Chicago's Prohibition-era speakeasies; her trademark pose — sitting on top of the piano — dates from her 1925 performances at Billy Rose's Backstage Club. She was Julie LaVerne in the 1927 Broadway Show Boat (and in the 1929 and 1936 films and a 1932 revival) and starred in Sweet Adeline (1929–31). She was arrested at her own club, Chez Morgan, on 30 December 1927 for liquor violations (charges later dropped). She struggled with alcoholism and died in Chicago on 8 October 1941, aged 41, of cirrhosis; she had collapsed on stage while performing in George White's Scandals of 1942. The odd detail: The Depression's most famous \"sings sadly on top of a piano\" singer came from a Vermilion County coal town and died at 41 after collapsing on stage.",
