      "summary": "Henrietta Knapp Wriedt (b. Oswego, NY, 1862) was a paid \"direct voice\" medium whose spirit guide was \"John Sharp,\" supposedly an 18th-century Glaswegian; voices spoke through a trumpet in the dark. W. T. Stead and Vice-Admiral William Usborne Moore (The Voices, 1913) vouched for her; W. B. Yeats sat with her and doubted. In Norway, physicist Kristian Birkeland switched on the light mid-séance and snatched her trumpets: the \"spirit\" noises were chemical explosions of potassium and water or lycopodium powder, and the voices were her own through a hidden telescoping aluminium tube. She kept working and died in Detroit in 1942. The odd detail: Exposed by the man who explained the aurora — and it made no difference; Detroit kept booking her for thirty more years.",
