      "summary": "Isadore Blumenfeld, born 8 September 1900 at Râmnicu Sărat, Romania, was brought to North Minneapolis in 1902 and grew up in its Jewish enclave. First arrested in 1920 in a brothel; 1924 arrested for the death of taxi driver Charles Goldberg outside the Vienna Cafe on Nicollet (MNopedia: he admitted the shooting, claimed it was an accident and was never tried — Blumenfeld would be buried near Goldberg in 1981); 1928 arrested for shooting two police officers at the Cotton Club (not charged). Bootlegging, then gambling and labour racketeering, then liquor licensing through shell companies. 1933: charged in New Orleans with rum smuggling; tried in Oklahoma for laundering ransom from the Urschel kidnapping — acquitted. 1934: convicted of running a still, served a year in the Hennepin County workhouse. Walter Liggett was murdered in December 1935; Blumenfeld was tried and acquitted on 19 February 1936 despite identification by Liggett's widow. He disliked \"Kid Cann\" (no one knows its origin) and used \"Dr. Ferguson\" or \"Fergie.\" He died at Mount Sinai Hospital, Minneapolis, on 24 June 1981 per MNopedia (Wikipedia says 21 June; the sources differ). The odd detail: The Twin Cities' great Jewish mob boss was buried in a Jewish cemetery near the taxi driver he was once arrested for killing.",
