      "summary": "The Compañía Fundidora de Fierro y Acero de Monterrey was constituted on 5 May 1900, the first integrated iron-and-steel enterprise in Latin America. Vicente Ferrara (the driving spirit), Eugenio Kelly (a US banker), León Signoret (French) and Antonio Basagoiti (Spanish) formalised it; the first board also seated Isaac Garza, José A. Muguerza, Francisco G. Sada, Valentín Rivero y Gajá, Adolfo Zambrano and an Ernesto Madero, with local shareholders in dozens. Wikipedia lists the Milmo, Madero and Garza-Sada families and the Guggenheims among the capital. It made iron, steel, coke and slag: rails, wire rod, structural steel, train wheels. It ran for 86 years (nationalised 1977, bankrupt 9 May 1986, 7,000 workers at the end). The odd detail: four of the men who founded the brewery ten years earlier (see next entry) were on the steelworks' founding roster — beer money built the steel town.",
