      "summary": "Acting on the Commission's result, Major William C. Gorgas, Havana's chief sanitary officer under Wood, formed a \"Stegomyia brigade\" and an \"Anopheles brigade\". Inspectors went house to house hunting stagnant water; householders were fined when larvae were found on their premises (unverified — V_M03: fines and the two \"brigades\" are not on the EBSCO or Lind pages read); water was treated, cisterns screened, sick houses fumigated and quarantined. Between 1890 and 1900 an average of 462 people a year had died of yellow fever in Havana; 12 died in January–February 1901, and the last death of the occupation was on 28 September 1901. October 1901 was the first October in Havana's recorded history without a case (unverified — V_M03 as worded; consistent with the 28 September last death, but no page read says it). Gorgas took the method to the Panama Canal. The odd detail: an occupying army with the right to enter every house in the city and search it — for water. The first great American intrusion into Havana's private rooms was a mosquito hunt.",
