      "summary": "Four brothers — Walter, Edward, John and Henry Fromm — read in 1901 that silver-fox pelts sold for $1,200 in London and formed \"the Company.\" In 1904 they began growing ginseng, learning it from a neighbour, Reinhold Dietsch; in 1913 they raised their first silver-fox pup and carved a marker on Rib Mountain. In 1915 half an acre of ginseng brought $3,500 and they mortgaged the family farm for $6,500 to buy three breeding foxes; by 1919 ginseng sold $40,000 a year (unverified — V_R42: the Wikipedia article read gives 3 acres of ginseng by 1915 and $45,000–$115,000 a year through the 1920s, not the $3,500 or $40,000 figures). By 1929 they were the world's largest producers of both. A 13-row semi-automated ginseng planter was their own design; they funded a University of Minnesota vet (Dr. Robert G. Green) whose work produced fox encephalitis and distemper vaccines. In 1935 they held fur auctions at their own farm instead of sending pelts to New York, London or Paris; at the height of the Depression (1937) the firm did about $10 million of business with 180+ employees, 500 acres of ginseng and 36,000 silver foxes (search-result summary of the Wikipedia/Wisconsin 101 text) (unverified — V_R42: not on the Wikipedia page; wi101 page unreachable). They sat on their ginseng reserve through the Depression. The odd detail: Four farm brothers in the Wisconsin hills ran the world's largest silver fox herd and its largest ginseng garden, and the price of one had been set in London.",
