      "summary": "On 12 June 1901 a language mix-up over a horse (yegua vs caballo) led Deputy Boone Choate to misunderstand Gregorio Cortez; Sheriff W. T. Morris shot at Cortez's brother Romaldo and Cortez killed him \"in self-defense.\" Cortez then evaded hundreds of pursuers for about ten days — \"nearly 400 miles on horseback and more than 100 miles on foot\" — until his capture on 22 June. He was convicted (of killing another sheriff, Robert Glover, at the Battle of Belmont — a charge many questioned), sentenced to life in 1905, and pardoned by Gov. Oscar Colquitt in 1913; he died in 1916, aged 40, cause disputed. The corrido made him the border's emblematic outlaw hero; the scholar Américo Paredes documented eleven variants in his 1958 book (With His Pistol in His Hand); a 1982 film (The Ballad of Gregorio Cortez, Edward James Olmos) followed. The odd detail: a folk hero created by a mistranslation of two Spanish words for a horse.",
