      "summary": "Maass, a 25-year-old contract nurse from New Jersey, volunteered for the continuing yellow-fever inoculation experiments in Havana (run after Reed's board by the Cuban physician Juan Guiteras, not named on the page I read — UNVERIFIED). Volunteers were paid $100, and another $100 if they fell ill. She was bitten by infected mosquitoes repeatedly between March and August 1901 and died on 24 August 1901. Her mother called it \"little short of murder.\" Her death ended human yellow-fever experiments. She was buried in the Colón Cemetery with military honours and moved to Fairmount Cemetery, Newark, on 20 February 1902. The odd detail: the price of a human trial in American Havana was a hundred dollars, doubled if the fever took.",
