      "id": "R23-023",
      "title": "The German-American National Alliance — the hyphen's lobby",
      "date_start": "1901-10-06",
      "date_end": "1918",
      "date_label": "6 October 1901 to 1918",
      "category": "Imperial Germany in Chicago and the Midwest, 1871–1917: the template for a victor's presence in the 1930s",
      "tags": [
        "Imperial",
        "Germany",
        "German-American National Alliance",
        "hyphen",
        "BENDS"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Charles J. Hexamer",
        "Max Eberhardt",
        "Ferdinand Walther",
        "Charles Christmann"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "founded Philadelphia",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=founded%20Philadelphia%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "Chicago branch meetings at the Northside Turner Hall",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "the Auditorium, the Bush Temple",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Chicago%20branch%20meetings%20at%20the%20Northside%20Turner%20Hall%2C%20the%20Auditorium%2C%20the%20Bush%20Temple"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Founded 6 Oct 1901 in Philadelphia with Dr. Charles J. Hexamer as president (to 1917), the Alliance claimed about 2.5 million members in 45 states by 1916 (inflated by overlapping society memberships). Its programme: German-language teaching, the German American Historical Society (1901), Americana Germanica, resistance to prohibition (a Dec 1903 petition against increased enforcement), German Day (Chicago's first, 1906 — above). Its Chicago branch was led by Dr. Max Eberhardt (1906) and Ferdinand Walther (1914). On 11 Aug 1914 (reported 12 Aug) a special meeting at the Northside Turner Hall voted relief \"for the wounded and to assist the widows and orphans in the old fatherland\" and a resolution — German-Americans \"approximately one-quarter of the U.S. population\" — demanding that every Chicago editor \"impartially convey the views of these people\"; copies went to every newspaper in the city. Through 1914–17 it fought for an arms embargo with Richard Bartholdt's American Independence Union; its war-relief work drew brewers' money and, with it, Senate scrutiny. It dissolved itself in April 1918; Congress revoked its charter in July 1918 and Wilson signed on 31 Aug 1918. A successor, the German American Citizens League, was headquartered in Chicago. The odd detail: \"one-quarter of the U.S. population\" — the Alliance's own arithmetic in August 1914; and the German American Citizens League, which considered itself the Alliance's successor, was headquartered in Chicago, not Philadelphia.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the Czech paper's charge — \"an exact directory of all German nationals, their businesses, their financial status\" — is the Alliance's Chicago card file. Whoever keeps it in 1936 knows German Chicago better than the census.",
