      "summary": "In 1902 the International Harvester Company was formed from the five largest US harvester makers (the deal is described as J. P. Morgan's — via search). In October 1902 Molina's export house, Olegario Molina y Cía, signed a secret contract with it: per the Hispanic American Historical Review study of the Molinas, the firm was to \"employ every effort\" to depress the price of sisal fibre and pay only the prices Harvester dictated, with 10,000 bales placed at its disposal to sell low (unverified — V_M15: a Spanish account of the contract text reads 100 mil pacas (100,000), and the price-depressing effort was to begin Monday 27 October 1902). Hulme summarises the result: Harvester bought effectively the entire crop at a price it could fix, having supplied the credit that built the industry. Molina had taken office as governor on 1 February 1902 (term to 27 March 1907); he was then Díaz's Secretary of Development from 21 March 1907 until his resignation on 24 March 1911, as the Revolution began; he stayed in Mérida until 1914, then fled to Paris and Havana. The odd detail: the contract was signed the same year Molina became governor — the state's chief executive was also the trust's Yucatán buyer.",
