      "summary": "Sylbaris (also given as Louis-Auguste Cyparis) was arrested on 2 May 1902 for wounding a man with a cutlass in a drunken quarrel over money (unverified — V_M10: Wikipedia says he stabbed a man \"in either a bar fight or a street brawl (sources vary)\"), and put in solitary on 7 May (for eight days, for an escape attempt) for trying to escape. The cell was a bomb-proof magazine, partly underground, with only a narrow grating in the door — facing away from the mountain. The surge came through the grating and burned his hands, arms, legs and back; his clothes did not catch. Rescuers from Morne-Rouge heard him screaming on 11 May, three days later, and dug him out. He was pardoned and toured with Barnum & Bailey as \"the man who lived through Doomsday\". He died in Panama in 1929, having worked on the canal. The odd detail: the city's one famous survivor lived because he was being punished — and died in the American Canal Zone's shadow.",
