      "summary": "Kaiser Wilhelm II sent his brother, Admiral Prince Heinrich, on a goodwill tour of the United States in February–March 1902 (Freedom of the City of New York 25 Feb; Harvard LL.D. 6 March; Philadelphia 10 March), run on a special train by the Pennsylvania Railroad, whose printed itinerary survives. Chicago's turn came on the evening of Monday 3 March 1902 (the Abendpost of 4 March reports \"last night\"): arrival at Union Depot 6:30 p.m., \"reception by the Mayor, the German Consul, and a Reception Committee,\" a drive with military escort to the Auditorium, dinner at 7, the choral festival at the Armory at 9, the ball at 10:30 with supper after. Mayor Carter Harrison Jr., A. J. Eddy, Consul Dr. Walther Wever and the council's reception committee boarded the Prince's private car; Harrison said \"I bid you Chicago's welcome!\" and the Prince said \"Thank you.\" The streets from the depot to the Auditorium were packed; about 3,000 members of the German clubs marched with torches behind an honorary cavalry squadron and several bands. At the First Regiment Armory the Prince \"invoked three cheers for his Imperial brother,\" Thies J. Lefens gave the German-language address, and the German and American singing societies sang. A banquet for 160 followed at the Auditorium Hotel with Harrison as toastmaster and the Prince replying \"in English\"; the night ended with a festival ball — \"the most elegant affair this city had ever seen\" — in which the Prince led the grand march with Mrs. Carter Harrison. The Abendpost (3 March) framed the whole tour as the Kaiser's answer to \"British incitements\" in the American press; Chicago's Germans, who had held a protest meeting against that press, claimed they \"started the ball rolling\" for the welcome. The odd detail: the Kaiser's brother opened the ball with the mayor's wife, and the German clubs marched by torchlight through the Loop — in 1902 that was civic pride, not a security problem.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
