      "summary": "After weeks of ash, tremors and warnings, Pelée erupted at about 8:00 a.m. on 8 May 1902 (Ascension Day); a pyroclastic surge reached the city at 8:02 and killed some 28,000–29,000 people in minutes — the deadliest eruption of the twentieth century. An election was due and the city had not been evacuated: Governor Louis Mouttet and his wife stayed and died. Ships in the roadstead burned — the Canadian liner Roraima was reduced to a burning wreck and the cable ship Grappler sank. People had taken the Soufrière's eruption on St Vincent the day before (Part E) as a sign that pressure had been relieved. On 20 May a second blast killed some 2,000 rescuers and salvagers. From October a lava spine — the \"Tower of Pelée\" — grew up to 15 m a day before collapsing in March 1903. The odd detail: the Soufrière across the channel had seemed to prove the danger past, and the governor stayed to show it (the often-told claim that the town paper and an official commission publicly declared the mountain safe is UNVERIFIED this session).",
