      "summary": "Swampland sold off after 1851 to fund schools, East Chicago was a city without streets or utilities in the 1890s (3,411 people in 1900). Inland Steel arrived in 1903; capacity grew from 60,000 tons to a million by 1917 and population passed 24,000 by 1910. The Indiana Harbor Ship Canal linked the lake to the Grand Calumet by 1907. In the war-labour shortage several thousand Mexicans came to the mills; in 1932 alone 1,800 were deported (R10 has the repatriation). Nicknames: \"Arsenal of America\", \"Workshop of America\"; 70+ nationalities. (East Chicago's police and Sgt Martin Zarkovich, and Officer O'Malley's death, are in R02.) The odd detail: A city of seventy nationalities that shipped out 1,800 of its own residents in a single year.",
