      "summary": "Allan Kardec's writings reached the island in the mid-19th century through contraband; by 1856 the newspaper La Guirnalda mentioned spiritism and spirit-evocation among the upper class. Colonial Spanish law banned spiritist publications and Catholic priests refused services to spiritists and their families. Two variants formed: an educated professional one (\"scientific\" mesa blanca, seated around a white-linen-covered table, mostly urban and middle-class, studying Kardec) and a popular rural one. A Federación de Espiritistas de Puerto Rico was founded in 1903 with Doña Guillermina Massanet Fermaint as honorary president, building libraries and newspapers. By the 1920s Kardec's The Gospel According to Spiritism was outselling the Bible on the island; the Librería Campos in San Juan reportedly sold over 30,000 copies in 1935. (Compare the Mexican President Madero, the Revolution's spiritist, in M01.) The odd detail: the popular book of a Frenchman's séances outsold the Bible in an American territory whose new masters were Protestants and Catholics.",
