      "summary": "Benjamin Purnell's \"Seventh Messenger\" colony demanded celibacy, vegetarianism, uncut hair and beards and common property. After a 1923 grand jury, Purnell was arrested in Nov 1926 on statutory rape charges; the trial (religious fraud, \"gross immoralities,\" perjury) ran to 15,000+ pages and was covered in the US, Canada, Britain and Australia; the court ordered a receivership (reversed by the Michigan Supreme Court). Purnell died 16 Dec 1927 of diabetes and TB; his followers waited three days for the prophesied resurrection before burying him on 19 Dec. In 1930 the colony split between Judge H. T. Dewhirst and Purnell's widow Mary: 217 went with Mary (farmers and ballplayers) to \"Mary's City of David\"; Dewhirst kept the musicians and park staff. The bearded barnstorming baseball teams (Grover Cleveland Alexander, Satchel Paige, Babe Didrikson played with them) spawned unlicensed bearded imitators; Eden Springs had a zoo and miniature trains; the colony's jazz bands signed with Columbia. Mary's side later ran an Orthodox-Jewish vacation resort, Paradise Park, with a synagogue. The odd detail: They sat three days with the body in mid-December 1927 waiting for it to rise.",
