      "summary": "Vito Guardalabene, from Santa Flavia, Sicily (arrived 1903, naturalised 1911), was \"the king\" of the Third Ward by about 1915 and died in his bed on 6 Feb 1921. His son Giovanni Battista \"Peter\" Guardalabene ran things until 1924, then handed over to a relative, Giuseppe \"Big Joe\" D'Amato, who died of pneumonia at 41 on 28 March 1927. Joseph Vallone (from Prizzi, arrived 1907), who co-owned a wholesale grocery that sold moonshiners their sugar and yeast, ran the family from 1927 to 1949; in the early 1930s he was one of 45 defendants in a federal bootlegging indictment. When the Commission was formed in 1931 it ruled that Milwaukee \"would remain under the control of and answer directly to the Chicago Outfit.\" Vallone groomed Sam Ferrara; Frank Balistrieri (b. 27 May 1918) was a boy in the Third Ward through the decade and married the daughter of the later boss John Alioto. The odd detail: The boss of Milwaukee in the 1930s was a wholesale grocer — the Depression's moonshine ran on his sugar.",
