      "summary": "Julius Arthur Nieuwland (1878–1936), Belgian-born Holy Cross priest, was professor of botany (1904–18) and organic chemistry (1918–36) at Notre Dame. His 1904 doctoral thesis on acetylene contained a paragraph on the reaction of acetylene with arsenic trichloride, producing a poisonous product with a \"nauseating and penetrating odor\" that briefly hospitalised him. In 1918, when the U.S. chemical-weapons programme wanted a new gas, Winford Lee Lewis (aided by James Conant) refined it into an oily yellow liquid — \"Lewisite,\" the \"dew of death\"; by November 1918, 150 tons were ready to ship from Ohio when the armistice came, and it was dumped in the Atlantic. In 1920 Nieuwland showed acetylene could be polymerised to divinylacetylene, a rubber-like material; his work with DuPont underlies neoprene. He considered Lewisite humane: \"If a man goes to the hospital suffering from gas, he is as useless as if he were dead.\" (unverified — V_R41: the cited Notre Dame Magazine page carries a different Nieuwland line, \"Today the primary aim in war is not to kill but to incapacitate\"; this wording was not found on it) He died of a heart attack in 1936 (search summary: 11 June) while visiting the Catholic University lab where he first made it. The odd detail: A priest-botanist's one accidental paragraph in a thesis became the most feared poison of 1918, and he died on a visit to the room where he had first made it.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the Lewisite chain (Notre Dame Magazine); DuPont/neoprene and the June date from search summaries. • He considered Lewisite humane: \"If a man goes to the hospital suffering from gas, he is as useless as if he were dead.\" (unverified — V_R41: the cited Notre Dame Magazine page carries a different Nieuwland line, \"Today the primary aim in war is not to kill but to incapacitate\"; this wording was not found on it) He died of a heart attack in 1936 (search summary: 11 June) while visiting the Catholic University lab where he first made it.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A Holy Cross priest at a Midwest Catholic university (near IHS's Jesuit orbit) whose one paragraph could kill a city; he died in June 1936, months before the alpha's autumn. Who wants the notebook?",
