      "summary": "The city's great amusement park (July 2, 1904 – Oct 3, 1967; 74 acres). The Bobs (1924, Prior & Church) was the decade's terrifying wooden coaster; Aladdin's Castle the funhouse; the Pair-O-Chutes (1937, first called \"Eye-Full\") a free-fall parachute drop; the \"African Dip\" dunk tank put Black men in a cage for white crowds to sink until Mike Royko and the NAACP forced it out in the 1950s. Legend of the 1967 closing as crime-driven is thin; white flight did it. The five-abreast carousel survives at Six Flags Over Georgia. The odd detail: In 1937 Chicagoans paid to be dropped from a tower under a parachute — a fair-era thrill turned permanent.",
