      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Incheon",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(Jemulpo) → Salina Cruz (Oaxaca) → across the Isthmus by rail → Progreso → 32 henequen haciendas",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Incheon%20%20%E2%86%92%20Salina%20Cruz%20%20%E2%86%92%20across%20the%20Isthmus%20by%20rail%20%E2%86%92%20Progreso%20%E2%86%92%2032%20henequen%20haciendas%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "In 1905, 1,033 Koreans signed four-year (some sources: five-year) labour contracts and sailed on the British steamer Ilford. They landed at Salina Cruz, crossed the Isthmus of Tehuantepec (see 35 — presumably by the railway then being rebuilt), took a boat from the Gulf side and arrived at Progreso in May 1905 (the Mexico News Daily article gives 14 May 1905 for the landing at Progreso; the \"4 May\" date is unsupported). Sold into the hacienda system, they were worked as the Maya were. In 1910 Korea was annexed by Japan, leaving them stateless. They founded schools (Sung-Mu, 1910; Hae-Dong, 1917, San Sebastián, the first recognised by Mexico's education ministry) and — when the hacienda economy collapsed — about 300 left for Cuba in 1921 to cut sugar. Their descendants (Aenikkaeng in the community's word) are in Mérida, with a monument inscribed with all 1,033 names. The odd detail: they were part of Turner's count of slaves (about \"3,000 Koreans\") four years after they landed; and they crossed the Isthmus two years before the rebuilt Tehuantepec railway formally reopened (1907).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the 1,033, the ship, 1910 and the 1921 Cuba move; Medium on the day-level dates.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a Korean sugar-cutter in 1930s occupied Havana who was born a henequen slave and speaks Maya; the Circle's map would treat such a person as invisible, and that is the point.",
