      "summary": "Frances Densmore (1867–1957) began transcribing Ojibwe music in 1905 at Grand Portage and Grand Marais and over the next five years recorded nearly five hundred songs, chiefly from the White Earth, Leech Lake, Red Lake and Lac du Flambeau reservations, for the Smithsonian's Bureau of American Ethnology (more than 2,500 songs on wax cylinders in her lifetime; last recordings, of Seminoles, in 1954 at age 87). Her first major book, Chippewa Music, BAE Bulletin 45 (1910), documented 50 songs. She realized Ojibwe song was sacred and often inappropriate to record — yet in the early years she did record some Midéwiwin (\"Grand Medicine Society\") songs. The Library of Congress's 1907–1910 Chippewa collection has 30 songs from 16 singers in six genres: dream songs, war songs, miscellaneous songs, songs used to treat the sick, Mide songs and love songs. The odd detail: The Ojibwe songs of the Mide — a religious society whose rituals were closed to outsiders — survive as cylinders because a woman from Red Wing asked, and some singers agreed.",
