      "summary": "A spiritualist and fortune-teller (who switched to \"electro-therapy\" when the city changed the law), Lindloff buried her husband Julius Graunke (1905, \"sunstroke\"), daughter Frieda (1908, \"typhoid\"), second husband William Lindloff (1910, \"heart trouble\"), daughter Alma (1911, \"heart trouble\") and son Arthur (1912, \"pancreatic trouble\"), plus, police suspected, boarders and neighbours — arsenic (a neighbour claimed she tested it on dogs first; Selzer calls that a stock trope of poisoner stories). Convicted in 1912 of Arthur's murder; 25 years; she died of cancer in 1914 with her appeal pending. Coroner Peter Hoffman posed as a medium and told her he could see the spirits accusing her; she answered that the spirits stood to her left and said she was innocent. She told the court the spirits had promised acquittal. The odd detail: Coroner Hoffman's séance interrogation — a Cook County coroner pretending to see ghosts to break a witch.",
