      "summary": "John Patrick Looney (1865–1942), born in Ottawa, Illinois, admitted to the bar in 1889, lost a legislative race and blamed the Rock Island Argus. He founded the Rock Island News as a blackmail sheet: he bought the Mirror Lounge in 1905, put a law office on the ground floor and the paper upstairs (unverified — V_R38: Wikipedia names Drost as running \"the Mirror Saloon\"; the 1905 purchase and the layout are not on a page read), and prominent citizens paid \"kill fees\" to keep their secrets out of print. By 1921 he controlled some 150 gambling dens and brothels, and his method included photographing men with his prostitutes. Bel Air had three storeys with three tiers of porches, a third-floor ballroom and an alleged 100-foot tunnel to a stone barn; a 1908 attack on the property, which Looney blamed on rival W. W. Wilmerton, started a private feud in downtown Rock Island. The house fell to its mortgage holder once he went to prison; today it has lost its third floor and porches. The odd detail: The paper's editor, Daniel Drost, said in later life that he could neither read nor write — he edited the blackmail sheet and kept it running while claiming illiteracy.",
