      "summary": "After a 1905 tornado flattened reservation timber, the US Forest Service took a hand in managing the Menominee forest and clear-cut it until 1926, taking about 70% of the saleable timber; when Interior regained control, cutting was cut back to let the forest regrow. In 1934 the tribe sued the Forest Service for the damage (it won $8.5 million in 1952). The Menominee were among the wealthiest tribes because of their timber. Catholic missions were strong: St. Joseph's boarding school at Keshena was run by the Franciscans. (unverified — V_R07) The odd detail: A tornado, then the federal foresters, took more of the forest than the tribe's own mill ever would.",
