      "summary": "Founded 1905 as the South Side's suburban counterpart to the Loop clubs; Marshall & Fox's first Mediterranean Revival clubhouse (1906) was replaced by their grander one in 1916 — ballroom, theatre, dining rooms, stables and a riding ring, a nine-hole golf course, tennis, a bowling green, and a private beach on the lake. Built as a Protestant-only club, it later admitted Irish Catholics; Jews were kept out from the 1930s (in 1967 the club considered opening membership to Jews \"for the first time since the 1930s\" and African Americans \"for the first time ever,\" and declined; members left as the neighbourhood integrated, the club liquidated in 1973 and was sold to the Park District in 1975 for $9.775 million). Garrard B. Winston of 1508 N. State Street was a member here as well as at the Saddle and Cycle. Its horse shows and its beach were the South Side's society summer. The odd detail: The decade this file covers is the decade a Chicago club that had admitted Jews decided to stop — the period's own social categories, hardening as the money got scarce.",
