      "summary": "Friedrich Nottebohm (b. Hamburg, 1881) moved to Guatemala in 1905 and built the family business (Nottebohm Hermanos — a brother house of the Hamburg Nottebohms above). In October 1939, weeks into the Second World War, he obtained Liechtenstein citizenship. When Guatemala declared war on Germany it treated him as an enemy alien anyway: he was arrested in 1943, interned in the United States, and his property confiscated. Liechtenstein sued; in 1955 the International Court of Justice ruled against him, founding the doctrine of \"effective nationality\" — a nationality needs \"a meaningful connection\" to the state; his was bought \"to substitute for his status as a national of a belligerent State that of a national of a neutral State.\" The odd detail: a Hamburg coffee man's bought passport became one of the most-cited cases in international law.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — in the setting, the passport trade runs the other way: German planters in American-occupied zones buy German papers because Germany won. A Liechtenstein or Danzig passport is what you buy when you want to vanish from both empires' files.",
