      "summary": "Born at Lemes in the Kingdom of Hungary, Alex \"Shondor\" Birns reached Ellis Island on 18 March 1907 and grew up on Woodland. On 9 November 1920 his mother, tending a bootleg still, was burned when a faulty gas connection exploded; she died the next morning, and the boy (15 by his birth date; Wikipedia says 13) spent time in a Jewish orphanage when his father was arrested for bootlegging. Convicted of car theft on 14 November 1925, aged 20 (18 months in the Mansfield Reformatory), 60 days and $500 for bribery in 1933, he racked up 18 arrests in 12 years; he fought in the dry-cleaning racket wars with Maxie Diamond's E. 55th Street gang, and by the late 1930s was collecting protection from whorehouses with the Cleveland family's backing and cultivating judges and police. In 1942 he was interned at McAlester, Oklahoma, as an \"enemy alien\" (released 1944). A car bomb killed him in 1975. The odd detail: A Cleveland racketeer whose mother was killed by the family still — and who was later locked up as an enemy alien of a country (Hungary) he left at two.",
