      "summary": "Voliva seized John Alexander Dowie's bankrupt city and church in 1906–07 (renamed the Christian Catholic Apostolic Church). Zion banned movie theatres, dancing, chewing gum, driving over 5 mph, make-up, high heels and bathing suits; girls from 12 covered their shoulders; the Zion Guard boarded trains to arrest smokers. Its schools taught that the earth is a flat disc centred on the North Pole and ringed by an impassable ice wall (unverified — V_R05); globes were banned and hymns rewritten (\"terrestrial ball\" → \"terrestrial plane\") (unverified — V_R05). From 23 June 1923 his WCBD (870 kHz; 500 watts at sign-on, 5,000 watts from 2 Feb 1925, moved to 1080 kHz in Nov 1928, sold to Gene T. Dyer in 1936) broadcast it; he offered $5,000 to anyone who could disprove a flat earth (on his terms; never paid). He prophesied the end of the world for 1923, 1927, 1930, 1934 and 1935. Zion Industries (Scottish lace, Zion fig bars, White Dove chocolates) made him ~$5 million by 1927 and went bankrupt in the Depression. In 1935 he started a Zion Passion Play on the Oberammergau model. In 1937 a disgruntled employee burned the Shiloh Tabernacle and Voliva went bankrupt; he died at Billings Memorial Hospital, Chicago, 11 Oct 1942, having confessed to misusing church funds — he had claimed his diet of Brazil nuts and buttermilk would let him live to 120. The odd detail: He went to the Scopes trial in 1925 to complain that Bryan \"doesn't go far enough\" — Bryan should have rejected astronomy too.",
