      "summary": "In 1906 the young lawyer Fernando Ortiz published Los negros brujos in the framework of criminal anthropology — the brujo as a \"social type\" to be studied and reformed rather than executed (Encyclopedia.com's summary). He later rejected that framing and spent his life documenting Afro-Cuban culture: the Sociedad de Folklore Cubano (1923 or 1928 — sources differ), the Sociedad de Estudios Afrocubanos (1937) with its journal Estudios Afrocubanos, the 1936–37 batá concert, and Contrapunteo cubano del tabaco y el azúcar (1940), which coined \"transculturation\". He began in Machado's Liberal Party and broke with it. The odd detail: the man whose first book is the period's hostile framing wrote the book that made the framing obsolete — the same author on both sides of the setting's \"three layers\".",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (the folklore society's founding year is inconsistent across sources).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Ortiz as the ideal NPC expert for an investigator in 1930s Havana — and his 1906 police-file photographs of \"brujos\" (the scholarship discusses a visual archive) as the record a cult could use to find the old practitioners who know it.",
