      "summary": "Created by an act signed 18 May 1905 and opened 3 December 1906, it replaced the city's justices of the peace: one chief justice and 27 associate judges, elected. It tried civil claims and all non-felony criminal cases, and in Chicago it held the preliminary hearings in felony cases — the stage at which (see \"the fixer\") most cases died. It was seen as the first court of its kind in America. The odd detail: a whole city's petty crime, divorce-and-desertion, boys' cases and felony prelims ran through 28 elected judges — every one a party nominee.",
