      "summary": "On 1 June 1906 Mexican miners struck over piecework and an unequal wage: American workers earned about $5 a day, Mexicans a maximum of $3. The company employed ~5,360 Mexicans and 2,200 Americans in a town of about 23,000 that Col. William C. Greene controlled outright (food, banking, water, land). The walkout began in the lumberyard and ended in gunfire; Wikipedia gives ~33 workers dead and 22 wounded (other sources: \"more than 20 miners and six company men\"). Greene telegraphed Arizona and 275 volunteers from Bisbee, Douglas and Naco under Capt. Thomas H. Rynning of the Arizona Rangers crossed into Mexico, against the orders of Arizona's governor; Sonora's Governor Rafael Izábal accepted them and sent forty Rurales from Hermosillo; Col. Emilio Kosterlitzky (next entry) made the Americans surrender their weapons before he took control. Leaders of the Partido Liberal Mexicano — Juan José Ríos, Manuel M. Diéguez, Esteban Baca Calderón — were blamed and sentenced to up to fifteen years, released May 1911 after Díaz fell. The strike ended 6 June. The odd detail: the first time American armed men crossed the line to put down a Mexican strike (unverified — V_M08: \"first\" is not in any source read; Wikipedia confirms only that the 275 crossed \"against the orders\" of Arizona's Governor Kibbey at Greene's telegraphed plea) was at the invitation of a Sonoran governor, and it was the Russian-born Mexican colonel who disarmed them. The affair helped convince Mexico that Díaz was selling the country to foreigners.",
