      "summary": "Col. William Cornell Greene's Cananea Consolidated Copper Company employed about 5,360 Mexican workers and 2,200 Americans in 1906. When Greene announced that American miners would earn $5 a day while Mexicans were capped at $3, the Mexicans walked out on 1 June chanting \"cinco pesos, ocho horas.\" They demanded the firing of unpopular foremen, a 75% Mexican hiring quota and equal advancement. Armed Americans, including company employees, fired on the crowd — about 33 workers died and 22 were wounded — after the lumber-yard manager George Metcalf turned a hose and then a gun on strikers and the miners set the yard ablaze. Capt. Thomas H. Rynning led 275 Arizona Rangers and other volunteers from Bisbee, Douglas and Naco across the border, against the Arizona governor's orders; Col. Emilio Kosterlitsky's Mexican federal troops and Governor Rafael Izábal's rurales reinforced the company. The strike ended on 6 June; three leaders got 15-year sentences (freed in 1911). The Mexican Liberal Party's press made Cananea a symbol of Díaz's dependence on foreign capital. The odd detail: 275 American law-enforcement volunteers crossed an international line, against their own governor's orders, to help put down a strike in a Mexican town — four years before the Revolution began.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (single summary; numbers are per the Wikipedia article and vary in sources).",
