      "summary": "Estrada Palma's party rigged the 1905 election against the Liberal José Miguel Gómez; the Liberals rose in the \"Little August War\" of 1906 and both sides asked for American intervention. Roosevelt sent William H. Taft and Robert Bacon (arrived 19 September); Estrada Palma resigned on 28 September and the next day Taft proclaimed himself provisional governor, to rule \"only long enough to restore order\". Charles E. Magoon took over on 13 October 1906. The Army of Cuban Pacification — about 425 officers and 6,196 men — built 92 km of roads and manned thirty posts. Gómez won the supervised election of 14 November 1908 and took office on 28 January 1909; the last US troops left by 6 February 1909. Cuban memory made Magoon a byword for graft and the botella (the sinecure \"bottle\" handed to a political client) — a charge American historians have disputed (UNVERIFIED on the page read). The odd detail: a sovereign republic's president resigns and a US cabinet secretary, standing in Havana, simply names himself governor the next morning — using the treaty clause the Cubans had voted in by 16 to 11.",
