      "summary": "On the eve of 6 October 1906 — the 223rd anniversary of the first German settlers' landing at Germantown, Pennsylvania, in 1683 — the Chicago branch of the German-American National Alliance held the city's first Deutscher Tag, a banquet for about 500 because there had been too little time to organise a parade. Dr. Max Eberhardt, president of the Chicago branch, presided; the national president Dr. C. J. Hexamer of Philadelphia was guest of honour, with University of Chicago and Northwestern professors and the consular officials. The programme had set-piece speeches on the German language, German-American patriotism, the German press and the women's societies, a toast to the Alliance, and a \"national hymn\" composed for the occasion. Eberhardt: the Alliance \"endeavors to awaken the sense of unity among the population of German origin in America.\" By 1910 German Day had Hexamer as keynote speaker at a full festival (26 Sept 1910), and by the 1930s it filled Soldier Field (see R04, R15). The odd detail: Chicago's German Day, the festival that draws 40,000 to Soldier Field in the 1930s, began as a 500-cover banquet organised in a hurry by a lobbying group founded five years earlier.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the \"national hymn composed for the occasion\" in 1906 is sung every German Day since. Nobody remembers who wrote it.",
