      "summary": "The Canal Zone Health Department opened Palo Seco in 1907 to isolate people with leprosy (Hansen's disease). Between 1904 and 1933 320 people were isolated there: 142 died, 18 absconded, 18 were repatriated; the average age at onset was 31, at death 40. There was a store with a $1.50 monthly allowance; movies two or three times a week, pool, fishing, swimming, dances; two churches and a chapel; chaulmoogra oil topically from 1908 and by injection from 1921 (by 1930, 65 patients were injected; 20 of 39 examined improved); nine required amputations. In 1919 the colony issued a special currency of its own so that patients handled no Zone money (the \"tokens\" were withdrawn in 1952). The colony was converted to long-term care in 1977 after the Carter–Torrijos treaties. The odd detail: a money of their own — leprosarium coins that were good only at Palo Seco, because the Zone's own money could not be trusted to leave.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (a scholarly medical-history article).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a Zone place of confinement with its own currency — a closed society under American medical authority, a chapel and a ward. A place that is \"cured\" on paper and never left. The Zone's confinement pattern (see the leprosaria in the Chicago sweep's R31) repeats here.",
