      "summary": "Founded 1907 as the Attic Club by the novelist Hamlin Garland (Pulitzer 1922 for A Daughter of the Middle Border) \"to encourage, foster and develop higher standards of art, literature and craftsmanship\"; renamed the Cliff Dwellers January 18, 1909 — after Henry Blake Fuller's novel of that name, though Fuller refused to join, or after the cliff-dwelling Indians of the Southwest, for a club \"perching on high ledges.\" Its presidents in this decade were the Chicago Symphony's conductor Frederick Stock (1927–28), the ex-mayor Carter Harrison IV (1929) and the architect Thomas E. Tallmadge (1932–33); women were barred until 1984. Garland himself had moved to Hollywood in 1929 and spent the decade on the other thing he had pursued since 1891: psychical research, published as Forty Years of Psychic Research (1936) and The Mystery of the Buried Crosses (1939), the latter written to \"prove the legitimacy of psychic mediums.\" He died March 4, 1940. The odd detail: The founder of the city's arts club spent the 1930s digging for buried crosses on the instructions of a medium — while his club dined nine floors above Michigan Avenue with the Symphony's conductor in the chair.",
