      "summary": "Fred A. Busse (1866–1914), a coal dealer (Northwestern Coal Co.), state legislator (1894–98), state treasurer (1902–05) and Chicago postmaster (1905–07, appointed by Theodore Roosevelt), beat Mayor Edward F. Dunne in 1907 and served to April 1911 — the first mayor of German parentage (unverified — V_R23) and the first four-year term. His administration was \"noted for its extensive corruption and presence of organized crime,\" and he told reformers they could \"always get me any evening at J. C. Murphy's saloon\"; but he created the 328-member Chicago Plan Commission (1909) that Wacker chaired. He died of heart disease on 9 July 1914, three weeks before the war, and is at Graceland. The odd detail: the German mayor held court in an Irish saloon and gave the city its Plan from there.",
