      "summary": "Established in 1907, the Cook County Juvenile Detention Home held children \"temporarily awaiting action by the Juvenile Court\"; the Board of Education ran a school inside; capacity in 1918 was about 110 (the scan reads \"11Q\"). In the same building sat the Juvenile Psychopathic Institute (founded 1909 by William Healy — the first child-guidance clinic in the world), which studied \"repeaters and other 'problem cases'\" of the court; in 1917 the State added an out-patient department under Dr. Herman M. Adler, \"State Criminologist\", which grew into the Institute for Juvenile Research where Clifford Shaw worked (see The Jack-Roller). Its superintendent in 1918 was Mrs. June Purcell Guild. A 1919 study examined 131 delinquent girls held there in 1917. The Jack-Roller was first put here at six and a half. The odd detail: The first thing Cook County did with a runaway six-year-old was hold him in the building where the State measured children's minds.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for 1918; the Detention Home's 1930s address (it moved with the Juvenile Court to a new building on the West Side in the 1920s — UNVERIFIED) — see Leads. • 1909; not named in the directory page — from general knowledge, UNVERIFIED this session",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Institute's files on \"repeaters\" are the best index of Chicago children who see things. Section 13 has a man on the Institute's staff; so, the players will discover, does someone else.",
