      "summary": "In Jamaican belief a duppy is a soul of the dead that can take many shapes. Walter Jekyll's Jamaican Song and Story (1907; the lines below are in Alice Werner's Introduction, pp. xlvii–xlix,) records duppies as \"the souls of the dead, capable of assuming various forms of men and other animals\"; warns that whistling at night lets a duppy \"catch your voice\"; and names the monstrous forms — the Rolling Calf, the three-foot horse, long-bubby Susan — calling the Rolling Calf \"the spirit of a person not good enough for heaven or bad enough for hell\". Later tellings (Martha Beckwith, Black Roadways, 1929) give it fiery eyes and a dragging chain, make it the ghost of butchers, murderers and obeah-men, and say it fears the moon; obeah-men can set one on a man. Related figures: Old Hige (the Jamaican skin-shedding witch, cousin of the soucouyant) and River Mumma. The odd detail: you hear a Rolling Calf before you see it — the chain on the road.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for Jekyll 1907 (read); Medium for Beckwith's specifics (secondary summary).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a Rolling Calf case in an American-garrisoned Kingston: a butcher who supplied the base's mess, dead, and a Marine sentry who heard a chain on the parade ground. The setting's rule that a thing dismissed as superstition may be real.",
