      "summary": "Brinkley attended Chicago's unaccredited Bennett Medical College (eclectic medicine) in 1907–09 and bought his final year at the Kansas City Eclectic Medical University for $100, graduating 7 May 1915. His goat-testicle transplants cost $750 and by 1930 were advertised for \"27 ailments, ranging from dementia to emphysema to flatulence\"; he had signed 42 death certificates when Kansas revoked his licence in 1930 (\"organized charlatanism ... quite beyond the invention of the humble mountebank\") and the Federal Radio Commission pulled his station KFKB six months later. He ran for governor of Kansas as a write-in in 1930 (180,000 votes, 29.5%, tens of thousands of ballots thrown out for misspelling) and 1932 (244,607, 30.6%, losing to Alf Landon), then built XER/XERA across the Rio Grande — 50,000 watts, then 150,000, then a million, \"far and away the most powerful radio station on the planet,\" heard in Canada on clear nights, said to make bedsprings hum and car headlights glow. Prostate operations went for up to $1,000; his Brinkley Pharmaceutical Association of pharmacies cleared about $14,000 a week. He lost the 1939 Fishbein libel trial and was ruined. In 1939 he gave $629 to the Silver Legion. The odd detail: a Chicago diploma mill produced the man whose voice, from Mexico, reached the whole Midwest at night — a signal strong enough to bleed into telephone calls.",
