      "summary": "The Work People's College was founded in 1907 by the Finnish Socialist Federation and slid into the orbit of the IWW in 1914–15; it closed in 1941. Finnish co-op stores and Finn Halls formed a parallel social world; after a 1929 Moscow directive demanding communist control of the co-ops the Twin Ports Finns split, some leaving the Party to defend democratic control. The Mesaba Cooperative Park (1929) became a community center and concert hall — \"banned from public accommodations across the Iron Range in the '20s, Finns opened their own\" (MinnPost) — and, according to the FBI, a \"hotbed of Communist activity\"; it hosted Farmer-Labor and CIO gatherings. In the 1930s Finns supplied about 40 per cent of the American Communist Party's membership (search summary). The \"Red Exodus\" took Twin Ports families to Soviet Karelia; many of the men were murdered under Stalin (the father of the Duluth author Mayme Sevander among them). The odd detail: Excluded from the Range's hotels and dance halls, the Finns simply built their own — a cooperative park, a college and a newspaper — and became the backbone of American communism.",
