      "summary": "Physician-anarchist Ben Reitman opened the Chicago branch of the Hobo College in 1907 (the biggest centre of the International Brotherhood Welfare Association), earned the title \"King of the Hobos\", offered Hobo Hall to Emma Goldman in 1908 and became the \"Great Grand Passion\" of her life for eight years. In the 1930s he founded the Chicago Society for the Prevention of Venereal Disease and wrote Sister of the Road: The Autobiography of Box-Car Bertha (1937; Scorsese's 1972 film). He died of a heart attack in Chicago in 1943, aged 63, buried at Waldheim (Forest Home), Forest Park — the anarchists' cemetery, with the Haymarket martyrs. The odd detail: A doctor who ran a college for tramps, treated the Levee's diseases, and wrote a woman hobo's autobiography for her.",
