      "summary": "Born on 4 April 1866 in Záboří, Bohemia, to a Jewish family, Sabath emigrated at 15 and served in the House from 4 March 1907 to his death on 6 November 1952 — 45 years. He became Dean of the House on 1 April 1934 and chaired the Rules Committee from 3 January 1939. A leading opponent of Prohibition and immigration restriction, he introduced bills every year from 1925 to 1933 to amend the Eighteenth Amendment and the Volstead Act; in 1929 he said \"the bootlegging and gang killings … are not the by-product but the direct product of the Volstead Act, and the supporters of this crime breeding legislation must claim the new cult of American criminals entirely as their own.\" He met the Lubavitcher Rebbe during the 1930 visit and arranged the 1924 immigration hearing (above). He is buried at Forest Home Cemetery, Forest Park — next door to Jewish Waldheim. The odd detail: \"The new cult of American criminals\" — a congressman's own word for the Outfit.",
