      "summary": "The Canadian Pacific's Edwardian lake liners — Keewatin launched 6 July 1907, 3,856 GRT, 16 knots, 288 passengers in 108 staterooms with a crew of 86 — ran the railway's water link across Huron and Superior; the passage took two and a half days each way, half a day of it in the Soo locks. After the Noronic fire of 1949 they were fitted with sprinklers and fire bulkheads (December 1950); Keewatin's last passenger run was 29 November 1965. The odd detail: The Canadian shore's equivalent of an ocean liner, timetabled to a transcontinental railway, spending half a day in the locks at the Soo where every ore boat on the lakes passed her.",
