      "summary": "The isthmus is 200 km (124 mi) across at the narrowest point; the pass is 224 m. Failed contractors (1888, 1893) left a line \"completed\" in 1894 with deficient ports; the British contractor Weetman Pearson (S. Pearson & Son; later Lord Cowdray) began work on 10 December 1899 (unverified — V_M15: a search finds the final concession granted 11 November 1899; the 10 December date was not found), re-laying and regrading a 308 km line and building deep-water harbours at both ends (heavy dredging, long breakwaters), inaugurated in January 1907 — \"the greatest of our undertakings,\" in his words; the total contract was about £2.5 million. It ran oil-burning locomotives, freighted sugar to Philadelphia and New York, and carried the American-Hawaiian Steamship Company's connecting service — a 25-day San Francisco–New York run. Pearson's harbour survey also led to oil: the Minatitlán refinery, the Mexican Eagle company (31 August 1908) and, in December 1910, the runaway well Potrero del Llano No. 4 (over 100,000 barrels a day). Under Díaz Pearson's contract barred him from selling the isthmus properties to American interests. Then the Panama Canal opened (1914), the Revolution and the Great War intervened, and freight collapsed. In April 1917 Carranza's government \"violently took possession\" of the railway (the words are the British Embassy's, 31 Dec 1917 — a British view), and on 14 December 1917 Pearson and the Mexican government dissolved their partnership and divided the assets — about £400,000 in cash plus shipping interests, including a 30% stake in the American-Hawaiian Steamship Company, valued at £1.25–1.5 million (a British embassy memorandum of 31 December 1917 calls this a private business settlement, not aid to Carranza); in March 1919 Royal Dutch Shell bought 35% of Mexican Eagle. The railway \"continued to handle substantial passenger traffic well into the fifties\" but \"ceased to be a significant carrier of freight.\" The odd detail: the same year Mexico seized the railway (April 1917) is the year the setting's Southern War ends and the treaty is signed.",
