      "summary": "At about 3:30 p.m. on 14 January 1907 a magnitude-6.2 earthquake damaged every building in Kingston; fires burned three hours before anyone could check them, gutting the business and warehouse districts; about 1,000 died and a small tsunami followed (the sea drew back ~80 m at Annotto Bay). Among the dead: Sir James Fergusson, a former colonial governor, and the cricketer Bertie Verley. Rear-Admiral Charles H. Davis Jr. came from Guantánamo with the torpedo boat Whipple and the battleships Indiana and Missouri and landed men and supplies — with President Theodore Roosevelt's approval and without going through the governor. Governor Sir Alexander Swettenham refused American surgeons and asked the Americans to leave; American papers erupted on 21 January; Whitehall apologised to Washington, and Swettenham resigned, his career over (he died in Switzerland in 1933). A formal letter of 19 January from him thanking Davis survives in Foreign Relations. The odd detail: the first time American sailors landed uninvited on a British Caribbean island in the new century, London apologised to Washington — and sacked its own governor.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the quake and the incident's outline; the wording of Swettenham's rude letter (often retold with a sarcastic \"burglary\" comparison) UNVERIFIED — I did not read it.",
