      "summary": "Section 2 of the Act said an American lost citizenship by \"naturalization in a foreign state\", by \"taking an oath of allegiance to a foreign state\", or (for the naturalised) by long residence abroad. Section 3 took citizenship away from an American woman who married a foreigner. But the Act also said expatriation could happen only in peacetime — no citizen could expatriate himself while the United States was at war. It stood until the Nationality Act of 1940. The odd detail: this is the legal basis of the rulebook's volunteer's closed door — and it contains a wartime trapdoor the rulebook does not yet use.",
