      "summary": "Jesús García Corona (born Hermosillo 13 November 1881; a railway water-boy at 17 who rose to fireman and engineer) was resting in Nacozari on 7 November 1907 at about 3 p.m. when hay on the roof of a dynamite car caught fire from a failing locomotive smokebox. He drove the train in reverse, downhill, at full steam, six kilometres out of town before it exploded. He died; his body was found some 20 feet from the engine with, the story says, his hand still on the throttle. The town was renamed Nacozari de García, the American Red Cross declared him a \"Hero of Humanity\", and 7 November is the Día del Ferrocarrilero. The corrido \"Máquina 501\" commemorates him; the town's link to the world was the Douglas line. The odd detail: the national hero of Mexican railway workers died working for an American copper company on a line that ran to Arizona — and the corrido remembers the engine's number.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the date, drive and the honours; death toll and dynamite tonnage not confirmed (do not print numbers without a source).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a 1930s Nacozari with a monument to a man who drove death away from a town — and a coal-tender in the yard that smells of burnt dynamite every 7 November. A ghost train is folklore-cheap; \"what was really in that car?\" is better.",
