      "summary": "In Chapter IV of Barbarous Mexico, \"The Contract Slaves of Valle Nacional,\" Turner described a system in which \"enganchadores\" ran \"employment offices\" in Mexico City and elsewhere, took a $5 \"advance,\" locked the recruit in a casa del enganchador, and shipped him under a false contract to the tobacco plantations, at about $45 a man ($22.50 for a woman or child) in 1908; the jefes políticos sold petty offenders directly, kept a toll at Tuztepec and, he says, paid $10,000 a year for the office out of the traffic. He put the flow at 15,000 new slaves a year and claimed that all but a very few (his words: perhaps five per cent) died within seven or eight months; one manager told him the cheapest thing was to let them die, since there were plenty more where they came from, burial fees ($1.50) were \"a constant item of expense,\" dormitories held 70–400 people locked in nightly, and one-third of the slaves were children under fifteen. These are Turner's own claims from an undercover trip; treat the totals as his, not as audited figures. The odd detail: one plantation was named La Sepultura, and the owners mostly Spanish.",
