      "summary": "The Texan lawyer William F. Buckley Sr. moved to Mexico in 1908, founded Buckley & Buckley with his brother Claude to represent American and European oil companies, opened a Tampico office with his brother Edmund in 1912 and founded the Pantepec Oil Company in 1913. He backed Peláez's revolt and lobbied (through the American Association of Mexico) to amend Article 27 of the 1917 Constitution, which vested subsoil oil in the nation. In 1921 the Obregón government expelled him: he was allowed to gather his personal belongings and a few law books in Tampico, put on a ship, and his Mexican assets were seized. (His son William F. Buckley Jr., born 1925, spoke Spanish as his first language.) The odd detail: the founder of an American conservative dynasty was deported from Tampico with only \"a few law books.\"",
