      "summary": "Hans Kundt (1869–1939), a Mecklenburg officer, arrived in Bolivia in 1908 to lead a training mission and from 1911 reorganised its army \"following the pattern of the Prussian Army\". He fought on the Eastern Front in the Great War (Generalleutnant), then returned as Bolivia's chief of staff and war minister, took Bolivian citizenship and led its 1920s rearmament. Recalled from exile to command in the Chaco War against Paraguay, he threw frontal assaults at Paraguayan lines, ignored air reconnaissance and saw his tanks stall in the scrub; after the disaster at Campo Vía he was relieved on 12 December 1933. He died in Lugano in 1939. The odd detail: a Prussian general became a Bolivian citizen to keep command of its army.",
