      "summary": "Born in Detroit on 10 April 1865, Binga came to Chicago for the 1893 fair, went into real estate (1896–98), opened a private bank in 1908 and a state-chartered one in 1921, moving to new offices in 1924 and building the $325,000 Binga Arcade at 35th and State. His purchase of a house on South Park Avenue got his home and offices bombed at least five times. Heavily loaded with Black Belt real-estate loans, the bank could not survive the crash; in 1930 the Chicago Clearinghouse — to which he had paid dues for a decade (unverified — V_R14) — refused to help, and the Illinois Auditor General closed it (Wikipedia gives June 1930; another account says state auditors shut it on 31 July 1930). Binga was indicted for embezzlement in March 1931; the first trial (1932) hung, the second (1933) convicted him, and he drew ten years. Paroled from Joliet in 1938 after a 10,000-signature petition and a plea to the parole board by his attorney, Clarence Darrow, he was fully pardoned by Governor Dwight H. Green in 1941 and died 13 June 1950 at St. Luke's Hospital; buried Oak Woods Cemetery. The odd detail: The richest Black man in Chicago, bombed five times for buying a house, ended the decade as a church handyman — the fall was total and public, and everyone in Bronzeville had money in that vault.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the dated events; the two closure dates (June vs 31 July 1930) conflict between sources — the state closure order and the final shuttering may be different events. • in 1930 the Chicago Clearinghouse — to which he had paid dues for a decade (unverified — V_R14) — refused to help, and the Illinois Auditor General closed it (Wikipedia gives June 1930; • from memory; UNVERIFIED this session",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — What was in the Binga vault at 35th and State when the auditors sealed it? Depositors' savings — and, in a module, something a customer left in a safe-deposit box in 1929 that nobody can now get at legally. The Kabbalahs contract the Church for work it forbids its own; a bank in receivership is work nobody wants.",
