      "summary": "Fire broke out in the morning in a building with only two exits and an open wooden interior inside a masonry shell that drew flames up like a chimney. In the narrow vestibule children \"stumbled and climbed on top of one another, forming a pile that completely blocked the exit.\" 172 pupils, 2 teachers and 1 rescuer died. The inquest blamed heating pipes against exposed joists; rumours blamed the janitor, Fritz Hirter, overheated boilers, or girls smoking in a basement closet; the 2003 plaque says \"unknown origin.\" The popular story that the doors opened inward is false — they opened outward. Parents would not let a school be rebuilt on the site, which became a memorial garden. The odd detail: The last survivor was the janitor's own daughter, Ella Hirter Smith (died 2001, aged 99), who married Otto Smith in 1928 — a man who lost his brother Willie in the same fire.",
