      "id": "R29-050",
      "title": "The Springfield race riot",
      "date_start": "1908-08-14",
      "date_end": "1908-08-14",
      "date_label": "14–16 August 1908 (living memory)",
      "category": "Chicago's hinterland, 1925–1940: the collar counties, downstate Illinois and Northwest Indiana",
      "tags": [
        "Chicago",
        "hinterland",
        "Springfield",
        "living memory"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Mabel Hallam",
        "George Richardson",
        "William Donnegan"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Springfield — the jail",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Loper's restaurant, the \"Badlands\" district",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Springfield%20%E2%80%94%20the%20jail%2C%20Loper%27s%20restaurant%2C%20the%20%22Badlands%22%20district%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "two blocks from Lincoln's home",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=two%20blocks%20from%20Lincoln%27s%20home%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Two Black men were jailed — Joe James (charged with killing Clergy Ballard) and George Richardson (accused of raping Mabel Hallam on 12 August). When the sheriff spirited them away, a white crowd of about 5,000 burned the Badlands, destroyed Loper's restaurant (its owner had lent the car), and lynched Scott Burton and 84-year-old William Donnegan; at least 17 died and $150,000 of property burned. Hallam later admitted she made up the assault to hide an affair her husband had discovered. Over 100 indictments; one minor conviction; James was hanged, Richardson freed. The riot led directly to the founding of the NAACP. The odd detail: In Lincoln's home town, the lie that started it was confessed — and it changed nothing for the dead.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Pairs with Lindsay's midnight Lincoln and the 1931 tomb (Part G): the ghost the town's Black residents talk about is not Lincoln's. A Golden Circle mark for the riot's organisers, never tried.",
