      "summary": "Back from Central America in 1909, Holmdahl answered a want-ad — \"Wanted: Man with military experience, who had nerve and is single\" — and was handed a $100 bill by Mexican revolutionaries (Magonista) who sent him to Nogales, Culiacán and Mazatlán posing as a New York mining-company representative to count the Díaz garrisons at Tepic. The governor showed him the arsenal. When the governor was tipped off, Holmdahl escaped over the rooftops on a lariat, shot a policeman in the street, and — in his telling — shattered the stained-glass window above a fat priest's head to clear a church door. Caught near Tuxpán, dragged ten miles behind a horse to Rosamorada prison, beaten, and asked by a Díaz agent and a priest to sign a confession, he kicked the priest. A friendly guard freed him. He then took a captain's commission from Col. Emilio Kosterlitzky's Sonoran Rural Police (double agent from the start, he says), guarded a railroad's gold shipments near Mazatlán with 200 men, and in early 1911 changed sides on the road when captured horse-thieves said they served Madero. \"Vámonos a Holmdahl\" became a village cry; he took most of Nayarit's coastal towns; a failed Tepic jailbreak (betrayed by deserters) cost 300 executions. In May 1911, with Martín Espinosa, he took Rosario (using dynamite raided from the Buena Noche mine: 22 men, 27 cases) and then Rosamorada, where 700 prisoners were in the jail. His solution to the mob: shoot the worst — 112 killers picked from the prison book at midnight, six officers as the firing squad, 500 others freed with five pesos each. When Espinosa made himself a governor at Tepic, Holmdahl attacked him with 280 Cora men carrying bows and an old brass cannon; after 36 hours two-thirds of his men, all seven officers, were dead and he was wounded. He then rode to Madero. The odd detail: the priest is the villain of the story twice — the informant with the confession paper, the fat priest at the church door — in the memoir of a man who told the priest he had \"no religion\".",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Low–Medium. One man's memoir, transcribed into Wikipedia; the numbers (300, 112, 700, 500, 3,000, 36 hours) are his. The Rosamorada prison massacre would be a serious war-crimes claim if true; no independent source found.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the priest-informant as a Church problem for IHS: in Díaz's Nayarit a priest carried the Díaz agent's confession into a prison cell. An IHS priest of the 1930s reading that memoir has a lead on a much older habit. For the Guild: a hired hunter whose self-reported body count nobody can check — the classic Guild recruit.",
