      "summary": "Son of Frederick Wacker, a Württemberg-born brewer (Wacker & Birk Brewing and Malting), Charles Henry Wacker (1856–1929) took over the brewery in 1884, then spent 1909–1926 as chairman of the Chicago Plan Commission selling Burnham's plan to the city (\"Wacker's Manual\" in every school). In the same years he was president of the Deutsche und Oesterreichisch-Ungarische Hilfsgesellschaft — the German and Austro-Hungarian Aid Society — which he chaired through the neutrality years (he was billed with Dernburg at Medinah Temple in Dec 1914) and after: on 10 Oct 1919 at the Lincoln Club he reported $22,700 received in ten days for the starving in Germany and Austria, and the meeting pledged $26,269.25 more after Jane Addams described milk at one-seventeenth of pre-war and \"a loaf of white bread … shared by ten patients.\" Wacker Drive was named for him in 1926; he died at Lake Geneva in 1929. The odd detail: the man whose name is on the Loop's river drive was the German war-relief chairman of Chicago from 1914 to 1919, and nobody now remembers the second job.",
