      "summary": "Born in Milwaukee in 1878, Clement joined the Chicago police in 1909 to patrol department stores for pickpockets and shoplifters, and in 1913 — the year the city appointed its first squad of policewomen — became its first woman detective. She worked in disguise, carried a revolver, and was a press favourite; in 1919 she wrote, produced and starred as herself in a feature film, Dregs of the City. In one case she reopened the death of a young woman written off as typhoid-related and showed it was murder, against her male colleagues' conclusion. In mid-1926 she was demoted from the Detective Bureau to a district station, went on medical leave with diabetes, and died on 26 December 1926, aged 49. The odd detail: A serving Chicago detective made a movie about herself — and (per period press accounts summarised on her Wikipedia page; the censorship detail is UNVERIFIED here) the city's own censor board refused to pass it. (unverified — V_R28)",
