      "summary": "A \"rural French\" château-style house built 1894 (architect H. Edwards Ficken of New York) for Northwestern official Robert Sheppard. Charles G. Dawes lived here from 1909 until his death in 1951; his widow stayed until 1957. The real Dawes won the 1925 Nobel Peace Prize for the German reparations plan, was Coolidge's vice-president, ambassador to Britain, and (general knowledge, not on the page — see R03) in 1932 head of the Reconstruction Finance Corporation just before its loan to his own bank. Descendant of William Dawes, who rode with Paul Revere. The odd detail: The one national figure the setting makes president (1927–29) came home to a suburban house you can still tour — a president's house on an Evanston side street.",
