      "summary": "A coal car of hay for the mules caught fire from a wall lantern and burned unnoticed for 45 minutes; attempts to fight it spread it to the timbers; reversing the great fan set the fan house, escape ladders and stairs alight; sealing the shafts filled the mine with black damp. 259 men and boys died (and about three dozen mules). Twenty-one miners survived eight days behind a wall they built themselves, drinking seep water, until a rescue party found them. Illinois passed new mine-safety rules in 1910 and workmen's compensation in 1911. The odd detail: Twenty-one men bricked themselves into the dark and came out alive after eight days.",
