      "summary": "Frederick Joubert \"Fritz\" Duquesne (born Cape Colony, 21 September 1877), a Boer artillery officer, was arrested 11 October 1901 for plotting to sabotage Cape Town and kill Lord Kitchener; he escaped the Castle of Good Hope and, on 25 June 1902, a Bermuda POW camp (swimming 1.5 miles). He called himself \"the Black Panther\". Wikipedia says he served as Roosevelt's \"personal shooting instructor\" and accompanied him on African hunts and wrote safari articles (an unverified boast in a fabulist's life) (unverified — V_M19: repeated by Wikipedia with a note that Duquesne's claims are disputed; the only support seen is his own 1909 Hampton's Magazine articles). In 1910, with Burnham and Rep. Robert Broussard, he lobbied the House Agriculture Committee to import hippopotamuses into Louisiana's bayous as meat; the American Hippo Bill narrowly failed. In WWI he ran sabotage from Brazil as \"Frederick Fredericks,\" planting time bombs in mineral-sample cases on British ships (about 22 vessels). He was arrested 17 November 1917, escaped Bellevue on 25 May 1919 disguised as a woman, was recaptured 23 May 1932, and led the Duquesne spy ring for Germany (arrested 28 June 1941, 18-year sentence; released 1954); he died on 24 May 1956 on Welfare Island. Wikipedia lists flights to Mexico and Europe before his 1926 return to New York, and in 1933 he wrote to Burnham that he \"once craved the honor of killing him.\" The odd detail: an assassin's target and his would-be killer became partners in a scheme to put hippos in the Louisiana bayous — and the assassin claims he taught Roosevelt to shoot.",
