      "summary": "The 1910 census counted 14,332 Slovenes in Cleveland — ECH calls it then the third-largest Slovene city on earth and the largest Slovene settlement in America. Parishes: St. Vitus on St. Clair (organised 1893), St. Lawrence in Newburgh (1901), St. Mary in Collinwood (1906). The Slovenian National Home on St. Clair (built 1924) seated more than 1,000. Sokol from 1897, more than 40 singing societies over the years, and papers from Narodna Beseda (1899) to Ameriška Domovina. Frank J. Lausche — the judge who finally closed the Harvard Club (R07), later mayor, governor and senator — and the writer Louis Adamic came out of this world. The odd detail: A Slovenian neighbourhood big enough to rank among the world's Slovene cities — in a country most Americans could not have found on a map.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A thousand-seat national hall where the old country's quarrels are argued in a language the police do not speak — a meeting place nobody outside notices.",
