      "summary": "Lawrence D. Taylor's article The Great Adventure: Mercenaries in the Mexican Revolution, 1910–1915 (The Americas, July 1986) counts \"several hundred\" foreign fighters in total, notes that not all were professional mercenaries — many wanted \"pay or love of adventure\" — and says recruits learned of jobs through press coverage or \"frequenting border-town social centers.\" Rates: Tracy Richardson and Sam Dreben, $500 in gold a month; Captain Hector Worden (aviator), $1,500 a month; Edwin C. Parsons (aviator), $200 a month in gold. Didier Masson's Sonora contract: $300 a month, $50 a flight and $250+ a bombing sortie; the $5,000 was the aeroplane-purchase fee (Wikipedia). Taylor names Richardson, Dreben, Garibaldi and Thord-Gray as notable figures. He concludes the foreign soldiers \"significantly impacted\" operations, especially in machine-gunnery and aviation. The odd detail: one pilot commanded three times a machine gunner's wage and another a fraction of it — in the Revolution the market for the air was still finding its price.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
