      "summary": "\"Big Jim\" Colosimo opened his café in 1910 and \"it quickly became a popular destination for prominent Chicagoans and affluent visitors, gaining nationwide fame\" — the room where the Gold Coast met the Levee, with Caruso-class entertainment (legend) and Dale Winter, the singer he married weeks before he died. On May 11, 1920, a gunman waiting in the cloakroom shot him in the vestibule, \"behind his right ear,\" and Johnny Torrio (and, the story goes, the young Capone) inherited the business. His funeral drew 5,000 mourners; the pallbearers, actual and honorary, \"included three judges, eight aldermen, a congressman, and several ward bosses.\" The café stayed open under other hands into the 1940s (UNVERIFIED this session), a name every 1930s Chicagoan knew. The odd detail: Eight aldermen and three judges carried a brothel-keeper's coffin in public — the rich and the mob at one funeral, 1920, and nobody ashamed.",
