      "summary": "Austrian-born (Nieder-Rehbach, 1860; in Milwaukee from 1881), editor of Vorwärts! and the Milwaukee Leader, founder of the Social Democratic Party (1898) and the Socialist Party (1901), Berger was the first Socialist in Congress (1911–13). Indicted under the Espionage Act for his anti-war paper, he was convicted on 20 Feb 1919 and sentenced by Landis in Chicago to twenty years; Milwaukee re-elected him anyway (1918), the House refused to seat him twice (Nov 1919, Jan 1920), the Supreme Court reversed the conviction on 31 Jan 1921 (Landis should have recused), and he served 1923–29 and chaired the party (1926). He was killed by a streetcar in Milwaukee on 7 Aug 1929. Milwaukee's Socialist mayors — Emil Seidel (1910–12) and Daniel Hoan (1916–40) — ran \"sewer socialism\" through the whole of the setting's decade. The odd detail: the judge who gave a German-American congressman twenty years for opposing the war was the same Landis who, two years later, became the first Commissioner of Baseball.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a German-language socialist daily in Milwaukee that nobody ever suppressed is the one paper in the Midwest that prints what the consulate does not want printed.",
