      "summary": "Legend says a mute patient recorded as \"Manual Bookbinder\" alias \"A. Bookbinder\" (1878–1910), grave marker 713, worked on the hospital's burial crew — the name came from the printing house where he had worked before commitment. At every burial he leaned on the Graveyard Elm and wept. When he died, hundreds of patients and staff attended; the pallbearers found the coffin strangely light; a wailing came from the elm; everyone, Dr. Zeller included, turned to see Old Book weeping by the tree — and when the lid was opened, his body lay inside and the crying stopped. The elm then began to die; workmen who tried to cut it heard it wail, and it was only removed after lightning struck it. The odd detail: A numbered grave marker — 713 — for a man the story says attended his own burial.",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Folklore. First attestation: Zeller's own writings (the memoir Befriending the Bereft, 1920s — Wikipedia's \"Old Book\" page calls it his \"diary\"; which text, and whether the grave number and dates come from hospital records, is UNVERIFIED). Popularised by modern ghost-tour writers.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Three layers: the period's reading (a superintendent's sentimental tale), the folklore (the crying elm), and what is there — a patient who did not stop working after he died, and a burial crew in the 1930s that still counts one man more than the payroll does.",
