      "summary": "Chester Hjortur Thordarson (1867–1945), born in Iceland, came to the U.S. with his family in 1873 and became an inventor with nearly a hundred patents; in 1910 he began buying Rock Island — 775 acres; construction of his estate began in 1920 after clearing 30 acres on the southwest side. In 1923 he advertised in the Winnipeg News for Icelandic men to build the boathouse, decorated with characters from the Norse runic alphabet; the \"Icelandic Castle\" hall is 140 by 70 feet with 18-foot windows and a fireplace for 8-foot logs; Halldór Einarsson, 29, was brought from Iceland to carve oak furniture (two huge tables, twenty chairs, a king's and a queen's chair) with scenes from Norse mythology. Several buildings were designed by Frederick Dinkelberg; there is a water tower; the island also holds the Potawatomi (Pottawatomie) Lighthouse of 1836 and Icelandic thyme Thordarson planted. Wisconsin bought the island from his heirs in 1965. (He made his fortune in Chicago in electrical manufacture, as most accounts say — not confirmed on the pages read.) The odd detail: A self-made Chicago inventor built a Viking hall on a Lake Michigan island in the Depression and staffed it from Winnipeg.",
