      "summary": "Pearson's Mexican Eagle (El Águila) company brought in Potrero del Llano No. 4 on 27 December 1910; it threw oil 250 feet into the air and ran wild for roughly two to three months at an estimated 100,000–110,000 barrels a day, the oil caught in earthen pits dug around it. Capping began in late January 1911. Tamed, it gave up to 45,000 barrels a day and more than 25 million barrels in its first three years — the most productive well in the Western Hemisphere until Cerro Azul. In August 1914 an electrical storm ignited the seepages around the well and the fire burned for six months; afterwards a concrete bunker was built around the wellhead, both against lightning and \"to help defend it from revolutionists.\" El Águila's young geologist on the ground was Everette Lee DeGolyer. The odd detail: a lightning strike set the ground itself on fire around the well, and the company answered by entombing the wellhead in concrete — a blockhouse against the sky and the rebels at once.",
