      "summary": "Five-year-old Elsie Paroubek was kidnapped in April 1911. Alderman Anton J. Cermak moved in the City Council (1 May 1911) for a $500 police reward; with $500 from the Bohemian Charitable Association and $200 from the State, the reward reached $1,200. Denní Hlasatel (12 May 1911) reported she had been \"kidnapped, held prisoner, and finally murdered and thrown into the drainage canal.\" Mayor Carter Harrison, Judges Sabath and Uhlíř and Cermak sent flowers; police had to reinforce the guard at the house; freethinker Jaromír R. Pšenka gave the only rite — a funeral oration — before burial at the Bohemian National Cemetery. The Czechs in Chicago article says the case \"mobilized the Czech community.\" The odd detail: the child's funeral had no priest — only a freethinker's oration — and the man who raised the reward was the future mayor.",
