      "summary": "The Ypiranga was built in 1908 by Krupp's Germaniawerft at Kiel for the Hamburg-America Line (HAPAG). On 31 May 1911 the fallen dictator Porfirio Díaz and his family boarded her at Veracruz; she called at Havana on 4 June for farewells and landed him at Le Havre (he died in Paris in 1915). In April 1914 she arrived at Veracruz carrying rifles, machine guns and ammunition for Huerta; the report of that cargo is what triggered Wilson's seizure of the port on 21 April (see M01). The United States tried to stop the delivery and backed down; she landed the arms at Puerto México instead (April–May 1914). Laid up in Hamburg through the war, seized by Britain on 2 April 1919, renamed Assyria (1921) and Colonial (1929); sank off Scotland under tow to the breakers in September 1950. The odd detail: one German ship sailed away with the old regime (1911) and sailed back three years later with the guns to save the next one — and the Americans let her land them at the next port down the coast.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on Díaz and the 1914 delivery; the page gave no cargo origin, quantities or landing date (UNVERIFIED — the \"Russian rifles bought via an American arms dealer\" story is widely told but not read this session). Correction to the lane brief: the Ypiranga did not carry Huerta into exile — the cruiser SMS Dresden did (next entry).",
