      "summary": "Miller, a young Indiana-born naturalist with literary training, collected birds and mammals for the AMNH under Frank Chapman, in expeditions financed largely by Roosevelt (per a search summary). On the Roosevelt–Rondon overland march he handled the mammals while Cherrie handled the birds (Roosevelt's own division of labour); he did not run the River of Doubt — Roosevelt has him on the bank with Capt. Amilcar's Ji-Paraná party waving the canoes off; Miller wrote In the Wilds of South America (1918), covering expeditions from 1911 to 1916, including the Roosevelt–Rondon party (October 1913 – June 1914). A source describes notes for 450+ specimens and 97 species. The odd detail: Roosevelt paid for the expeditions that produced one of his party's own naturalists' memoirs.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (search summaries; the book itself is public domain — lead).",
