      "summary": "In April 1911 more than 6,000 furniture workers struck; the strike lasted four months until leaders of the Christian Reformed Church — whose Dutch American members were the majority of the labour movement — publicly denounced it, a local classis ruling that members should not belong to labour unions (search summaries). In the 1920s the Klan arrived: an estimated 5,000 members in greater Grand Rapids (80,000 in Michigan), a Klan \"club\" at South High School, and on 4 July 1925 about 3,000 Klansmen marched through the west side — a district of Catholic immigrants from Poland, Germany and Lithuania — before a rally at John Ball Park. Members had pitched a tent city near the Bridge Street hillside in the weeks before, and crosses burned on a hill on 3 July. A 1924 parade had been postponed for weather. The city's Black population was under 3,000. The furniture industry's peak was over by the 1930s. Kalamazoo's Klan peaked in 1924 and faded by about 1926, with early membership from Van Buren and South Bend (search summary). The odd detail: The Klan chose 4 July for a march through the Polish, German and Lithuanian Catholic west side of a city where the Dutch Reformed church had once killed a strike by decree.",
