      "summary": "Samuel Zemurray was born 18 January 1877 in Kishinev, emigrated at 14 in 1891 and by 21 had $100,000 buying overripe bananas in Mobile (\"Sam the Banana Man\"). With Ashbell Hubbard he bought the Cuyamel Fruit Company (1905) and by 1910 held 5,000 acres on Honduras's Cuyamel River. Fearing a British debt restructuring would ruin him, he orchestrated a coup: mercenary Lee Christmas gathered about 100 fighters in New Orleans (including the Jewish soldier of fortune Sam Dreben), who sailed aboard the Hornet (Wikipedia says a former Navy vessel, not Zemurray's yacht); the uprising installed former president Manuel Bonilla, who took office 1 February 1912 and rewarded Zemurray with tax and land concessions. In 1929 Zemurray sold Cuyamel to United Fruit for $31.5 million in stock and retired; in 1933, after United Fruit stock fell 90 percent, he gathered proxies and forced a boardroom takeover. He led United Fruit into the 1950s (including the 1954 Guatemala coup) and died 30 November 1961. The odd detail: an immigrant boy from Kishinev who bought ripe bananas at a Mobile dock arranged a Central American government — and eventually captured the company that had been his competitor.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (Wikipedia; the coup's two dates — 1911 launch, 1912 inauguration — are consistent).",
