      "summary": "The Leader was launched on 7 Dec 1911 by the Social Democratic Publishing Company, funded chiefly by the wealthy radical Elizabeth H. Thomas, with Berger as editor-in-chief and John M. Work, Ernest Untermann and James R. \"Jim\" Howe writing editorials. It printed 31,874 copies a day in its first month and 50,000 by April 1916; Berger claimed three-quarters of its readers were not Socialists. On 22 Sept (EMKE: October) 1917 Postmaster General Albert Burleson revoked its second-class mailing privilege, \"eliminating 5 out of 6 mail subscribers in one blow\"; in August 1918 even first-class mail to the office was stamped \"Mail to This Address Undeliverable Under Espionage Act\"; the Justice Department raided it twice and seized the subscriber lists. Privileges came back only in June 1921. Circulation ran 48,200–49,100 through 1922–29, 38,300 in 1930 and 21,200 in 1940; Berger died leaving $130,000 of debt on it. Sold on 20 April 1938 to three ex-Sentinel men, it became the New Milwaukee Leader, then (Jan 1939) the Milwaukee Evening Post, then the Federated Trades Council's Milwaukee Post (Sept 1940); it died on 23 May 1942. The odd detail: One of only three English-language socialist dailies in the United States, and it lived in the most German city in the country.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (the two sources give the revocation month as September and October 1917 respectively).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The one paper in the ring that prints what the Journal will not — a Section 13 agent's back channel, and the place a Golden Circle leak would be planted to discredit the Socialists.",
