      "summary": "In July 1912 the heavyweight champion opened the Café de Champion, an interracial nightclub on 31st Street, and met there the 18-year-old Lucille Cameron who became his second wife that December. On 11 September 1912 his first wife, Etta Terry Duryea, shot herself upstairs at the café, worn down by his abuse and infidelity and the city's hostility to the marriage. In 1913 a Chicago jury convicted him under the Mann Act for acts committed before the law existed; he fled for seven years, came back in 1920, and served his time at Leavenworth until 9 July 1921. He fought into the 1930s and died in a car crash near Franklinton, North Carolina, on 10 June 1946; he is buried at Graceland beside Etta. Teddy Roe's 1952 funeral was later called the biggest for any Black Chicagoan since Johnson's in 1946. The odd detail: The champion's wife died by her own hand above his nightclub, two months after it opened; the club died with her.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Café de Champion's upstairs room at 41 West 31st, twenty years on — a ghost story Bronzeville tells about the building. Legend material, labelled as such.",
