      "summary": "Founded in 1912 as Maimonides Hospital \"with a mission of serving poor immigrants from Europe while providing training to Jewish physicians, primarily of Eastern European descent\"; it closed in 1918 and reopened in 1919 as Mount Sinai Hospital with 60 beds. Morris Kurtzon founded it to give the West Side \"a suitable hospital, one where Jewish doctors could practice without facing exclusion\" — and, unlike other hospitals, it had a kosher kitchen. It was the city's second Jewish hospital, the Eastern European counterpart to the German-Jewish Michael Reese (1881; see R23). The odd detail: A whole hospital founded partly because Jewish doctors could not get privileges elsewhere — and partly so an Orthodox patient could eat.",
