      "summary": "The textile millionaire Col. George Fabyan built a private research estate: a Japanese garden, a private zoo, a Roman-style pool, a lighthouse, and a Dutch windmill moved to the site in 1914. His Riverbank Laboratories (building 1912, founded 1913) housed a team hunting ciphers that \"proved\" Francis Bacon wrote Shakespeare (led by Elizabeth Wells Gallup); a young geneticist-turned-photographer William F. Friedman and a Shakespeare scholar Elizebeth Smith met there, married, and became America's founding codebreakers (their book later disproved the Bacon ciphers). In 1913 Fabyan hired Harvard's Wallace Sabine to tune an acoustic levitation machine built to specifications decoded from a Bacon text; Sabine persuaded him it would not work, and the lab became a serious acoustics house under Paul Sabine. The NSA's 1993 plaque credits Riverbank with cryptologic training \"on the eve of America's entry into World War I\". Fabyan died 17 May 1936 (pleurisy); his widow Nelle died 1939; Kane County bought the estate for $70,500. The odd detail: A millionaire paid a Harvard physicist to make a machine that would lift objects by sound, from instructions he believed were hidden in Shakespeare.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the estate, staff, the levitation commission and the death date. The famous \"furniture hung from chains\" detail is not on the pages read — UNVERIFIED.",
