      "summary": "Elizabeth Plankinton gave the organ; the hall kept its own orchestra for decades. Taft spoke there on 27 Oct 1911. On 14 Oct 1912 Theodore Roosevelt, shot in the chest outside the Gilpatrick Hotel across the street, delivered his speech anyway. Woodrow Wilson spoke there on 31 Jan 1916. Caruso sang on 13 May 1919, Sousa played on 8 Nov 1924 and 21 Sept 1929, and Percy Grainger premiered Lincolnshire Posy there on 7 March 1937. The 8,000-strong anti-Prohibition rally of 1922 was here. On 24 Dec 1937 the Auditorium burned (extent of damage not on the page). (unverified — V_R27) It was the city's convention hall, circus ground and political arena through the decade. The odd detail: The building where the Hunter Guild's founder gave a speech with a bullet in him, and where — in the setting's own year of the break — Wilson spoke seven months before his removal (unverified — V_R27).",
