      "summary": "Carl August Hamann began an anatomical skeleton collection in 1893; T. Wingate Todd (born Sheffield 15 January 1885; Manchester medical degree 1907) took charge at Western Reserve in 1912 and assembled most of it — \"over 3000\" human skeletons and more than 900 primate skeletons — before his death on 28 December 1938. From 1920 he also directed the Hamann Museum of Comparative Anthropology and Anatomy, and in 1928 launched the Brush Foundation's study of 4,500 healthy Cleveland children. The collection moved later to the Cleveland Museum of Natural History (itself founded 1920, opened in a Euclid Avenue millionaires'-row mansion on 24 June 1922). The odd detail: Through the whole Torso decade, a university two miles from Kingsbury Run was quietly adding the skeletons of Cleveland's dead to a catalogue of more than three thousand — and its collector died four months after the last Torso bodies were found.",
