      "summary": "John Francis Noll, pastor of St. Mary's in Huntington, founded the weekly Our Sunday Visitor in 1912 and made it the most popular Catholic newsweekly in the country (a search summary says over a million circulation), donating its profits to religious, educational and charitable causes. Bishop of Fort Wayne from 1925, he kept editing the paper until his death on 31 July 1956; he built a preparatory seminary, high schools and an orphanage, and reorganised diocesan charities in the Depression. He fought the 1920s Klan's anti-Catholic campaign in print; the Klan held its own event in Fort Wayne in November 1923. He also \"collaborated with the anti-Semitic radio priest, Reverend Charles Coughlin\" (Wikipedia's wording). Hammond's Bishop Noll Institute is named for him. The odd detail: The best-selling Catholic paper in America was edited from a small-city rectory 25 miles from Fort Wayne, by a man who fought the Klan and allied with Coughlin.",
