      "summary": "Michael Kruszka (b. 28 Sept 1860, Słabomierz, Prussia) founded the Kuryer Polski in June 1888 — the first successful Polish daily in America; its 20th-anniversary edition (27 June 1908) ran 66 pages for a claimed 70,000 Milwaukee readers. He sat in the Assembly (1890) and the state Senate (1892–97, the first Polish state senator in the country) and fought the German-run archdiocese for Polish bishops. Archbishop Messmer funded the rival Nowiny Polskie in 1906, and on 12 Feb 1912 the archdiocese declared reading the Kuryer (or Kruszka's Chicago Dziennik Narodowy) a sin, denying absolution to subscribers. Rome answered by naming Edward Kozlowski Milwaukee's first Polish bishop in Nov 1913 — 50,000 celebrated at St. Stanislaus. The quarrel left three Polish National Catholic churches in the city. Kruszka died of a stroke on 2 Dec 1918; Mayor Hoan ordered the flags to half-staff. The Kuryer ran until 1962; in the 1930s it still faced the church's Nowiny Polskie across the South Side. The odd detail: For a generation a Milwaukee Pole had to choose between his newspaper and his confessor.",
