      "summary": "Captain Herman Schuenemann and his brother August had been selling Christmas trees off schooners at Chicago since the 1890s; August had already died when the S. Thal sank in a November 1898 storm. On 23 Nov 1912 the three-masted Rouse Simmons foundered in a gale bound for the Clark Street dock with about 5,500 trees; all 17 aboard died. His widow Barbara and their two daughters kept selling Christmas trees in Chicago (later bringing them by train); the lake trade itself died out by about 1920. In 1924 a fishing net brought up the captain's wallet. The wreck was found only in 1971 by diver Gordon Kent Bellrichard, trees still in the hold. The area is said to have frequent ghost sightings (none dated on the page — treat as later). The odd detail: In 1924 the lake gave back the captain's wallet — twelve years down — while his widow was still selling trees in the city.",
