      "summary": "The Loyal Order of Moose founded Mooseheart in 1913 as a home for widows and children of Moose members, nicknamed \"the Child City\"; it was originally open to white children only. Its driving force was James J. Davis, the Welsh-born \"Iron Puddler,\" who had joined the Moose in 1906 as its 247th member and built it into an international order as Director-General. Davis served as Secretary of Labor from March 5, 1921 to November 30, 1930 under three consecutive presidents, then as U.S. senator from Pennsylvania (1930–45). The odd detail: a whole town run by a fraternal order, with its own dormitories (a 1920 railroad magazine photographed them) — a closed community of orphans an hour from Chicago.",
