      "summary": "Anthony Fiala (1869–1950) was head of the art department of the Brooklyn Daily Eagle (1894–99) and commanded the Ziegler Polar Expedition (1903–05), which left Tromsø in July 1903, reached 82° 4' N in Franz Josef Land, and lost its ship America in Teplitz Bay; William S. Champ rescued the party at Cape Dillon in July 1905. He joined the Roosevelt party as quartermaster — a choice that, per a search summary, \"raised many eyebrows\". (unverified — V_M19: not on any page read; Roosevelt writes that Fiala ran the outfit \"with his usual efficiency\") Father John Augustine Zahm, C.S.C., a Holy Cross priest of Notre Dame University, first urged Roosevelt to make a South American trip and organised the original Amazon-cruise plan (the plan the River of Doubt trip replaced); he went as far as Utiarity Falls and turned back to Tapirapuã with Jacob Sigg in late January 1914. Fiala left the same place on 3 February with Lt. Lauriadó to descend the Papagaio–Juruena–Tapajós and lost his baggage in rapids. Neither ran the River of Doubt (Wikipedia's expedition page wrongly has both following the Ji-Paraná). The odd detail: the founder of the setting's mercenary Guild took toward the Amazon a Catholic priest — and an explorer who had lost a ship in the Arctic — both of whom left before the river.",
