      "summary": "Hermosillo had about 14,000 people at the start of the 20th century. After Madero's murder, Carranza (governor of Coahuila) sought refuge in Hermosillo and there launched the Constitutionalist movement — the city's claim to be \"the revolutionary capital of the country.\" Villa-loyal forces were expelled from the city by Gen. Manuel M. Diéguez — the same Diéguez who had been a Partido Liberal Mexicano organiser sentenced to fifteen years after Cananea (1906). From the late 19th century to the 1920s Chinese immigrants ran shoe and clothing factories in Hermosillo before the leagues (1925 Liga Nacionalista Pro-Raza) drove many out. The odd detail: the striker who went to prison for Cananea in 1906 became the general who drove Villa out of the state capital.",
