      "summary": "The city had 19,520 people in 1895 and 45,595 in early 1931; it had had a telephone since 1881, a railway since 16 September 1882, electric trams from August 1908 and the Chuvíscar dam (inaugurated 16 September 1908). Francisco Villa arrived on 8 December 1913 to take the provisional governorship and that month decreed the Bank of the State of Chihuahua, guaranteed by the estates he had confiscated (see the Terrazas–Creel entry); it closed for good in 1915. Carranza came from Juárez in 1914 and was cheered from the Government Palace balcony. Villa's widow María Luz Corral de Villa kept his Quinta Luz as a museum; the Museo Histórico de la Revolución preserves Villa's bullet-riddled Dodge (the car he was shot in at Parral in 1923, M01). Miguel Hidalgo was shot in the former Loreto convent's courtyard on 30 July 1811. The odd detail: the state bank that the rebel governor opened in 1913, with a landlord's cattle as collateral, lasted about as long as the collateral did — two years.",
