      "summary": "The Chicago, Duluth & Georgian Bay Transit Company (founded on $250,000 of preferred stock from about 35 investors) ran the lakes' great cruise pair: North American (launched 16 January 1913, 280 ft) and South American (launched 21 February 1914 at Ecorse, 2,662 GRT, 2,200-hp engine, three coal boilers) on week-long cruises from Chicago through the 1930s. On 9 September 1924 the South American burned in winter lay-up at Holland; she was rebuilt with a second stack and converted to oil. She ran until 1967, was sold to the Seafarers' union at Piney Point, and was scrapped at Baltimore in 1992. The odd detail: A Chicago summer institution — a white cruise ship out of the river to Mackinac — with a decade of Depression passengers who could still afford a week on the water. (Popular accounts of college-student crews and 1930s fares are NOT in the pages read — UNVERIFIED.)",
