      "summary": "Tagore first came to Chicago in January 1913 at the urging of Harriet Monroe, whose Poetry was printing him, and spoke at the University of Chicago; there he began his lifelong friendship with Harriet Vaughn Moody (1857–1932), widow of the poet William Vaughn Moody, which ran by letter and visit until her death. On his 1916–17 lecture tour he spent ten days at Urbana (22–31 Dec 1916) with Professor and Mrs. Seymour, lecturing on \"Western Nationalism\" and \"Nationalism in India,\" and looked at the moon through the Illinois Observatory's telescope, reportedly for the first time; he then fell ill and cancelled much of the tour. In March 1918 national newspapers reported that Tagore \"had been accused of plotting with the German government to support a revolution in India\"; Seymour defended him in the Daily Illini. His son Rathindranath, an Illinois alumnus, visited in February 1921; Sushama Tagore lectured there in September 1927. For 1925–1940 no Tagore stop in Chicago was found in pages read — within the setting's decade he is a Chicago memory and correspondent, not a visitor. The odd detail: The Nobel poet was publicly accused of being a German agent — in the one war the setting says Germany won.",
