      "synopsis": "America's first transcontinental motor road ran south of Chicago, not through it. Its showpiece was the \"Ideal Section\" between Dyer and Schererville, built 1922–23: 1.3 miles of concrete 40 ft wide and 10 in thick on a 110-ft right-of-way, curves banked for 35 mph, guard rails, no grade crossings, a footpath for pedestrians, paid largely by US Rubber's president C. B. Seger ($130,000) as a demonstration.",
