      "summary": "John D. Rockefeller's daughter, wife (1895–1921) of reaper heir Harold F. McCormick. In 1913 she went to Zürich for depression, was analysed by C. G. Jung, stayed eight years, became a lay Jungian analyst with 50-plus patients, paid to have Jung translated into English and bankrolled the Zürich Psychological Club. She paid James Joyce a monthly stipend Feb 1918–Oct 1919 while he wrote Ulysses, then cut him off. Divorced December 1921 (the hearing took fifty minutes). Back in Chicago she ran a rigid salon, sponsored the Woman's World's Fairs (1925–28), and in February 1923 told the press she was the reincarnation of Tutankhamun's child bride: \"I married King Tutankhamen when I was only sixteen years old. I was his first wife.\" She celebrated Christmas on December 15. In 1919 she gave the wedding-gift land that became Brookfield Zoo. Her fortune, moved into real estate through the Edith Rockefeller McCormick Trust (1923), was wrecked by the crash; she died of cancer on August 25, 1932, aged 59, and was buried at Graceland. The odd detail: A Rockefeller heiress, a trained analyst of the unconscious, who publicly claimed a pharaoh's marriage bed — and set her own date for Christmas.",
