      "summary": "The Afro-Cuban street comparsas (costumed carnival troupes with conga drums, historically tied to the colonial cabildos and Día de Reyes) were restricted and banned in the 1910s (1913–1916 in different accounts) as \"Afro-Cuban manifestations\". They were re-authorised in 1937 for the Prado celebrations — partly for the tourists — under a new organisation. The 1937 troupes had names a GM could use tomorrow: El Alacrán (the scorpion), Los Marqueses de Atarés, Los Componedores de Bateas, Los Chinos Buenos, Los Dandies, Las Boyeras, Los Guaracheros de Regla. The odd detail: a troupe named for the \"Marquises of Atarés\" — the fort where men were shot after surrender four years before — dancing down the Prado.",
