      "summary": "Leafy Anderson, born in Wisconsin in 1887, founded the Eternal Life Spiritualist Church in Chicago in 1913 and moved to New Orleans in 1919–20, where her spirit guide — Black Hawk, the Sauk war chief — and her mix of Protestant and Catholic imagery, mediumship and healing became the Spiritual church movement; she died in 1927 and her successors (Mother Catherine Seals of the Temple of the Innocent Blood) spread congregations to St. Louis, Indiana, Memphis, Little Rock and Pensacola. The Chicago branch of the movement got its cathedral when Rev. Clarence H. Cobbs founded the First Church of Deliverance on 8 May 1929 with nine members in his mother's basement; a decade later (1939) Walter T. Bailey — Illinois's first licensed Black architect — built him a streamlined white church on Wabash, a rare Moderne house of worship, with twin towers added in 1946. (From memory, UNVERIFIED this session: Cobbs broadcast a midnight radio service from 1934, was the first church to put a Hammond organ in the choir loft, and drew thousands with prophecy, \"blessed\" handkerchiefs and a flamboyant style the Baptists disliked.) The odd detail: A Sauk war chief as the spirit guide of Black Chicago's Spiritualist mothers; and a Moderne church that looks like a radio station — because it was one.",
