      "summary": "In November 1913 Terre Haute's mayor Donn M. Roberts ran a vote-fraud machine: 132-plus people were indicted and 100-plus convicted; the methods included a $6,000 slush fund from saloons and gambling houses, 2,500 false registration cards, \"floaters\" paid $5–$8 a vote, and one voter who cast 22 ballots. Roberts was arrested 17 January 1914 (bond $8,000), sentenced to six years at Fort Leavenworth and fined $2,000; Circuit Judge Eli Redman got five years and died in Leavenworth; Sheriff Dennis Shea got five years. A 1916 report counted 900 prostitutes and 400 madams, and four gambling houses on Wabash Avenue open from 9 a.m. to midnight. Roberts lost a comeback mayoral race in 1921 by 552 votes. Prohibition killed the breweries and fed the bootleggers. A city that had been a byword for open vice within living memory was the one that struck in 1935. The odd detail: A convicted mayor nearly returned to office eight years after Leavenworth, and the brothels were counted to the head — 900 women, 400 madams.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium — one local-history site; the federal record not checked.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The \"arch conspirator\" city is where a cult, or a Golden Circle cell, could trade in votes and vice with almost no shame; the 1935 strike is then the Chamber of Commerce's fear of losing the last thing it did not own.",
