      "summary": "McBride came to Cleveland in 1913 as circulation manager of the Cleveland News in the era when circulation men were chosen for being \"comfortable fighting with fists, clubs, knives, chains and ... handguns.\" In 1931 he took control of the Zone Cab Company, which merged with Yellow Cab into the city's dominant taxi fleet, with cab lines in Akron and Canton. By the late 1930s he was running a horse-racing wire for bookmakers — the business behind Continental Press and Empire News, worked out of Cleveland with Morris \"Mushy\" Wexler and Sam \"Gameboy\" Miller. A 1940 federal indictment named 18 people including McBride and Wexler; McBride was never arrested or prosecuted. In 1944–46 he founded the Cleveland Browns. The odd detail: The man who controlled Cleveland's cabs also controlled the wire that told every bookie in America who won — and then founded the city's football team.",
