      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Mallard Island",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Rainy Lake, near Ranier (Koochiching County, Minnesota), a mile south of the Canadian border",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Mallard%20Island%2C%20Rainy%20Lake%2C%20near%20Ranier%20%2C%20a%20mile%20south%20of%20the%20Canadian%20border%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Ernest Carl Oberholtzer (1884–1977, born in Davenport, Iowa) left Harvard after one year and first came to the border lakes in 1906; he made a 3,000-mile canoe voyage in 1909 and moved to Rainy Lake in 1913; he bought Mallard Island in 1922 and lived there over fifty years. With the Ojibwe guide Dedaabaswewidang (\"He Who Echoes Far Off,\" Billy Magee) he canoed 2,000 miles to Hudson Bay in 1912 (search summary). He founded the Quetico-Superior Council in 1928 to oppose the dam-and-industrialization plans of Edward Backus; the Shipstead-Newton-Nolan Act, signed by Herbert Hoover in 1930, barred logging and lake-level changes on federal land in the Rainy Lake watershed — the first statute in which Congress explicitly ordered federal land kept in its wilderness state (per a search summary). FDR named him first chairman of the President's Quetico-Superior Committee in 1934 (he held it until 1968); he was one of eight founding members of the Wilderness Society. He spoke fluent Ojibwe and never married. The odd detail: A Harvard-educated aesthete stopped a paper-and-power empire's dams — with a canoe, an island and an Ojibwe partner.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A man who speaks Ojibwe, lives alone on a rock in a border lake and has the ear of two presidents is a contact worth having: a Secret Service consultant on the northern border, or a witness to the Quetico's oddities — and a reminder that \"protecting the wilderness\" can be a cover for protecting something in it.",
