      "summary": "L'Italia (23 Mar 1913) reprinted the Record-Herald's account of the confession of forger Chick Nunn: \"a thoroughly organized Sicilian Mafiusi exists in Chicago,\" recruiting criminals \"of other nationalities,\" branching from Black Hand letters into forgery and arson. Its head was said to be \"ostensibly an excommunicated Italian priest, master of fourteen languages, owner of a macaroni factory, of several disreputable houses and interested in two banks,\" posing as a notary and bondsman, once jailed at Joliet for counterfeiting dimes — while the true leader \"attends no Camorrist meetings and attempts to dictate only in international relations with the 'Black Hand' of other countries.\" Six years earlier (16 Nov 1907) Unione Siciliana president Joseph Mirabella had called the Italian consul and prominent Italians to found a \"White Hand\" society to \"exterminate\" the Black Hand. The odd detail: a defrocked polyglot priest running extortion from a macaroni factory, with a hidden master who deals only in \"international relations.\"",
