      "summary": "Both the Revolution's first president (Francisco Madero, a Coahuilan landowner) and the man who took it over (Venustiano Carranza, another Coahuilan landowner and governor) came from Coahuila. After Madero's murder (M01) Carranza issued the Plan of Guadalupe on 26 March 1913 at the Hacienda de Guadalupe, calling for Huerta's ouster and constitutional government and making himself First Chief of the Constitutionalist Army; weeks later delegates met in Monclova, agreed to fight on and informed the newly inaugurated President Wilson of their declaration of war. The plan is the foundational paper of the Constitutionalists — the movement that wrote the 1917 Constitution (M01). Saltillo, the state capital, holds the 1746–1810 Cathedral of Santiago; Coahuila got its railway in 1880 and telegraph, telephone and street-lighting networks in 1890. The odd detail: the \"First Chief\" was made First Chief at a hacienda, not a capital — and the paper's foundation is a landowners' plan, to overthrow a landowner's murderer.",
