      "summary": "The parish was organised in 1896 by Carpatho-Rusyn immigrants — Cleveland's first Eastern Orthodox parish. Architect Frederick C. Baird designed the church (groundbreaking 1911, consecrated 20 July 1913, $70,000) from photographs of Moscow's Cathedral of Christ the Saviour, with one large, four medium and eight small copper onion domes for Christ and the twelve apostles; the iconostasis icons came from Russia. It hosted three All-American Sobors of the Russian Orthodox church in America — in 1919, 1934 and 1946. Its long-serving pastor was Fr. Jason Kappanadze (his likeness went into 1953 murals). The odd detail: A copy of Moscow's great cathedral, built from photographs, stood in Tremont in the 1930s — and the Moscow original was blown up in 1931 on Stalin's orders, leaving Cleveland's copy as the one still standing.",
