      "summary": "The former President left New York on 4 October 1913 with his son Kermit and the AMNH naturalists George K. Cherrie and Leo E. Miller. In Brazil, Foreign Minister Lauro Müller proposed replacing the original route with exploration of the unmapped Rio da Dúvida (River of Doubt), and the Brazilian Colonel Cândido Rondon, director of the Indian Protection Service, took charge. The party went Cáceres → Tapirapuã → across the Parecis plateau → the river, reaching it on 27 February 1914. The party that went downriver numbered 22, of whom three were Americans (Roosevelt, Kermit, Cherrie). Zahm turned back from Utiarity Falls in late January, Fiala left on 3 February to descend the Papagaio–Juruena separately, and Miller went with Capt. Amilcar's Ji-Paraná party — none of the three ran the River of Doubt. They lost canoes, starved, drowned a man (15 March), suffered a murder (early April), and Roosevelt's leg wound turned septic with fever. The expedition met rubber-tappers at the Aripuanã confluence on 15 April 1914 and was reunited with relief parties by about 26 April. Roosevelt was treated in Manaus and reached New York some weeks later; his health \"never fully recovered\". The river was renamed Rio Roosevelt in 1914. British explorer George Miller Dyott confirmed the discovery in 1927, and a 1992 expedition led by Charles Haskell and Elizabeth McKnight, with Roosevelt's great-grandson Tweed, ran the river in 33 days. The odd detail: Roosevelt's Amazon trip was the Guild's founding voyage in the setting and, in the real record, a trip whose overland approach included a Notre Dame priest who had urged it (Zahm, who turned back before the river), an Arctic explorer as quartermaster (Fiala, who left on 3 February — neither ran the River of Doubt), and a Brazilian colonel whose creed was never to kill.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the outline (three agreeing sources); the end date differs by source (15 vs 26 April).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — every member of the party has a possible Guild dimension (next entries). Because the setting says he \"stumbles into something bigger,\" the what is the owner's to decide; the real river offers three unexplained things: being watched without being seen (the Cinta Larga, per later retellings; \"a dozen days\" was unsupported), a murder that led to an abandoned man, and a river whose true course was unknown to Brazil's own maps.",
