      "summary": "Hurricane-force winds on four of the five lakes, peaking 9 Nov; waves over 35 feet with rogues near 50; 19 ships destroyed and 19 stranded, over 250 dead, ~68,300 tons of cargo lost — the worst Great Lakes disaster on record. Lake Huron took the Charles S. Price (28), Argus (28), Isaac M. Scott (28) and the Canadian James Carruthers (22). Cleveland got 22 inches of snow with 79 mph gusts; streetcar crews were stranded in their cars for days. The Price was found floating upside down on 10 Nov and went unidentified until 15 Nov — \"the mystery ship\" was front-page news across the country. The Weather Bureau could neither predict the storm nor its wind shifts. The odd detail: a great freighter floating keel-up for five days off Port Huron with no one able to say which ship it was; legend adds that some of the Price's dead were found wearing life jackets from the Regina (legend / UNVERIFIED — not in the page read).",
