      "summary": "The 71-year-old Bierce left Washington in October 1913, crossed at El Paso, attached himself to Villa's army as an observer and saw the battle of Tierra Blanca. His last confirmed letter, dated Chihuahua 26 December 1913 to Blanche Partington, ends: \"As to me, I leave here tomorrow for an unknown destination.\" To his niece he had written that if he was \"stood up against a Mexican stone wall and shot to rags\" he would think it \"a pretty good way to depart this life.\" His daughter Helen asked the State Department to search; Army chief of staff Hugh L. Scott and consular officials investigated and Felix Sommerfeld, Villa's U.S. agent, looked too; Villa's men gave contradictory accounts and the outbreak of the Great War stopped the search. The competing endings: (1) Sierra Mojada: a Mexican oral tradition, recorded by a retired priest James Lienert, holds he was shot by a firing squad in the town cemetery (Lienert put up a gravestone in 2004 — a cenotaph exists); (2) soldier of fortune \"Tex\" O'Reilly claimed federales shot him in a cantina in Sierra Mojada as a spy and that he found \"two old empty envelopes left by the Americano\"; (3) killed at Ojinaga in the battle across from Presidio, Texas (unverified — V_M06: not on the Wikipedia or LitHub pages read); (4) suicide at the Grand Canyon (Partington's own view — the \"letter to Partington\" was never found, only a secretary's notebook copy) (unverified — V_M06 as attributed: Wikipedia credits the skeptic Joe Nickell and LitHub says \"some scholars\"; not Partington); (5) an elderly man calling himself \"Ambrosia\" who died of pneumonia at Marfa, Texas, 17 January 1914, per a hitchhiker's tale collected by Jake Silverstein (2002) [the date and Silverstein confirmed on LitHub; UNVERIFIED — V_M06 that it was a \"hitchhiker's tale\": LitHub says a 2002 letter]. Carlos Fuentes fictionalised the story in The Old Gringo (1985; film 1989). The odd detail: Bierce had himself written \"To be a Gringo in Mexico — ah, that is euthanasia!\" and his most famous story turns on a man hanged whose escape is a dream — a writer whose vanishing was pre-written.",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the itinerary and the letters; every ending is unverified, and the Sierra Mojada, O'Reilly and Marfa accounts are tradition/anecdote (labelled as such). • (3) killed at Ojinaga in the battle across from Presidio, Texas (unverified — V_M06: not on the Wikipedia or LitHub pages read); • (4) suicide at the Grand Canyon (Partington's own view — the \"letter to Partington\" was never found, only a secretary's notebook copy) (unverified — V_M06 as attributed: Wikipedia credits the skeptic Joe Nickell and LitHub says \"some scholars\"; not Partington); • (5) an elderly man calling himself \"Ambrosia\" who died of pneumonia at Marfa, Texas, 17 January 1914, per a hitchhiker's tale collected by Jake Silverstein (2002) [the date and Silverstein confirmed on LitHub; UNVERIFIED — V_M06 that it was a \"hitchhiker's tale\": LitHub says a 2002 letter].",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — five deaths for one man, in five places, each told by a different witness — a lovely three-layers case: the period says a cranky old man vanished; the folklore says four different graves; the files say why Villa's men (or Villa's German friend) gave \"contradictory accounts\". A Section 13 cold file that starts with a genuine American literary wound.",
