      "summary": "El Paso became the first American city to restrict cannabis (dated 1914 in some sources, 1915 in others). The city's stated worry was that the drug caused violent behaviour among Mexicans, \"Negroes, prostitutes, pimps and a criminal class of whites.\" The setting for the ordinance was a 1913 incident in which a man killed a police officer in Ciudad Juárez while chasing an El Paso couple. Chief Deputy Stanley Good of the El Paso Sheriff's Department said the drug made users \"devoid of fear\" and that \"a large percentage of the crimes committed are by men saturated with the drug.\" A 1917 report described El Paso as a \"hot-bed of marihuana fiends.\" Texas banned transfers in 1919, possession with intent to sell in 1923, and made possession a felony in 1931, with life sentences possible for small amounts. The odd detail: the country's first marijuana law was a border city's answer to a Revolution-era crime committed across the river — and was justified by what \"Mexicans\" did on the drug.",
