      "summary": "The four days at Veracruz produced 56 Medals of Honor (Wikipedia's list: 1 Army, 9 Marine Corps, 46 Navy; CIMSEC says 55 for the sea services) — more than for any single action before or since. One reason: it was the first action in which Navy and Marine officers were eligible. Recipients included the landing's admiral Frank Friday Fletcher, his nephew Lt. Frank Jack Fletcher (later the carrier admiral of our world's Pacific war), Lt. Col. Wendell C. Neville, Maj. Randolph Berkeley, the future naval-aviation chief William A. Moffett, and Chief Boatswain John C. McCloy, who already held one from the Boxer Rebellion. Maj. Smedley Butler got one — and sent it back, writing that he \"had done nothing which entitled me to this supreme decoration.\" The Navy Department ordered him to keep it and to wear it on all appropriate occasions. He won a second in Haiti the next year (Part G). The odd detail: the most decorated fight in American history up to then was a four-day street battle against cadets, snipers and released convicts — and the servicemen themselves called the medals cheap.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
