      "summary": "Born at Schleiz, Thuringia, in 1855, in America from 1872, a St. Louis German-language editor, Bartholdt sat in Congress as a Republican from Missouri 1893–1915 (chairman of Immigration and of Public Buildings), was president of the Interparliamentary Union (1904) and proposed a \"League of Peace\" of federated nations, and in 1914 wanted Esperanto taught in American schools. In the war he led the American Independence Union for an embargo on arms to all belligerents, introduced the embargo bills, and did not stand again in 1914; his memoir is From Steerage to Congress (1930); he died in St. Louis in 1932. The odd detail: the man who led the German-American peace lobby was also America's leading Esperantist and a founder of the idea of a League of Nations.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
