      "summary": "The American military government collected Veracruz's customs duties and taxes for seven months. Ordered to take every fund \"from whatever source derived\" with him, Funston landed at Galveston on 26 November 1914 and put the Mexican money in the First National Bank's strong room. On 1 January 1915 Maj. Blanton Winship shipped it to the New Orleans subtreasury: one sealed field safe with 1,539,051.20 Mexican pesos, plus one sealed wooden box with 1,065,000 pesos — 2,604,051.20 pesos in all, with the military treasurer's books. Secretary of War Lindley Garrison was still accounting for it to the State Department on 26 January 1916. (Who finally received it — Carranza's government? — was not settled this session.) The odd detail: a port's entire revenue sat for over a year in a sealed field safe and a wooden box in a New Orleans vault while two Mexican governments each claimed to be Mexico.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (figures from the primary document read this session). Winship's later Puerto Rico governorship is from general knowledge — UNVERIFIED this session.",
