      "summary": "The Paris American colony founded the American Ambulance in 1914. A. Piatt Andrew, made Inspector of Ambulances in early 1915, persuaded the French Army's Capt. Aimé Doumenc to let volunteers drive at the front, and on 15 April 1915 the French created the American Ambulance Field Service under French Army command. Sections of about 30 drivers each were assigned to individual French divisions, driving Model T ambulances bought from the Ford plant at Levallois-Perret; some drivers wore acting-officer uniform and rank. By 1917 there were 33 sections and about 1,200 volunteers; with transport work added it passed 2,500 by autumn 1917. 127 AFS men were killed by the war's end; many won the Croix de Guerre. The odd detail: a college boy in an AFS section wore a French uniform, took French orders and carried French wounded — and swore no oath, which is why his papers stayed clean (§31's rule, exactly).",
