      "summary": "Richard Norton, archaeologist and son of the Harvard professor Charles Eliot Norton, founded the American Volunteer Motor Ambulance Corps in London in autumn 1914 with two cars and four drivers, backed by the British Red Cross and St John Ambulance. The banker Henry Herman Harjes of Morgan, Harjes & Co. supplied money and vehicles, and it became the Norton-Harjes Ambulance Corps. By 1917 it had 13 sections, 600 volunteer drivers and 300 ambulances. Its volunteers included Robert W. Service (1915; Rhymes of a Red Cross Man, 1916), John Dos Passos, Archibald MacLeish and E. E. Cummings — whose arrest by the French and internment became The Enormous Room (1922). The odd detail: the corps that carried the Allied wounded was financed through a J. P. Morgan partner house — the bank whose loans to the Allies the setting's America never extended.",
