      "summary": "Kurt Albert Jahnke (b. 17 February 1882, Gnesen, Prussia) emigrated to America in 1899, naturalised, and served in the US Marines in the Philippines. From August 1914 he worked sabotage and intelligence out of San Francisco under Consul-General Franz Bopp; with Witzke he is credited with the Mare Island explosion and \"likely\" Black Tom (Jersey City, 30 July 1916). After April 1917 the pair ran German naval sabotage from Mexico City. A radio-history paper (Bart Lee, 2006, citing its sources) says German Telefunken engineers who had got out of the Sayville, Long Island station (call sign WSL) set up a 100 kW spark transmitter in Mexico to talk to Nauen; US Navy direction-finders in Los Angeles and San Diego fixed it at Chapultepec; and in 1918 the British Secret Service sent two agents who smashed its Audion valves at Ixtapalapa, \"putting the German agent Herr Kurt Jahnke out of business\". (unverified — V_M13: no other source found for the British sabotage.) He resurfaced in the late 1930s running the \"Jahnke Büro\", a private intelligence service reporting to Rudolf Hess; SMERSH took him in April 1945 and he was shot in Moscow on 22 April 1950. The odd detail: the Kaiser's master saboteur in Mexico had worn the uniform of the US Marine Corps — the service that garrisons the setting's hemisphere.",
