      "summary": "Heinrich von Eckardt, a Baltic German (law at Jena, 1881–85), had served in Istanbul, Tehran, Belgrade, Athens, Havana and Cetinje before becoming Germany's minister to Mexico in 1914, a post he held to 1918. In private he called Carranza's ministries \"prototypes of vulgarity and depravity\" and Carranza a \"pedant mediocrity\". The Zimmermann Telegram (16 Jan 1917) reached him via Ambassador Bernstorff in Washington on 19 January; he put it to Foreign Secretary Cándido Aguilar about a month later. Aguilar was sympathetic; Aguilar and Carranza declined, partly because the telegram became public so soon. Eckardt died at Jena on 3 March 1944. The odd detail: the man who carried Germany's offer of Texas to Carranza despised him — and had served in Havana before Mexico.",
