      "summary": "Franz Wachendorf (b. Koblenz, 1884; d. 1938 (unverified — V_M13: no death year on the Wikipedia page)) took the name \"Horst von der Goltz\" while serving in Pancho Villa's army, where he became a major. In 1914 German officials sent him to blow up Canada's Welland Canal, the Great Lakes' link past Niagara; \"Papen gave Goltz $500 and a recommendation for Consul Carl A. Luederitz in Baltimore\" for forged papers; the job was abandoned in the face of Canadian guards. Arrested in London in 1914 for failing to register, he traded information to British naval intelligence, came back to the US in March 1916 and testified against the German saboteurs and diplomats — then wrote My Adventures as a German Secret Service Agent (1917) and played himself in the Committee on Public Information's propaganda film The Prussian Cur (1918). The odd detail: a Villista major who tried to dynamite a Great Lakes canal, then turned state's evidence and starred as himself in a propaganda film.",
