      "summary": "The prioress, María Elías del Santísimo Sacramento (a Mme. María Thiery), wrote that \"our temples are closed and our churches are profaned … entering on horseback, trampling the relics and throwing the hosts on the floor,\" that \"in some churches the Carrancistas have pretended to say Mass and done obscene things,\" and that nuns \"are taken to the barracks.\" The letter was circulated as an open letter and, in Canudas's telling, helped make the religious conflict international. The historian Canudas, writing from the Constitutionalist side, notes the claim and answers it with the counter-charge that the clergy backed Huerta. Separately, a Mexican Church history summarised in a 2007 review says the Church's temples, seminaries and schools were confiscated between 1914 and 1919 and used as barracks, stables and stores, and that Obregón returned them after taking office in 1920 — a claim I have only in a search summary, not a page I read. The odd detail: the loudest account of Carrancista desecration reached the United States from a Mexican convent in Havana — exile networks already ran through the city the setting puts at the centre of the Golden Circle's map.",
