      "summary": "Kelley, a Canadian-born priest, founded the Catholic Church Extension Society in 1905 with Chicago's Archbishop James Quigley; it moved to Chicago in January 1907 and ran Extension magazine (over 3 million subscribers in his time) and the famous railway chapel cars. When the Constitutionalist revolution drove all of Mexico's bishops into exile in 1914, Kelley became their representative in the United States. On 23 February 1915 he wrote to President Wilson that the exiled bishops wanted no intervention, only guarantees of liberty of conscience, and that the Constitutionalist movement (he named Gen. Antonio I. Villarreal) was \"antireligious and persecuting\" — \"economic and agrarian questions … were only side issues; the great issue of Mexican politics was that of liberty of conscience.\" He thought Villa and Zapata might be reachable; Carranza not. The same year he published, in Chicago, The Book of Red and Yellow: Being a Story of Blood and a Yellow Streak (1915) — affidavits of outrages against priests and nuns by Constitutionalist troops, sent also to the Secretary of State. At the 1919 Paris Peace Conference he represented the Mexican bishops (and quietly worked on the Roman Question with Orlando). He became Bishop of Oklahoma in 1924 and wrote Blood-Drenched Altars (1935, see Part D). The odd detail: the man who spoke for Mexico's exiled episcopate in Washington and at Versailles did it from a Chicago office that also ran chapels on railway wheels — the Church's own rolling network across the American West.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (the 1915 letter is in FRUS; the book's title/date from catalogue listings). The Society's exact 1915 street address not confirmed this session.",
