      "summary": "Founded 1914 as a society dancing-and-dining club run by women: Mrs. Joseph G. Coleman president, Mrs. Howard Linn vice-president, Robert G. McGann secretary, Robert H. McCormick treasurer, with Mrs. John Alden Carpenter (Rue Winterbotham Carpenter, later of the Arts Club), Arthur Aldis, Harold A. Howard, Eames MacVeagh and Honoré Palmer among the governors. The first house — pink-and-white stucco over tile, Directoire interiors, an 80-by-40-foot dance hall, card rooms, dressing rooms, kitchen — was designed by Ernest Walker, Harold Howard and Mrs. Carpenter herself and opened with 400 members. In 1916 members made a silent film, Cousin Jim, for charity. When the Palmer estate sold the ground in 1927–28 the building came down and the club moved to a new marbled, pastel Art Deco house at 195 E. Delaware. The annual December Ball — first Friday of December, 250 to dinner and 400 more for dancing — was the fixed point of the society year; during Prohibition the club was raided for serving liquor (Chicagology, from Tribune files; date of the raid not given). On repeal night 1933 the Casino was one of the society rooms that drank legally at midnight (see R16). The odd detail: A club founded, designed and governed by women, with a bylaw that a non-member may enter only once a month and never unescorted — and a Prohibition raid on the most respectable address in the city.",
