      "summary": "The Bohemian National Alliance was founded in Chicago on September 6, 1914, to rally Czech-Americans for independence from Austria-Hungary; it later became the Czech National Alliance (unverified — V_R10), published The Bohemian Review from 1917, and dissolved in 1918 once Czechoslovakia was independent. Tomáš Masaryk, who had lectured at the University of Chicago in 1902 and 1907 through his friend, Chicago industrialist Charles R. Crane, was welcomed by more than 150,000 Chicagoans on May 5, 1918; on October 18, 1918, he gave Wilson the Washington Declaration of independence. Anton Cermak, born in Kladno, Bohemia, on May 9, 1873, beat William Hale Thompson for mayor with 58 percent on April 6, 1931 (\"it's true I didn't come over on the Mayflower, but I came over as soon as I could\"), and was shot at Miami's Bayfront Park on February 15, 1933 by Giuseppe Zangara, dying March 6. The odd detail: Chicago was the capital of a country in exile — 150,000 people in the streets for a professor who did not yet have a state.",
