      "summary": "Crowley reached New York on the Lusitania in October 1914, freelanced for Vanity Fair and for the astrologer Evangeline Adams, and from January 1915 wrote propaganda for the pro-German weekly The Fatherland. He toured the West with Jeanne Robert Foster (Vancouver, Detroit, Seattle, San Francisco, Santa Cruz, Los Angeles, San Diego, Tijuana, the Grand Canyon); in Detroit he \"experimented with peyote at Parke-Davis.\" At Evangeline Adams's cabin on Lake Pasquaney in 1916 he proclaimed himself Master Therion; he wintered in New Orleans (\"his favourite US city\") in 1916–17, made the 1918 Esopus Island retreat, took Leah Hirsig as his Scarlet Woman, exhibited paintings at the Liberal Club, and left for London in December 1919. He never returned to the United States. So: no Crowley in Chicago in the 1930s — any Chicago Crowley is a rumour, a book (R05's de Laurence pirated him) or an impostor. The odd detail: The Great Beast's American income came partly from German propaganda — in a world where Germany won, he backed the winner.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the itinerary; the Detroit O.T.O./Ryerson scandal of 1919 (widely told) was not in the page read — UNVERIFIED.",
