      "summary": "In March 1914 (5–7 March, per Wikipedia's Butler page) Butler ran a covert spy mission as \"Mr. Johnson\", gathering intelligence on Mexican positions; on 22 April 1914 he won his first Medal of Honor at Veracruz. On 17 November 1915 he led the assault on Fort Rivière in Haiti (second Medal of Honor) and then organised and commanded the Gendarmerie d'Haïti; he later said he and his men \"hunted the Cacos like pigs\" (Wikipedia). In October 1912 he had commanded the attack on Coyotepe Hill in Nicaragua, and wrote privately to his parents that American investors were financing the revolution for profit. From 7 January 1924 to 23 December 1925 he was Director of Public Safety of Philadelphia under Mayor W. Freeland Kendrick; within 48 hours he raided over 900 speakeasies and imposed military-style discipline. In November 1934, before the McCormack–Dickstein committee, he testified that bond salesman Gerald P. MacGuire told him wealthy businessmen planned a fascist takeover; the committee's 1935 report said there was \"no question that these attempts were discussed, were planned, and might have been placed in execution when and if the financial backers deemed it expedient\" and that it verified all pertinent statements but one (\"alarmingly true\" is the New York Times's November 1934 paraphrase, not the report's wording); the accused denied it under oath and historians remain sceptical. He published Old Gimlet Eye (1933, with Lowell Thomas) and War Is a Racket (1935), and died on 21 June 1940 at the Naval Hospital, Philadelphia. The odd detail: the Marine who took the Caribbean's forts ran the anti-Prohibition raids of a big American city — and in 1934 told Congress that bond salesmen were plotting a coup.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (Wikipedia); M05 already carries the \"gangster for capitalism\" quote and Coyotepe. • commanding Camp Pontanezen at Brest, 1918 — from memory, UNVERIFIED",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the general as the Guild's honest enemy: PEOPLE_OF_THE_TIMELINE lists him as a lead. A retired Marine who names conspirators in Congress is a Section 13 or Secret Service contact who has already been ignored once; the Guild, which \"keeps no records\", considers his testimony the exact thing to avoid.",
