      "summary": "George Sylvester Viereck (Munich 1884 – 1962) came to America at eleven, and in 1907 was \"the most widely-discussed young literary man in the United States\" (Saturday Evening Post) for his poems Nineveh. In the Great War he edited The Fatherland, a German-sponsored weekly, and defended Germany even after the Lusitania; in 1919 he was expelled from clubs and the Poetry Society of America. In the 1930s he became a Nazi propagandist — he spoke to 20,000 at Madison Square Garden in 1934 and ran a scheme paying congressmen to put German propaganda into the Congressional Record and mail it under their franks — which is how he came to fund Senator Lundeen's \"Islands for War Debts\" committee (M10). Convicted under the Foreign Agents Registration Act in 1942, he served 1943–47 and wrote Men Into Beasts. The odd detail: the congressman's frank — the free-postage privilege — was the delivery system for a foreign government's propaganda.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"islands for debts\" as a German idea laundered through American senators — which makes the fire sale the Golden Circle's map drawn with the Kaiser's pen. Who is using whom is the three-layer question.",
