      "summary": "The family moved from La Crosse to the isolated farm in 1914 (unverified — V2_R06). Augusta Gein, nominally Lutheran and ferociously devout, read her two sons the Old Testament and Revelation every afternoon — death, murder, divine retribution — and taught them that women were sin; George Gein drank. Through the 1930s Ed, shy, prone to random laughter, was a good reader who did odd jobs and baby-sat for neighbours, who thought him harmless. George died of heart failure on 1 Apr 1940; Henry died on 16 May 1944 in a marsh fire (unverified — V2_R06) on the property, found face down (Ed, who had reported him missing, led the searchers straight to the body (unverified — V2_R06); bruises on the head; no autopsy; \"asphyxiation\"). Augusta died 29 Dec 1945 after two strokes, the second after seeing a neighbour's \"harlot.\" Everything Gein is known for came after 1945 — out of the setting's range — and is only noted here. The odd detail: Every afternoon in the 1930s (unverified — V2_R06), Revelation read aloud in a farmhouse kitchen with no electricity (unverified — V_R06), to two grown men.",
