      "summary": "A local-history feature in Milenio (Tampico) says no place in Mexico was \"so famous for its prostitution and good atmosphere in its bars, cantinas, dance halls and gambling houses as Tampico,\" with the boom from 1914 and the decline after 1925. Named houses include the Montparnasse and the Tívoli — the latter with \"una enorme cara de diablo\" (an enormous devil's face) as its façade — and establishments run by women (María de la O; Casa Lola). Foreign women came with the oil money; Agustín Lara played and sang there. The interim governor Emilio Portes Gil wrote to President Calles about the violence. Relocation was first discussed in 1938; the last houses were closed in February 1959. The odd detail: the Tívoli's door was a giant devil's mouth — you entered the oil port's best-known dance hall through the Devil's face.",
