      "summary": "After a failed siege (31 December–4 January) in which 130 captured revolutionaries were executed by the Federals, Villa arrived on 5 January and on the evening of 10 January his Division of the North (~5,500) penetrated the town through the cemetery and drove the Federal army (Gen. Salvador Mercado, ~4,500 including at least 12 generals) toward the Rio Grande. 3,352 Federal soldiers (including 8 generals) and 1,607 women crossed into Texas and were interned, moved to Marfa and then to Fort Bliss. Villa's men took 14 cannons, 100,000 rounds and 2,000 Mauser rifles for 35 dead. Five days before (5 January) Villa had signed a contract with the Mutual Film Company to allow cameramen to film his operations, for a $25,000 advance. The odd detail: the Revolution's best-known battle-film contract was signed during the siege — and the battle's finish was fought through a cemetery.",
