      "summary": "In early April 1914, with the party starving, a camarada named Julio de Lima shot and killed Manoel Vicente da Paixão, the porters' supervisor (Roosevelt calls him a corporal and acting sergeant of the engineer corps), who had caught him stealing food. After the expedition failed to capture him, they abandoned him in the jungle. Roosevelt later described the affair in Through the Brazilian Wilderness and it is discussed in Candice Millard's The River of Doubt. The odd detail: the only murder on the expedition was a food theft in the weeks the party was near starvation — and the killer was left in the forest rather than brought out for trial.",
