      "summary": "Around noon Commodore Manuel Azueta came to the academy and urged its roughly 90 cadets and instructors to fight; the director, Capt. Rafael Carrión, organised the defence. At about 3:20 p.m. American picket boats engaged the academy with small cannon; about 3:30 p.m. the cruiser USS Prairie answered the cadets' rifle fire with 3-inch guns. Cadet Virgilio Uribe was mortally wounded. The commodore's son, former cadet and artillery Lt. José Azueta, 19, worked a machine gun at the street corner until hit three times (two in the legs, one in the left arm). At 7 p.m. the commodore ordered the academy abandoned; the cadets climbed out through the shattered dining-room windows and marched along the railway, reaching Tejería at 12:30 a.m. on 22 April. When Admiral Fletcher sent American doctors to the wounded Azueta, he threw them out — \"let those dogs get out, I don't want to see them.\" He died on 10 May 1914. Spanish Wikipedia notes a myth-correction: the building was defended on 22 April by armed civilians and militia, not by the cadets, who had already left. The odd detail: the dying lieutenant refused the enemy's medicine — and the national legend credits the cadets with a second day's fight they were not there for.",
