      "summary": "Constitutionalist troops under Gen. Álvaro Obregón entered Guadalajara on 8 July 1914 and Gen. Manuel M. Diéguez took the state government. Soldiers occupied churches including the Cathedral, dug in them \"looking for weapons\", and — according to the cathedral chapter's memorial to Carranza — scattered consecrated hosts, took the ciboria and monstrances, stripped the jewels and vessels, and exhumed and desecrated the remains of bishops from the Cathedral's tombs (the soldiers apparently expected treasure). The chapter put the Cathedral's loss at over 800,000 pesos of objects. On 21 July 1914 more than a hundred priests were jailed; Canudas counts over 120 priests of all nationalities in the Escobedo Penitentiary, held incommunicado on charges of conspiracy and hiding arms. Soldiers are said to have ridden into the Cathedral on horseback, and Diéguez is said to have demolished the Cathedral's atrium that year (both from a search summary, not a page I could read). Foreign priests (Jesuits, Marists, Salesians and others) were given five days to leave in early August 1914 and were put on a train to Manzanillo. The churches were reopened when Villa took Guadalajara on 17 December 1914. The odd detail: the soldiers were digging in the Cathedral for weapons, and what they opened were the tombs of bishops; the sources I read say only that the remains were \"desecrated,\" not what the tombs held.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the occupation, the priests jailed and the expulsions; Medium on the 800,000-peso figure (the chapter's own estimate) and on the horse detail (a search summary, not a page I read).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"Secular destruction of consecrated ground\" (ALTERNATE_HISTORY §29.3) with a tomb in it: soldiers who came looking for weapons opened a bishop's tomb, and a canon writing the chapter's inventory of losses knows one entry is not on the treasure list. An IHS archivist finds it in the 1914 memorial in the 1930s. Treat the soldiers as ordinary men in a violent year, not as a cult.",
