      "summary": "On the night of 1–2 August 1914 the consulate had still had no official word of Germany's declaration of war on Russia; the Consul-General, Geissler, was away on leave and Vice-Consul Baron Kurt von Reiswitz was in charge. He told the Sonntagpost that \"as soon as he receives official notice of the declaration of war, he will issue the call to service.\" Reservists gathered in halls across German Chicago to discuss \"ways and means of getting back to Europe,\" some proposing to go by way of Holland because \"German reservists will very likely be halted by the English or French.\" On 3 Aug the Abendpost reckoned fewer than 1,000 men in the city actually liable to serve; Reiswitz said he regretted missing his own regiment. By 6 Aug the Tribune reported about 4,000 men registered at the German and Austrian consulates together — and that both consuls, Reiswitz and the Austrian Hugo Sylvestin, had given up: Reiswitz \"discourag[ed] German reservists from making futile efforts to reach their native country\" since the British would stop the ships. Reiswitz remained in charge of the Chicago consulate through the neutrality years and was named in the 1917 Chicago \"Hindu conspiracy\" indictment (see next entries). The odd detail: four thousand Chicagoans signed up at the German and Austro-Hungarian consulates together to fight for the Kaiser and the Emperor-King in five days, and were sent home by their own consuls because the Royal Navy was in the way.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Section 13 has a photostat of the 1914 reservist register. So does the Golden Circle. Names on it keep turning up at the scenes of things.",
