      "summary": "By the Bryan–Chamorro Treaty (signed 5 August 1914, ratified June 1916) Nicaragua received $3 million — most of it redirected to U.S. creditors to manage its debt — and the U.S. got \"full rights over any future canal built through Nicaragua,\" a renewable 99-year option on a naval base in the Gulf of Fonseca, and a renewable 99-year lease on the Great and Little Corn Islands in the Caribbean. El Salvador and Costa Rica contested the Fonseca clause; the Central American Court of Justice ruled in their favour in 1917, the U.S. ignored it, and the Court collapsed in 1918. The Wilson administration had proposed a Platt-style intervention clause; the Senate struck it. The treaty was abrogated only in 1970. The odd detail: the U.S. paid for a canal it never intended to build — the purchase was of an option, to keep anyone else from digging one.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a canal option that keeps a rival canal from ever being dug is a seal — something whose whole purpose is to keep a thing shut. A shut Nicaragua route as a real place a cult would want opened.",
