      "summary": "Colonel Eleuterio Ávila, governor in 1914, decreed peonage abolished on 11 September 1914 (\"little effect\"); on 8 February 1915 governor Toribio V. de los Santos decreed it again, forbidding hacienda tokens. Carranza then sent General Salvador Alvarado, appointed governor on 27 February 1915, who reached Mérida by 19 March 1915 and put down the separatist rising of Abel Ortiz Argumedo. He cancelled workers' debts, banned confinement and corporal punishment, revived the Comisión Reguladora del Mercado de Henequén (November 1915) to control the crop's sale, and built schools and libraries. He also \"closed churches, confiscating icons and relics\" (Wikipedia) — see 6. He was a \"socialist\" in the pre-1917 sense of a liberal modernizer (Hulme), and left the henequales alone. He served as Treasury Secretary in 1920, backed the De la Huerta rebellion, and was killed on 10 June 1924. The odd detail: the same general who freed the peons closed the churches — and a scholarly article dates Yucatán's post-revolutionary prohibition (\"Dry Law\") to 1915–35, so the temperance and the anticlericalism arrive together.",
