      "summary": "The American Red Cross sent the chartered SS Red Cross — the Hamburg-America liner Hamburg under a new name and flag, commanded by Capt. Armistead Rust, U.S.N. (ret.) — from New York in mid-September 1914 with some 170 surgeons and nurses (one account: 126 nurses, 30 physicians) in units of a surgeon director, two assistants and twelve nurses. Only American-born staff were sent, to avoid passport and neutrality questions. The units for Germany and Austria were given a special train to Berlin; others went to Britain, France and Russia, and a Serbian unit via Salonika. The nurses' superintendent was Helen Scott Hay — born near Lanark, Illinois (1869), trained at the Illinois Training School for Nurses in Chicago (1895), nursing superintendent at Cook County Hospital 1906–12 — who became matron of the American Red Cross hospital at Kiev (1914–15) and then founded a nurses' training school in Bulgaria at the Tsaritsa's invitation. The odd detail: American Red Cross nurses worked in German military hospitals in Silesia in 1914–15 — the period-true way for a Chicago nurse to be a Europe veteran on the winning side without ever swearing an oath.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on Hay and the ship; Medium on the exact city list (Red Cross history via search).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — for a player: \"I nursed in Gleiwitz.\" A Chicago nurse with a German Red Cross commendation is welcome in the setting's German consulate and suspect everywhere else. Cook County Hospital is a real 1930s place she could still work.",
