      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Medinah Temple",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Cass (now Wabash), Ontario & Erie Streets (capacity given as 4,000–5,000)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Medinah%20Temple%2C%20Cass%20%2C%20Ontario%20%26%20Erie%20Streets"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The German and Austro-Hungarian Relief Association booked Medinah Temple for Thursday 10 Dec 1914 at 8 p.m. (free admission; reserved seats $1; societies told to bring their flags to the stage) to hear Dr. Bernhard Dernburg, the former Colonial Secretary who ran Berlin's propaganda bureau in America in 1914–15, \"representing the German Red Cross.\" The hall filled before the doors could cope. Ferdinand Walther opened; Harry Rubens in the chair called it a \"war of liberation\" like 1813; Prof. George L. Scherger: \"It is not we who are violating America's neutrality, but the lying propaganda press of this country\"; Rabbi Emil G. Hirsch of Sinai: \"Deutschtum is rather a matter of mind and spirit\" than of race, and Germany's armaments were self-preservation; Dernburg on Belgium: \"Two duties which we had to perform were opposing each other. The less important of the two had to make way for the greater one,\" and the Reichstag \"controlled all appropriations.\" Red Cross girls sold cornflowers and ribbons; Chicago was said to lead the nation in relief giving. Alongside Dernburg, Prof. Eugen Kühnemann of Breslau (Harvard exchange professor 1906–07 and 1908–09; first Carl Schurz professor at Wisconsin, 1912) toured 137 cities in 36 states, giving 121 lectures in English and 275 in German between 1914 and 1917 — two of them the Chicago Kaiser's-birthday speeches above. In Jan 1919 the same Abendpost listed Dernburg among German officials who deserved prison. The odd detail: the rabbi of Chicago's leading Reform temple shared a stage with the Kaiser's propaganda chief and defined Deutschtum for him.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the cornflower — the Kaiser's flower — worn in Chicago lapels since 1914 as the badge of the German loyalist; in the 1930s it is how the consulate's friends know each other in a room.",
