      "summary": "Opened in 1915 under Dr. Theodore B. Sachs as its first president, the city's own TB colony held 950 beds — about 300 in infirmary buildings for the bedridden and 650 in cottages for walking patients — in 28 original cottages (20 adult, 8 children) plus 12 later ones; in 1923 Jens Jensen laid out a children's campground and pool in the northwest corner. It ran through the 1970s (redeveloped as North Park Village from 1977; National Register 2019). In the 1930s it was where the city's consumptives went to rest, eat and, often, die, in a walled prairie-style village at the edge of the city. The odd detail: a walled village of nearly a thousand of the tubercular, with its own children's camp and pool, inside the city limits.",
