      "summary": "David Hamilton Jackson (born 1884) organised the general strike of 24 January – 26 February 1916 (40 days in Wikipedia's count) of St. Croix's cane workers, who were then paid about 20 cents a day (10–20 cents in one source); the strike crippled a dozen estates and stopped the great Bethlehem Sugar Factory at harvest [estates and factory detail UNVERIFIED — V_M05], and the agreement with the Planters Association raised wages to 35 cents (a 75 percent raise), with a nine-hour day. In 1915 he travelled to Denmark and argued for repeal of a 1779 censorship ordinance that had kept independent newspapers from being published; he published The Herald, the first V.I. newspaper independent of the crown (first issue 1 November 1915), and the story is that its first edition was tacked to the Grove Place baobab, the crowd began a spontaneous celebration, a bull was slaughtered and roast beef and bread distributed. 1 November is now Liberty Day, also called D. Hamilton Jackson Day or Bull and Bread Day. The odd detail: a freedom-of-the-press day marked by roasting a bull.",
