      "summary": "In May 1915 Albert Kaltschmidt — a wealthy German-born industrialist, president of the Marine City Salt company — called a meeting of pro-German men in Detroit to attack munitions production for the Allies. He supplied 156 sticks of dynamite; on June 21, 1915, Charles F. Respa bombed the Peabody Overall Co. factory in Walkerville, Ontario, while a second bomb at the Windsor Armoury failed to explode, sparing about 200 soldiers. Tried in 1917 (the charge included a plot on the Detroit Screw Works), Kaltschmidt got four years at Leavenworth and a $20,000 fine from Judge Arthur J. Tuttle; he was deported in February 1921, came back in 1927 and was deported again. In the same war, per the Hindu–German Conspiracy record, Herambalal Gupta and \"the German consul at Chicago\" sent German operatives George Paul Boehm, Henry Schult and Albert Wehde toward Siam via Manila to train Indian revolutionaries. The odd detail: a salt-company president ran a bombing cell across the Detroit River; the night watchman at the target factory, William Lefler, was one of his men.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — living memory of Germany as patron of other people's revolutions in the Great Lakes — the old Detroit cell's surviving members are exactly the people a 1930s German consul would call on.",
