      "summary": "Anton Casimir Dilger (b. 1884, Front Royal, Virginia), raised in Germany from age nine, a Heidelberg/Munich doctor (doctorate summa cum laude, 1912; grandson of the anatomist Friedrich Tiedemann), came back to the United States in 1915 carrying cultures of anthrax and glanders. With his brother Carl he grew them in a house laboratory in Chevy Chase, and Baltimore stevedores were given bottles of liquid culture to infect horses bound for the Allies. Under suspicion he fled to Mexico under the alias \"Delmar\", then to Spain, where he died of influenza in October 1918. The programme also reached Romania, Norway, Spain and South America. The odd detail: the Kaiser's germ-warfare doctor was a Virginian, and his last hiding place in the hemisphere was Mexico.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
