      "summary": "In March 1919 the Abendpost argued against a bill to deport the interned. Among the \"prominent\" internees it named Frederico Stallfuhrt, \"allegedly a spy in the service of Rintelen\" (Franz von Rintelen, the naval-intelligence officer who ran sabotage from New York in 1915 — general knowledge), who was trying to get out by claiming Mexican citizenship; and H. A. Seebohn, manager of a chemical factory \"now operated under the supervision of the U.S. Alien Property Custodian.\" The camps held bankers, orchestra conductors, journalists and ships' officers; \"only a few of those who are in Government custody are considered to be dangerous.\" Other Abendpost items give the scale: \"a half million enemy aliens were registered as such\" under wartime rules, and \"only one out of every seventeen thousand\" was interned; in 1933 the paper still remembered the wartime fingerprinting of enemy aliens as the precedent for fingerprinting the liquor trade. The odd detail: a Rintelen agent trying to walk out of an American internment camp on Mexican papers — in the setting, where Mexico is an American occupation zone, that is not a footnote but a plot.",
