      "summary": "Berlin's war on British India ran through American consulates. The German consul at Chicago (Reiswitz) worked with the Bengali revolutionary Herambalal Gupta to send three Chicago Germans — George Paul Boehm, Henry Schult and Albert Wehde — to Siam by way of Manila to train Indian recruits at a jungle base near the Siam–Burma border for Ghadar volunteers coming from China and Canada. Papen bought $200,000 of small arms through Krupp's agent Hans Tauscher for the Annie Larsen plan, and in April 1915 arranged a second shipment through Tauscher (7,300 Springfields, 1,930 pistols, 10 Gatling guns, nearly 3,000,000 cartridges); the schooner Annie Larsen was seized at Grays Harbor on 29 June 1915. The San Francisco trial (20 Nov 1917–24 Apr 1918; 29 convicted; Consul General Franz Bopp among them) ended with Ram Singh shooting Ram Chandra dead in the courtroom and Marshal Holohan shooting Ram Singh. Wehde, a Chicago jeweller, was sentenced to three years for \"supplying weapons to East Indian revolutionaries … violating U.S. neutrality laws,\" served nine months at Leavenworth in 1921 and was pardoned by Harding (BENDS: Harding is never president in the setting — Leonard Wood holds office 1921–27). In prison he watched an Oklahoma official fabricate fingerprints to claim a $33,000 reward, set out to prove prints could be forged, \"succeeded in devising a method by which he is able to obtain a deceiving forgery,\" and co-wrote Finger-Prints Can Be Forged (with the journalist John Nicholas Beffel, 1924) (unverified — V_R23); defence lawyers used it — an accused murderer at Morrison, Illinois, was acquitted, and courts in New Orleans and Jersey City refused to convict on prints alone. The Chicago indictment itself (Oct 1917, naming Reiswitz in absentia with Gustav Jacobsen and Boehm — general knowledge) could not be confirmed this session: UNVERIFIED. The odd detail: the Chicago jeweller who ran guns for the Kaiser to Burma came out of Leavenworth as America's first fingerprint-forgery expert, and by 1926 the Abendpost could write that \"the assertion that fingerprints are positive proof of guilt has been fairly well shaken by Wehde.\"",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the Siam mission, the SF trial and Wehde's prison and book (Wikipedia + FLPS); the Chicago trial's particulars are UNVERIFIED. • … be forged, \"succeeded in devising a method by which he is able to obtain a deceiving forgery,\" and co-wrote Finger-Prints Can Be Forged (with the journalist John Nicholas Beffel, 1924) (unverified — V_R23); • The Chicago indictment itself (Oct 1917, naming Reiswitz in absentia with Gustav Jacobsen and Boehm — general knowledge) could not be confirmed this session: UNVERIFIED.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Section 13's forensic men learn in 1926 that fingerprints can be forged — from a German agent's book. When a vampire's prints turn up at a scene, is it the vampire or Wehde's method? The Secret Service has a different question: who paid for the Springfields.",
