      "summary": "On January 4, 1913, Henry Bourne Joy of Packard and 108 leading citizens re-founded the 1887 Detroit Athletic Club as the auto men's house, and Albert Kahn gave them a Roman palazzo (the Palazzo Borghese for the body, the Farnese's windows for the fourth-floor Grill Room) in 1915. The Grosse Pointe Yacht Club — an ice-boat club of 1910, a yacht club from 1918 — opened Guy Lowell's Mediterranean clubhouse with its Venetian campanile on July 4, 1929 (Lowell had died at sea before finishing the plans; the budget overran; the big dining room seated 250) — and the Depression foreclosed it \"despite attempts from the club to solicit more members\"; the members bought it back from creditors at the start of the war. The odd detail: A yacht club opened four months before the crash and lost its own clubhouse; the auto barons kept their palazzo.",
