      "summary": "Born in Chicago 25 March 1881, Gray studied at the University of Chicago 1897–1903 with honours in astronomy, mathematics, Greek and Latin (Phi Beta Kappa). In October 1915, aged 34, she walked out of city life to live alone in a beach shack; reporters who found her after her first winter named her \"Diana of the Dunes\". Around 1920–21 she took up with Paul Wilson, a fisherman and carpenter. In 1922 a man's body was found near Wren's Nest; Wilson was suspected and cleared; a deputy sheriff, Eugene Frank, accused the pair of robberies and in a confrontation Wilson was shot in the foot and Gray's skull was fractured. A manuscript she had written vanished and was never recovered. Folklore says her ghost haunts the shore (the 1982 compilation Hoosier Folk Legends has an entry \"Ghost of Diana of the Dunes\"). The odd detail: A Phi Beta Kappa astronomer lived naked-to-the-weather in a driftwood hut, a stranger's corpse turned up at her door, and her one manuscript disappeared.",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on her life to 1922. ⟨resolved in R36 (2026-09-30): she died 9 February 1925 — the source Wikipedia itself cites says 1925⟩ CONFLICT on the death (as first researched): the Wikipedia article read today says she \"died at Ogden Dunes of a heart attack on March 8, 1930\" (aged 48); the brief and many popular accounts say February 1925 (often \"uremic poisoning\", after the beating). Could not settle it this session (loc.gov and Google Books rate-limited). Treat the date as UNVERIFIED until a newspaper obituary is read. (unverified — V_R29: a second attempt — nps.gov, in.gov, and a FindAGrave search — also failed to settle it; Wikipedia's 8 March 1930 is the only figure with a live citation, \"1925\" remains unsourced popular tradition)",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The lost manuscript is the lead — an astronomer's notes on what she watched in the night sky over the lake for years. Whether she died in 1925 or 1930 is itself a mystery a GM can use: the record disagrees because there were two deaths.",
