      "summary": "While Indiana (1907), Wisconsin, Michigan and dozens of other states passed compulsory sterilisation laws, Illinois never did. Sterilisation bills were introduced in 1925, 1929 and 1933 and failed each time. The only reported sterilisation in Illinois was in 1916, when a 65-year-old prisoner was offered the choice between prison and the operation. Instead, in May 1915 Illinois passed House Bills 654 and 655: HB 655 let a court permanently institutionalise anyone \"a respectable expert deemed feebleminded\" (on petition, with a court hearing and a physician's examination), and HB 654 empowered the state to build institutions to hold them. Illinois eugenicists argued that sexual segregation — locking the \"unfit\" away for life — was more effective and more humane than the knife. Chicago was nonetheless a eugenics centre: the Municipal Court's Psychopathic Laboratory printed Laughlin's model sterilisation law in 1922 (see R18). The odd detail: The state that refused to sterilise instead made the sentence indefinite: a 1915 hearing could put a woman away for the whole of her fertile life.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the no-law, the three bills and the 1915 acts (academic compilation); whether sterilisations happened informally in 1930s Illinois hospitals UNVERIFIED. • Bill sponsors not named in the source read — UNVERIFIED.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The 1933 bill's failure is the kind of thing the Golden Circle notices. Indefinite commitment on one expert's word is a tool any faction can use; in Umbra, the \"respectable expert\" is whoever signs the form — and a vampire's family doctor is very respectable.",
