      "summary": "Born 2 July 1872 on Manhattan's Lower East Side (German-American); ordained in Rome 8 June 1895 and said his first Mass at St. Peter's tomb. Auxiliary bishop of Brooklyn 1909; named Archbishop of Chicago 9 Dec 1915, installed 9 Feb 1916. Pius XI made him a cardinal-priest on 24 March 1924 (titular church Santa Maria del Popolo, Rome) — with his elevation Chicago became the first archdiocese west of the Allegheny Mountains to have a cardinal. He centralised the archdiocese's schools, built Quigley Preparatory Seminary in the city and the enormous St. Mary of the Lake Seminary in the village of Area (renamed Mundelein in 1924/25 in his honour). A friend of FDR and backer of the New Deal and of trade unions. Died 2 Oct 1939 at the seminary at 67 (\"heart disease\"); entombed behind the main altar of the seminary chapel. The odd detail: A whole town was renamed after him in his lifetime, and he is buried behind the altar of his own chapel.",
