      "summary": "Russell and Rowena Jelliffe, Oberlin graduates, opened \"The Neighborhood Association\" in 1915 for people of all races and creeds; plays began in 1917 and the Gilpin Players became the country's serious Black theatre company. Langston Hughes, a Cleveland Central High boy, made it his stage: the \"Hughes Era\" (1936–39) premiered When the Jack Hollers and Troubled Island (both 1936) among others, and Hughes called it \"the very nearest thing\" to a real Black theatre in America; in 1931 he and Zora Neale Hurston tried to stage Mule Bone there and fell out for life. The name Karamu (\"place of joyful gathering,\" Swahili) became official in 1941 (used earlier for the theatre — from memory; a 1939 fire destroyed the theatre building — from memory, UNVERIFIED). The odd detail: The Hughes–Hurston friendship ended over a Cleveland settlement-house production that never opened.",
