      "summary": "Social workers Russell and Rowena Jelliffe founded a Neighborhood Association in 1915, known as the Playhouse Settlement; interracial casts from 1917; the Dumas Dramatic Club (1920) became the Gilpin Players (1922). The theatre acquired in 1927 was named Karamu, Swahili for \"a place of joyful meeting\" (the whole settlement took the name in 1941). Langston Hughes worked with it through the 1930s, premiering several plays. A 1939 fire destroyed the theatre; the next year (1940) its dance troupe was praised by Life at the New York World's Fair. It was rebuilt in 1949 with help from Leonard Hanna Jr. and the Rockefeller Foundation. The odd detail: The theatre burned in 1939 and its dancers were praised by Life at the World's Fair the next year, 1940 — the company performing in New York while its home was ash.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
