      "summary": "By 1915 South Works had 10,000 workers and Mexicans were \"flowing in\" (Chicago Reader/Springs summaries). The FLPS gives a street-level picture: Alfonso Reyes lived at 8425 Mackinaw Ave.; Albert Velasco at 8722 Buffalo Ave.; Juvencio Horta at 9232 Burley Ave.; Joseph García's meat market stood at 9022 Burley Ave.; the mill's own Illinois Steel Company Hospital took the wounded; the Walkowiak mortuary (8803 Commercial Ave.) and Murphy's Funeral Parlor handled the dead. Mill wages ran 42.5–54 cents an hour. Irondale was the older name of the South Deering neighbourhood; residents counted all the Southeast Side neighbourhoods as one Mexican community (search summary of a South Chicago study). The colony's organisations included the Sociedad Mutualista Obreros Libres Mexicanos (Brandon Ave.), the Mexican Band of South Chicago, a Masonic lodge (entry 14) and, from May 1930, an auxiliary of the Mexican Blue Cross (entry 18). The Jackson Baur WPA survey of ~2,000 South Chicago Mexican families is in entry 46. The odd detail: the address book of one small killing is the address book of the colony — a boarding house, a meat market, a funeral parlour, all within a mile of the mill gate.",
