      "summary": "Anthony J. Schuler, SJ, first Bishop of El Paso (appointed 17 June 1915, retired 29 November 1942), made the border diocese a refuge \"especially during the Mexican Revolution, when thousands of refugees, amongst them persecuted bishops and priests, looked for shelter.\" Mexican Jesuits, exiled since 1917, moved their novitiate to El Paso in 1925 under him; he funded a Perpetual Adoration monastery joined by exiled nuns from Mexico City and Guadalajara; the Visualizing the Cristero War project credits him with $120,000 in assistance to refugees. Young names El Paso among the four main cities of the Cristero diaspora, and the Tenorio plan expected armed bands from El Paso. The odd detail: a Jesuit bishop running a diocese whose refugee priests could see the country they had fled from the cathedral steps.",
