      "summary": "Paul Peter Rhode was born 18 September 1871 in Wejherowo (Neustadt), Prussia, of Kashubian parents; he came to Chicago at nine, was ordained 17 June 1894, and was consecrated Auxiliary Bishop of Chicago on 29 July 1908 (appointed 22 May 1908) — the first Pole and Kashubian raised to an American bishopric; the case for a Polish bishop was reportedly carried to Pope Pius X. On 15 July 1915 he became sixth Bishop of Green Bay, serving until his death on 3 March 1945 aged 73. In thirty years he founded 10 parishes and 19 parochial schools and set up the diocesan Catholic Charities and a department of education. The odd detail: The bishop who watched over Wisconsin's Belgian shrine (he dedicated it in 1942) was a Chicago-raised Kashub who had been born a Prussian.",
