      "summary": "A manifesto dated San Diego, Texas, 6 January 1915 (actually signed in a Monterrey jail by Huertista prisoners) called for a \"Liberating Army of Races and Peoples\" to seize Arizona, New Mexico, California, Colorado and Texas as a republic, and for the execution of all white men over sixteen — with Germans notably exempted from the killing. Signers/leaders named include Basilio Ramos Jr. (captured in Brownsville), Luis de la Rosa (a former Brownsville deputy sheriff) and Aniceto Pizaña (associate of Ricardo Flores Magón). Raids began in July 1915 (five months late), peaking in August–September: 30 raids, 21 Americans killed, the Norias Ranch attack of 8 August (five raiders killed) and a train robbery at Tandy's Station (five killed, six wounded). Carranza's government supplied about half the raiders, then pulled back after Wilson recognised it on 19 October 1915. The reprisals: federal officials estimated 300+ Mexican Americans killed in late 1915–16, and the 1919 Canales inquiry put the total killed by law enforcement in 1914–19 at 300 to 5,000. The odd detail: the plan's authors spared German Americans from the massacre they proposed — which is why Americans at the time suspected German agents, though no evidence was ever found.",
