      "summary": "With Germany five months into the war, \"hundreds of German-Americans from various professions and social classes\" met at the Germania Club for a \"solemn gathering.\" Club president H. O. Lang opened; the main speaker was Prof. Eugen Kühnemann of the University of Breslau, then touring America for the German cause: \"Never, in all the world's history, has a responsibility such as Kaiser Wilhelm II has to bear been placed on the shoulders of one man\"; the war \"has united the people.\" The Chicago Symphony Orchestra under Albert Ulrich played Weber's Jubilee Overture and the Radetzky March; the Germania Male Chorus led the hall in a Reformation hymn that \"made a particularly deep impression.\" The banker Fritz von Frantzius presented a life-size portrait of the Kaiser in uniform, painted in Berlin ten years earlier by the Chicago artist Frank A. Werner. Mayor Carter Harrison declined to attend; his rival Robert M. Sweitzer (German-American, county clerk, running for mayor that spring) was there. The paper's line: \"The Germans, in making the Kaiser's birthday a national holiday, do not honor a man; they honor themselves.\" The odd detail: the city's symphony orchestra played the Kaiser's birthday party in wartime, and the mayor's absence was news.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the Werner portrait hangs in the Germania Club through the 1930s. Something is painted under it. The night of the Kaiser's birthday, when the club is full and the lights are low for the tableau, is when the vampire \"conspiring in high society\" holds its own court upstairs.",
