      "summary": "Edward Mandell Stone, Harvard A.B. 1907 (class of 1908), law student and one-time private secretary in Buenos Aires, was living in France when war came and enlisted at once as a private in the Foreign Legion (2nd Regiment, Battalion C). In October 1914 he went into the line near Craonne with a machine-gun section; on 15 February 1915 he was hit by shrapnel on guard duty and died at Romilly on 27 February — usually counted the first American killed in the Great War. Search summaries (Western Front Association / Wikipedia) give his birth as 5 January 1888 in Chicago, son of the Chicago industrialist Henry Baldwin Stone ⟨V_M14: per the same memoir a Burlington railroad general manager, later president of the Chicago Telephone Company⟩. The odd detail: a classmate's epitaph — \"a fellow, happy, rich, strong, with a promising life before him, who did not hesitate to volunteer\" — for the first American of a war America never joined.",
