      "summary": "Franz von Rintelen landed on 3 April 1915 on a false Swiss passport as \"Emil V. Gasche\", posed as \"Frederick Hansen\", and ran sabotage through front companies (the Bridgeport Projectile Company; the Transatlantic Trust Co. at 57 William Street), $500,000 through the \"Labor's National Peace Council\" to foment munitions strikes, and Dr. Walter Scheele's time-delay incendiary \"cigars\" — reportedly $10 million of material on 36 ships. Victoriano Huerta, exiled via Jamaica, Britain and Spain, reached New York in April 1915; he and Rintelen negotiated money for weapons and U-boat landings on the Mexican coast, Huerta offering to make war on the United States. US agents tapped and recorded the phone calls. On 27 June 1915 Huerta took the train toward Newman, New Mexico, to meet Pascual Orozco and armed men, and was arrested by an Army colonel for sedition; rearrested 3 July; jailed again 8 December; died 13 January 1916 (cirrhosis or cancer; \"poisoning by the US was widely suspected\"). Buried in El Paso (Wikipedia: Evergreen Cemetery). Rintelen was caught by the British in August 1915, interned at Donington Hall, extradited, and served time in Atlanta; he wrote The Dark Invader (1933), moved to Britain, opposed the Nazis and died in London 30 May 1949. The odd detail: the ex-dictator's German money talks happened in a Fifth Avenue hotel on a tapped line — the Americans knew the plot in the words of its authors.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the dates and arrest; Medium on the sabotage figures (Wikipedia citing later accounts). (Wikipedia's Rintelen page says Southampton; other accounts say Falmouth — UNVERIFIED.)",
