      "summary": "In the battles of Celaya (April 1915) Álvaro Obregón broke Villa's Division of the North with trenches, wire and machine guns learned from the European papers. In the prolonged fighting at Trinidad and Santa Ana del Conde (29 April – 5 June 1915) a shell blew off his right arm; he nearly died. The arm was embalmed and kept, and from the monument's opening (1935) on the site of his assassination it was displayed in a jar of formaldehyde until it was incinerated in 1989 and replaced with a marble sculpture. The odd detail: Obregón's own joke: his men could not find the arm until a comrade held up a gold azteca coin — and the arm \"came skipping up… reached up and grasped it in its fingers.\"",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the arm and its display/incineration; the date 3 June is the usual one (the article gives the 29 April – 5 June battle range).",
