      "summary": "Ohio's hospital for the criminally insane opened in July 1915, built for 1,100 patients and starting with one. It took dangerous and homicidal patients from other state hospitals and mentally ill inmates from Ohio's prisons — including, by extension, men from the Ohio Penitentiary. For much of its history it functioned largely as a warehouse; patients staged dramatic protests, and there were more than 300 escapes by 1978. George Remus was committed there 30 December 1927 and freed in June 1928 (above). The odd detail: The famous inmate planted a fruit orchard on the grounds for the others — and it was named for him.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (search summaries only; no page read in full).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Ohio has one place where the state hides the men it will not hang. A Section 13 asset kept \"insane\" is the FBI's favourite loose end; Lima is where the players go to interview him.",
