      "summary": "Salvador Alvarado was the Constitutionalist governor and military commander of the peninsula; his arrival \"sharpened\" the religious conflict (Canudas). Under his predecessor, in 1914, 37 foreign secular priests and 8 regulars had been expelled within five days, and by 1918 state law let only six priests function (Signos Históricos). On 24 September 1915 after speeches in the main plaza against \"fanaticism,\" a crowd — the historian Ángel Gutiérrez Romero says dockworkers from Progreso — broke into the Cathedral. They smashed the 17th–18th-century baroque and churrigueresque altarpieces, the treasury and the Walcker organ (German-built, installed 1902), wrecked the pulpit and its stair, and beheaded or burned the images of San Ildefonso (patron), San Juan Nepomuceno, Our Lady of Mercy, the Purísima, the Santo Niño de Atocha, Sts. Peter, Paul and Andrew, San Isidro and the Trinity. In the chapel of the Cristo de las Ampollas — the Cathedral's wealthiest and best-loved image — the municipal band, forced by the crowd, is said to have played La Cucaracha while the mob tried to burn the crucifix; when it would not take fire they threw it in the street. Police took it to the military headquarters and its fate is unknown: the cathedral's own history says the original was destroyed, burned or stolen, and Archbishop Martín Tritschler had a copy carved in Querétaro and blessed it on 24 September 1919, the fourth anniversary of the sack (PorEsto; the copy was paid for by Rafael Quintero). Three days after the sack the building was handed to canon José Correa and Father Pablo Ortiz. In 1916 the Chapels of San José and of the Rosary were demolished to separate the Cathedral from the Episcopal Palace; the gap is now the pedestrian Pasaje de la Revolución. The odd detail: the legend of the image is that it was carved for the church at Ichmul by a stranger sculptor (the folk story says an angel), and survived that church's fire with blisters in the paint — ampollas — and here it was, 270 years later, a crowd unable to burn it again. (Legend, from the Ichmul tradition; not a documented event.)",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the date and the sack; Medium on the Christ's fate — sources disagree (destroyed/unknown vs. \"survived\"); the La Cucaracha detail is from one local history.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the IHS's own long file on a crucifix that was already said to have come through a fire and a stranger's hands, and was lost in 1915 with the police station as its last known address. The 1919 copy from Querétaro is the visible image; someone in the 1930s asks which one. Report the devotion with respect; the hook is the object's history, not the people who venerate it.",
