      "summary": "Rodolfo Fierro, Villa's most feared officer — el carnicero (\"the butcher\") — is credited with leaping onto a moving locomotive at Tierra Blanca (1913) and running across the boxcar roofs to shoot the boilerman and conductor, and with shooting captured prisoners as they ran across a field. On 14 October 1915, marching toward Cananea with Villa's column, he was thrown from his horse into quicksand at the Casas Grandes lagoon; the accepted account is that a belt heavy with gold coins pulled him under. His body was reportedly retrieved by the Japanese photographer Kingo Nonaka and buried in Chihuahua. (Fierro is usually named as the man who killed the rancher Benton.) The odd detail: the most feared executioner of the Revolution drowned in a lagoon in the desert because of his own gold.",
