      "summary": "Delegates of the Slovak League of America (Ivan Daxner, Albert Pavol Mamatey) and the Czech National Association (Ludvík Fisher, Josef Tvrzický-Kramer) met in the freethinkers' hall on Broadway and signed a five-point programme: independence for the Czech and Slovak lands, a federal union with Slovak autonomy (its own legislature and administration), free elections, democratic government, and amendment only with both parties' consent. Mamatey was elected chairman and Emanuel Voska vice-chairman. It was superseded by the Pittsburgh Agreement of May 1918, and the real Czechoslovakia was proclaimed 28 October 1918. About 360 Czech and Slovak volunteers from the Cleveland area joined the Czechoslovak Legions. The odd detail: One of the founding documents of a European country was signed in a Cleveland dance-and-lecture hall on Broadway — by Americans, about a state that did not yet exist.",
