      "summary": "On the night of 1 November 1915 Pancho Villa (about 15,000 men) attacked the Carrancista garrison of Plutarco Elías Calles (about 6,500) at Agua Prieta. As the Villista cavalry charged, two searchlights lit the field for Calles's machine guns; most sources put the lights on the Mexican side, but Villa was certain they were on the American side. The US had allowed Carranza to move troops and supplies by rail through Arizona — and refused Villa's request (at Naco) to send his wounded home the same way. The repulse broke Villa's grip on Sonora and helped Carranza win American recognition. The odd detail: Villa never forgave the searchlights; four months later he raided Columbus, New Mexico (March 1916) — some period voices called it revenge. In the setting this is the cause of the war.",
