      "summary": "On 12 November 1915 Dr. Harry J. Haiselden, chief surgeon of Chicago's German-American Hospital, examined a newborn with severe disabilities, Allan Bollinger, decided that surgery could save his life — and advised the parents to do nothing. The baby died five days later. Haiselden did the same with Grace Werder (2 Dec 1915), the daughter of William and Eva Meter (24 July 1917) and Paul Hodzima (Nov 1917, possibly after narcotics he prescribed). A coroner's jury cleared him. He then co-wrote and starred in a silent film dramatising the case, The Black Stork (1917); the National Board of Review nearly banned it and demanded 18 changes; re-released in 1918 as Are You Fit to Marry?, it \"continued to appear in theaters until as late as 1942.\" The Chicago Medical Society moved against him and he was eventually expelled over his eugenics lectures. He died in Havana on 18 June 1919 of a cerebral haemorrhage. The odd detail: A Chicago surgeon who let babies die played himself in a film about it — and the film was still in cinemas all through the 1930s.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. • name UNVERIFIED here • it was renamed Grant Hospital in 1918 — UNVERIFIED here — in our history because of the war; in Umbra's timeline it likely kept its German name: BENDS — check",
