      "id": "M14-029",
      "title": "The 97th Battalion CEF — the first \"American Legion\"",
      "date_start": "1915-12-22",
      "date_end": "1915-12-22",
      "date_label": "authorised 22 December 1915",
      "category": "The Southern War's real soldiers, and the volunteers who went to Europe (1914–1917)",
      "tags": [
        "Southern",
        "soldiers",
        "Battalion CEF",
        "American Legion"
      ],
      "people": [
        "A. B. Clarke",
        "W. L. Jolly"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Toronto",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(recruited and mobilised)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Toronto"
        },
        {
          "name": "Aldershot",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Nova Scotia",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Aldershot%2C%20Nova%20Scotia%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "England",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=England%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Canada raised battalions of Americans resident in Canada (and, quietly, recruited from the States). The 97th Battalion (American Legion), CEF was authorised on 22 December 1915 in Toronto under Lt.-Col. A. B. Clarke. \"The American government protested the title of 'American Legion,'\" the country being neutral; the protest held the unit at Aldershot, N.S., in May 1916, officers resigned and men deserted, and it could proceed only after dropping the name. On 19 September 1916, 31 officers and 798 other ranks sailed on the Olympic; within a month the battalion was broken up for reinforcements (270 to the Royal Canadian Regiment's depot, 428 to Princess Patricia's). The 211th, 212th and 237th were also \"American Legion\" battalions. The odd detail: the first organisation called the American Legion [\"first\" UNVERIFIED — V_M14: the cited page says only that Washington protested the title] was a Canadian battalion of Americans who had sworn to the King — and Washington objected to its name.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. • the first organisation called the American Legion [\"first\" UNVERIFIED — V_M14: the cited page says only that Washington protested the title] was a Canadian battalion of Americans who had sworn to the King — and Washington objected to its name.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — for a player: the man who cannot join the VFW. A 97th veteran swore to George V, lost his papers (Part D), fought for a Britain that lost, and calls himself \"American Legion\" in a country where the phrase means nothing — or means Canada.",
