      "summary": "The occupation revived an old Haitian road-labour obligation (the corvée) to build a highway system for military transport, since Haiti's revenue was going to debt service. Johnson (1920) writes that \"the Occupation seized men wherever it could find them\", forcibly taken \"to toil for months\", worked under armed guards, herded into compounds at night, and that \"those attempting to escape were shot\"; families were not told where they had gone. The corvée was abolished on 1 October 1918 but kept in practice in places. Michel-Rolph Trouillot's estimate, cited by Sciences Po's mass-violence network, is about 5,500 deaths in the labour camps. The State Department's history names forced labour, press censorship and racial segregation as the causes of the peasant rising. The odd detail: the escape attempts are why the caco war's second round happened — men fled the road gangs into the hills and joined the rebels, and Charlemagne Péralte himself had been sentenced to labour in convict dress.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the practice and the abolition date; the death toll is an estimate (Trouillot 1990). The revived law's date (an 1864 rural code is often cited) — UNVERIFIED this session.",
