      "summary": "The Drake (Benjamin Marshall & Charles Fox, 1920, $10 million; WGN's first studios when WDAP was renamed in 1924) was the Gold Coast's ballroom and debutante hotel through the decade (its Cape Cod Room bar still carries DiMaggio and Monroe's initials; the \"Woman in Red\" ghost legend attaches to a 1920 New Year's Eve — folklore, not on the page). The Edgewater Beach (Marshall & Fox, June 3, 1916; 19-storey tower 1924; the Passaggio) had a 1,200-foot private beach, seaplane service downtown, its own radio station WEBH, orchestras in the Marine Dining Room in winter and dancing on the Beach Walk under the stars in summer. Presidents and stars stayed; it closed abruptly December 21, 1967 and was demolished 1969–71. The odd detail: In summer the North Side's rich danced on a boardwalk over the lake with the band's broadcast going out over the water.",
