      "summary": "Lothar Witzke (b. 15 May 1895, Kreis Koschmin, Prussia) was a lieutenant on the cruiser SMS Dresden — the ship that carried Huerta to Jamaica (Part A). After her scuttling he escaped Chilean internment and reached San Francisco in May 1916 as a seaman on the SS Calusa, joining Kurt Jahnke under Consul-General Franz Bopp. US intelligence tied him to the Mare Island munitions explosion (July 1917 per his page); his part in Black Tom (30 July 1916) is disputed. The pair moved to Mexico City after April 1917. He was arrested at 10 a.m. on 1 February 1918 near Nogales, Arizona, travelling as \"Pablo Waberski\", with a cryptogram sewn into his jacket sleeve; cryptographers working with Herbert Yardley broke it; a military tribunal at Fort Sam Houston sentenced him to death — the setting's point of interest, since he is usually described as the only German spy sentenced to death in the US in the Great War (the page read did not say so — UNVERIFIED). Wilson commuted it to life on 27 May 1920; he was pardoned and released 26 September 1923 and deported to Berlin. The odd detail: the 424-letter cipher sewn into his clothes was broken after three days of continuous work by two University of Chicago Chaucer scholars — Professor John Matthews Manly (51, joined MI-8 in October 1917) and his former student Edith Rickert — and it proved that Germany still wanted Mexico in the war. A Chicago hook for the core book.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on arrest, alias, sentence and release; the Mare Island date conflicts (Witzke page: July 1917; Jahnke page: April 1917) — UNVERIFIED which. • the page read did not say so — UNVERIFIED",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Witzke is the thread that runs from the Dresden (Huerta's exile) to a cipher in a sleeve on the Arizona line. A Section 13 file on \"Waberski\" should be thicker than the War Department's, because Section 13 knows what else was in the sleeve.",
