      "summary": "William Grant Seaman (native of Wakarusa, Indiana; DePauw B.A., Boston University Ph.D.; nicknamed \"Sunny Jim\") came to Gary in 1916 at the request of Chicago's Bishop Thomas Nicholson and led the church until 1929, when he left \"under unclear circumstances.\" He served industrial workers and \"fifty different ethnic/racial groups,\" opposed the Klan's Birth of a Nation, and tried unsuccessfully to integrate Methodist Hospital, drawing Klan opposition — while writing in 1920 that Black residents were \"very ignorant, and to a surprising degree morally undeveloped.\" The congregation stayed exclusively white: Black residents used the community hall and gym but not the pews, and a 1973 report admitted that \"Blacks are not likely to come to a church which they 'feel' has excluded them.\" The downtown church opened in October 1926 at a cost given here as about $350,000 (R29 gives $800,000 from Wikipedia — a discrepancy). It had a gym, rooftop garden, tennis court, community hall with a \"moving picture outfit,\" stage, shops and a cafeteria. Black Gary worshipped at Trinity M.E. Church and First Baptist. The church closed in 1975. The odd detail: The pastor who fought the Klan and tried to integrate the hospital left after thirteen years for reasons nobody wrote down.",
