      "summary": "The campaign was organised, not spontaneous. José María Arana founded the first anti-Chinese leagues in Cananea in 1916 (with schoolteacher Serapio Dávila, who justified it from the 1917 Constitution). Cananea's mayor Julián S. González set a 1 January 1920 deadline to expel every Chinese; Gov. Adolfo de la Huerta gave \"veiled approval.\" Law followed: 1919 (an 80% Mexican-hiring rule and separate Chinese neighbourhoods), 1921 (most Chinese immigration barred), 1923 (a ban on marriages between Mexican women and Chinese men — the women denounced as \"traitors\"). The Liga Nacionalista Pro-Raza (Hermosillo, 1925), the Comité Pro-Raza (1930, José Ángel Espinoza, who published El problema chino en México and El ejemplo de Sonora in 1931) and the \"green guards\" of Sonora stoned shops. In August 1931 Gov. Rodolfo Elías Calles ordered mass deportation: Chinese were trucked to the border, entire families \"pushed through gaps in the border fence.\" At Nogales, 181 were waiting and 46 crossed into the U.S. in one night; Ack Wong, a Chinese vice-consul, was jailed at Cananea and freed only after an American consular agent intervened. More than 3,500 were expelled from Sonora; those who could fled to Sinaloa, Chihuahua and Baja California. The Hoover administration later deported the expelled Chinese to China from San Francisco. The Chinese population of Mexico fell from about 26,000 (1920s) to 618 documented in 1940; Sonora's fell to 92 and Sinaloa's from 2,123 to 165. The odd detail: a schoolteacher wrote the theory, a Governor named Calles signed the trucks, and the United States — which itself excluded the Chinese — paid to ship the survivors home.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
