      "summary": "The capital's long name — Villa Carmona (1564), San Juan Bautista de Villahermosa (1641), Villahermosa del Puerto (1792), San Juan Bautista de Tabasco (from 4 November 1826) — was changed by decree of Governor Francisco J. Múgica on 3 February 1916, when \"Villahermosa\" was written as one word: that is a Múgica decree of 1916, not Garrido's, though popular accounts credit Garrido. Garrido's own renaming came later: he changed the names of villages, hamlets and towns with religious names to names of heroes, teachers, regional liberators and \"wise men.\" Two named cases: Barra de Santa Ana (named for the 26 July feast of St. Anne) became Pueblo de Andrés Sánchez Magallanes in 1934, where the Red Shirts destroyed religious images and stopped the parish church from being finished; San Carlos became Epigmenio Antonio (later Benito Juárez). The odd detail: a governor named a town after a teacher to replace a saint — and stopped a church from being completed in the town named for a saint's day.",
