      "summary": "The \"sunrise yellow\" hotel with a 1,200-foot private beach ran the Beach Walk — an open-air marble-tiled dance floor and bandshell on the lake side, \"the only one of its kind in the United States\" (unverified — V2_R16) — in summer, and the Marine Dining Room in winter. The hotel's own station WEBH (studio: the glass-walled \"Crystal Studio\" on the lobby level, broadcasting Tue–Sat 10 p.m.–2 a.m. and Sundays 6–10 p.m. in 1923–24, silent Mondays) closed in November 1928; after 1929 the bands were carried live on network radio. Over the years the bandstand held Wayne King, Ted Fio Rito and Dan Russo (their Oriole Orchestra was there in 1924), Benny Goodman, Tommy Dorsey, Glenn Miller, Artie Shaw and Xavier Cugat. Guests over the years included FDR, Chaplin, Bette Davis. (In 1949 Ruth Steinhagen shot ballplayer Eddie Waitkus here — the seed of The Natural — outside the window but worth knowing.) The odd detail: The radio studio had soundproof triple-paned windows so hotel guests could stand and watch the broadcast — a fishbowl of musicians at midnight.",
