      "summary": "Built early in the century as a fishing lodge, Summerwind was bought in 1916 by Robert Patterson Lamont, who hired Chicago architects (Tallmadge and Watson are named in some accounts) for a two-year remodel; a search summary calls him U.S. Secretary of Commerce (he served in Herbert Hoover's cabinet — from memory). Legend: Lamont fired a pistol at a ghost one night and the family abandoned the house (the page read gives no date; the \"mid-1930s\", \"basement door\", \"at dinner\" and \"twice\" details are on no page read) (unverified — V_R42) — a legend with no documented date or source. The later \"haunting\" of the Hinshaws in the 1970s and the ghost of the 18th-century explorer Jonathan Carver come from Raymond Bober's 1979 book The Carver Effect (pen name Wolfgang von Bober); Wikipedia notes at least two earlier residents denied the house was haunted and locals say the stories did not start until the book appeared, amplified by a 1980 Life feature. The house was destroyed by fire after a lightning strike on 19 June 1988. The odd detail: A real Chicago industrialist and future Cabinet member owns a \"haunted\" house — in a ghost story first printed forty-odd years after his supposed shot (unverified — V_R42: the Lamont pistol legend's first printing is undated; the record only shows Bober's 1979 book and the 1980 Life feature).",
