      "summary": "Edna Anne Wheeler married Guy Ballard in Chicago in 1916 (R05). After his Mount Shasta claim and Unveiled Mysteries (1934) the couple ran the movement from Chicago; the I AM page read this session says it \"had up to a million followers in 1938\" and drew 6,000 to the Shrine Auditorium in Los Angeles in 1935. Guy died of arteriosclerosis on 29 December 1939; Edna and their son Donald were then charged with eighteen counts of mail fraud (the page gives 1942 for the charge; other accounts give a 1940 indictment — UNVERIFIED which), and the resulting United States v. Ballard (1944) became the Supreme Court's ruling that a jury may not judge the truth of a religious belief. She moved the movement to Santa Fe in 1942, recorded thousands more \"dictations,\" and led it until her death on 10 February 1971. The odd detail: The largest new religion built in Chicago in the decade was, from 1940, a widow's — and the courts could try her honesty but not her god.",
