      "summary": "On 13–16 January 1916, under Governor Alvarado, the First Feminist Congress of Yucatán met at the Peón Contreras Theater in Mérida with 620 delegates, mostly teachers, to propose reforms of women's social, educational and legal rights — the first such congress in Mexico. On 7 November 1922 Rosa Torre González, nominated by the Socialist Party of the Southeast, won a seat on the Mérida city council — the first Mexican woman elected to public office. On 18 November 1923 three women of the Liga Rita Cetina Gutiérrez were elected to the state legislature: Beatriz Peniche Barrera (2nd district, Mérida), Elvia Carrillo Puerto (Felipe's sister, the Izamal district — nicknamed \"the Red Nun\") and Raquel Dzib Cicero (3rd district, Mérida). Weeks later the De la Huerta rebellion and Felipe's murder ended the experiment; Elvia fled. The odd detail: three women were elected to a state legislature in Mexico one month before the governor who backed them was shot; the vote came after the US granted women the ballot (1920) and long before Mexico did (1953, from memory).",
