      "summary": "Workers at Republic Iron and Steel struck first, demanding a raise from 19.5 to 25 cents an hour; Sheet & Tube's men followed days later, and around 16,000 people were out of work between the two plants. On Friday 7 January pickets gathered at the north gate; someone in the crowd reportedly fired a shot or threw a stone, and company-hired guards (described in one account as off-duty police) fired into the crowd — three dead and at least 27 hurt (unverified — V_R43: the Vindicator page read says only \"several fatalities and dozens of injuries\"). The village then burned. Accounts of the damage differ: one local account says about 100 business blocks and residences and $1 million; a centennial account says 34 buildings and $1.5 million (unverified — V_R43: search summary; the Vindicator page read says \"nearly 100 business blocks and residences\" and $1,000,000). Rioters cut fire hoses, burned company records at the headquarters, and burned all but two of the village's 20 saloons. The sheriff wired the governor: \"Please send National Guard to village of East Youngstown at once. Lives are being taken and many portions of East Youngstown are being burned.\" Three regiments of the Ohio National Guard arrived about 3 a.m.; a summary of the centennial account says Gov. Frank Willis sent 24 companies. Workers went back within days at 22 cents an hour, and Sheet & Tube built four housing sites at about $20 a month. The odd detail: The rioters burned the company's own records at headquarters — and eighteen of the village's twenty saloons.",
