      "summary": "Two celebrations ran the same night. At the Germania Club, president Ernest J. Kruetgen affirmed that German-Americans were \"good American citizens,\" and Prof. Kühnemann spoke again: \"Here it is different. We assert our pride because we are of German origin, and are willing to make sacrifices in this new land.\" He asked the hall to fund the rebuilding of Ragnit, the East Prussian border town wrecked in the Russian invasion. Across the river the Teutonia — here the German-Austro-Hungarian Alliance, not the 1924 Nazi club of the same name (see R10) — filled the North Side Turnhall so far past capacity that police shut the entrance under the fire code; inside: Ballmann's orchestra, a comedy about army life, and a tableau in honour of the Emperor. The odd detail: a Chicago police fire-code closure of a Kaiser's-birthday party, eighteen months before Chicago was told the Kaiser was the enemy.",
