      "summary": "Drilled by Herbert Wylie for Edward L. Doheny's Mexican Petroleum / Huasteca company, Cerro Azul No. 4 came in in February 1916 at some 260,000 barrels a day, a column of oil 598 feet high that could be heard 16 miles away at Casiano and sprayed oil over a two-mile radius (unverified — V_M01); it produced over 57 million barrels in 14 years. Across the same country, from 1915 to 1920, Gen. Manuel Peláez — a Huasteca landowner and broker to the oil companies — kept Carranza's armies out, taxing the companies to pay a small, well-armed force; the companies paid him protection and he organised guardias blancas (\"white guards\") for them. The odd detail: the world's greatest gusher came in one month before the Columbus raid — and the oil it poured was guarded, not by either Mexican government, but by a private army on the oilmen's payroll.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the gusher numbers (from search summaries of the company article); Medium on the white-guards detail. • Doheny's Mexican Petroleum / Huasteca company, Cerro Azul No. 4 came in in February 1916 at some 260,000 barrels a day, a column of oil 598 feet high that could be heard 16 miles away at Casiano and sprayed oil over a two-mile radius (unverified — V_M01);",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a private army of \"white guards\" paid in oil money is the Golden Circle's native auxiliary in all but name. What did the Huastec villages say came up with the oil at 598 feet?",
