      "summary": "Brig. Gen. John J. Pershing crossed on 14 March 1916 with about 6,600 men, eventually more than 10,000. The Army ran its first motor-truck convoys (Jeffery Quads, FWDs) and flew the 1st Aero Squadron's eight Curtiss JN-3s (two lost en route). Apache Scouts under Lt. James Shannon rode with it. At Parral (12 April) Maj. Tompkins's 128 troopers were chased out of town by some 500 Mexicans (2 US dead). Villa, shot in the leg, hid for weeks near Santa Cruz de Herrera. Raiders hit Glenn Springs, Texas (5 May). On 18 June Wilson called up the National Guard — over 140,000 men went to the border. After Carrizal (next entry) the column sat in camp; it withdrew 28 January – 5 February 1917. The odd detail: Pershing's supply line was the first in the US Army to run on trucks and his scouts on aeroplanes — and his most reliable trackers were Apaches, in the Sierra their grandfathers had fought the Army from.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the Guard call-up of 140,000 is the setting's \"forties veteran\" generation — mechanics, truck drivers, pilots of the first air war the Army ever fought, and Apache scouts nobody thanked.",
