      "summary": "Authorised on 21 March 1916 and deployed at Luxeuil on 20 April, the Escadrille Américaine (renamed Lafayette under German protest) began with seven Americans: Norman Prince, William Thaw, Kiffin Rockwell, Victor Chapman, James R. McConnell, Elliott Cowdin and Bert Hall. Raoul Lufbery became its ace with 16 confirmed victories; two lion cubs, Whiskey and Soda, lived with the pilots. Wider, the Lafayette Flying Corps counted 267 American volunteers in French aviation (255 brevetted). Chicago's own was Andrew Courtney Campbell Jr. of Kenilworth Avenue, Kenilworth, who entered French aviation in July 1916, flew with the Escadrille from 30 May 1917, was known for stunting and wild chances, and was killed fighting three German aircraft north of Soissons near Pargny on 1 October 1917; a memorial hospital in France was planned in his name. The odd detail: the pilots' lion cubs — a Montmartre-bought mascot for men who were, on paper, soldiers of the Foreign Legion seconded to aviation.",
