      "summary": "Tampico was the Mexican stronghold of the Industrial Workers of the World and the anarcho-syndicalist Casa del Obrero Mundial (COM) — the idea likely arriving with sailors of the IWW's Marine Transport Workers' Industrial Union. In April 1916 protests over living conditions became a strike that shut most oil companies and public services. In April 1917 an IWW-led strike against El Águila spread to at least six other oil companies, the longshoremen and boatmen — a general strike of 15,000 workers that halted all oil production and was violently repressed. Another large strike followed in November 1917; in July 1920 the IWW and COM led a general strike of 10,000 oil workers. Named organisers include Pedro Coria (from Arizona), Ramón Parreño, Francisco Gamallo, Rafael Zamudio, Víctor Martínez and José Zapata. B. Traven, arriving 1924, absorbed this world; his first Mexican novel was published in Germany as Der Wobbly. The odd detail: the world's second-busiest oil port (unverified — V_M09: no source read gives Tampico this rank) was stopped cold, twice in a year (April and November 1917), by a union whose idea came in on the ships.",
