      "summary": "After 11 p.m., between 60 and 200 raiders (probably no more than 80) — Villistas and Carrancistas together under Lt. Col. Natividad Álvarez — hit Glenn Springs, a hamlet with a candelilla wax factory (owned by \"Captain\" C. D. Wood and W. K. Ellis) and a store kept by C. G. Compton. Nine troopers of the 14th Cavalry under Sgt. Charles E. Smyth held an adobe until it was set alight. Privates William Cohen, Stephen J. Coloe and Lawrence K. Rogers were killed, four others wounded or badly burned, and Compton's four-year-old son was killed. At Boquillas the raiders looted Jesse Deemer's store and took Deemer and his assistant Monroe Payne, a Black Seminole, as hostages. Col. Frederick W. Sibley and Maj. George T. Langhorne crossed on 11 May — Langhorne with a Cadillac touring car — and recovered the hostages at El Pino, Coahuila, by 15 May. The odd detail: the spring itself was named for the first settler, killed on the spot by Comanches — the raid was the second massacre at the same water.",
