      "summary": "Texas, Arizona and New Mexico Guards were called on 8 May 1916, after Glenn Springs. On 18 June, under the brand-new National Defense Act of 1916 (signed 3 June), Secretary of War Newton D. Baker telegraphed the governors of all the states with Guard units and the District of Columbia. The figure depends on the source — NARA's Prologue says Wilson \"called out 110,000\"; the partial call is given as 158,664; M01 and the Guard's own histories say more than 140,000 went to the border. They did not cross into Mexico: they were a show of force and a border guard while the regulars were inside Chihuahua. Three more raids hit the Texas side that summer (Glenn Springs 5 May, San Ygnacio 15 June, and one on 31 July). The odd detail: it was the first federal call-up of the Guard under the 1916 Act — the law that made the militia a federal reserve was a fortnight old when it was used.",
