      "summary": "The 1st Illinois Infantry — organised in Chicago on 14 September 1874 as the 1st Battalion of the Illinois State Guard, a Spanish War regiment (mustered at Springfield 13 May 1898, served in Cuba) — was mustered into federal service 26 June 1916 and stationed at San Antonio. Illinois had no ready mobilisation camp, so it used the state fairgrounds at Springfield, and dispatched units south so fast that Illinois afterwards boasted the 1st was the very first Guard unit to reach the border near San Antonio. It was mustered out on 4 October 1916. The Rock Island Argus of 30 June headlined \"SEVENTH PUT ON TRAIN FOR SAN ANTON\" and reported \"two regiments of the Illinois guardsmen will reach San Antonio late today\"; the Chicago Day Book of 14 August reported Governor Edward F. Dunne on his way to the Illinois militia camp near San Antonio. The odd detail: a regiment that had already occupied Cuba in 1898 went to the next war in the same state fairgrounds' cattle barns — and the setting's \"Cuba they simply never left\" was, for the 1st Illinois, a place they had been.",
