      "summary": "The 8th Illinois was raised in 1898 after Chicago's Black community protested being shut out of the Spanish War; Gov. John Tanner authorised it under Col. John R. Marshall, and it garrisoned Cuba (to 1899). The legislature then built it an armory — the first built to honour an African-American unit. On 30 June 1916 it was federalised at Camp Dunne, Springfield, and on the day it reported it was the only regiment in the United States Army with a complete complement of Black commissioned officers, second lieutenant to colonel (Lt. Col. James H. Johnson and Maj. William H. Roberts were the white exceptions, per one source ⟨V_M14: that source, the Western Front Association article, is describing the July 1917 federalisation, not June 1916⟩). Under Col. Franklin A. Denison it went to San Antonio and patrolled the Rio Grande after bandits and Mexican army deserters. White soldiers there refused to salute its officers. Mustered out at Springfield 27 October 1916. Many of its men were Great Migration arrivals the Chicago Defender had drawn north. The odd detail: its colonel, Franklin Denison, was born in San Antonio (1862) — Lincoln University, valedictorian of Union College of Law (1890), Chicago's first Black assistant prosecutor — and came home to the city of his birth commanding a regiment whose officers the white troops would not salute.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the regiment, Denison and the dates; the saluting incident is from the Reader; the \"all officers but two\" detail is via search (Medium).",
