      "summary": "Alan Seeger (b. 1888, New York; Harvard 1910), a bohemian poet living in Paris, joined the French Foreign Legion when war broke out in 1914 and served two years on the Western Front. He was killed in the attack on Belloy-en-Santerre on 4 July 1916 — a fellow legionnaire, Rif Baer, remembered his \"tall silhouette\" against the green of the cornfield, running forward with bayonet fixed. His poem \"I Have a Rendezvous with Death\" (\"…At some disputed barricade\") was published after his death in his Poems (1916) and became the American volunteer's anthem. The Paris monument to American volunteers bears his likeness and lines, and 23 other Foreign Legion names on its base. The odd detail: he died on the Fourth of July, in a foreign legion, for a country not his own — the day America celebrated independence.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — for a player: a Legion veteran who knew Seeger and knows the poem by heart — and knows it was written by a man who expected exactly what he got. For the occult layer: \"a rendezvous\" is a phrase a cult could take literally.",
