      "summary": "In 1917 the Swiss geologist Walter Staub found a 145-cm sandstone statue of a naked youth on top of the great two-level \"Western Structure\" at El Consuelo. The figure has a deformed skull, filed upper teeth, stretched earlobes, and one side of the body covered in carved glyphs; it dates to the Huastec Postclassic (c. 900–1521). It went to Mexico City and stands in the National Museum of Anthropology — one of the masterpieces of Mesoamerican sculpture. The odd detail: it was found by a geologist — the oil boom's prospectors walked the Huasteca looking for anticlines and found the old gods instead (unverified — V_M09: Staub is confirmed as a Swiss geologist (later a professor at Bern and Berlin) who published on the find in 1919; that he was working for an oil company was not found).",
