      "summary": "Earthquakes began 17 November 1917, with stronger shocks on 25 and 29 December 1917 and 3 and 24 January 1918, ruining settlements around Amatitlán and destroying buildings in Guatemala City and Antigua Guatemala. The semi-official Diario de Centro América first reported the damage, then began criticising the slow, inefficient recovery; it complained of debris lying in the streets in May 1918 and of \"excessive luxury, impunity and terror.\" The Unionist Party grew from the disaster: a founding document signed by 51 citizens on 25 December 1919, official recognition on 1 March 1920; Bishop Piñol y Batres began preaching against the government in 1919 — the Church's first opposition. On 11 March 1920 the army fired on an unarmed Unionist demonstration, beginning the \"Tragic Week\"; Estrada Cabrera surrendered on 14 April 1920 at La Palma; the Assembly declared him \"unable to continue\" and he was sent to prison for life; he died 24 September 1924. The odd detail: the earthquakes brought down the government — the state's failure to clear the rubble was what the newspaper wrote about.",
