      "summary": "Cincinnati's Germans settled Over-the-Rhine from the 1840s (Christian Moerlein's brewery, 1853, became the city's largest); the city's Turnverein of 1848 is the oldest in America (general knowledge, UNVERIFIED here), and Cincinnati stood with Chicago and New York as a brewing capital. In 1917–18, with 25,000 Cincinnatians in uniform: the Cincinnati Symphony's Austrian conductor Dr. Ernst Kunwald (1912–17; he had given America its first Mahler Third) was arrested by federal marshals on 8 Dec 1917, the day after the declaration of war on Austria-Hungary, after the Daughters of the American Revolution demanded he be kept off the podium; interned without trial at Fort Oglethorpe from 12 Jan 1918 (Karl Muck of the Boston Symphony joined him there in March), the evidence including that he conducted European classics and was loyal to his emperor; deported in 1919; he ended in Königsberg and Berlin and died in Vienna in 1939. The concertmaster Emil Heerman was arrested and released into the Conservatory's custody; \"the city's library removed pro-German books and the public schools discontinued German language classes\"; and the council renamed the streets — German Street became English Street, Berlin Street became Woodward (Wikipedia's Anti-German sentiment; the fuller list — Bremen to Republic, Hamburg to Stonewall, and the rest — is general knowledge, UNVERIFIED). The odd detail: a city that named its canal \"the Rhine\" renamed German Street \"English Street\" and locked up its orchestra's conductor.",
