      "summary": "In April 1917, under Governor J. A. A. Burnquist, the legislature created a Commission of Public Safety with near-dictatorial powers \"to support the war effort at home and root out disloyal elements.\" It watched the Nonpartisan League and the Industrial Workers of the World, and Germans — then the single largest ethnic group in Minnesota. The commission urged English-only schooling; Frederick Bergmeier, editor of the St. Paul Volkszeitung, was jailed by federal authorities \"for the duration of the war\"; Albert Steinhauser of the New Ulm Post was expelled from the Editorial Association; on 25 July 1917 the New Ulm draft rally led to New Ulm's mayor Louis Fritsche and city attorney Albert Pfaender being removed from office; on 1 April 1918 the statue \"Germania\" was taken down from the Germania Life Insurance Building in St. Paul and the firm renamed itself Guardian Life; on 19 August 1918 John Meints was kidnapped, tarred, feathered and whipped (he won a $6,000 settlement in 1922). The commission was abolished 14 April 1919 (MNopedia); the 107–12 vote is UNVERIFIED — V_R39. The odd detail: The war-era commission's powers were so broad that they were quietly folded, in effect, into every public official's oath — a promise to back Wilson's war policy.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High as history; the divergence is a design choice. • the 107–12 vote is UNVERIFIED — V_R39.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Minnesota that never had the Commission is the Minnesota where the Volkszeitung's editor is a respected voice of Berlin's cultural mission — and where the Golden Circle needs a different reason than \"loyalty\" to run the local Klan against them.",
