      "summary": "The islands were governed by naval officers — the first seven governors were all naval officers — until 1931. Congress did not grant citizenship to most Virgin Islanders until 25 February 1927, which the islanders resented. Under naval administration the total cultivated area fell by about 60 percent and the population fell about 16 percent between 1917 and 1930. In March 1931 President Hoover cruised to the Caribbean and stopped in the islands; a week before his visit his order moving the administration from the Navy to the Interior Department took effect and the first civilian governor, Dr. Paul M. Pearson, had just arrived. On the visit Hoover called them an \"effective poorhouse\" and said \"Viewed from every point except remote naval contingencies, it was unfortunate that we ever acquired these islands,\" but that having assumed the responsibility \"we must do our best to help the inhabitants.\" The odd detail: the president who administered the departure of the Navy also insulted his own new possession to its face, and the line lasted.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for dates and the quote (multiple summaries agree); Medium for the percentages (one page).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"an effective poorhouse\" with a Navy that never left is a place where nobody is watching the naval station — a Secret Service man's leave posting. St. Croix's 60-percent-fallow estates as the Golden Circle's cheap land.",
