      "summary": "William H. Moran — a counterfeiting expert with 54 years in the Service — was Chief from 1917 to 1936, the longest-serving chief. Under him the Service added official protection of the President's family, created the White House Police Force (1922), handled the 1928 switch to smaller-size currency, and investigated Teapot Dome. He \"was often at odds with FBI Director J. Edgar Hoover over issues of jurisdiction.\" His successor was Frank J. Wilson (1937–46), the Treasury man who built the Capone tax case. The odd detail: the real Secret Service chief spent the 1930s in turf fights with J. Edgar Hoover — which is exactly the rivalry the setting keeps: the Secret Service (\"the old guard\") against K. Edgar Hoover's FBI and its Section 13 (\"the newcomer\").",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
