      "summary": "The only whaleback ever built for passengers (launched at Superior 3 Dec 1892; 362 ft; about 5,000 passengers; designed by Alexander McDougall) carried 1.7–2.0 million people to the 1893 fair and from 1899 ran the Goodrich Line's daily Chicago–Milwaukee round trip with a two-hour stop in Milwaukee. On 30 June 1917, turning in the Milwaukee River, her bow sheared two legs off the Yahr-Lang water tower; the tank fell across her decks, dumping about 25,000 gallons on the passengers and killing sixteen. She was laid up in 1933 as the Depression killed the excursion trade and cut up at Manitowoc in 1936. She is thought to have carried more passengers than any other vessel in Great Lakes history. The odd detail: Sixteen people drowned on a ship's deck, in a river, under a falling water tank.",
