      "summary": "Frances Xavier Cabrini, the Italian-born founder of the Missionary Sisters of the Sacred Heart and of Columbus Hospital in Chicago (1905), naturalized in 1909, died there suddenly of chronic endocarditis on 22 Dec 1917, aged 67; her body lay in state at the hospital before going to New York. Two miracles were credited for beatification: Peter Smith, blinded in 1921 at New York's Columbus Hospital by a nurse's \"50% silver nitrate solution,\" whose eyes were normal 72 hours after contact with a Cabrini relic; and Sister Delphina Grazioli, dying after four operations in Seattle (1925–29), who saw Cabrini in a vision and recovered. In 1933 the sisters exhumed her body and divided it; her arm bone went to Chicago. Pius XI beatified her on 13 Nov 1938; she was canonized in 1946 (first US citizen saint), and 120,000 attended a thanksgiving Mass at Soldier Field. The odd detail: during the 1930s a saint-in-waiting's body was being cut into relics, and one arm was coming to a Lincoln Park hospital.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
