      "summary": "The decade's treatments arrived in order. Malaria fever therapy for general paresis (Wagner-Jauregg, 1917; Nobel Prize 1927) meant injecting a patient with malaria-infected blood to burn out neurosyphilis, then curing the malaria with quinine; an Illinois Medical Journal paper of Feb 1941 describes a series of paretics of whom 65 were treated with malaria, 50 with typhoid fever, 39 with both, warning that malaria is \"drastic\" and dangerous in deteriorated cases. Cook County Hospital used artificial fever machines for other diseases. Insulin coma (Sakel, published 1933; brought to the US in 1936) meant a course of 50–60 comas, six days a week for about two months, at a death rate of roughly 1–5%. Metrazol convulsions (Meduna, 1934) followed; by 1940 the American Journal of Psychiatry carried \"Metrazol Shock Therapy, Elgin State Hospital\" by Charles F. Read and the Elgin staff, and a paper noting that at the Chicago State Hospital they had begun to pre-medicate patients because Metrazol's worst effects were \"fear and fractures\"; a Jacksonville paper of 1941 studied \"the element of fear in Metrazol therapy\". Hydrotherapy — wet packs and the \"continuous tub\" in which an agitated patient was held in warm water for hours — was standard: Kankakee's 1910 hospital building was built for it and a \"safe continuous tub\" was designed in Illinois in the 1920s. Lobotomy reached America only in 1936 (see R13). The odd detail: In an Illinois state hospital of the 1930s a doctor could give a man malaria on purpose, and another could give him typhoid on purpose, as treatment.",
