      "summary": "As Marines came ashore at San Pedro de Macorís on 10 January 1917, Gregorio Urbano Gilbert — eighteen, a linotypist and store clerk born in Puerto Plata (25 May 1898) to a Dominican mother and a Bahamian father — loaded a revolver and fired on them, killing an officer, C. H. Burton. Captured and sentenced to death, commuted to life, he was released on 22 October 1922, went to Cuba, Curaçao and Nicaragua, and fought with Sandino against the Marines there (from 1928). He came home in 1929, lived to fight the second American landing in 1965, and died on 29 November 1970; in 2019 he was declared a National Hero — \"hero of two peoples\". The odd detail: one teenage clerk's revolver links the Dominican occupation to Sandino's war — the same Marines, the same man, two countries.",
