      "summary": "Foreign Secretary Arthur Zimmermann offered Mexico alliance if the United States entered the war, with Germany's help to \"reconquer her lost territory in New Mexico, Texas and Arizona,\" and asked Carranza to bring in Japan. Room 40 (Nigel de Grey) broke it. Wilson released it on 1 March 1917; on 3 March Zimmermann said, \"I cannot deny it. It is true.\" Carranza's military commission judged the offer unworkable — Mexico's civil war, US strength, German money that could not be delivered, English-speaking territory that could not be held — and Japan publicly refused. Behind it lay German agents: Franz von Rintelen had given Huerta money in 1915 to start a US–Mexican war (Huerta was arrested at Newman, New Mexico, and died in El Paso in January 1916), and Lothar Witzke worked from Mexico City. The odd detail: Germany's diplomats could send it only through Washington's own cable, which Wilson had lent them for peace talks.",
