      "summary": "President Mario García Menocal's disputed re-election in November 1916 set off a Liberal revolt in February 1917 known by its campaign song, \"La Chambelona\". The gunboat USS Paducah landed men on 12 February 1917; by March the Liberal forces were broken and their leader, ex-president José Miguel Gómez, captured after the fight at Caicaje. Cuba declared war on Germany on 7 April 1917, the day after the United States. Marines came in earnest from 25 August 1917 (under 1,000 at first, another 1,000 in December) and set up permanent camps in Oriente, patrolling the sugar districts; the special envoy Henry Morgan urged troops at once because \"the 1918 sugar harvest was in danger\". Officially it was support for a wartime ally; in fact, protection of American sugar. Cuba produced a record harvest in 1918. The last Marines left on 15 February 1922. The odd detail: a whole revolution known by a conga song — \"¡Aé, aé, aé la Chambelona!\" (the refrain is standard; the words UNVERIFIED on the page read).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. • the refrain is standard; the words UNVERIFIED on the page read",
