      "summary": "Funston — who had commanded the American force at Veracruz in 1914 and then the Southern Department (Pershing's superior) — died of a heart attack on 19 February 1917 at the St. Anthony Hotel in San Antonio — listening to an orchestra and, by the report, holding six-year-old Inez Harriett Silverberg in his arms. President Wilson had favoured him to command the American Expeditionary Force; Pershing was given it instead. He lay in state at the Alamo and at San Francisco City Hall (the first person so honoured there) and was buried at the San Francisco National Cemetery. The odd detail: the man Wilson wanted to command the war in France died with a child in his arms, in a hotel with an orchestra playing, about six weeks before America's April 1917 entry — and the Alamo was his lying-in-state.",
