      "summary": "In March 1917 Morley approached the Office of Naval Intelligence and offered a list of Mexican- and Central-American-specialist anthropologists as agents, their fieldwork as cover. He was commissioned an ensign in the Naval Coast Defense Reserve (unverified — V_M15), and for two years travelled more than 2,000 miles along the Central American coast hunting secret German submarine bases and building an agent network that watched German nationals; he filed over 10,000 pages of reports. Other archaeologists, among them Herbert J. Spinden and Samuel K. Lothrop, were recruited at his instigation. The story is told in Charles H. Harris and Louis R. Sadler, The Archaeologist Was a Spy (2003); the Doughboy Foundation's page calls him \"Espionage Agent 53.\" The odd detail: Spinden, later the co-leader of the 1926 New York Times expedition (see 12), was on the same spy list.",
