      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Trinidad and Tobago",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(the ordinance) — the Spiritual (\"Shouter\") Baptist churches of Port of Spain, the south and Tobago",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Trinidad%20and%20Tobago%20%20%E2%80%94%20the%20Spiritual%20%20Baptist%20churches%20of%20Port%20of%20Spain%2C%20the%20south%20and%20Tobago%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "On 16 November 1917 the colonial government outlawed the Spiritual Baptist faith (passed 16 November; the Obeah Histories text gives \"Commencement 28th November, 1917\"). The ordinance forbade, among other things, ringing bells, holding lit candles in public places and \"shaking\" of any sort; taking part in a Shouter service, lending a building for one, or obstructing the police carried a $240 fine. The stated reason was noise and \"unseemly\" worship — singing, bell-ringing, dancing, trembling, falling. It stood for 34 years, until 30 March 1951; since 1996 the date has been a national holiday, Spiritual Baptist Liberation Day. Obeah Histories documents prosecutions from 1918 (\"Teacher Bailey\"). The odd detail: a bell and a candle were contraband in a Christian colony.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (dates and provisions from two official/academic summaries).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a banned church meeting in a cane-field at night, a police raid, and something in the \"mourning\" room the police weren't looking for. Respect: the overlay is never the faith; it is what hunts the faithful when they cannot call the police.",
