      "summary": "On 9 Sept 1917 Rev. Giuliani, an Italian Protestant evangelist, held a \"loyalty\" rally in Bay View; anarchists disrupted it, police fired, two men were killed and eleven Italian anarchists were arrested and charged with conspiracy to murder Giuliani; federal agents raided three IWW offices at the same time. Shortly before their trial, a twenty-pound black-powder bomb with an acid-delay detonator (unverified — V_R27) was found beside Giuliani's church by a social worker, Maude L. Richter; the church janitor, Sam Mazzone, carried it to the Central Station, where it went off at 7:33 p.m. as detectives examined it. Nine policemen died — Henry Deckert, Frank Caswin, Frederick Kaiser, David O'Brien, Stephen Stecker, Charles Seehawer, Edward Spindler, Al Templin, Paul Weiler — and one civilian, Catherine Walker, who had come in to report a robbery; six more were hurt. The bomb was never solved (Galleanists were suspected; later interviews pointed to Mario Buda and Carlo Valdinoci, who had gone to Mexico to dodge the draft). In December 1917 the eleven were each given 25 years; Emma Goldman published The Milwaukee Frame-Up; Clarence Darrow won their release on appeal before the Wisconsin Supreme Court in 1918, and they were deported. Two further plots failed, including a bomb left at prosecutor Winfred Zabel's house. It remained the deadliest single day for American police until 11 Sept 2001. The odd detail: The bomb was carried across downtown in a janitor's arms and sat on a station-house desk while nine men leaned over it.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (EMKE's \"Wartime Milwaukee\" entry dates only the Bay View riot, to September 1917, and gives the bomb no date; EMKE's police-department entry says \"a youngster\" carried the bomb, against Wikipedia's janitor; the Bay View shooting was 9 Sept, the bomb 24 Nov). • Shortly before their trial, a twenty-pound black-powder bomb with an acid-delay detonator (unverified — V_R27) was found beside Giuliani's church by a social worker, Maude L. • the widows' pensions, the survivors' promotions (Kluchesky, Laubenheimer) and the unsolved question of who built the acid-delay fuse (unverified — V_R27) are a ready-made haunting of the Central Station.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Nine policemen dead in one room in 1917 is the founding wound of the Milwaukee force that polices the 1930s; the widows' pensions, the survivors' promotions (Kluchesky, Laubenheimer) and the unsolved question of who built the acid-delay fuse (unverified — V_R27) are a ready-made haunting of the Central Station.",
