      "summary": "When America entered the war (6 Apr 1917) German names came down across Chicago. The Eitel brothers' Bismarck Gardens beer garden (opened 31 May 1896, 4,500 diners by 1914) became the Marigold Gardens in December 1917 — the Eitels resisted until public pressure won. Their Bismarck Hotel was \"temporarily renamed\" the Randolph Hotel. The Germania Club (founded 1865 as the Germania Männerchor, which sang at Lincoln's Chicago funeral) became the Lincoln Club, and reverted in 1921. Lubeck, Frankfort and Hamburg Streets became Dickens, Charleston and Shakespeare. Downstate, German-born Robert Prager was dragged from the Collinsville jail and lynched as a \"spy\" (Apr 1918). The odd detail: the Eitels named the hotel \"with permission from Otto von Bismarck\"; in the 1930s the rebuilt Bismarck Hotel (1926, Randolph/LaSalle/Wells, 600 beds, 2,500-seat Palace Theater) is where the German World's Fair committee meets (see below).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (the 3750lsd page gives December 1917; some postcards say 1915 — trust 1917).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a period map of Umbra Chicago should keep the German street names — a quiet tell to players that this is not their history. An old Chicagoan in the real record remembers the renaming; nobody in Umbra does.",
