      "summary": "Named for developer Eugene S. Robbins, the village was incorporated 14 December 1917 with Thomas J. Kellar as founding mayor — \"people who got tired of the white fights and the crowded city\", he said, wanting to \"raise chickens, make gardens, and be a little more free\". It was the only municipality in the North governed entirely by Black Americans. From 1930 to 1933 it had the first airport owned and operated by Black Americans, co-founded by John C. Robinson and Cornelius Coffey, with the only flight school where Black students could learn to fly — until a tornado destroyed the runway's hangar in 1933. On summer weekends Black Chicagoans crowded its picnic grounds and nightclubs. (Population 431 in 1920.) The odd detail: An airfield carried off by a tornado in 1933 — and with it the only flight school in the North that would take Black students.",
