      "summary": "In Seabrook's book the Haitian farmer Constant Polynice tells this as true. In the big cane season of spring 1918, HASCO (paying \"twenty or thirty cents a day\") offered a bonus for new workers. The headman Ti Joseph of Colombier registered a gang who stared \"vacant-eyed like cattle\" and gave no names — Joseph said they were mountain people who did not understand the plains' creole. They were zombies he and his wife Croyance had taken from their graves, fed only unsalted food. At Fête-Dieu Croyance, pitying them, took them to see the procession and bought them tablettes pistaches — peanut sweets; the baker had salted the nuts. Tasting salt, they knew they were dead, walked back to Morne-au-Diable, clawed at their own graves and fell dead. Their families took Ti Joseph's name and a sweat-stained shirt to a bokor beyond Trou Caïman for a ouanga; Seabrook's teller says Joseph was later killed. The odd detail: salt breaks the spell — Mars (1945) confirms the belief independently: \"The Zombis are supposed to eat no salt.\"",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High that Seabrook printed it; folklore/hearsay as to events — label it \"as Seabrook's informant told it\".",
