      "summary": "On June 22, 1918, an empty troop train ran into the Hagenbeck-Wallace circus train near Hessville/Ivanhoe, Indiana; as many as 86 died. 56–61 circus people were buried in the Showmen's League of America's 750-plot section (bought 1913; Buffalo Bill was the League's first president), many under \"Unknown Male\" markers. Five stone elephants — four at the corners, one in the centre — all with lowered trunks (mourning). Locals say ghostly elephants trumpet at night; Brookfield Zoo, which kept elephants until 2010, is a mile or two away. The odd detail: The graves are marked \"Unknown Male No. 1, 2, 3…\" because roustabouts joined the circus under nicknames.",
