      "summary": "George Brumder built it in 1896 for his Germania Publishing Company — the largest German-language publishing house in America (unverified — V_R27) — and crowned it with copper domes shaped like spiked helmets and a ten-foot, three-ton bronze Germania over the door (the source names no sculptor; the ornamental ironworker Cyril Colnik stored the statue for a while after its removal). In 1918 the statue was removed \"discreetly during the night\" and the building renamed the Brumder Building; the statue's fate is unknown (melted for scrap in the next war, or in the Smithsonian, by the two theories). Brumder had died in 1910; in 1927 the presses were pulled out of the basement and it became Milwaukee's first underground parking garage. The Germania newspaper (1873) merged with the Herold in 1906 and printed its last edition in 1924; the Milwaukee Herold ran to 1982; the women's magazine Die Deutsche Hausfrau (first printed by the rival Herold Company in 1904, not by Brumder) had 132,000 subscribers in 1907 and still 40,000–50,000 after the war. The odd detail: The building kept its Prussian helmets on the roof and lost only the woman over the door; the domes are still there.",
