      "summary": "The Nunnemacher Grand Opera House, renamed Das Neue Deutsche Stadt-Theater, was damaged by arson in 1893 and destroyed by fire in January 1895; Captain Frederick Pabst ordered a replacement from Otto Strack the same year and it opened on 9 Nov 1895 at a cost of $300,000 — 1,339 seats, fireproofed, with elevators and air conditioning, deep red fabrics, white and green marble, Greek statues and a two-ton chandelier of 33,000 hand-strung Austrian crystals. It booked only German-language performances until 1918, when the war forced English on it; the resident German company survived in reduced form until 1931 (R07). A major 1928 renovation \"compromised\" Strack's design (reversed in 1976). Rachmaninoff, Pavlova and Olivier played it. The Pabst family owned it until 1953. The odd detail: The city's grandest theatre was built by a brewer to replace a German opera house that had been set on fire twice.",
