      "summary": "Berger — born 28 Feb 1860 at Nieder-Rehbach in the Austrian Empire (now Romania), Jewish, in Milwaukee from 1881, first a German teacher in the public schools, then editor of the Wisconsin Vorwärts (1892) and founder of the Milwaukee Leader (7 Dec 1911) — was the first Socialist ever elected to Congress (1910). In our history he was indicted under the Espionage Act in February 1918 for the Leader's anti-war editorials, won his House seat anyway that November, was tried before Judge Kenesaw Mountain Landis from 9 Dec 1918, convicted on 20 Feb 1919 and sentenced to twenty years; the House refused to seat him on 10 Nov 1919, Milwaukee re-elected him in a special election on 19 Dec 1919, and the House refused him again on 10 Jan 1920. The Supreme Court threw the conviction out on 31 Jan 1921 (Berger v. United States — Landis should have stepped aside). He sat again 1923–29, lost to William Stafford in 1928, and chaired the Socialist Party nationally from 20 Oct 1926 (succeeding Debs). On 16 July 1929 a streetcar struck him at Third and Clarke; he died of the skull fracture on 7 Aug 1929. Some 75,000 people filed past his body in City Hall. The odd detail: His widow Meta Schlichting Berger (married 1897) sat on the Milwaukee School Board 1909–39 and the University of Wisconsin Board of Regents 1928–34 — the Socialist widow governing the schools of the German city for the whole decade.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
