      "summary": "After Shell's Caribbean Petroleum Company struck oil at Mene Grande on Venezuela's Lake Maracaibo (31 July 1914), Royal Dutch Shell chose deep-water, politically stable Curaçao to refine it: construction of the Isla refinery began in May 1918, run by the Curaçaosche Petroleum Maatschappij (from 1925 the CPIM). Shallow-draught \"lake tankers\" shuttled crude across from the Maracaibo bar. By 1930 CPIM employed more than 7,880 people, many of them migrants from other islands; the refinery was called the largest in the world. The odd detail: a Dutch trading island of 30,000 people became, in a decade, the refinery of a Venezuelan dictator's oil.",
