      "summary": "On 30 May 1918, ~5,000 people in thirty Ukrainian societies marched along Chicago Ave. for an independent Ukraine. The paramilitary Sitch (Sich — named for the Zaporozhian Cossack stronghold; the FLPS translation gives no gloss) organization built Chicago branches from 1919 and later formed the United Hetman Organizations — monarchists loyal to the Hetman. On 1 Jan 1930 the Sitch paper announced formal ties with the Illinois National Guard (members already served in the 132nd Infantry) to \"acquire more military training,\" arguing that \"a Ukrainian has no country of his own as yet,\" so American service betrays only \"the Austrian, Polish, Russian, or Roumanian government.\" In Sept 1934 the First Ukrainian Aviation School in Chicago elected parent-curators (Michael Petriw, Peter Hawrysh, Tymko Bahriy, Paul Vysochan, Theodore Mamchuk) at a meeting held in the United Hetman Organizations' office, Dr. O. Tarnawsky presiding (Nash Styah, 15 Sept 1934; the item does not say the Hetmanites ran the school), and on 30 Sept 1934 blessed their second airplane, the Lviv, at the St. Nicholas picnic grove, with the plane Ukraine flying in from Detroit — rides at \"an exceptionally low price,\" admission 25 cents. The odd detail: a Chicago émigré militia training in the National Guard and buying warplanes for a monarch who had no throne.",
