      "summary": "Chicago-born (21 May 1894), Forbes joined the Tribune in 1918. In 1921 she went to County Wexford and came back in steerage disguised as an Irish immigrant; her 13-part series on the abuse of immigrants at Ellis Island forced congressional action and the removal of the Ellis Island commissioner. She covered the Leopold–Loeb hearings of 1924, where she met fellow reporter John Origen Herrick — they married on 6 September 1924, and the judge reportedly delayed sentencing to accommodate the wedding. In March 1930 she interviewed Al Capone, who told her: \"All I ever did is sell beer and whiskey to our best people.\" From 1933 she was one of Eleanor Roosevelt's \"faithful four\" at the First Lady's women-only press conferences and president of the Women's National Press Club (1933–35). She resigned from the Tribune in May 1934 after criticism from Col. McCormick, and wrote for the New York Daily News and Country Gentleman. She died in Santa Fe on 17 December 1962. The odd detail: The judge in the \"crime of the century\" is said to have moved his sentencing date around a reporter's wedding.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The model for a woman reporter PC: undercover in steerage, a Capone interview, a Washington desk. Her 1934 break with McCormick can be given an occult reason — a story the Tribune spiked.",
