      "summary": "From about 1918, as Czechs moved west, Jews (mostly Russian and Eastern European) moving out of Maxwell Street made North Lawndale \"the largest Jewish settlement in Chicago, containing approximately one-quarter of the city's Jewish population.\" In 1927 the Jewish People's Institute (formerly the Chicago Hebrew Institute) opened a one-million-dollar building on Douglas Blvd. The Yiddish Forward used the opening to describe the old split between \"we\" — Eastern European immigrant Jews \"with disturbed souls\" — and \"they,\" \"the calm, patient, wealthy Jews of Western Europe … the German Jews,\" who were \"only related to their Jewish brothers by throwing sops to them\"; after the war, it said, prosperity and intermarriage were dissolving the partition between the two \"tribes.\" The JPI's players won the Rockefeller McCormick Silver Cup in the Drama League tournament (1931) and it opened a natural history museum in 1933 (unverified — V_R15). The odd detail: the Forward's \"two tribes\" — the \"German\" Jews and the \"Russian\" Jews of Chicago, as the period itself sorted them.",
