      "summary": "The 1918 directory's language is the period's own: the Crittenton Anchorage (est. 1888, capacity 22, free) \"provides a home and employment for unfortunate and erring girls and women\"; the Salvation Army home (Ensign Louise V. Andrews in charge, capacity 45) gives \"hospital care for unmarried mothers and erring women\" and \"helps them to become good mothers and housekeepers\"; Beulah (1896, 40 beds) takes \"unmarried mothers and helpless, deserted wives\"; St. Margaret's (1911) let mothers and babies stay two years; the Maternity and Infant Hospital on Kedzie charged $7 a week and \"secures homes\" for the infants. St. Vincent's Infant Asylum (Daughters of Charity; est. 1880; capacity 300, free, city-subsidised) took foundlings and destitute children under four from the Juvenile Court, ran a maternity ward \"for unfortunate women\", and \"places children in families\" — the Catholic adoption pipeline. The Chicago Foundlings' Home cared for \"infant waifs and homeless, penniless mothers with infants\". The odd detail: The same La Salle Street block: the Salvation Army's rescue home at No. 1332 and the nuns' foundling asylum at No. 721 — the two ends of one road for a pregnant girl with no husband.",
