      "summary": "After a raid on the Brite Ranch on Christmas Day 1917, Texas Rangers of Company B under Capt. James Monroe Fox, four local ranchers (Buck Pool, John Pool, Tom Snyder, Raymond Fitzgerald) and soldiers of the 8th U.S. Cavalry separated 15 unarmed Mexican-descent men and boys from their families and shot them; the victims included Antonio Castañeda, Longino Flores and Pedro Herrera. Fox's report claimed residents had fired on the Rangers; investigation showed the victims \"did not die in a shootout, but were killed while they were unarmed and in Texas Ranger custody.\" Governor William P. Hobby disbanded Company B in June 1918, fired five Rangers and transferred seven; Fox resigned; no one was prosecuted. The survivors fled to Mexico; in 1926 Mexican attorneys filed twelve claims with the U.S.–Mexico General Claims Commission. The Texas Historical Commission put up a marker in 2018. The odd detail: the massacre led to the disbanding of a Ranger company, which the state treated as the punishment.",
