      "summary": "The Navy collier Cyclops, overloaded with 10,800 long tons of manganese ore from Brazil for munitions, made an unscheduled stop at Barbados and sailed on 4 March 1918 with 306 crew and passengers; she was never seen again. Her captain, George Worley, had been born Johan Frederick Wichmann in Germany; crewmen described him chasing an ensign round the deck with a pistol and doing his rounds in long underwear and a derby hat. Aboard as a passenger was the US Consul-General in Rio, Alfred L. M. Gottschalk, said in a contemporary telegram to be as hated for pro-German sympathies as Worley. Theories: a U-boat (Germany denied it), structural failure, a shifted wet cargo; the Navy's historians favour a sudden storm. The odd detail: a German-born captain and a pro-German consul on a ship carrying war metal — and no wreck, no signal, no wreckage.",
