      "summary": "Five armed Mexicans robbed the terminal of 9,427.81 pesos ($5,562.40) and killed four Americans — the machinist Alfred Esparcia, assistant cashier L. R. Millard, warehouse clerk L. A. Dunn and commissary clerk R. M. Cooper — and the Mexican foreman Natividad Flores. Consul Dawson stressed that \"no resistance was made by any of the men at the terminal.\" Mexico's Foreign Office replied on 17 July 1918 that it had directed the authorities to arrest and punish the guilty. Dawson's covering report carries a line a GM can use verbatim: banditry \"has become so rife all through this territory that it has largely ceased to be the subject of comment and is accepted as a part of each day's experiences, even within the limits of the city of Tampico.\" The odd detail: five men dead for five and a half thousand dollars, with no one resisting — the consul himself could find \"no apparent cause\" for the killing.",
