      "id": "M06-007",
      "title": "The Battle of Ambos Nogales",
      "date_start": "1918-08-27",
      "date_end": "1918-08-27",
      "date_label": "27 August 1918",
      "category": "The border and the crime of the Mexican north, 1910–1940",
      "tags": [
        "border",
        "crime",
        "Battle of Ambos Nogales",
        "BENDS"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Zeferino Gil Lamadrid",
        "Pvt. William Klint",
        "Felix B. Peñaloza",
        "Lt. Col. Frederick J. Herman",
        "Gen. DeRosey Cabell",
        "Plutarco Elías Calles"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Nogales",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Arizona and Nogales, Sonora — International Street and the customs house",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Nogales%2C%20Arizona%20and%20Nogales%2C%20Sonora%20%E2%80%94%20International%20Street%20and%20the%20customs%20house"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "A Mexican carpenter, Zeferino Gil Lamadrid, tried to cross into Arizona without inspection; a U.S. Customs inspector (Arthur G. Barber) shouted for him to halt while Mexican customs officers told him to stay put. Private William Klint raised his rifle and a shot was fired (a warning shot, it may be). Within minutes the two towns were in a gunfight: Lt. Col. Frederick Herman rushed three troops of the 10th Cavalry (Black cavalry) and three companies of the 35th Infantry to the line. Felix B. Peñaloza, mayor of Nogales, Sonora, 53, was mortally wounded by a shot from the Arizona side while walking toward the line under a white flag tied to his cane. US dead: 4–5 (Klint, Lt. Loftus, Cpls. Lots and Whitworth, Capt. Hungerford, per the article); Mexican: an official 15, but the U.S. counted \"129 new graves\". A three-year-old, Julia Medina, was among the dead. The trigger for the ceasefire at 7:45 p.m. was a white flag over the Mexican customs house. Rumours that German agents had led the attack (an anonymous letter to Herman; \"two German agents\" among the dead) were searched for in local, state and national archives and not found. Gov. Plutarco Elías Calles and Gen. DeRosey Cabell agreed on the first permanent border fence, two miles down International Street. The odd detail: the first permanent fence on the US–Mexico border was built because of a fight that began when a carpenter would not be inspected — and the wartime rumour that German agents were behind it left no trace in any archive.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the outline; casualty counts differ by source (15 vs. 125+ Mexican dead).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the first fence on the border went up after a fight in which a mayor was shot under a white flag and the Army blamed \"German agents\" — in a setting where Germany really did win and really does back revolutions, the rumour that the archive cannot confirm is precisely the kind a Section 13 file would keep alive.",
