      "summary": "On 12 October 1918, in a drought, with 60-plus-mph winds, sparks from railroad locomotives set the northern Minnesota forest afire. 453 people died; the towns of Cloquet, Moose Lake, Kettle River, Brookston and Arnold were destroyed (38 communities affected); about 250,000 acres burned, damages $72 million, about 52,000 injured or displaced, about 12,000 homeless; about 4,700 Cloquet residents fled to Duluth and Superior. An AP correspondent reported \"75 bodies piled in a burned building\" at Moose Lake and 100 more on the roads. National Guard units rescued 200 TB patients from the Nopeming Sanatorium; the Red Cross built over 200 temporary shelters by 15 November; Cloquet's Northwest Paper Company was running again within a week. The odd detail: Wikipedia ranks it the third-deadliest wildfire on record (behind Peshtigo 1871 and one other); an AP man counted \"75 bodies piled in a burned building\" in a single town.",
