      "summary": "In the autumn of 1919 Marine Commandant George Barnett wrote a \"personal and confidential\" letter to Col. John H. Russell complaining that \"practically indiscriminate killing of natives has been going on for some time\", which prompted the first inquiry. The letter was published in October 1920, in the middle of the presidential campaign. Abuses were concentrated in the north under Maj. Clark H. Wells, relieved in 1920 after Barnett found he had falsified reports. Gendarmes testified to prisoner executions; two officers, Freeman Lang and Dorcas Williams, were implicated; Gaillard records 19 caco prisoners executed at Hinche in January 1919 under a Captain Lavoie. The Navy, the Corps and later the Senate each found \"only a few\" substantiated cases. The odd detail: the Corps' own commandant wrote the sentence the occupation's critics quoted for years.",
