      "summary": "Sugar sold at 1.93 cents a pound in July 1914 (unverified — V_M03: Wikipedia's Dance of the Millions page returned 404); wartime demand (and Allied price controls) kept it at a few cents until controls ended; then it ran to a peak of 22.5 cents on 20 May 1920. Cuba built 25 new mills in the war years and borrowed against the crop; mansions went up in Vedado and Miramar. By 18 November 1920 sugar was 4¾ cents, and below 3 cents in December (Cuba Center; Encyclopedia.com says \"less than 4 cents\" by December); the government declared a moratorium (October 1920 — month via general accounts, UNVERIFIED here); twenty banks had collapsed by summer 1921, Cuban-owned banks worst, foreign banks surviving and taking over the mortgaged mills. The Miramar developer José López Rodríguez, \"el gallego Pote\", who had received millions in unsecured loans, hanged himself. The historian Hugh Thomas: the rest of 1920 passed \"in a dream-like atmosphere, more reminiscent of a film comedy than real life.\" The odd detail: \"Pote\" — the king of the boom's building lots — hanging himself in the middle of the suburb he had sold.",
