      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Hibbing",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "on the Mesabi Iron Range",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Hibbing%2C%20on%20the%20Mesabi%20Iron%20Range%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "the two-mile move from North Hibbing to the present town",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20two-mile%20move%20from%20North%20Hibbing%20to%20the%20present%20town%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The Oliver Iron Mining Company (a U.S. Steel subsidiary) wanted the ore under North Hibbing, and under Mayor Victor Power the council approved the move. From 1919 to 1921 about 188 buildings (a search summary says 185 houses and 20 businesses, cost about $16 million) were moved two miles south by horses, logs, farm tractors, a steam crawler and steel cables; big buildings were cut in two or three pieces, and crews lifted the electric lines with long poles. The Sellers Hotel collapsed in transit in 1920; people who came back after an absence reportedly got lost in the new town. The Oliver Company paid for sewers, electricity and public buildings in the new town. By 1935 the remaining North Hibbing structures were demolished and their owners paid little; the city sold its last North Hibbing property to the company in 1955. The odd detail: A whole town was moved by tractor and rope, and the old town was eaten — the largest open-pit iron mine in Minnesota took its place, and the people of the moved town got lost in their own streets.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the outline (numbers vary 185–188).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A district where a town's \"old streets\" are a hole in the earth, and where the mining company owned the ground under the church, is a landscape built for a hunt: the cult that met in the basements of North Hibbing didn't get moved.",
