      "summary": "After Gary annexed Miller in 1919, U.S. Steel — which Wikipedia says \"illegally claimed ownership\" of the shore — \"bought\" 120 acres and gave it to the city as a park. George W. Maher and Sons designed the Aquatorium (1921), a bathhouse with bathing-suit rentals, showers and a second-floor viewing gallery, built on six acres from six basic cast blocks (about 90 percent of the structure — an early precast-concrete modular building); the recreation pavilion followed in 1923–24 at $350,000. The bathing beach itself was contested: the Gary American campaigned to integrate Marquette Beach. The Aquatorium closed in 1971, was rescued in 1991 (the Chanute Aquatorium Society coined \"Aquatorium,\" \"place to view the water\"), and restored in 1998–2000 as a museum of Chanute and the Tuskegee Airmen; NRHP listing 25 November 1994. The odd detail: The land the steel company \"bought\" from itself to give to the city is the very ground Chanute flew on, now a museum to flight, in a building that closed for twenty years.",
