      "summary": "Ernest G. Liebold bought the Dearborn Independent for Henry Ford in 1918. From May 22, 1920, it ran \"The International Jew\" for 91 consecutive weeks, written chiefly by editor William J. Cameron under Ford's direction, and promoted the forged Protocols of the Elders of Zion. Ford dealers had subscription quotas, and circulation claims reached 700,000–900,000. Aaron Sapiro's 1927 libel suit forced a public apology (June/July 1927) — whose signature, evidence suggests, Harry Bennett forged — and the paper closed in December 1927. On his 75th birthday, July 30, 1938, Ford accepted the Grand Cross of the German Eagle; GM's James Mooney got the Order's 1st Class on June 9, 1938, and Charles Lindbergh the Order with Star on October 19, 1938. The odd detail: the car dealers of America were made to sell a hate newspaper on quota.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Dearborn as the Golden Circle's printing house — the Protocols forgery was itself born of a secret-police forgery; a Section 13 file could trace which lodge fed Cameron his \"documents.\"",
