      "summary": "An interfraternity honour society calling itself the Ku Klux Klan (founded at the University of Illinois in 1906, unrelated to the political Klan) opened a Wisconsin chapter in May 1919, founded by Gordon B. Bilderback, A. Ingwersen, Robert Lorenz, C. E. Lovejoy and Robert Tutwiler. It eventually reached thirteen chapters; in April 1923 it renamed itself Tu-Mas to separate itself from the resurgent Second Klan and was defunct by 1939. The actor Fredric March was a member at Wisconsin. Separately the Second Ku Klux Klan set up an affiliated local fraternity, Kappa Beta Lambda (\"Klansmen Be Loyal\"), at Wisconsin in 1923 (the Wisconsin Union Theater page dates it to 1924) — the only chapter ever, dissolved by 1927. A UW report by Stephen Kantrowitz and Floyd Rose to the chancellor (commissioned by Chancellor Rebecca Blank) later described Klan activity on campus and in Madison in the 1920s; I did not read the report itself. The odd detail: In the same years the campus was the intellectual base of La Follette progressivism, it had two organisations called \"Klan,\" one a joke and one not.",
