      "summary": "Mexican workers first came to the mills in numbers as strikebreakers and labour-shortage hires in 1919; by the 1930 census Indiana Harbor held 5,343 Mexicans (about 20 percent of its foreign-born white population), and Gary another 3,486 — in all 9,007 Mexicans in Lake County, out of 9,642 in the whole state. Some historians call it the most densely packed Mexican community in the country: about 5,000 people in under one square mile, known as La Colonia del Harbor. The colonia founded mutual-aid societies, a community newspaper, and a Mexican Independence Day tradition, and between 1922 and 1928 self-funded a church (the first of three buildings of Our Lady of Guadalupe), a community library, and a music and theatre programme. Labour recruitment from Texas to the Midwest was restricted by \"Emigrant Agent\" acts in the 1920s. The odd detail: A colonia of 5,000 in a square mile that had its own library and classical theatre productions, self-funded, in a mill district with no city services worth naming.",
