      "summary": "Prohibition began on 17 January 1920; rum-running followed within weeks. On the Mexican border the smugglers, the tequileros, were often \"three to six farm boys, usually on horses, guiding a few well-packed mules\" — many of them former soldiers of the Revolution — working with Tejano intermediaries. One intercepted mule train carried 550 bottles (worth $1,375); a strong mule carried 50 or more. In February 1927, in Jim Hogg County, Texas, mounted customs inspectors and smugglers traded shots; one smuggler was killed, six horses and several hundred bottles seized. The publisher of The Line Riders (a book on this subject) describes it as a long and deadly \"liquor war\" on the Rio Grande, with late-night shootouts and horseback pursuits. Enforcement was uneven: \"local farmer\" moonshiners were usually left alone while cross-border tequila traders were prosecuted fiercely. Nuevo Laredo's Cadillac Bar (founded by Achille \"Mayo\" Mehault) served Laredo's drinkers who crossed \"as though they were walking across a street.\" Smuggling peaked 1919–28 and had \"come to a quiet end\" in Prohibition's last five years. The odd detail: the Prohibition frontier's front line was mules carrying 50 bottles each, driven by veterans of Villa's and Carranza's armies.",
