      "summary": "Importing liquor into the Bahamas was legal, and after the colonial government raised its duties in 1919 Nassau became the warehouse of American Prohibition: 914 gallons of Scotch landed in 1919, 386,000 gallons in 1920. Schooners carried it north to \"Rum Row\" off New York and west to Florida; the Lucerne Hotel was bootleggers' headquarters and held an annual Bootleggers' Ball. The windfall dredged Nassau harbour, resurfaced roads, and paid for a sewer system and a 2,300-volt diesel generator. Gertrude \"Cleo\" Lythgoe (born Bowling Green, Ohio, 1888) arrived in 1922 as agent for a Scotch house, the only woman with a Bahamas wholesale liquor licence, working from a Market Street warehouse; Bill McCoy ran whisky north in the schooner Arethusa/Tomoka until the cutter Seneca took him on 15 November 1923. Washington asked London to stop the trade; Britain refused. The US pushed the territorial limit out to 12 miles on 21 April 1924. The odd detail: American Prohibition built the modern Nassau — the sewer, the power plant and the harbour were all paid for in duty on whisky bound for Chicago's and New York's speakeasies.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
