      "summary": "In 1918 the city's nine breweries employed 6,000 and produced $35 million; under Prohibition they turned to near beer, soda, candy, cheese \"and even snowplows.\" At one point in 1921 the whole eastern district of Wisconsin had one federal Prohibition agent; the police \"sponge squads\" visited speakeasies occasionally and anyone could drink \"without much fear of arrest.\" Italians distilled from apples, potatoes and cherries and used teenagers to carry it; the rich drove out to Club Madrid; doctors wrote prescriptions. The Wisconsin Anti-Prohibition Association (1922) filled the Auditorium with 8,000; in 1929 the state repealed its Severson enforcement act 350,337 to 196,402, Milwaukee voting six to one for repeal. The Chicago war reached the city twice: in Nov 1927 Chicago police, on an anonymous tip, arrested the Milwaukee gunman Angelo La Mantio and four others hired by Joe Aiello to kill Capone and Lombardo — La Mantio confessed when apartment receipts were found in his pockets, and Capone posted two dozen gunmen outside the station to wait for Aiello; and on 23 May 1931 Aiello's nephew Frank Aiello Jr. was shot dead through the window of his Milwaukee home while playing cards, a killing police tied to the Chicago feud although he had no known rackets. Milwaukee bootlegging otherwise \"went on without the volume of violence\" of other cities. The odd detail: A man shot at his own card table through his own window in Milwaukee for being the nephew of a dead Chicagoan.",
