      "summary": "After the door photograph, the Marines buried Péralte secretly — a Marine sergeant, Julian A. Kelly, reported in 1937 that it had been near Caracol, about 35 km east of Cap-Haïtien. After the occupation ended, his remains were exhumed and given a national funeral at Cap-Haïtien, attended by President Sténio Vincent; most sources date it 1935, one gives 26 November 1934 (with his mother present and an immense crowd). The odd detail: the occupier hid a body so it could not become a shrine; the country's first act of the \"second independence\" was to dig it up.",
