      "summary": "Allen (born Salt Lake City 23 March 1884) became Ohio's first woman assistant county prosecutor in 1919, was elected a Common Pleas judge in Cleveland in 1920 on a non-partisan ticket, insisted on the full range of cases and tried nearly 900 of them, and in 1921 sentenced the gangster Frank Motto to death. In November 1922 she was elected to the Ohio Supreme Court — the first woman on any state high court — and re-elected in 1928. Roosevelt nominated her to the Sixth Circuit on 6 March 1934; she was confirmed on 15 March and commissioned 21 March — the first woman Article III federal judge — and sat 25 years (chief judge 1958–59). When Brandeis retired in 1939 she was talked of for the Supreme Court and never nominated. She died at Waite Hill, Ohio, 12 September 1966. The odd detail: A woman judge in Cleveland put a gangster in the electric chair in 1921 — thirteen years before Chicago's courts would seat a woman on the felony bench.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Sixth Circuit covers Ohio, Michigan, Kentucky and Tennessee — the Black Legion's whole territory. A federal appeals judge who will hear a woman investigator's evidence is a rare door; IHS at John Carroll would know her from Cleveland.",
