      "summary": "James Henry Breasted, the Egyptologist who coined \"Fertile Crescent\", founded the Oriental Institute in May 1919 on a $50,000 five-year pledge from John D. Rockefeller Jr.; he backed Carter's Tutankhamun work (1922–27) and was among the first scholars in the tomb. The Institute's Khorsabad expedition dug the palace of Sargon II and shipped a colossal human-headed winged bull (lamassu) to Chicago, installed in the new building's Assyrian gallery (about 40 tons, 16 feet — UNVERIFIED figures this session). Breasted died in New York on December 2, 1935, of a streptococcus infection on returning from his last expedition; the newspapers of the day filed him under \"the curse of Tutankhamun\" (legend says — not on the page read). The odd detail: The man who opened Tut's tomb with Carter died of an infection caught on the way home from Egypt, in the year the bull went up in Hyde Park.",
