      "summary": "The Packers were founded 11 August 1919 by Curly Lambeau and George Whitney Calhoun; Lambeau got $500 for uniforms from the Indian Packing Company, where he was a shipping clerk; his girlfriend Agnes Aylward suggested the name. They joined the American Professional Football Association in 1921 but the franchise was revoked when \"college players\" were used in a post-season game; Lambeau appealed and paid a $50 fee to re-enter. A rainstorm that fell \"3/100th of an inch\" short of insurance coverage pushed them to near-bankruptcy by the end of 1922. In 1923 the \"Hungry Five\" local businessmen formed the Green Bay Football Corporation, a publicly owned stock structure that survives. The Packers won three straight NFL championships (1929, 1930, 1931), with a record of 12–0–1 in 1929 (the only undefeated season in their history), 10–3–1 and 12–2; Hall of Famers included Cal Hubbard and Mike Michalske; Don Hutson signed in 1935; they beat the Boston Redskins 21–6 for the 1936 title. Johnny \"Blood\" McNally, a Hall of Fame halfback, used a pseudonym early in his career. The odd detail: The team that won the first \"three-peat\" was owned by the town, because in 1923 nobody in Green Bay could afford it alone.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A packing company's $500 in 1919 and a public stock offering in 1923 — a town-owned team as an alibi for a town-owned secret. The 1936 title game is a Sunday when half of Wisconsin is at a stadium and the other half is not.",
