      "summary": "Founded 24 June 1919 by William C. Linton, with Anthony Overton, Jesse Binga and Oscar De Priest among its backers, the Whip had 65,000 copies in circulation within a year (against the Defender's 185,000) and, after Linton died in March 1922, was run by the Yale-educated lawyer Joseph D. Bibb (with A. C. MacNeal — from memory) as the Black Belt's militant paper. Its great campaign was the \"Don't Spend Your Money Where You Can't Work\" boycott of white-owned stores that would not hire Black clerks — begun in 1929–30 as the Depression bit (dates from memory) — which Wikipedia credits with over 15,000 jobs for Black Chicagoans and which spread to Cleveland (John O. Holly's Future Outlook League, 1935), Harlem and other cities. The paper folded in 1939. The odd detail: The picket line as an economic weapon was a Chicago Black newspaper's invention, financed by the same banker whose bank had just failed.",
