      "summary": "Eight White Sox threw the 1919 Series; a Cook County jury acquitted them on August 2, 1921, and Commissioner Landis banned them anyway on August 3, 1921 (\"regardless of the verdict of juries\"). Buck Weaver, who took no money but sat in the meetings, applied for reinstatement on January 13, 1922 and kept petitioning every commissioner for the rest of his life (he lived in Chicago, playing semi-pro ball); Shoeless Joe recanted his confession and died in 1951 still banned; ringleader Chick Gandil played semi-pro and confessed in print only in 1956. The Sox fell to seventh in 1921 and did not contend again until 1936. Whether Arnold Rothstein directed the fix is still argued. The odd detail: A Chicago jury said not guilty; a judge in Chicago said banned for life the next day. Which one was the law?",
