      "summary": "By 1919 about 2,699 Black workers were on the payroll of the U.S. Steel mills in Gary, in a Black community that had grown from roughly 400 people in 1906 to more than 5,000 in 1920. Louis H. Caldwell — born in Copiah County, Mississippi, a Pullman porter who worked his way through law school, in Gary from 1915 and one of only three Black attorneys there, a founding officer of the Gary NAACP — and steelworker C. D. Elston, who quit his job to work for the Allied Iron and Steel Council, began holding separate meetings for Black workers in February 1919 and spoke \"at almost every\" ethnic workers' gathering across the city through September. When the strike began, roughly 85 percent of the regional workforce joined it; one account says the company offered $5 to Black workers who would scab and that most took the money and left. Later Black steelworker organizers named in the same account include George Kimbley (first Black union staff representative) and William Young (first Black grievance man, hired 1922). The odd detail: The famous story is that Black workers were brought in as strikebreakers — the local record shows a Black lawyer and a Black steelworker addressing Slovak, Serb and Polish meetings for the union that spring.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (numbers from a single advocacy summary; the outline is consistent with the Discover Indiana material).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Caldwell is the sort of man a Kabbalah or IHS contact would need in Gary — a lawyer everyone trusts and no one watches. The Steel Trust's spy files on him would list every meeting he addressed; a GM can read them as a map of the town's ethnic fault lines.",
