      "summary": "At about 4:55 p.m. Goodyear's hydrogen blimp Wingfoot Air Express, flying from Grant Park to the White City amusement park at 1,200 feet, caught fire \"for unknown reasons\" and fell through the bank's skylight into the crowded banking floor. Thirteen died — ten bank employees and three from the airship — and 27 on the ground were hurt. Pilot Jack Boettner and chief mechanic Harry Wacker parachuted clear; mechanic Carl Weaver's parachute caught fire; passenger Earl Davenport tangled in the gondola cables; photographer Milton Norton broke both legs landing and died in hospital. It was the worst airship accident in the country to that date; Chicago wrote new aviation rules, closed the Grant Park airstrip, and the search for a proper field led to Chicago Air Park — Municipal Airport. The odd detail: the burning airship came through the ceiling onto the tellers' cages during the afternoon rush; every Loop clerk in 1934 remembers where they were.",
