      "summary": "The national strike began 22 September 1919. To replace strikers, mills brought in Black and Mexican labour: one summary puts it at 30,000–40,000 Black and Mexican workers brought in nationally. At Inland Steel (Indiana Harbor) the company had ~90 Mexicans in 1918, recruited several hundred more for the strike and housed and fed them inside the plant gates; it hired ~3,600 Mexicans in 1919–25 and by 1926 had 2,526 (about 30% of 7,000 workers). A South Chicago account (search summary; not confirmed in a primary source I read) says some managers feared Black strikebreakers would trigger violence in South Chicago and so turned to Mexicans; by 1925 Mexicans were about 8 percent of the region's mill workforce and the second-largest immigrant group on the mill floor after Poles (search summary). The odd detail: men were fed and slept inside the mill gate for the duration; a strike that built two communities (Black and Mexican) that the Depression then turned on.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for Inland's numbers; Medium for the South Chicago \"fear of violence\" claim and the national totals (search summaries).",
