      "summary": "The national strike began 22 September 1919 (350,000 workers across the country); in Gary about 85 percent of steelworkers refused to work. They worked twelve-hour shifts, 66-hour weeks. Discover Indiana dates the trigger to 5 October (Wikipedia's Gary page says 4 October): strikers wrecked a trolley carrying replacement workers on Broadway and a brick hit a strike supporter, and police responded. On 6 October the mayor called out seven Indiana militia units and federal troops under General Leonard Wood arrived; by evening the whole city was under Army control (R36 has the full Wood story). The company ordered 30,000+ Black workers from the South as replacements, and Mexican workers were hired as non-union replacements — the first Mexicans in the mills in numbers; some were housed on mill grounds. The strike ended in Gary within a month of the Army's arrival and nationally in January 1920. The eight-hour day arrived in 1923. The Black share of the mill workforce went from 8.8% (1919) to 20.5% (1923); the Mexican share grew to 8% by 1925. The odd detail: The strike that was crushed in October 1919 built the two communities — Black and Mexican — that the Depression then turned on.",
