      "summary": "After a scuffle on 4 October between strikers and strikebreakers, the Gary mayor called out Indiana militia; on 6 October 1919 federal regulars \"under the command of General Leonard Wood\" came from Fort Sheridan and \"martial law was at once proclaimed.\" By the end of that day \"the entire city of Gary was controlled by the US Army,\" and all public rallies and picketing were prohibited. The steel strike leader William Z. Foster, in his 1920 book, says soldiers arrested strike leaders and pickets and made them split wood and sweep the streets in front of City Hall while families watched from windows; that steel officials told strikers the \"U.S.\" in U.S. Steel meant government ownership and threatened deportation or Fort Leavenworth; and that \"spectacular midnight raids\" broke a strike that had been 97 percent effective. Foster also wrote that the operation might have been \"staged to further his [Wood's] political fortunes.\" The strike \"effectively ended in Gary less than a month later\" though it lasted nationally until January 1920. A web-search summary of a Northwest Indiana Times centennial story gives 4,000+ federal troops and Wood \"in charge of Gary, East Chicago and Indiana Harbor\" — not read; UNVERIFIED. Wikipedia's page on the strike says the Army took over on 6 October but does not name Wood. The odd detail: In the setting the man who becomes President for 1921–27 ran a Great Lakes steel city under martial law in 1919, and a strike leader accused him of staging it for his career.",
