      "summary": "The Sociedad Colombo-Alemana de Transportes Aéreos was founded in Barranquilla on 5 December 1919 by Colombian and German partners and began operations on 5 September 1920 — the first airline in Latin America. It flew Junkers F 13 and W 34 seaplanes that landed on the Magdalena River where no airfields existed. Its principal shareholder was the Austrian industrialist Peter Paul von Bauer. Washington feared that German pilots flying within range of the Panama Canal might spy on it or \"convert civilian aircraft into bombers, in order to attack the Panama Canal\"; the US Government forced von Bauer to sell his shares to Pan American Airways, and in June 1940 SCADTA was shut down and merged by Colombia with the state airline SACO to form Avianca. The odd detail: for twenty years the first airline in the hemisphere was flown by German pilots in German seaplanes a few hundred miles from the Canal.",
