      "summary": "Cayo Báez, a young peasant, was seized by occupation forces and tortured with a heated machete to make him reveal where resistance fighters were hiding; he survived (sixteen others, one source says, were tortured and shot). His scarred body was photographed and the picture was published by Horacio Blanco Fombona's magazine Las Letras; it travelled through the foreign press and became the occupation's emblematic image of abuse — the Dominican counterpart to the Péralte photograph. The odd detail: two occupations, two photographs: in Haiti the occupier made the picture and lost control of it; in Santo Domingo the occupied made the picture and the world saw it.",
