      "summary": "A Chicago criminal-defence lawyer said to earn $500,000 a year, George Remus \"memorized the Volstead Act\" and found the loophole that let him buy distilleries and pharmacies to make and sell bonded liquor for \"medicinal\" use. He moved to Cincinnati because within 300 miles of it about 80 per cent of all American bourbon was made and stored. Within three years he had about $40 million and 3,000 employees and controlled many distilleries, including Fleischmann. His \"Death Valley Farm\" in Westwood was an elaborate underground facility with concealed tunnels. On New Year's Eve 1922 his \"Marble Palace\" mansion hosted 100 couples, who left with diamond stickpins and automobiles. Mabel Walker Willebrandt, a California lawyer appointed Assistant Attorney General in 1921, pursued him relentlessly; convicted in 1925, he served time in Atlanta. The odd detail: The legal drug trade was the cover — \"medicinal\" permits, warehouse withdrawals through doctors and pharmacies — and the most powerful bootlegger in America ran it from a mansion in Cincinnati, not Chicago.",
