      "summary": "Chicago's oldest small park (1842) had been \"Bughouse Square\" since the 1890s (after the mental hospitals nearby). On warm nights soapboxers held forth on everything from sex to Communism; the 1920s–30s were the apex, when the revolutionary left was joined by \"poets, religionists, and cranks\", hoboes, artists and writers, in the manner of Speakers' Corner; the crowd elected its own honorary \"king\". The Dil Pickle Club grew out of this scene. The Newberry Library's later Bughouse Square Debates (1986–2021) revived it. The odd detail: The city's free-speech forum sat opposite its rarest-books library, one street apart, with the mental hospitals that named it a few blocks away.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
