      "summary": "A French colony next to British Newfoundland and Canada: when the Volstead Act took effect (January 1920), Canadian distillers could not ship to the United States but could ship to France. The islands became North America's liquor depot: in 1931 the New York Times reported 1,815,271 US gallons of whisky imported from Canada in twelve months, most of it bound for the US. Warehouses ran out; distillers rented islanders' basements, and some four million bottles sat in store at any time (unverified — V_M10: the Smithsonian page gives \"4 million liters\" of whiskey and a 1929 import of 1,533,485 gallons; the 1,815,271-gallon NYT figure is confirmed on Wikipedia). Legend (labelled): Al Capone visited, stayed at the Hôtel Robert and gave a nervous islander his straw hat, which is still shown there [V_M10: the Smithsonian article quotes Daniel Okrent, \"There's no evidence to support that Al Capone ever went anywhere near Saint Pierre\"; the Hôtel Robert and hat details were not found in any page read]. Repeal (December 1933) put the islands back into depression. (On 24 December 1941 Free French forces seized them from Vichy.) The odd detail: a French colony's economy was the Chicago Outfit's supply chain.",
