      "summary": "Canada's provincial prohibitions never banned manufacturing liquor for export, so Canadian distillers could legally make what American smugglers illegally carried; the Windsor–Detroit crossing became the industrial-scale route, with a widely repeated claim that 75% of all liquor smuggled into the US in Prohibition came through Detroit. Hiram Walker's distillery (founded 1858; the company town of Walkerville incorporated 7 April 1890 with Walker as first mayor) made Canadian Club; in 1926 the Walker family sold it to Harry C. Hatch for $15 million. The Ambassador Bridge opened in 1929 and the Detroit–Windsor Tunnel — 5,160 ft long, about $25 million, the third underwater vehicular tunnel in the US — on 3 November 1930, three years before repeal. The odd detail: Legend says Hiram Walker ran a whisky pipeline under the Detroit River during the Civil War; historians put that down to jealous competitors — he simply loaded jugs onto American ferries.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on dates and the sale; Medium on the \"75%\" figure (repeated widely, not sourced in what I read).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A legend of a pipe under the river that historians say never existed — the three-layers method: period reading (jealous rivals), folklore (the pipeline), what is actually down there.",
