      "summary": "Through Prohibition the Uihleins' Schlitz made \"malt syrup, bakery products, and sodas, among other items,\" and for a while chocolate and candy at a separate plant; the family — William J. Uihlein had brought America's first pure yeast culture from Copenhagen in 1883; Joseph Uihlein Sr. ran a refrigerator-car line in the 1890s — kept the company through three generations to 1976. After repeal in April 1933 Schlitz \"returned to its position as a national brewing leader,\" the largest brewer in America by 1947. The family's money later built the Performing Arts Center and the Schlitz Audubon nature reserve on land it had held since the 1880s. The odd detail: The beer barons of the ring spent the dry decade selling malt syrup (the home-brewer's raw material, sold with a straight face) and chocolate — and were richer at repeal than before.",
