      "summary": "By the standard estimate, 75 per cent of all the liquor smuggled into the United States during Prohibition came through Detroit. Ontario's \"export docks\" legally cleared cases for Cuba or Mexico that returned across a mile of river in rowboats, speedboats and, in winter, cars on the ice; Harry Low of Windsor's rum-running group grew rich enough to buy the Carling brewery in London, Ontario; Hamilton's Rocco Perri exported for the old distillers (Seagram, Gooderham & Worts) and \"helped these companies obtain a large share of the American market\". The Coast Guard's Detroit base and its 75-ft and 36-ft picket boats fought a running \"rum war\" on the river until repeal (5 December 1933). (Specific Coast Guard shootings on the river and the 1930 Canadian export ban are standard history but NOT confirmed in the pages read this session — see Leads.) The odd detail: The only international border in America you could drive a Model T across on foot-thick ice, with the cars that went through the ice still down there.",
