      "summary": "Aurora's first wave of Mexican immigrants, arriving after 1910 and in larger numbers in the 1920s when overseas immigration was restricted, found work with the CB&Q at the West Eola Reclamation Plant. The railroad supplied 20 decommissioned boxcars for workers and families; the settlement — called \"El Camp\" by the people who lived there — grew into \"a thriving, tight-knit community\" (a search summary of an NIU capstone puts it at about 1,000 people by the late 1920s (unverified — V_R38: no population figure in the article read)), and was dismantled in 1934 because of the Depression. The east-side neighbourhoods shaped by these migrants are Aurora's working-class core. The odd detail: A town in the city of lights had a village made from retired railway cars whose whole existence — company boxcars for company men — ended in the same year the Dillinger gunman John Hamilton was secretly buried in the Aurora–Oswego country (R02).",
