      "summary": "Born in Kansas 22 April 1891, Quinby specialised at the Post in \"cases where women were tried for murders of husbands, boyfriends, and lovers\" — more than 1,000 bylined stories on a paper that rarely gave bylines — and was Maurine Watkins's rival for the 1924 Murderess Row scoops. She interviewed Capone in jail on the tax case and \"shared a candy bar with him,\" covered his sister's wedding, rode an elephant in a parade and wrote it up, freelanced for the true-crime magazines, and in 1931 published Murder for Love, a book on women killers. In the Depression she reported on unemployed and homeless women in Chicago. She married Bruce Griggs, a Milwaukee Journal man, in 1932; he was killed in a car crash in December 1933. In January 1934 she went to the Journal on space rates, and that November began \"Dear Mrs. Griggs,\" the advice column she wrote — more than 15,000 of them — until 1985. She died in 1991, aged 100. The odd detail: The woman who wrote the book on Chicago's husband-killers spent the next fifty years telling Milwaukee wives what to do about theirs.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
