      "summary": "Tomás Garrido Canabal ran Tabasco (governor 1920–24 and 1931–34; its master to 1935) as a \"laboratory of the Revolution\": prohibition, \"rationalist\" schools, and war on the Church. Priests had to marry to serve. His Red Shirts (Camisas Rojas), a youth militia who sang the Internationale, burned saints' images in public bonfires. The Cathedral of Esquipulas in Villahermosa was closed (1928), sacked and its images burned (1930) to become a \"Rationalist School,\" and demolished in 1934; the Convent of Oxolotán was bombed on his orders. On his farm he kept a bull named God, an ox and a hog named Pope, a cow named Mary, and a donkey named Christ; his children were named Lenin and Zoila Libertad. Cárdenas made him Agriculture Secretary (1934), then forced him into exile in Costa Rica (1935); he died in Los Angeles (1943). The odd detail: the farm menagerie of blasphemies — a GM can put the donkey named Christ in a scene.",
