      "summary": "Nels Anderson's 1923 study The Hobo calls West Madison \"the slave market\" because the employment agencies were there: men \"bargain for jobs in distant places with the 'man catchers',\" scanning jobs \"chalked on the windows.\" On 14 August 1922 there were 39 licensed agencies of this kind — 18 on Canal Street, 13 on West Madison — placing an estimated 250,000 homeless men a year; the Dearborn Street public office refused \"dead line men,\" those living on Madison west of Canal, and clerks advised promising applicants to borrow a relative's address. In 1934–35 Sutherland and Locke found the shelter men still working West Madison — where men were \"recruited for the gandy or railroad jobs\" — and also: sorting potatoes at the Randolph Street market for 75 cents a day; freight-house work at 40 cents an hour, closed to strangers because regulars paid kickbacks to the bosses; docks, hoping to unload a boat; certain street corners where truck drivers picked up men who would work for almost nothing; and bill-peddling, where \"all push and shove to reach the man who is giving out the numbers,\" for $2.50 a day. On 5 September 1934 they counted 125 men in line at 20 \"L\" exits on Wells Street between Jackson and Lake, waiting from 5:30 a.m. for commuters' discarded newspapers — over 4,000 papers given away that morning, some resold at the shelters for a penny. The odd detail: \"Dead line men\": in 1923 an address on West Madison west of Canal was enough to make you unemployable at the state's own labour exchange.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for what the two studies report. The three public-office addresses are 1923; whether they moved by the 1930s is UNVERIFIED.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The owner asked where people stood in line for jobs: a \"man catcher\" chalking \"Railroad — ship tonight — Wisconsin\" on a West Madison window is the perfect lure for a cult that needs men who will not be missed (the Hunter Guild recruits the same way).",
