      "summary": "Rothkopf ran liquor production for the \"Cleveland Four\" (with Moe Dalitz, Morris Kleinman and Sam Tucker) and was credited with the \"erection and operation of the largest illegal distilleries ever found in the United States.\" By the early 1930s he was a partner in Prospect Advertising Co., a gambling front, and later in the Pettibone Club (Geauga County, 1939–49, per the Dalitz article) and the Jungle Inn. Police wanted him as a witness in the Potter murder (1931). In 1937 he and Max Diamond were convicted of tax evasion on $150,000 of liquor sales — four years and $5,000 each. He died of carbon monoxide in his car on his estate in 1956, a year after his wife Blanche's suicide. The odd detail: \"The largest illegal distilleries ever found in the United States\" were Cleveland's.",
