      "summary": "Chicle — the latex of the sapodilla tree — was the base of American chewing gum. Wrigley's (Chicago) built a 1920s \"bull market\" in Yucatecan chicle by sending gum to every address in every state's phone book; Philip Wrigley blended chicle from Campeche and Quintana Roo with chilte from Talpa, Jalisco. By the 1920s the average American chewed over a hundred sticks a year; by 1930 the US imported some 15 million pounds of chicle. Chicleros (tappers) scored the bark in zigzags for months in the forest; they lived under debt-servitude because the chicle companies sold them all their food; foreign gum firms were given land grants that displaced local Maya; over-bleeding killed much of the tree stock. In the 1940s synthetic bases ended the boom (general knowledge; not verified this session). The east-coast Maya's ferocity, Heyden says, had been softened by money brought by \"red men\" (North Americans) searching for chicle and for ruins. The odd detail: the lost Maya city Calakmul was found in the same chicle forest by a botanist studying how to tap sapodilla for the gum industry (see 19).",
