      "summary": "The Chicago gangsters retreated north. Al Capone had a home on a private lake five miles north of Couderay in the 1920s and 1930s — now a tourist site called \"The Hideout\", bought in spring 2010 by the Lac Courte Oreilles Band of Lake Superior Chippewa after the previous owners' bankruptcy. Ralph \"Bottles\" Capone — whose nickname came from running the organisation's legitimate soft-drink and bottling business — was convicted of tax evasion (three years, Leavenworth, then McNeil Island per the 1933 Treasury file); in the 1930s he bought a home in Mercer and later ran the Rex Hotel and Billy's Bar there; he died in Hurley, Wisconsin on November 22, 1974. \"Polack Joe\" Saltis, the Back of the Yards beer boss named on the 1930 Public Enemies list, lost his organisation when his political ally John \"Dingbat\" Oberta was found shot dead in his car with his chauffeur Sam Malaga on March 5, 1930 (the Saltis article's \"March 25\" is an error; the McErlane article and the record give March 5) and \"quickly retired to his home on Barker Lake in Winter, Wisconsin\". The Jack Zuta killing (Delafield, Aug 1, 1930) shows the lakes were not always safe. The odd detail: Capone's lake retreat now belongs to the Ojibwe nation whose reservation surrounds it.",
