      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Halls clustered on the Near North and West Sides",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(Clark Street, Madison Street, the Loop fringe) — Cressey's book maps the \"taxi-dance districts.\"",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Halls%20clustered%20on%20the%20Near%20North%20and%20West%20Sides%20%20%E2%80%94%20Cressey%27s%20book%20maps%20the%20%22taxi-dance%20districts.%22%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The ticket-a-dance system reached Chicago about 1920: Godfrey Johnson of the Mader-Johnson Dance Studios copied a New York \"ten-cent-ticket-a-dance\" scheme for his third-floor hall and found \"a rough hoodlum element from Clark Street\" replacing his pupils; Greek immigrant Nicholas Philocrates and others then opened halls that dropped the pretence of instruction. A dance cost a dime; the girl kept roughly half. Paul Goalby Cressey (1900–1955) (unverified — V2_R16), of the University of Chicago sociology department, published The Taxi-Dance Hall: A Sociological Study in Commercialized Recreation and City Life in 1932 from field notes and interviews with dancers, patrons and owners; his dancers were 15 to 28 years old, largely daughters of Polish, Swedish, German, French and Dutch immigrant homes, two-thirds without a father's support, many using aliases; his patrons were Italians, Poles, Greeks, Jews, lonely newcomers, older men and — his most-discussed group — Filipino men, who were welcome nowhere else. Reformers and the Juvenile Protective Association fought the halls for licensing and police supervision through the decade (unverified — V2_R16). The odd detail: The girls' aliases: a taxi-dancer could be three different names in three halls, and a lot of them were.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the book and the system; Medium on the specific Chicago hall count (not given). • Paul Goalby Cressey (1900–1955) (unverified — V2_R16), of the University of Chicago sociology department, published The Taxi-Dance Hall: A Sociological Study in Commercialized Recreation and City Life in 1932 from field notes and interviews with dancers, patrons and owners; • Reformers and the Juvenile Protective Association fought the halls for licensing and police supervision through the decade (unverified — V2_R16).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A taxi-dance girl with three names in three halls is a ready-made informant (or a fae changeling hiding in plain sight); the dime tickets are an untraceable currency for passing messages.",
