      "places": [
        {
          "name": "University of Chicago",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Hyde Park",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=University%20of%20Chicago%2C%20Hyde%20Park"
        },
        {
          "name": "Hull House",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(Breckinridge lived there on vacations)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Hull%20House%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Sophonisba Breckinridge (born Lexington, Kentucky, 1 April 1866; first woman admitted to the Kentucky bar, 1895) and Edith Abbott built the Chicago School of Civics and Philanthropy into the University of Chicago's Graduate School of Social Service Administration (merged 1920), founded Social Service Review (1927), and in the 1930s helped design and sell the Social Security Act of 1935. In 1933 Roosevelt sent Breckinridge to the Pan-American Conference at Montevideo, the first woman to represent the United States at such a conference. Edith's sister Grace Abbott (born Grand Island, Nebraska, 17 November 1878), who had run the Immigrants' Protective League in Chicago 1908–17, headed the federal Children's Bureau 1921–34, came home to teach at SSA, helped draft the Social Security Act, and died in Chicago on 19 June 1939, aged 60; her doctors had found multiple myeloma in 1938, and the Times obituary said \"anemia\" because cancer was not spoken of. Breckinridge died in Chicago 30 July 1948. The odd detail: The social-work profession in America was, in effect, designed by three unmarried women in Hyde Park; the paperwork a Depression investigator fills in is theirs.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — SSA graduates staff every relief office and juvenile court in Cook County — a woman investigator PC with an SSA certificate has a reason to be anywhere. The Immigrants' Protective League files (1908–17) list every girl who got off a train at Dearborn Station and was met by the wrong man.",
