      "summary": "Moses Annenberg rose from Chicago newsboy to a gang leader in the city's newspaper circulation wars (working for the Tribune, then Hearst), bought the Daily Racing Form in 1922, and controlled the General News Bureau / Nationwide News Service — \"the sole distributors of racetrack and other gambling results nationwide\" — the wire that told every bookie's handbook the results in time to pay out. James M. Ragen ran its day-to-day offices by the early 1930s. On August 11, 1939 Annenberg was indicted for evading $3,258,809.97 in income tax for 1932–36; forced by political opponents, he sold the wire to Ragen on November 15, 1939; on April 4, 1940 he pleaded guilty to one count ($1.2 million) and got three years and a fine reported as $8 million. (He had bought The Philadelphia Inquirer in 1936.) Ragen's Continental Press later fought off the Outfit until he was shot at State and Pershing on June 24, 1946 and died on August 15, 1946 \"following a mysteriously administered dose of mercury\" in hospital. The odd detail: The man who owned the nation's gambling news also owned a great city newspaper — and his successor survived a shotgun ambush only to be poisoned with mercury in his hospital bed.",
