      "summary": "R07 has the outline (1916–40, Garden Homes, the parks). The texture: Hoan (born 12 March 1881 in Waukesha; a failed Chicago restaurant before law school; city attorney 1910–16, elected with a 7,300-vote plurality of ~59,000) made Milwaukee only the twelfth U.S. city with comprehensive zoning (1920), bought food by the carload and sold it at city markets from 1917, ran a debt-amortization fund that made the city debt-free, and, in danger of missing the city payroll in January 1933, issued \"baby bonds\" — small-denomination bonds redeemable in four years with interest (EMKE does not say wages were paid in them). In 1932 he had his only Common Council majority (12 of 27 aldermen Socialist, plus two non-Socialist Polish aldermen who sided with him) and won with more than 60%; in 1936 he ran as the Farmer-Labor Progressive Federation candidate and got 54% (unverified — V_R27). He was president of the U.S. Conference of Mayors in 1934–35, ran for national party chairman in 1932 (lost 105–86 to Morris Hillquit), and on 6 April 1936 was on the cover of Time, which called Milwaukee \"a paragon of cities.\" His 1936 book City Government argued a civilization \"should be judged by the amount of service that the people in a community demand and can afford to pay for, and not by how low a tax rate they may have.\" The party behind him had about 3,000 dues-payers in a city of nearly 580,000 (1936). He lost in 1940 to the 32-year-old Carl Zeidler — the last sitting Milwaukee mayor ever defeated — and quit the party in 1941. The odd detail: The Socialist mayor's scrip: in the winter of 1933 a Milwaukee policeman's pay was a city bond. (unverified — V_R27)",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. • in 1936 he ran as the Farmer-Labor Progressive Federation candidate and got 54% (unverified — V_R27). • The Socialist mayor's scrip: in the winter of 1933 a Milwaukee policeman's pay was a city bond. (unverified — V_R27)",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The cleanest city hall in America, run for 24 years by a man whose whole party fits in a Turner hall — cult versus population inverted: the cult is the honest one. Section 13's Milwaukee file on Hoan is thin, and that worries them more than a thick one would.",
