      "summary": "Commissioned a second lieutenant in 1917, Dickerson served in the 365th Infantry (92nd Division) at Camp Grant and in France; he took his law degree at the University of Chicago in 1920, among its first Black graduates, and in 1921 became general counsel to the new Liberty Life Insurance Company of Illinois — which in 1929 merged into Supreme Liberty Life, the biggest Black insurer in the North (from memory; the Supreme Life Wikipedia page was not found). Under Governor Henry Horner he was Illinois Assistant Attorney General 1933–39; in 1939 he was elected alderman of the 2nd Ward, the first Black Democrat to hold it (to 1943), argued Hansberry v. Lee to victory in 1940, sat on Roosevelt's Fair Employment Practices Committee as acting chairman, and ran Supreme Liberty Life as president from 1955 to 1971. The odd detail: The Black Belt's leading Democrat of 1939 was a Republican governor's… no: a Democratic governor's assistant attorney general — Horner, the Jewish reformer the Kelly-Nash machine tried to dump in 1936 — which put Dickerson on the reform side of the machine that owned his ward.",
