      "summary": "Born in Lidköping, Sweden, 27 August 1872, Anderson emigrated at sixteen (1888), washed dishes in Michigan, and became a shoe stitcher in West Pullman, president of the women stitchers' Local 94 of the Boot and Shoe Workers in 1900, and a leader of the Chicago WTUL. In March 1920 she became the first director of the U.S. Women's Bureau and held it for 24 years under five presidents. Her Bureau's 1930s reports documented women's wages, Black women workers, and the union contracts that paid \"a set pay for men and a separate lower set pay for women\" while professing equal pay. She never married; she died in 1964. The odd detail: The federal official who knew what every working woman in America earned had learned it stitching shoes in West Pullman.",
