      "summary": "Three brothers — Edward P. (the leader), George and McKissack \"Mack\" Jones — turned a nickel game run out of the back of their tailor shop into the largest policy syndicate in the country. They bought a Ben Franklin variety store on 47th Street (about 150 employees, described as the only Black-owned department store in the world) which doubled as the front for the policy bank, and they poured policy money into Bronzeville businesses, professionals and artists. Mack Jones died in May 1944 in a car crash caused by a drunk driver; his estate was probated in March 1948 at $444,826.77. On 11 May 1946 the Outfit (Sam Giancana directing from a second car) kidnapped Ed Jones in front of his wife; a policeman who gave chase was hit by shotgun fire. George negotiated the ransom down from $250,000 to $100,000; Ed was released on 24 May 1946, after about thirteen days (the source gives no release location), with adhesive tape over his eyes and cotton in his ears, told \"cooperate or die.\" The Joneses left for Mexico, reportedly on a $200,000-a-year allowance from the Outfit, and their partner Teddy Roe kept the wheels. The odd detail: Ed Jones met Giancana in federal prison (a tax case, late 1930s/1940 — UNVERIFIED this session) and, according to Chepesiuk, later lent him $100,000 to start a stolen-goods vending-machine racket (12,000 machines, 500 employees, about $8 million a year) — the loan that financed the men who kidnapped him.",
