      "summary": "William C. Durant, founder of General Motors and of the Durant-Dort carriage firm in Flint, lost control of GM in 1920 (settling for $21,000,000 with the du Pont and McLaughlin interests) and built Durant Motors (Durant, Star, Flint and Eagle brands), which failed in 1933. On Black Tuesday 1929 he joined Rockefeller family members buying stock to stop the slide, and it cost him; by 1936 he was bankrupt. He then opened \"a bowling alley/fast food restaurant in Flint, Michigan, working the kitchen in person,\" and he and his second wife Catherine lived on a $10,000-a-year pension arranged by Alfred Sloan from R. S. McLaughlin, Marr and du Pont. In 1942 he tried a cinnabar mine in Nevada and suffered a stroke; he went into a coma on 13 March 1947 and died days later, and is buried at Woodlawn Cemetery in the Bronx. The odd detail: The man whose 1907 invitation brought Charles Mott's axle firm to Flint ended up working the kitchen of a Flint bowling alley himself; the 1936–37 sit-down (R07, R24) happened inside the company he founded.",
