      "summary": "James Martin, born 8 March 1883 in Stanton, Tennessee, came to Chicago at 14 and made money in real estate in the 1920s before running the West Side's policy and gambling. His independence drew the same pressure the South Side kings later felt: in October 1940 a bomb of six sticks of dynamite was found at his tavern; in May 1941 three gunmen kidnapped him demanding $50,000 (they left with $300); on 15 November 1950 John Philip Cerone shot him in the arm, shoulder and neck, after which Martin fled to Mexico. The odd detail: The kidnappers wanted $50,000 and settled for $300 — the West Side king kept his money somewhere else.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (single source; the 1930s scale of Martin's operation is not given).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The West Side Black district — Lake Street under the L tracks — is a second, smaller Black Chicago most modules forget; a gambling house that survives a six-stick bomb has either luck or a ward.",
