      "summary": "From the late 1920s a gathering place for \"gamblers, racketeers, sports figures,\" at its height in the 1930s–40s. In 1937 Morris \"Mushy\" Wexler opened the Theatrical (grill and bar); neighbours included Taystee Barbeque, Frank Ciccia's barbershop, the Grogshop, Kornman's, the Roxy burlesque near E. 9th, Freddie's, the French Quarter and Mickey's Show Bar. Cleveland Press columnist Winsor French led the \"Jolly Set\" of reporters who drank there. The Empire News Service — a telegraph wire feeding race results to bookies — worked from the street. Its south side was nicknamed \"the Gaza Strip.\" The odd detail: A bookies' racing wire operated on the same block as the reporters' favourite bar — the two information networks of the city sat side by side.",
