      "summary": "Frank \"Frankie\" LaPorte (born 7 October 1901 in Sambiase, Calabria; also Liperetto, Lipperatti, Liparotta) was caporegime of the Chicago Heights crew of the Outfit, a low-profile \"power broker\" who, per biographer Laurance Bergreen, may have been the real power behind \"Capone as front boss.\" His closest associates were Ross Prio, Louis Campagna and Capone himself. The Sin Strip in Calumet City — speakeasies, gambling dens and brothels serving the whole Midwest (\"the place to go … before Las Vegas\") — is attributed mainly to him; police actively avoided it, at risk of violence (unverified — V_R37: the cited chicagoganghistory page does not mention LaPorte or police avoidance). In the 1930s Repeal made the speakeasies legal nightclubs. A local product: Carter's Special Table Beer. A 2 June 1959 Chicago Daily News exposé named LaPorte, followed by a state raid: 98 arrests and seizure of business records. He died in Flossmoor on 30 October 1972. (R29 has the strip's overview; this entry adds the man behind it.) The odd detail: The most important man on the Sin Strip had no famous name — a \"power broker\" whose exposure came thirty years after Capone's.",
