      "summary": "On New Year's Day 1920 federal agents arrested 186 mostly-Swedish socialists in Rockford on suspicion of subversion — the largest total among the 37 cities hit that night (another local-history page gives 140–180). Eleven were indicted; the case of Arthur Person went to trial with Clarence Darrow defending, and Person was acquitted, after which the other charges were dropped. The roots were in a Swedish socialist society formed around 1906 that ran a Saturday school teaching English, \"humanistic values\" and fine arts; in 1917 137 members refused to register for the draft and most drew a year in prison. Two of the society's men, Herman Hallstrom and C. Henry Bloom, later served five terms each as Rockford mayor. The odd detail: The men federal agents arrested for radicalism went on to run the city — two of them for five terms apiece.",
