      "id": "R07-043",
      "title": "The Duluth lynchings",
      "date_start": "1920-06-15",
      "date_end": "1920-06-15",
      "date_label": "15 June 1920 (living memory)",
      "category": "The Great Lakes ring beyond Chicago, 1929–1939",
      "tags": [
        "Great Lakes ring",
        "Duluth",
        "lynchings",
        "living memory"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Elias Clayton",
        "Elmer Jackson",
        "Isaac McGhie",
        "Irene Tusken"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Duluth city jail",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Duluth%20city%20jail"
        },
        {
          "name": "the light pole at 1st Street and 2nd Avenue East",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20light%20pole%20at%201st%20Street%20and%202nd%20Avenue%20East%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The John Robinson Circus came to Duluth on 14 June 1920. A white teenager claimed that six Black circus workers had assaulted and robbed him and 19-year-old Irene Tusken; a physician found no evidence of rape or assault. Newspapers spread the rumour; a mob of 1,000 to 10,000 gathered at the jail, a commissioner ordered police not to resist, and the mob took Elias Clayton, Elmer Jackson and Isaac McGhie, held a \"kangaroo court,\" and hanged them from a light pole. Of 37 indictments, three men were convicted of rioting; none served more than 15 months; no one was charged with murder. Minnesota passed an anti-lynching law in April 1921; Duluth dedicated the Clayton Jackson McGhie Memorial in 2003. The odd detail: The photograph of the lynching was sold as a postcard (general knowledge — UNVERIFIED in my read).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. • general knowledge — UNVERIFIED in my read",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A northern port city's lynching in the same year as the Klan's second rise — part of the Golden Circle's reach up the Mississippi; a circus that came to town and left three men behind.",
