      "summary": "The former heavyweight champion Jack Johnson, convicted in 1913 under the Mann Act by an all-white jury (a year and a day), had fled the country and spent seven years in Europe, South America and Mexico, fighting where he could. On 20 July 1920 he walked with his wife from Tijuana across the border and presented his passport to a San Diego County deputy sheriff. He was taken to Los Angeles, back to Chicago, held for months at Joliet and the Geneva jail, and entered Leavenworth on 19 September 1920 to a crowd of cheering inmates; he served one year. The odd detail: the most-hunted Black man in America gave himself up by handing his passport to a county deputy sheriff at the line, with his wife beside him.",
