      "summary": "On 20 August 1920 the Canton Bulldogs' owner Ralph Hay met the managers of the Akron Pros, Cleveland Tigers and Dayton Triangles to form the American Professional Football Conference. On 17 September a second meeting in Hay's Hupmobile showroom took in the Rock Island Independents, Muncie Flyers, Decatur Staleys, Racine Cardinals, Massillon Tigers, Chicago Cardinals and Hammond Pros (unverified — V_R43: Wikipedia's APFA page lists Hammond, Muncie, Rochester Jeffersons, Rock Island, Decatur and Racine (Chicago) Cardinals — the Racine and Chicago Cardinals are one club, and Massillon is not listed), and the body became the American Professional Football Association (later the NFL), with Jim Thorpe, the 1912 Olympic champion and Bulldogs star, as president. Lore says Halas, Thorpe and the others dined together that evening in the place across the street. The odd detail: Chicago's football — the Decatur Staleys, later the Bears, and the Cardinals — was invented in a Canton car showroom by a Bulldogs owner who sold Hupmobiles.",
