      "summary": "Levi General (Deskaheh), a Cayuga, was born 15 March 1873 in Tuscarora Township, Ontario, became a hereditary Royaner in 1917 and Speaker of the Six Nations Council. In August 1921 he sailed to London with the lawyer George P. Decker, on a passport issued by the Six Nations themselves, because Canada would have stopped him; Winston Churchill, then at the Colonial Office, refused to accept the petition and sent it back to Ottawa. In 1922 the Netherlands' foreign minister, Herman Adriaan van Karnebeek, forwarded it to the League of Nations; the Swiss \"Bureau International pour la Défense des Indigènes\" backed it. By 1923 Canada had built RCMP barracks on Six Nations land, searched homes and restricted wood-cutting. On 14 July 1923 Deskaheh sailed for Geneva and stayed eighteen months, lecturing in Geneva, Bern, Lausanne, Lucerne, Winterthur and Zurich; Ireland, Panama, Persia, Japan and Estonia offered support; he cited the Two Row Wampum. He never addressed the Assembly; leaving on 3 January 1925 he left a proclamation at the League's offices. On 10 March 1925 he made his best-known speech by radio in Rochester (\"Over in Ottawa, they call that policy 'Indian Advancement'… we say it is tyranny\"). He died on 27 June 1925 at the Tuscarora Reservation (pneumonia, at Chief Clinton Rickard's house); about 2,000 mourners followed the casket from the Sour Spring Longhouse on 30 June. The odd detail: He travelled on a Haudenosaunee passport — issued by the Confederacy — and the Colonial Office's Churchill refused to accept his petition and returned it to Ottawa's hands. Historian Laurence Hauptman notes the Geneva lectures were \"not effective in changing British or Canadian positions.\"",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
