      "summary": "The Klan began organising in Sangamon County in 1921. In September 1922 over 185 men were initiated into \"Abraham Lincoln Council No. 3\" near Auburn; in May 1923 650 novices were installed at the fairgrounds before some 6,000 spectators under a 14-foot flaming cross; the peak was 11 October 1924, when about 2,000 Klansmen paraded from the Statehouse to the fairgrounds (the Springfield contingent about 400), with three floats — one against Ellis Island, one showing a crucifix (unverified — V_R38: the page read names only the Ellis Island and parochial-school floats), one reading \"The public school system is good enough for us.\" An August 1926 rally at Camp Lincoln burned an 80-foot cross, \"the largest fiery cross ever built in Illinois.\" The Klan's Grand Titan Charles Wanless and 12 council members were suspended by the state Klan office in March 1924 in a fight over Supreme Court candidates. The oral-history interviewee J. R. \"Bud\" Fitzpatrick recalled that \"the blacks were almost totally immune from the deal\" and (per the earlier read) that Springfield \"experienced no Klan violence\"; the Klan's targets were Catholics and immigrants (Italians, French, Germans, English). Klan news vanished from the Springfield papers after 1927. (An Illinois state legislator, Ashby, an insurance salesman, won a House seat in Nov 1924 with 44,771 votes (unverified — V_R38).) The odd detail: One of the Klan's three floats in the 1924 parade was against Ellis Island — and in the same city, an Italian consular agent would be shot in the street eight years later (see the liquor-war entry; no link is claimed).",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the dates and numbers (SangamonLink); the \"no violence\" claim is an oral-history recollection. • … when about 2,000 Klansmen paraded from the Statehouse to the fairgrounds (the Springfield contingent about 400), with three floats — one against Ellis Island, one showing a crucifix (unverified — V_R38: the page read names only the Ellis Island and parochial-school floats), one reading \"The public school system is good enough for us.\" An August 1926 rally at Camp Lincoln burned an … • An Illinois state legislator, Ashby, an insurance salesman, won a House seat in Nov 1924 with 44,771 votes (unverified — V_R38).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A Klan whose Springfield chapter is named for Abraham Lincoln and which meets at Camp Lincoln is a Golden Circle veneer: the Circle's \"old cause\" wearing the martyred president's name; the state capital holds both the Klan's fiery crosses and the martyr's tomb.",
