      "summary": "A. J. Balaban and Sam Katz (company 1916; Paramount/Famous Players took control 1926; first to air-condition theatres; lavish stage shows; Rapp & Rapp architects) ran over a hundred houses, 50+ in Chicago. The Chicago (October 26, 1921; 3,880 seats; 26-rank Wurlitzer Opus 434 designed with Jesse Crawford; the \"CHICAGO\" marquee of pinwheels) kept stage shows until the 1950s — Sally Rand's four arrests in one day happened here, August 4, 1933 (unverified — V_R16) (see W03 entry below). The Uptown (August 18, 1925; 4,381 seats; \"An Acre of Seats in a Magic City\"; 12,000 queued opening night and women fainted; a staff of 130 including a nurse and firemen; a 34-piece house orchestra until the stage shows were cut for economy). The Oriental (1926; ~3,250 seats) went mostly to film in the 1930s but kept Duke Ellington's orchestra, organist Hal Pearl and — October 1934 — the Garland Sisters, with the youngest, Judy. John Eberson's Paradise (September 14, 1928; 3,500 seats; \"the world's most beautiful theater\") was ruined by its own dome: the acoustics could not carry talking pictures and the crowd walked to the Marbro. The Marks Brothers' Granada (1926; 3,400 seats; Levy & Klein) went to B&K in 1934. The odd detail: The Oriental was built on the Iroquois fire's ground; the Paradise was killed by sound. (The \"ghost alley\" behind the Oriental is folklore — legend says the Iroquois dead still crowd Couch Place.)",
