      "summary": "In 1921, a Gold Medal Flour jigsaw-puzzle promotion in the Saturday Evening Post brought back 30,000 completed puzzles and several hundred letters with cooking questions. The company invented a woman to answer them: \"Betty\" (cheerful, all-American) plus \"Crocker\" (after company director William Crocker). In 1924 she got a voice on The Betty Crocker Cooking School of the Air, \"the country's first radio cooking program,\" with actresses (Blanche Ingersoll, Marjorie Husted) playing her anonymously. General Mills was formed by merger in 1928. In 1936 the artist Neysa McMein painted her first official portrait. (By 1945 Fortune ranked her the second most popular woman in America after Eleanor Roosevelt.) The odd detail: The promotion drew 30,000 completed puzzles and several hundred letters with cooking questions — and the company's answer was to invent a woman rather than admit that nobody in particular was writing back.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
