      "summary": "Bessie Coleman, a manicurist at the White Sox Barber Shop from 1915, was sent to France by Robert Abbott and Jesse Binga and took her international licence on 15 June 1921; six weeks after her first American show (3 September 1922) she flew figure-eights and loops at the Checkerboard Airdrome for a crowd honouring the 370th; she died in a fall at Jacksonville on 30 April 1926 and 10,000 mourners led by Ida B. Wells buried her at Lincoln Cemetery, where Black pilots still drop flowers from the air. John C. Robinson, refused by the Curtiss-Wright school, took a janitor's job there until an instructor got him in as its first Black student; with Cornelius Coffey he founded the Challenger Air Pilots' Association and an airfield at Robbins, then moved to Harlem Airport in Oak Lawn. In January 1935 he volunteered for Ethiopia; Haile Selassie commissioned him in April; on 8 August 1935 the rival Black aviator Hubert Julian assaulted him in Addis Ababa; from 3 October he flew reconnaissance against the Italian invasion, saw Adwa bombed, was nearly shot down by six fighters, and fled with the American military attaché on 30 April 1936 — the \"Brown Condor\" came home to Chicago that spring (the airport crowds and Bronzeville parade are from memory, UNVERIFIED). Willa Brown began lessons at Harlem Field in 1934, took a mechanic's licence at Curtiss-Wright in 1935, and in 1936 walked into the Defender newsroom in white jodhpurs, white jacket and white boots — \"all the typewriters suddenly went silent,\" Enoch Waters wrote; private licence 1938, commercial 1939 (first Black woman with either), co-founder of the National Airmen's Association (1939) and the Coffey School (1940), whose students became Tuskegee airmen. The odd detail: The flowers dropped on Bessie Coleman's grave from the air; and a Chicago flyer commanding an emperor's air force against Mussolini's bombers a year before Joe Louis fought Schmeling.",
