      "summary": "Born in Proton Township, Grey County, 24 March 1890, Macphail was the first woman elected to the Canadian House of Commons (1921, Progressive), and held her seat until 1940. Through the 1930s she was the country's loudest voice on prison reform; her campaign brought about the Archambault Commission (1936) whose report became the basis of post-war penitentiary reform, and in 1939 she founded the Elizabeth Fry Society of Canada for women in the justice system. She was the first Canadian woman delegate to the League of Nations at Geneva, on disarmament. She lost in 1940 and again in a Saskatoon by-election on 19 August 1940, and died in Toronto 13 February 1954. Her line: \"Most women think politics aren't lady-like. Well, I'm no lady. I'm a human being.\" The odd detail: The Ontario shore's one woman MP spent her decade walking the ranges of Kingston Penitentiary.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Kingston Penitentiary, on the lake, holds whatever Ontario locked up in the 1930s; Macphail is the one official who has been inside every range and would talk to an American investigator.",
