      "summary": "ʻAbdu'l-Bahá laid a symbolic cornerstone in 1912 (a limestone block donated by Nettie Tobin). Louis Bourgeois's design was chosen; Foundation Hall was mostly complete by 1922 and used for meetings. Work stalled in the 1920s for lack of money and local opposition; the George A. Fuller Co. raised the superstructure 1930–31; John J. Earley's studio began the ornamental concrete skin (portland cement with two kinds of quartz) in 1932. Everything comes in nines: nine entrances, alcoves, dome sections, fountains. The exterior was finished Jan 1943; dedication 2 May 1953. The odd detail: While it stood half-built, Wilmette rumour held that there was a live white whale inside — and that captured German submarines were being refuelled there.",
