      "summary": "José Fidencio Constantino Síntora, born 17 October 1898 in Yuriria, Guanajuato, came to Espinazo in 1921 or 1923 and began healing there in 1925 (Agostoni 2018; the WRSP profile gives 1925 as the year he came) to join his friend Enrique López de la Fuente, administrator of a hacienda owned by a German immigrant, Teodoro von Wernich (confirmed by Agostoni: \"empresario alemán y espiritista\"), who (per the WRSP profile) \"may have introduced Fidencio to Spiritism.\" After a 1927 vision under a pepper tree (pirul), he healed without charge: tumours removed with pieces of glass, paralysis treated with a swing, cleansings in mud and in his own bathwater and in the pool called the Charco, fruit thrown into the crowd as mass healing. On 8 February 1928 President Plutarco Elías Calles — the anticlerical strongman of the Cristero War — spent about three hours in Espinazo with him (a skin disease; later said to be leprosy) and reportedly pulled him into his private train. The New York Times ran \"Cures by Healer Stir Mexico City\" (22 Feb 1928). Shanties grew to hundreds, then thousands; Espinazo got a post office and telegraph. Nuevo León prosecuted him for illegal medicine (from 18 Feb 1929). He died 19 October 1938, days before 40; believers hold that doctors' autopsy incision in his throat drew warm blood — proof he was alive, in trance, when they cut. His mediums (cajitas, materias) still channel him; pilgrimages each March and October. The odd detail: the man who shut Mexico's churches sought out a folk healer in a boxcar town — and the healer's patron was a German hacendado who taught Spiritism.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the 8 Feb 1928 visit, the 22 Feb 1928 New York Times piece and the 19 Oct 1938 death; the visit's length (three vs six hours) and his arrival year (1921/23 vs 1925) vary by source (Agostoni 2018 also records that the 1929 illegal-medicine case was dismissed); the leprosy is a later disclosure (Medium); the von Wernich–Spiritism link is the source's \"may have\".",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Germany as patron in miniature — the German landowner, the Spiritist books. Three layers: the period says charlatan or saint; the folklore says the body was alive under the knife; the setting can let the files say who ordered the autopsy. The healer and his pilgrims stay good; the question is who wanted him opened.",
