      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Standard Oil",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(Indiana) refinery, Whiting, Indiana — Still No. 95 in the 8th battery of pressure stills",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Standard%20Oil%20%20refinery%2C%20Whiting%2C%20Indiana%20%E2%80%94%20Still%20No.%2095%20in%20the%208th%20battery%20of%20pressure%20stills"
        },
        {
          "name": "Passavant Hospital",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Chicago",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Passavant%20Hospital%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "At 8:15–8:30 a.m. on 4 July 1921, about 45 minutes before the town's Independence Day parade, a pressure still holding a couple of hundred barrels of oil at about 700 degrees Fahrenheit failed; a steel cylinder from the top flew 100 feet into another still, and oil and gas sprayed 200 yards, igniting on contact and catching four stills in two batteries. Eight men died and 44 were injured (24 seriously). Names of the dead include John Dzurovcak (32, a Slovak fireman with five children), Thomas Reed, Fred Harbrecht, Gustav Hokasson (\"Gus Johnson,\" Swede), George Wildrick, Fred Engle, Frank Stout, and Joseph Paylo, assistant chief of the refinery fire department and a First World War veteran (his funeral on 7 July had the Standard Oil Band and 40–50 vehicles). Bodies were identified by clothing, watches and jewellery; wives crowded the refinery fence. The inquest (8 July, Coroner E. E. Evans) heard that the still was six years old and had been inspected 10–12 hours before, with pressure at 93–95 pounds; the coroner found it \"one of those things that happen now and then due to no one's fault.\" Standard Oil called it \"an act of God.\" The parade went ahead on schedule and the fireworks were fired that evening. The cost was about $2 million; the refinery had had no pressure-still deaths in eight years; 120 of the country's 700 pressure stills were in Whiting. The odd detail: The company statement invoked God and his \"mysterious way\" for a six-year-old steel vessel operated by human beings — and the town held its Fourth of July parade forty-five minutes later.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (a detailed local-society account with named victims; the \"no pressure-still deaths\" claim is the page's).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The company blamed God and the coroner agreed. An IHS priest who noticed that a \"cracked still\" burst on a holy day, or who knows what the eight dead had in common, would treat the \"act of God\" line as the company's admission of the unaccountable — \"cracking\" has an obvious double meaning for a setting about what is under the world.",
