      "source_heading": "\"An act of God\": the Whiting still explosion — 4 July 1921",
      "source_line": 195,
      "cited_by_v2": [],
      "collection": "Chicago & Great Lakes",
      "synopsis": "At 8:15–8:30 a.m. on 4 July 1921, about 45 minutes before the town's Independence Day parade, a pressure still holding a couple of hundred barrels of oil at about 700 degrees Fahrenheit failed; a steel cylinder from the top flew 100 feet into another still, and oil and gas sprayed 200 yards, igniting on contact and catching four stills in two batteries. Eight men died and 44 were injured (24 seriously).",
