      "summary": "Born 12 March 1866 at Wutzlhofen by Regensburg, Bavaria; trained at St. Vincent Archabbey (Pennsylvania); ordained 1889; vicar general of Grand Rapids from 1903; consecrated auxiliary bishop and then named the first bishop of the new Diocese of Toledo in 1911; bishop of Cleveland from 8 Sept 1921. During the war years he was one of four bishops on the administrative committee of the National Catholic War Council, and he lobbied Rome successfully to let the body continue in peacetime (it became the National Catholic Welfare Council, whose permanent committee he joined); he helped found the National Councils of Catholic Men and of Catholic Women. In Cleveland he expanded Catholic Charities, consolidated the boys' orphanages at Parmadale, began the Rose-Mary Home, moved St. Mary Seminary, and opened a Sisters' College (1928 at the cathedral per ECH's cathedral article; 1931 per its biography). He \"actively supported the trade-union movement, and used radio to evangelize and instruct.\" Rome gave him the personal title of archbishop in 1939; diabetes brought a coadjutor, Edward F. Hoban, in 1942. He is buried in a crypt beneath the altar of St. John Cathedral. The odd detail: The bishop of the IHS city spent the Great War years persuading Rome to keep an American bishops' committee alive after the emergency ended — a man who knew how to turn a temporary war body into a permanent institution.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (ECH). The Sisters' College date conflicts inside ECH (1928 vs 1931).",
