      "summary": "The Tampico fortune built Greystone — 55 rooms, 46,000 sq ft, over $3.1 million, finished in 1928 for Doheny's son Edward Jr. (\"Ned\"). In November 1921 Ned and his secretary and boyhood friend Hugh Plunkett had carried $100,000 in cash to Interior Secretary Albert B. Fall at the Wardman Park Hotel — the loan at the heart of Teapot Dome (the Elk Hills naval reserve lease). Both were due to testify when, on 16 February 1929, they were found shot dead in a Greystone guest bedroom. The official story: Plunkett, \"nervous,\" killed Ned and himself. But the gun was Ned's, and Ned was not buried in the family's Catholic cemetery; the two men lie at Forest Lawn, Glendale, a few hundred yards apart. Doheny Sr. was twice acquitted of bribery; Fall was convicted of taking it. Doheny died 8 September 1935; his widow destroyed his papers. The odd detail: a Catholic oil king's son is buried outside consecrated ground — the family's own verdict, whatever the coroner said.",
