      "places": [
        {
          "name": "the old Basilica of Guadalupe",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Tepeyac, north of Mexico City (see M12 for the site)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20old%20Basilica%20of%20Guadalupe%2C%20Tepeyac%2C%20north%20of%20Mexico%20City"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "On the morning of 14 November 1921, around 10:30, after Mass, a bomb of dynamite hidden in a flower arrangement placed at the foot of the Virgin's image exploded on the main altar. It shattered the windows, pulverised the marble beneath the picture, wrecked the altar rail and steps and the brass candlesticks, and twisted a 34-kilo bronze-and-iron crucifix into an arc. The tilma and its glass were undamaged. The bomber, Luciano Pérez (sources give Pérez Carrillo and Pérez Carpio), was described as an employee of the Private Secretariat of the Presidency (one source: a railroad worker; one: an army soldier) with, in the sacristan's notarial record, \"saffron-coloured hair.\" Authorities stopped a lynching, reportedly on Obregón's own order. The crucifix — the Santo Cristo del Atentado — is displayed in the new Basilica; repairs raised the image about a metre. The English Wikipedia says the bent crucifix was preserved. The odd detail: the notary's record from the Basilica's sacristan Ignacio Díaz de León describes the bomber's hair; the crucifix bent in front of the image, not around it.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on date, bomb, damage and crucifix; Medium on the bomber's name and employment (sources differ); the bomber's motive and sentence are not on any page I read.",
