      "summary": "The Tribune's 1922 international design competition ($100,000 in prizes, $50,000 to the winner, 260-plus entries) was won by Howells & Hood's neo-Gothic tower, completed 1925, 463 feet, 36 floors; Eliel Saarinen's second-place entry changed skyscraper design more than the winner. Before construction, Col. Robert R. McCormick had Tribune correspondents bring back rocks and bricks from historic sites, and the practice continued for decades: 149 stones are now embedded at street level (Great Pyramid, Taj Mahal, Great Wall, Parthenon, Edinburgh Castle), plus, from 1999 to 2011, a moon rock on loan. The lobby's Hall of Inscriptions carries quotations on press freedom. Sculptor René Chambellan hid the architects in the entrance: a Robin Hood (Hood) and a howling dog (Howells). The odd detail: A newspaper that sent its reporters out to chip pieces off the world's holy places and mortar them into its wall — in a 1930s setting, some of those stones have been there less than a decade.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (the count of 149 is today's; the 1930s count was smaller — UNVERIFIED how many by 1936).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A wall of relics from every temple on earth, assembled by a man who thinks in empires — the obvious ward, or the obvious summoning circle, depending on who told the correspondents what to take.",
