      "summary": "The Mexican colony's own cultural life ran on a mutual-aid society, the Círculo de Obreros Católicos \"San José\" (early 1920s). Its Cuadro Dramático, under director J. Jesús Cabrera, staged nine full productions between March 1927 and May 1928, including The Count of Monte Cristo; Cabrera either returned to Mexico or died in Indiana Harbor in 1929 (the source is unsure). A classical wind band, later called Obreros Mexicanos (\"Mexican Workers,\" led by Professor López and then M. G. Arcos), first played a 1922 benefit at Szabo Hall for the Carmelite Home Orphanage, and gave free Sunday concerts at Alder Park (1927), the East Chicago Labor Day parade, the Lake County Fair and an Easter sunrise service (1928). The community library sat in the Figueroa Print Shop, whose owner Francisco Figueroa edited the Spanish-language newspaper El Amigo del Hogar. Indiana Harbor's Mexican population grew from over 300 in 1920 to over 5,300 in 1930 — about 1,700 percent. The odd detail: A theatre company that staged The Count of Monte Cristo — a story of a man wronged, imprisoned and returned — for a colony that would be put on trains four years later.",
