      "summary": "Four Sicilian-born brothers from near Palermo — Frank (1893–1974), Sam (1889–1978), Tony (1900–1999) and Joseph \"Diamond Joe\" (1906–1981) — ran organised crime in Springfield and Rockford: Frank is \"Springfield's godfather,\" Joe a high-ranking Rockford mob figure. Frank was arrested for distilling in 1922; in 1930 he was charged in two major federal conspiracies (Corn Products/Fleischmann and Hubinger), and in 1933 he, Vincent Salvo and Jasper Blanda got two years at Leavenworth and $10,000 fines (unverified — V_R38: the page read gives the two-year maximum term, not the fines or \"only major conviction\") — his only major conviction. The warehouses at 10th and Carpenter and Third and Carpenter supplied 21 area stills. After repeal the operation moved to gambling through Modern Distributing Co. (in the wives' names, Lena and Nina), controlling \"punchboards, slot machines, dice and poker games\" (per Robert Kennedy's 1959 questioning) — the Post-Dispatch said in 1948 it paid \"thousands of dollars a week\" in bribes. Jasper Blanda moved to Rockford and became underboss there; Vito Impastato, a former St. Louis \"Green Gang\" man, was Springfield's strongarm. Later: Frank was arrested at the 1957 Apalachin meeting; James De Rosa, a rival pinball man, was killed in 1957 and found decapitated by a dog; over 1,000 attended Frank's 1974 wake; none of the brothers had children. The odd detail: The Springfield and Rockford rackets are the same family — the brothers ran \"Springfield and Rockford,\" and Blanda, convicted with Frank Zito in Springfield in 1933, ended as Rockford's underboss (the town of Musso and the Giovingos, above).",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the biography (a local-history page drawing on the Post-Dispatch and FBI records); the 1957–1974 material is post-window. • … in two major federal conspiracies (Corn Products/Fleischmann and Hubinger), and in 1933 he, Vincent Salvo and Jasper Blanda got two years at Leavenworth and $10,000 fines (unverified — V_R38: the page read gives the two-year maximum term, not the fines or \"only major conviction\") — his only major conviction.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Two Illinois cities, one family of Sicilian brothers, 21 stills fed from two warehouses: the fixers a cult uses when it needs a \"supply\" that isn't liquor. The model-citizen home shrine and the brother with the \"church and charity\" record are the three layers of truth in one household.",
