      "summary": "C. Howard Crane's Fox opened September 21, 1928 with Street Angel — over 5,000 seats in Burmese, Chinese, Indian and Persian ornament; the 1928 opening production had a 60-piece orchestra, a 50-voice choir and the 32-girl Tillerettes; Shirley Temple made appearances; admission 35 cents (a figure the page gives for the war years, when it grossed $75,000 a week, 24 hours a day). The Graystone (\"Detroit's Million Dollar Ballroom,\" opened March 7, 1922, 3,000 dancers, Gothic Revival by Rupert Koch) was one of the \"six great ballrooms\" of the city before the crash. On Thanksgiving Day 1934 the Lions' first owner, radio man George A. Richards (WJR), put his new team against the Bears at the University of Detroit Stadium — 26,000 came, the Bears won 19–16, and Richards' NBC Blue Network hook-up carried it nationally: the holiday game was a gimmick to sell a new franchise. The odd detail: The NFL's oldest tradition began as a radio owner's stunt against Chicago.",
