      "summary": "The \"layover\" was more than one man: Frank Sommer (chief 1922–23) and Thomas A. Brown (chief 1930–32) both kept the O'Connor system and profited from gangsters while ostensibly controlling crime. Thomas Dahill was the reformer: on 5 August 1932 he declared war on \"hoodlums\" and \"gun toters.\" The kidnappings — William Hamm Jr. on 15 June 1933 and Edward Bremer on 17 January 1934 — violated the system's sole rule (no crimes inside city limits) and brought the federal government in. Leon Gleckman, \"the Al Capone of St. Paul,\" set up his bootlegging headquarters in the St. Paul Hotel on 18 January 1930. Karpis was arrested 1 May 1936 and got life at Alcatraz on 3 August 1936. MNopedia notes that a 1925 St. Paul Daily News series, based on months of wiretaps, had already exposed the corruption and led to resignations and convictions of policemen — the system survived its own exposure. The odd detail: The city's rule was \"no crimes in St. Paul,\" and both big kidnappings were done in St. Paul, by its guests — the guests broke the one rule.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. (R07 and R02 hold the same story from other angles; this entry adds the chiefs and dates.)",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A city's peace is a covenant — and the chiefs who kept it are the ones who know who signed. Whoever still holds the ledger of \"guests\" registered in 1930 is holding a list of everyone the Hunter Guild should have caught.",
