      "summary": "George R. Dale (1867–1936) edited the Muncie Post-Democrat from about 1920. His satirical column \"Koo Koo Side Lights\" mocked the Klan: from 10 November 1922 he lampooned Helen Jackson, a fraudulent \"escaped nun\" lecturer, and from December 1923 he called Daisy Douglass Barr, the Quaker minister who headed the women's Klan auxiliary, the \"Quakeress Fakeress.\" He printed Klan membership names from stolen lists; hooded men shot at him and attacked his house; he was jailed repeatedly (the story was that inmates applauded when he came back) (unverified — V_R41: not in the cited Hoosier State Chronicles article, which mentions only \"Klansmen shooting at him and attacking his home\"). He won the 1929 mayoral election and, taking office, immediately fired all 42 police officers. A 1932 Prohibition indictment followed and President Roosevelt pardoned him on Christmas Eve 1933. He died at home on 27 March 1936. The odd detail: An anti-Klan editor became mayor and fired the whole police force on his first day; the Prohibition men indicted him; the President pardoned him at Christmas.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the Hoosier State Chronicles dates; the mayoral term and the jailing story are from search summaries. • he was jailed repeatedly (the story was that inmates applauded when he came back) (unverified — V_R41: not in the cited Hoosier State Chronicles article, which mentions only \"Klansmen shooting at him and attacking his home\").",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A searchable real newspaper archive full of the Klan's stolen membership rolls — a photocopied 1925 roster is a fine Golden Circle clue. A mayor who fired 42 cops in a day knows whom the cops answered to.",
