      "summary": "The Cook County Coroner was an elected politician, not a doctor: Oscar Wolff (Republican, 4 Dec 1922–Dec 1928), then Dr. Herman N. Bundesen (Democrat, Dec 1928–18 Nov 1931, who ran the St. Valentine's Day inquest and resigned to become city Health Commissioner), then Frank J. Walsh (Democrat, appointed 18 Nov 1931, elected 1932 and 1938, died in office 12 Feb 1940). The office was \"riddled with patronage and corruption\" and coroner's juries \"often acted as a rubber-stamp\" for police and prosecutors. Deaths were ruled by a six-man coroner's jury at an inquest (unverified — V_R13); the coroner's physician (a pathologist) did the cutting. The coroner system survived until 6 Dec 1976, when an appointed medical examiner replaced it. The odd detail: the man who ruled on every violent death in Chicago from 1931 to 1940 was Frank J. Walsh, a machine Democrat with no medical training, sitting between a Republican and a publicity-hungry doctor who once drew a pistol on a dumper with a pickaxe.",
