      "summary": "Bundesen was Chicago's Health Commissioner 1 Feb 1922–Jan 1928 and again 1931–1960, Cook County Coroner Dec 1928–Nov 1931, and President of the Board of Health from 1932. He drove dairy inspections from about 4,300 a year (1920) to 56,127 (1928), cut diphtheria and infant mortality, pushed venereal-disease clinics and prophylactics (denounced as immoral), and opposed the city's first birth-control clinic in 1923. As coroner he chased the cameras after every big murder, the massacre above all. In 1936 he challenged Governor Henry Horner for the Democratic nomination, won Cook County and lost the state — campaigning in monocle and spats and demonstrating his abdominal muscles. He died of pancreatic cancer on 25 Aug 1960. The odd detail: the coroner of the St. Valentine's Day Massacre wrote best-selling baby-care books and posed for muscle photographs.",
