      "summary": "Rev. Florian Chodniewicz, 60, first pastor of St. Florian parish (founded 2 October 1905; school completed 1907), was fatally shot \"shortly after midnight on 22 January 1922\" when a burglar broke into the rectory to steal sacramental wine and the priest interrupted him. A physician saw him at the scene; he was taken to St. Margaret's Hospital in Hammond that morning and died that evening. Police classed it as \"prohibition-related.\" Charles Majewski was charged and released for lack of evidence; nearly two years later Edward Frenenski confessed but police did not believe him, and he was convicted of other crimes. The case is one of Chicago's unsolved cold cases. A local rumour attaches a haunted-rectory story to the murder, but it gets \"the pastor, church and denomination wrong.\" The odd detail: A priest killed for the one liquid Prohibition allowed him to have, and the case tied up by two men — one released, one disbelieved — leaving it open.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for what the neighbourhood site says; the \"unsolved\" status is the site's own description.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A murdered priest who could not be avenged, a burglar after the wine and a rectory rumour that got everything wrong: the IHS's Chicago office would keep the file open, and the \"wrong denomination\" haunting is a good red herring. A vampire or fae would not have needed to break in; someone else wanted what was in the sacristy safe.",
