      "summary": "It began when sex workers in La Huaca stopped paying their landlords in February 1922; days later they held a symbolic burning of mattresses, and the neighbours joined (one English-language account, libcom's Marín article, has the mattresses thrown into the street in early March 1922 — V_M09). The Sindicato Revolucionario de Inquilinos was founded on 10 February 1922 under the anarchist tailor-agitator Herón Proal (profession from general knowledge — UNVERIFIED) with anarchists and communists; soon more than half the city — about 30,000 people — was on rent strike, and the movement spread to Mexico City (10,000–15,000 marchers), Minatitlán, Villahermosa, Mérida, Puebla, Ciudad Juárez, Monterrey and others. A key organiser of the women was María Luisa Marín. The odd detail: Mexico's first great urban movement was started by the prostitutes of a port quarter who burned their mattresses in the street.",
