      "summary": "About 6,000 conservative Mennonites left Manitoba and Saskatchewan after the provinces required public schools to fly the Union flag and enforced attendance; the first train left Plum Coulee, Manitoba, on 1 March 1922. They negotiated a privilegium (guarantees of schools, exemption from military service and language) with President Álvaro Obregón and settled in Chihuahua in 1922–25: about 3,200 Reinländer from Manitoba and 1,200 from Swift Current on ~230,000 acres in the Bustillos valley, and about 950 from Saskatchewan on 35,000 acres near Nuevo Ideal, Durango. They kept Plautdietsch (Low German) and named their villages after their Prussian, Russian and Canadian homes — Steinbach, Rosenort, Schönwiese. The population grew to some 100,000 in Mexico within a century. The odd detail: they left a British dominion over a flag and a schoolbook, to farm a state that had just lost thousands to a revolution and where the Church had been forbidden.",
