      "summary": "During the national United Mine Workers strike, W. J. Lester broke his agreement with the union and brought in 50 strikebreakers. Union miners besieged the mine; guards killed three of them. After the strikebreakers surrendered they were marched toward Herrin, taken into woods, told to run and shot as they ran; six were executed near Smith's Garage; in Herrin Cemetery a crowd of about 1,000 watched the rest beaten, shot and their throats cut. Dead: 20 strikebreakers and the superintendent, plus 3 union miners — 23. The coroner's jury blamed the company; two trials acquitted all six men indicted; the union bought Lester's mine to end the litigation. Eight lost graves of victims were found in the potter's field only in 2013–15. The odd detail: The killings ended in a cemetery, before a thousand spectators, and no one was ever convicted.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. The 1923–26 Klan war that followed in Williamson County (Prohibition raider S. Glenn Young; the European Hotel shootout of January 1925) could not be confirmed on pages read this session — see Leads (the county page cites Masatomo Ayabe, \"Ku Kluxers in a Coal Mining Community\", 2009; Paul M. Angle, Bloody Williamson, 1952).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A county where a mob killed 23 in a graveyard and the law shrugged is a county where anything can be done in public. The unmarked graves in the potter's field are the lead.",
