      "summary": "Dr. S. M. Melamed listed ten beliefs held by Chicago Jews that he called \"superstitions\" rather than religion — ironic jabs at an otherwise liberal community: that \"every dead body must be brought to the synagogue and a funeral oration must be delivered\"; that weddings must be held in downtown hotels; that newcomers are not intelligent; that during the Torah reading one should chatter and gossip (the FLPS summary's wording is garbled here, so paraphrased); that \"by giving to charity, one becomes poor\"; that small donations to Zionist funds discharge a Jew's obligations; and assumptions about Reform rabbis and newspaper editors. The odd detail: Chicago's real \"Jewish superstitions,\" according to its own editor, were about hotels, funerals and fundraising — not amulets.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (FLPS summary, not the full translation).",
