      "summary": "On 31 July 1922 William Gabel, who held cancelled cheques endorsed by Looney, was murdered; a gang war followed in which 12 people died. On 6 October 1922, while the crowd watched the World Series report on the scoreboard, Looney's one-time lieutenant Daniel Drost and another ex-lieutenant, Anthony Billburg, with two other men, ambushed Looney, his son and a bodyguard; Connor Looney, 21, drew a gun and turned on the attackers and was shot dead in the car. By 26 October every Looney saloon and brothel was shut, six stills smashed, an arms cache seized at one of his homes, and the News ceased (unverified — V_R38: the 26 October sweep, the six stills and the arms cache are not on a page read). Looney fled and was arrested in Belen, New Mexico, on 28 November 1923 (recognised from a wanted poster at the post office; Wikipedia's \"November 1924\" is wrong); he was convicted in December 1925 of the Gabel murder (and, per other accounts, of conspiracy), given 14 years and served about 8½, released 7 April 1934 at 68 (unverified — V_R38: release date from a Daily Herald search summary only; the Rock Island city page says \"nine years\"). He died in 1942 in an El Paso, Texas, tuberculosis sanitarium. Drost served the same 8½ years at Joliet, then lived on a retirement grant doing menial jobs in a downtown Rock Island rooming house; he died in 1939 and is buried in Chippiannock Cemetery (North Public Lot 22, Grave 6) in an unmarked grave near Connor Looney's unmarked one. The odd detail: The betrayer and the man he shot at lie a few paces apart in the same cemetery, both in unmarked graves — and Drost had paid for Looney's daughter Kathleen to enter a St. Louis convent, an act Looney never forgave (unverified — V_R38: the convent payment is on the River Cities' Reader page; \"never forgave\" is not).",
