      "summary": "The Karzas brothers' first palace, by theatre architects Rapp & Rapp, opened December 6, 1922 and was sold as \"The World's Most Beautiful Ballroom\" (its own 1925–28 radio station was WMBB — the initials). Main floor 100 × 140 feet, reportedly 3,000 dancers, air-conditioned, coats and ties for men and gowns for women, floor men to enforce it, many WGN live broadcasts. Through the 1930s it stood in a neighbourhood whose Black population was growing block by block, and the ballroom was — by long-standing accounts — a whites-only house until the 1950s (that policy is widely stated; not confirmed from a page read this session, so UNVERIFIED here). Demolished 1967. The odd detail: A palace named for Versailles' Trianon, with its own radio call letters bragging about itself, at the edge of the Black Belt.",
