      "summary": "Francis Henry Haserot, president of the Haserot Company (a Cleveland food-canning and wholesale firm (unverified — V_R25)), commissioned a bronze from the Danish-born Ohio sculptor Herman Matzen after his wife died in 1919. Cast in 1923 and titled The Angel of Death Victorious, it is a seated angel on polished marble holding an inverted torch (the classical sign of a life put out), about 5.4 ft by 8.4 ft. Oxidation has drawn black streaks down the angel's face, which read as tears. The odd detail: The angel weeps black — a chemical accident on the bronze that the city's legend-tellers call tears.",
