      "summary": "On his second American spiritualist tour (3 April–4 August 1923: New York, Rochester, Hydesville, Cleveland, Pittsburgh, Cincinnati, Indianapolis, Chicago, St. Louis, Kansas City, Denver, Salt Lake City, Los Angeles, San Francisco, Seattle, Vancouver) Doyle's lectures \"took two forms, the photographic and the philosophical.\" After the lonely eastern circuit he wrote, \"I found my wife and family all well and happy at the Chicago hotel\" — the Auditorium — where he had promised himself \"a few days of rest.\" Instead he held séances and meetings in the hotel and answered press attacks. He tested a young trumpet medium, Bruce Kemp, at the Auditorium and found the sitting remarkable: the \"voices travelled all around the room.\" A \"Mrs. Blaine\" helped the family with drives along the lake and to the parks (identity UNVERIFIED). The odd detail: The creator of Sherlock Holmes vouched for a Chicago trumpet medium in 1923; three years later Houdini put a reformed Chicago trumpet medium on his bill on Clark Street (see the Houdini entry below).",
