      "summary": "Incorporated 1893 as West Hammond; residents voted in 1923 to rename it Calumet City (adopted 1924). When Indiana went dry in 1916, West Hammond became the drinking place for Northwest Indiana; under Prohibition Capone's people ran it, and the town earned the name \"Sin City\" — liquor, gambling and prostitution along a \"Sin Strip\" that police avoided at the risk of violence. The Outfit's Frank LaPorte is believed to have built and kept the strip; a 1959 state raid made 98 arrests. The odd detail: A town changed its own name to escape its reputation — and kept the reputation for another 35 years.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the rename and the reputation; the strip's 1930s specifics (named clubs) not on the page.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A state line down the middle of a street is a jurisdiction trick for the Outfit and for anything else: one step east and Illinois warrants are paper. Section 13's agents can't follow a quarry across without federal cause — the thing knows it.",
