      "summary": "The Klan held two enormous gatherings at the Buckeye Lake amusement park: over 75,000 people in 1923 and nearly 100,000 in 1925. The 1923 event had Imperial Wizard Hiram Wesley Evans and Indiana Grand Dragon D. C. Stephenson (the organiser), guides in cloaks and hoods, three bands, three crosses (the largest set ablaze) and the induction of about 1,700 new members, ending with fireworks that spelled an American flag overlaid with \"KKK\"; one Klansman died of a heart attack from the heat. The four-day 1925 event added weddings and a skit on moral decline, and denounced evolution after the Scopes trial. Stephenson was convicted within months — life sentence, 16 November 1925 — of the abduction and murder of Madge Oberholtzer (already in the setting, R06). The Klan's Ohio total is put at about 450,000 (search summary), with Akron said to hold the largest membership of any city by 1925 and Dayton put in the top six cities by share — 15 to 20 per cent of its population, with rallies of 10,000-plus at the old Montgomery County Fairgrounds (search summary; secondary) (unverified — V_R43: none of these figures is on the Wikipedia page read). Klan-endorsed politicians won offices but the order's influence had largely gone by 1928. The odd detail: The 1923 induction of some 1,700 — three crosses, three bands, the largest cross burned — was staged at an amusement-park lake near the state capital, and its organiser was in a cell for murder two years later (R06, R07).",
