      "summary": "Harry Steenbock showed in 1923–24 that ultraviolet irradiation of food raised its vitamin D and cured rickets in rats. He patented the process with $300 of his own money; refusing a $1 million offer from Quaker Oats, he persuaded nine alumni at $100 each to found the Wisconsin Alumni Research Foundation (WARF, 1925), the first university technology-transfer office; on 19 February 1927 WARF licensed the process to Quaker Oats for cereals, and later pharmaceutical firms sold it as Viosterol. The patent ran to 1945, \"by which time rickets had all but been eliminated.\" Harry Harlow came to Madison in 1930; in 1932 he founded a breeding colony of rhesus macaques and built a primate research facility by renovating a vacant building; his early work at the Vilas Zoo produced the Wisconsin General Test Apparatus and the concept of \"learning sets.\" (The surrogate-mother experiments are 1950s — outside the window.) The odd detail: The vitamin was made by shining an ultraviolet lamp on breakfast cereal, and the university kept the money.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A lamp that gives food a hidden nutrient; a zoo colony of caged primates under a professor who studies how they learn to learn. Section 13 would want the lamp, the IHS would want to know what else a \"sun in a bottle\" does to things that avoid light.",
