      "summary": "A mission chapel opened in 1923 for Mexican steelworkers brought to South Chicago by Illinois Steel; Cardinal Mundelein gave it to the Claretians in 1924. Fr. James Tort, a Barcelona-born Claretian, became pastor in 1927; with \"about 90% of his parishioners … without jobs,\" he began devotions to St. Jude, patron of desperate causes — the first novena was held on 17 Feb 1929, eight months before the Crash — and the shrine drew thousands through the Depression. A WPA interviewer in Dec 1936 recorded 5,700 members (8,020 Mexicans counting children, by the parish's 1935 census), four Masses a day, six societies (the Holy Name society 420; the Archicofradía 575; Sagrado Corazón 620), English/Spanish classes for ~80 adults, and weekly plays: \"All of the 8,020 Mexicans are catholics … others non-catholics do not interest us.\" The odd detail: America's national shrine of hopeless causes began in a Mexican steelworkers' church, in February 1929 — before the Crash.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — IHS's South Side desk: a Spanish-speaking Claretian house with a relic shrine drawing thousands of the desperate — the perfect cover for priests who hunt what feeds on desperation.",
