      "summary": "The State Highway Department began an auto ferry across the Straits in 1923 with the Ariel, a 95-ft boat converted to carry 20 cars; the service grew fast on second-hand ferries and ran summers only until regular winter service began in 1936, using the leased railroad icebreaker Sainte Marie (II). The railroads' own crossing was the Chief Wawatam (Toledo, launched 26 August 1911, in service 18 October 1911; 338 ft; three triple-expansion engines and a bow propeller that sucked water from under the ice so the ship's weight broke it), carrying 18–26 railcars year-round, with winter crossings that could take many hours. The state ferries ended with the Vacationland's last crossing on 1 November 1957 when the bridge opened. The odd detail: Until 1936 Michigan's two peninsulas were not connected by road in winter at all; and the machine that connected them by rail was a ship that ate ice from underneath.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
