      "summary": "Frances Toor (1890–1956), a Berkeley-trained anthropologist who moved to Mexico City in 1922, founded Mexican Folkways in 1925 with a small subsidy from the Mexican education ministry (unverified — V_M02: the subsidy is not on the Wikipedia page read) and published it irregularly until 1937 — the period's English-language window on Mexican popular religion (she later documented Santa Muerte prayers, see above). She was among the first outside visitors to Janitzio's Night of the Dead in 1923, and advised readers that before dawn \"so many candles are lit that it is possible to take pictures\" in the island cemetery. The Purépecha islanders' vigil — candle-lit boats crossing to the church and graveyard overnight — became the period's most photographed Día de Muertos; the 40-metre Morelos monument on the island's summit was begun in 1933. The odd detail: a folklorist's photography tip — enough candles to shoot by — marks the moment a private vigil became a tourist sight.",
