      "summary": "Goodyear executive William C. Young visited Friedrichshafen in October 1922 to propose a joint venture to Hugo Eckener; the Goodyear-Zeppelin Corporation was formally created 1923–24, with Goodyear holding two-thirds and Luftschiffbau Zeppelin one-third, and Goodyear getting the use of Zeppelin patents. In November 1924 Dr. Karl Arnstein — Zeppelin's chief stress engineer, a Prague-born Jew — arrived in Akron as vice-president of engineering with a team of 12 hand-picked Zeppelin engineers; the Zeppelin captain Ernst Lehmann also went to Akron. To build the Navy's two giant rigid airships the company raised the Airdock (built 20 April – 25 November 1929, $2.2 million): 1,175 ft long, 325 ft wide, 211 ft high, 13 steel arches, 364,000 sq ft of floor with no interior supports — the largest such building in the world at completion, its design inspired by a 1913 Dresden hangar. Each end closes with two semi-spherical \"orange-peel\" doors of 600 tons each, running on 40 wheels on curved rails. The partnership frayed after 1939 and was dissolved in December 1940 (Goodyear's own history says 1941). The odd detail: The building makes its own weather — when humidity is high and the temperature changes suddenly, condensation falls inside as a fine mist, \"the illusion of rain,\" per the designer.",
