      "summary": "Sinai, Chicago's first Reform congregation (1861), held its first Sunday service in January 1874 and kept Sunday services for 69 years; men and women sat together and the requirement to face east was removed. When Emil G. Hirsch died in 1923 (R23) the pulpit went to Louis L. Mann, rabbi of Mishkan Israel in New Haven and a Yale lecturer in comparative ethics, who held it until 1962 and lectured in the University of Chicago's Oriental Languages department from 1924. Time (1943) lists his causes: a pioneer with Margaret Sanger in the American Birth Control League, director of the National Crime Prevention Institute, Hoover's White House Conference on Child Health and Protection, Ickes's Housing Commission. Sinai's Sunday School had 600 pupils and employed a psychiatrist; its Forum — 29 years old by 1943 — drew 1,800 to 3,500 people on Monday nights, \"roughly half Jewish, half non-Jewish,\" at $5 for a 15-lecture series, with speakers like Walter Duranty and Will Durant. Mann: \"If I had nine lives I should be a pastor nine times.\" The odd detail: A Reform temple whose Sunday school kept a psychiatrist on staff, and whose rabbi told Time \"it is not too important to know who Abraham's grandpa was.\"",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (Mann's birth date, 25 Jan 1890, came from a search summary of prabook — consistent with \"53\" in 1943). • address in the 1930s commonly given as Grand Blvd./South Parkway at 46th St. — UNVERIFIED",
