      "summary": "Alma Reed (born San Francisco 1889) arrived in Yucatán in early 1923 (via the SS Mexico from New York; she reached Progreso in February) as the New York Times's correspondent with the visiting archaeologists. She met Carrillo Puerto at a reception on 14 February 1923; the party visited Uxmal on 15 February. They fell in love; that summer the poet Luis Rosado Vega wrote the words and Ricardo Palmerín the music of the song \"Peregrina\" at the governor's request — it began as a Cuban-style danza and later became a bolero; Rosado Vega said the inspiration came in an afternoon rain on the road into San Sebastián. They were engaged in July 1923 and planned to marry in San Francisco (in January 1924 per Hulme; March 1924 per another account) and live at \"Villa Aurora.\" The telegram announcing his death reached her at the Fairmont Hotel in San Francisco, addressed to Pixan Halal — her Maya name. She wore a copper bell from the Sacred Cenote in a red-triangle setting inscribed, in Maya, \"I will not forget.\" She later founded the Delphic Studios in New York (1928), became José Clemente Orozco's patron, and died on 20 November 1966. She was buried in Mérida's General Cemetery, and used to visit his tomb each 3 January. The odd detail: her April 1923 Times scoop on the Sacred Cenote (see 9) outed Thompson as a smuggler at the very moment her lover was building an alliance (Carnegie, the planters, the Maya) in which Thompson was a guide — Hulme notes she \"paid no heed\" to the consequences.",
