      "summary": "On 13 December 1933, two days before repeal took hold, the Illinois State Journal tallied Prohibition's bodies in the county: an official count of 20, 18 documented, \"all marked on the official ledgers as unsolved.\" A few of the eighteen, from the local-history page: Sam Feliccia, stabbed and weighted into the Sangamon River (found after 26 Dec 1923; rumour said he had collected protection money from bootleggers and failed to protect them); John Napoli, beaten and throat cut, in the river at Riverton (1 May 1925); Bert Zumwalt, a Chicago & Alton Railroad special agent, shotgunned in his car (22 Dec 1925); Jasper Aiello, shot by three men from a black sedan at Ninth and Miller (14 Feb 1927), and his uncles Frank and Salvatore \"Bob\" Aiello, killed with sawed-off shotguns by two masked men at the Blue Bird Cafe on 11 Nov 1927 — an uncle had been shot dead in St. Louis the day before (\"a St. Louis–Chicago–Springfield bootleggers war\"); two unidentified men in the river with railroad iron on their bodies (March–April 1928); Cesare Sansone (20 May 1929), \"friendly with government prohibition agents\" days after raids; Dominic Tarro, the \"bootleg king of Benld\" who built the Coliseum dance hall (2 May 1930); ex-\"booze squad\" cop-turned-detective George Mann (shot 3 Nov 1930, died two weeks later, said he knew the shooters, was dismissed from the force in 1927); a man \"tortured to death and taken for a boat ride\" at the Chinkapin Bridge (22 Nov 1930); Charles Dawson, gambler and ex-bootlegger, shot at Seventh and Washington (28 Dec 1931); and on 16 April 1932 John Picco, the Italian consular agent, shotgunned as he walked with his nine-year-old son — his food warehouse at Third and Carpenter had exploded in 1922 and his home was bombed twice. The page names Fleischmann Yeast and Corn Products Refining Co. as accused suppliers of the bootleggers' raw material. The odd detail: Springfield's ledger ends with the Italian consul's agent shot in the street in front of his own child, and no one ever identified a killer in any of the eighteen.",
