      "summary": "The Carnegie Institution of Washington approved a Chichén project in December 1913; the Mexican Revolution and the Great War delayed it. Its Yucatán front was the Archaeological Institute of Yucatan, announced in late October 1922; on 15 February 1923 its scouting party (with Carnegie's president John C. Merriam and trustee William Barclay Parsons) toured Uxmal with Governor Carrillo Puerto. The Mexican government granted the Carnegie a ten-year permit on 3 July 1923 (later extended another ten). Excavation began in the 1923–24 season under Sylvanus G. Morley, with Earl H. Morris as field director; from January 1925 there were four full seasons on the Temple of the Warriors (with the \"complex of a thousand columns\" that suggested Toltec influence). Ann Axtell Morris spent 1925–28 painting scale watercolours of the murals the moment they were uncovered, and the French-Mexican painter Jean Charlot copied the carved columns; the result, The Temple of the Warriors at Chichén Itzá, Yucatan (Carnegie, 1931, by Earl and Ann Morris and Charlot), is the primary visual record because the murals themselves faded within decades of exposure. The project wound down in 1940. The odd detail: a painter from the Mexican muralist circle spent seasons copying a Toltec-Maya temple's carved columns for an American foundation.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"the murals faded; only the watercolours survive\" — a faction can own the copies and edit the record. Morley's team is a ready-made front for the Secret Service, IHS or Hunter Guild.",
