      "summary": "After a decline in henequen prices, the Casta Divina turned to \"cultural tourism\": a Compañía Impulsora del Turismo a las Ruinas (7 May 1921) and an Asociación Conservadora de Monumentos Arqueológicos de Yucatán (1922; Cantón president, Carrillo Puerto honorary president, with Edward H. Thompson among the members). Carrillo Puerto's answer was a road: built \"in double-quick time\" by communal work-gangs, the Dzitas–Chichén Itzá road was opened on 14 July 1923 with a plaque from the Liga de Albañiles honouring \"the Maya builders of the grandiose work of their monuments,\" and a ten-day festival for which thousands of Maya were brought from all over the peninsula, most of whom had never seen Chichén Itzá; ancient dances, music and drama were recreated; Cantón spoke, then the governor, in Maya. He called the road \"a sociological bridge\" between the Maya's splendid past and their present. In September the Eighth Mexican Press Congress met in Mérida: on 1 September Carrillo Puerto met the delegates at Progreso and read a telegram announcing US recognition of the Mexican government; he took them all to Chichén the next day, and Alma Reed, a delegate, filed that the journalists backed \"an intensive campaign for the enforcement of the agrarian laws\" — after which the henequeneros planted their own story in the New York Times about socialism ruining the industry. The odd detail: thousands of Maya, most seeing Chichén Itzá for the first time, were brought there by a socialist governor for a festival in the same fortnight the Carnegie permit was granted (3 July) and only three months after Alma Reed had exposed Thompson's smuggling from the same site.",
