      "summary": "The mystery author \"B. Traven\" arrived in Mexico in the summer of 1924 and lived in and around Tampico in the 1920s, working as the heroes of his books do — cotton picker, oilfield roustabout, cattle driver, baker, waiter (the five jobs of The Cotton-Pickers, 1926; first published in Germany as Der Wobbly). The Death Ship (1926) and The Treasure of the Sierra Madre (German 1927; English 1935) followed; Treasure opens in Tampico, where Dobbs and Curtin scrounge for oil-field work and sleep at the Hotel Oso Negro (\"Black Bear\"), a vermin-ridden flophouse where they hear old Howard's gold stories. Traven was very probably Ret Marut, a German actor and anarchist editor (Der Ziegelbrenner) who fled the suppression of the Bavarian Soviet Republic in 1919; in 1926 he was the photographer of a UNAM expedition to Chiapas. John Huston's 1948 film won three Oscars; Traven dealt with it as \"Hal Croves.\" The odd detail: the greatest American treasure-curse story of the century was written in German by an anonymous anarchist fugitive who had worked the Tampico oil fields.",
