      "summary": "A December 1928 Social Service Review piece (in the FLPS) says Mexicans held everything from clerical jobs at Montgomery Ward and Wrigley to industrial work at International Harvester and Western Electric; South Chicago Mexicans were mostly steelworkers at 42.5–54 cents an hour on rotating shifts; many were seasonal men who went to Michigan and Minnesota beet fields and came back to a winter with little work; about 162 pupils (9.8 percent) of Mexican schoolchildren were in Catholic schools. Redfield's 1924 informants told him some firms had a rule against hiring Mexicans, big firms took only a few, and a few seemed to be pushing them out; Mexicans competed with Black workers in some plants and with Poles, who got the better jobs, in others; firms that wanted to keep Mexican labour offered to pay the fare back to the border. One paint firm hired Mexicans, informants said, because \"their colour\" kept the paint from harming them — a period racial belief, reported as such. The odd detail: the same colony had a Montgomery Ward clerk and a South Works furnace hand, and a firm that hired men for a supposed immunity to lead paint.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the FLPS summary; Medium for Redfield's informants' generalisations.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the \"colour and paint\" belief is a period superstition that a factory doctor could turn into a real poisoning; a real lead-paint plant is a place investigators can visit.",
