      "summary": "The Church Federation survey by Robert C. Jones (1928–31) counted 7,000–8,000 Mexicans here, \"in the poorest quarters in the city,\" with cheap rents and the employment agencies and plants close by. Neighbours: Greeks between Harrison and Polk, Italians west of Halsted (Polk–Roosevelt), Poles east of Halsted, Jews and Russians north of Roosevelt Road. Institutions Jones lists: Hull House (classes, clubs), St. Mark's Presbyterian-Congregational Church, St. Francis (Catholic; \"nearly a thousand\" at its special Mass for Mexicans), the Old Town Boys' Club (200+ Mexican boys), and a settlement house for mothers' and daughters' clubs. A 1927 scrapbook headline says the Maxwell Street \"ghetto\" was \"changing its complexion\" toward Mexican and Black residents (listing only). A search summary of the Redfield diary edition says ~2,500 Mexicans lived at the Hull House end when he walked it in 1924 (UNVERIFIED — not in the chapter I read). City-wide, ~20,000 Mexicans by the late 1920s, falling to ~14,000 by 1939 (Wikipedia). The odd detail: Redfield's first field day, 5 October 1924, was a walk through this district; the Hull House colonia is the first place a sociologist looked.",
