      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Pleasant Prairie",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Kenosha County, Wisconsin, just over the Illinois line on the lakeshore (80th St. south to Tobin Rd./116th St.)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Pleasant%20Prairie%2C%20Kenosha%20County%2C%20Wisconsin%2C%20just%20over%20the%20Illinois%20line%20on%20the%20lakeshore"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "In 1924 Edith Rockefeller McCormick bought a 1,554-acre lakefront parcel (some accounts say 1,800 acres) and planned a whole new town: a business district, golf course, playground, Tudor-style houses, and a \"Lake Shore Drive\" as its main street. A national naming contest was won by Elmer Huge of La Porte, Indiana, who took the $1,500 prize for \"Edithton Beach\". Streets and curbs were laid. When the Depression hit, her debts mounted and the project died; the only remnants were \"curbs and streets leading nowhere\". In 1946 developer Joseph Shaffron bought it, renamed it Carol Beach after his daughter, and built modestly; the undeveloped part is now the Chiwaukee Prairie State Natural Area, a protected wetland. The odd detail: For the whole 1930s a grid of paved, named, empty streets ran through prairie on the lake north of the state line — a town platted by a woman who believed she had been an Egyptian queen, with nobody in it.",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the outline; UNVERIFIED: Krenn & Dato as her developers (remembered; they were her real-estate firm in Chicago).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — An empty street grid on the state line with a \"Lake Shore Drive\" and no houses is where things meet that must not meet in Chicago — a fae market, a Golden Circle rendezvous, a body dump. The naming contest means a stranger from Indiana chose the name; ask why.",
