      "summary": "Redfield's diary shows a contest for the Mexican soul among Protestant denominations and the Catholic Church, each offering help and preaching. One Protestant missionary, sure she had made a convert, was stunned when the family demanded a Catholic funeral at a death. In the press: El Buen Samaritano (June 1924) advertised gospel services by evangelist Filemón Sánchez Hernández; Methodist Temple hosted temperance lecturer Edith M. de Rivera (Mar 1930); El Buen Pastor ran quarterly conferences (Sept 1931) and a field day at a forest preserve that drew about 200 (Aug 1932); its women's group planned an Independence Day celebration at Marcy Center; Rev. Carlos M. Gurrola, after six years with the Baptists, was given farewell dinners on leaving for California (Aug 1932); a Dr. Eliud García Treviño founded an anti-alcohol committee with the Baptist Youth Union (Sept 1931); a birth-control lecture was given at a Methodist church (30 May 1931). The odd detail: most aid the colony received in the 1920s came from Protestant organisations — the colony learned to be quietly Catholic in front of the Methodists.",
