      "summary": "Redfield (1924–25) looked for organisations and found few: a Club Benito Juárez (charity; its members had already left Chicago), a Sociedad Hispano-Americana (just founded), a Club Anáhuac and a Mexican Woman's Club (~50 members). Four years later México records dozens: the Sociedad Femenil Mexicana (women's relief for colony children; Hull House Bowen Hall dances; a masquerade on 15 Dec 1928; its founder's death mourned in April 1929); La Alianza Hispano-Americana Lodge 170; the Sociedad Fraternal Mexicana; the Sociedad Mutualista Obreros Libres Mexicanos (South Chicago, Brandon Ave.; president O. B. Hernández in 1930; new officers Sept 1931); the Sociedad Miguel Hidalgo y Costilla (benefit dances, Feb 1930); the Unión Benéfica Española and the Sociedad Española de Chicago (eighteenth anniversary in April 1933, so founded 1915; \"oldest society of its kind in Chicago\"); the Club Latino-Americano Cultural, which paid the 1929 burial expenses of Valentín Guevara, who died at Cook County Hospital; the Pan-American Club; the Sociedad Hijos de México; Club Azteca; Centro Cultural Morelos (Aug 1932); plus a Comité Pro-Vasconcelos (June 1929) and an Ortiz Rubio committee (Aug 1929). A Nov 1928 commentary titled \"The Exclusiveness of the Mexican Societies\" argued the colony needed distinctly Mexican organisations; in Dec 1928 Javier Mondragón proposed a Mexican Center for cultural and mutual aid. The odd detail: the count went from four half-dead clubs (1924) to dozens (1928) — the colony organised in four years, before the Depression made it necessary.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
