      "summary": "Balbo's 24 Savoia-Marchetti S.55 flying boats left Orbetello on July 1, 1933 and landed on Lake Michigan near Burnham Park on July 15; the Sioux made him \"Chief Flying Eagle,\" Roosevelt gave him the Distinguished Flying Cross, and Chicago renamed 7th Street \"Balbo Drive.\" Mussolini then sent a 2,000-year-old column from Ostia (13 feet tall, greenish breccia), which arrived in 1934 during the fair's second season and still stands; its travertine base reads in part: \"Fascist Italy, by command of Benito Mussolini, presents to Chicago exaltation, symbol, memorial of the Atlantic Squadron led by Balbo.\" Antifascists protested at the installation. Behind it lay a decade of organising: the Fascist League of North America (founded 1924 in New York under Count Paolo Ignazio Thaon di Revel) grew from nearly forty fasci formed by mid-1923; fights between fascists and the Anti-Fascist Alliance killed at least a dozen people, split evenly; Mussolini had it dissolved in 1929 after a Harper's exposé, \"Mussolini's American empire.\" The odd detail: a Roman column older than Christianity, carved with a Fascist dedication, stands on the Chicago lakefront.",
