      "summary": "Harris, heir to the Massey-Harris farm-machinery fortune, became interested in philosophy and Eastern thought about 1910 (probably via journalist Roy Mitchell) and formally joined the Toronto Lodge of the Theosophical Society in 1924, writing widely on spirituality and art; he was, per the page, \"the only member of the Group of Seven\" to align with European and American modernism, using abstraction to \"express ideas of the spirit.\" His money financed the Group's boxcar trips of 1918–19 and (with Dr. James MacCallum) the Toronto Studio Building. In 1934 he married Bess Housser after leaving his first wife (there were bigamy threats from her family), and moved to Hanover, New Hampshire; later to Santa Fe (1938) and Vancouver (1940). The odd detail: The Group's mountains — the national symbol of the Canadian landscape — were painted by a man who, publicly, thought he was painting spirit; his fortune bought the room where Tom Thomson worked.",
