      "summary": "The SciELO study \"Quemando santos para iluminar conciencias\" (on the \"de-fanaticising\" project) says images were piled in plazas and parks and incinerated, with brigades searching homes for sacred objects and possessors jailed. A film by Ignacio Illán Cortés shows a crowd led by Garrido watching a crucifix burn as a symbol of moving from an Old Regime to progress. At Paraíso the government held a Bible burning and a Methodist pastor was jailed and tortured; the faithful tried to free him from the municipal jail. At Subteniente García, after a saint-burning, townspeople stormed the Municipal Palace and burned Garrido's portrait — an oral-history line (Archivo de la palabra, Instituto Mora) quotes: \"Allá en Subteniente, alguna vez hicieron la quemazón del retrato de Garrido.\" The Red Shirts were founded in 1931 and dissolved in 1935. The odd detail: a Methodist pastor was tortured for a Bible — the anticlerical state persecuted a Protestant too.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (oral history and one study; dates missing).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the film reel of the bonfire is a plausible artefact of a 1930s investigation: what did the film-maker see in the flames that isn't in the projected image? Keep the villagers as villagers; the hook is the camera.",
