      "summary": "William S. Sadler, Chicago surgeon-turned-psychiatrist (consulting psychiatrist at Columbus Hospital; psychiatric counselling from 1930), was a professional debunker of mediums — The Truth About Spiritualism (1923) (unverified — V_R19), The Mind at Mischief (1929), investigations with a Northwestern psychologist and the stage magician Howard Thurston. Yet between 1906 and 1911 a woman had brought him a husband with a strange sleep condition who claimed contact with beings from elsewhere, and Sadler could not explain him. In 1924 he began Sunday teas at the house for up to fifty; by 1925 the \"Forum\" had thirty members, closed its meetings and required a pledge of secrecy; that year a large handwritten document appeared; papers kept appearing into the 1930s and then ceased, after which Sadler led the group openly; in 1935 Sadler concluded they were genuine. The result, The Urantia Book, was published in 1955. His wife and partner Dr. Lena Kellogg Sadler (1875–1939), a physician and Chautauqua lecturer, sat in the Forum until her death. The odd detail: The city's best-known exposer of fake mediums ran, in his own drawing room, a sworn secret society transcribing a sleeping man — for eleven years of the decade.",
