      "summary": "Harry Hoxsey, an ex-coal-miner, opened his first cancer clinic at Taylorville in 1924 selling a family herbal \"tonic\" (licorice, red clover, burdock, barberry, cascara, prickly ash, buckthorn, potassium iodide) and a caustic paste (antimony, zinc, bloodroot, arsenic, sulphur, talc). Repeatedly enjoined for practising without a licence, he opened seventeen clinics in all, moving as the law closed in. In 1930 he joined the Iowa broadcaster Norman G. Baker at the Baker Institute in Muscatine; the partnership ended in lawsuits and another injunction. By 1936 he was in Dallas, where his clinic grew into one of the largest private cancer centres in the world (8,000 patients and $1.5 million a year by the 1950s). The AMA and Fishbein called it fraud for thirty years. The odd detail: a Depression-era cancer cure sold from a coal town in Illinois by a man whose father, he said, had learned it from a horse.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the outline; the \"horse\" origin story is folklore/self-promotion (from memory, labelled).",
