      "summary": "Bimini, fifty miles from Florida, was the last stop for Nassau's liquor: sailing ships brought it from Nassau and transferred it to big barges anchored off Gun Cay as floating warehouses, where American speedboats loaded. After their first house burned on 18 November 1934, Henry and Helen Duncombe built the Compleat Angler hotel (12 rooms and a rowdy bar, finished 1935) out of the timber of a derelict liquor barge, the Dreamland, which had supplied American rum-runners since 1924. Ernest Hemingway stayed there 1935–37, fished from his boat Pilar, and worked on To Have and Have Not (1937) — his Prohibition-smuggling novel. The odd detail: the famous bar was built of a bootleg barge's planks.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
