      "summary": "The Detroit & Cleveland Navigation Company's twins — Greater Buffalo launched 27 October 1924 at American Ship Building, Lorain: 518.7 ft, 7,739 GRT, 1,500 passengers, 103 cars, 1,000 tons of freight, an orchestra and dining saloon, a crew of 300 — were among the largest sidewheelers ever built on the lakes. Both were taken out of service from 1930 through 1935 \"due to union disputes and financial losses,\" pulled again in 1938, and back in 1939. The Navy took Greater Buffalo on 7 August 1942 and commissioned her as the training carrier USS Sable (IX-81) on 8 May 1943 at Navy Pier. D&C's fleet in the window also included City of Detroit III (1912), City of Cleveland III (1907), Eastern States (1901) and Western States (1902); the company had bought the Detroit & Buffalo Steamship Co. in 1909. The odd detail: Two 500-foot floating hotels with orchestras tied up idle for five straight Depression years, their 300-man crews on the beach — the lake's biggest unemployment line.",
