      "summary": "Born January 30, 1901, in Illingen, Württemberg, Pfaus emigrated in 1924 and, by his own story, drifted as a hobo, forester, prospector, cowboy and Chicago policeman (the 1930 census lists him as a machinist). From 1933 he led the Chicago Friends of New Germany, belonged to the Germania Club with Walter Kappe and Heinz Spanknöbel, and edited Chicago's Weckruf und Beobachter; from 1936 he informed for the Nazi party press office. In 1938, at the German consul's suggestion, he went back to Germany and headed the American-Canadian-Irish section of the Hamburg propaganda outfit the Deutscher Fichte-Bund. As Abwehr agent \"Stier\" (Bull), he went to Ireland February 2–14, 1939, and met IRA leaders Seán Russell and Jim O'Donovan to open the German–IRA channel. The odd detail: the man who opened Germany's line to the IRA had, by his own account, been a Chicago cop and a cowboy.",
