      "summary": "Morris Fishbein edited the Journal of the American Medical Association from Chicago 1924–1950 and made the AMA the national scourge of quackery: Fads and Quackery in Healing (1932) went after homeopathy, osteopathy, chiropractic, Christian Science and radionics; the AMA's Bureau of Investigation under Arthur J. Cramp kept files on every nostrum. In 1938 Fishbein's two-part \"Modern Medical Charlatans\" in Hygeia named John R. Brinkley; Brinkley sued for $250,000 in 1939 and lost — the jury found he \"should be considered a charlatan and a quack in the ordinary, well-understood meaning of those words,\" and the verdict loosed over $3 million in suits against him. In the same year, 1938, Fishbein and the AMA were indicted under the Sherman Act for fighting a Washington group-health cooperative; the AMA was convicted and fined $2,500, Fishbein acquitted. The odd detail: the country's file on every fake cure, every radium tonic, every gland doctor, sat in a Chicago office on Dearborn Street — the single best research library for an investigator of the strange.",
