      "summary": "J. Burgess, Frank and Herbert Book hired Louis Kamper for a string of buildings on Washington Boulevard; the Book-Cadillac Hotel opened on 8 December 1924 as the tallest building in Detroit and the tallest hotel in the world, and their 38-storey Book Tower (1926) was briefly the city's tallest. A second Book Tower of 81 storeys — \"designed to be the world's tallest building\" — was cancelled in the Depression. The Purple Gang used the hotel, and Abe Bernstein kept a residence on its top floor until his death in 1968. On 2 May 1939, in the lobby, Lou Gehrig told Yankees manager Joe McCarthy to leave him out of that day's game against the Tigers, ending his 2,130-game streak; the hotel's history also has him collapsing on its grand staircase that day. The odd detail: The Purples' leader lived above the city's grandest ballroom for four decades.",
