      "summary": "Born in Karlsruhe on 2 May 1880, Valentiner worked under Wilhelm Bode at Berlin's Kaiser Friedrich Museum from 1905 and was the Metropolitan Museum's first curator of decorative arts (1907). When the Great War began he went home to serve in the German army; after front-line service in 1916 he was appointed to the General Staff in Berlin. He came to Detroit as adviser and then director of the DIA (1924–45), became an American citizen around 1930, saw Diego Rivera's San Francisco work and persuaded Edsel Ford to underwrite the Detroit Industry murals. He left in 1945 only because of a city civil-service age limit. The odd detail: The man who brought a Mexican revolutionary to paint Ford's factory had sat on the Kaiser's General Staff in 1916–18.",
