      "summary": "At 3:45 p.m. on 5 February 1924 (unverified — V_R39: MNopedia gives no time; another summary says about 3:30 p.m.), fifteen minutes before shift's end, the water and bog of Foley Lake broke into the mine and flooded the 200-foot shaft, where 48 miners were working at the 165- and 175-foot levels. In under twenty minutes the shaft filled to within 15–20 feet of the surface. Seven climbed out; 41 drowned — Minnesota's worst mining disaster. Thirty-eight of the 41 were married; they left more than 80 children. The last body was recovered nine months later. The mine, first worked for ore in 1917 and owned by George H. Crosby, had shipped about 70,000 tons that year. The site is now the Milford Mine Memorial Park (developed from 2010; National Register of Historic Places, 2011). The odd detail: The mine was worked so close under a lake that the bog itself came down the shaft — and it took nine months to find the last man.",
