      "summary": "The Capone syndicate bought the Harlem Inn on February 29, 1924 in the name of Louis Lipschultz (Jack Guzik's brother-in-law), who deeded it on January 7, 1925 to Jeannette Keithly, Guzik's sister-in-law. It was a brothel; the Rev. Hoover's association had earlier forced the sheriff to raid it. When police hunted Capone after the McSwiggin murder in April 1926 they raided the Inn and found firearms, \"a large amount of dynamite\", whiskey — and the daily books. Eighteen days of records (April 6–25, 1926) showed $6,398 in takings, projected to $129,000 a year. Each sheet listed every woman by name in column one, her night's income, the house's cut, a 10% charge \"for towel service\", her net, her commission on liquor sold, and her total. The odd detail: The house charged its women for towels — in writing, by name, every night — and kept dynamite in the same building as the ledgers.",
