      "summary": "On 6 March 1924 the Courier reported that the City Council had unanimously adopted Arvey's resolution against the immigration bill in Congress; he spoke on \"how the new immigration bill discriminates against certain nationalities, and how it recognizes other nationalities as undesirable immigrants.\" A delegation — Arvey (chairman), Petlak, Nelson, Klinke, Guernsey and Franhauser — was to leave as soon as Congressman A. J. Sabath arranged a hearing before the immigration committee. Alderman Schwartz argued from a 1750 Pennsylvania dispute that \"a country always gains by immigration, and … a country that has no immigration is fated either to remain stationary or to retrogress.\" Adamkiewicz, Petlak and Burns spoke in favour; no one spoke against. Earlier Courier items that week: a council committee hearing (27 Feb) and \"Persecution of Immigrants Unanimously Condemned by Council Committee and Representatives of All Nationalities in Chicago\" (5 Mar). The odd detail: Polish, Scandinavian, German and Jewish aldermen voting as one bloc — the period's ethnic categories uniting against a law that ranked them.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
