      "summary": "On 3 Apr 1924 Annan shot her lover Harry Kalstedt in her bedroom, the bullet passing sideways through his torso; by some papers she played the record \"Hula Lou\" over and over and danced while he bled out, then had her husband call the police. Self-defence against rape, then a quarrel, then \"we both reached for the gun.\" Acquitted 25 May 1924 (the Wikipedia summary misprints 1925) and within hours announced she would divorce her husband as \"too slow.\" With Belva Gaertner and the rest of the \"Murderess Row\" she became Maurine Watkins's Chicago (1926). She died of tuberculosis in 1928, aged 28. The odd detail: \"Hula Lou\" on repeat — and the acquittal, which is why Mildred Bolton, twelve years later, thought \"they just don't convict women for killing in Cook County.\"",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the frame; the 46th St address is from a search summary, UNVERIFIED on the page. • Her flat at 817 E. 46th St (per the search summary) (unverified — V2_R06), Chicago; • Belva Gaertner — the original Velma (unverified — V2_R06);",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The 1924 acquittals are the folklore layer under every 1930s Chicago woman's murder trial — the jury's belief that a pretty woman cannot be hanged. Bolton (1936) and Wynekoop (1934) are the decade breaking that spell.",
