      "summary": "WLS's Saturday-night National Barn Dance began 19 Apr 1924; from 1931 it played to a paying house at the Eighth Street Theatre — \"the only known radio program to charge an admission fee\" — with crowds standing in the snow to get in. NBC carried it from 1933 (Alka-Seltzer). The cast made country music's first national stars: Gene Autry, Lulu Belle and Scotty, Patsy Montana, Red Foley, George Gobel, Smiley Burnette, the Williams Brothers (with the boy Andy Williams). It ran on WLS until 1959. The odd detail: the farm-belt's Saturday night came from a Loop theatre with a box office.",
