      "summary": "The Indiana Klan planned a conference and parade in South Bend. On 17 May 1924 Notre Dame students blocked Klansmen getting off their trains and tore off their robes and regalia; \"before noon all of the Klansmen traffic directors had been 'unmasked and unrobed'\". Several hundred students gathered at the Klan building throwing rocks and smashing windows. On 19 May thousands massed downtown again and only the arrival of university president Fr. Matthew Walsh stopped a fight; the next day coach Knute Rockne told a campus rally to obey Walsh. The klavern broke up days later, and the students' hostility is credited with helping bring down the Indiana Klan. (South Bend in the decade: Studebaker, Oliver Chilled Plow, Singer, Bendix — whose workers \"staged the first sit-in strike in American history in 1936\", by the city page's claim; Dillinger's last bank job, 30 June 1934, R02.) The odd detail: Catholic college boys stripping hooded Klansmen in the street, a football coach as peacemaker.",
