      "summary": "Congress created the Border Patrol on 28 May 1924 in the Department of Labor (Labor Appropriation Act; Immigration Act of 1924). Before that, \"mounted watchmen\" of the Department of Commerce and Labor — fewer than 75 men, based at El Paso — had patrolled since 1904 chiefly to stop Chinese immigration, and \"mounted inspectors\" were added in March 1915. The first Border Patrol station was Detroit (June 1924), the second El Paso (July 1924). Deaths came at once: Patrol Inspector James F. Mankin (on duty 28 July, died 14 September 1924), Frank N. Clark, shot near Cordova Island, El Paso, on 13 December 1924 (by smugglers' spotters — CBP; the first death by hostile fire), and Joseph Riley, first on the northern border, who died 6 April 1925 after the patrol car's tie rod broke and it went over a bank near Eureka, Montana. In El Paso district alone, 238 gunfights and 23 patrol inspectors killed between 1924 and 1933. The odd detail: the Border Patrol's first station was in Detroit — the Great Lakes liquor river, not the Rio Grande — and its ancestors (the El Paso \"mounted watchmen\" of 1904) were created to watch for Chinese, not liquor; the liquor war found it, not the other way round.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the foundation and the first-station facts (Wikipedia); Medium on the names and dates of the dead (search summaries of CBP/Honor First pages — not read in full).",
