      "summary": "During Prohibition Caesar (Cesare) Cardini, an Italian immigrant who had run restaurants in San Diego, moved to Tijuana and opened Caesar's, catering to Americans who wanted drinks and a meal legally. His daughter Rosa told the story that on 4 July 1924, a holiday rush having emptied the kitchen, her father improvised a salad of romaine, garlic-infused oil, coddled egg, lemon, Worcestershire sauce, Parmesan and croutons, tossed at the table for show. (The recipe was published by Rosa in 1986 (unverified — V_M06: Wikipedia cites a 1987 interview with Rosa).) His Tijuana restaurant remained a destination for \"early foodies\" and Hollywood stars; Cardini opened in San Diego in 1936. The odd detail: the signature dish of the Prohibition border is an accident of running out of food on the Fourth of July.",
