      "summary": "Redfield signed a contract with the Local Community Research Project (Rockefeller-funded) to study \"Los mexicanos en Chicago,\" starting 1 October 1924 at $500 (a half-time fellowship), supervised by Ernest W. Burgess and with monthly reports to Fay-Cooper Cole; his father-in-law, Robert E. Park, was one of the department's leading sociologists. He began fieldwork on 5 October 1924 with a visit to Hull House; on 31 October 1924, after his would-be interpreter failed to show twice, he began interviewing in Spanish himself. He made twenty visits to Mexican neighbourhoods, drew maps, read social-agency case files and collected the Mexican papers; his contacts were Manuel Bueno and Samuel Cohen (Brighton Park). On 31 January 1925 he attended a dance in the colony with his wife. The study ended in April 1925; his diary was never written up. He went on to Tepoztlán (Nov 1926), and the diary was published in 2008 (Arias & Durand, Mexicanos en Chicago). His original interest in Chicago's Mexican migrants was cut from the published Tepoztlán book. The odd detail: a 1924 sociologist's Mexican study was never finished, but the field notebook survives — the best day-by-day record of the colony's first decade.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (editors' account of the diary and its context).",
