      "summary": "On 17 September 1924 Governor-General Lord Byng, on the report of investigator Andrew Thorburn Thompson, ordered that the hereditary Confederacy Council be replaced by a council elected under the Indian Act. On 7 October 1924 an Indian agent and armed RCMP officers entered the Council House, announced that Canada would recognise only the elected council, removed the hereditary chiefs (under the orders of Duncan Campbell Scott) and seized wampum and records. The first election, on 21 October 1924, was held under Section 74 of the Indian Act (the six electoral districts came only with the 4th Elected Council in 1927 and lasted until 2019); the elected council met the next day and its opponents called it the \"Mounties Council.\" According to Chief Dave Hill Sr., secretary of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy Council, only 26 people voted, most or all of them twice. The Confederacy persisted as a parallel government; the 2013 election turnout was 5.15 per cent. The odd detail: An election under a law the community did not accept is said to have drawn 26 votes — Hill's figure (a reported claim, not an audited count).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on 7 and 21 October; the \"26 votes\" is Hill's claim, as quoted.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The wampums are the archive: a real, seized, contested archive is a natural MacGuffin — but the ethically and dramatically right approach is a story where the players return them, not steal them.",
