      "places": [
        {
          "name": "New Orleans → Tampico → Veracruz → the Tuxtla Mountains → Tabasco",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(La Venta, near the Veracruz line) → Chiapas and Guatemala",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=New%20Orleans%20%E2%86%92%20Tampico%20%E2%86%92%20Veracruz%20%E2%86%92%20the%20Tuxtla%20Mountains%20%E2%86%92%20Tabasco%20%20%E2%86%92%20Chiapas%20and%20Guatemala%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The Tulane University of Louisiana expedition to Middle America, financed by an anonymous endowment set up in March 1924, left New Orleans on 19 February 1925 under Frans Blom (Danish-born archaeologist) and Oliver La Farge (ethnologist), and its record, Tribes and Temples (Tulane, 1926 — I saw volume 1 in the archive listing), contains among the first scholarly accounts of the Olmec heartland. In the Tuxtlas they were warned of \"hostile Indians\" and found instead the Popoluca and Nahua villages of Ocozotepec, Mecayapan and Tatahuicapa friendly (abandoned Piedra Labrada was burnt). At La Venta they documented stelae, altars and a colossal head (facing south at the base of the large pyramid, Complex C-1), which is today catalogued as La Venta Monument 1 — a 24-ton, 2.41 m head (search-summary figures). Blom (1893–1963) had come to Mexico in 1919 to work in the oil industry (the page does not say where; the period's oil fields were on the Gulf coast), left in 1922 with malaria, studied with Morley at Harvard, joined Tulane in 1923 and mapped Palenque that year, dug Uaxactún in 1924, and in 1926 headed Tulane's new Department of Middle American Research. (Later: an alcohol problem, marriage to the Swiss photographer Gertrude Duby in 1943, Casa Na Bolom in San Cristóbal in 1950 — outside the period.) The odd detail: the man who first put the Olmec on the scholarly map was an ex-oilman from the Mexican oil boom — the same oil economy the setting turns into an American enclave at Tampico.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on Blom, the departure date and the finding; Medium on the exact head details (search-summary level).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — New Orleans is \"the hinge between the mainland and an occupied Caribbean.\" A Tulane expedition is a plausible front for anything the New Orleans book needs to send south. La Venta lies inside the Tabasco that Garrido Canabal would rule (M01).",
