      "summary": "D. C. Stephenson, Grand Dragon of the Indiana Klan from 1923, who boasted \"I am the law in Indiana,\" abducted Madge Oberholtzer, a 28-year-old state education official, on 15 March 1925 and forced her onto his private train to Chicago; at the Indiana Hotel in Hammond he assaulted her repeatedly. She obtained mercuric chloride tablets from a druggist and swallowed three; she gave a signed dying statement on 28 March and died 14 April of infection from bite wounds and the poison. Stephenson was convicted; when Governor Edward Jackson refused clemency, he exposed the politicians on the Klan's payroll — the Indianapolis Times won the 1928 Pulitzer, and Jackson and Indianapolis mayor John Duvall were indicted. Indiana Klan membership fell from over 250,000 to about 4,000 by 1928. He served 31 years. The odd detail: His revenge was a record of whom he'd bought — the fabled \"black box\" of his files.",
