      "id": "R13-054",
      "title": "Tribune Tower and the stones of the world",
      "date_start": "1925",
      "date_end": "1925",
      "date_label": "1925 (stones collected from the 1920s on)",
      "category": "Science, forensics, medicine, radio & media, weather & disasters: Chicago and the Great Lakes, 1929–1939",
      "tags": [
        "science",
        "media",
        "Tribune Tower",
        "stones"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Robert R. McCormick",
        "John Mead Howells",
        "Raymond Hood"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "435 N. Michigan Avenue",
          "address": "435 N. Michigan Avenue",
          "geography": "Chicago",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=435%20N.%20Michigan%20Avenue%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "WGN's studios inside",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=WGN%27s%20studios%20inside%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The Tribune's 1922 competition ($100,000 in prizes, 260 entries; Howells & Hood's Gothic tower won, Eliel Saarinen second) produced the 463-foot, 36-floor tower finished in 1925. Col. McCormick had Tribune correspondents bring back stones from famous buildings and sites, mortared into the street-level walls — 149 today, including pieces of the Great Pyramid, Westminster Abbey, Notre Dame, the Taj Mahal and Edinburgh Castle (a moon rock was shown 1999–2011). Carved near the entrance are Robin Hood (for Hood) and a howling dog (for Howells); the lobby walls carry quotations on the freedom of the press. WGN broadcast from the tower until 2018. The odd detail: a Gothic tower on Michigan Avenue with fragments of a hundred sacred and royal buildings set into its skin — the most occult-looking object in the Loop, built by a newspaper.",
