      "summary": "The Jones Island plant, built 1925, had the largest wastewater capacity in the world and was the first plant in the U.S. to succeed in making fertilizer from activated sludge. Milorganite — \"Milwaukee Organic Nitrogen\", the winning entry in a 1925 naming contest in National Fertilizer Magazine — went on sale in 1926 as the first pelletized fertilizer in the U.S. The sludge (spent microbes) is dried with air at 900–1,200 °F and pelletized; it was pushed to golf courses in its early decades. The programme grew out of the city's Socialist administrations elected from 1911 (\"sewer socialism\"), with University of Wisconsin agricultural researchers. The odd detail: A socialist city dried its sewage in furnaces and sold it to country clubs' greens.",
