      "summary": "Pastor William Grant Seaman (in Gary from 1916) wanted a church big enough to fight the brothels and saloons around it. US Steel donated the site and paid nearly half of the $800,000 cost; Elbert Gary donated the organ. The nine-storey complex (1925–26) held a 3,000-seat sanctuary, Seaman Hall (a 1,000-seat theatre), offices, a gym, a Sunday school and dining rooms; a rooftop garden was never finished. By 1927 the congregation was 1,700. (It closed in 1975 and is now a roofless ruin.) The odd detail: The Steel Trust built a skyscraper church with a theatre in it in its own company town — God and the mill under one corporate roof.",
