      "summary": "Gerardo Machado, a Liberal and a veteran general of the independence war, won in 1924 on the slogan \"Water, roads and schools\" and took office on 20 May 1925. He built the Carretera Central and the Capitolio (1926–29, finished 20 May 1929), modelled on the US Capitol and the Paris Panthéon, with a bronze Statue of the Republic about 15 m (about 49 ft) tall inside. Having promised one term, he pushed through constitutional amendments in 1927 (the prórroga de poderes) and was re-elected unopposed in 1928. In the centre of the Capitolio's hall a 25-carat diamond marks Kilometre Zero of the national road system — said to have belonged to Tsar Nicholas II and to have been sold to Cuba by a Turkish merchant. (It was stolen on 25 March 1946 — the thieves left a note, \"2:45 to 3:15 — 25 carats\" — and mysteriously returned to President Grau on 2 June 1947; a replica is there now.) The odd detail: the centre point of every distance in Cuba is a jewel with a dead Tsar's name on it. The setting has no Russian revolution — so if the story is true, how did the Tsar's diamond get to Havana at all? (The legend's provenance is itself UNVERIFIED — it is legend.)",
