      "summary": "Timothy Drew (b. 8 Jan 1886), who had founded a Canaanite Temple in Newark in 1913, moved to Chicago about 1925 because \"the Midwest was closer to Islam,\" and registered the temple in 1926. Members carried \"nationality cards\" as Moorish Americans, took \"Bey\" or \"El\" suffixes, and wore fezzes (black for ushers) and turbans. Their Circle Seven Koran borrowed its first 19 chapters from The Aquarian Gospel of Jesus the Christ (1908) and the rest from a Rosicrucian text, Unto Thee I Grant. By late 1929 the Tribune estimated 35,000 members in 17 temples. On 15 March 1929 the dissident business manager Claude Green-Bey was stabbed to death at Unity Hall; Drew Ali was arrested on his return but not indicted. He died at home on 20 July 1929, aged 43 — \"tuberculosis broncho-pneumonia\" officially; followers blamed police beatings or rival members; one said \"The Prophet was not ill; his work was done.\" Funeral 25 July at the Pythian Temple. His chauffeur John Givens-El then announced he was Drew Ali reincarnated; Charles Kirkman-Bey and Edward Mealy El also claimed the succession. On 26 Sept 1929 the Tribune headlined \"Seize 60 After So. Side Cult Tragedy.\" The odd detail: The prophet's chauffeur declared himself the prophet reincarnated — and the scripture was half a Rosicrucian pamphlet.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High; the Sept 1929 \"tragedy\" (a shootout that killed police officers, by standard accounts) is UNVERIFIED in detail here.",
