      "summary": "The Indiana Historical Society holds the Ku Klux Klan, Crown Point, Indiana (Lake County) Records, covering 1913–32 and including minutes of Klan No. 72 from May 1925 to September 1929, financial records and dues receipts (1923–31), the Kligrapp's (secretary's) papers, and references to an executive committee in Hammond. Much of the correspondence is from J. A. Colescott, Imperial Representative for Indiana and Michigan (later Grand Dragon of Ohio). The Klan's Lake County following was built against the region's Catholic immigrants from eastern Europe and Mexico; Lake County's population passed 260,000 in 1930, with first- and second-generation Americans a majority. In Gary itself the pool of Klan-eligible whites was small, but it occasionally paraded. The odd detail: A complete dues-and-minutes record from a steel county where the Klan's likely targets were a majority of the electorate.",
