      "summary": "The Indiana Klan peaked at about 250,000 members — some 30 percent of native-born white men — averaging 2,000 new members a week from July 1922 to July 1923; over 100,000 people attended the July 4, 1923 Kokomo gathering where D. C. Stephenson was proclaimed Grand Dragon (\"I am the law in Indiana\"). In May 1924 Notre Dame students fought a Klan parade in South Bend. Stephenson was sentenced to life on November 16, 1925 for the abduction, rape and death of Madge Oberholtzer. When Governor Ed Jackson refused him clemency in 1927, Stephenson gave the Indianapolis Times lists of officials the Klan had paid, leading to indictments of Jackson and Indianapolis Mayor John Duvall (30 days in jail); the paper won the 1928 Pulitzer. By the end of the decade Indiana membership was about 4,000. The odd detail: the Klan's empire fell not to a raid but to its own paymaster's revenge from a prison cell.",
