      "summary": "Victor Lawson died in Aug 1925 without directing what should happen to his paper; his business manager Walter A. Strong bought it for $13.7 million, the highest price ever paid for a newspaper, and ran it until his death in May 1931. Strong built the 26-story Art Deco Daily News Building in 1929 — one of the first air-rights buildings over railway tracks — with printing plant, radio studios, a riverside plaza and a fountain to Lawson. The paper's WMAQ (first broadcast 12 Apr 1922, managed from Feb 1922 by Judith C. Waller, one of the first women in American broadcasting) became a subsidiary in 1930 and was sold to NBC in 1931; W9XAP television was licensed in 1930. The Daily News kept \"a first-class foreign news service\" and was widely syndicated. (Frank Knox bought it in 1931 and was Landon's running mate in 1936 — from memory, UNVERIFIED this session.) The odd detail: a newspaper built its skyscraper on air — a deck over the trains — and started a television station the year the Depression began.",
