      "summary": "On 29 March 1925 the outgoing Johnson appointed William J. Fulton to finish out his term; Fulton served eight months, made Alice Johnson (the mayor's wife) the second woman in the U.S. to hold a public finance office, and lost the November election to Floyd E. Williams, who had won with \"open and proud\" Klan support (Indiana's Klan peaked in 1923–25 under D. C. Stephenson — see R07). Williams served until 1930 and was declared bankrupt in 1933. Johnson, released in Nov 1925 and pardoned in March 1929 by President Calvin Coolidge, won a third term; the Indiana Attorney General and the city council fought it, the Indiana Supreme Court threw out their injunctions, and he was inaugurated 3 January 1930. In 1932 he was charged with using city materials and labour on his Miller summer home and a rock garden (not convicted); he died 15 June 1938. William Fulton shot himself on 8 May 1930 at his home at 7500 Oak Avenue; his wife Mary found the body. The odd detail: A convicted mayor pardoned in March 1929 and running the city again the following January — and the man he had appointed to keep the seat warm shot himself four months later.",
