      "summary": "Goddard (b. 30 Oct 1891, Baltimore; Johns Hopkins BA 1911, MD 1915) was an Army officer — Ordnance, Military Police and Medical Corps at various times, retiring a colonel — who in April 1925 founded the Bureau of Forensic Ballistics in New York with C. E. Waite, Philip O. Gravelle (who built the comparison microscope) and John H. Fisher; he coined the phrase \"forensic ballistics\" in Army Ordnance that year. In 1927 he re-examined the Sacco–Vanzetti bullets with Gravelle's microscope; in 1929 he examined the St. Valentine's Day cartridges and proved the guns were not police weapons; from 1929 he was professor of police science at Northwestern and editor of the American Journal of Police Science. He was also military editor of the Encyclopædia Britannica. After the war he commanded the Army crime laboratory in Japan. He died 22 Feb 1955. The odd detail: Goddard and Leonarde Keeler — the two founders of American scientific crime detection, in the same building — were both born on 30 October, twelve years apart.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
