      "summary": "The occupation produced a shelf of American books that fixed the period's image of Haiti. Capt. John Houston Craige (b. c. 1890; first saw Haiti in 1912; joined the gendarmerie in 1925, posted to Hinche, ran a broadcasting station in Port-au-Prince, then chief of police of Port-au-Prince) wrote Black Bagdad (1933) and Cannibal Cousins (1934) — \"perceptive if often exaggerated\", in his encyclopedia entry's words, and the titles tell you the rest. Faustin Wirkus's The White King of La Gonave (1931). Arthur J. Burks, a Marine stationed in the Dominican Republic, became a million-words-a-year pulp writer (~800 stories from 1920), including Caribbean \"voodoo\" horror later collected as Black Medicine (1966) — E. F. Bleiler: racial bias \"to the point of grotesqueness\"; late in life he gave psychic readings. And Smedley Butler, who ran the Haitian gendarmerie 1915–18, retired in 1931 and toured with War Is a Racket (Round Table Press, 1935): he had helped make \"Haiti and Cuba a decent place for the National City Bank boys to collect revenues in\". The odd detail: the same occupation produced both the pulp \"voodoo\" story (Burks) and the most famous American confession of imperial racketeering (Butler) — written by Marines of the same generation.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
