      "summary": "The Chicago, Milwaukee & St. Paul — Alexander Mitchell's railroad — bankrupted itself on the Pacific Extension (built and electrified 1914–20 over 645 miles, $257 million against a $60 million estimate; the largest electrification in the world), went into receivership in 1925, re-emerged as the Chicago, Milwaukee, St. Paul & Pacific in 1928 with 11,248 miles by 1929, and filed again in 1935, staying under trustees until 1 Dec 1945. In the middle of the second bankruptcy its own Menomonee Valley shops built the streamlined Hiawatha (May 1935; R18), the fastest scheduled train in the world. The odd detail: The city's railroad was bankrupt for the whole decade and building 100-mph trains in its own shops while it was.",
