      "summary": "A breakaway from the Ku Klux Klan founded about 1925 by William Jacob Sheperd, the Black Legion was led by Virgil (\"V. H.\") Effinger of Lima, Ohio, reputed commander of the \"Western District\" (Michigan, Ohio, Indiana). Arthur F. Lupp Sr. set up the Michigan wing in Highland Park in 1931 and styled himself \"major general\" (Wikipedia: \"who styled himself its major general\"). The Michigan order was organised like an army — 5 brigades, 16 regiments, 64 battalions and 256 companies — with dues of 10 cents a month. Membership estimates run wildly: the FBI's ~135,000; historian Peter Amann 60,000–100,000; historian John Earl Haynes only a few hundred; Michigan at peak 20,000–30,000, a third in Detroit. After the Poole killing (May 12–13, 1936; R06 covers the murder), nearly 50 Legionnaires were convicted of murder, conspiracy, kidnapping, arson and perjury, and the order collapsed. The odd detail: the Legion's paper army had \"Brigade No. 3\" covering thirteen named Michigan counties — commanded by a prison guard, Ray Ernest, who organised floggings.",
