      "summary": "Kappe came to the U.S. in 1925 and worked in a farm-implement factory in Kankakee, Illinois, before moving to Chicago to write for the German-language papers. He was one of the leaders of the Teutonia Society, ran the Deutsche Zeitung (August 1933–1934) and founded the Deutscher Weckruf und Beobachter (1935), and became press secretary of the German American Bund, organising its AV Publishing Company and five other Bund corporations in 1936 before Fritz Kuhn pushed him out. He went back to Germany in 1937, joined Abwehr II (sabotage), and in 1942 organised Operation Pastorius, which landed saboteurs on Long Island and at Ponte Vedra Beach, Florida; the Florida team boarded trains for Chicago and Cincinnati. One saboteur, Herbert Haupt, was a Chicago man who asked his father to buy him a car for his \"business for the German government\"; six saboteurs were electrocuted August 8, 1942, and sixteen people, including Haupt's parents, were arrested. Kappe died on the Eastern Front in 1944. The odd detail: the sabotage chief who planned landings on American beaches learned America on a Kankakee factory floor.",
