      "summary": "Pullman was one of the largest single employers of Black Americans in the country — about 10,000 porters and 200 maids in 1926 — and every one of them was \"George\" to the passengers (of 12,000 porters and waiters that year only 362 were actually named George; the white \"Society for the Prevention of Calling Sleeping Car Porters George\" claimed 31,000 members and in 1926 got Pullman to put name racks in the cars). A porter worked 400 hours or 11,000 miles a month for a $72.50 minimum (average wage $78.11 plus $58.15 in tips, against $33.82 in expenses for meals, lodging, uniform and supplies), slept three or four hours deducted from pay, was charged for anything a passenger stole, and could never be a conductor ($150 for 240 hours, white only). Five hundred porters founded the Brotherhood in Harlem on 25 August 1925 under A. Philip Randolph — who did not work for Pullman and so could not be fired; Webster ran Chicago. Pullman answered with spies, firings, a company union, and friendly ministers and Black newspapers. A 1928 strike was called and postponed (the Chicago History Museum holds the broadside); membership fell to 658 in 1933; then the amended Railway Labor Act (1934) let the porters vote, the Brotherhood beat the company union and was certified on 1 June 1935, took an AFL charter that year — the first Black-led union to get one — and signed its first contract on 25 August 1937 (effective October): a 240-hour month, time-and-a-half after 260, about $1.25–1.5 million a year in raises, and Pullman paying for uniforms after ten years. The odd detail: The museum's 1929 AFL membership certificate belongs to Archibald Motley — the porter whose son painted Bronzeville's nightlife. And in 1934 a porter averaged 73 hours a week at 27.8 cents an hour while a factory hand did 37 hours at 54.8 cents.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — For investigators the porters are the network: 10,000 men who see every passenger on every overnight train in America, who carry the Defender south and letters north, whose company already runs spies among them. A porter who saw something on the Panama Limited between Chicago and New Orleans is the best witness in the game — and the one the Pullman spies report on.",
