      "summary": "José de Jesús Fidencio Constantino Síntora, born 17 October 1898, began from about 1925 to perform healings — reportedly surgical ones, without anaesthesia. By 1927 more than 4,000 people \"from different class levels\" were attending. President Plutarco Elías Calles visited on 8 February 1928, reportedly suffering from nodular leprosy (not then publicly known). Fidencio's methods included tossing objects from a Peruvian pepper tree to the crowds and a sulphurous mud pool that followers held healing. Nuevo León charged him with illegally practising medicine, and doctors called him a fraud. He died at Espinazo on 19 October 1938, aged 40. Today the Fidencista Christian Church honours him and \"cajitas\" (channellers) are said to continue his work; the Catholic Church does not recognise sainthood. His following extends across northern Mexico and the U.S. Southwest. The odd detail: the president of Mexico is said to have gone in secret to a village healer for a disease the papers were not to name — nine days after his government dynamited Christ the King on the Cubilete (30 January 1928, M01).",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on dates and the outline; the Calles visit and leprosy diagnosis are widely reported but private (Medium); the \"surgeries\" are from devotional and hostile sources alike.",
