      "summary": "The third Palmer House (1923–25, 25 storeys, 2,268 rooms — briefly the largest hotel in the world) ran the Empire Room supper club, with Shep Fields among its 1930s bands (Veloz and Yolanda, Hildegarde — remembered, not on the page); the earlier Palmer House's barbershop floor had silver dollars set in the tiles. The Stevens (opened May 2, 1927, 3,000 rooms, then the largest hotel in the world) had a rooftop miniature-golf course (the \"High-Ho Club\"), a cinema, an ice-cream shop and a drugstore; the Stevens family went bankrupt in the Depression \"like four out of five American hotels\", the owners were charged with financial corruption and their conviction reversed unanimously on appeal; the Army bought it for $6 million in 1942 for air cadets. Conrad Hilton took it in 1945. The odd detail: The two biggest hotels in the world, four blocks apart, one with silver dollars in its floor and the other bankrupt with golf on the roof.",
