      "summary": "Potter Palmer moved from Prairie Avenue in 1882, filled the swamp that became Lake Shore Drive and built the 42-room castle at 1350 N. Lake Shore Drive (sold to Vincent Bendix 1928, bought back 1933, empty — see R08/R16). Confirmed addresses this session: 1365 N. Astor — the Charnley house (Sullivan and Wright, 1891–92), owned from 1918 to the 1960s by the real-estate man James B. Waller Jr.; 1340 N. State Parkway — Dr. George Swift Isham's 1899 house (later the Playboy Mansion); 1300 N. Dearborn — the Three Arts Club (1914); 1200 block of Astor — the Edward G. Pauling apartments (Adler & Sullivan, 1886); 4 W. Burton Place — the Madlener house (1902); 1524 N. Lake Shore Drive — the Countiss house (1917); 1000 N. Lake Shore Drive — Edith Rockefeller McCormick (see R08); 1100 N. Lake Shore Drive — the Insulls' town apartment; 1508 N. State — the banker Garrard B. Winston; 120 E. Bellevue Place — the Fortnightly (see R16); 1365 N. Dearborn — the Racquet Club (see R16); 167/195 E. Delaware — the Casino Club; the Ambassador hotels at State and Goethe. Remembered but UNVERIFIED this session (no page read): 1500 N. Astor, the Patterson–McCormick mansion (Stanford White 1893, David Adler's addition 1927, Cyrus McCormick II); 1555 N. State Parkway, the Archbishop's residence (1885; Cardinal Mundelein's town house); 1444 N. Astor, the Edward P. Russell house (Holabird & Root Art Deco, 1929); 1550 N. State Parkway (Marshall & Fox 1912, one apartment to a floor); 1406 N. Astor, Joseph T. Ryerson (Adler 1921). Harvey Zorbaugh's The Gold Coast and the Slum (University of Chicago Press, 1929; his Ph.D. under Robert Park) made this square mile the textbook contrast: the Social Register on the Drive, the rooming houses and Little Hell a few blocks west. The odd detail: The city's whole society could be walked in twenty minutes — and Zorbaugh's 1929 map put the slum within earshot of every one of these front doors.",
