      "summary": "Giancana was first arrested in 1925 for auto theft and by 20 had been the prime subject of three murder investigations without being tried. Convicted of burglary and larceny in 1929, he served three years and nine months in Joliet (released 1932), then worked as a driver and gunman for the Outfit. In 1939 he was convicted of bootlegging — making moonshine after repeal — and got four years, split between Leavenworth and Terre Haute, serving three years and two months. On release in 1942 he persuaded Tony Accardo to take over Chicago's Black-run policy (numbers) lottery; his crew \"convinced Eddie Jones to quit his racket and leave the country\", and the takeover ended with the murder of policy boss Theodore Roe on August 4, 1952. The odd detail: After repeal made liquor legal, the future boss of the Outfit went to federal prison for moonshining.",
