      "summary": "Hired in 1925, Mackenberg went ahead of Houdini in disguise (with his niece Julia Sawyer, showgirl Alberta Chapman, Clifford M. Eddy Jr., Robert H. Gysel), using pun names like \"Frances Raud\" and \"Alicia Bunck,\" sometimes wearing an unnecessary hearing aid. On 18 May 1926 she testified to a House committee on the anti-fortune-telling bill that a medium, Jane B. Coates, claimed senators consulted her and that \"table tipping seances are held at the White House\" with the President's family — a \"fracas,\" officially denied. After Houdini's death she kept going for 20+ years: over 1,000 mediums investigated, all frauds by her count; she lectured on \"Debunking the Ghost Racket\" and worked with the Chicago Tribune; mediums \"contacted\" about three dozen dead husbands she never had. The odd detail: Spirits kept bringing her messages from husbands — she was single.",
