      "summary": "José de Jesús Fidencio Constantino Síntora (born 17 October 1898) began healing the sick at Espinazo around 1925; by 1927 more than 4,000 people from every class came to his sessions. On 8 February 1928 President Plutarco Elías Calles himself came for a healing (records say Calles had nodular leprosy, unknown to the public). Fidencio became famous for operations without anaesthetic and without pain, threw objects at the crowds from beside a pepper tree and submerged followers in a sulphur-rich mud pool. Doctors opposed him and Nuevo León charged him with illegally practising medicine; authorities called him a fraud. He died on 19 October 1938, two days after his 40th birthday. The Fidencista Christian Church venerates him today; the Catholic Church has never recognised him. The odd detail: the president who shot the priests and closed the churches went in person to a Nuevo León peasant healer, on the same trains the pilgrims used.",
