      "summary": "The Panamanian government tried to suppress Guna dress, custom and schooling. At a conference beginning 12 February 1925 (Ailigandí) the Guna, led by Nele Kantule (chief of Ustupu) and Simral Colman (chief of Ailigandí), prepared a revolt; fighting began 22 February, hostilities ceased by 27 February and a peace was signed 4 March 1925 in the presence of the American minister, John G. South; fewer than thirty died, including 27 Panamanian policemen. The revolt was supported by Richard Oglesby Marsh, an American explorer who had come in 1924 seeking \"white Indians\" (light-skinned Guna, actually albinos) in the Darién and stayed as an advocate — the Guna's spokesman who later brought a delegation to North America. An American warship was sent to the Gulf of San Blas. The Republic of Tule's flag — yellow with red stripes top and bottom and a swastika in the middle — was adopted. The odd detail: 1925 — the swastika in the middle of a Guna flag, years before it meant anything else; in the setting, where there is no Nazi Germany, it still doesn't.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (one summary; sources conflict on the treaty date).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"white Indians\" hunted by a Golden-Age explorer is a period-perfect expedition frame (Marsh's 1924 Darién expedition). The IHS or Hunter Guild sends an outsider to something he does not understand and he switches sides. Respect: the Guna are a living people who won a real, negotiated autonomy — they are the protagonists of their own story.",
