      "summary": "Alice Mabel Gray died of uremic poisoning in the early morning of 9 February 1925, after Paul Wilson tapped on the window of the Ogden Dunes developer Samuel Reck on the night of 8 February and asked him to fetch a doctor. Reck drove along the frozen beach to Gary for Dr. De Long; she was already in a coma and died about 5 a.m. \"in Paul's arms.\" Wikipedia's page for her gives 8 March 1930, heart attack — but the article Wikipedia cites for it (David Hoppe, \"Child of the Northwest Wind,\" Traces of Indiana and Midwestern History, Spring 1997) itself says she \"was buried in February 1925\" and quotes the Indianapolis News obituary \"Diana of Dunes Dies As She Lived, In North Indiana Sand.\" So the Wikipedia date contradicts its own footnote; 1925 is the date. Other details from the National Park Service and Hoppe: she took a University of Chicago degree, studied higher mathematics in Germany, worked as a \"computer\" at the U.S. Naval Observatory, and left the city by train on Halloween 1915, aged 34. Paul Wilson moved into her cabin around late 1918; in 1922 Deputy Sheriff Eugene Frank attacked them both without evidence, fracturing her skull. Hoppe reports that the photographer Ted Urice, researching in the 1970s, found no marriage licence and concluded that Wilson's beatings caused the uremic poisoning. Her funeral in Gary \"became like a carnival\" with Duneland souvenirs on sale; Wilson apparently broke down over the casket, pulled a pistol, swore revenge and was restrained by police. She was buried in an unmarked grave; she had wanted cremation on a pyre atop Mount Tom, with her ashes scattered on \"the Northwest Wind.\" The odd detail: The 1930s dune-shackers were repeating the legend of a woman already five years dead — and the funeral was a souvenir stall.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for 9 February 1925 (three sources agree, one of them Wikipedia's own citation). Medium for the abuse claim (Urice's finding as reported by Hoppe). Wilson's surname is UNVERIFIED. • others say a Texas rattlesnake hunter — UNVERIFIED · Samuel Reck — Ogden Dunes developer, eyewitness · Dr.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A dead Naval Observatory mathematician the dunes will not let go of; the 1930s Duneland story is her legend told over a grave nobody marked. Cremation refused by the family is the loose thread — a Hunter Guild or IHS interest in why she wanted her ashes on the wind.",
