      "summary": "The village was Mechanics Grove (1840s), Holcomb (1850s), Rockefeller (1885–1909, for William Rockefeller Jr., a Wisconsin Central stockholder), and then Area (July 1909–April 1925) — named for Arthur Sheldon's school of salesmanship, whose motto was \"Ability, Reliability, Endurance, Action\". Sheldon's estate failed; Archbishop Mundelein bought it in 1921 for his seminary — including a lake once called \"Mud Lake\", which Sheldon had dammed and renamed \"Lake Eara\". In December 1924 railroad men asked that the village be renamed \"Mundelein for the cardinal\"; the state approved in April 1925. On 24 June 1926, the closing day of the Eucharistic Congress (R05), about half a million people came: 820 trains passed through Mundelein station from dawn to midnight, and 18,000 cars. In 1928 Insull's Public Service Company opened a \"Model Farm\" there. The odd detail: A town named after a salesmen's creed became a town named after a cardinal — and the seminary lake is a mud pond with an anagram's name.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
