      "summary": "Samuel Insull's wife, the former Broadway actress Gladys Wallis (born Margaret Anna Bird; on stage with W. H. Crane's company from 1892; married Insull May 22, 1899, at 24 to his 41), returned to the stage in a Chicago charity revival of Sheridan's The School for Scandal in June 1925, then played the same part on Broadway that October. The revival — the utilities king's wife playing Lady Teazle for charity — was the sort of event society bought boxes for; Orson Welles's Citizen Kane borrowed the pattern of a magnate promoting his wife's stage career. She outlived the crash and her husband, dying September 23, 1953. The odd detail: The richest man in Chicago's wife, in her fifties, playing an eighteenth-century flirt for charity while his holding companies were being built on paper.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on dates; the Chicago theatre UNVERIFIED (the Studebaker is remembered).",
