      "summary": "City manager William R. Hopkins pushed through the bonds; about 100,000 people jammed the roads for the 1 July 1925 opening, when night airmail between New York and Chicago began. It claims the first US airport control tower (1929), the first with airplane-to-tower radio (1930), the first airfield lighting system with a 3-million-candlepower beacon (1930), and the first passenger terminal (1929). Traffic grew from 4,000 aircraft in 1925 to 17,600 in 1928. It hosted the National Air Races in 1929, 1931–32, 1934–35 and 1937–39 — not 1930, 1933 or 1936; Cleveland hosted again 1946–49 (ten-day meets over Labor Day; the Thompson pylon trophy and the Bendix cross-country; the 1929 Women's Air Derby from Santa Monica ended here, won by Louise Thaden in the heavy class (unverified — V_R25)). The odd detail: A 3-million-candlepower beacon swept the sky over Cleveland's southwest every night from 1930.",
