      "summary": "A Liga de Inquilinos y Subsistencia (Tenants' League) was formed in June 1925 with the General Workers' Union; the rent strike began 1 October, spread to Colón on 9 October, and bakers, butchers, chauffeurs and streetcar workers struck in solidarity. On 10 October the Panamanian police fired on demonstrators in Santa Ana Plaza and killed four strikers, wounding at least seven. On 12 October at 1:30 pm three battalions of the 33rd U.S. Infantry crossed from Fort Clayton into the city \"ordered to break apart any gathering of more than five people\" (another source says 600 troops with fixed bayonets), at President Rodolfo Chiari's request; they left on 23 October. Organisers including Blázquez de Pedro were deported; a Rent Claims Commission was set up; another rent strike came in August 1932; at least 16 immigrants were deported afterward. West Indians, who made up much of Panama's working class, took no leading role, which deepened the split between Hispanic and West Indian workers. The odd detail: a rent strike in one country ended by another country's infantry regiment, at the host president's request, \"to break apart any gathering of more than five.\"",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for dates; Medium for troop numbers (sources differ).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — American troops step across a line to end a rent strike is the empire in one afternoon. A GM's Panama City is a city where an American officer can order a crowd of six to disperse.",
