      "summary": "The Abendpost (8 Nov 1925) reported Dr. Oskar von Miller, founder of Munich's Deutsches Museum, in Chicago as a guest of utility magnate Samuel Insull and Rufus Dawes, brought over by industrialists to advise on a science museum \"after the pattern of the one built by him in Munich\" — the institution that became the Museum of Science and Industry (Julius Rosenwald's museum, opened in stages from 1933). Introduced at the German Club by A. F. W. Siebel, he said: \"During the war, when we all were starving … you helped us,\" and boasted that Munich's museum passages run nine miles. The odd detail: Chicago's great science museum was modelled on a German one, and its German godfather was hosted by the brother of Charles Dawes — the setting's own president of 1927–29.",
