      "summary": "The standard printed collection of Puerto Rican folktales for this era is Rafael Ramírez de Arellano's Folklore portorriqueño, published in Madrid in 1926, which includes the \"Juan Bobo\" cycle; the Juan Bobo tales arrived from Spain in the oral tradition and have attached to a fool-hero for nearly two centuries. It is the earliest widely cited printed source — earlier collections may exist that I did not find. Puerto Rican legend beyond that (ghosts, La Llorona-type figures, the cuco, coquí stories) — I did not source specific pre-1940 attestations for any; the widely repeated chupacabra is 1995 and must not be back-dated. The odd detail: the fool-hero Juan Bobo is a Spanish import — not an \"island\" creation — which is exactly how Puerto Ricans were themselves imported.",
