      "summary": "In the summer of 1926 — the weeks the Calles Law came into force (31 July) and the churches fell silent — the Harris Foundation's institute on Mexico heard Moisés Sáenz, Sub-Secretary of Public Education in the Calles government, a Protestant educator and the architect of rural schooling, lecture on \"Foreign Investments and Mexican Nationalism,\" \"Humanism and the Mexican Laborer\" and \"Integrating Mexico Through Education,\" alongside the Berkeley historian Herbert I. Priestley. Sáenz told his Chicago audience that in colonial days \"too many churches were built in Mexico, forty-five hundred being built in a period of two hundred years … the Indian was made to pay for the stones and to put them into place as well. The church once built, the priest had an income for life,\" and that religion in his country \"can mean no unifying influence.\" (The same summer's institute also produced José Vasconcelos and Manuel Gamio's Aspects of Mexican Civilization — UNVERIFIED this session.) The odd detail: while Chicago was still glowing from the June 1926 Eucharistic Congress (R05), Calles's own deputy minister stood a few miles away making the government's case against the Church's stones.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (the published lectures). • The same summer's institute also produced José Vasconcelos and Manuel Gamio's Aspects of Mexican Civilization — UNVERIFIED this session.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the Eucharistic Congress and the anticlerical lectures six weeks and a few miles apart — the IHS and the revolution's schoolmen in the same city the same summer. A Hyde Park lecture hall is where an IHS priest first sees the face of the man whose schools will empty his seminaries.",
