      "id": "R29-019",
      "title": "Route 66 starts at Jackson and Michigan",
      "date_start": "1926",
      "date_end": "1926",
      "date_label": "1926; Illinois paved first",
      "category": "Chicago's hinterland, 1925–1940: the collar counties, downstate Illinois and Northwest Indiana",
      "tags": [
        "Chicago",
        "hinterland",
        "Route",
        "Jackson",
        "Michigan"
      ],
      "people": [
        "—"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "From Jackson Blvd at Michigan Ave",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(extended in 1938 past Buckingham Fountain to Lake Shore Drive), out through Cicero, Berwyn, Joliet, Wilmington, Dwight, Pontiac, Bloomington, Springfield, Litc",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=From%20Jackson%20Blvd%20at%20Michigan%20Ave%20%2C%20out%20through%20Cicero%2C%20Berwyn%2C%20Joliet%2C%20Wilmington%2C%20Dwight%2C%20Pontiac%2C%20Bloomington%2C%20Springfield%2C%20Litchfield%2C%20Mount%20Olive%2C%20to%20the%20C"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "US 66 was designated in 1926 with its eastern terminus in the Loop. Illinois had already paved most of the roads that made up 66 and was the first state to finish its segment. Surviving 1920s–30s roadside places: Dell Rhea's Chicken Basket (Willowbrook), Ambler's Texaco (Dwight), the Soulsby Service Station (Mount Olive), and the Ariston Café in Litchfield — \"the longest-operating restaurant along the former US 66\". The odd detail: The Mother Road's \"Mile 0\" is a Loop street corner beside the Art Institute; its first night stop out of Chicago is Dwight, home of the gold cure (next entry).",
