      "summary": "Ground was broken on 7 August 1907; the Pro-Cathedral of St. Mary was built by the French-American architect Emmanuel Louis Masqueray (Beaux-Arts; foundations of Rockville granite, walls of white Vermont granite) and finished in 1914, replacing the Church of the Immaculate Conception (1871). In 1926 Pope Pius XI made it a minor basilica — the first in the United States. The nave barrel vault is 82 ft; the dome rises 138 ft; the cross tops out at about 200 ft; 1,600 seats. It was consecrated in 1941 by Archbishop Dennis Dougherty of Philadelphia during the Ninth National Eucharistic Congress held in the Twin Cities (outside the window). The odd detail: The building that Rome named \"the first basilica in America\" was called a \"Pro-Cathedral\" for 12 years and its formal consecration waited fifteen years after that.",
