      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Cook County — inquests held by deputy coroners wherever the body was",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(the Survey's figures are county-wide)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Cook%20County%20%E2%80%94%20inquests%20held%20by%20deputy%20coroners%20wherever%20the%20body%20was%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Illinois law required the coroner to hold an inquest on every sudden or violent death and on every felonious homicide, before a jury of \"six good and lawful men of the neighborhood\". In practice the Survey found the phrase read as \"such men as the deputy coroners may select, so long as they reside in Cook County\". The jurors were paid one dollar each per hearing. In January 1928 thirteen deputy coroners held 447 inquests in the county and used 918 jurors in all. The State's Attorney of Cook County sent no one to inquests \"except at an occasional inquest of unusual publicity value\". The verdict could find a death accidental, leave it \"open\" (person or persons unknown), or order a named person held to the grand jury — for manslaughter or murder. The odd detail: 447 inquests in one winter month — fifteen dead a day passing before six men paid a dollar apiece.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (primary survey, figures quoted). The exact verdict wording used in the 1930s is not quoted in what I read — \"open verdict\" is the Survey's own phrase.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The inquest is the first public document on any death the party touches. A GM can hand players the transcript: six names, a deputy coroner, a verdict — and one juror who sat on every inquest the thing ever caused.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Internet Archive: illinoiscrimesur00illi",
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/illinoiscrimesur00illi",
          "publisher": "Internet Archive",
          "kind": "book or scanned text",
          "supports": "pp. 309, 633–634, 673"
        }
      ],
