      "summary": "Young's archival work in Mexico City found that consular officers, army officials and intelligence agents of the Calles government — in the Ministry of Foreign Relations and the Confidential Department of the Ministry of the Interior (Gobernación) — \"collected ample information about Cristero activism abroad,\" while the Liga \"maintained constant contact with exiles in the United States, and eventually even established a US headquarters.\" Mexico's ban on the Knights' Columbia magazine and the Mexican Congress's 25 November 1926 debate on its November issue show the state reading the American Catholic press closely. In Los Angeles in December 1934 the Mexican consul discouraged emigrants from joining the Guadalupe processions. Most of the letters Americans wrote to Calles in 1926 applauded his stand against the Church — one of the exceptions was a petition of 23 April 1926 from Mexican parents in Perris, California. The odd detail: the anticlerical state ran its own counter-intelligence on American soil against Catholic exiles — through its consulates — while ordinary Americans wrote fan letters to Calles.",
