      "summary": "The team formed in 1926 at the Giles Post, all Wendell Phillips boys — Walter \"Toots\" Wright, Byron \"Fats\" Long, Willis \"Kid\" Oliver, Andy Washington, Al \"Runt\" Pullins — and went 101–16 in its first season. Abe Saperstein, a short white Chicagoan, became coach-manager-promoter and occasional player; the first barnstorming trip was 48 miles to Hinckley in a Ford Model T with a tattered map, for $75. As the Savoy Big Five (1926–27) they played before dances at the Savoy; by 1928–29 Saperstein had renamed them the \"New York Harlem Globe Trotters\" — \"Harlem\" for the capital of Black America, \"Globetrotters\" to pretend they had been anywhere. Inman Jackson brought the comedy in 1929; in 1935 the Original Celtics forfeited a 32–32 game rather than lose to them; in 1940 they won the World Professional Basketball Tournament in Chicago. The odd detail: A Chicago team called \"New York Harlem\" that had never left Illinois.",
