      "summary": "Samuel Insull's companies built State Line in 1926–29, an Art Deco plant by Graham, Anderson, Probst & White. Its Unit 1, 208 megawatts, built by General Electric at Schenectady, was on its 1929 opening the largest turbine-generator ever built, raising Chicago-area capacity by more than 15 per cent to 1,518 MW. Insull meant the plant to reach over 1,000 MW — the world's first gigawatt plant; it never did. Commonwealth Edison added Unit 2 in 1938. It shut on 31 March 2012. The odd detail: The machine that lit Chicago in the 1930s stood just over the state line, in Indiana — the city's biggest generator sat in another state's jurisdiction.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
