      "summary": "Lutherans were 28% of the city's church members by 1890, split between the strict Missouri Synod and the Wisconsin Synod (which merged into the Joint Synod of Wisconsin and Other States in 1917 and \"by 1930 … had become a primarily English-speaking synod\"); by 1910 there were six English-language Lutheran churches. In 1926–29 the Wisconsin Synod expelled or lost a group of pastors and congregations in the \"Protes'tant Controversy\" (the apostrophe is theirs), who formed the Protes'tant Conference; the two synods' joint high school split for doctrine in 1951. Gerhard Bading, a Lutheran, was health commissioner (1906–10) and mayor (1912–16 — the man Hoan beat). The odd detail: A schism whose name is spelled with an apostrophe, and a college on State Street that trained only pastors for 83 years.",
