      "summary": "The Klan registered in Ontario on 1 January 1925 when C. Lewis Fowler of New York, John H. Hawkins of Virginia and Richard L. Cowan of Toronto signed an agreement (1 Dec 1924); the robes carried a maple leaf. In June 1926 dynamite exploded in the furnace room of St. Mary's Church, Barrie — the man who set it admitted he acted on Klan orders. On 28 February 1930 about 75 robed men from Hamilton marched through Oakville, burned a cross, took Isabella Jones, a white woman, to her relatives and forced Ira Junius Johnson, a Black man, into a car, then surrounded his house and burned a cross in the yard — to stop their planned marriage. Police Chief David Kerr, who recognised \"prominent Hamiltonian businessmen,\" made no arrest at the scene; Mayor J. B. Moat said the Klan had acted \"quite properly\" (quoted in the Toronto Star, per a search summary). The accused — William A. Phillips (a Hamilton chiropractor), Ernest Taylor (a police interpreter and Presbyterian minister), Harold C. Orme and William Mahony — were charged with being disguised by night; only Phillips was convicted, fined $50; the Ontario Court of Appeal called it \"mob law\" and gave him three months. The couple married on 24 March 1930, had two children, and Johnson worked as a gardener until 1966. The odd detail: One of the accused was a police interpreter who was also a Presbyterian minister; another was a chiropractor; the Mayor and the Chief had known them.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the outline; the mayor's quote is from a search summary.",
