      "summary": "Begun July 1926, the tower was topped out 18 August 1927 and opened to tenants in 1928; the Union Terminal complex was dedicated 28 June 1930. 52 storeys, 708 ft to the roof (771 ft to the spire), set on caissons driven 280 ft down, it was the second-tallest building in the world when completed and the tallest in North America outside New York until 1964. The observation deck on the 42nd floor was reached by riding to 32 and changing lifts. The whole complex cost $179 million. It was the Van Sweringens' headquarters (R07). The odd detail: The tallest building between New York and the Pacific was essentially the lid of a railway station — a skyscraper standing on a hole full of trains.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
