      "summary": "On 27 December 1926 federal agents padlocked 29 saloons in one day. A federal Prohibition investigator, Frank Buckley, wrote in 1929 that Hurley \"has the distinction of being the worst community in the State\" and that \"gambling, prostitution, bootlegging, and dope are about the chief occupations of the place\" (a Wisconsin Magazine of History note adds that local officers \"looked the other way\" in an economy that depended on drinking, gambling and prostitution). In 1931 a raid closed 42 saloons and arrested 60 people — \"one out of every forty Hurley residents.\" Popular accounts say Silver Street had nearly 200 saloons, sometimes disguised as soda shops, and tunnels under the Montreal River; that Al Capone and John Dillinger used it as a second home — legend; Wikipedia documents none of it (it does note that Capone's brother Ralph died in Hurley in 1974). The odd detail: The state line was the business model: step across the Montreal River and you were in Michigan, with a different sheriff.",
