      "summary": "Colt built 15,000 Model 1921 Thompsons for Auto-Ordnance; at $200 (later $225, with 50-round drums at $5 and 20-round magazines at $3 — see R03) civilian sales were poor until police bought in. About 200 were sold in 1926 to the U.S. Postal Inspection Service against mail robbers. From 1928 Federal Laboratories took over distribution — the same Pittsburgh firm whose president testified to the La Follette Committee in 1937 about selling machine guns and tear gas to employers. The Bureau of Investigation bought Thompsons in 1933 after the Kansas City Massacre. The Thompsons from the St. Valentine's Day Massacre are still held by the Berrien County Sheriff's Department in Michigan (see R13 on Fred Burke). The odd detail: one company sold Thompsons to the police, to the Post Office, and to the steel companies fighting unions — and tear gas to all three.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Follow the Thompson's serial number and you reach Federal Laboratories' order book — which lists every police department, company guard force and private buyer. The Hunter Guild is in it.",
