      "summary": "Jack McGurn became a part-owner around 1926. Singer-comic Joe E. Lewis refused to renew his contract and went to a rival club; McGurn's men slashed his throat (1927) — he survived, and the story became the 1957 film The Joker Is Wild. At about 7 a.m. on April 26, 1933 a fire starting in the basement below a Walgreen drugstore at 4800 N. Broadway did an estimated $100,000 damage and made the Green Mill Ballroom a \"total loss\" (Tribune and Daily News). The present Green Mill lounge only occupied its site from 1935 — three years after Capone went to prison — though a curved booth facing both doors is still called \"Al Capone's booth\". A \"popular story\" says patrons could escape through coal tunnels via a trapdoor behind the bar. The odd detail: \"Capone's booth\" is in a room Capone could never have sat in.",
