      "summary": "Albert Kahn built the Fords' house from 1926 (the shell in a year; the family in by 1928; interiors to 1929) with Jens Jensen's long-meadow landscape: the Gallery panelled in sixteenth-century linenfold oak from demolished English manors, a staircase from Lyveden, a dining room from New Place at Upminster, a library from Deene Park; in 1930 Clara Ford gave her granddaughter Josephine a child-sized playhouse with working electricity and plumbing. Across the Pointes, Horace Dodge's widow Anna Thompson Dodge had Horace Trumbauer replace her 1912 Kahn house with a Louis XV château, 1931–34, at $4 million: 75 rooms, over 42,000 square feet, 15 fireplaces, 40 French doors, a 60-by-36-foot music room with the old house's organ, a bureau made for Catherine the Great and four of Marie Antoinette's chairs. The odd detail: In the worst years of the Depression Detroit's two auto widows-and-heirs were importing whole English rooms and a French queen's chairs — one house Tudor, one Bourbon, a mile apart.",
