      "summary": "Two French officers in the 1740s or 1750s quarrelled over a woman; one killed the other, cut off the head, threw it into Lake Ontario and dropped the body down the well in the French Castle's first-floor room; the headless officer is said to walk under a full moon. The British filled the well in and covered it, fearing the French had poisoned it; caretakers reconstructed it in the 1920s. The fort was itself restored between 1926 and 1934 — the same years as the Depression's worst. The odd detail: The well was sealed by one army as poisoned and re-opened by antiquarians in the 1920s, and the ghost story is pinned to the well.",
