      "summary": "Born 13 May 1914 near LaFayette, Alabama (11 pounds; a speech impediment; his father committed to an asylum in 1916), Joe Louis Barrow came to Detroit in 1926 after Klan intimidation, lived on Catherine Street, learned cabinet-making at Bronson Vocational and boxing at the Brewster centre. He won the Detroit Golden Gloves novice light-heavyweight title in 1933, the Chicago Golden Gloves open division and the national AAU in 1934 (amateur record 50–4, 43 by KO). Detroit bookmaker John Roxborough, who had seen him box in 1931, took him on, brought in Chicago numbers man Julian Black (unverified — V_R14) and trainer Jack Blackburn (Chicago), and in 1935 had Louis sign away half his future income to them. His first pro fight was in Chicago on 4 July 1934 — a first-round knockout of Jack Kracken for $59 — and he won all twelve fights that year. Roxborough and Black wrote his rules: no photographs with white women, no gloating, no fixed fights, clean living — the anti-Jack-Johnson. He married Marva Trotter, a secretary he met at the gym, in 1935, and beat Carnera (25 June 1935) and Baer (24 September 1935) before Schmeling knocked him out in twelve at Yankee Stadium on 19 June 1936. The odd detail: The champion's managers were both policy bankers — Detroit's and Chicago's numbers money built the Brown Bomber (unverified — V_R14) — and his trainer had done four years and eight months for manslaughter.",
