      "summary": "On 28 Nov 1936 the Indianapolis Times reported that Charles Mazey, the Secret Service agent in charge in Indianapolis since the 1926 retirement of Capt. Thomas E. Halls (above), was leaving to take charge of the St. Paul district. His successor was Thomas A. Gallagher, five years with the bureau. Mazey was \"credited with directing investigations which resulted in the breaking up of two big alleged counterfeiting gangs\": the Pete Davitto gang at Terre Haute and the Dante Colza ring in Michigan City. In June 1936 he had warned veterans to watch their mail for bonus cheques (see C). By 1938–39 George B. Loy is named as the Indianapolis head (above, and C). The odd detail: the old guard's line of succession in one small office runs Halls (Jesse James days) → Mazey → Gallagher → Loy, and Mazey's reward was St. Paul, the gangster haven.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (OCR read). The Davitto and Colza cases themselves were not researched.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a posting ladder for Secret Service player characters. Indianapolis is the quiet office where you learn the trade; St. Paul, where the Twin Cities' police let the bank robbers rest, is where the Service sends a man it trusts.",
