      "summary": "Houdini sent undercover investigators ahead of him — up to ten days before each show (unverified — V_R05) — to sit with local mediums; he then exposed them from the stage, Chicago among the cities. On 22 Oct 1926 in Montreal a student, J. Gordon Whitehead, struck him repeatedly in the abdomen; he played Detroit's Garrick Theater on 24 Oct with a 104°F fever and died of peritonitis at Grace Hospital, Detroit, on 31 Oct 1926 (Halloween). He and his wife Bess had agreed a code — \"Rosabelle, believe.\" Medium Arthur Ford claimed to deliver it in 1929 (Bess later said it was fabricated). Bess held a séance every Halloween for ten years and ended them in 1936; in 1943 she said \"ten years is long enough to wait for any man.\" The odd detail: The great debunker died in Detroit on Halloween — and the last official attempt to reach him fell on Halloween 1936, two months before the alpha's solstice.",
