      "summary": "Born Joseph Louis Barrow in Alabama on 13 May 1914, his family moved to Detroit's Black Bottom in 1926 after Klan intimidation; he found boxing at the Brewster Street Recreation Center. He won the 1934 Chicago Golden Gloves and turned pro on 4 July 1934 in Chicago, managed by John Roxborough (a Black Detroit bookmaker) and Chicago promoter Julian Black, trained by Jack \"Chappie\" Blackburn. Germany's Max Schmeling knocked him out in the 12th round at Yankee Stadium on 19 June 1936. Louis took the heavyweight title from James J. Braddock in Chicago on 22 June 1937 (8th-round KO), and destroyed Schmeling in the rematch on 22 June 1938 in two minutes and four seconds. The odd detail: His manager was a numbers man — the same trade as Stephanie St. Clair in Harlem, already in the setting.",
