      "summary": "August Derleth (born 24 February 1909) sold his first story, the vampire tale \"Bat's Belfry,\" to Weird Tales in 1926 at age 16 and became one of the magazine's most frequent contributors under his own name and as \"Stephen Grendon.\" He wrote to H. P. Lovecraft in 1926 and the two exchanged about 1,000 letters over 11 years and never met. When Lovecraft died on 15 March 1937, Derleth phoned Robert Bloch (Milwaukee — R27's Fictioneers) with the news. In 1939, with Donald Wandrei, Derleth founded Arkham House in Sauk City to publish Lovecraft in hardcover; its first book was The Outsider and Others. He held a Guggenheim (1938) for the \"Sac Prairie Saga,\" his regional fiction, poems and nature writing. (Weird Tales was a Chicago-published pulp in this period — from memory; not confirmed on a page read.) The odd detail: The world's leading Lovecraft press began in an attic in a Wisconsin farm town, run by a man who had never met the author.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Lovecraft circle is the world's richest source of invented horror — and in the setting some of it is not invented. What does a Wisconsin writer in 1936 know from letters that Section 13 needs?",
