      "summary": "After the Shakespeare Garden (1916), journalist Leo Weidenthal proposed a garden for each nationality; with Charles J. Wolfram and Jennie K. Zwick he founded the Civic Progress League (1925), which became the Cultural Garden League (1926). Dedications: Hebrew (5 May 1926 — the poet Hayim Nahman Bialik planted three cedars of Lebanon); German (2 June 1929 — a replica of Ernst Rietschel's Goethe–Schiller monument, first set up in Wade Park in 1907); Italian (12 Oct 1930); Slovak (23 Oct 1932); Hungarian (21 Oct 1934); Polish (28 Oct 1934); Czech (6 Oct 1935); Lithuanian (11 Oct 1936); Rusin (25 June 1939); Irish (29 Oct 1939); Greek (2 June 1940). City manager William R. Hopkins backed it; the WPA built much of it. The odd detail: Each nation got its garden in a long chain down one valley — Czech, Slovak and Hungarian gardens a few hundred yards apart, dedicated in the 1930s by communities whose old countries (in the real record) had just torn up the Habsburg map.",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A valley of national shrines, each with its own statues and soil — a map of Europe laid out in Cleveland, which a summoner could read as a ritual diagram. The IHS seminary sits on the hill above it.",
