      "summary": "Houdini's full-evening \"3 Shows in One\" (magic, escapes, spiritualist exposure) opened at the Princess on Monday 8 March 1926 and ran eight weeks to 1 May — the longest single-theatre engagement the show ever had, and his last appearance in Chicago. The Tribune ads promised \"600 seats at $1 and $1.50 every night\"; by week six there were 300 seats at $1 and 50-cent matinees; in week seven the bill added \"reformed trumpet medium Annie Benninghofen\" as an attraction. Six months later he was dead in Detroit (R05). The odd detail: A reformed trumpet medium on the bill in the seventh week — a woman who had made the dead speak through a tin horn, now demonstrating how, nightly, on Clark Street.",
