      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Pony Inn saloon",
          "address": "5613 W. 12th Street",
          "geography": "5613 W. 12th Street (now Roosevelt Road), Cicero",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Pony%20Inn%20saloon%2C%205613%20W.%2012th%20Street%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "William H. McSwiggin, a Cicero-raised assistant state's attorney known as the \"Hanging Prosecutor\", was out drinking with boyhood friends — the West Side O'Donnell gang's James J. Doherty and Thomas \"Red\" Duffy — when a machine gun opened up from a car outside the Pony Inn, killing all three. (Wilson's Treasury report dates it to the night of April 26.) McSwiggin had earlier prosecuted Myles O'Donnell and Doherty in the Tancl-Klimas murder case, in which they were acquitted, amid rumours that he had thrown it for his friends. Capone was accused and vanished for months; per Wilson, Treasury agents eventually found him and handed him to the Chicago police, who announced they had no evidence to hold him. The police raids that night seized the Hawthorne Smoke Shop ledger — which would convict Capone five years later. The odd detail: The prosecutor was drinking with the gangsters he had prosecuted — and the raid after his murder produced the book that finally jailed Capone.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High; date discrepancy (April 26 vs 27) noted.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A prosecutor who threw a murder case for his friends, then died beside them. Three layers: the period saw a gang hit; the rumour saw a crooked prosecutor; underneath, the Tancl-Klimas case he threw had a witness that wasn't human.",
