      "summary": "The Los Angeles evangelist Aimee Semple McPherson of Angelus Temple vanished from the beach on 18 May 1926 after going for a swim; two people died during the search for her supposedly drowned body. She reappeared on 23 June 1926 walking out of the desert at Agua Prieta, Sonora, collapsing before a Mexican couple, R. R. Gonzales and his wife; she was taken to a hospital in Douglas, Arizona, where staff picked cactus spines from her legs. She said she had been kidnapped by two men and a woman (\"Steve\" and \"Rose\") who lured her to their car with a plea to pray for a dying baby, drugged her, and held her for ransom in a shack in the Mexican desert; she escaped by rubbing her bonds against the jagged lid of a tin can — which she demonstrated to reporters several times. The alternative, printed widely: she had spent the time with her married radio engineer Kenneth G. Ormiston in a cottage in Carmel, which he had rented as \"George E. McIntyre\" with an unidentified \"Miss X\" (identified as Elizabeth Tovey, a Seattle nurse). District Attorney Asa Keyes dropped the case on 10 January 1927 when a chief witness (Lorraine Wiseman-Sielaff) proved unreliable. The odd detail: the hospital in Douglas found only cactus spines in her legs and one blistered toe after a supposed desert ordeal — the mismatch skeptics seized on (detail from the Wikipedia article's hospital account).",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the dates, places and outcome (Wikipedia). The distance she claimed to have walked: about 20 miles. The nurse's \"cactus spines … blistered toe\" detail is in the Wikipedia article (confirmed).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — five weeks, a desert, a shack, a can lid; a famous woman who vanished from a beach and came back barefoot from an occupied territory the Army says it has pacified. The IHS would want her file (a Protestant celebrity healer); the Golden Circle would want her flock.",
