      "summary": "President Plutarco Elías Calles's \"Law for Reforming the Penal Code\" (signed 14 June, in force 31 July 1926) enforced Article 130: a 500-peso fine for wearing clerical dress in public, up to five years' prison for a priest criticising the government, foreign priests expelled, convents, monasteries and religious schools closed. The bishops answered by suspending public worship from the day it took effect and backing an economic boycott (14 July). States went further: Tabasco required every priest to be married; Chihuahua allowed one priest for the whole state. Mexico had about 4,500 priests before; by 1934 there were 334 licensed priests for 15 million people, and by 1935 17 states had none. The law stood until 26 December 1938. The odd detail: for three years (1926–29) no Mass was said publicly anywhere in Mexico — the churches stayed open, in lay hands, with no priest at the altar.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
