      "summary": "The first Eucharistic Congress held in the United States; theme \"The Peace of Christ in the Reign of Christ.\" Papal legate Cardinal Giovanni Bonzano led a party of cardinals and bishops; the opening procession counted 10 cardinals, 60 archbishops and ~300 bishops, with 3,000 priests assigned just to hear confessions. On Children's Day (21 June) a choir of 62,000 children sang the Mass of the Angels at Soldier Field before 400,000+; the Soldier Field altar stood 30 ft high on a 214 × 224 ft platform. For the closing procession at the seminary, 820 trains (5,200 cars) ran Chicago–Mundelein, one every 40 seconds for eight hours; ~18,000 automobiles; estimates of 800,000 to a million people. Sisters baked 4.5 million hosts. A loudspeaker rig at Mundelein was called the largest public-address system ever built; Fox filmed a 96-minute documentary. The odd detail: 12,000 people were already waiting at the seminary gates when they opened at 4 a.m.; the diocesan paper later claimed 8.3 million total attendance (double-counted).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the figures as reported; crowd figures are period estimates.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A million people in one field for one consecrated host is the largest \"ward\" ever raised on American soil — and the reason Chicago's monsters went quiet for years afterwards. Something that crossed the ground in 1926 would remember.",
