      "summary": "The Church of St. Joseph was built in 1925 and dedicated on 25 June 1926 by Cardinal William O'Connell of Boston; Pius XI canonised the North American (Canadian) Martyrs — Jesuits killed in Huronia in the 1640s — in 1930. The shrine keeps relics of Jean de Brébeuf (including his skull), Gabriel Lalemant and Charles Garnier. Its interior is shaped like an overturned canoe (Ildège Bourrie). It has no insulation: it closes in winter and the reliquaries are removed. The odd detail: The skull of a Jesuit martyred by fire and torture sits in a canoe-shaped church that must be emptied of its relics every winter.",
