      "places": [
        {
          "name": "NW corner of Lawrence and Winthrop Avenues",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Uptown (a block from the Green Mill, about a block east of the Uptown Theatre)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=NW%20corner%20of%20Lawrence%20and%20Winthrop%20Avenues%2C%20Uptown%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Andrew and William Karzas, who made their first money in nickelodeons, opened the Aragon on July 15, 1926 (the first event the night before was an Elks beauty contest); Huszagh & Hill built the Spanish-Baroque shell and movie-palace designer John Eberson dressed the inside as a Moorish courtyard under a night sky. The 21,036-square-foot spring-loaded floor (cork and felt padding) held about 5,000 dancers. The house rules were strict: dress code, no alcohol, \"dance watchdogs\" (hostesses from the Juvenile Protective Association), the Charleston and space-hogging novelty dances banned, a waltz every third number, and tuxedoed floor men who broke up \"wild dancing and/or displays of affection.\" Wayne King, \"the Waltz King,\" took over the bandstand in spring 1927 and WGN began live broadcasts from the Aragon that year, six nights a week for decades. The acoustics were so bad at first (\"when we'd hit a chord, you'd hear it three times coming back at you\" — Ted Fio Rito) that management reportedly spent $70,000 on the ceiling before soprano Claudia Muzio sang there in January 1928. The odd detail: Trumpeter Wingy Manone's band was made to play the compulsory waltzes in paper hats; when a visiting jazzman heckled (\"take off the false whiskers, Wingy, I know you!\"), the band broke off a waltz for \"Jazzin' Babies Blues\" — and the next night Wayne King was back on the stand.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — 5,000 people, no drink, chaperones on the floor and a radio wire to the whole Midwest — a place to pass a message in a waltz, and a \"third echo\" that the sound men never quite fixed.",
