      "id": "R43-004",
      "title": "The murder of Don Mellett, Canton's crusading editor",
      "date_start": "1926-07-16",
      "date_end": "1926-07-16",
      "date_label": "16 July 1926 (living memory)",
      "category": "Ohio beyond Cleveland and Toledo, 1920–1940: the cities and their communities",
      "tags": [
        "beyond",
        "Cleveland",
        "Don Mellett",
        "Canton's",
        "living memory"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Don Mellett",
        "S. A. Lengel",
        "Patrick McDermott",
        "Ben Rudner",
        "Louis Mazer",
        "Ora Slater"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Canton",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Stark County, Ohio — Mellett's own garage",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Canton%2C%20Stark%20County%2C%20Ohio%20%E2%80%94%20Mellett%27s%20own%20garage"
        },
        {
          "name": "the Canton Daily News",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20Canton%20Daily%20News%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Donald Ring Mellett (born Elwood, Indiana, 26 September 1891) became editor of the Canton Daily News in January 1925 and began an anti-vice, anti-corruption campaign aimed at Canton's police chief, S. A. Lengel; he pressured the mayor to suspend the chief. On 16 July 1926, aged 34, he was shot dead in his garage. A bootleg-and-police alliance had hired an ex-convict from Pennsylvania, Patrick Eugene McDermott, who with Steve Kascholk was introduced through the bootlegger Ben Rudner to another bootlegger, Louis Mazer; McDermott was the triggerman. An outside detective, Ora Slater, is credited with solving it. A former Canton police detective was convicted as the plot's originator and implicated Chief Lengel, who — nearly a year and a half later — was acquitted; Wikipedia says four people, including Rudner, got life terms. The Daily News and Mellett, posthumously, took the 1927 Pulitzer Prize for public service. The odd detail: The gunman was imported, an ex-con with no local ties — and the case was broken not by Canton's police but by a detective from out of town. Wikipedia's page (as summarised) says McDermott later escaped and appeared on an FBI Ten Most Wanted list — UNVERIFIED and hard to square with the list's 1950 start.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the murder, motive and the Pulitzer; Medium on the sentences and roles (sources word them inconsistently). • Wikipedia's page (as summarised) says McDermott later escaped and appeared on an FBI Ten Most Wanted list — UNVERIFIED and hard to square with the list's 1950 start.",
