      "summary": "Mexico's own Knights had grown from 400 in 1918 to almost 6,000 in 51 councils by 1924, and over half the founding members of the Liga (March 1925) were Knights. At Philadelphia in August 1926 Supreme Knight James A. Flaherty called Mexico \"one of the most violent and unjust religious persecutions of modern times\"; the Order voted a $1 million \"Mexican Fund\" to educate the public and aid the persecuted, and on 1 September 1926 Flaherty and the Supreme Officers met President Coolidge. Columbia denounced the persecution in its October–December 1926 issues and was banned by the Mexican government; the Mexican legislature debated its November issue on 25 November 1926. The Knights raised more than $1 million for exiles, circulated five million leaflets, and pressed for intervention; about 70 Mexican Knights died among the Cristeros, and two-thirds of the Mexican councils were shut. Exiled Mexican Knights founded Tepeyac Council 2635 in Los Angeles after the 1926 convention (active to 1940). (The arreglos and the K of C totals are also summarised in M01.) The odd detail: a Mexican K of C council in exile named for Tepeyac — the hill of the Guadalupe apparition — meeting in Los Angeles for fourteen years.",
