      "summary": "Robert Redfield, who joined the University of Chicago faculty in 1927, did fieldwork in Tepoztlán from November 1926 to July 1927 and published Tepoztlán, a Mexican Village: A Study of Folk Life (1930) — the founding text of his \"folk–urban continuum\". He records the ruined temple above the town, \"known as the 'house of Tepozteco'\", and \"a dance-drama [that] commemorates the valorous defense of Tepoztlan by Tepoztecatl\", the pre-Hispanic deity-hero (linked to the pulque god Ome Tochtli); his Chapter IX is \"Magic and Medicine\"; his photographs include a household santo and the leaping chinelos of carnival. The odd detail: the University of Chicago's founding study of \"folk\" Mexico was written during the Cristero War, in a town whose old god still had his house on the cliff.",
