      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Barnes-Hecker Mine",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "about 4 miles west of Ishpeming, Marquette Range, Michigan (Cleveland-Cliffs)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Barnes-Hecker%20Mine%2C%20about%204%20miles%20west%20of%20Ishpeming%2C%20Marquette%20Range%2C%20Michigan"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "On 3 November 1926, after routine blasting, a stope cave-in broke through to the surface, opening a pit 300 feet long and 200 feet wide that drained North Lake into the mine; the workings, under a 200-foot water table layer of \"quicksand\" on three levels at 600, 800 and 1,000 feet, filled in about 15 minutes. Fifty-one men died — the worst mining accident in Michigan's history; one man, Rutherford \"Wilfred\" Wills, led colleagues up an emergency ladder to the surface in 14 minutes, and three other miners had left just before. County mine inspector William E. Hill, recently re-elected, died on his inspection visit. Only ten bodies were recovered; Cleveland-Cliffs abandoned and sealed the mine on 20 November 1926 with 41 bodies entombed and paid double workers' compensation. It left 42 widows and 132 minor children. A memorial stone was set near Negaunee in 1971. Ishpeming's population was 9,238 in 1930 (down 12% from 10,500 in 1920), and the town claims to be the birthplace of organised skiing in America (the National Ski Association was founded there 21 February 1905). The odd detail: The tomb sits under a 300-by-200-foot pit that a lake poured through, and the county mine inspector, just re-elected, was among the dead.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the figures (Wikipedia + multiple summaries; the sole-survivor count differs between sources).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A sealed mine with 41 men still inside and a lake above it — the perfect \"what did the miners hear before the collapse\" mission; Cornish \"Tommyknockers\" (R07) already have a home in the range.",
