      "summary": "Born Lucinda Madden in Woodstock, Georgia on 14 Jan 1874, she came to Chicago in 1910, found Pentecostalism at the Stone Church by 1912, and in 1920 founded the Langley Avenue All Nations Pentecostal Church — the first Chicago church founded by a woman pastor. Its building was completed in Dec 1926. At peak it had 3,000 members; her weekly faith-healing sessions were credited (by admirers) with healing more than 200,000 people; in the Depression the church fed thousands; it was among the first Black churches to broadcast services by radio. At her death (18 June 1952) 60,000+ attended — then the largest funeral in Chicago's history — and her casket went to Lincoln Cemetery by horse and wagon. The odd detail: A healer credited with 200,000 cures — more than the population of most American cities.",
