      "summary": "Julia G. Young's Mexican Exodus (Oxford, 2015) opens with Simón Tenorio, 36, of Del Rio, drawn in by his neighbour, the exiled priest Fr. Pascual Robles (who ran a paper, La Razón). In midsummer 1927 the two drove to San Antonio and met, in the Robert E. Lee Hotel, the Liga's chief Luis Bustos, its purchasing agent Juan Lainé, and Bishop Manríquez y Zárate. Tenorio's orders: recruit a band, cross into Coahuila, meet a larger expedition in the foothills of the Sierra del Burro, then join armed bands from Laredo and El Paso for a Catholic rising. Seven men crossed on 6 August 1927 carrying a manifesto \"to overthrow the Bolshevik tyranny in its most recent incarnation, Callismo\"; Genaro Valadez's suitcase held scapulars, rosaries, mass books, portraits of Guadalupe, and letters — one from Robles, one from a well-wisher in Detroit warning they would \"sacrifice your lives … without glory.\" The Liga's promised war-horses were worn-out nags; the other bands never appeared. Tenorio surrendered on 7 August and informed; two men were shot dead in the desert; he was questioned by Mexican officials and then by men from the US Department of Justice, taken to Torreón — and never seen again. A US inquiry in December 1927 found no trace; he was presumed dead. The garrison chief was commended for avoiding \"alarm or scandal.\" The odd detail: the rising was planned in a downtown hotel's rooms by a bishop, a purchasing agent and a newspaper priest — and died of bad horses; its informer vanished into a Mexican army lock-up.",
