      "summary": "A former German parish on the Near West Side, St. Francis was taken over by the Claretians as administrators in 1927; in their first December there they held a novena to Our Lady of Guadalupe, and through the 1920s its school became ever more Mexican. With Our Lady of Guadalupe in South Chicago, it was one of the archdiocese's two Mexican parishes of the 1920s — though Mundelein opposed new national parishes in principle. Chicago had about 3,000 Mexicans c. 1920, about 20,000 by the late 1920s, falling to about 14,000 by the end of the 1930s after the repatriations (M06). The mutualistas of the colonias preached Mexican nationalism, sometimes in tension with the Spanish (Catalan) Claretian priests (inference from the sources' framing, not a documented quarrel). The odd detail: Chicago's Mexican Catholics were pastored by Spanish Claretians in German churches — the Church's own \"three layers.\"",
