      "summary": "Julia Buccola Petta (1892–17 March 1921) died in childbirth with a stillborn son, Filippo, and was buried in her wedding dress. About six years later her mother Filomena won permission (method unknown) to have her exhumed, paid for by Julia's brother Henry. The body was found \"mostly intact\" — though the baby and the arm holding him had decayed. The monument carries her wedding photograph, a statue from it, and a photograph taken at the exhumation. Legend says the mother dreamed Julia told her she was still alive — Wikipedia notes no contemporary source backs that legend. The odd detail: A photograph of the exhumed corpse is displayed on the grave, and the husband's surname and the child's name are absent from the stone.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High (exhumation year approximate; the dreams are legend).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Three layers exactly: the period's reading (an Italian family's miracle), the folklore (a mother's dreams), what is there (a vampire's bride kept \"mostly intact\" in the same cemetery as Capone). The Chicago Aurora & Elgin ran funeral trains to Mount Carmel until 1934.",
