      "summary": "Indiana Avenue was the commercial and cultural heart of Black Indianapolis: the Indianapolis Leader (the first Black-owned paper, from the 1870s), the Indianapolis Recorder, and clubs that from the 1920s to the 1960s fed jazz players including Freddie Hubbard, Wes Montgomery and J. J. Johnson. The Madam Walker Theatre, which \"Madam Walker's daughter helped build,\" opened in 1927 and \"quickly became known as the 'Crown Jewel of the Avenue.'\" Crispus Attucks High School stood a few blocks away. The district declined when desegregation drew the Black middle class out in the late 1950s; the Walker Building faced closure by 1965 and IUPUI's campus later cleared much of the rest. The odd detail: The theatre was named for a woman who made a fortune selling hair care to Black women and opened in the same year the Klan's mayor was on trial for corruption in the city's courts.",
