      "summary": "George F. Schreiber designed a Neo-Gothic Masonic cathedral built 1927–1929 for $2.5 million. Its 212-foot \"Singing Tower\" holds a 54-bell carillon weighing 56,372 pounds. The building's dimensions in feet are all divisible by three (the three degrees of Freemasonry) and many by 33 (the Scottish Rite's degrees). The entrance sculpture shows King Solomon, the King of Tyre and three stonemasons from Solomon's Temple. There are over 100 stained-glass windows of the degrees and the liberal arts, a sprung ballroom 99 feet square (a 66-foot dance floor) and a theatre of about 1,100 seats. The odd detail: It was finished in 1929, the year of the crash, and was designed so that no measurement in feet escaped the number three.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the building facts (one page).",
