      "summary": "The Lonardo brothers, Sicilians from Licata, controlled the corn sugar that bootleggers needed; the Porrello brothers wanted it. On 13 October 1927 \"Big Joe\" Lonardo and his brother John went to a Porrello barbershop to negotiate and were shot dead. On 5 December 1928 Cleveland police broke up one of the earliest-known Mafia summits in America at the Hotel Statler — Joseph Porrello with New York's Joe Profaci and Vincent Mangano among the guests; several were arrested and bailed quickly. Then the Porrellos were hunted down: Joe Porrello shot at the Venetian Restaurant on 5 July 1930; Vincenzo \"Jim\" Porrello three weeks later in a grocery; Raymond Porrello's house bombed on 15 August 1930 (he lived). Frank Milano, based on the Mayfield Road Mob of Little Italy, took over in 1930 and allied with the Jewish Cleveland Syndicate. The odd detail: The war was over sugar — the corn sugar that turned home stills into a business.",
