      "summary": "The Institute began in 1903 as the Chicago Hebrew Institute, founded by \"the city's established Jewish community to support new immigrants.\" Its 1927 building on Douglas Boulevard (Grunsfeld & Klaber, architects; National Register 15 Nov 1978) held evening classes, a Hebrew school and eventually a junior college, a theatre, recreational areas, and a Naturalization Bureau — it was \"the first point of contact in Chicago for new immigrants.\" On 10 April 1931 the Forward announced that the Jewish Institute Theater, directed by Charles Freeman, would stage That Man's Soul \"tomorrow and Sunday\" — \"a famous Chicago gangster drama\" giving \"a glimpse of the life of the underworld,\" played by \"many talented young players.\" The building is now the Lawndale Community Academy. (R15 has the million-dollar cost and the Forward's \"two tribes\" essay.) The odd detail: A year after the Lingle and Zuta killings, Lawndale's community-centre amateurs were acting out the underworld on their own stage.",
