      "summary": "A Century of Progress was incorporated in January 1928 (Rufus C. Dawes president from 1927; Lenox R. Lohr general manager; Daniel Burnham Jr. secretary (unverified — V2_R04)). It floated a $10 million gold-note issue on 28 Oct 1929 — the day before Black Tuesday — and Chicagoans raised $5 million more (unverified — V2_R04); Hoover proclaimed the fair and handed the proclamation to Dawes at the White House. (unverified — V2_R04) Two seasons (27 May–12 Nov 1933; 26 May–31 Oct 1934) drew 48,469,227 total admissions (39,052,236 paid); half the notes were retired by the end of 1933 and all by the end of 1934 — \"the first time an international fair had paid for itself.\" Buildings were five-ply Douglas fir plywood and ribbed-metal siding, painted as the \"Rainbow City\" against 1893's White City. Rufus Dawes was the brother of Charles G. Dawes — Vice President, Nobel laureate, Dawes Plan author, Central Republic Bank chairman — and was himself on the 1924 Dawes Plan commission on German reparations; from 1934 he also ran the Museum of Science and Industry, until his death in 1940. The odd detail: The fair's president spent 1924 deciding how much Germany should pay the Allies, then spent 1933 hosting a fair Germany could not afford to attend (unverified — V2_R04).",
