      "summary": "James W. Stevens and his son Ernest (of the Illinois Life Insurance Company and the Hotel La Salle) opened it May 2, 1927 — 3,000 rooms, about $30 million, \"more than ten times the cost of Yankee Stadium,\" a city within a city: bowling alley, barber, movie theatre, drugstore, ice-cream shop, and the \"High-Ho Club\" miniature golf course on the roof. The Depression ruined the family: the hotel went bankrupt, the state charged the Stevenses with looting the insurance company, they were convicted of embezzlement and the conviction was reversed unanimously on appeal; Ernest's son, a boy who had watched the called shot from the Wrigley stands, became Justice John Paul Stevens. In 1942 the Army bought the hotel for $6 million and put 10,000 cadets in it. The odd detail: A rooftop golf course over 3,000 empty rooms.",
