      "summary": "Fresh from Cornell in 1927, Bourke-White opened a commercial studio in Cleveland and photographed architecture and industry. Otis Steel's guards first refused her — steel-making was a defence industry and they doubted a woman could stand the heat of the floor; once inside she found the film of the day blind to the red-orange glow of molten steel, and solved it by lighting the pours with magnesium flares, producing \"some of the best steel-factory photographs of that era.\" Fortune took her in 1929; her photograph of the Fort Peck Dam spillway ran on the cover of the first issue of Life (23 November 1936), and her February 1937 picture of Louisville's Black flood refugees queued under a billboard reading \"World's Highest Standard of Living\" became a Depression icon. The odd detail: The magnesium flares: a woman photographer setting off white fire in a Cleveland mill at night because her film could not see the colour of steel.",
