      "summary": "Wright came from Memphis in 1927 and clerked at the post office; on relief by 1931, he found the John Reed Club in 1932 and joined it and the Communist Party in late 1933; he worked for the Federal Writers' Project and founded the South Side Writers Group (Wikipedia says 1933; most accounts give 1936) with Arna Bontemps and Margaret Walker among the members. In 1937 he left for New York as Harlem editor of the Daily Worker. Native Son (1940) put Bigger Thomas in a Black Belt kitchenette and drew on the 1938 Chicago \"brick moron\" murder case of Robert Nixon (from memory — UNVERIFIED this session). (From memory, also UNVERIFIED: his job cleaning animal cages in the Michael Reese Hospital research lab, which became the essay \"The Man Who Went to Chicago.\") The odd detail: The great novel of Black Chicago was written by a post-office clerk who left the city three years before he published it.",
