      "summary": "Davis came to Chicago in 1927 and wrote for the Evening Bulletin, the Whip and the Gary American; his first long poem was \"Chicago's Congo, Sonata for an Orchestra.\" A socialite, Frances Norton Manning, introduced him to Norman Forgue of the Black Cat Press, which published Black Man's Verse in 1935; that year he became managing editor of the Associated Negro Press (executive editor until 1947) — the wire that fed every Black paper in the country. He covered Joe Louis, started a photography club, took a Rosenwald fellowship in 1937, was in the Federal Writers' Project and, from 1936, the South Side Writers Group with Wright and Margaret Walker, and the League of American Writers. The odd detail: The Black press's national wire service was a few rooms in Chicago run by an editor who was also the city's leading modernist poet — every lynching report and every Joe Louis dispatch crossed his desk.",
