      "summary": "Born in Zanesville, Ohio, August 3, 1858, sole heir after her brother Clarence (d. 1913) and sister Lucy Maud (d. 1920) to the family's grain-elevator fortune, Kate Sturges Buckingham commissioned the $750,000 Versailles-inspired fountain in 1924 in Clarence's memory and gave the Art Institute Lucy's 400 Chinese ritual bronzes, Clarence's thousands of Japanese prints (1925) and her own medieval sculpture and tapestries (1924) — every gift in a dead sibling's name, never her own. She funded the Alexander Hamilton monument in Lincoln Park, never married, and \"requested that her name be removed from the Social Register.\" She died December 14, 1937, aged 79. The odd detail: The woman who gave the city its most public monument struck herself off the list of society — and gave everything under the names of the dead.",
