      "summary": "John C. Montana built a taxi empire (Van Dyke Taxicab Co. and subsidiaries), owned the Empire State Brewery, and after repeal handled alcohol distribution. A Republican, he sat on Buffalo's Common Council 1927–1931 for the Niagara District, chairing the Housing and Slum Clearance Committee and the Labor Relations Committee; he ran for Congress unsuccessfully in 1938. He was underboss (consigliere) to Magaddino and attended a Chicago Mafia summit in 1931. He was president of the Federation of Italian-American Societies; a police officers' club named him Buffalo's \"Man of the Year\" in 1956; at Apalachin in 1957 he claimed car trouble, then testified alone before the Senate. He died 18 March 1964. The odd detail: The man who chaired the city's slum-clearance committee in 1927–31 was the underboss of a Mafia family — and the Central Terminal (and the Canal Street clearances) went up in the same years.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the outline (one Wikipedia page).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"No one is clean,\" concrete version: the alderman who decides which neighbourhoods are cleared, the taxi fleet that knows every fare, the brewery that reopens the week beer is legal. A vampire in high society could hardly ask for a better front.",
