      "id": "M06-038",
      "title": "Tayopa, the lost Jesuit silver mine",
      "date_start": "1927",
      "date_end": "1939",
      "date_label": "folklore, attested 1927 and 1939",
      "category": "The border and the crime of the Mexican north, 1910–1940",
      "tags": [
        "border",
        "crime",
        "Tayopa",
        "Jesuit",
        "folklore"
      ],
      "people": [
        "C. B. Ruggles",
        "J. Frank Dobie",
        "\"Custard\""
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "somewhere in the Sierra Madre of Sonora or Chihuahua",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(accounts differ)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=somewhere%20in%20the%20Sierra%20Madre%20of%20Sonora%20or%20Chihuahua%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Tayopa is a legendary lost silver mine. The story goes that the Jesuits of the seventeenth century worked rich mines in secret because the Spanish crown had criminalised Jesuit mine ownership, and that a town, Guadalupe de Tayopa, was built nearby with a fully equipped Jesuit church. One documented modern episode: in 1927, \"a latterday frontiersman\" and Tayopa hunter, C. B. Ruggles, and the writer J. Frank Dobie, were approached at their camp at La Quiparita, a valley west of Chihuahua, by a man who gave his name as Custard, who carried a highly stylised map placing Tayopa in the Sierra Madre (unverified — V_M06: the Ruggles/Dobie/Custard 1927 episode is on neither the Wikipedia nor the treasure-hunting-blog page read). Dobie later told the story in Apache Gold and Yaqui Silver (1939) (Dobie's 1939 book on Tayopa confirmed on Wikipedia). The odd detail: a treasure hunter's camp visited by a stranger with a map — the story is the legend, as told by the folklorist who took it down.",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Folklore. First print attestation in the summary above is Dobie (1939), with a 1927 camp story; the seventeenth-century Jesuit origin is the legend's own claim (Low). • … were approached at their camp at La Quiparita, a valley west of Chihuahua, by a man who gave his name as Custard, who carried a highly stylised map placing Tayopa in the Sierra Madre (unverified — V_M06: the Ruggles/Dobie/Custard 1927 episode is on neither the Wikipedia nor the treasure-hunting-blog page read).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a Jesuit silver mine that the crown criminalised — the IHS is a Catholic order that works in secret. In the setting the Church's old order and the treasure are one file. What the mine paid for is the question.",
