      "summary": "M07 covers the Claretians' takeover of an old German parish (M07 says 1927; the Chicago Catholic says they began ministering there in 1926, and the Chicago History Museum dates a separate Spanish-language service to 1925) and its Guadalupe novena; this entry adds what Chicago's own surveys record. Jones's Church Federation survey (1928–31) says \"nearly a thousand worshippers often kneel\" at the special Mass for the Mexicans at St. Francis, with a Guadalupe shrine at the side of the church (a crowd at one Mass, not the parish's size); Redfield (1924) heard that Mexicans were \"indifferent\" churchgoers — mostly women — promising to come to celebrations and not coming, accepting help without committing. The parish's athletic team (the St. Francis Wildcats) formed in the 1930s. A Sept 1930 Hull House \"musical-literary program\" benefited a church rendered \"San Marcos\" (probably St. Mark's — UNVERIFIED). The odd detail: a ~1,000-person parish serving a colony of 7–8,000 — on paper only a seventh of the Mexicans on Halsted went to the parish that served them. (unverified — V_M18: Jones's \"nearly a thousand\" is the crowd at a special Mass, not the parish's roll, and the Chicago History Museum says St. Francis drew up to 5,000 a Sunday at its peak, so the \"one-seventh\" inference is not supported.)",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (the 1,000 is one survey's figure). • exact street address UNVERIFIED — M07 gives 813 W. Roosevelt • probably St. Mark's — UNVERIFIED • a ~1,000-person parish serving a colony of 7–8,000 — on paper only a seventh of the Mexicans on Halsted went to the parish that served them. (unverified — V_M18: Jones's \"nearly a thousand\" is the crowd at a special Mass, not the parish's roll, and the Chicago History Museum says St. Francis drew up to 5,000 a Sunday at its peak, so the \"one-seventh\" inference is not supported.)",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the IHS desk's inventory of \"Catholic in name\" Mexicans is what a monster with a cult's patience would prey on: a colony the Church had counted low.",
