      "summary": "The lumber baron Nathan Paine and his wife Jessie Kimberly Paine hired Ithaca architect Bryant Fleming (on the advice of Jessie's sister Mary K. Shirk) to design a Tudor Revival mansion; work began in 1927 and the exterior was done by 1930; the Depression halted work in 1932 and, per the Wikipedia article, the family never lived there — no reason is given. They resumed in 1946 and set up a nonprofit; Nathan died in 1947 at 77; the house opened to the public in 1948 as an art gallery and arboretum. Jessie, who lived in La Jolla, California, was museum president until her death in 1973 at age 100. The house holds French Barbizon paintings, sculpture, tapestries, Persian rugs and English and American silver; the grounds have twenty designed \"outdoor rooms.\" The odd detail: A finished-looking exterior sat empty for fourteen years while the owners lived elsewhere; the plan for who it was for shifted from home to museum without an announcement.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on dates; the family's reason for not living there is UNVERIFIED; the Kimberly–Clark connection of Jessie's family is not confirmed on the pages read. • address from memory — UNVERIFIED",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"Where were rich parties held\" in the owner's list — this one, a house built as an English manor in Wisconsin and never used. Vampires \"conspiring in high society\" would love a mansion with a finished front and an unlived-in interior.",
