      "summary": "At 639 ft the Bradley was the longest freighter and largest self-unloader on the lakes when she entered service on 28 July 1927 — Carl D. Bradley called her \"the last word in freighter construction\" — and she held the \"Queen of the Lakes\" title 22 years. In 1929 she set a cargo record of 18,114 long tons of limestone, three hundred railcars' worth, much of it bound for Gary, Indiana. (Beyond the window: she broke up in a Lake Michigan storm on 18 November 1958; 33 of 35 died, 23 of them from Rogers City, pop. 3,873 — 23 widows and 53 fatherless children.) The odd detail: A single company town on Lake Huron that sent its men out on the biggest ship in the world to feed Gary's furnaces — and a ship named for a living executive.",
