      "summary": "José de Jesús Manríquez y Zárate (b. León, Guanajuato, 1884), first Bishop of Huejutla (Hidalgo), defied Calles in a pastoral of 10 March 1926, was imprisoned for a year and exiled on 24 April 1927. In the United States he became the openly militant voice of Catholic armed defence: the project names him among those who \"smuggled weapons into Mexico,\" and he condemned the 21 June 1929 arreglos as a shameful modus vivendi. José Vasconcelos called him \"the standard-bearer\" of the persecuted faith and the de facto leader of the militant Church. He stayed out seventeen years, returning on 8 March 1944; he died in 1951. The odd detail: a bishop who spent longer in American exile than the whole Cristero War lasted — and argued, from American pulpits, for the right to take up arms.",
