      "summary": "Italy's Marchese de Pinedo, with Carlo Del Prete and mechanic Vitale Zacchetti, left Cagliari on 13 February 1927 in the Savoia-Marchetti S.55 flying boat Santa Maria, crossed to Brazil (24 Feb), flew the Mato Grosso, and entered the United States at New Orleans on 29 March — \"the first foreign airplane entering the U.S.\" — to the delight of Italian-Americans up the Mississippi. At a refuelling stop on Theodore Roosevelt Lake, Arizona, \"a teenage volunteer helping to refuel the Santa Maria carelessly discarded a cigarette that ignited gasoline on the water's surface\"; the plane burned and its engines sank 60 feet. An identical Santa Maria II reached New York by ship on 1 May, was christened on 8 May, and the crew resumed: up the Mississippi to Chicago, then an 11-hour flight from Chicago to Montreal on 17 May. They left Trepassey, Newfoundland on 22 May, ran short of fuel, were towed 200 miles to the Azores (30 May) and reached Ostia on 16 June — 29,180 miles in 124 days. The Chicago Italian community's Italo-American National Union later listed a $1,000 gift \"for the air flight of General De Pinedo\" in its ledger. The odd detail: The first foreign aircraft to enter the United States was destroyed by a boy's cigarette on a desert lake — and its replacement sat on Lake Michigan six years before Balbo's twenty-four.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
