      "summary": "Nelson, a Bible-quoting drifter with long arms and a stocky build, went door to door as a prospective roomer, strangled landladies and hid the bodies under the nearest bed or in trunks and attics — around twenty women across the continent in sixteen months. He came down the lakes in the first week of June 1927 — Buffalo, Detroit, Chicago in six days — then crossed to Manitoba, killed twice in Winnipeg, and was taken at Killarney, Manitoba, on 16 June as \"Virgil Wilson.\" Tried in Winnipeg from 1 Nov 1927, convicted in forty minutes, hanged 13 Jan 1928. The papers' names for him: the \"Dark Strangler,\" the \"Gorilla Man,\" the \"Gorilla Killer.\" A jail guard noted his fixation on the Proverbs about harlots. The odd detail: Six days, three lake cities, three landladies (unverified — V2_R06) — the killer moving along the Great Lakes ring like a schedule.",
