      "summary": "104,943 paid $2,658,660 — the first $2 million gate in entertainment history; Tunney's purse a record $990,445. Graham McNamee called it to 60+ NBC stations. In round seven Dempsey put Tunney down for the first time in his career, then stood over him instead of going to the neutral corner as the new rule (which Dempsey's own camp had asked for) required; referee Dave Barry would not start the count until he moved, Tunney was down about 13 seconds by the film and got up at \"nine,\" and won the decision. The 20-foot ring was bigger than Dempsey's preferred 16. The original referee, Davy Miller, had been replaced by Barry over rumours of Al Capone's influence. The odd detail: The referee was changed because the mob was said to own him — and the count that followed is still argued.",
