      "summary": "In 1926 six Black students were transferred into Emerson to relieve overcrowding; in September 1927 eighteen more came from the Virginia Street School, bringing Black enrolment to 24 out of about 2,800. Over 600 white students walked out on Monday 26 September 1927, carrying a banner reading \"We won't go back until Emerson is white,\" and by the third day 1,357 — about three-quarters of the high-school students — were out. On 30 September the city council settled it: the eighteen were sent back to Virginia Street and money was voted for a separate all-Black high school (Roosevelt) and a temporary school. By November 1927 no Black students remained at Emerson. (A 1928 Emerson yearbook account dates the walkout to 22 September and gives about 900 strikers out of roughly 3,000; single-source and inconsistent with Discover Indiana.) The odd detail: A student-led strike, and its demand was the pupils' own — the adults' compromise was the order to build a school for the excluded. A locked classroom door (and one remark) are the reported trigger.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the outcome; day counts and numbers vary by source (26 Sept per Discover Indiana and Wikipedia; 22 Sept per the yearbook).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A schoolyard walkout of a thousand white teenagers in a single morning with a banner already lettered: someone organised it. The Golden Circle's second-coming Klan (Mayor Floyd Williams was Klan-backed) makes a good candidate.",
