      "summary": "In 1927 the Hearst press printed documents said to come from President Calles's own archives, alleging Mexico had paid several US senators more than $1 million and was backing Sacasa's rebels in Nicaragua against the United States. The State Department found them forgeries and Secretary of State Frank Kellogg said so. A Senate committee held hearings on \"Alleged payments by the Mexican Government to United States Senators\" (15 December 1927 – 7 January 1928) and cleared the senators; Hearst was cleared of wrongdoing but his papers' credibility suffered. Hearst's own vast Chihuahua holdings (the Babícora ranch) were threatened with expropriation at the time. The odd detail: the forgeries came to the American public from a press lord who owned nearly a million acres of the country he was accusing.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the forgery and the hearings; the Babícora link is from a secondary summary (Medium).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the American press's framing of Mexico — bandits, fanatics, Bolshevik priests-haters — manufactured on demand. A forgery shop that supplies the Hearst desk is a Golden Circle instrument that needs no magic.",
