      "summary": "Garvey (born 17 August 1887, St Ann's Bay), founder of the UNIA and the Black Star Line, was convicted of mail fraud in 1923 and sent to Atlanta. His sentence was commuted on 18 November 1927 and he was deported: on 2 December 1927 he spoke a farewell from the deck of the United Fruit steamer SS Saramacca in New Orleans and sailed at 12:15 p.m. for Cristóbal and Jamaica. Kingston met him with bands and addresses at the Ward Theatre. In Jamaica he founded the People's Political Party (1929), sat briefly on the Kingston and St Andrew council, and was jailed for contempt the same year; he left for London in 1935 and died there on 10 June 1940. The odd detail: America's most watched Black leader was shipped out on a fruit-company banana boat by way of the Canal Zone.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the dates of commutation and sailing (two independent pages, via search summary); High for the biography. • Esplanade Avenue wharf area — UNVERIFIED",
