      "summary": "Opened on Thanksgiving Eve 1927 as the largest dance hall on the South Side, surpassing Lincoln Gardens, with a triple sub-floor, lavish decor and a checkroom for 6,000 hats and coats. From 1927 to 1940 it ran two bands a night, continuous music — Armstrong, Basie, Ellington, Hines, Holiday, Fitzgerald — and, as a hall for hire, staged boxing exhibitions, figure skating, roller skating and basketball: the house team, the Savoy Big Five, was renamed the Harlem Globetrotters. Most patrons were Black, but a growing white crowd came south to dance. It closed in 1948 and was demolished in the early 1970s. The odd detail: The checkroom for 6,000 coats — a room-sized inventory of who is in the building on any given night.",
