      "summary": "The Canadian freighter Kamloops vanished around 7 December 1927 with all 22 crew. The next May, bodies were found on Isle Royale at Twelve O'Clock Point; of nine recovered, five were identified and four buried unidentified at Thunder Bay. In December 1928 a trapper found a note in a bottle from assistant stewardess Alice Bettridge: \"I am the last one left alive, freezing and starving to death on Isle Royale in Lake Superior.\" Divers found the wreck on 21 August 1977 in over 260 ft of water; a crewman's body still lies under an engine-room stair, nicknamed \"Old Whitey.\" Isle Royale itself became a National Park on 3 April 1940; its moose, which appeared around 1905–1912, may have been deliberately introduced for a private hunting preserve; the Park Service later removed the Scandinavian fishing communities. The odd detail: A written message from a woman alone on an island, found by a trapper a year after she died — and a body that the cold lake has kept intact for a century.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"The last one left alive\" — the island that keeps its dead. Superior is cold enough that bodies don't surface; for an occult game, a lake that preserves its drowned is a ready-made mythology.",
