      "summary": "The Genesee Theatre opened on 25 December 1927 (Steinberg, architect; about $1 million; 2,403 seats; Spanish Renaissance interior with Caen stone, Italian marble and a hammered-silver dome; land bought in 1926 for $130,000); it closed in 1989 and reopened in 2004 after a $23 million restoration. Jack Benny — born Benjamin Kubelsky, 14 February 1894 — rented the Clayton Street house with his family in 1909–10, and played violin in the pit of the Barrison Theatre in 1910. On 25 June 1939 the world premiere of his film Man About Town was held at the Genesee during \"Jack Benny Days,\" with the radio cast (a search summary says Dorothy Lamour was among them (unverified — V_R38: not on the Waukegan city page)) brought to town — \"one of the largest gatherings ever in Jack's home town.\" Benny mentioned Waukegan in broadcasts so often that it \"put Waukegan on the map.\" The odd detail: The town's silver-domed picture palace, built as the Depression's shadow fell, held the Hollywood premiere of its own violinist's film — and the town's most famous radio joke about it had been running for years.",
