      "summary": "The primary campaign remembered as the Pineapple Primary combined electoral competition with shootings and bombings. A retrospective based on Tribune coverage and interviews with Chicago political observers reports sixty-two bombings and two political figures killed, including attacks on the homes of a senator and a candidate for state's attorney. The nickname referred to contemporary slang for a grenade. This is a useful setting for ward offices, campaign handbills, threatened households and poll watchers. It also reminds a campaign designer that danger could follow respectable political activity into residential streets, rather than remaining confined to a hidden criminal district.",
