      "summary": "Parker Henderson, who ran the Ponce de Leon Hotel in Miami where Capone stayed (room 804), cashed Capone's Western Union money orders of $1,000 to $5,000, negotiated the purchase of the Palm Island house for Capone for $40,000 (Capone himself said $50,000 cash), and held title before transferring it to Mae Capone. At Capone's request he bought six revolvers at a Miami pawnshop with Capone's money and handed them over. When New York police traced the revolver found near the body of Brooklyn boss Frankie Yale — shot July 1, 1928 (date from general record, not the report) — to that pawnshop purchase, Henderson was arrested for the murder; Capone told him not to worry, and the case was dropped. Capone gave him a diamond belt buckle. The odd detail: The gun that killed Frankie Yale was traced by its serial number to a pawnshop in Miami and to Capone's money — and nothing happened.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for Henderson's sworn account as recorded; the Yale date is general knowledge, not checked this session (Medium).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Six pawnshop revolvers, one accounted for. The other five are a scavenger hunt: each turns up at a killing in a different Great Lakes city over the decade.",
