      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Streeterville",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "the made land east of Michigan Ave. north of the river",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Streeterville%2C%20the%20made%20land%20east%20of%20Michigan%20Ave.%20north%20of%20the%20river"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "On July 10, 1886, George Wellington Streeter ran his steamboat Reutan aground off Superior Street (he blamed a storm the weather records don't show), got N. K. Fairbank's leave to stay, refused to leave, forged federal titles, invited contractors to dump rubble around the boat, and claimed the growing 186 acres as the \"United States District of Lake Michigan\", outside Illinois law — a 1902 witness quoted him: \"We'll get a million out of it.\" He met evictions with shotguns and boiling water, staged squatter \"invasions\", was convicted of manslaughter in 1902 and sent to prison (the acquittal story is the legend version), and died of pneumonia January 22, 1921, aged 84, the mayor at his funeral. His heirs kept suing until April 1928, when the courts ruled for Chicago Title and Trust. The Hancock tower stands where his shanty was. The odd detail: The Gold Coast's newest luxury district was, within living memory, a self-declared independent territory defended with a shotgun.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Made land is unconsecrated land; the \"District\" is the fae's foothold in the city, and the 1928 quieting of title is a treaty somebody signed.",
