      "summary": "The Fisher brothers built it with money from selling Fisher Body to GM; Albert Kahn Associates designed it, Joseph Nathaniel French as chief architect, echoing Saarinen's Tribune Tower design. Its roof was tiled in gold leaf and floodlit; the three-storey barrel-vaulted lobby, in forty kinds of marble, was decorated by the Hungarian Géza Maróti; the Fisher Theatre opened in Aztec-Mayan revival style with live macaws and banana trees. Radio station WJR leased space in December 1928 and still bills itself as broadcasting \"from the golden tower\" — the station that carried Father Coughlin's first sermons in 1926 (R05). In 1970 staff found a sealed storeroom holding flags of 75 nations made in 1928 to welcome foreign visitors. The odd detail: A sealed room nobody knew about for 42 years, full of flags for a reception that never happened.",
