      "summary": "Johns-Manville made asbestos building materials at Waukegan from 1928: transite pipe and board and pipe and block insulation (through 1985); the site carries a 150-acre asbestos disposal area where about three million cubic yards of waste were left; it was named a Superfund site in 1983, the buildings were demolished 2000–01 and EPA cleanup ended in 2017. Lawsuit records and legal-site summaries assert that the company knew the dangers of asbestos as early as the 1930s (not shown on the EPA-derived page I read). The town's mill workforce in those years: Swedes, Finns, Lithuanians (a Lithuanian Hall was built in 1929 at 901 S. Lincoln Ave), Slovenes and Armenians — search-summary claims about Waukegan's immigrant industrial labour, from pages not read (unverified — V_R38). The odd detail: The plant that opened on the Waukegan lakeshore in 1928 left a 150-acre asbestos dump that took until 2017 for the EPA to finish capping.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the plant, dates and waste; Medium-Low for \"knew in the 1930s\" (claim from lawsuit documents, not read) and for the ethnic mix. • Lincoln Ave), Slovenes and Armenians — search-summary claims about Waukegan's immigrant industrial labour, from pages not read (unverified — V_R38).",
