      "summary": "In 1928 President Calvin Coolidge spent about three months on the Brule River. Superior Central High School served as the White House offices from 15 June to 10 September 1928; he reportedly arrived at about 8:30 and left before noon. Wisconsin residents built a new railroad depot, telephone and telegraph lines, roads and an airport for the visit. On 16 July 1928 he received the presidential nominee Herbert Hoover and endorsed him from the steps of Central High. The Duluth Herald observed that he seemed \"more anxious to master the paddling of a canoe against the Brule rapids\" than to learn what was going on. He left with new fly-fishing and canoe skills and a wish to return that he never fulfilled. The odd detail: For almost twelve weeks in 1928 a Wisconsin high school housed the executive offices of the United States.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A presidency parked for a summer in an out-of-the-way lakeshore town with a new telegraph line, a brand-new airport and a secret-service perimeter is the kind of thing a conspiracy needs a summer to prepare for: what was the Secret Service guarding in Superior in the summer of 1928?",
