      "summary": "Ten-year-old Billy Ranieri's kidnapping became a national story in 1928. A December newspaper reported the convictions of Angelo Petitti and Andrew Cappellano and sentences of twenty-five years, while Cappellano's young son was acquitted. A January 1929 Oregon editorial described a witness murdered, others beaten, and threats against the judge, prosecutors and witnesses. These are contemporary reports, not a modern reconstruction of every allegation. The case offers a valuable route beyond familiar bootlegging stories: family fear, ransom negotiations, an outlying farm, bilingual communities and the practical problem of persuading ordinary people to testify.",
