      "summary": "On 15 October 1928 a Masonic lodge named Lux in Umbra (\"Light in the Shadow\") was founded in South Chicago \"under the auspices of the Great Orient and the Valley of Mexico Lodge of the Republic of Mexico\"; its elected president was Luis Álvarez del Castillo, who held \"a high degree in the powerful Universal Union.\" It was Lodge No. 50 by February 1929, held a literary-musical winter-solstice festival in January 1929 under Masonic sponsorship, a summer festival at the West Side Auditorium (June 1929) and an initiation banquet at Casa Alex (Oct 1929); on 9 Nov 1929 it gave notice that it would \"re-assume its work on the 15th of this month at its new Temple, 1010 South Halsted Street\". The FLPS notes other Mexican Masonic lodges met in Chicago in 1929. The odd detail: the lodge's own name is a motto with the word Umbra in it — and it held a solstice festival.",
