      "summary": "For a century the annual Chicago city directory (name, occupation, home address) was the investigator's first tool; general directories are available online for 1843–1917 and 1923, and the brief's lead — confirmed as the standard genealogists' account but not re-read this session — is that the last general Chicago directory was the 1928–29 edition (unverified — V_R18). Through the 1930s the telephone directory (Illinois Bell) took its place for anyone with a phone, and the selective Chicago Blue Book of society (1890–1915) and social registers (1899–1922) had already stopped. The odd detail: for the entire Depression decade Chicago had no city directory — a person without a telephone was, on paper, nowhere.",
