      "summary": "Sent to Nicaragua in December 1928 to serve with the Nicaraguan National Guard detachment fighting Sandino, Lewis \"Chesty\" Puller earned his first Navy Cross for leading five successive engagements against superior numbers of \"bandit forces\" between 16 February and 19 August 1930; the locals called him \"El Tigre\". In September 1932 he took a company of 40 Guardsmen on a patrol 100 miles north of Jinotega; after a six-day march they were ambushed by roughly 150 rebels — only two of his men died, six were wounded, and the attackers were \"decimated and scattered\"; he was ambushed twice more on the way back. His second Navy Cross came for the fight near El Sauce on 26 December 1932 — described as the last major battle of the Sandino rebellion. The odd detail: the Marine's name in the mountain war was in the enemy's language: the Nicaraguans called him \"El Tigre\".",
