      "summary": "The consul in the FLPS years is Rafael Aveleyra (1930–32). Adolfo Domínguez arrived as vice-consul 9 April 1929; his wife Milla Domínguez, a soprano, directed the Mexican Hour on WCFL (Mar 1930). The consulate's active work: it asked for the extradition of the con man José Durán from Juárez (Oct 1928); it found the parents of Genaro Arias, a 15-year-old found sleeping in a railroad car (Aug 1932); it reported (Aug 1932) that beach-access restrictions on Twelfth Street would end (listing); it sponsored the Independence Day festival at the Coliseum Theatre (Sept 1931) and the Cinco de Mayo events. The colony's papers repeatedly demanded accountability: F. P. Miranda (23 Nov 1929) asked the vice-consul to investigate why a festival with nearly 6,000 attendees netted only $175; Prof. F. Ortega Rodríguez (12 Sept 1931) protested that Aveleyra had invited only \"prominent Mexican citizens\" to plan the festival's finances. In Jan 1931 El Nacional asked the consulate to complain to Chicago's police chief. A general summary says the consulate opened in Chicago in 1884 (one secondary source — UNVERIFIED). The odd detail: the consul who found a lost boy in a boxcar and requested a swindler's arrest was also being asked, in print, where the Independence Day money went.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the letters and the boy; Medium on dates of tenure. • one secondary source — UNVERIFIED",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the consulate is the natural go-between for the Mexico book's cast in Chicago (M06 Part E makes the same point). The missing festival money and the lost boy are two loose ends a consular clerk could tie together.",
