      "summary": "The colony's dance bands and orchestras appear in almost every notice: the Orquesta Típica Mexicana Morales and the \"Morales sisters\" (Café de Alex, Jan 1932; a benefit banquet at Mrs. Morales' home, Nov 1929), the Torreblanca típica (a Dec 1930 relief benefit), the Orquesta Típica Obreros Libres Mexicanos and the South Chicago Serenaders under Cirilo Rodríguez (both organised Nov 1929), Los Diablos Rojos (Hull House, March 1929), a women's string orchestra directed by Isaiah Medina Campa (June 1929), the Típica Mexicana de Señoritas (banquet 1931), Las Musas Latinas (a July 1933 picnic band) and the Mexican Band of South Chicago, which a June 1928 editorial (\"This Is the Way to Do It!\") called the colony's pride, played a Puebla anniversary event (Apr 1929) and a Pilsen Park concert and dance (May 1929). Frank Garry's orchestra and Prof. José Reyes's orchestra are also named for dances. The odd detail: a women's orchestra formed in June 1929 — in a colony where the caseworkers found married women rarely left the house.",
