      "summary": "The Italian Chamber of Commerce bulletin reports that in late 1928 General Italo Balbo, \"Under-Secretary of Aviation in the Fascist Cabinet,\" filled the Palmer House banquet hall; Mayor Thompson and Governor Len Small sent representatives, conductor Giorgio Polacco and singers of the Civic Opera attended, ex-consul Conte Giulio Bolognesi was toastmaster. In June 1927 the Chicago colony had already sent the King a solid-gold laurel crown with ten rubies and a platinum ribbon and Mussolini a solid-silver Roman sword with a gold eagle's-head hilt, the crown made by E. Toniatti's Venetian Jewelry Manufacturing Co. (35 E. Wacker), the sword by an unnamed American firm that altered the design, both designed by Mario Zacchi of the Daprato Statuary Co., displayed in Mandel Brothers' State Street window. On Italian Day, 15 July 1934 — the first anniversary of Balbo's 1933 air-armada landing — the Roman column donated by Mussolini was dedicated before the Italian pavilion, with Bishop Sheil present and Balbo speaking by radio from Rome; Judge John Sbarbaro chaired the committee. The odd detail: Balbo's 1933 landing on Lake Michigan was his second Chicago triumph; and Chicago Italians sent Rome a ruby crown.",
