      "summary": "Helen Wawrzyniak (1908–87) married Lester Gillis in 1928 and had two children; when the family fled to San Antonio in late 1933 their son Ronald was four. She was at Little Bohemia on 20 April 1934 with the other men's girlfriends and was one of the three women captured inside the lodge after the Bureau's raid; after interrogation all three were convicted of harbouring and paroled. On 27 November 1934 she was in the stolen Ford V8 at Barrington when Nelson fought agents Cowley and Hollis — he shouted to her to take cover in a ditch; he was hit nine times (the papers said 17), killed Hollis with a shot to the head, and died at 7:35 p.m. His body was found, after a telephone-company employee's tip, wrapped in an Indian-pattern blanket in a ditch at the northeast corner of St. Paul's Lutheran Cemetery in Skokie. Helen said she had wrapped him because \"He always hated being cold.\" She walked Chicago's streets as a fugitive for several days, surrendered on Thanksgiving Day, and served a year for harbouring. She and Nelson lie together at St. Joseph's Cemetery, River Grove. The odd detail: The blanket, and the reason for it.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A widow who laid a corpse in a cemetery ditch at night and then walked the city for three days is a survivor NPC with a story to sell — and Section 13 (the FBI's own) has the file on what she did in those three days.",
