      "summary": "A Republican alderman indicted for graft in 1917 (he resigned from the Council, hired Clarence Darrow, and was acquitted), De Priest won the 1st District in 1928 (47.8% to Harry Baker's 40.3%) and served three terms, 1929–35 — the only Black member of Congress. In 1929 First Lady Lou Hoover's invitation of Jessie De Priest to the congressional wives' tea set off a national storm; De Priest fought the segregation of the House restaurant, insisting Howard students eat in the public room rather than the basement reserved for Black employees. In September 1930 he received an extortion letter signed \"the Brooklyn Rats\" demanding $10,000; investigators traced it to a Chicago political figure, Julius Link, who got six months. He lost in 1934 to Democrat Arthur Mitchell, and died 12 May 1951, buried at Graceland. The odd detail: The \"Brooklyn Rats\" letter — a Chicago politician writing a mob-style shakedown to the only Black congressman.",
