      "summary": "Oris Paxton (1879–1936) and Mantis James (1881–1935) Van Sweringen controlled at their 1928 peak 30,000 miles of railroad worth $3 billion — the Nickel Plate, the Erie, the Chesapeake & Ohio — and built Shaker Heights under \"Shaker Standards\" that barred certain groups. They never married, \"seldom gave interviews,\" \"avoided Cleveland society, living at their Hunting Valley estate,\" and \"shared a common bedroom in their 54-room mansion.\" On May 1, 1935, they defaulted on $48 million of loans from J. P. Morgan & Co., which ordered the collateral auctioned in September 1935; with backers they bought their own empire back \"for just over $3 million.\" Mantis died December 12, 1935; Oris on November 22, 1936, with a net worth under $3,000. Their shared tombstone reads \"Brothers.\" The odd detail: Two men who owned the roads to half the country slept in one room of a 54-room house, and were buried under one word.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The IHS's American seat is John Carroll University, outside Cleveland; the brothers who avoided society and died within a year of each other, broke, are the file on its desk. A vampire pair who never appeared in public and kept one room is the simplest reading; the owner's rule says find the third layer.",
