      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Taylor St and Racine Ave",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "the Near West Side \"Little Italy,\" Chicago",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Taylor%20St%20and%20Racine%20Ave%2C%20the%20Near%20West%20Side%20%22Little%20Italy%2C%22%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "A short, dumpy grandmother in her sixties, Warshovsky set fires for landlords who wanted the insurance — $170 a job in one documented case, the owner collecting some $11,200 — using a fuse device of her own design that let her get clear before the building caught. In a 1928 fire at Taylor and Racine a disabled seventeen-year-old boy who could not get out died. She confessed to \"at least a dozen\" fires and later testified she had \"started more fires than she could remember\"; the state sought death for the owner, not for her, and she turned state's evidence and became a professional prosecution witness in arson trials, using what the Tribune called \"firebug phrases\" and gestures on the stand. The odd detail: The city's professional arsonist became the city's professional arson witness.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium — single local-history page from Tribune files; her own sentence is unclear even to the author.",
