      "summary": "Born in Monterey, Virginia, 24 October 1896, Joyner was the first Black graduate of the A. B. Moler Beauty School (1916), opened her own salon, and from 1920 supervised 200 Walker beauty schools as national adviser, training some 15,000 stylists. In 1928 she patented a permanent-wave machine with a scalp protector (U.S. patent 1,693,515) that went into salons serving both Black and white women — the patent was assigned to the Walker company, and she was paid little. She helped organise the Bud Billiken Parade (R14) and ran the Defender's charity network through the decade; in 1945 she founded the United Beauty School Owners and Teachers Association with Mary McLeod Bethune. She died in Chicago 27 December 1994, aged 98. The odd detail: A Chicago beautician's patent hung over the heads of white women under the dryers on the North Shore, and she never saw the money.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
