      "summary": "Calumet and Hecla's copper output peaked at 100 million pounds in 1906 and had fallen to 15 million by 1921; in 1923 it merged Ahmeek, Allouez, Osceola and Centennial into the Calumet and Hecla Consolidated Copper Company. In the Depression the workforce fell \"from 4900 in 1928–29 to 1300 in 1933,\" and the conglomerate mine's operations ended in 1939 after 3.275 billion pounds. The company had built hundreds of company houses, libraries, bowling alleys, and, in 1868, the first industrial hospital in the United States. The Quincy Mine at Hancock mined continuously from 1856 to 1931 and again from 1937 to 1945 (search summary). The 1913 strike and the Italian Hall disaster (R07) were within living memory. The odd detail: The company that built the first industrial hospital in America (1868) cut its workforce from 4,900 to 1,300 in five years.",
