      "summary": "After a 1902 fire the Sanitarium had been rebuilt in stone; in 1928 it added the \"Towers,\" a 14-story high-rise with 265 guest rooms facing Champion Street. Its guests included Thomas Edison, Amelia Earhart, Warren G. Harding, Henry Ford and J. C. Penney; its regimen included hydrotherapy, \"the first electric light bath,\" vegetarian and whole-grain diets and exercise. The 1929 crash emptied it of rich patients and \"the institution went into receivership in 1933.\" Dr. John Harvey Kellogg — disfellowshipped by the Seventh-day Adventists in 1907 — kept managing the place until 1943. In 1942 the U.S. Army bought the main building for $2,341,000 and made it Percy Jones General Hospital. The odd detail: A vegetarian, whole-grain, water-cure regimen was sold to the rich in a 14-story tower that opened the year before the crash, and it went into receivership four years after.",
