      "summary": "In 1928 the Smithsonian's new secretary, Charles Abbot, offered to revise a museum label crediting Samuel Langley's Aerodrome as \"the first man-carrying aeroplane capable of flight\" — but only if Orville Wright would publicly say he believed the Langley machine capable of flight (unverified — V_R43: the Smithsonian's own page says only that Abbot offered to modify the label \"not in confession of error, but in a gesture of good will\"). Orville took it as an insult and lent the original 1903 Flyer to the Science Museum of London that year. The Smithsonian did not apologise until 1942, when it reported that Abbot had tendered apologies to Orville for misleading statements by former officials. Orville died on 30 January 1948, and the Flyer came home and was unveiled at the Smithsonian's Arts and Industries Building on 17 December 1948. The odd detail: In every 1930s year the machine that ended Dayton's claim to fame sat in a London museum, 3,000 miles from its makers, because of an argument about a label.",
