      "summary": "After two Marine patrols were ambushed and cut off by Sandino's forces, 1st Lt. Christian F. Schilt made ten flights into the besieged town over 6–8 January 1928, evacuating 18 casualties and carrying in a replacement commander and medical supplies. The Marines on the ground burned and levelled part of the town's main street to make a landing strip; his O2U Corsair biplane had no brakes, so men dragged it to a stop by the wings as it touched down. He received the Medal of Honor. The flights are counted the Marine Corps' first medical evacuation; Nicaragua was also the scene of the first Marine close-air-support bombing (Ocotal, M05). Two Medals of Honor and about 100 Navy Crosses were awarded in the campaign; about 4,000 Marines were in Nicaragua at the 1928 peak, under 1,000 by 1930, and 136 Marines died across the deployment. The odd detail: the first Marine air ambulance landed on a town street that the Marines themselves had burned to make room — no brakes, men hauling the wings.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High (three agreeing search summaries; not read in full).",
