      "summary": "Calvin Coolidge left Washington by train on 13 January 1928, sailed from Key West aboard the battleship USS Texas early on 15 January, reached Havana that afternoon and addressed the Pan-American Conference on 16 January; he met Machado and returned to Key West on the 17th. Cuba was the only foreign country he visited in office, and he remained the only sitting president to visit Cuba for most of the century. His party included Secretary of State Kellogg, the former Secretary Charles Evans Hughes (who then led the US delegation and defended the right of intervention in the conference sessions), Ambassador Dwight Morrow, and the writers H. L. Mencken and Will Rogers. The odd detail: Mencken and Will Rogers on the same battleship, sent to watch a president praise a dictator the month the dictator was arranging his own unopposed re-election.",
