      "summary": "Toribio Romo González (b. 16 April 1900) was shot by government troops at dawn on 25 February 1928; his sister María was present and cried, \"Courage, Father Toribio … merciful Christ, receive him! Long live Christ the King!\" Canonised 21 May 2000. FOLKLORE (attested from the late 1970s): migrants crossing the US–Mexico desert began to report a young priest who guided them, gave them water or money and told them to visit him at Santa Ana — later recognised as St. Toribio; he is now called the patron of migrants. The irony the record keeps: in 1920 he wrote a play warning people against going north. The odd detail: a priest who wrote against emigration became, fifty years after his death, the saint who walks the border with those who go.",
