      "summary": "Freeman Gosden and Charles Correll, two white minstrel-trained performers who had done Sam 'n' Henry on WGN (1926–28), began Amos 'n' Andy on WMAQ on 19 Mar 1928; Pepsodent took it to NBC Blue on 19 Aug 1929 and coast-to-coast on 28 Nov 1929. Andy's courtship of the Harlem beautician Madame Queen and the 1931 breach-of-promise trial held some 40 million listeners in 1930–31; a planned murder-investigation storyline was dropped after the National Association of Chiefs of Police protested. NBC's standing order was to interrupt the broadcast only for matters of national importance and SOS calls. The pair and announcer Bill Hay split $100,000 a year. In 1931 the Pittsburgh Courier's Robert L. Vann ran a campaign and more than 700,000 Black Americans petitioned the Federal Radio Commission against the show's stereotypes. RKO's Check and Double Check (1930, with Duke Ellington's orchestra) was the studio's biggest hit until King Kong. Female parts went to actresses from 1935; the show left Chicago for Hollywood in 1935. The odd detail: the police chiefs' association killed a radio murder plot; the whole country stopped at 7 p.m. (Chicago time — 6 p.m.) for fifteen minutes.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
