      "summary": "Dwight W. Morrow, J. P. Morgan partner, ambassador from 29 October 1927 to 17 September 1930, opened his \"ham and eggs\" breakfast diplomacy with Calles and brought Will Rogers and Charles Lindbergh south for goodwill (his daughter Anne met Lindbergh there). Morrow's channel to the Church was Fr. John J. Burke, CSP, founder and general secretary of the National Catholic Welfare Conference (and former editor of the Catholic World), who on 4 April 1928 met Calles secretly for about four hours (unverified — V_M07: the date is confirmed, the duration only from a search snippet) in the old island fortress of San Juan de Ulúa, with Morrow as intermediary; the DHIAL says \"nothing was achieved\" there, but it opened the channel. In June 1929 the two exiled prelates, Ruiz y Flores and Pascual Díaz, travelled from St. Louis into Mexico in Morrow's own railroad car, were lodged at the Legorreta home, negotiated with President Portes Gil on 12–13 June, and the arreglos — drafted in Morrow's office — were announced on 21 June 1929; church bells rang across Mexico on 27 June. (The Mexican side — casualties, the executions after the peace — is in M01.) Burke died in New York on 30 October 1936; Morrow on 5 October 1931. The odd detail: the Church's peace was negotiated in a colonial island fortress that had been Mexico's most notorious political prison, and the bishops came home riding in an American banker's private Pullman.",
