      "summary": "In the six months before the Republican primary of April 10, 1928, Chicago saw 62 bombings and at least two politicians killed — the \"pineapple\" was period slang for a hand grenade. On March 21, 1928 \"Diamond Joe\" Esposito — Nineteenth Ward Republican committeeman, owner of the Bella Napoli Café where Paul Ricca waited tables — was shot down after a day's campaigning within sight of his home near 800 S. Oakley; his bodyguards were untouched and no one was charged. On March 26 bombs hit the homes of U.S. Senator Charles Deneen (457 W. 61st Place) and his State's Attorney candidate John A. Swanson (7217 Crandon Avenue) at the same time. On election day Octavius C. Granady — a reform candidate for 20th Ward committeeman, a lawyer from the U.S. Virgin Islands — was chased in his car and killed. Voters turned on the violence: Deneen's slate won, Louis Emmerson beat Governor Len Small by 400,000 votes and Swanson beat Robert Crowe by 120,000, crushing Thompson's faction. The odd detail: Two bombs went off at two candidates' houses on opposite sides of the city on the same night — and the bombing backfired so badly the voters threw the bombers' friends out.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Granady was chased through the streets on election day — a Caribbean-born lawyer in a city where the Golden Circle's map is centred on Havana. His killers were Thompson men in the record; the setting can ask who pointed them at him.",
