      "summary": "The Mexican steelworkers' mission (founded after Mundelein's decision of July 1923; first Claretian October 1924; first church a converted army barracks at 9024 S. Mackinaw Ave.; Fr. James Tort, C.M.F., pastor from 1925) bought land at 91st and Brandon in 1926. On 14 April 1928 the cornerstone of its new church was laid by Rt. Rev. Pascual Díaz y Barreto, the exiled Bishop of Tabasco, who was living in New York after his expulsion in 1927 (unverified — V_M07: the reprinted caption, \"South Chicago church and Mexican center dedicated by bishop of Tabasco,\" does not say where he lived; sources say only that much of his exile was spent in the US). Díaz — a Jesuit of Huichol descent, born in Zapopan — would, fourteen months later, take part in the arreglos with Portes Gil and become Archbishop of Mexico (appointed 25 June, installed 28 June 1929). The St. Jude shrine begun here in 1929 is in R05. The odd detail: the stone of Chicago's first Mexican church was laid by the man who would end the Cristero War — an Indigenous Jesuit bishop exiled from the Tabasco of Garrido Canabal's Red Shirts.",
