      "summary": "Ulises Armand Sanabria, aged 21 (b. 5 Sep 1906), put Chicago's first mechanical television station on the air through WCFL on 12 Jun 1928, months after Jenkins and Baird's first demonstrations; by 1929 he ran W9XAO with WIBO and on 19 May 1929 was first to send picture and sound on one wave band; Milton Berle performed on his station in 1929. The Daily News licensed its own experimental station W9XAP in 1930 and on 27 Aug 1930 sent a variety programme with WMAQ's Bill Hay. By 1934 Sanabria projected a picture 30 feet wide; he made picture tubes until 1955 and founded the American Television Institute, which gave the first B.S. degrees in television engineering and trained 6,000 students. The odd detail: a teenager's 45-line television was on the air in Chicago five years before the fair; a handful of Loop hobbyists watched Bill Hay on a spinning disc in 1930.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the station dates; Medium on W9XAP's programme schedule.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Forty-five lines is enough to see a face. The Daily News engineers keep a filmed record of a 1931 test in which the studio was empty and the screen was not.",
