      "summary": "Three American businessmen — the \"Border Barons\" Wirt G. Bowman (who already ran Tijuana's Foreign Club), Baron Long (California hotelier and nightclub owner) and James N. Crofton (racing man) — built a resort for roughly $10 million, on land owned by Gen. Abelardo L. Rodríguez, then military commander and governor of Baja California (later president of Mexico, 1932–34, and himself an investor). It opened 22 June 1928: a 500-room hotel, a casino, a spa with Turkish steam baths and a minaret tower, designed by a 19-year-old architect, Wayne McAllister. A second stage (December 1929) added an Olympic-size pool, an 18-hole golf course, horse and greyhound tracks, a tropical aviary, bungalows, a private radio station and an airstrip. The casino's showpiece was the Gold Room, with gold chips and a rumoured $500 minimum wager. Guests included Clark Gable, Jean Harlow, the Marx Brothers, Marlene Dietrich, Bing Crosby, Charlie Chaplin and Jack Dempsey; Rita Hayworth was discovered dancing there. President Cárdenas outlawed gambling in 1935 and the resort died; the buildings reopened as a school (a junior high, 1939, by one account) and the blue-tiled minaret, the spa shell and the bungalows survive. The odd detail: the resort had its own private airstrip and radio station — Hollywood flew in for the weekend and the casino's takings were driven north to San Diego banks in a Cadillac (see the next entry).",
