      "summary": "The Unione Siciliana — a Sicilian fraternal benefit society whose Chicago presidency the gangs fought over — lost four heads in a row. Antonio Lombardo, Capone's consigliere and president since November 1925, was gunned down with bodyguard Joseph Ferrara at Madison and Dearborn on September 7, 1928 (bodyguard Joe Lolordo survived), allegedly by the Aiello and North Side gangs in retaliation for Frankie Yale's murder. His successor Pasqualino Lolordo was shot in his own home on January 8, 1929, allegedly on Moran's and Aiello's orders — a prelude to the Massacre five weeks later. Joseph \"Hop Toad\" Giunta was found beaten and shot near Hammond on May 8, 1929 (see Scalise entry). Joe Aiello, finally elected with New York's blessing, took 59 bullets on October 23, 1930. Lombardo lies in a mausoleum at Mount Carmel. The odd detail: A mutual-aid society for Sicilian immigrants — insurance and burial benefits — became the most dangerous title in Chicago.",
