      "summary": "The storm later named for Okeechobee hit Guadeloupe on 12 September 1928 and killed about 1,200, leaving three-quarters of the people homeless and destroying 85–95% of the banana crop. Montserrat lost 42 and nearly starved; Martinique lost 3. It struck Puerto Rico as a Category 5 on 13 September (312 dead, 500,000+ homeless) and Florida on 17 September, where Lake Okeechobee's dike broke: at least 2,500 drowned, about three-quarters of them Black migrant farm workers; 1,600 lie in a mass grave in West Palm Beach. The odd detail: one storm killed on a French island, a British island, an American island and the American mainland in five days — the Caribbean as one weather system under four flags.",
