      "summary": "The storm crossed Puerto Rico on 13 September 1928 (San Felipe day) with a 15-mile eye taking eight hours; San Juan recorded sustained winds of 160 mph before the anemometer failed. 312 dead, 24,728 houses destroyed and 192,444 damaged, over 500,000 people homeless, about $50 million in damage; half the coffee trees and half the shade trees were lost and the coffee industry never regained its place as an exporter. On Guadeloupe some 1,200 died. Days later the same storm drowned at least 2,500 in Florida, mostly around Lake Okeechobee (1,600 in a mass grave at Port Mayaca). The Red Cross spent over $3.2 million on Puerto Rico and the Virgin Islands and supplied 128,513 families. The odd detail: the island's name for it, San Felipe Segundo, is a saint's-day name (\"the second San Felipe\" — an earlier hurricane is remembered as the first; the 1876 date is my recollection, UNVERIFIED). It fell on the same island where the 1899 San Ciriaco hurricane (living memory in 1928) had killed thousands — figure not sourced here.",
