      "summary": "A man calling himself José Durán, also \"A. B. Deming,\" opened a fake agency and registered over 200 Mexican men from 15 to 22 October 1928 for \"jobs at the Navy Yard in Cleveland\" at \"five dollars a day minimum,\" free transportation, furnished rooms, coal, stoves and bedding \"guaranteed for six months.\" He took $5–15 from each — about $3,000 — timed to the week after the seasonal men were paid, and vanished on 20 October, five days before the train. At the depot the \"employer, Mr. Deming\" proved not to exist; the victims filed complaints at the Desplaines station. On 31 October the paper reported him (now spelled \"José Durand,\" and the victims now \"more than three hundred\") arrested in Ciudad Juárez by Mexican authorities at the Mexican consul's request. México ran repeated warnings against \"unscrupulous employment agencies,\" fake utility-company agents and a \"fraud ring.\" The odd detail: the con used a government shipyard's name — and the consul, not the Chicago police, got the man.",
