      "id": "M05-046",
      "title": "Ciénaga",
      "date_start": "1928-12-05",
      "date_end": "1928-12-05",
      "date_label": "the banana massacre, 5–6 December 1928 (Colombia)",
      "category": "Puerto Rico, the Virgin Islands, the Canal Zone, Nicaragua and the banana republics, 1898–1940",
      "tags": [
        "Puerto",
        "Virgin",
        "Ciénaga"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Carlos Cortés Vargas"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Ciénaga",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Magdalena department, near Santa Marta, Colombia (United Fruit's zone)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Ci%C3%A9naga%2C%20Magdalena%20department%2C%20near%20Santa%20Marta%2C%20Colombia%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "On 12 November 1928 at least 25,000 United Fruit workers struck with nine demands: no hiring through subcontractors, collective insurance, accident compensation, sanitary dormitories and six-day weeks, higher wages for the lowest paid, weekly wages, abolition of company stores, cash instead of coupons, and better hospitals. Gen. Carlos Cortés Vargas brought about 700 troops; on 5 December about 1,500 workers and families gathered in Ciénaga's square after Sunday mass; soldiers put machine guns on the rooftops, closed off the streets and, after a five-minute warning, opened fire on the crowd, including children. The official toll was 47; estimates run from hundreds to 2,000. A U.S. diplomatic dispatch of 29 December 1928 quoted United Fruit's legal advisor as saying the dead were \"between five and six hundred,\" and by 16 January 1929 company representatives said the figure \"exceeded 1000.\" U.S. officials framed the strike as communist-influenced: a Santa Marta consulate telegram called it \"a manifestation with a subversive tendency.\" García Márquez's One Hundred Years of Solitude put the fictional death toll near 3,000. The odd detail: the U.S. consulate's telegram counts the dead more than the government does — the company's own lawyer said 500–600.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the event and the diplomatic numbers; the true toll is unknown.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a mass grave and a missing count; the massacre of a crowd after Mass in front of a church. IHS in Ciénaga: a priest who counted the dead. Colombia is a country the Circle does not hold — a place where an investigator crosses a line.",
