      "summary": "The Survey defined him plainly: \"The 'fixer' is just what the word indicates. As to qualifications, he has none, except that he may be a person of some small political influence.\" His work was done early: the underworld preferred to kill a case at the preliminary hearing, and a fixer who let a case get past that stage was counted a failure. Where that failed, fixers worked through intermediaries to have murder charges reduced to manslaughter. The Survey also names a First Ward Levee \"overlord\" who ran a new tenderloin district by \"settling with the people down town.\" The odd detail: the Survey's single-sentence job description — no qualifications but \"some small political influence\".",
