      "summary": "Wirt C. Rowland of Smith, Hinchman & Grylls designed the Union Trust Company's 1928–29 tower in orange \"Guardian brick\" (first sold as \"Union Trust Brick\"), with Pewabic and Rookwood tile in a three-storey vaulted lobby and Monel metal for all exposed metalwork. Ezra Winter's mosaic over the main lobby desk shows a pine tree with the Union Trust's mission, and his banking-hall mural maps Michigan's industries. Union Trust had merged with the National Bank of Commerce in 1928 to form the Guardian Detroit Union Group; it failed in 1933 and went into receivership as the \"New Union Building Corporation\". The building had the first automatic elevators that levelled themselves with the floors. The odd detail: A bank built like a church — and nicknamed one — that failed within four years of opening and dragged the country into a bank holiday.",
