      "summary": "The idea of a monument to Columbus in Santo Domingo went back to the historian Antonio del Monte y Tejada (1852); the Fifth Pan-American Conference (Santiago de Chile, 1923) resolved that the governments of the Americas should build it. An international design competition drew 455 entrants from 48 countries; the jury, meeting in 1931, included Frank Lloyd Wright, Eliel Saarinen and Uruguay's Horacio Acosta y Lara, and chose the young Scottish architect Joseph Lea Gleave's cross-shaped design. Foundations were dug only in 1948; it was finished in 1992. It is meant to hold the remains the Dominican Republic says are Columbus's (found in the cathedral in 1877); Seville says DNA shows its own are his. The odd detail: Frank Lloyd Wright judged a monument to Columbus for the first city of the Americas in the year after San Zenón flattened it.",
