      "id": "M18-038",
      "title": "38. Halsted and Taylor — Italian gangs and the colony's plan to write Washington, 1929–31",
      "date_start": "1929",
      "date_end": "1931",
      "date_label": "1929 – 1931",
      "category": "Mexican Chicago, 1916–1940: the colonias and their people",
      "tags": [
        "Mexican",
        "Chicago",
        "Halsted",
        "Taylor",
        "Italian"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Ignacio M. Valle",
        "Concepción García"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Halsted and Taylor Streets",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(Near West Side)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Halsted%20and%20Taylor%20Streets%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "a Mexican store owned by Ignacio M. Valle",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=a%20Mexican%20store%20owned%20by%20Ignacio%20M.%20Valle%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "South Chicago",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=South%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "El Nacional (20 May 1931) reports Italian gang members organising systematic robberies of Mexican workers around Halsted and Taylor, with \"political protection\" that got offenders out of jail fast. A Sunday afternoon attack by about ten on two Mexican railroad workers shopping for supplies drove one victim into Valle's store; leaving for a tailor's shop he was attacked again, used a knife and was pelted with stones; the fleeing attackers damaged \"El Arte Mexicano\" to the tune of $500, and police traced an abandoned getaway car by a stolen licence plate. Two other young men had been beaten by six or seven at the same corner earlier. The colony planned to petition the consul, then the Mexican embassy and \"Washington.\" On 27 May a Mexican was hospitalised after an \"ethnic gang attack\" in South Chicago; on 8 June 1929, México reported that Italian boys were implicated in the killing of Concepción García. Redfield's informants (1924) had already noted Mexican shops caused frictions with Jewish, Polish and (less) Irish shopkeepers; no incident involving Irish youths turned up in what I read. The odd detail: the getaway car had a stolen plate and the shop was a Mexican importer that sold the records the colony danced to.",
