      "summary": "(1) Sunday 20 Sept 1931 (reported 26 Sept): an unidentified Mexican nicknamed \"El Veneno\" (\"The Poison\") was found at daybreak in an empty lot at Taylor and Canal, \"many lesions,\" and one blow that \"almost severed the head\"; he had run a bandit crew that burglarised West Side houses and had many rivals; police had tracked him. (2) April 1931: Albert Velasco, 20 (8722 Buffalo Ave.), drunk, shot Juvencio Horta, 25 (9232 Burley Ave.), an Illinois Steel employee with no local relatives, at Joseph García's meat market over a remark to Irene Lavonage, a Polish woman (3221 E. 91st St.); Velasco told police, \"they cannot do anything to me because I am a minor\"; the inquest was at Murphy's Funeral Parlor. (3) Bartolo Guerra was sentenced to twenty years (Feb 1929) for the September 1928 murder of a compatriot, Ángel Sandoval. (4) Nine Mexicans were acquitted (May 1931) in the death of John Patlon near Chicago Heights. (5) An unidentified Mexican's decomposed body was found (18 Apr 1929). (6) Manuel Romeo got two years and $400 (Aug 1931) for assault; Jerry Brosman was shot by Santiago Rivera (Aug 1931). The odd detail: a man called \"The Poison\" found nearly beheaded in an empty lot on Taylor Street, with a colony of witnesses and no arrests.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for (1) and (2); the others are listing summaries.",
