      "summary": "Pelée came back to life in 1929 and erupted through 1932, raising a second dome (unverified — V_M10: \"slightly higher than 1902's\" is contradicted by Wikipedia's Mount Pelée, which calls the 1929 lava plug \"smaller than the 1902\" one) — without a single death (confirmed: the danger area was evacuated at once), because this time the mountain was watched. The American volcanologist Frank Alvord Perret (born Hartford, 1867; once an assistant in Thomas Edison's laboratories; a research associate of the Carnegie Institution of Washington) was among the first scientists there; in 1931 he built a small observation hut on Morne Lenard, less than 3 km from the summit, the first permanent station on the mountain, and watched glowing avalanches pour down the valleys on either side of it, sometimes only just missing him. He founded Saint-Pierre's volcano museum (opened 1933) and published The Eruption of Mt. Pelée, 1929–1932 (Carnegie Institution, 1935). The odd detail: an American sat in a shack on the killer mountain for three years and wrote down every breath it took.",
