      "summary": "The Mexican Aero Club of Chicago, an educational aviation club \"established in 1929,\" expanded to Gary and Indiana Harbor (Mar 1931) and was incorporated under Illinois law (Apr 1931); it stressed \"education and service to the homeland and the community.\" Luis G. Herrera organised a Kansas City subsidiary (Feb 1932); a Pro-Vuelo Committee ran a national-holiday festival (Aug 1931); in 1928 Greer College of Chicago advertised Spanish-language courses in electricity, mechanics, radio and aviation; an April 1933 notice announced a Mexican military aircraft flyover for the Fifth of May. In Aug 1932 the press reported an \"aviation captain\" arrested for swindling a Colombian visitor, then Captain Venegas freed on $1,000 bond after an embezzlement conviction, found guilty (27 Aug) of taking a student's payment — and, in the same month (20 Aug), the death of Major Jaime Venegas, military instructor (probably the same man; the FLPS listings do not reconcile the sequence). The odd detail: a Chicago Mexican flying club whose instructor was convicted of embezzling a student's fee and whose obituary ran a week before the verdict.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-Low (listings only; the Venegas timeline is inconsistent).",
