      "summary": "The northwoods and the southern Wisconsin lakes were where Chicago's outlaws went to be tourists. Emil Wanatka built Little Bohemia in 1929; five years later it held the Dillinger gang for two nights and became a museum of the fight, still serving dinner. Agents reached it by flying to Rhinelander and driving through the night in whatever cars they could take, some standing on the running boards. Hobart Hermanson, ex-bootlegger (unverified — V2_R02), kept an inn at Lake Como near Lake Geneva where Nelson had wintered before; in November 1934 Hermanson himself told the Bureau Nelson was coming back, and three agents lived in his house for nearly three weeks until the afternoon the Ford V-8 drove up. Mercer, farther north, was Ralph Capone's town — Al's brother is listed among its notable residents — and the whole Iron–Vilas county belt of resorts was the Outfit's summer country. The lodge has its ghost stories (a Northwoods News feature asks whether the CCC worker haunts it — folklore, labelled). The odd detail: The Bureau's stakeout at Lake Como lost Nelson because its only car had gone for groceries; Little Bohemia's owner had to be tipped into talking by his wife with a note in a cigarette pack.",
