      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Chicago Board of Education",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Builders Building, La Salle Street (board meetings)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Chicago%20Board%20of%20Education%2C%20Builders%20Building%2C%20La%20Salle%20Street"
        },
        {
          "name": "every public school in the city",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=every%20public%20school%20in%20the%20city%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The finding aid of the Chicago Teachers' Federation records at the Chicago History Museum (as quoted in search results; page itself returned 403) describes the \"payless paydays\" of 1929–1934, \"when teachers' salary checks were distributed on schedule on only nine of 46 paydays,\" and when \"scrip or tax anticipation warrants were frequently tendered to teachers in lieu of cash.\" The Board began issuing scrip (IOUs) in 1931; many businesses refused it or took it only at a discount; the teachers' union won a court injunction against scrip, and the Board answered with outright payless paydays. Between January 1931 and May 1933 teachers received their monthly salary only three times. By April 1933 the city's roughly 14,000 teachers had \"received only two weeks' salary in cash since last June\"; the average teacher was owed six to eight months' back pay. The warrants were redeemable only when property owners paid their taxes — which, during the tax strike (see Part 2), they were not doing. The odd detail: The money was not \"gone\" — it was locked up. Tax-anticipation warrants were a promissory note against taxes the city's richest property owners were refusing to pay, and the Loop banks, which held the city's paper and the owners' accounts, declined to buy more of it at face value. The teachers concluded, correctly, that the bankers and the tax strikers were the same people.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the overall shape; Medium for \"nine of 46 paydays\" (seen in the Chicago History Museum finding-aid text via search, page not loadable).",
