      "summary": "Roy Elonza Davis was a founding member of the second Ku Klux Klan in 1915, co-wrote its 1921 constitution, bylaws and rituals, and was expelled in 1923. Around 1930 he moved to Jeffersonville and founded the First Pentecostal Baptist Church of God, holding healing revivals and, from October 1933, a radio broadcast. In 1929 he baptised and ordained a young William Branham, calling him his \"Saint Timothy.\" When Davis's church burned in March–April 1934, Branham took over the congregation; by November 1936 it was registered as the Billie Branham Pentecostal Tabernacle. Branham's followers later said that at a June 11, 1933 river baptism a bright light descended and a voice spoke over him — though no newspaper covered it. Davis was arrested at least 17 times between 1916 and 1961 (fraud, forgery, a 1930 Mann Act charge, cross burning); he became Imperial Wizard of the Original Knights of the KKK (1959–64) and died August 12, 1966. The odd detail: the man who wrote the Klan's rituals ordained the most famous American faith healer of the next generation — and his church burned the year the young man took it over.",
