      "summary": "Martin, corporate secretary of the Larkin soap-and-mail-order company, hired Wright to design the Larkin Administration Building, then his own house (built 1903–05) and his lakeside summer place Graycliff. He had been Wright's chief patron and lender; a search-result summary says he lent Wright over $70,000 that was never repaid. In the 1929 crash \"overnight, he lost almost everything.\" After several strokes he died on 17 December 1935, aged 70. His family abandoned the house in 1937; the city took it in 1946, the Catholic Diocese bought it in 1951, and it stood vacant for nearly two decades. The Blue Sky Mausoleum that Wright designed for Martin's grave in Forest Lawn was not built until 2004. The odd detail: When Wright's autobiography appeared, Martin — once a millionaire — is said to have been too poor to buy the $6 copy; and his wife Isabelle was nearly blind in the house built around light.",
