      "summary": "On the eve of the Depression Chicago had the Midwest's largest Mexican community, 25,000+ (Chicago sources via search summary; Wikipedia gives ~20,000 in the 1920s falling to ~14,000 by 1940; one summary says a ~75 percent drop — the sources conflict). El Nacional (20 Feb 1932) reported more Mexicans leaving the US than entering in the 22 months to Oct 1931. But El Nacional also reported on 7 May 1932 that a railroad would hire 1,500 day laborers for track repair and on 17 Feb 1934 that the Chicago & North Western was about to hire \"hundreds of Mexican workers.\" Ernest Burgess began a 1933 household survey (\"Mexicans on relief\"): 939 households and 4,272 people were censused and 42 families interviewed (per Arias & Durand). By Jan 1936 La Defensa said Cook County social agencies had paid the fares of \"many Mexican families\" to the border (R15). Also earlier: México (23 Jan 1929) wrote a column on the border patrol's deportation practices, and on 17 Aug 1929 reported a federal raid in the Calumet district that netted prisoners facing deportation. The odd detail: the railroads that had recruited the colony in the boom were hiring track gangs again as early as spring 1932, while the same paper carried the consul's departure fares.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (figures conflict; the survey's results were not found). • BENDS in destination and in the 1924 Johnson–Reed quota law, whose existence in the setting is UNVERIFIED for this file.",
