      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Chicago",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "South Chicago, Gary and East Chicago (Indiana Harbor), Detroit",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Chicago%2C%20South%20Chicago%2C%20Gary%20and%20East%20Chicago%20%2C%20Detroit"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Nationally ~82,000 Mexicans were formally deported 1929–39 and 300,000 to 2 million \"repatriated,\" peaking in 1931; up to a third of US Mexicans had gone by 1934. The Midwest held ~3% of US Mexicans but supplied ~10% of repatriates. In Gary in 1931 people took reduced-rate train tickets home; by 1932 \"repressive measures\" forced \"reluctant voyagers\"; in Detroit a man told his consul police had \"dragged\" him to the train. In Chicago the paper México protested as early as 17 Nov 1929 that honest workers \"are not exiles or outcasts\" and \"repatriation is for the outcasts\" — \"Down with the hypocrites and exploiters!\" By Jan 1936 La Defensa reported that \"since the depression the Cook County Social Agencies have paid the expenses of many Mexican families to the Mexican Border,\" and that Mexico had budgeted ~2 million pesos to take them onward. The odd detail: Cook County relief bought one-way tickets to the border as a cheaper alternative to relief.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (national figures are ranges).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the repatriation trains are where people vanish: the Golden Circle — whose map centres on Havana and the Gulf — uses the county's one-way tickets to move bodies, and nobody counts who arrived.",
