      "id": "R28-018",
      "title": "Blanche Noyes — Ohio's first licensed woman pilot; the 1936 Bendix; a husband dead in the snow",
      "date_start": "1929",
      "date_end": "1936",
      "date_label": "1929–1936",
      "category": "Women of 1930s Chicago and the Great Lakes",
      "tags": [
        "Women",
        "Chicago",
        "Blanche Noyes",
        "Ohio's",
        "Bendix"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Blanche Noyes",
        "Dewey Noyes",
        "Louise Thaden"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Cleveland",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(born there 23 June 1900; the Cleveland Air Races terminus)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Cleveland"
        },
        {
          "name": "Washington from August 1936",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Washington%20from%20August%201936"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "An actress until she married the pilot Dewey Noyes in 1928, Blanche soloed after four hours' instruction and took her licence in June 1929 — the first woman licensed in Ohio. In the first Women's Air Derby that August (Santa Monica to Cleveland, twenty pilots) her aeroplane caught fire in the air near Pecos; she landed, put the fire out, patched the ship and finished fourth in the heavy class. Dewey Noyes was killed on 11 December 1935 when his plane went down in fog and snow. Nine months later, on 4 September 1936, Noyes and Louise Thaden won the Bendix Trophy — the first year women were allowed to race the men — New York to Los Angeles in 14 hours 55 minutes in a Beechcraft Staggerwing. That August she had joined the Bureau of Air Commerce's Air Marking Group, painting town names on rooftops at 15-mile intervals for pilots who flew by looking down; for years, the National Air and Space Museum notes, she was the only woman pilot allowed to fly a government aircraft. The odd detail: Air marking — a government woman flying across the Midwest making towns paint their names on their roofs — is a perfect cover for looking at everything from above.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A Section 13 asset with a federal aeroplane and a reason to overfly every county in Ohio and Michigan: \"Just marking the roofs, sheriff.\" The Black Legion counties are on her route.",
