      "summary": "A local-history map of Peoria's mayhem lists: Officer Arthur Aiello killed on 29 August 1929 at Spring and Adams, in the city's first open touring police car; the 13 November 1933 \"big shoot-out\" at Larson's barber shop, where Detective Robert Moran and Guy West were killed and a customer wounded — the same shoot-out R17 tells from the Klutas counterfeiting side (Russell Hughes drew, two detectives were wounded, Hughes died in R17's account; the blog names West and Moran as the dead — the two accounts differ on who died); and, in April 1936, a murder-suicide on the second floor of City Hall, where David Roe killed Wilma Roe and himself. The same source lists earlier Prohibition-era unsolved killings: a railroad detective named Gifford shot in a 1922 shoot-out, and a brewery guard named Szolds found shot in front of a closed brewery in 1922 with nothing stolen; and Constable Arthur Smith ambushed at Nebraska and Sterling, 12 June 1922. The odd detail: The first open police touring car in the city killed its first officer within the year, and the 1936 murder-suicide happened in the very building where the gambling-house permits were argued.",
