      "summary": "After the St. Valentine's Day Massacre (14 Feb 1929) the coroner's jury foreman, Burt A. Massee (Palmolive money), paid for a private crime laboratory attached to Northwestern's law school — \"the first institution of its kind in the United States.\" Calvin Goddard, the Army-officer ballistics expert who had proved the massacre guns were not police Thompsons, ran it from 1929 (director as of 1932; he left c. 1933–34). Departments: firearms, chemistry/toxicology, document examination, photography, and a \"psychology\" department under Leonarde Keeler (the polygraph). It examined blood, bullets, fingerprints, handwriting, poisons, textiles and \"60+ other\" evidence types for police forces across the Midwest, and published The American Journal of Police Science from 1930 (merged into the Journal of Criminal Law and Criminology in 1932). In 1938 the City of Chicago bought the whole outfit for $25,000; it moved from 222 E. Superior to police headquarters, 1121 S. State, in 1941; Fred E. Inbau stayed on to train police. Facilities at the transfer: two chemical laboratories, a photographic darkroom, a polygraph chamber, a document examiner's room, a library of 1,000+ volumes, and an exhibits room with hundreds of weapons. The odd detail: the lab was private for nine years — paid for by a soap fortune, housed in a law school, and any sheriff in the Midwest could ship it a bullet. A player investigator in 1934 would go to Superior Street, not to a police station.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on founding, addresses, 1938 sale and price; Medium on Goddard's exact departure year (sources give 1933 or 1934). • UNVERIFIED this session",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The private lab is the perfect civilian door for Section 13 — an FBI field office can \"consult\" it without a police record. Put a locked drawer in the exhibits room: bullets that came back from the comparison microscope with marks no gun makes.",
