      "summary": "Dunham joined the University of Chicago's anthropology department in 1929 (Radcliffe-Brown, Sapir, Herskovits, Lloyd Warner, Malinowski among her teachers), studied ballet with Ludmilla Speranzeva from 1928, formed \"Ballets Nègres\" in 1931 (Negro Rhapsody at the Chicago Beaux Arts Ball), opened the Negro Dance Group school in 1933, danced La Guiablesse (1933–34) and was a guest with the Chicago Opera ballet 1934–36. Rosenwald and Guggenheim fellowships in 1935 took her to Jamaica (living in the Maroon village of Accompong), Martinique, Trinidad and Haiti, where from early 1936 she studied Vodun rites and dances from inside; she came back to Chicago in spring 1936, took her Ph.B. in August 1936, wrote a master's thesis on the dances of Haiti (1938) but never finished the degree, took A Negro Dance Evening to the 92nd Street Y in 1937, and premiered L'Ag'Ya for the Federal Theatre Project on 27 January 1938 with designer John Pratt, whom she later married. The odd detail: A University of Chicago anthropology student, on Sears money, initiated into Haitian Vodou in 1936 (unverified — V_R14) and back on the South Side by spring — dancing what she had learned in a federal theatre two years later.",
