      "summary": "Theodore Roosevelt Jr. was Governor of Puerto Rico from 9 September 1929 to 30 January 1932 (Hoover's appointee). He worked at Depression relief, tried to bring in manufacturing and philanthropy, was the first governor to try to learn Spanish (\"20 words a day\"), appointed Carlos E. Chardón the first Puerto Rican chancellor of the university in 1931, and was nicknamed \"El Jíbaro de La Fortaleza\" (\"the Hillbilly of the Governor's Mansion\"). He then served as Governor-General of the Philippines (29 Feb 1932–15 July 1933; \"One Shot Teddy\" after a tamaraw hunt). Blanton Winship (born Macon, Georgia, 1869; Judge Advocate General of the Army 1931–33; Spanish War, Villa Expedition and WWI veteran) governed from 5 February 1934 to 25 June 1939 (removed by FDR, 12 May 1939, after Marcantonio's charges). On 25 July 1938, at a parade marking the 40th anniversary of the 1898 landing, Ángel Esteban Antongiorgi fired on him and was killed by police; an officer also died (Wikipedia says two died, 36 wounded in the stampede). The odd detail: the first American governor to study Spanish is the son of the president who founded the Hunter Guild — and in 1929 he was away for most of the year on a zoological hunt with his brother Kermit (\"first Westerner known to have shot a panda\").",
