      "summary": "During the Depression, some 300,000 to 2 million people (estimates vary widely; Wikipedia says most scholarly estimates run 500,000 to 1 million) were deported or pressured to \"repatriate\" to Mexico — an estimated 40–60% of the voluntary returnees were U.S. citizens, mostly American-born children. Only about 82,000 were formally deported; the rest left under threat, welfare pressure and raids. President Hoover's Secretary of Labor William N. Doak pushed for more agents; the peak year was 1931, and rates fell under Roosevelt. Los Angeles, with the largest Mexican population outside Mexico, staged raids by federal, sheriff's and city police, and put people on trains. Mexico itself paid for the return of over 90,000 between July 1930 and June 1931 and tried to found farm colonies for returnees. By 1934 roughly one-third of the Mexicans in the U.S. had gone. California formally apologised in 2005; Los Angeles County in 2012, with a memorial. The odd detail: roughly half the \"Mexicans\" sent to Mexico were American citizens who had never seen it — and some were sent from the Midwest in special trains funded by the steel companies that had employed them (see next entry).",
