      "summary": "The North Side gang \"never fully recovered\" after February 1929. Moran broke the code of silence by blaming Capone publicly, kept a shrinking hold into the early 1930s, and fell back on petty crime — mail fraud and robbery. On April 30, 1939 he was convicted of conspiring to cash $62,000 of stolen American Express cheques; by the 1940s he was \"almost penniless … only 17 years after being one of the richest gangsters in Chicago\". Arrested in 1946 for a 1945 tavern robbery in Dayton, Ohio (20 years), then re-arrested on release in 1956 for a 1945 bank robbery in Ansonia, Ohio (10 more), he died of lung cancer in Leavenworth on February 25, 1957. The odd detail: The man the Massacre was meant for outlived almost everyone involved — and ended as a small-town tavern robber in Ohio.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Moran went around the corner for coffee on February 14, 1929. In the setting he knows why he was late — someone told him to be — and he has spent ten years waiting for that someone to collect.",
