      "summary": "Chicago flushed its sewage away from the lake by reversing the river and pulling Lake Michigan water down the Sanitary and Ship Canal. Wisconsin and the other lake states sued, saying the diversion was lowering the lakes and hurting their harbours. On 14 January 1929, in Wisconsin v. Illinois, a unanimous Supreme Court held that federal equity could force a state to take positive action to stop harming others, and a special master set limits on the diversion — the decree that pushed the Sanitary District into its great treatment-plant building programme of the Depression years. The District had committed itself to building treatment plants by a 1919 ordinance and finished its first plant in 1922; Edward Kelly was its chief engineer in the 1920s, and Pat Nash's firm earned $14 million from it (see Kelly–Nash). The odd detail: The city's great 1930s political machine grew out of the one agency that controlled where Chicago's filth went — and a federal court was watching how much lake water it drank.",
