      "summary": "After the death of \"Mother\" Della Hedgepath, Clarence H. Cobbs gathered a congregation in his mother's house, then a State Street storefront in May 1929, then a brick building at 4315 S. Wabash in 1933. It was a Spiritualist church — part of the Black Spiritual church movement — and from 1935 broadcast on WSBC; its \"Midnight Broadcast\" set a pattern later religious shows followed. In 1939 Walter T. Bailey (Illinois's first licensed Black architect) designed its new building, and gospel composer Kenneth Morris persuaded Cobbs to install a Hammond organ — \"the most unusual thing you ever heard,\" which drew visitors across the city. By the early 1940s it had 9,000+ members. The odd detail: A Spiritualist church broadcasting at midnight — the séance tradition put on the radio in Bronzeville.",
