      "summary": "The sweep's other files already hold the decade's women victims and the killers who preyed on women; this entry indexes them so a GM does not hunt. Lonely-hearts and matrimonial killers: Harry Powers of Quiet Dell and the Park Ridge family (R06); Johann Hoch, Chicago's Bluebeard (R06, living memory); Belle Gunness of La Porte (R06, living memory). Women murdered and unidentified: Cleveland's \"Lady of the Lake\" (5 September 1934) and the Torso victims, Rose Wallace among them (R06). Taken: Mary McElroy of Kansas City (R02); two-year-old Mary Agnes Moroney, Chicago, 15 May 1930, unsolved (R06); Elsie Paroubek, 1911 (above). Killed by a doctor's hand: Rheta Wynekoop on the operating table, 21 November 1933 (R06). Women who killed and the courts that judged them: Beulah Annan and Murderess Row (R06), the Torso Murderesses Dunkel and Smith (R06), Mildred Bolton (R06), Eleanor Jarman (R06), Tillie Klimek (R06), Anna Marie Hahn (R06), Marie Porter (above). What the record does not show: Wikipedia's lists of unsolved murders and of mysterious disappearances contain no woman vanished or killed unsolved in the Great Lakes states between 1929 and 1939 (both lists were read this session with that question) — the famous 1930s disappearances are elsewhere (New York's Agnes Tufverson, a Michigan-born lawyer who vanished in December 1933 after marrying a Yugoslav officer, is the nearest; UNVERIFIED, no page reached). The gap is itself usable: the region's missing women of the decade are in the newspapers, not the encyclopedias. The odd detail: Of the two women executed in America in 1938, both were Great Lakes cases; of the famous vanished women of the decade, none was.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the cross-references; the negative result is only as good as the lists. • New York's Agnes Tufverson, a Michigan-born lawyer who vanished in December 1933 after marrying a Yugoslav officer, is the nearest; UNVERIFIED, no page reached",
