      "summary": "Mexico's great revolutionary education minister (1921–24) ran for president in 1929 against the Calles machine, lost to Pascual Ortiz Rubio in a disputed election, and left the country again. Over the following decade he turned to a militant Catholic conservatism — he called the exiled Bishop Manríquez y Zárate \"the standard-bearer\" of the persecuted faith — and in 1940 edited the magazine Timón, writing that Hitler, \"although he disposes of absolute power, finds himself a thousand leagues from Caesarism.\" Wikipedia lists him among Mexican collaborators with Nazi Germany (the claim that Timón was German-funded is widely repeated but not confirmed this session). The odd detail: the same man bridges the revolution, the Cristero bishops and Berlin.",
