      "summary": "Born in New Orleans on 7 October 1891, brought to Chicago in 1894 by his Pullman-porter father, raised in a white Englewood neighbourhood, Motley graduated from the Art Institute in 1918, married his high-school sweetheart Edith Granzo in 1924 (her German immigrant parents disowned her), won the Harmon award in 1928 and the first one-man show by a Black artist in New York (22 of 26 paintings sold), and a Guggenheim in 1929 for a year in Paris, where he studied the Louvre's masters rather than African art. In the 1930s he turned to the night streets and clubs of Bronzeville in a hot modernist palette and worked for the WPA on murals. (From memory, UNVERIFIED this session: Black Belt, 1934; Saturday Night; The Picnic; his objection to being called a \"Negro artist.\") The odd detail: The painter of Bronzeville's nightlife grew up among white neighbours in Englewood, married a German girl, and learned his colour in the Louvre.",
