      "summary": "The 1885 Board of Trade (W. W. Boyington; a 320-ft clock tower with a 4,500-lb bell, removed in 1895) stood on caissons in muck and was \"rendered structurally unsound in the 1920s when construction began across the street on the Federal Reserve Bank\"; it was demolished in 1929 and the 605-ft Art Deco Board of Trade opened on 9 June 1930. Through the Depression, owners pulled down tall old buildings that no longer paid their taxes and put up one- or two-storey \"taxpayers\" — shops whose rents covered the tax bill. The Masonic Temple (1892, 21 storeys) went in 1939, replaced by a two-storey taxpayer with a Walgreens; D. H. Burnham's Great Northern Hotel (1892, 16 storeys, 500 rooms, one of the city's first fireproof hotels) went in 1940, replaced by a single-storey taxpayer. The odd detail: Depression Chicago got shorter: some of the most famous towers of the 1890s were cut down to shopfronts to save on the tax bill.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A Loop address that was a sixteen-storey hotel in 1938 and a cigar store in 1940 — the upper floors are gone, but the old basement and sub-basement remain under the new shop, and nobody catalogued what was left down there.",
