      "summary": "Augusta Lenska, a Russian-born mezzo-contralto of the Chicago Civic Opera and the Chicago Symphony in the 1910s and 1920s (the New York Times praised her dramatic ability in 1927), lost her life savings in 1929 in worthless stock of Samuel Insull's empire. On 23 February 1933 she was struck by a streetcar in Chicago in what was suspected to be a suicide attempt; the papers gave her age as 51. By the 1940 census she was an inmate of Kankakee State Hospital, and she was still there in 1950. The odd detail: Insull built the Civic Opera House; his stock ruined one of its singers; she ended in an asylum whose wards had tunnels for steam.",
