      "summary": "Roe was born 26 August 1898 in Galliano, Louisiana, raised in Little Rock, and came to Chicago at 31 (c. 1929) to work for tailor-turned-policy-king Ed Jones. Through the 1930s he rose to be the Joneses' operating partner. When the Outfit kidnapped Ed Jones in 1946 Roe paid the $100,000 ransom, then refused to hand over his own share; offered $250,000 by Giancana to quit, he answered \"I'll die first.\" (unverified — V_R14) On 19 June 1951 he shot and killed Outfit man \"Fat Lenny\" Caifano during an attempt to kidnap him, was charged, and was acquitted after the evidence was suppressed. Already diagnosed with stomach cancer (1 August 1947), he was shotgunned outside his home at about 10 p.m. on 4 August 1952; Leonard Patrick, David Yaras and William Block were arrested for it. His funeral drew an 81-car procession, the largest for a Black Chicagoan since Jack Johnson's in 1946. The odd detail: His partner Robert Wilkins ran a welding shop that manufactured the policy wheels for the Belmont and \"Old Reliable\" books — and was murdered in mid-September 1946, a week after an attempt on Roe.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (the two sources agree on dates and addresses). • offered $250,000 by Giancana to quit, he answered \"I'll die first.\" (unverified — V_R14) On 19 June 1951 he shot and killed Outfit man \"Fat Lenny\" Caifano during an attempt to kidnap him, was charged, and was acquitted after the evidence was suppressed.",
