      "summary": "Founded 1848 by the Rev. Richard K. Todd (from Vermont), renamed repeatedly (Woodstock University 1861 … Todd Seminary for Boys 1873, Todd School for Boys 1930). Headmaster Roger Hill (1929–54) ran it as a progressive school where boys were \"created creators\": a working farm, a radio station, a theatre company with touring buses, film production, and flight training with a simulator. Welles attended September 1926–1931 (ages 11–16), directing 8–10 plays a year (a Dr. Faustus, an experimental Everyman); the Todd troupe won first prize at the 1933 Century of Progress drama festival with Twelfth Night; Hill and Welles co-wrote Everybody's Shakespeare (1934–41, 100,000+ copies by 1942). Winter campus: Todd Island, Marathon Key, Florida; summer camp: Camp Tosebo, Onekama, Michigan. Closed 1954; only Rogers Hall survives (apartments). The odd detail: A boys' boarding school an hour from Chicago with its own radio station and its own airfield — boys could be flown in and out without passing a railway station. (Later alumni: physicist Robert R. Wilson; cartoonist Gahan Wilson.)",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A school that trains boys to broadcast, fly and perform is exactly what the Hunter Guild or FBI Section 13 would scout for recruits; Hill's radio station is a second transmitter in a town that already has a lead.",
