      "summary": "México (6 Feb 1929) reported a demonstration in Kenosha, Wisconsin by Spanish-speaking workers from Chicago, Milwaukee, Racine and nearby, over the assassination of the labour organiser Julio Antonio Mella in Mexico City (killed 10 Jan 1929 — general knowledge, not read this session). Marchers arrived on foot, in cars and trucks, carrying signs attacking U.S. imperialism, paraded through the streets while residents cheered, and blamed \"the governor of Cuba and his Wall Street advisers\" for the murder; the paper noted the march was \"more effective and luckier than the last one,\" not stopped by local police. Discrepancy: the listing's headline calls the victim \"Julio Acosta,\" while the article text I fetched says Mella — I could not reconcile the two. The odd detail: a Cuban communist's murder in Mexico City drew a march through a Wisconsin lake town.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (the victim's name is inconsistent in the FLPS record).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a Havana-based conspiracy's map (the Golden Circle) meets a Chicago march; the Chicago–Milwaukee–Racine network that organised it is a ready-made roster of Cuban and Mexican exiles the Secret Service would want.",
