      "summary": "On February 17, 1929 — three days after the St. Valentine's Day Massacre — Capone was served in Florida with a subpoena to appear before a federal grand jury in Chicago, and did not come (the usual account is that he pleaded illness in Miami; the FBI's page on this was not reachable this session). On May 16, 1929, leaving a gangland conference in Atlantic City, he was arrested in Philadelphia for carrying a concealed weapon and given a year by Judge John E. Walsh; he was moved to Eastern State Penitentiary on August 8, 1929 and released in March 1930. On May 8, 1930 he was arrested in Miami on the orders of the mayor, city manager and public-safety director. On March 2, 1931 Judge Wilkerson sentenced him to six months in Cook County Jail for contempt of court over the ignored subpoena — the first federal sentence he ever received. At his July 30, 1931 plea hearing Wilkerson refused a proposed deal of two and a half years at Leavenworth. The odd detail: The first federal punishment the most notorious gangster in America ever got was for not answering a subpoena.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on dates; the Atlantic City conference as the reason for the Philadelphia trip is common knowledge but not checked this session (Medium). Chicagology's summary also gives the Eastern State term as \"May 17, 1928 – March 17, 1930\", an evident typo for 1929.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Capone went looking to be arrested in Philadelphia — a year in a Quaker penitentiary with thick stone walls was the safest place in America after the Massacre. The question for the GM is what he was hiding from that the Outfit's guns couldn't stop.",
