      "id": "R01-008",
      "title": "The St. Valentine's Day Massacre",
      "date_start": "1929-02-14",
      "date_end": "1929-02-14",
      "date_label": "February 14, 1929, about 10:30 a.m.",
      "category": "The Outfit and gangland Chicago, 1929–1939",
      "tags": [
        "gangland",
        "the Outfit",
        "St. Valentine's Day Massacre",
        "living memory"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Bugs Moran",
        "Frank Gusenberg",
        "Calvin Goddard",
        "Fred Burke",
        "Byron Bolton"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "SMC Cartage Company garage",
          "address": "2122 N. Clark Street",
          "geography": "2122 N. Clark Street, Lincoln Park",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=SMC%20Cartage%20Company%20garage%2C%202122%20N.%20Clark%20Street%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Four to six men — two dressed as police — arrived in a black Cadillac made up as a police car, lined seven men against the garage wall and fired about 70 rounds from Thompson guns and one shotgun blast. Dead: Albert Kachellek (alias James Clark, Moran's second and brother-in-law), Adam Heyer (bookkeeper), Albert Weinshank (cleaning-and-dyeing), Frank and Peter Gusenberg (enforcers), Reinhardt Schwimmer (optician turned gambler) and John May (mechanic). May's dog Highball's barking brought the neighbours. Frank Gusenberg, still alive when police arrived, told them \"No one shot me.\" Bugs Moran saw the \"police car\", took it for a raid and went round the corner for coffee. Calvin Goddard's ballistics work cleared the Chicago police's Thompsons. In December 1929 Fred Burke shot Patrolman Charles Skelly in St. Joseph, Michigan; police searching his house found two Thompsons identified as the massacre guns. Byron Bolton's 1935 confession named Fred Goetz, Gus Winkler, Fred Burke, Ray Nugent and Bob Carey. The garage was demolished in 1967; the wall's bricks went to a Canadian businessman and now the Mob Museum in Las Vegas. The odd detail: The massacre guns were found in Michigan because of a traffic accident and a dead patrolman, ten months later — and that same day Capone was sitting in a Florida prosecutor's office with a stenographer taking down every word.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. (The Miami hearing is dated Feb 14, 1929 in the report; the hour of the hearing is not stated, so it is a same-day alibi, not a same-hour one.)",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Already a Capone-era landmark in the setting. Proposal: the garage was chosen because of what the seven men were storing, not who they were — and the bricks were sold to a Canadian, off American soil, because someone knew what a wall that had seen that could hold.",
