      "summary": "Mexican workers were brought to South Chicago by Illinois Steel in 1923; Mundelein gave the mission to the Claretian Fathers in 1924. The Barcelona-born pastor Fr. James Tort, C.M.F., held the first solemn novena to St. Jude Thaddeus on 17 Feb 1929 — before the crash. As the mills laid men off, \"about 90% of his parishioners were without jobs,\" and the novenas to the patron of desperate causes drew thousands through the 1930s; the devotion spread nationally from this one South Chicago church. The odd detail: America's great devotion to the saint of lost causes began in a Mexican steel-town parish eight months before Black Tuesday — as if someone knew.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for date/founder; a Chicago police St. Jude League (commonly dated 1932) is UNVERIFIED here.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A congregation from the occupied south praying to the saint of hopeless causes, next to the blast furnaces — the right church for a Mexican-born IHS priest, and the right place for something that came north with the Southern War.",
