      "summary": "On 4 January 1930 the Mexican president-elect Pascual Ortiz Rubio arrived at Michigan Central, welcomed by \"thousands of sympathizers\"; with him came José María Puig Casauranc (governor of the Federal District), his private secretary Lt.-Col. Hernández and Rafael Quintanilla of the embassy; Consul Aveleyra met him; the Union League Club hosted a luncheon for the Chamber of Commerce with 35 business leaders; he visited the paper and toured the city, and was due to address the colony at Ashland Auditorium. The colony had already split into factions: in March 1929 two colonists left to join ex-General Escobar's rebellion, which México condemned as \"the product of treachery\"; a painter known as Gallito was called to Mexico City by the Secretary of War (23 March) and then reported captured at the border by rebels (26 March); a Comité Pro-Vasconcelos met at Hull House (June 1929); an Ortiz Rubio Committee formed in Chicago and Gary (Aug 1929). (Ortiz Rubio's inauguration and the attempt on his life on 5 Feb 1930 are general knowledge, not checked this session.) The odd detail: a painter nicknamed Gallito walked out of Halsted Street into a civil war.",
