      "summary": "On 17 Mar 1929 the Chief of Detectives' office broadcast a test call over WGN to three radio-equipped squads (\"To squads 1, 37A and 38A\"): one answered from Wentworth and 22nd in two minutes, one parked at Wieboldt's (School St. and N. Ashland) in thirty seconds, and the third had to walk five blocks to a phone. Col. McCormick's Tribune put up $10,000; Commissioner William F. Russell fitted the cars. The bulletins interrupted WGN's programmes \"several times each night,\" crowds beat the police to crime scenes, and criminals listened for their getaways. The City Council authorised a police-owned system on 10 Jul 1929; on 16 Jun 1930 the first police station opened, with Zenith's E. F. McDonald Jr. heading the technical committee. By the end of 1930 the department ran three stations (WPDB, WPDC, WPDD on 1714 kHz) with 96 radio cars reaching scenes in three to five minutes, and a centralised Complaint Room at headquarters. Two-way radio came only in 1939 (unverified — V_R13). The odd detail: for a year every Chicagoan with a radio could hear the police dispatch between dance numbers — and did.",
