      "id": "R37-018",
      "title": "The Battle of the Century",
      "date_start": "1929-03-07",
      "date_end": "1929-03-07",
      "date_label": "Whiting Community Center, 7 March 1929",
      "category": "Gary and the Calumet Region, 1906–1940: the steel towns and their communities",
      "tags": [
        "Calumet",
        "Region",
        "Battle",
        "Century",
        "BENDS"
      ],
      "people": [
        "John D. Rockefeller Jr.",
        "Robert W. Stewart",
        "Winthrop W. Aldrich",
        "Charles Evans Hughes",
        "Frank J. Hogan",
        "Harry Sinclair"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Whiting Memorial Community House",
          "address": "1938 Clark Street",
          "geography": "1938 Clark Street, Whiting, Indiana",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Whiting%20Memorial%20Community%20House%2C%201938%20Clark%20Street%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The annual stockholders' meeting of Standard Oil of Indiana was held at Whiting on 7 March 1929, and became a proxy war: John D. Rockefeller Jr. demanded the removal of chairman Colonel Robert W. Stewart, who had been implicated in two 1920s oil scandals — the Continental Trading Company (four oil men including Harry Sinclair bought Canadian oil at $1.50 a barrel, resold it to their own companies at $1.75 and pocketed over $8 million, distributed as Liberty Bonds) and Teapot Dome (bonds from that scheme turned up in the bribe to Interior Secretary Fall). Stewart was tried for contempt of the Senate and acquitted in June 1928, and was then also acquitted on a perjury charge (the local-society page's \"never charged\" is wrong; TIME, \"The Congress: Stewart Acquitted,\" https://time.com/archive/6777309/the-congress-stewart-acquitted/); under Senate questioning he said \"That is none of your business, Senator.\" About 58,000 stockholders held 9-plus million shares (the page gives the holder count and the shares outstanding, not a tally of votes cast); the community house was fitted with telegraph instruments and loudspeakers for 2,500 stockholders; police checked credentials at the doors and firemen stood by. Rockefeller was in Egypt; his brother-in-law Winthrop Aldrich led about 200 Eastern stockholders; Charles Evans Hughes was his counsel and Frank J. Hogan Stewart's. The Associated Press said it opened \"with all the solemnity of a church gathering.\" Stewart had about two-to-one support among individual holders, but Rockefeller's block of shares won; Stewart conceded: \"I'm afraid, my friends, that the 'noes' have it, if the vote is taken by shares.\" The local Gary Times saw Stewart \"surrounded by his own partisans\" facing \"an ominous group of silent, moneyed men.\" The odd detail: The great corporate coup of the decade was fought in a town's swimming-pool-and-bowling-alley community centre with loudspeakers strung through the rooms — and the man who won it did not attend, because he was in Egypt.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the outline (a single local-history article; figures as given).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — March 1929, three weeks after St. Valentine's Day (R01) and three days after Dawes leaves the presidency in the setting: a man who handled millions in secret oil bonds is voted out in a refinery-town gymnasium by an absent man's proxies. Who else held bonds from the Continental deal, and which of them did the Golden Circle or the Hunter Guild pay through? A Secret Service ledger (the service's real specialty was following bonds and bills, see R33) is the tool.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "wrhistoricalsociety.com: standard oils battle of the century",
          "url": "https://www.wrhistoricalsociety.com/standard-oils-battle-of-the-century",
          "publisher": "wrhistoricalsociety.com",
          "kind": "web page cited in the 2026-09-26 sweep",
          "supports": "read"
        },
        {
          "title": "Whiting Memorial Community House — Wikipedia",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whiting_Memorial_Community_House",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "kind": "encyclopedia article (follow its citations for strong claims)",
          "supports": "read"
        }
      ],
