      "id": "R15-011",
      "title": "Dr. Louis E. Schmidt expelled for treating the poor cheaply",
      "date_start": "1929-04",
      "date_end": "1929-04",
      "date_label": "April 1929",
      "category": "Immigrant and Ethnic Chicago in the 1930s",
      "tags": [
        "ethnic Chicago",
        "immigrants",
        "Dr. Louis E. Schmidt",
        "expelled",
        "treating"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Dr. Louis E. Schmidt",
        "Dr. Herman N. Bundesen"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Chicago Medical Society meeting",
          "address": "185 N. Wabash Ave.",
          "geography": "185 N. Wabash Ave. (behind locked doors)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Chicago%20Medical%20Society%20meeting%2C%20185%20N.%20Wabash%20Ave"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The Chicago Medical Society moved to expel urologist Dr. Louis Ernst Schmidt, about 60, president of the Public Health Institute and the Illinois Hygiene League, for \"unethical conduct\": his institute advertised in newspapers and treated patients for a nominal fee or free. Several hundred of the Society's 4,500 members attended; reporters listened through the door. Health Commissioner Dr. Herman Bundesen stood with him (\"Solidarity … between Doctor Bundesen and Doctor Schmidt,\" 11 Apr 1929). The odd detail: the German press called him \"world famous\" and cast the expulsion as doctors protecting their fees; the institute was a venereal-disease clinic (the Public Health Institute) — the city's discreet door.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the expulsion move; \"VD clinic\" characterisation is from general knowledge of the Public Health Institute — Medium.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a cheap, advertised clinic that asks no questions is where a vampire's victims go for \"anaemia\" — Dr. Schmidt's records are an investigator's lead.",
