      "summary": "Founded 1898 — \"the first athletic club for women in the United States\" — the club opened Philip B. Maher's seven-storey French-style building on April 24, 1929 (Tribune, April 23, 1929: \"opens its doors tomorrow with reception for the membership\"): a Georgian living room the width of the second floor, a swimming pool, \"modernistic\" dining rooms, a top-floor ballroom, a library with a Lely portrait, a card room, a \"personal service\" floor of massage, shampoo and manicure, and bedrooms so suburban members could stay in town. The design committee were Mrs. Hudson (chairman), Mrs. Brower, Mrs. William Sherman, Mrs. Frank P. Graves, Mrs. Nelson Pelouze and Mrs. M. Clement. It was thus six months old when the market broke. The odd detail: The North Shore's wives kept bedrooms above Michigan Avenue for the nights the last train had gone — a society women's house in town that husbands could not enter.",
