      "summary": "US Ambassador Dwight Morrow (a J. P. Morgan partner) brokered the arreglos (\"arrangements\") between President Emilio Portes Gil and the bishops, signed 21 June 1929: worship resumed, the laws stayed on the books. The war had cost an estimated 90,000 dead (56,882 federal soldiers, ~30,000 Cristeros, plus civilians; one estimate 250,000). Cristero general Enrique Gorostieta, a retired Federal officer hired by the League, was killed on 2 June 1929, three weeks before the peace. After it, the government executed roughly 500 Cristero leaders and 5,000 other Cristeros despite the truce. North of the border the Knights of Columbus had raised over $1 million for Mexican Catholic exiles, circulated 5 million leaflets, and lobbied President Coolidge for intervention. The odd detail: Morrow's daughter Anne met Charles Lindbergh in Mexico City when he flew there in December 1927 — the peace-broker's house is where America's most famous marriage began (LEAD: confirm).",
