      "summary": "The New York Central opened Buffalo Central Terminal on 22 June 1929 with 2,200 guests — a $14 million Art Deco station by Fellheimer & Wagner with a 17-storey tower and a 225-ft concourse, built out east to relieve downtown crossings and designed for 3,200 passengers an hour and 400 daily train trips (about 200 trains called there at the opening); it never reached its projections. Two years later the city finished its 32-storey, 398-ft City Hall by Dietel, Wade & Jones (completed 10 November 1931; dedicated 1 July 1932 for the centennial), at $6,851,546.85 one of the costliest in the nation. Architect John Wade called its style \"Americanesque\" and spoke of Babylonian influences; its columns are carved as giant octagonal nuts with rivet heads and the lintel as a saw, with friezes by Albert Stewart and sculpture by René Paul Chambellan, and bands of American Indian motifs interrupted by stone eagles. The odd detail: A station built for a boom that never came, and a City Hall its architect called Babylonian.",
