      "summary": "Twenty women pilots (18 American, 2 foreign; 40 of 70 applicants had qualified with 100 solo hours) took off on 18–19 August 1929 for the first women's cross-country race. Marvel Crosson crashed in the Gila River valley and was killed on 19 August, apparently poisoned by carbon monoxide; Claire Fahy withdrew when her wing wires were found eaten through — possibly by acid, and sabotage was whispered; Blanche Noyes (above) put out a fire in the air. Will Rogers called it the \"Powder Puff Derby.\" Louise Thaden won the heavy class in 20 hours 19 minutes 4 seconds on 26 August, and an estimated 18,000 people met the fliers at Cleveland. Out of the race came the Ninety-Nines, the women pilots' organisation (founded that November; detail from general knowledge, UNVERIFIED here). (unverified — V_R28) The odd detail: Acid-eaten wing wires on a woman's aeroplane in a race the press treated as a joke.",
