      "summary": "The villagers protested when the government replaced their patronal feast with a secular \"Great Yucca Fair.\" On 28 August 1929 about thirty state militiamen fired on them. The DHIAL dictionary says 22 people were killed and nine more shot on the spot, twelve arrested (the article's own figures are not reconciled); the church was burned afterwards and houses were destroyed (DHIAL: a reprisal by the state force). Context the DHIAL text adds: the fair was run by the Liga Central de Resistencia, and in DHIAL's own hedged account (\"parece ser\") some villagers shouting \"Viva Cristo Rey\" attacked the federal forces first, wounding three police, before the militia fired; the people fled into the church. The odd detail: the state replaced the saint's-day fair with a fair for a root vegetable.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-Low (one Church-partisan source; toll unreconciled).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — cult versus population reversed: the state is the small fanatic group. A feast day replaced by an agricultural fair is a perfect period detail; the GM's question is what the saint's day was for.",
