      "summary": "Out of office since the May 1929 election, Churchill was crossing North America with his brother Jack, his son Randolph and his nephew Johnny, riding in a private rail car provided by the financier Bernard Baruch. In Chicago on 2–4 October he stayed at the Drake; P. D. Armour took him through the stockyards; he saw his first prize fight at the Coliseum, toured the Gary steel mills and one of Samuel Insull's power plants, and addressed the Commercial Club at the Blackstone before 300 people. Col. Robert R. McCormick was in New York on business, so he had the Tribune cartoonist John T. McCutcheon host a lunch with the editors and take the Churchills through the Tribune building. Baruch attended and went on with Churchill to New York — where, three weeks later, the market broke. The odd detail: The future Prime Minister's first prize fight was in the Coliseum, the hall built out of the walls of Libby Prison (cross-ref R16) — and his host for the day was Insull, whose empire would be in ruins within three years.",
