      "summary": "Captain Robert H. McKay took the rail-car ferry Milwaukee out into a 37-mph gale carrying 27 railcars of lumber, perishable food, bathtubs and Nash automobiles. Cars broke loose, the sea gate at the stern was bent in and the stern flooded. All 52 died; 15 bodies were recovered, one man's watch stopped at 9:35. A message case found on 27 October read, in part: \"The ship is making water fast … sea gate is bent in and can't keep the water out … Seas are tremendous. Things look bad.\" The wreck was found in April 1972. The odd detail: The last cargo manifest reads like a joke — bathtubs and Nash cars — and the captain sailed into a gale anyway, a week before the crash.",
