      "summary": "The Grand Trunk train ferry Milwaukee (built 1902) sailed for Michigan at about 2 p.m. on 22 Oct 1929 into a gale with 27 railcars aboard — lumber, perishables, bathtubs and Nash automobiles. She had no radio. Loose cars smashed the stern sea gate and water came in; Captain Robert H. McKay turned back for Milwaukee and never arrived. All 52 men were lost. A message case later recovered read: \"The ship is making water fast. We have turned around and headed for Milwaukee. Pumps are working, but sea gate is bent in and can't keep the water out.\" The same autumn took the Andaste (9 Oct) and the Senator (31 Oct 1929) — the week of the Crash. The odd detail: a ship full of new automobiles sinking in the week the stock market died, with no radio to call for help — the last words in a brass case.",
