      "id": "M04-012",
      "title": "Marchaterre — \"À bas la misère\"",
      "date_start": "1929-12-06",
      "date_end": "1929-12-06",
      "date_label": "6 December 1929",
      "category": "Haiti and the Dominican Republic under American occupation, 1915–1940",
      "tags": [
        "Haiti",
        "Dominican",
        "Marchaterre"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Herbert Hoover",
        "W. Cameron Forbes",
        "Louis Borno",
        "Sténio Vincent"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Marchaterre",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "near Les Cayes, southern Haiti",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Marchaterre%2C%20near%20Les%20Cayes%2C%20southern%20Haiti%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "It began on 31 October 1929 as a strike at the agricultural school at Damien over cuts to scholarships for Haitians, and widened into strikes against censorship, customs, taxes and hardship. On 6 December 1929 about 1,500 peasants marched, chanting \"À bas la misère\" (\"down with misery\") and protesting the arrest of three leaders. Marines with machine guns blocked the road; after stones were thrown, they fired. 12 to 22 Haitians were killed and 21 to 51 wounded (sources vary). Hoover ordered an inquiry the next day; the Forbes Commission arrived on 28 February 1930, called the occupation a failure, and set in motion the 1930 elections that made Sténio Vincent president and the withdrawal agreement of 7 August 1933. The odd detail: a students' scholarship strike at an agricultural school is the thread that pulls down the occupation — in the real record.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on date and place; casualty range as given.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Marchaterre as an annual date the Garde watches; the dead of 1929 as a grievance a cult can recruit on — the setting's \"cult versus population\": the population marches, the cult waits.",
