      "summary": "In 1930 James J. Gentry, theatre editor of Anthony Overton's Chicago Bee, coined \"Bronzeville\" for the district the city called the \"Black Belt\" (and worse), after the bronze skin of its residents; the Defender took the name up and ran an annual \"Mayor of Bronzeville\" contest (from memory — UNVERIFIED this session). The district's core, Douglas, counted 50,285 people in 1930 and 53,124 in 1940; the wider Black Belt ran from Cermak Road (22nd) south toward 60th, between State and the lake, and by the 1930s held most of the city's Black population — a narrow seven-mile strip. The Ida B. Wells-Barnett House stands at 3624 South King Drive; Forum Hall (1897, Samuel Atwater Treat) on 43rd Street was a meeting hall. The odd detail: The name was a newspaperman's act of rebranding in the first year of the Depression — the neighbourhood renamed itself in the Bee while its banks were failing on the same block.",
