      "summary": "David Adler (1882–1949), the North Shore's society architect, designed 45 country houses in his career, 27 in the Chicago area, working with Henry Dangler until Dangler's death in 1917, then with Robert Work, then alone after an honorary licence in 1928; his sister Frances Adler Elkins decorated many of them. He was widowed in 1930 when his wife was killed in a car accident in Europe. The Lester Armour house (Lake Bluff, 1931) and the Reed and Clow houses date from the Depression years — the North Shore was still building palaces while the Loop's banks closed. The odd detail: The decade's great North Shore houses were built during the Depression by a widower whose wife died on a French road (unverified — V_R19) the year after the crash.",
