      "summary": "The Abendpost in 1930 listed the city's German monuments. Schiller (Wilhelm Pelargus, Stuttgart; gift of the Schwabenverein), \"the first one erected in Chicago in honor of a German,\" presented 15 May 1886 — rescheduled from 9 May because of the Haymarket bomb of 4 May. Beethoven bust (Johannes Gelert; given by the musician Carl Wolfsohn), 19 June 1887. Alexander von Humboldt (Felix Görling; ten-foot bronze given by the brewer F. J. Dewes), dedicated 16 Oct 1892 before 20,000. Fritz Reuter (Plattdeutsch poet; gift of the Low German societies), 14 May 1893. Altgeld (Gutzon Borglum), 6 Sept 1915. Theodore Thomas memorial (Albin Polasek), 24 Apr 1924. Lessing (Polasek; $25,000 from Henry Frank), Washington Park, 2 Nov 1930, with Henry Horner and Edward J. Kelly present (in 1930 a judge and the South Park Board president; the FLPS summary styles them by their later titles). The odd detail: the German community's first statue in Chicago was unveiled a week late because of an anarchist bomb thrown, in part, by Germans — and the police who cleared the Haymarket marched at the Goethe unveiling.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for dates and donors; Medium on the 1930 attendees' titles.",
