      "summary": "Milwaukee's Black population was under 900 in 1900, 1,500 in 1915, 2,200 in 1920 and 7,500 in 1930 — tiny beside Chicago's, packed onto the old Uihlein Hill. The Urban League and the NAACP branch both date from 1919; Wilbur and Ardie Halyard's Columbia Building & Loan gave the community its own mortgages; J. Anthony Josey moved his paper to Milwaukee in 1925 as the Wisconsin Enterprise-Blade; the Capuchins' St. Benedict the Moor mission (1908) was the Black Catholic parish. Walnut Street held hotels, funeral parlours, pool halls, taverns (the Gold Coast, the 700 Tap), doctors' and lawyers' offices; St. Mark AME (1869), Calvary Baptist (1890s), St. Matthew CME, Greater Galilee, storefront churches, the Booker T. Washington YMCA and the Prince Hall Masons. The Nation of Islam's Muhammad Mosque Number Three opened on McKinley Boulevard in 1935 — Milwaukee's temple was the third in the movement, after Detroit and Chicago. (unverified — V_R27) The odd detail: Temple No. 3 of Elijah Muhammad's movement was in Milwaukee, in a district of 7,500 people, a year after Fard vanished (1934).",
