      "summary": "Around 1930, \"a few weeks\" after Jewish lawyer Joseph Schachner moved in, a burning cross was placed in front of his home — likely by local Ku Klux Klan members, whose aim was to discourage both Catholics and Jews from moving to Oak Park. By the 1930s hundreds of Jewish families lived there anyway; in 1933 they formed the West Suburban Jewish Community Center, with Schachner as its first president. When the Center planned a permanent temple in 1939 it met resistance, and in the late 1930s the village passed an ordinance requiring the consent of 51% of residents within 400 feet of any new institutional building. The odd detail: The man whose lawn the Klan burned became the first president of the suburb's Jewish centre three years later.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High (a 2026 local-paper article drawing on Michael Zmora's research in the Chicago Jewish Historical Society Fall 2024 quarterly; the year is \"around 1930\"). • the lawyer's street address not given — UNVERIFIED",
