      "summary": "The 1930 Board of Trade tower placed a monumental aluminum Ceres over Chicago’s commodity markets. Architecturally it anchored the end of LaSalle Street; ornament throughout the building associated grain, agricultural labor and commerce. The Chicago Architecture Center identifies the statue’s faceless design, John Storrs as its sculptor, and Holabird and Root as architects. An observation deck once occupied the pyramidal roof, offering a legitimate public route into the upper reaches of the financial district. This is a strong real location for linking abstract prices to material food, harvests and deprivation, without making the classical decoration evidence of occult activity.",
