      "summary": "In nine months across 1930 and 1931 two anthropologists — Wendell C. Bennett (American Museum of Natural History) and Robert M. Zingg (still a Chicago doctoral student) — ran the University of Chicago Expedition of Tarahumara Ethnography; Zingg also surveyed and dug in the headwaters of the Urique and Batopilas rivers in spring 1931. The University of Chicago Press published The Tarahumara: An Indian Tribe of Northern Mexico in 1935; the expedition's collection is now at the Milwaukee Public Museum. It was one of the first modern studies of a northern Mexican Indian people. The odd detail: the standard 1930s English book on the sierra came from Chicago, printed by Chicago's own university press — the first book's city produced the north's ethnography.",
