      "summary": "In 1930 the 68-year-old anti-lynching crusader ran for the Illinois State Senate as an independent and lost to the Republican Adelbert Roberts. She died in Chicago on 25 March 1931 of uremia (unverified — V_R14), leaving her autobiography Crusade for Justice unfinished (her daughter Alfreda Duster published it in 1970), and was buried at Oak Woods beside her husband, the lawyer and editor Ferdinand Barnett. She had founded the Negro Fellowship League in 1908 — a reading room and shelter for young Black men the YMCA excluded, the first Black settlement house in Chicago. The Ida B. Wells Homes, the first public-housing project named for a woman of colour, followed at the end of the decade (Wikipedia: 1939; the project opened in 1941). The odd detail: She ran for office in the first year of the Depression and lost to a Black Republican — the old guard beating the older guard.",
