      "summary": "In 1929 Ernest Augustus, Duke of Brunswick, head of the House of Hanover, sold 82 pieces of the Guelph Treasure — the medieval church treasure of Brunswick Cathedral, gathered by the Welf dynasty — to four Frankfurt dealers (Saemy Rosenberg, Isaak Rosenbaum, Julius Falk Goldschmidt, Zacharias Hackenbroch) for 7.5 million Reichsmarks. The dealers toured it through the United States in 1930–31, and the Cleveland Museum of Art bought nine pieces, more than any other buyer. Among them: the Portable Altar of Countess Gertrude (c. 1045; gold, cloisonné enamel, porphyry, gems, pearls, niello over wood; accession 1931.462, gift of the John Huntington Art and Polytechnic Trust), inscribed \"Gertrude offers to Christ, to live joyfully in him, this stone that glistens with gems and gold.\" The treasure included arm reliquaries (one of St. Blaise), crosses and dome reliquaries. The 42 unsold pieces went in 1935 for 4.25 million Reichsmarks, in a deal in the Netherlands, to agents of Hermann Göring, and are now in Berlin's Bode Museum. The odd detail: The Gertrude altar has a trap door in its base that opens on small relics of saints wrapped in silk — a working reliquary, relics still inside, in a Cleveland museum from 1931.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
