      "summary": "Daniel A. Serritella, born August 29, 1898, was Chicago's Sealer of Weights and Measures under Thompson and a Republican state senator for the First District from 1930 to 1942, \"widely considered an ally of Al Capone, and one of his de facto representatives in both city and state government\". The Secret Six investigated his office and found \"hundreds of instances of 'short-weighting'\" costing Chicago consumers \"millions of dollars\"; in 1932 he and his deputy Harry Hochstein were convicted of taking bribes from merchants to let them rig their scales — $2,000 fines and a year in the county jail each — but the conviction was reversed on appeal and neither served a day. One account credits him with organising Capone's soup kitchen (see that entry). In 1953 he was declared mentally incompetent. The odd detail: The city official in charge of honest weights was convicted of selling crooked scales.",
