      "summary": "The Bee (founded 1925 by Anthony Overton; edited in turn by Chandler Owen, Ida B. Wells and Olive Diggs, with a mostly female staff; conservative, Republican, and — after first backing the Pullman porters — turned against them) had a theatre editor, James J. Gentry, who had run beauty contests since 1916. In 1930 he coined \"Bronzeville\" and the paper began an annual \"Mayor of Bronzeville\" election by ballot — a ceremonial mayor for the Black Belt, chosen by its own votes. When Gentry left in 1932 he took the idea to the Defender, which ran it for decades. The Bee died in 1947, a year after Overton. The odd detail: A city-within-the-city with its own elected, powerless mayor — chosen by newspaper coupon — from the first year of the Depression.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The fae understand elections by acclamation. Whoever is \"Mayor of Bronzeville\" this year holds a title the city itself gave — which is exactly the kind of title the Golden Circle would want to buy, or a vampire to hold.",
