      "summary": "Mark Hanna's daughter and Senator Medill McCormick's widow (he died 1925) won the state's at-large House seat in 1928 with 1,711,651 votes — more than any other member that year. In September 1929 she announced for the Senate, beat the incumbent Charles S. Deneen in the 1930 Republican primary 51 to 35 per cent (the first woman nominated for the Senate by a major party), and testified that the primary had cost her $252,572 of her own money plus relatives' funds — the spending became the issue, and she lost the general to Democrat J. Hamilton Lewis 64 to 31 in the crash's first election. In 1930 she bought three Rockford papers (Rockford Consolidated Newspapers, the Register-Republic and Morning Star); her model dairy farm at Byron \"remained open as other similar farms closed for being unprofitable.\" On March 9, 1932, she married Congressman Albert G. Simms and went to New Mexico. The odd detail: The Tribune family's widow bought a Senate nomination in public, lost, bought a newspaper chain, and married a congressman from the House floor — all inside three years.",
