      "summary": "From 1930 Machado ruled by terror: the student Rafael Trejo was killed in a protest on 30 September 1930; nine newspapers were closed by decree on 9 January 1931 and their editors arrested; and the government created the Porra (\"the bludgeon\") — a secret police and death squad (dated by some to 1932) under Col. Antonio Jiménez that beat, shot and \"disappeared\" opponents; the ley de fuga (\"shot while escaping\") was its signature. The ABC and student groups answered with bombs and assassinations. When the new US ambassador Sumner Welles arrived on the United Fruit liner Peten in May 1933, TIME reported that the Havana press gave more space to a boatman killed by a shark in the harbour — the actress Ann Harding saw it: \"a fin cut through the water … and there was a scream and he was dragged down.\" (Havana's persistent story that Machado's men fed prisoners to the harbour sharks is legend — I found no source for it this session.) The odd detail: the shark that upstaged an ambassador — and the city's rumour that the sharks had been taught the taste by the Porra.",
