      "summary": "Jesse Binga opened a private bank in 1908 and chartered the Binga State Bank in 1921 at 35th and State — the flagship institution of Black Chicago. He built the Binga Arcade, a $325,000 five-storey office block with a rooftop ballroom, and leaned too hard on its rents. The Illinois auditor closed the bank in 1930 (Wikipedia says June 1930). Binga was indicted for embezzlement in March 1931; his first trial (1932) hung; the second (1933) convicted him, and he got ten years in Joliet. Paroled in 1938 into the custody of a Catholic priest, Rev. Joseph F. Eckert, he worked as a janitor at St. Anselm's Church for $15 a week, was pardoned by Gov. Dwight Green in 1941, and died 13 June 1950. The odd detail: His house at 5922 S. Park had been bombed at least five times by whites who wanted him out of the neighbourhood — and he outlasted every bomb only to be ruined by a bank run.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High (single source; the exact closing date — often given as 31 July 1930 — not reconciled; deposits lost not given).",
