      "summary": "Between 1929 and 1939 about 82,000 people were formally deported to Mexico and an estimated 500,000–1,000,000 \"repatriated,\" peaking in 1931 (138,000 departures by one estimate). The Midwest held only about 3 percent of the U.S. Mexican population but perhaps 10 percent of repatriates. In Gary, 1931 departures were largely voluntary, on reduced-rate train tickets; by 1932 local governments and relief agencies used \"repressive measures.\" In Detroit, by 1932, one Mexican national told the consul that police had \"dragged\" him to the train station against his will after he had proved his residency the year before. The odd detail: the steel towns of the south shore of Lake Michigan emptied their Mexican neighbourhoods by train, at a discount.",
