      "summary": "Hangar One cost $3 million with a one-gigacandela night light, sliding doors and an upstairs restaurant and lounge. The National Air Races ran there August 23–September 1, 1930 (Lindbergh, Wiley Post, Jimmy Doolittle and Art Chester attended the field's races of 1930 and 1933); on the last day, September 1, Marine Captain Arthur Page crashed his modified Curtiss Hawk F6C-3 in the Thompson Trophy race and died that day. The 1933 International Air Races were flown there in conjunction with the Century of Progress (September 1933 (unverified — V2_R16)). Wiley Post tried for an altitude record from the field in 1934; the Navy began building a reserve air base on the site on January 4, 1937 and bought it outright in 1940. The odd detail: The Thompson Trophy race's first fatality (unverified — V2_R16), on the last day, in front of the Labor Day crowd.",
