      "summary": "The 1930 census counted 19,593 Polish-born in a city of 569,000 (the Kuryer had claimed 100,000 Poles by descent in 1915). Between 1908 and 1932 ten Milwaukee Polish socialists won city or state office, against eight Democrats, four Republicans and ten non-partisans; Louis Kotecki was city comptroller 1912–33; John C. Kleczka (Republican, 1918) was the first Pole in Congress. In Nov 1932 the four South Side wards with the largest Polish populations gave Roosevelt 92.6% (the city gave him 67%). The Casimir Pulaski Council (1928) spoke for as many as 100 Polish societies; the Kosciuszko Guard (1874) still drilled; Fr. Felix Baran ran St. Josaphat 1914–42. The neighbourhood's signature was the \"Polish flat\" — a one-storey frame house jacked three or four feet up to make a basement apartment underneath. The odd detail: A whole district of houses raised off the ground to put a second family in the cellar.",
