      "source_heading": "\"Restored by the Unemployed Councils\" — turning the gas and lights back on — 1930–1934 (also in 09-22 archive: H13)",
      "source_line": 270,
      "cited_by_v2": [],
      "collection": "Chicago & Great Lakes",
      "synopsis": "Chicago's Unemployed Councils claimed 22,000 members and 45 branches in the early 1930s; the rival (socialist-leaning) Chicago Workers' Committee on Unemployment claimed 25,000 members in 1932 and 52 branches in 1933. They were organised in block committees (one or two city blocks each) that sent delegates to neighbourhood councils and on to a central council; about 20 women's committees formed in 1933.",
