      "summary": "A man calling himself Wallace D. Fard arrived in Detroit on 4 July 1930 and went door to door selling silks to recent Black migrants from the South, saying they were the kind their ancestors in Mecca had worn. Within three years his Allah Temple of Islam — later the Nation of Islam — claimed about 25,000 followers in Detroit, Chicago and elsewhere, with a Fruit of Islam men's guard and a Muslim Girls' Training class. On 20 November 1932 a follower, Robert Harris (\"Robert Karriem\"), killed a lodger, James J. Smith, at an altar during what Smith believed was a ceremony, saying: \"I had to kill somebody, I could not forsake my gods.\" Harris was found insane. Police put Fard on a train out of Detroit on 7 December 1932; he came back secretly in January 1933, was arrested preaching on 25 May 1933, arrested in Chicago on 26 September 1933, and vanished in 1934 — the last word a letter from Mexico in March 1934. Elijah Muhammad succeeded him. The odd detail: The founder of a religion simply disappears, and his last letter comes from Mexico.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the dates; membership figure is the movement's claim. R05 covers religion/cults; the 09-22 archive has the Chicago congregation (C08) and Noble Drew Ali (C09) — this entry is the Detroit side.",
