      "summary": "The endurance contests of the 1920s became a Depression survival lottery: 25-cent admission for spectators, couples shuffling for weeks for a purse worth a year's wages, promoters like Seltzer running 40-day shows. Nationally, Homer Morehouse died on the floor after 87 hours (1923), a Seattle girl tried to kill herself after her sleep-drunk partner hit her, and cities banned the shows (Boston and Los Angeles 1923, New Jersey and Seattle 1928, New York 1933); by the late 1930s they were gone. The longest of all is usually credited to Chicago — Merry Garden Ballroom, August 29, 1930 to April 1, 1931, 5,152 hours, Mike Ritof and Edith Boudreaux, $2,000 — but no page read this session confirms it: UNVERIFIED, see leads. The odd detail: Spectators paid a quarter to watch people fail to fall asleep.",
