      "summary": "Oris Paxton (1879–1936) and Mantis James (1881–1935) Van Sweringen built Shaker Heights, bought the Nickel Plate railroad in 1916, and by 1929 controlled a $3 billion, 30,000-mile railroad empire through stacked holding companies, plus stock in Goodyear, Midland Steel and White Motor. Their Terminal Tower complex opened in June 1930, as the crash was eating them. On 1 May 1935 they defaulted on $48 million in loans from J. P. Morgan & Co., which ordered the collateral auctioned in September; a friendly bid bought it back for just over $3 million. Mantis died 12 December 1935; Oris 22 November 1936. The odd detail: The brothers never married, lived together at Daisy Hill, and died eleven months apart as their empire was auctioned.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Van Sweringens laid out the Fairmount Circle where John Carroll stands — two inseparable bachelors who owned a quarter of the continent's rails, then lost it in one year and died one after the other. A vampire-society layer writes itself.",
