      "notes": [
        {
          "label": "The instrument and the volume",
          "text": "Keeler's patent was filed 30 Jul 1925 and issued 13 Jan 1931 (No. 1,788,434); the machine — blood pressure, pulse and breathing traces on a moving chart, with a galvanic skin-response channel added in the early-to-mid 1930s — packed into a case about 16 × 8 × 9 inches and cost about $1,500, built first by Western Electro Mechanical and from 1936 by Associated Research, Inc. of Chicago. By 1932 lie-detection had overtaken ballistics as the lab's main business; Keeler ran 2,171+ examinations in 1935–38 alone. (The trade-history page that gives these figures dates the Keeler Polygraph Institute to 1948, against Wikipedia's 1938 — the 1938 date is his departure from Northwestern; the school is later.) Ness brought him to Cleveland for the Sweeney test."
        }
      ],
