      "summary": "An Ann Arbor-born (29 June 1892) mathematician who taught physics at the College of the City of Detroit and wrote an education column for the Detroit News, Gomon ran the Detroit birth-control movement in the 1920s (later president of its Planned Parenthood chapter), joined Frank Murphy's 1930 mayoral campaign, became his executive secretary and chair of the Mayor's Unemployment Committee in the worst city of the Depression, and from 1933 to 1938 directed the Detroit Housing Commission, building the city's first public housing. She ran for office in 1929, 1935 and 1941 and lost each time; in the war she directed women personnel at Willow Run. She died 13 November 1975. The odd detail: The person who actually ran Detroit's relief in 1931–33 was the mayor's woman secretary, an ex-physics teacher.",
