      "summary": "Bishop (from 1936 Archbishop) John J. Cantwell worked \"with the Knights of Columbus and others to welcome thousands who were fleeing.\" Refugee families began the city's annual December procession to Our Lady of Guadalupe in 1931 (the Archdiocese calls it the city's oldest religious festival). Cantwell also organised a Cristero procession of 40,000 — \"the largest demonstration in the history of Los Angeles\" (per the Journal of San Diego History; Visualizing the Cristero War puts 40,000 at a December 1934 Guadalupe procession protesting Mexican policy, which the Mexican consul discouraged). In 1941 Archbishop Luis María Martínez of Mexico City gave Cantwell a fragment of the tilma of Guadalupe in thanks for the refuge. The odd detail: a relic of the Guadalupe image itself — a piece of the cloak — carried north to Los Angeles as a thank-you for sheltering refugees.",
