      "summary": "Havana's seafront road and promenade, begun in 1901 under the US military government (Wood's occupation) and extended in stages: 1901–02 from the Prado to Calle Crespo (the first roughly 500 m, with a US-built steel-reinforced-concrete roundabout at the Prado, where bands played Cuban music on Sundays); 1902–21 as far as the Maine monument; 1927–30 along the mouth of the bay; by 1923 it had reached K and L streets in Vedado, \"where the United States Embassy was built\" (Wikipedia's wording). The 1922 guide calls it a \"substantial sea wall\" with a \"broad concrete promenade and macadamized driveway,\" a bandstand of twenty Ionic columns, and \"one of the noblest among the cities of the world.\" Its purpose was to keep the sea out: the winter frentes fríos (cold fronts) throw waves over it onto the buildings behind. The Miramar Hotel, the first in Cuba whose waiters wore dinner jackets and gold-buttoned vests, stood facing the first roundabout. The odd detail: The Malecón is an American work with a Cuban soul: laid out by the occupation in 1901, finished by Machado's engineers in 1930, and used every 15 February as the platform for the annual Maine Day speech at the foot of the Maine monument — in 1941 by \"the incumbent American Ambassador,\" with Cuban and American flags on the city (1941 guide).",
