      "theme": "Part 1 — The spine: Old Havana, the Malecón, the Prado and the government",
      "entry_type": "place",
      "why_go": "Everyone the city did not want to see was put here: the sick, the mad, the unclaimed, the dead. Case files, asylum records and orphan registers all live in this quarter.",
      "notes": [
        {
          "label": "Also here — the Casa de Beneficencia (San Lázaro and Belascoain, across from the Malecón)",
          "text": "an orphanage for illegitimate children (the 1941 guide says \"established by Bishop Valdes in 1709\" — that date is wrong: the foundling home began as the Casa Cuna, founded by Bishop Diego Evelino de Compostela in 1687 and completed by Bishop Jerónimo Valdés about 1710–11, and the San Lázaro/Belascoain building is the Real Casa de Beneficencia y Maternidad of 1794 under Governor Luis de las Casas), still run by the Sisters of Charity in 1941, \"famous for the revolving contraption at a side door where the mother places her child … the turnstile whisks around, depositing the baby inside, thus preserving its anonymity.\" All children left there take the founding bishop's surname (1941 guide). Next door in 1941 stood \"the astounding Cerveza Polar sign which shows honest-to-goodness movies from twilight until the early hours,\" and on the next block \"a miniature Coney Island\" with merry-go-rounds and side-shows."
        }
      ],
