      "summary": "The seat of the Republic's presidents from Menocal (who inaugurated it in 1920) to Batista. Designed by the Cuban architect Rodolfo Maruri and the Belgian Paul Belau (who also designed the Centro Gallego), decorated by Tiffany Studios of New York. The 1941 guide calls it \"an amazing conglomeration of about seventeen different architectural styles, topped off by a glass dome like the top layer on a wedding cake,\" with a special clubroom on the ground floor for the palace guards; a fragment of the original city wall \"is still preserved opposite the palace.\" Before 1920 the residence of the head of state had been the Palace of the Captains General on the Plaza de Armas (1922 guide: built 1777 \"to replace the first church\"; the Governor-General's residence), which the military governors Brooke and Wood used and which then became the city hall. The odd detail: The 1922 guide devotes a paragraph to the old Carcel at the foot of the Prado — which \"is not infrequently mistaken by tourists for the Palace\" — so that in the same visitors' mind the country's palace and its jail were the same building on the seafront.",
