      "summary": "Opened 30 December 1930 as the National Hotel of Cuba, designed by McKim, Mead & White, financed by the National City Bank of New York, built in fourteen months by Purdy & Henderson, and run by the American managers of the Plaza, Savoy-Plaza and Copley Plaza hotels. Sevillian-Roman-Moorish-Art Deco, on two Greek crosses so most rooms see the sea; steel frame, coral-stone detail. The site had been the Santa Clara Battery (completed 1797 per the 1922 guide; the Count of Ricla's title), and the 1941 guide says \"some of the old guns still mount guard.\" The gardens were \"legendary,\" with a tiled pool and tennis courts; the hotel ran a beach club 15 minutes away with a \"perambulating bar\" and free shuttle (locker 60¢, cabana $2); the cab fare to town was 40¢. It was renamed Hotel Nacional de Cuba in 1939 and bought by the Chicago developer Arnold Kirkeby in July 1943 (out of window). The odd detail: The hill is Taganana, named for a Canary Islands cave where the Guanche princess Cathaysa was said to have hidden before she was captured and sold into slavery in 1494; a parallel Cuban legend has a native girl of the same name hiding in a cave beneath this hill, and the novelist Cirilo Villaverde wrote La Cueva de Taganana about it. (The tunnels the hotel now shows tourists date from the 1962 missile crisis — do not put them in a 1930s scene.)",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (dates, architects, financing); the cave legend is literary folklore (Villaverde), not a surveyed cave.",
