      "summary": "Opened 22 March 1908 as the Hotel Sevilla, a four-storey Moorish-revival building by Arellano y Mendoza; bought in 1919 by John McEntee Bowman and Charles Francis Flynn and renamed the Sevilla-Biltmore; in 1924 the Bowman-Biltmore company added a ten-storey tower wing with a rooftop ballroom by the New York architects Schultze & Weaver. The 1922 guide says the hotel had a post-office branch; the 1941 guide calls it \"a bit older than the Nacional, not quite as sumptuous\" but \"a completely modern hotel,\" one block from the Prado, a 20¢ cab ride anywhere in the city, and mentions an arcade with a Bohío bar. The Mercado Colón — a huge, half-condemned market \"sprawled over a full city block\" bounded by Zulueta, Trocadero, Monserrate and Animas — stood across the street. In 1939 the Italian-Uruguayan Amleto Battisti y Lora bought it; Wikipedia says cocaine arrived weekly from Madrid by airliner and was sold in the Longchamps Restaurant in its arcade at $15–50 a gram. The odd detail: Al Capone is said to have booked the whole sixth floor (Wikipedia's version; a citation there gives room 615 — the tellings differ) — legend, unverified; M03 handles the Capone tale. The Sevilla is also where Graham Greene's fictional Wormold is recruited (1958 novel — out of window).",
