      "summary": "A neo-classical three-storey masonry building at the corner of Animas and Zulueta, on the same block as the Plaza Hotel. Its owner, José Abeal Otero, is said to have moved the house from food to liquor once Prohibition sent American tourists to Havana. It offered \"over 80 cocktails in addition to the bar's own brand of 12-year-old rum,\" and had a single long mahogany bar. About 90 percent of its clientele was American. The 1941 guide is unimpressed: \"backless stools,\" drinks \"no better than average,\" \"too many tourists worship at this dubious shrine … many one-day cruisers get there, sit there, drink there, and don't stir from there until sailing.\" A fire closed it in 1965; it reopened in 2013. Founding date: 1917 per the Oxford Companion to Spirits & Cocktails (\"before opening the bar in 1917\"); some sources say 1918. The odd detail: The one-day cruisers who never leave the stool. The tourists it served came off ships in the morning and reboarded in the evening; the bar was the entire island most of them ever saw.",
