      "summary": "A bar and fish restaurant that opened in 1817 as La Piña de Plata (\"The Silver Pineapple\"), renamed El Florida, then Floridita. The Catalan Constantino \"Constante\" Ribalaigua Vert began there as a bartender in 1914, became owner in 1918, and is credited with the frozen daiquiri in the early 1930s; the house became a school of bartenders. Ernest Hemingway rented room 511 at the Ambos Mundos 1932–39 and drank here. The 1941 guide calls it \"a focal point for Cuban men-about-town,\" open with a cocktail hour 6 to 9 when \"practically every businessman in Havana stops there for a quick one\", and jokes that \"someday someone will be smart enough to wire it for sound, then retire handsomely on the money he makes from blackmailing, for the Floridita is the clearing house for the city's news, scandal and gossip.\" Grace Moore or Hemingway might \"wander casually into\" it. The odd detail: The 1941 authors' idea of a wired bar: everyone in Havana assumed the Floridita was the place where secrets were exchanged and knew it. The Florida Hotel, \"a narrowly small but tall building,\" stood \"hard by the corner.\"",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for founding, owners and role; the frozen-daiquiri invention is Wikipedia's credit, not verified from a period source.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The bar everyone assumes is bugged. A Section 13 or Secret Service operative installs a bug and finds a second, older one already in the wall.",
