      "summary": "The home of the Centro Gallego, the mutual-aid society of Havana's Galician immigrants, built 1914–15 by the Belgian architect Paul Belau (who also designed the Presidential Palace) with the American engineers Purdy & Henderson, around the old concert hall of the Tacón Theatre (1838) — the hall that had hosted Sarah Bernhardt and Enrico Caruso and later Anna Pavlova. A Baroque Revival palace with about 97 marble statues by Giuseppe Moretti and Geneva Mercer. The 1922 guide puts the membership at over 25,000; the 1941 guide says each of the Centro Gallego and Centro Asturiano had \"about forty thousand members paying monthly dues of two dollars, which entitles them to free medical and dental care, legal advice, education, hospitalization and even free burials.\" The buildings are \"baroque palaces, lavish with marble, loaded with frescoes, murals, statuary and gilt in the best Paris-Opera-House tradition,\" with marble stairs winding to ballrooms, then \"mahogany-paneled libraries, schoolrooms, gymnasiums and billiard rooms.\" Each society ran hospitals (\"quintas\") and old-folks' homes in the suburbs; the Hijas de Galicia had a beach club for women. In Carnival they ran weekend balls. The odd detail: \"Cubans really live in their clubs\" (1941 guide). A man who belonged to the Centro from his first schoolday to his burial never had to deal with anyone outside it — a perfect place for someone to vanish within a community that looks after its own.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
