      "summary": "The Centro Asturiano (founded 1886 by fifty members of the Asturian colony, for medical care, instruction and recreation) built its present palace in 1927 to the architect Manuel Bustos (Wikipedia's spelling; also given as del Busto); the 1922 guide puts its old club house \"on Zulueta street, opposite Central Park,\" with a ballroom fitted up for $35,000, chandeliers imported at $11,500, a library of 5,000 volumes and night schools; its Covadonga Sanitarium was at the Cerro. (Today the building is part of the Museo Nacional de Bellas Artes, but \"after the 1959 revolution it housed the Supreme Court\" — out of window.) The Centro de Dependientes — the clerks' association, founded 1880, 27,000+ members in 1922, \"an ornate million-dollar building on the Prado,\" sanatorium of La Purísima Concepción — ran the Carnival's best masked balls (the 1941 guide: \"Nothing like them ever happens in our country; probably the saturnalias of the ancient Romans are the nearest\"). The Casino Español, \"the Castilian club,\" dues $7 a month, with \"paintings of all the Marqueses of Pinar del Río — the Club's patron family,\" trophy rooms of Toledo steel and framed letters \"signed in the flourishing handwriting of the late King Alfonso of Spain\"; the 1941 authors were startled to see \"a large room given over to poker tables — what a brash American touch in the midst of all this feudal splendor!\" The 1922 guide also lists, at Prado 83, the German Club and the American Club. The odd detail: In 1922 the American Club and the German Club shared a street number, Prado 83 — the two great foreign colonies at one door.",
