      "summary": "Havana's cathedral, Catedral de la Virgen María de la Concepción, the seat of the Archdiocese, begun by the Jesuits in 1748, completed 1777, consecrated 1782 (the 1922 guide gives 1704 and two older bells dated 1664 and 1698); a Baroque church of coral stone, \"fossilized marine fauna and flora\" visible in the walls, asymmetrical towers, thirty by forty-nine metres. It housed the remains believed to be Columbus's from 1796 to 1898 (moved to Seville when Spain left) — but \"the authorities of the Cathedral of Santo Domingo discovered other bones, which they claimed to identify as the real, original and only genuine remains\" (1922 guide). In the plaza opposite: the palace of the Conde de Bayona (1720), in 1941 the Havana Club Rum Company's free bar, \"with its wildly overgrown patio and cool, dim salas,\" and a cobbled square \"paved in the same cobblestones originally laid down by the Spaniards\" (1941 guide). The cathedral museum (25¢, 1941): antique vestments in virgin gold, \"the six-hundred-pound, nine-feet-tall, sterling-silver monstrance carried in the Corpus Christi processions.\" The 1941 authors object to a \"modernization\": \"the hoary old walls are painted to resemble a theatrical back-drop, and the holy water is dispensed from … liquid soap containers.\" A statue of St Christopher, patron of Havana (cast 1632 in Seville by Martín de Andújar Cantos), is inside, and the seminary of San Carlos adjoins. The odd detail: Two graves claim Columbus. The Cathedral held one set until 1898 — first in a niche in the chancel wall, then \"in a magnificent tomb erected in the center of the church under the dome\" (1922 guide) — while Santo Domingo's cathedral claims the other and built \"a costly tomb of sculptured marble\" for it. The 1922 guide says the Havana bones were taken \"upon the evacuation of Havana by the Spanish.\"",
