      "summary": "The Palace of the Captains General (built 1776–92, under Governor Felipe Fonsdeviela, Marqués de la Torre, completed under Luis de las Casas — 1922 guide: built 1777 on the site of the parish church) was, in order: the Spanish governors' residence; the seat of the US military government 1898–1902 (the era of Brooke and Wood); the Presidential Palace of the Republic 1902–1920; then the city hall (Ayuntamiento) — the 1920s to 1967 (now the Museo de la Ciudad). Its street, Calle Tacón, was paved with wooden blocks in 1841 to muffle carriage noise near the government buildings (Spanish Wikipedia). Beside the Palace is the Castillo de la Real Fuerza (1558–77), the oldest stone fort in the Americas, whose 1634 Giraldilla weathervane stands atop it (M03); El Templete and its ceiba are on the same square (M03). The odd detail: The building that had been the seat of the US military government in 1898–1902 was, from 1920, Havana's city hall — the ordinary municipal government sitting in the rooms the occupation once used. The street outside is paved with wood (Calle Tacón, 1841) so carriages would not disturb the officials inside.",
