      "summary": "One of the oldest churches in Havana — founded 1635, rebuilt and enlarged 1648 — a coral-stone Baroque-Mudéjar church about 60 m long, austere, with a wooden ceiling of crossed beams and a three-level bell tower (1808). Its extraordinary status: by papal bull (1772) and royal decree of Charles III (1773) it was the only \"immune church\" in Havana — persecuted people could claim asylum inside it. Catacombs lie behind the altar area (funerary crypts were found in 1953; out of window). Tied to a confraternity of free Black Havanans: confirmed — in 1636 a cofradía de negros libres asked leave to build a small hermitage on Calle Cuba (Oficina del Historiador, lahabana.gob.cu), which also confirms the 1772 papal bull and an 1855 inscription \"única iglesia inmune en esta ciudad\"; the 1773 decree of Charles III rests on es.wikipedia alone, and the founding year is 1635 (es.wikipedia) or a 1636 petition (Oficina). The odd detail: The catacombs' funerary crypts predate the Colón Cemetery (1876) — burial under the church had been the custom before the cemeteries (Espada opened 1804–06).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium: the sanctuary bull/decree and the address are what the page gave; the confraternity claim is now confirmed (see above); what \"immunity\" meant in 1930s law is unknown.",
