      "summary": "The Franciscan church and convent on the Plaza de San Francisco, by the waterfront (built 1730, \"suggesting a fortress rather than a place of worship,\" with a three-storey tower used as a landmark and pirate lookout), had become the Cuban Post Office and the Telegraph Company (1941 guide). The Santa Clara Convent (Calles Luz, Cuba and Sol), \"one of the city's oldest buildings,\" was in 1941 the Department of Public Works: its \"three-hundred-year-old patios\" preserved, plus \"three of the first buildings ever erected on the island — the first public baths, a sailors' home, the first slaughter house — all little stone huts with corrugated tiles, dating from 1519\" (the guide's claim — UNVERIFIED), the nuns' cemetery, the monastic cells and a sculptured mahogany ceiling in the oratory. El Santo Cristo (facing Calle Villegas at the Plaza del Cristo) is another 16th-century church, \"two ancient towers rise above a gaudily tiled roof,\" a ceiling of great square beams. The odd detail: Havana's post office was a convent, and its Public Works department sat on a nuns' cemetery — the postal and municipal records of the Republic were kept in churches that had lost their congregations.",
