      "summary": "The Jesuit Colegio de Belén, chartered by Isabella II in 1854 in the old convent and convalescent hospital of Our Lady of Belén, with a meteorological observatory from 1857 whose director from 1870, Father Benito Viñes (1837–93), \"very likely issued the first-ever hurricane warning\" on 11 September 1875, and from 1876 built a telegraph network of weather posts across the Caribbean (twenty stations by 1888) and wrote Practical Hints in Regard to the West Indian Hurricanes (1885; translated by the US Navy). In 1925 the school opened a new monumental campus in Marianao — a \"pan-optical\" concrete-and-steel building with nine radial pavilions, sixty acres, next to the estate that became the Tropicana (Morales & Cía; \"unlimited budget\"). Fidel Castro was an alumnus (out of window). The odd detail: The school moved out of Old Havana in 1925 to a campus designed like a Panopticon, and the old site became the \"Palacio de Educación\" (unverified — V_M11: Wikipedia attaches that name to the school's complex and says it now houses the Instituto Técnico Militar, which is the Marianao building, so the name may belong to the Marianao campus, not the old site).",
