      "summary": "The Castillo de San Salvador de la Punta, the small fortress on Havana's western side of the harbour entrance: lookouts from 1559, works begun about 1590 by the engineer Battista Antonelli, a heavy copper chain laid across the mouth to El Morro in 1630 (a lattice of chains and wooden beams in 1762; \"some fragments … still remain\"), the bulk of it dismantled to three bastions. The 1941 guide says the chain used to be dragged across to Morro every night at nine, \"then the cannon at Cabañas was fired to signify the closing of the port for the night. Though the port is no longer closed, the cannon still booms out the nightly signal.\" Next to La Punta stood a preserved fragment of wall (1922 guide): \"the ground in front of the wall was a place of public execution; it was here that certain students of the University of Havana were sacrificed\" — the eight medical students shot on 27 November 1871 (drawn by lot from 43 accused of defacing the glass over the tomb of Colonel Castañón in the Espada Cemetery; the 1922 guide says \"the oldest sixteen\" — wrong: modern accounts give the eight as aged 16 to 19, and say three were chosen by lot; the 1922 guide's account). Boats to the forts left from the Punta Landing, Caballería and Machina wharves, ten cents each way. The odd detail: The 9 o'clock gun outlived the chain — the harbour is not closed at night, but every night the gun still says it is. The 1941 guide: \"When the cannon roars, Cubans look at their watches and say 'Nine o'clock' in tones of great surprise, though they should be used to it by this time.\"",
