      "summary": "The great harbour fort, designed by Battista Antonelli, finished 1589 (the 1922 guide: built 1587–97), 100–120 ft above the sea, moats 70 ft deep, a light tower (lit 1845–46) \"visible eighteen miles at sea\" (1922 guide). Taken by the British in 1762 (Lord Albemarle; the Spanish commander Velasco was mortally wounded in the final assault, after a British mine was blown under a bastion). By the 1920s–40s it was a sightseeing stop — boats left the Machina Wharf and Punta landing for ten cents; its 1930s garrison and use are not confirmed here. The 1941 guide: \"Don't miss the cells below sea level, with chutes cut through the solid rock from which prisoners were thrown to the sharks. Contrary to popular belief, however, this is no longer the accepted method of execution in Cuba.\" The odd detail: The \"prisoners thrown to the sharks\" chutes are a tourist-guide tale in a 1941 book — a rumour with a date and an author, ready to be a real thing. The 1941 authors' joke (\"no longer the accepted method\") is the source. M03 has the actual shark incident, in Havana harbour in 1930–33 (the Porra's shark).",
