      "summary": "A fort built 1767–79 on the hill overlooking the city from the west, \"named after Charles of Bourbon, Prince of Asturias\" — an irregular pentagon, two bastions and a redan, deep trenches and mine galleries, a garrison for 1,000 and 60 cannon, and a system of red-brick tunnels linking its outposts. It served as Cuba's principal detention facility for over a century (Wikipedia): by 1922 the federal prisoners of the old Carcel had been moved to Príncipe, and the 1941 guide calls it \"an old bastioned fortification, now used as a jail.\" Its prisoners included José Martí's teacher Rafael María de Mendive, Julio Antonio Mella, Eduardo Chibás, Raúl Roa and Juan Marinello, and \"El Caballero de París\" (per Wikipedia). It overlooks the Avenida Carlos III toward the Quinta de los Molinos — Weyler's \"summer residence,\" in 1941 the University's agricultural experiment station and botanical garden. The odd detail: The 1941 guide records a rumour: \"Rumor says that secret passages, tunneled underneath, connect it with La Punta on the Malecón. Interesting equipment for a prison, if true.\" A tunnel from the hill to the harbour mouth is what the 1941 guide passes on as a rumour; the fort's real red-brick galleries make it not impossible.",
