      "summary": "Reached by a five-cent ferry from the Luz wharf (a ten-minute ride, 5¢ round trip in 1941) that also carried passengers for the United Railways' trains to Matanzas and beyond. A working town of sugar and tobacco warehouses — the 1922 guide names the builder Juan Eduardo Fesser, who has a statue there — a railway terminal, once a bull-ring town, and the seat of the sanctuary of Our Lady of Regla (Nuestra Señora de Regla), a Black Madonna, patroness of Havana Bay and of sailors and boatmen: a hermitage founded by the pilgrim Manuel Antonio (1687/1690), destroyed by the 1692 hurricane, rebuilt 1694 with an image donated by Pedro de Aranda, named patron of Regla on 23 December 1714; her feast is 8 September, with a procession by boat across the bay. The 1922 guide describes the church walls as hung with offerings of miniature arms, legs, hearts and other silver tokens given to the Virgin for answered prayers. In Santería she is Yemayá, the orisha of the sea. Regla is also where the first Abakuá lodge (Efik Butón) was founded in 1836 (M03 Part E), and one of the two places of the 1919 \"brujo\" lynching panic (M03). The 1941 guide describes an evening there: the Mexico Bar on the main street, \"fully a block long,\" with Mexican murals and \"Cuban flag by the yard,\" and \"the only reservadas we have seen complete with victrolas and wash-basins\"; the Plaza Facciolo with the Palacio Municipal (city hall, police station and town hospital in one), whose festival lights from the previous 4 September had never been taken down; and the evening paseo, girls walking clockwise, young men counter-clockwise. The odd detail: The last piracy legend of the coast. The 1922 guide records that a Regla pirate's \"magnificent palace\" had red stucco that constantly peeled off, and neighbours said \"so much blood is mixed with it that it cannot stick\"; the guide says the last act of piracy on the coast was the brig Halcione in 1839, all pirates garrotted in Havana. A folk saying, recorded in a 1922 tourist guide, that is unusually good as a haunted-house line.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the sanctuary's history; Medium for the 1930s town, which rests on two tourist guides; the peeling-stucco line is folklore first attested in the 1922 guide.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Cult versus population: the Virgin's procession across the bay each 8 September is the single largest crowd of the year and everyone is on the water. The Golden Circle wants a night when the harbour is full of boats and no one is looking at the shore; the IHS's local priest knows the boatmen's devotion is real and will not hand them to the Circle. The peeling red stucco is a ready-made haunted-house detail; the Abakuá lodge of 1836 is not a villain.",
