      "summary": "A colonial township about five kilometres south-east of Havana — founded as an Indian town in the sixteenth century (the 1922 guide: \"the poverty stricken remnants of various tribes being gathered together here\"), briefly Cuba's capital in 1555 after the French pirate Jacques de Sores attacked Havana (\"Like putting Havana in Guanabacoa\" is the saying for fitting something too large into too small a space). It became by 1854 \"the fashionable watering place of the island\" for the summer (1922 guide); it was \"famous for its historical Santería\" and the home of the first African cabildo in Havana; and a Jewish community grew up around light manufacturing — Samuel Epstein's Sedanita knitting works in the late 1920s (200 workers, $75,000 of imported equipment) and Charles Shapiro's factory in the 1930s. The 1922 guide describes the mineral baths of Santa Rita and the church of Potosí outside the town \"containing the miracle-working image of Jesús Nazareno del Potosí\"; the 1941 guide: \"the colonial atmosphere preserved in the older homes (really palaces), the narrow streets, barred windows and wall-niches for figures of St. Anthony,\" the Church of San Francisco with its \"centuries-old statue of Our Lady of the Assumption, patron saint of the city\" (feast in August, processions of \"La Tutelar\"), the Convent of Saint Anthony with cloisters of carved mahogany, the Loma del Indio with \"mineral springs discovered by the Siboneys and still in use today for medicinal purposes,\" and the town's specialty papas rellenas. Ernesto Lecuona and Rita Montaner were born there. The odd detail: Florida Indians were evacuated to Guanabacoa in 1763 (Apalachees and Yamasees, when Spain ceded Florida to Britain) — a refugee people settled in a town that had been built as a native reduction two centuries earlier.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
