      "summary": "The Country Club of Havana was founded \"thirty years ago\" (c. 1910–11, by the 1941 guide's count) by Americans and was, the guide argues, \"one of the most important factors in changing the social-behavior pattern of the Cubans\": before the golf course, Cuban gentlemen spent weekends in bars gambling and gossiping while their wives waited at home; after it, they took up sports and \"daughters of Cuba's first families lounge or play on the clubhouse grounds with the same freedom our own girls enjoy\" — the credit for \"the relaxation of the hitherto rigid dueña system.\" Country Club Park, around it, was where \"the sumptuous homes and estates of Cuba's millionaires put even Beverly Hills in the shade\"; at its entrance \"a lake shaped like the island of Cuba, illuminated at night,\" \"a favored Lovers' Lane.\" Rents in the Country Club or Miramar section, 1941: $130 a month for a furnished house. The Havana Yacht Club in Marianao, founded 1886, \"the first yacht club around Havana … the citadel of upper-crust society,\" with a collection of original etchings of early Havana; the Miramar Yacht Club and the Jaimanitas and Rovers clubs are others. The 1922 guide lists the American Club at Prado 83 (with the German Club) as the colony's city address, and Wikipedia's Malecón text records a US Embassy at K and L streets, Vedado, by 1923. The odd detail: A lake shaped like Cuba at the entrance to the millionaires' park, lit at night, with lovers' lane on its banks: the map of the island as a pond in the American suburb.",
