      "summary": "The showpiece. The 1941 guide: \"No one will believe you really went to Havana unless you visit the Casino. The nymphs dancing round the fountains in the gardens are just as enchanting by moonlight as they have been described … a Continental rather than Cuban air; it might have been transplanted from the Riviera.\" A winter house (December through March): roulette, chemin-de-fer, craps, twenty-one, bird-cage and baccarat; a ballroom with \"Cuban and American orchestras\" and a floor show \"imported from Broadway\" (the Chester Hale Girls the previous season); a bar with deep leather chairs and \"an impressive row of slot machines.\" The guide adds that \"it's the Cubans who do most of the heavy gambling\" — a young Havana blood begged the croupiers not to let him play after the fifteenth of each month. The Casino de la Playa (\"Summer Casino\"), \"right across from its more famous relative, and set in the same aristocratic formal gardens,\" is \"a smaller edition\" open April through November, with an indoor dance floor that \"rises high when the floor show goes on.\" The odd detail: A twin gambling house that opens when the first closes — winter for the Big Casino, summer for the little one, on either side of one road. The guide's advice for novices: \"Don't try to break the bank; you won't.\"",
