      "summary": "\"The roadside honky-tonks\" (1941 guide): fritas is Cuban slang for hamburger, and the name covers \"the whole cluster of refreshment stands, cabarets, shooting galleries and dance-halls along Quinta Avenida out toward Marianao.\" The guide names the Café de los Tres Hermanos (\"the one to visit, if only for Chori's sake\" — a drummer and tympanist), the Pennsylvania (an open-air restaurant with a \"large, cool, dimly lit cabaret with French windows … a haunt of middle-class Cubans\"), Panchin (\"dance and drink in semi-darkness … certainly the hottest [music] in town\"; the guide says a flash photograph there on a Tuesday night \"would catch every middle-class businessman in town, out on the town\"), and Tio Sam's, \"a tumbledown, open-air building … a Negro dance-hall\" where a dance cost two cents (against ten cents elsewhere) and \"the proprietor trots around, busily punching each taxi-dancer's ticket.\" Also Casanova and Club Flotante. The guide's descriptions are the authors' outsiders' voice, condescending and fetishising; I use them for names, prices and layout only. The odd detail: Two cents a dance against ten elsewhere — the price of admission to the poorest hall was the price of a punched ticket, and the proprietor kept the tickets.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (one guide, with its own bias; venues and prices are its 1940–41 snapshot).",
