      "summary": "The 1941 guide's short list of central rooms. The Edén Concert, \"in the heart of town … within walking distance of almost any hotel,\" where \"almost any night you can find a few politicians, sugar planters, congressmen and local celebrities at the bar or on the dance floor,\" with a \"stone dance floor open to the night\" and \"professional hostesses\" who took a percentage of the drinks (the Tropicana grew out of a 1930s \"Edén Concert\" — see the Tropicana entry). Montmartre, in Vedado, \"atop an office building directly behind the Nacional Hotel,\" with a glass floor lit from beneath and patterned like a roulette layout and \"a complete gaming room.\" Bajo la Luna (\"under the moon\"), the Nacional's patio night club with a smooth marble floor, \"rarely crowded,\" where \"curfew rings at midnight.\" Tony's Club, in the basement of the Hotel Regina, \"off the tourist path,\" all colourful ceramic tile including the stairs and \"a long old-style charcoal stove in one corner.\" \"None of the night clubs wakes up until eleven; all are at their gayest from twelve to three.\" The odd detail: A dance floor that is a roulette layout, lit from below, on the roof of an office building behind Havana's grandest hotel — and a patio club at the same hotel with a midnight curfew bell.",
