      "summary": "The 1922 guide's recommended tour: \"A visit to a large tobacco factory will give much to interest and instruct, and one may well devote an hour to this phase of Havana industry, as shown at the establishment of Messrs. H. Upmann & Co., Paseo de Tacón 159–169. (Permits may be had at Mr. Foster's office.)\" The visitor follows \"the successive steps of manipulation by which the raw leaf is converted into the finished product — the unfolding of the baled leaf and its moistening, stripping from the stem and sorting according to lighter or darker shades, the actual making of the cigar, and the subsequent sorting, labeling, packing and sealing,\" each done by workers \"whose deftness and skill excite admiration.\" Tobacco for the best cigars came from the Vuelta Abajo in Pinar del Río. The 1941 guide adds that a Havana cigar \"is handmade and, machine age or no, will always be handmade\" — prescribed by law — about fifteen cents each, and lists the recognised brands (La Corona, Ramón Allones, H. Upmann, Hoyo de Monterrey, Romeo y Julieta). The odd detail: Touring a factory required a permit from \"Mr. Foster's office\" — the 1922 guide's own foreign gatekeeper. (The tobacco-factory lectores, the men who read aloud to the rollers, are a well-known Havana institution that I did not find in either guide — a lead.)",
