      "summary": "\"A large native dance-hall in the city patronized by lower-class Cubans-without-women\" (1941 guide): hostesses who danced with unaccompanied men — the guide says they \"average about thirty dances each night, and double that on Saturdays and Sundays\" — to danzón, son, fox trot and \"shag,\" with four orchestras and a large crowd on the Tuesday and Friday verbenas; it was \"entirely correct\" for men to bring their own partners. Other halls: the Habana-Sport and the Martí y Belona. The guide says many Americans went to learn the Cuban dances. The odd detail: Dancers paid by the dance, 30 dances a night, 60 on weekends — a record of who danced with whom, kept on tickets.",
