      "summary": "One of the oldest and largest Chinatowns in Latin America. The first businesses opened 1858; from 1874 Chinese settlers on Zanja and Dragones (\"Dragons\") streets established small shops, bars, laundries; at the start of the twentieth century about 10,000 Chinese lived in ten city blocks, with pharmacies, silk shops, restaurants, cinemas, theatres for Chinese opera (the 1922 guide: \"the Chinese theatre is on Zanja street\") and a chamber of commerce that functioned as a stock exchange. The Casino Chung Wah (founded 1893), the Chinese community's social club, was headquartered there; by mid-century there were about 60 Chinese associations across Cuba; the newspaper Kwong Wah Po (\"Brilliant China\") has been published by the Chung Wah since 20 March 1928. Havana's Chinese also had their own cemetery (the Cementerio General Chino, in Nuevo Vedado). The Chinese in Cuba came first as coolie contract labourers — some 141,000 between 1847 and 1874 — and then in a 1920s wave of about 30,000 Cantonese (mostly men, rapid intermarriage). The 1922 guide's period framing calls the community \"John Chinaman,\" notes the census counts (2,751 — the same page also prints 2,794 — Chinese in Havana in 1899) and that the coolies \"were imported under a contract to serve eight years at $4 a month, and the planters paid $400 for them\" and \"were not altogether satisfactory as human chattels, being much given to suicide\" — that phrase is the guide's callousness, not a fact to repeat. In the 1941 guide the visible Chinese Havana is the vegetable vendor of Vedado with the shoulder-pole and a cheap restaurant, Flor de Consulado; the market gardens on the city's outskirts were \"largely in their hands.\" The odd detail: A Chinatown with its own stock exchange and its own cemetery — a self-organised community on ten blocks, whose chamber of commerce traded like a bourse. M03 lists \"the Chinese of Havana (Wood's 15 May 1902 exclusion order)\" as a gap; the 1902 order is not confirmed here.",
