      "summary": "The oldest US overseas naval base. It rests on the Platt Amendment (Article VII) and the Cuban–American lease agreements of February and July 1903 — no fixed expiry, $2,000 a year in gold coin, in 1934 changed unilaterally to dollars under the Gold Reserve Act at $3,386.25. The station covers about 45 square miles of land and water on three sections (Leeward Point, Windward Point and the bay), and was in the 1920s–30s a prime area for winter training for the Atlantic fleet; the fleet range system began as early as 1905. The Marines' Camp McCalla dates from 1898, and a monument on McCalla Hill commemorates one Navy officer and five Marines who died in the Battle of Guantánamo Bay. The 1941 guide notes it as \"forty miles east of Santiago, now one of our most flourishing naval bases.\" (Its water pipeline from the Yateras River dates from 1939.) The odd detail: The lease has \"no fixed expiration date,\" and Article 3 of the second agreement forbids \"commercial or other enterprise\" within the leased areas. An American base whose rent the Cuban government would later refuse to cash.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the 1903 lease, 1934 payment and the fleet-training role; the base's 1930s local labour arrangements are not on what I read.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Three layers of truth: the lease says coaling; the Navy says training; the file says a place to keep things that cannot be kept on an island the Circle can reach. See M03: the Platt Amendment and the Guantánamo lease.",
