      "id": "M12-001",
      "title": "The Zócalo (Plaza de la Constitución)",
      "date_start": "1930",
      "date_end": "1939",
      "date_label": "1930s (place entry)",
      "category": "A GM's gazetteer of 1920s–30s Mexico City and its surroundings",
      "tags": [
        "gazetteer",
        "Mexico",
        "Zócalo",
        "Plaza",
        "Constitución"
      ],
      "people": [],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "The Zócalo (Plaza de la Constitución)",
          "address": "the main square, Centro Histórico",
          "geography": "the main square, Centro Histórico",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=The%20Z%C3%B3calo%20%2C%20the%20main%20square%2C%20Centro%20Hist%C3%B3rico%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The great square: the Metropolitan Cathedral on the north, the National Palace on the east, the Federal District buildings on the south, the Portal de Mercaderes on the west, the Nacional Monte de Piedad at the NW corner and the Templo Mayor site just NE, out of sight. The plaza was re-landscaped in 1914 (the century-old ash trees taken out, lawns and paths laid, palms planted at each corner); the 1878 iron kiosk (donated by Antonio Escandón, modelled on one in the Bois de Boulogne) still stood in it; the trams and lighting had been electric since 1894; market stalls kept creeping back in. The Wikipedia page records no specific 1920s–30s works — treat the plaza's 1930s look as the 1914 layout (UNVERIFIED for later changes). The Grito is shouted from the Palace balcony to this square every 15 September; 20 November 1935 it is the Battle of the Zócalo (Gold Shirts vs. taxi drivers — see M01). The odd detail: \"Zócalo\" means plinth. The name comes from the base of a monument to independence that Santa Anna began and never finished — the plaza is named for the pedestal of a statue that was never raised. \"Though 'buried long ago,'\" the article says, the name stuck.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the dates above; Low for the 1920s–30s appearance beyond the 1914 layout. • UNVERIFIED for later changes",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a plinth with nothing on it. Someone finally intends to raise the thing the pedestal was made for — and the trial of that intent is a Zócalo rally on 15 September, with the Grito as the trigger.",
