      "summary": "The largest cathedral in the Americas, built 1573–1813 (interior consecrated 1667; towers, façade and dome finished last) on soft lake clay that has been sinking it ever since. Inside: the Altar of Forgiveness and the Altar of the Kings (1718–1737, Jerónimo de Balbás, Churrigueresque, 13.75 × 25 × 7.5 m — \"la cueva dorada\", the golden cave; the Altar of Forgiveness carries the first estípite column in the Americas) and two of the largest 18th-century organs in the Americas. The page records that the building was closed \"in the days of the Cristero War\" (dates not given; the Calles Law and the silence of the bells are 14 June / 31 July 1926 — see M01). The odd detail: The Sun Stone hung on the cathedral's west tower for 95 years (1790 → the National Museum, Aug 1885 — M02). Later (post-1940, so marked as modern): the 1947 bell accident that killed a novice, after which the bell was \"punished\" by taking out its clapper — \"la castigada\"/\"la muda\" until 2000; the 1967 fire; a 1742 time capsule found in 2007. None of these is period, but \"the mute bell\" is a ready-made local legend if the GM lets a 1930s bell be silent for its own reasons.",
