      "summary": "The seat of the federal executive and, in the 1920s–30s, of the Ministries of Finance, War, Justice, the Interior and Foreign Relations and the Supreme Court; the president no longer lived in it (the residence had moved to Chapultepec Castle and, from 1934, to Los Pinos). Under Calles an engineer (Alberto J. Pani) and an architect (Augusto Petriccioli) added a third floor in 1926–29 and the red tezontle façade. Diego Rivera painted \"The Epic of the Mexican People\" on the main stairwell 1929–35: 450 m², three panels — pre-Hispanic Mexico (Quetzalcóatl at the centre); the Conquest and Independence; and, on the left, a critique of early-20th-century capitalism; eleven further panels on the middle floor were left unfinished. The Independence Bell rung by Hidalgo at Dolores on 16 Sep 1810 hung over the balcony (moved here 1896; its niche rebuilt 1926–30). The odd detail: The palace stands on Moctezuma II's \"New Houses\"; the stairway mural shows it. Recent digs beside it (post-1940 — modern) uncovered part of the Casa Denegrida, \"the Black House\" — a windowless, black-painted chamber where the emperor consulted seers, per the article. And, from the period's own book of street legends (González Obregón, 1922): every morning at seven the Viceroy Revilla Gigedo was shaved by the barber Teodoro Guerrero while he read complaints left overnight in a box at the palace's main door — the first \"complaint box\" in the building, older than the ministries.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the mural dates and the third floor; the \"Black House\" is a modern find (date not stated).",
