      "summary": "A narrow street behind Santo Domingo, once crossed by an acequia (canal). The legend is period-attested in print: González Obregón, 1922, retells it from an older source, saying it is recorded in the Life of Padre Don José Vidal (printed 1752 at the Colegio de San Ildefonso) and in Francisco de Sedano's Noticias de México, and set \"between 1670 and 1680\". Legend: a priest lived in sin at house no. 3, calle de la Puerta Falsa de Santo Domingo; his compadre, a blacksmith on Rejas de Balvanera (the \"Casa del Pujavante\"), warned him in vain. One night two men (the text says dos negros — the period's framing, which then turns them into demons) brought the priest's mule to be shod at once; the blacksmith did it and rebuked the men for beating the animal. In the morning the priest was still in bed with his companion, denied sending anyone — and the woman was dead, with the same four horseshoes nailed to her hands and feet, a bit in her mouth and the marks of the beating on her. The men, they concluded, were demons. Three witnesses (the parish priest of Santa Catarina, Padre Vidal, and a Carmelite) buried her in the house floor and swore silence. The odd detail: González Obregón notes the blacksmith's house was called the \"Casa del Pujavante\" because over its door were carved a pujavante (farrier's paring knife) and crossed tongs — \"memoria\" of the miracle — which Sedano says he saw several times. The period's own historian names the carved lintel as the proof (the house has since been rebuilt).",
