      "summary": "Adamo Boari began the \"Gran Teatro de Ópera\" on 1 October 1904; it missed the 1910 centenary, \"hardly begun\" by 1913, and stalled with the Revolution; Federico Mariscal resumed work in 1932, redoing the interior in Art Deco, and it was inaugurated 29 November 1934 with La verdad sospechosa. Meanwhile the building had sunk about four metres into the clay. Its stage curtain is a Tiffany glass foldable panel of nearly a million pieces, weighing 24 tons, showing Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl. Rivera recreated Man at the Crossroads here in 1934 after the Rockefeller Center mural was destroyed and painted over in New York; Orozco's Katharsis and Siqueiros's New Democracy are on the walls. The odd detail: The opera house sank about four metres into the clay over the thirty years it took to finish — and was finished, in Art Deco, by a different architect than the Italian who began it. Its most famous object is a 24-ton glass curtain of two volcanoes, made in New York.",
