      "summary": "The federal penitentiary, opened in 1900 by Porfirio Díaz. Designed by Miguel S. Macedo (who was himself imprisoned there during the Revolution) on Bentham's panopticon plan — one guard-post sees every cell. It had 804 cells, built for 800 men, 180 women and 400 children, with workshops, a nursery and a medical section; in practice it was chronically over-full (the page gives the design capacity only; 1930s headcounts UNVERIFIED). Madero and Pino Suárez were murdered during their transfer from the National Palace to Lecumberri, 22 Feb 1913 (M01); José de León Toral, Obregón's assassin, was shot at Lecumberri on 9 Feb 1929 (M01, \"¡Viva Cristo Rey!\"). The page also lists Pancho Villa as escaping in 1912 — WRONG for Lecumberri: Villa was held at Belem, transferred to the Santiago Tlatelolco prison on 7 June 1912 and escaped there on Christmas Day 1912 (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pancho_Villa); Lecumberri's page has it loosely. Decommissioned 1976; turned over to the Archivo General de la Nación in 1980 — modern; the archive now holds the paper record of everything the prison held. The odd detail: The Black Palace was designed so one man in the tower could see every cell — and its own architect ended up a prisoner in it during the Revolution. Its second life as the national archive (1980, modern) means the panopticon's central tower now looks over documents, not prisoners.",
