      "summary": "A colonial house at Calle de Chavarría no. 4 (2ª Justo Sierra) whose cornice carries a stone arm in high relief holding a monstrance. González Obregón (1922) tells why: on 11 December 1676, the anniversary of the Guadalupe apparition, the church of San Agustín caught fire at seven in the evening \"beginning with the clock's weight\"; the church burned in under two hours, the fire lasting three days, while the viceroy-archbishop Fray Payo Enríquez de Rivera directed the fight to save the convent. As the crowd stood stunned, Captain Juan de Chavarría, about 58, strong, in captain's dress, pushed into the burning church, climbed the altar, took the monstrance in his right hand and carried it out — and the crowd knelt. Years later the new house went up with the arm and monstrance in a stone niche. Chavarría was baptized at the Sagrario on 4 June 1618, was made a Knight of Santiago on 26 Dec 1652, and died in the house on 29 Nov 1682 leaving about 500,000 pesos. (1922: \"the house still stands with slight modifications\".) The odd detail: González Obregón says no manuscript or printed book records who allowed Chavarría to carve the arm on his house — \"old tradition tells only the fire\". A stone arm holding a consecrated object, over a door, whose licence nobody kept.",
