      "summary": "A ruin, \"demolished not many years\" before 1922: a decayed 16th-century house popularly said to have been the home of Tomás Treviño de Sobremonte, a merchant burned alive by the Inquisition. González Obregón (1922) says he could find no historical foundation for the belief, but summarises the case from the printed abstract: reconciled at the Santo Domingo auto of 15 June 1625, relapsed, tried again; he married María Gómez with Jewish rites; he fasted for five years (the tribunal force-fed him to keep him alive); on being told of his sentence he cried he was a Jew and would die as one; he was burned at the auto of 11 April 1649, mounted on mules that \"bucked\" (the mob said the animals refused to carry a Jew — González Obregón says: because they refused to carry a poor man to such a death), taken by a horse led by an Indian who punched him, to the Quemadero between the convent of San Diego and the Alameda. As the fire took, he said only, kicking embers toward himself, \"¡Echen leña, que mi dinero me cuesta!\" (\"Add wood — my money paid for it,\" because his goods had been confiscated). The odd detail: In the same file: he supposedly buried a Christ figure under one doorway of his Guadalajara shop and sold cheaper to the customers who came in over it than to those who used the other door, and whipped a wooden Santo Niño every night (the image afterwards kept the scars and was venerated at Santo Domingo).",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High that the book says this; the specific acts are the Inquisition's printed allegations, not history — a source hostile to its victim (label it).",
