      "summary": "The street was named for the legend that in the Noche Triste (30 June 1520) Pedro de Alvarado vaulted the third canal of the Tacuba causeway with his lance. González Obregón (1922) opens his chapter by debunking it: \"false\" — as false as that Cortés burned his ships (he scuttled them), that Moctezuma died by a stone (Cortés ordered his death), and that Cortés wept under the famous ahuehuete of Popotla (\"no time to shed tears in his flight from the city\"). In the 1830s–40s a bridge and ditch were still visible on the street; by 1922 \"no trace of bridge or aqueduct remained\". The Spanish column left from Axayácatl's palace at the corner of Santa Teresa and 2ª Indio Triste on the night of 30 June 1520. The odd detail: The period's own official chronicler said the famous lament was invented — the tourist attraction was already declared fake in print.",
