      "summary": "Not in the capital. An early-18th-century stone mansion attributed to the alarife Ignacio Mariano de las Casas, with dog-shaped gargoyles as rain-spouts (hence the name); owned in the late 18th century by the philanthropist María Josefa Vergara y Hernández; Seminario Conciliar 1868–70, then a girls' school, then property of the Querétaro Archdiocese from 1906, and later a kindergarten. The legend: \"the aparecido (ghost) of the Casa de los Perros\" guards the house after its old owner's death — recorded in local chronicles and media but without early attestation. The odd detail: The famous \"House of the Dogs\" is not a Mexico City house — it is in Querétaro, roughly 200 km northwest of the capital (general knowledge). If a GM wants a Casa de los Perros in the capital, it has to be invented; no Mexico City house of that name was found.",
