      "summary": "The city's great wholesale-and-retail food market, on the grounds of the old La Merced monastery (founded 1594; only its Plateresque cloister survives). The first permanent market buildings went up in 1863; the district was \"nearly entire filled with market stalls\". It is also the classic tolerance zone — prostitution the police do not police. (The modern market building of 1957 and the Central de Abasto of the 1980s are out of period — not on the page read.) The page has no detail on the witchcraft market at Sonora, which is elsewhere. The odd detail: The page lists local legends — a woman named Doña Esperanza Goyoneche de Ruiz García who \"died here\" and an indigenous chief or Spanish spy who sat still, never moving, until he died of sadness — neither dated on the page read (FOLKLORE, no attestation).- Sources: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Merced_Market (read)",
