      "summary": "A working-class barrio built out of the closed El Volador market (closed 1901) — merchants moved here and made it a baratillo (discount and second-hand market); merchant inns became vecindades (crowded tenements). During the Cristero War (1926–29) refugees from Jalisco and Guanajuato settled and became shoemakers. Boxing culture began with Kid Azteca (first famous Tepito boxer, 1932), through the José \"Huitlacoche\" Medel gym. The name comes from the Nahuatl for \"small temple\"; a folk etymology says \"police whistles\" gave the barrio bravo nickname. (The Santa Muerte shrine of 2001 is modern — do not put it in the 1930s.) The odd detail: A market that moved itself here after the government closed its old site — the neighbourhood is a relocated institution. And it was made rich in shoemakers by a war in another state.",
