      "summary": "The Old Basilica (built 1695–1709) — the 1974–76 new basilica is modern. On 14 November 1921 a bomb hidden in a flower arrangement went off at the main altar, damaging the altar steps and windows but not the image; a bent metal crucifix from the blast was kept (M02 tells the rest). During the Cristero War's suspension of public worship (1926) \"the original image was secretly removed for safekeeping and replaced… by a copy painted by Rafael Aguirre\", and it was reinstalled in June 1929. On the hill: the Capilla del Pocito (1777–91), built around a healing well, and the Capilla del Cerrito where the apparition is placed; the page says the foundations of a pre-Hispanic Tonantzin shrine lie beneath later buildings. 12 December drew vast crowds; the old church suffered subsidence and periodic repairs. The odd detail: For roughly three years the nation's holiest image was not the original — a copy hung in the basilica while the real one was \"kept safe\" somewhere, and nobody outside the secret could say which.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Three layers: the period says a miracle, the folklore says a Tonantzin under the hill, the hidden fact says the copy was never returned — or the original was copied twice. IHS and the Church both need to know which one is the one in the case.",
