      "summary": "The castle on the hill was the official presidential residence from 1882 to 1934; residents listed: Porfirio Díaz (1876–80; 1884–1911), Carranza (1915–1920), Obregón (1920–24) and Calles (1924–28). Cárdenas broke with tradition and moved the residence to Los Pinos in 1934, then made the castle the National Museum of History (decreed February 1939; opened to the public 27 September 1944 — modern). History on the hill: the Niños Héroes, 13 September 1847, the young cadets who died defending the castle (their mural is over the main entrance); Emperor Maximilian and Carlota, 1864–67, who renovated it. (M01: a bomb was thrown at Obregón's car in Chapultepec on 13 November 1927.) The page carries no verified ghost stories; anything the GM uses about Carlota's ghost or the Niños Héroes is unsourced. The odd detail: A hilltop castle lived in by an emperor and then by a run of presidents (the page names Díaz, Carranza, Obregón and Calles for this window) was given up as a residence by Cárdenas, who then made it a museum of the very regimes that had lived in it.",
