      "summary": "The Colegio Alemán (the German school) was founded in 1894; a former campus stood at 43 Benjamín G. Hill in Hipódromo Condesa (now part of Universidad La Salle — modern use). The German ambassador Heinrich von Eckardt received the Zimmermann Telegram (1917) proposing a Mexican–German alliance (the page's account). M01 notes the German embassy supported the Gold Shirts (Camisas Doradas) in 1934–35. The German legation's street address in the 1930s, the German Club and the Casino Alemán are not confirmed on any page read (UNVERIFIED). The Condesa page I tried 404'd, so nothing on the racetrack quarter is confirmed here. The odd detail: A German school in a racetrack district named for a countess (Hipódromo Condesa) — the capital's German colony lived beside its horse-racing crowd.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium on the school; Low on the legation. • UNVERIFIED",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A schoolteacher at the Colegio is a Cabal-side (pro-German) agent; his students carry messages. The Golden Circle would want the same teacher.",
