      "summary": "A separate town until the Federal District was reorganised; it \"remained independent of Mexico City through the colonial period\" and was designated a borough in 1928 when the DF was divided into 16 administrative divisions (Wikipedia's wording; the Organic Law of 31 December 1928 created a Central Department and 13 delegations — the count of sixteen is later). Cortés lived here 1521–22 while Tenochtitlan was rebuilt — though \"Cortés never lived at the site\" of the Casa Municipal despite the plaque; he occupied lands of the local lord Juan de Guzmán Iztolinque, where La Conchita church now stands. The Casa Colorada allegedly housed La Malinche (unverified); local legend says Cuauhtémoc was tortured here. Frida Kahlo's Casa Azul, a deep-blue house on Londres Street (early 20th c.), is where she was born in 1907. Residents listed: Diego Rivera, Frida Kahlo, Dolores del Río, Salvador Novo, Octavio Paz, Mario Moreno (Cantinflas), and from 1937 Leon Trotsky. (M02: Callejón del Aguacate legend.) The odd detail: The plaque in the Casa Municipal says Cortés lived there — the page says he never did.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A village of artists is a network of safe houses; the IHS could use Cortés's not-house as a message drop — \"Cortés slept there.\"",
