      "summary": "A circular pyramid with a conical base, begun a few centuries BCE (Late Preclassic), abandoned c. 150–200 CE, then buried by the eruption of Xitle (dates given range from 245–315 CE to c. 400 CE), leaving the Pedregal de San Ángel lava field (about 80 km², over 10 m deep in places). Byron Cummings of the University of Arizona explored it 1922–25 with support from the National Geographic Society; he found a ritual chamber he named a \"kiva\", likening it to Southwestern US structures. Eduardo Noguera excavated burials at Cuicuilco A in 1939. (The University campus and the Telmex building built over it are modern.) The odd detail: An Arizona archaeologist looked at a buried Mexican pyramid and saw a Pueblo kiva — the Americans reading Mexico as an extension of the American Southwest, two decades before the setting's Southern War made that a border policy.",
