      "summary": "The Spanish-side Virgin of the capital: a 27 cm image that a soldier of Cortés (the Wikipedia page names \"Gonzalo Rodríguez de Villafuerte\"; Arqueología Mexicana names Juan Rodríguez de Villafuerte, https://arqueologiamexicana.mx/mexico-antiguo/la-virgen-de-los-remedios) carried in the Noche Triste and hid under a maguey. In 1810 the image was moved FROM the Naucalpan sanctuary TO the Cathedral in Mexico City (29–31 October 1810), where Viceroy Venegas dressed her as a general and proclaimed her patroness of Spain and guardian of the Spanish army (Wikipedia's Los Remedios page has the direction reversed; she went home in 1821–23, sources differ: http://cabezasdeaguila.blogspot.com/2010/07/el-traslado-de-la-virgen-de-los.html , https://www.milenio.com/cultura/la-virgen-de-los-remedios-una-imagen-con-grado-militar) — the royalists' answer to Hidalgo's Guadalupe. Feast day: 1 September, with white flowers. The page says nothing on 1920s–30s practice, the pyramid site or the drought processions (UNVERIFIED — commonly told, not read). The odd detail: A Virgin was made a general and given a rank in an army — and she outranked the Guadalupe that Hidalgo carried, in Spanish eyes.",
