      "summary": "A Carmelite hermitage begun 23 January 1606 (Friar Andrés de San Miguel: the first convent, ten hermitages and the wall); the first convent decayed by 1722; a second, adjoining, was finished in 1814; the Carmelites left in 1810 and the buildings served as a military headquarters. 1917: Carranza made it Mexico's first national park; 1937: the monastery was declared a national monument under Cárdenas. The forest: pines, oyamel firs, oaks, \"many brooks, ravines and waterfalls\"; its springs supplied Mexico City. \"Desierto\" means \"wild, sparsely populated place\"; \"Leones\" is the original landowner's surname, not the animals. The page has no documented catacombs, tunnels to the city or hauntings; if the GM wants them, they are unsourced. The odd detail: The \"Desert of the Lions\" is neither desert nor lions — \"desierto\" is an old word for a remote hermit retreat, and \"Leones\" is the first landlord's surname; it is a wet pine forest with waterfalls.",
