      "summary": "A pilgrimage sanctuary, reached on foot. Legend: about 1537, in a cave where the deity Oxtoteotl (per the page, an aspect of Tezcatlipoca) had been worshipped, the Augustinian friars Sebastián de Tolentino and Nicolás Perea went to destroy the idol and found instead a life-sized crucified Christ. The sanctuary was formally established in 1683, rebuilt 1721 and 1830; Charles III authorised its official title in 1783. Pilgrimages: first Friday of Lent, Holy Week, Pentecost; feast day 1 July. Pilgrims circle an ahuehuete tree three times, bathe in a sacred spring, wear flowers and dance to cleanse sins. (The modern figures — \"over two million annual visitors\", 50,000 on major feast days — are today's, not the 1930s.) The odd detail: The friars came to smash an idol and found a crucifix in its place — the pre-Hispanic god's cave is the Christian shrine.",
