      "summary": "A capital of \"between 80,000 and 90,000 inhabitants\" (the 1920 guide's figure) lying \"at the bottom of a deep gulf\" facing west under mountains of about 2,800 feet. Ships came in by alignment with two huge red range lights, \"one on the top of the Cathedral and the other on Fort National\"; a launch brought the customs officials alongside, passports were taken and kept overnight, and the dock was \"at the end of a long pier jutting out from the land\" (Kuser). The streets \"throughout most of the town\" had been put down under contract with an American firm in 1914, before the occupation, and were \"of excellent quality\" (Kuser); Franck (1920) found them \"excellently paved with asphalt\", \"scores of automobiles\", a small old-fashioned railway through the heart of the town, an electric-light service over the whole city, barefoot street-sweepers in palm-leaf hats. The old French street plan was wide but with poor sidewalks; the houses were mostly wooden, \"built as open as possible to every breeze\". The 1920 guide says an American colony of \"several hundred\" (not counting the forces of occupation) lived largely at La Coupe in the upper town, because the city \"hangs around the nineties\". The odd detail: The harbour is entered on a line between a church and a fort — the two lights are the cathedral's north tower and Fort National (Kuser; the cathedral's Wikipedia page independently says its north tower's cupola was the front light of a pair, via fetch summary).",
