      "summary": "Designed by the Paris-trained Haitian architect Georges Baussan (1874–1958), budget $350,000, work begun May 1914; the unfinished building was, per Wikipedia's fetch summary, \"partially destroyed by a mob\" after Sam's death in 1915, and finished in 1920 by U.S. naval engineers: \"white-painted reinforced concrete\", two storeys, central domed pavilion with matching end pavilions. Kuser (1919–20) says it is \"the fourth palace to be built on the same site, one of the others having been set on fire and destroyed, and the other two ruined through explosions\" — the presidents \"killed by means of their own powder\", because a president \"had been unable to trust anyone with the keeping of the national supply of ammunition and was forced to keep it in his own palace\". One wing was already occupied by the President and his family. Franck: \"unfinished, snow-white\", \"larger and more sumptuous than our own White House\", one of the two glazed buildings in Port-au-Prince (\"perhaps … in all Haiti\"). Seabrook (1929): a \"great white glaring stairway\" up past sentries, the palace \"more permanently and safely inhabited by his Excellency Louis Borno\" than his predecessors'. Wikipedia: the 2010 quake wrecked it; demolished 2012. The odd detail: The previous palace exploded with a president inside (8 August 1912 — Wikipedia, via fetch summary: President Leconte). The new palace was built by the Americans around a burnt-out shell of a lynch mob's work.",
