      "summary": "\"A laid-out park with a bronze of Dessalines, the 'Founder of Haitian Independence,' in the center; and at the end a grandstand\" (Kuser); Franck, in the sun: \"vast sun-scorched\", bare, with \"a Napoleonic statue of Dessalines, his telltale complexion disguised by the kindly bronze\" and \"a modest and deeply tanned Venus of Melos\", with a legend that it had once been wooded until a former president had it cleared of assassins' hiding-places. By 1929 Seabrook found \"a bandstand, lawns, monuments, drill grounds, and boulevards\", and every Easter the square filled with straw-stuffed effigies of Judas and Pilate's soldiers that the crowds, machetes in hand, tore up (a Catholic folk custom, in Seabrook's telling, where \"both Americans and upper-class Haitians\" watched). The Club Port-au-Prince (a young men's club) stood on the square \"next to Brigade Headquarters\" (Kuser). The Wikipedia page for the square returned nothing. The odd detail: Seabrook's own juxtaposition: the Easter crowd dragging effigies through the square in 1928 is \"the same identical crowd\" that in 1915 tore Sam apart at the legation and \"dragged his mangled torso at the end of a rope through this same Champs de Mars\". (His reading; the Easter rite itself is a Catholic custom, not \"voodoo\".)",
