      "summary": "Kuser was in the town for the caco raid at 4 a.m. on 15 January 1920 (\"3,000 cacos are marching into town by the Hasco Road\"): about a quarter-mile from town the column divided: one along the water front by the slaughterhouse, the other two inland \"around Belleair hill, by the radio station\". Marines \"opened fire with Brownings and machine guns\"; the cacos fought from corners and windows and fired some of the native \"cailles\" in the poor section. By daybreak \"over 150 were captured or killed\", three Marines wounded, one fatally; survivors were driven \"into a closed valley back of Belleair\" and machine-gunned. Solomen Janvier was found asleep in his own house and boasted every attack on the capital had been his. Numbers were 3,000, then 1,500 (\"as claimed by the men at Hasco\"), then Kuser's own guess of about 300 actual fighters; the market rumour next morning said 2,000,000 cacos and 1,000,000 dead. M04 (from Wikipedia) has this as Batraville's Bel-Air raid, \"over 300 killed or captured\". The two figures differ. The odd detail: A radio station on the hill above the poor quarter in 1920 — the Marines' wireless was on the caco route.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the eyewitness; the casualty count differs from M04's — flag.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The hill with the radio station is where a GM puts a listening post — and a place from which someone can see the town burn. The \"2,000,000 cacos\" rumour is a period model of how a rumour becomes a legend overnight.",
