      "summary": "Wikipedia (via fetch summary): founded 1831 by President Boyer and named for Alexandre Pétion; a residential and touristic hill town; the Club de Pétion-Ville was built \"in the 1930s\" (nine-hole golf, tennis, a pool); the Cabane Choucoune dance club was built in 1940 by Max Ewald. The 1920 guide: La Coupe, the upper-town district of wealthy natives and most of the American colony, \"at an altitude of 1,200 feet\", cooler, \"some fine residences … embowered among palms, breadfruit, mangoes\", a natural bath under lofty trees. Kuser rode from Pétion-Ville up mountain trails with mules and gendarme guides. Seabrook lived in a \"dilapidated but beautiful garden of several acres\" and \"faded pink\" house on a lane running toward Pétion-Ville, remembers a Marine officer's villa there where they played bridge, and names Dr Price-Mars as \"of Petionville\"; the market \"behind the church\" is where he bargained for gifts (M04 for Price-Mars). The odd detail: The Pétion-Ville club was built in the 1930s — a golf course made by the occupation era's money, on the ridge above the cane plain.",
