      "summary": "Niles (1926): Bizoton-les-Bains, \"a tiny village on the road out of Port-au-Prince, along the north shore of the southern peninsula\", small wooden houses with corrugated-iron roofs among thatched cottages; the poet Oswald Durand once lived there; gas-girls patrol the streets selling kerosene and the peasants light torches \"out along the Bizoton Road\"; it lies with Carrefour beyond. Niles also mentions \"the famous cannibalism case at Bizoton\" tried under President Geffrard — a sensational 19th-century prosecution (the killing was in December 1863 and the eight condemned were executed on 13 February 1864; historians dispute the case — no trial transcripts survive and the confessions were obtained under torture: https://www.executedtoday.com/2014/02/13/1864-bizoton-affair-executions/ and https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/the-trial-that-gave-vodou-a-bad-name-83801276/) that Haitian hosts told her had been used to slander their country. M04 places the 28 July 1915 landing \"at Bizoton/Port-au-Prince\". William Beebe's Beneath Tropic Seas (1928) photographs a \"Haitian Shore at Bizoton and the Coral Reefs\": his 1927 diving expedition worked from here (Beebe not read beyond the captions). The odd detail: One small village holds the most notorious \"Vodou\" trial in the foreign press, the Marines' first landing beach and a scientific diving base — three layers of one place.",
