      "summary": "Founded 1670 as Cap-Français by a dozen colonists under Bertrand d'Ogeron; capital of French Saint-Domingue 1711–1770; nearly destroyed by the 7 May 1842 earthquake (est. magnitude 8.1; \"about 10,000 dead\" is the Cap-Haïtien article's figure, but the earthquake's own Wikipedia article gives 5,300 — the sources conflict); the site of the Battle of Vertières, 18 November 1803 (Wikipedia via fetch summary). Wikipedia adds one occupation fact: its north–south streets were renamed with single letters by the Americans. Franck (1920): \"the second city of Haiti, sometimes called the 'capital of the North'\", once \"the Paris of America\", now with \"old stone ruins\" stretching out to the lighthouse, wide streets still stone-paved with French names, a cinema, business houses \"in most cases owned by foreigners\", the acrid smell of raw coffee spread on canvas in the squares and old women \"thigh-deep in coffee\" in the warehouses, a large market, a \"blue vaulted cathedral\", \"American marines are everywhere in evidence\", and a short railway (about twenty miles) to Grande-Rivière and Bahon. Franck also describes election day under the Marines: each voter dips his right forefinger in nitrate of silver (some district commanders use iodine) so that no man votes twice. The odd detail: The election was policed by a black fingernail — a stain that stays on the hand until election day is over, and which the mixed-race elite (\"gens de couleur\") tried to cheat by dipping the wrong finger (Franck's telling, with his condescension).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
