      "summary": "Marines took Monte Cristi on 1 June 1916 (M04). Franck (1920): \"a considerable town\", wide unpaved streets; the \"commander-in-chief of Monte Cristi, a Marine captain\" gave the travellers his whole house; the \"Morro\", \"a great bulking rectangular hill\"; in the plaza \"a near replica of the Eiffel Tower, its open-work steel frame crowned by a large four-faced clock some fifty feet above\". His tale (an anecdote at Dominican expense): a congressional candidate promised each town what it wanted; Monte Cristi asked for a town clock, the tower went up \"without waiting for … congressional sanction\", the bill never got approved, and \"with the sudden coming of the Americans\" the town was presented with a foreign-issued invoice reading \"To one clock and tower, Dr $16,000 — Please Remit\". M04: bodies were thrown into the sea at Monte Cristi in Oct 1937. The odd detail: The $16,000 clock tower the occupation billed the town for — \"the ridiculous Americans who had taken in charge the revenues of the country brought with them the absurd doctrine that municipalities should pay their bills\" (Franck's joke, whose mockery is his).",
