      "summary": "Wikipedia (via fetch summary): La Romana was founded in 1897 as an oil town; after 1917 the South Porto Rico Sugar Company's \"mammoth Central Romana mill\" made it \"a one-company town\"; the mill \"was the only sugar operation not taken over by Rafael Trujillo\" (Gulf+Western, Casa de Campo etc. are much later). Franck (early February 1920; the government boat ran there): \"though it is in Dominican territory, La Romana is virtually American, a vast estate belonging to a great sugar company of Porto Rico\": one of the \"huge centrals\" as in Cuba — \"big-business atmosphere\", \"long rows of excellent dwellings built of light coral rock along the edge of the jagged coast\", its own stores, clubs and movies, standard-gauge railway, \"bateys of white wooden huts raised on stilts\"; about a quarter-million acres, only 16,000 under cane; the company was pushing back \"the forests and the bandits\"; it employed \"from 7500 to 9000 men, of whom a bare hundred are Americans\", the rest \"chiefly Haitians and Porto Ricans\", plus West Indians; wages $1.20 a day for cane-cutters to $4 for mechanics, 12-hour shifts and a 20 percent bonus; English, Puerto Rican and Dominican schools. Franck's own gloss on the Dominicans (\"too Castilian to work for a living\") is period contempt, not a fact. The odd detail: The estate is fifteen times larger than the cane it grows (250,000 acres; 16,000 planted) — the rest is forest and pasture \"the company is pushing back\".",
