      "summary": "M04: Olivorio Mateo (\"Papá Liborio\") was born here (1876), vanished in a 1908 hurricane and returned after seven days as a healer-prophet; he was killed on 27 June 1922 (date confirmed) by a detachment of the Dominican constabulary (the \"P.N.D.\") under a Capt. Williams and Lt. Luna — not, in the Dominican accounts, by Marine riflemen as such (whether Williams was a US Marine is UNVERIFIED) — and his body was taken to San Juan, photographed and shown to thousands (the Acento piece says paraded \"throughout the city\"; a town-park display is in other accounts I did not open) to show he could die. M04 also puts the galipote legend in this same valley. I found no period source for the town itself (buildings, streets, garrison). The odd detail: The occupation's constabulary chose a town square as the place to show a dead messiah — the same logic as the Péralte door, one border away (M04).",
