      "summary": "Murphy, the judge who had presided over the 1925–26 Ossian Sweet trials (Clarence Darrow for the defence), won the mayoralty after the 1930 Bowles recall and faced 100,000 unemployed by summer 1931; he built relief committees and convened the first US Conference of Mayors in 1933. Roosevelt sent him to Manila as Governor-General (1933) and then High Commissioner (1935–36), where he sympathised with tenant farmers. As governor he refused to evict the Flint strikers. Roosevelt made him Attorney General in 1939 (he founded a Civil Liberties Unit) and put him on the Supreme Court in 1940. He never married. The odd detail: A Catholic bachelor who was mayor of the fourth city, colonial governor of the Philippines, and the man who kept the Guard from the strikers — all within seven years.",
