      "summary": "Robert R. McCormick, a 25% owner of WGN (named for the Tribune's motto \"World's Greatest Newspaper\"), put \"Chicagoland\" into common use (first documented on the Tribune's front page, July 27, 1926), named his Wheaton estate after the 1918 battle he had served in, and used the paper to impose his own simplified spelling (the crusade was \"quixotic\" and mocked by rivals). He compared the New Deal to Communism. For a period in 1935, to protest Rhode Island's Democratic judiciary, he displayed a 47-star flag outside the Tribune building with Rhode Island's star removed — until told it was illegal. His wife Amy died in 1939 and he became a near-recluse until remarrying in 1944. The odd detail: The most powerful publisher in the Midwest altered the national flag outside his own building to punish a state.",
