      "summary": "Authorized 1927, first patients December 1930; by 1936 a 1,200-acre self-contained town with farms, fire department, police and its own power plant. In 1939 a six-month typhoid epidemic — suspected contaminated water leaking into the supply — killed 37, sickened hundreds, and produced 45 runaways. Population peaked at 8,195 in 1954; closed December 31, 1985; the hospital cemetery remains. The odd detail: The newest asylum in the state, built as a model, poisoned itself through its own pipes in the decade's last year, and forty-five patients used the chaos to walk away.",
