      "summary": "Legend says a young woman in a white dance dress hitches rides on Archer Avenue and vanishes at the cemetery gate; she is said to have quarrelled with her boyfriend at the Oh Henry Ballroom, walked home, and been killed by a hit-and-run driver. Reports begin in the 1930s; the first named witness is Jerry Palus, who in 1939 danced with her at Liberty Grove and Hall and drove her to the cemetery, where she vanished. Candidate identities: Mary Bregovy (killed in a downtown auto crash 1934) and Anna \"Marija\" Norkus (d. 1927 coming home from the Oh Henry — but buried at St. Casimir, not Resurrection). The ballroom itself is real and dated: John Verderbar's Oh Henry Park (1921), burned 1930, rebuilt as the Oh Henry Ballroom for $100,000 and reopened May 3, 1931 (the version Mary would have danced in), renamed Willowbrook in 1959, destroyed by fire October 28, 2016. The odd detail: The ballroom Mary left burned twice — once in 1930, before the legend, and once in 2016; the 1931 rebuilding is the building of the legend. Palus's 1939 encounter is the only one inside the decade.",
