      "summary": "Joseph Weil's cons — fake banks staffed with hired \"customers\", oil land he did not own, a Colorado silver mine sold to Andrew Mellon's brother's circle for a reported $500,000, \"talking\" dogs, rigged prize fights, and (by his own claim) $2 million from Mussolini — added up, per biographer W. T. Brannon, to more than $8 million; he served only about six years in total (including Leavenworth). His 1948 autobiography's creed: \"Each of my victims had larceny in his heart.\" The odd detail: The classic Chicago con of the decade was a whole fake bank — a rented lobby, tellers, customers, all actors — run for one mark.",
