      "summary": "Brand new. Holabird & Root's Art Deco tower opened June 9, 1930: 44 storeys, 604 ft, grey Indiana limestone under a copper pyramid roof, built for $11.3 million — and Chicago's tallest building from 1930 until 1965. Inside, a six-storey trading room held what was then the world's largest trading floor (19,000 sq ft) with octagonal pits for each commodity. It closes the vista down the LaSalle Street banking canyon, so every banker on LaSalle looks south at it. On the roof stands John H. Storrs's Ceres, goddess of grain: 31 ft tall, 6,500 lb of aluminium in 40 pieces, a sheaf of wheat in one hand and a bag of corn in the other. The odd detail: Ceres has no face. Storrs told the Tribune (May 4, 1930) that \"the face, too, is suggested, inasmuch as there are no regular features\" — from the street she would read only as a silhouette. Within weeks the soot blackened her, and she was not cleaned for 12 years. Beneath the trading floor ran over 2,700 miles of hidden telephone and telegraph wire.",
