      "summary": "Opened May 2, 1927 as the largest hotel in the world: 3,000 rooms, about $30 million. Built by James W. Stevens and his son Ernest (the family also ran the Illinois Life Insurance Company and owned the Hotel La Salle), it sold itself as a \"City Within a City\": its own bowling alley, barber shop, movie theatre, ice-cream shop, drug store and a rooftop miniature-golf course, the \"High-Ho Club\". The odd detail: The Depression broke the Stevens family. Illinois charged the owners with financial corruption; they were convicted of embezzlement, then the conviction was reversed unanimously on appeal. The hotel went into receivership and by the late 1930s was valued at about $7 million — under a quarter of its cost. (In 1942 the Army bought it for barracks — out of window.)",
