      "summary": "New in 1928, built on a 1925 bond issue: a 13–14-storey stone-and-buff-brick building designed by Charles W. Kallal, housing the Detective Bureau, the First Precinct, the Bureau of Records and Identification, the Vehicle Bureau, the superintendent's office and several branch courts (Domestic Relations, Boys' Court, Speeders' Court). A covered prisoner concourse and three dedicated prisoner elevators moved the arrested to cells and courtrooms out of public view. In 1938 the police bought the Northwestern crime lab and moved it here. The odd detail: The 8th floor long housed the city's Film Review Board, which censored every movie shown in Chicago. Cost overruns of about $595,000 came from redesigns ordered mid-build by Commissioner Michael Hughes.",
