      "summary": "America's first independent forensic crime laboratory, founded in fall 1929 with money from Burt A. Massee — foreman of the coroner's jury on the St. Valentine's Day Massacre. Directed by Col. Calvin Goddard, the firearms-identification pioneer who matched the massacre bullets to Capone's Thompsons. It examined \"blood, bombs, bones, bullets, code messages, counterfeits, dust, finger and foot-prints\", ran training courses for police and published the American Journal of Police Science. Leonarde Keeler brought his lie detector in 1930 and headed the lab from 1936. In 1938 the Chicago police bought it for $25,000 and moved it into headquarters on South State. The odd detail: A massacre created American forensic science: the lab exists because a jury foreman was horrified by what he saw in a Clark Street garage.",
