      "summary": "Towertown, named for the Water Tower, was Chicago's bohemia — studios, tearooms, salons, speakeasies and forums west of the Gold Coast. At its heart was Washington Square Park (\"Bughouse Square\"), the city's oldest small park (1842), where from the 1910s to the 1960s soapbox orators argued everything from free love to revolution; the orators even elected their own \"king\". Around the corner, down Tooker Alley at 18 Tooker Place, was the Dill Pickle Club (1914/1917–1933), founded by IWW organiser John \"Jack\" Jones in an old barn: lectures, debates and plays. Its door read \"DANGER\" under a green light, with the order to \"Step High, Stoop Low and Leave Your Dignity Outside.\" Emma Goldman, Clarence Darrow, Ben Reitman, Big Bill Haywood and Aimee Semple McPherson spoke there; Sandburg, Sherwood Anderson and Ben Hecht drank there. The odd detail: Jones was arrested for selling liquor in 1931 and collected 150 citations in one winter; the club was finally shut in 1933 under a rarely used law against dance halls near churches. Bughouse Square was also a known meeting place for gay men.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A Sunday at the Pickle on \"The Existence of the Dead\" draws a speaker who is not on the bill. Newberry's rare-book room across the square holds the one book he quoted from.",
