      "summary": "The only island actually built from Burnham's 1909 Plan: a man-made peninsula of about 119 acres, joined by a causeway at 12th Street. Its first tenant, the Adler Planetarium, opened May 12, 1930 — the first planetarium in the United States, a 12-sided building by Ernest Grunsfeld Jr., paid for ($500,000) by Sears executive Max Adler. Its star projector was a Zeiss, made in Germany, the same technology unveiled at Munich in 1923. It also held the Mensing Collection of antique astronomical instruments bought at auction in Amsterdam. Director: Northwestern astronomer Philip Fox. In 1933–34 the island was the site of the Century of Progress fair (R04). The odd detail: The largest collection of historic scientific instruments in the Western Hemisphere — astrolabes, armillary spheres, old star charts — sits on an artificial island in the lake.",
