      "summary": "A hidden railway: about 60 miles of two-foot-gauge electric railway dug from 1899, in revenue service from August 15, 1906, run by the Chicago Tunnel Company. Egg-shaped tunnels 7½ ft high and 6 ft wide carried merchandise, coal in and ash out for downtown buildings, and excavation spoil. In 1914 it had 132 locomotives and more than 2,000 cars; in 1929 it still ran 200–300 train movements a day. It connected to 26 railroads and to Field's and Carson Pirie Scott by elevator, and blew cooled air to theatres. It went bankrupt in 1909 and was reorganised in 1912; it limped on until 1959. The odd detail: Every major store, hotel and office on the network has a door 40 ft down that the public never sees.",
