      "source_line": 496,
      "cited_by_v2": [],
      "collection": "Chicago & Great Lakes",
      "synopsis": "Chicago drinks through offshore \"cribs\" — stone and steel towers far out in the lake that draw water from near the bottom into tunnels about 200 ft under the lakebed. Nine were built 1865–1935: the Two-Mile Crib (1865), Four-Mile Crib (1891), 68th Street Crib (1892), the Carter H. Harrison Crib (1900), the Edward F. Dunne Crib (1909), and the William E. Dever Crib (1935) among them.",
