      "summary": "\"Dunning\" was a word before it was a neighbourhood. In 1851 the county chose this spot for its poorhouse and insane asylum; a separate asylum building followed in 1870, and in 1912 the county handed the whole property to the State of Illinois as the Chicago State Hospital (closed 1970). The paupers were moved out to Oak Forest in 1911, leaving Dunning an asylum. Next to it lay the Cook County potter's field: about 38,000 people buried between the 1850s and the 1920s — the destitute and the asylum's dead. The railroad depot took the name of a local landowner, David S. Dunning (1865). The odd detail: Burials there stopped in the 1920s, and by the 1930s the county's dead were being buried at Oak Forest instead — so the 38,000 lie in a field nobody tends, beside a working asylum. (Reburial of later discoveries and the 2002 Read–Dunning Memorial Park are modern.)",
