      "summary": "A square mile of pens, chutes, railroad sidings and packing houses — Swift, Armour and the rest — where the Midwest's animals came to die. You entered through the limestone Stone Gate (1877) on Exchange Avenue; the Stockyard Inn fed the buyers; the smell covered the South Side when the wind was wrong. On May 19, 1934, fire destroyed about 90 percent of the yards, including the Exchange Building and the Exposition building — about $6 million in damage, one employee and 8,000 cattle dead. The International Amphitheatre rose at 42nd & Halsted in 1934 for the International Live Stock Exposition. The odd detail: The 1934 fire cut power, gas and phones to the district (the 09-22 archive has this as H02/H03) — a whole district dark for a night.",
