      "summary": "The university neighbourhood (UofC founded 1891) along the Midway Plaisance, the 1893 fair's old amusement strip, running from Jackson Park to Washington Park. Frank Lloyd Wright's Robie House (1908). Restrictive covenants — backed by the university — kept Black families out. Promontory Point at 55th Street was built into the lake in the 1930s. The Oriental Institute, founded 1919 by Egyptologist James Henry Breasted with Rockefeller money, moved into its Gothic building at 1155 E. 58th St, completed 1930 and dedicated 1931: finds from Persepolis, Megiddo (the Megiddo ivories) and Luristan, a colossal Tutankhamun statue, and a 40-ton lamassu — a winged, human-headed bull — from Khorsabad. Leopold and Loeb grew up in neighbouring Kenwood. The odd detail: A 40-ton Assyrian guardian spirit stands in a Chicago basement-level gallery; the Institute's excavation at Megiddo — Armageddon — ran through the 1930s.",
