      "summary": "A Czech industrial town just over the city line, grown from 16,310 (1900) to 66,602 (1930). Capone moved his operation here to escape Chicago police; the 1924 town elections were gang-violent, and he ran things from the Hawthorne Inn, where on September 20, 1926 a motorcade of Weiss/Moran gunmen raked the first-floor restaurant with gunfire. The town's other giant: Western Electric's Hawthorne Works (1905–1983), up to 45,000 workers making telephone equipment — the site of the Hawthorne studies (1924–32) that gave us the \"Hawthorne effect\" (people change when watched). About 220 of its employees, many Czech, died in the 1915 Eastland capsizing on the way to a company picnic. The odd detail: The same crossroads held the gangster headquarters and the factory whose scientists proved that being watched changes behaviour.",
