      "synopsis": "Original note includes uncertainty or estimates: The name is older than the people — French settlers called the river-bottom land \"black\" for its soil (the Savoyard river underneath was buried as a sewer in 1827). By the 1920s and 30s restrictive covenants had made it Detroit's Black district; Coleman Young, who arrived in 1923, remembered Italian, Syrian, German and Jewish neighbours.",
