      "summary": "Chicago, like New York, Philadelphia and Boston, began dial service with numbers of three letters and four digits (3L-4N) — the first three letters of the exchange name, printed in capitals, then four digits (the owner's examples: WABash, HARrison, DEArborn). Directories and advertisements printed the significant letters of the exchange in bold capitals. A Chicago exchange name recorded in the reference is WRigley (for Wrigley Field). A pay-telephone call cost five cents in the 1930s (two cents in the late 1920s by the same source's national figures), and stayed a nickel into the 1950s. The pre-pay coin telephone debuted in Chicago in 1898. The odd detail: the number is the neighbourhood: each exchange name belonged to one central office serving one district, so an investigator could place a caller by his exchange (which district each Chicago exchange served is UNVERIFIED this session).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the 3L-4N format and the nickel; the specific Chicago exchange list (WABash, HARrison, DEArborn, etc.) and the neighbourhoods they served are from the prompt and period usage — UNVERIFIED this session; the date Chicago's Loop went fully dial is not confirmed. • which district each Chicago exchange served is UNVERIFIED this session",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A number with an exchange that closed in 1931 still rings. A GM can make exchanges into geography: every call the party traces lands them in a district.",
