      "summary": "The Press (Scripps-Howard) was the city's biggest paper, with circulation near 200,000 by the 1920s; Louis B. Seltzer became its 12th editor in 1928 and held the chair 38 years under the slogan \"The Newspaper That Serves Its Readers\" — a crusading, city-shaping editor (later notorious for the 1954 Sheppard campaign). The News belonged to Daniel R. Hanna Sr. from 1912; in 1932 Hanna's heirs handed it to Forest City Publishing Co., which suspended the Sunday News on 3 January 1933; it was \"staunchly Republican\" and never overtook the Press. The Plain Dealer was the morning paper of record. The odd detail: The News imported its circulation manager from Chicago's newspaper wars specifically to fight the Press on the street (see McBride).",
