      "summary": "Morris reports that Dean and many Legion leaders took regular target practice at the Wayne County Rifle and Pistol Club, \"regularly chartered\", which \"received its share of free ammunition from the United States War Department\". Congressman Samuel Dickstein of New York told the House he could trace the Legion's origin to correspondence between Dr Samuel J. Rubley, a captain in the Michigan National Guard's cavalry, and William Dudley Pelley of the Silver Shirts, and read a letter: \"…hope to have 50 Klansmen mounted in two weeks' time… I may be exceptionally blood-thirsty but I feel that the later winter snows will be tinged scarlet in the streets of Detroit.\" Each recruit was given a .38 bullet, and \"You can't quit the organization unless you are six feet under.\" The odd detail: Legion gunmen trained on federal ammunition, through a legal rifle club — the \"armoury\" was the War Department's.",
