      "summary": "Born in Wharton, Texas, on Christmas Day 1897, Edwards came to Chicago in 1931 as a social worker for the Joint Emergency Relief Commission, lived at the Abraham Lincoln Centre, worked with the Brotherhood of Sleeping Car Porters and the Progressive Miners, and wrote for the Associated Negro Press and the Defender on homelessness and international affairs. In the mid-1930s she led a travelling \"European Seminar of International Relations\" for social workers through Britain, Scandinavia, Austria, Germany and the Soviet Union, and then went to Spain to work with child refugees of the civil war, writing that \"Fascism degrades women.\" She died in New York 9 July 1953. The odd detail: A Cook County relief caseworker who took parties of social workers on tour through Europe's revolutions and came back to write it up for the Defender.",
