      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Chicago Police Department — the district stations",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(matrons), the Detective Bureau at City Hall, the Juvenile Court and the Morals Court of the Municipal Court",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Chicago%20Police%20Department%20%E2%80%94%20the%20district%20stations%20%2C%20the%20Detective%20Bureau%20at%20City%20Hall%2C%20the%20Juvenile%20Court%20and%20the%20Morals%20Court%20of%20the%20Municipal%20Court"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Women entered the department in 1885 as matrons to care for female prisoners. Marie Owens joined in 1891 with the rank of sergeant, enforcing child-labour and welfare law — recognised as the first woman police officer in the country — and retired in 1923. On 13 August 1913 the city formally appointed ten policewomen, Alice Clement among them (above). In 1918 Grace Wilson joined, \"possibly the first black female police officer in United States history.\" Their beat, in Chicago as elsewhere, was the \"protective\" one: lost children, \"children in street trades,\" truants and runaways, girls in dance halls and railway stations, the questioning of women prisoners and witnesses; a 1933 national handbook (Hutzel's, below) describes exactly this work. How many policewomen Chicago carried in the 1930s, their pay, and whether a formal Women's Bureau existed by then were NOT established from the pages read this session — see Leads. The odd detail: Sergeant Owens's rank — the first policewoman in America outranked most of the men in her station house, in 1891.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High on the milestones; Low on 1930s numbers (UNVERIFIED).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A policewoman PC in 1936 is, on paper, a child-and-girl protector who can walk into any dance hall, depot or rooming house without a warrant and question any woman without a man present — the widest civilian access in the city. Grace Wilson's 1918 hiring gives a Black policewoman PC a real precedent.",
