      "summary": "Ivan H. Walton of the University of Michigan spent the late 1930s — \"almost too late\" — tracking down elderly sailors of the schooner era in waterfront hospitals and bars and writing down their songs and lore; he recovered words and tunes for more than a hundred songs, some not heard for a century. He died in 1968 with the work unfinished; Joe Grimm edited it as Windjammers: Songs of the Great Lakes Sailors (Wayne State University Press, 1 February 2002; Lee Murdock transcribed the music; Michigan Notable Book 2003). The odd detail: The setting's own decade is the exact moment the last men who had sailed the lakes under canvas were dying in charity wards — and one professor was going bed to bed with a notebook.",
