      "summary": "Most of the city's \"orphans\" were not orphans. The Nursery and Half-Orphan Asylum took \"half-orphans and children of working mothers\" at $1 to $3 a week; the Orphan Asylum (capacity 200) took orphans and half-orphans aged 2–12; the Home for the Friendless (capacity 300 children and 50 women) was a temporary home for \"destitute women and children and mothers with infants\" — \"no boys over 12\". The Jewish homes were split by the community's own fault line: the Orthodox Marks Nathan home (capacity 400, free, ages 5–11) on the West Side and the German-Jewish Home for Jewish Orphans (244) on the South Side. In the Depression, these homes filled with children whose parents were alive, out of work and unable to feed them. The odd detail: \"Half-orphan\" was an admission category — the living parent paid by the week, like a boarding house, and visited on Sundays.",
