      "summary": "From 1930 \"Count\" Victor Lustig partnered with the Nebraska pharmacist William Watts and chemist Tom Shaw, who engraved the plates while Lustig ran a courier ring. The operation put \"thousands of dollars of counterfeit money (nicknamed 'Lustig money')\" into circulation \"each month for the next five years.\" He was arrested in New York on 10 May 1935. A key on him opened a Times Square subway locker holding \"$51,000 in counterfeit bills and the plates.\" He escaped the Federal House of Detention on 1 Sept 1935 by rope, was retaken 27 days later in Pittsburgh, and on 9 Dec 1935 got fifteen years plus five for the escape, served on Alcatraz. The money outlived him: in March 1939 Capt. Callaghan traced $17,880 in counterfeit $20s buried on a golf course to a two-million-dollar supply of Lustig notes. Indictments followed against Frankie Parker and August Englehart, and Englehart was alleged to have wholesaled the bills in Chicago at $30 per $100. (The 1939 paper says the supply was \"reportedly printed in Europe\", which differs from Wikipedia's Nebraska platemakers.) The odd detail: four years after Lustig went to Alcatraz, his money was still coming out of the ground, and Chicago was still the market price-setter: 30 cents on the dollar, the same rate von Buelow's Berlin notes fetched in 1933.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the Wikipedia facts and the 1939 report as printed. Where the notes were printed (Nebraska vs. \"Europe\") conflicts between sources, and the golf course's location is UNVERIFIED.",
