      "summary": "The 1938 register lists, at Mundelein, 43 Benedictine Sisters of Perpetual Adoration (mother house Clyde, Missouri; superior Sr. M. Thiadildis Kraus) whose stated \"Object\" was \"the Perpetual Adoration with the Most Blessed Sacrament exposed day and night,\" kept in the Perpetual Adoration Shrine of Our Lady of the Most Blessed Sacrament — \"a Memorial of the 28th International Eucharistic Congress\" (1926; R05) — and served by priests from St. Mary of the Lake. Two archdiocesan hospitals were run by the Poor Sisters of St. Francis Seraph of the Perpetual Adoration of Lafayette, Indiana: St. Francis, Evanston (26 sisters, a nursing school of 112) and St. James', Chicago Heights (17 sisters). The odd detail: The 1926 Congress left a permanent watch behind it: from then on, a sister was kneeling before the exposed Host at Mundelein every hour of every night, a few hundred yards from the seminary.",
