      "summary": "Elgin producers set up their own board of trade in 1870, where sale contracts were made and prices set; it established dairy prices for most of the country for about 40 years. Gail Borden (who patented sweetened condensed milk in 1853) opened his plant in 1865; in the late 1860s–70s it took 10,000 gallons a day from 6,000 cows on 107 nearby farms. The milk, butter and cheese sold from Elgin in 1892 equalled \"more than $300 million in today's dollars.\" The \"Elgin cut\" — the four-stick pound of butter — was popularised by the Elgin Butter Company and became the standard east of the Rockies (unverified — V_R38: not on the Northwest Quarterly page read). Search summaries add that as late as the 1930s Elgin alone had 30 dairies pasteurising and delivering milk, and that farmers in the 1930s glut accused Borden and other distributors of conspiring to depress prices (unverified — V_R38: the 30-dairies and the Borden accusation are search-summary claims, not on the page read). The county once had about 90,000 cows. The odd detail: A Fox River farm town's own board of trade set the butter price for most of the country for about forty years — and the stick of butter is still called the Elgin cut.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the Board and Borden figures; Medium for the \"30 dairies in the 1930s\" (search summary). • The milk, butter and cheese sold from Elgin in 1892 equalled \"more than $300 million in today's dollars.\" The \"Elgin cut\" — the four-stick pound of butter — was popularised by the Elgin Butter Company and became the standard east of the Rockies (unverified — V_R38: not on the Northwest Quarterly page read). • … late as the 1930s Elgin alone had 30 dairies pasteurising and delivering milk, and that farmers in the 1930s glut accused Borden and other distributors of conspiring to depress prices (unverified — V_R38: the 30-dairies and the Borden accusation are search-summary claims, not on the page read).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A price-fixing board is an oracle: what if the \"Elgin cut\" of butter was an offering, and the board's chalkboard had been reading something else? The farmers' 1930s revolt is the moment the pact fails.",
