      "summary": "From the county's timeline: 26 November 1930 — Rochester State Bank robbers arrested after a \"Bonnie and Clyde\"–style chase; 1931 — the Illinois Building, Springfield's first skyscraper (August); 12 September 1933 — the County Board ousts Sheriff Sam Metcalf \"on grounds of insanity\"; 18 February 1934 — the Illinois State Arsenal destroyed by fire set by a 10-year-old boy; August 1935 — a massive sale to liquidate the Edward Payne Stone Age Collection; 17 March 1937 — an explosion demolishes two houses on North Seventh Street, \"arson ring suspected, but authorities fail to obtain convictions\"; July 1935 — Lake Springfield dedicated; 1929 — St. John's Breadline opens; 1936 — the Springfield NAACP institutes the annual Webster Plaque; 1939 — the Illinois State Armory is desegregated \"under pressure from black residents\" and Springfield's Humphrey and Hunter Moody set an endurance-flying record (July–August). The riot's legacy: in 1908, on the search-summary account, 40 Black homes burned and about 2,000 Black residents were driven out (unverified — V_R38); the \"Badlands\" district where vice had been allowed to spread into the Black quarter is the riot's burnt ground; the NAACP took shape partly in response. The odd detail: A sheriff removed for \"insanity,\" a state arsenal burned down by a ten-year-old, and a \"Stone Age\" collection sold off in 1935 — all in the same five-year stretch of the county's timeline.",
