      "summary": "Black residents settled Rondo from the late 1800s, after Jewish families moved out; by the 1930s about half of St. Paul's Black population lived there. The neighborhood's institutions: St. Peter Claver Church (Catholic, established 1892 at 445 North Farrington), St. James African Methodist Episcopal (moved to 624 Central Ave. West in 1926), Pilgrim Baptist Church, the NAACP chapter (1913), the Sterling Club (1919, for African American professionals), the Credjafawn Social Club (1928, 678 Rondo — a grocery cooperative and credit union), and the Hallie Q. Brown Community Center (1929); newspapers included The Appeal and the St. Paul Recorder. Residents were Pullman porters and maids, doctors and lawyers, packinghouse hands and tradesmen. The Brotherhood of Sleeping Car Porters' St. Paul local (Local #3) held its first meetings on 13–14 January 1926 at Welcome Hall, Farrington and St. Anthony, with over 50 men attending. (unverified — V_R39: the Minnesota Spokesman-Recorder dates the first meeting 26 January 1926 at Welcome Hall (\"mid-January\" for the two calls); the 13–14 January dates, \"Local #3\" and the attendance are not on any page I could read) Interstate 94 (built 1956–68) later cut the neighborhood in half. The odd detail: The first Local #3 meetings, at Welcome Hall in 1926, were held by Rondo men who worked the sleeping cars — the labor-and-information network of the Twin Cities' Black world was, in large part, a railroad one.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. • Anthony, with over 50 men attending. (unverified — V_R39: the Minnesota Spokesman-Recorder dates the first meeting 26 January 1926 at Welcome Hall (\"mid-January\" for the two calls); the 13–14 January dates, \"Local #3\" and the attendance are not on any page I could read) Interstate 94 (built 1956–68) later cut the neighborhood in half.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A community whose information network runs along Pullman routes from Chicago to St. Paul is the natural clearing-house for a Bronzeville-to-Rondo chain of witnesses (cross-ref R14) — porters see the odd cargo before the Feds do.",
