      "summary": "At its peak in the 1930s Toronto had 96 Orange lodges; more than 8,000 Orangemen marched on 12 July 1932 past enthusiastic crowds (unverified — V_R40: the Canadian Encyclopedia page cited carries neither the 96 lodges nor the parade count; a search summary of that encyclopedia's Orange Order coverage gives 96 lodges and about nine thousand marchers at the height). Summaries of the Canadian Encyclopedia say that from 1850 to 1954 almost every mayor and alderman was an Orangeman, as were the chief of police and most policemen; the streak ended only with the mid-century election of Nathan Phillips. In the Depression many Orangemen stopped paying their dues; the Order's fall was completed by the war and Canada's turn from Britain toward America. The odd detail: The Order that ran Toronto's police and council marched on 12 July to celebrate the Battle of the Boyne — in a year (1932) when the Empire it swore to defend was, in the setting, a defeated, bankrupt Britain.",
