      "summary": "R08 has Weil's life. This entry is his 1930s record from the papers. Aug 1930: five months out of Leavenworth and promising \"legitimate business\", Weil was sought for selling Joseph Kindelberger of Kalamazoo $15,000 of stock \"in a Chinese oil well.\" Jan 1931: arrested, paraded through a Chicago lineup and taken to Rockford; he told the lineup, \"Sure, I am a con man–the best. But I've only taken from those who can afford the education.\" He beat that charge. Feb 1931: in jail at Gary. Apr 1931: held for taking $37,000 from Matthews Reinart, a retired Indianapolis grocer. Reinart was met at Martinsville by a \"J. A. Kirk\" and introduced to a \"G. A. Mayo\" who had made millions \"in Mexican oil\"; his stake grew to $300,000 on paper, and he handed over $37,000 \"collateral\" in Toledo. Dec 1939: \"Postal inspectors seized\" Weil in Chicago on mail-fraud charges for a $4,000 \"Mexican oil deal\" against a New York advertising man, Warren Edmonds. He faced a removal hearing before U.S. Commissioner Edwin K. Walker. He \"retired\" in 1941 after 27 months in the Atlanta federal prison for a phony oil-lease scheme. The odd detail: every one of his 1930s stings is oil in a far country: Chinese wells, Mexican oil, paper fortunes grown in a week.",
