      "summary": "Albert Parsons's widow kept speaking into her eighties. On May Day 1930 she spoke to thousands at the Ashland Auditorium (the speech was reprinted in congressional hearings on Communist activity). She worked for Angelo Herndon, Tom Mooney and the Scottsboro defendants, recovered from pleurisy in 1932 well enough to visit the 1933 fair, and joined the Communist Party in 1939. On March 7, 1942, she died in a fire at her house, about 91; her partner George Markstall died the next day of injuries from trying to save her. Her library of about 3,000 books was seized by the FBI after the fire and never returned; the Bureau denied knowledge of it. The odd detail: the government took the burned library of a 91-year-old anarchist and then said it didn't have it.",
