      "cited_by_v2": [],
      "collection": "Chicago & Great Lakes",
      "synopsis": "Original note includes uncertainty or estimates: \"Policy\" was the illegal but tolerated numbers lottery of Black Chicago: a player picked numbers (a \"gig\" of three), bet a nickel or a dime at a station, and the \"wheel\" — a named operation such as the Erie-Buffalo, Belmont or \"Old Reliable\" books — drew numbered balls from a drum twice or more a day. Slips came back in printed lists; runners, writers, pick-up men, cashiers and printers gave the Black Belt…",
