      "summary": "In 1946 the writer Carey McWilliams called Minneapolis \"the capital of anti-Semitism in the United States,\" with \"an iron curtain\" between Jews and non-Jews. The record (Minnesota Public Radio's oral-history documentary No Jews Allowed) lists what was closed to Jews in the 1930s: jobs at the banks, insurance companies and big corporations (Pillsbury, General Mills, 3M — which barred Jews from public-facing sales), membership in Kiwanis, Rotary, Lions and other service clubs, country clubs, and — for the Calhoun Beach Club — a sign that read \"No Jews, peddlers, dogs allowed.\" A 1946 survey found 40 per cent of the city's new subdivisions carried covenants against selling to Jews or African Americans. The pulpit voices included William Bell Riley (First Baptist Church, 1897–1942) and Luke Rader (River Lake Tabernacle; UNVERIFIED this session — brother of Chicago's Paul Rader, see R05), with Father Coughlin's radio sermons and Charles Lindbergh's name in the mix. The Jewish response was to build: Plymouth Avenue's institutions, a Jewish council (1940s) and Mount Sinai Hospital (late 1940s), founded after Jewish doctors were refused privileges elsewhere. The odd detail: The Twin Cities' biggest Jewish employer of the 1930s was, effectively, the Jewish community itself — because the flour mills and banks would not hire them.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the pattern; the quotes are from an MPR documentary (1992) summarizing the period. • River Lake Tabernacle; UNVERIFIED this session — brother of Chicago's Paul Rader, see R05",
