      "summary": "Mrs. Clothilde Marchand, wife of the sculptor Henri Marchand, who was making Seneca dioramas for the Buffalo museum, was killed with a hammer and a chloroform-soaked rag. A 60-year-old Seneca woman, Nancy Bowen, confessed; the UP headline of 8 Mar 1930 read \"OUIJA BOARD SEANCES LED TO ARTIST WIFE MURDER, INDIAN WOMAN CONFESSES.\" She was said to have acted at the instigation of Lila Jimerson (\"Red Lilac\"), 35, one of Marchand's models and his lover. On 17 Mar 1930 UP reported that the defence would offer \"a crudely-fashioned paper-doll effigy of the slain woman,\" small bottles of whisky and blackberry biscuits dug from the graves of three warriors on the reservation, as proof of an insanity case; Jimerson told reporters Bowen had tried three times to kill Mrs. Marchand by witchcraft first — once by carrying dirt from an Indian grave to Buffalo and placing it around the house, once by burning ground tobacco leaves and chanting to \"witches in air.\" In April the first trial became a mistrial and Jimerson pleaded guilty to second-degree murder from her hospital cot; Clarence Darrow was asked to appeal; she was ordered retried (June 1930) and a new jury was sworn in February 1931. (Outcome of the 1931 trial and Bowen's sentence: UNVERIFIED this session.) The trial exposed Marchand's many affairs with Seneca models, which he called \"a professional necessity.\" The odd detail: Grave-dirt ringed around a Buffalo house as a killing spell, and a paper doll of the victim as courtroom evidence — in 1930.",
