      "summary": "After construction superintendent Philip Meagher was shot on February 5, 1930, Col. Robert Isham Randolph, president of the Chicago Association of Commerce, formed a privately funded anti-crime committee on February 8, 1930. Its membership was secret; a 1932 Bureau of Investigation report named Randolph, Julius Rosenwald (Sears) and Frank J. Loesch, and Randolph later said utilities magnate Samuel Insull and Rosenwald were members. Randolph and Chief Detective Alexander Jamie persuaded U.S. Attorney George E. Q. Johnson to give Eliot Ness a special commission to go after the brewers and distillers. There are two conflicting accounts of a face-to-face meeting between Randolph and Capone; Capone is quoted as saying \"The Secret Six has licked the rackets. They've licked me.\" The group actually played little part in the tax conviction, collected false-arrest suits and corruption allegations, and died when the new mayor, Anton Cermak, withdrew police support after a female investigator, Shirley Kub, turned up at a bombing investigation. The odd detail: A private, secret committee of the city's richest men ran its own detectives and funded federal agents — and Insull, one of its backers, would himself flee the country as a fugitive in 1932 (Insull's flight is another lane's subject; not checked here).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on founding, founder and end date; Medium on membership (secret by design, reconstructed from a 1932 Bureau report). • and Ness's brother-in-law — UNVERIFIED this session",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A secret committee of millionaires that runs its own detectives is a perfect front. Half of the Six were fighting Capone; the other half needed him gone because he knew what they were — a Golden Circle cell or a vampire salon in business dress.",
