      "summary": "On 8 February 1930 the Chicago Association of Commerce formed its \"Crime Prevention and Punishment Committee,\" known as the Secret Six, led by Col. Robert Isham Randolph; FBI reports and the press named Julius Rosenwald (Sears), Frank J. Loesch, Samuel Insull, Edward E. Gore and George A. Paddock. Its chief detective, Alexander Jamie, said the name was \"a misnomer … maybe 150 members, maybe only six.\" The businessmen paid; dozens of hired agents ran stings and wiretaps, worked kidnapping and bombing cases, probed the tax assessor's bribes, and helped launch Eliot Ness's squad against the breweries. Capone himself said: \"The Secret Six has licked the rackets … They've licked me.\" It fell apart after a $30,000 false-arrest judgment, a shady bond-recovery deal with the gangster Gus Winkler, an unauthorised bomb investigation, a feud with State's Attorney John Swanson, and the hiring of a woman operative with a criminal record, Shirley Kub; Cermak pulled police support and it disbanded on 17 January 1933. The odd detail: One of the Six funding the war on the rackets was Samuel Insull — who by October 1932 was himself a fugitive from Cook County indictments.",
