      "summary": "Created by the Chicago Association of Commerce on February 8, 1930, three days after the shooting of construction superintendent Philip Meagher, the \"Secret Six\" was led by Col. Robert Isham Randolph; ex-Prohibition agent Alexander Jamie became chief investigator in October 1930. The claimed six have never been settled; names put forward include Randolph, utilities king Samuel Insull, Sears's Julius Rosenwald, Frank J. Loesch, Edward E. Gore and George A. Paddock — though one researcher argues it was really dozens of hired detectives. It backed Eliot Ness's special squad; it dissolved amid false arrests, corruption and even bigamy accusations against its agents, and lost police support under Mayor Anton Cermak. The odd detail: Chicago's anti-gang secret society kept its own membership secret so well that historians still argue who the six were.",
