      "summary": "On 6 March 1930 the Communist Party and the Trade Union Unity League brought some 50,000 people into the Chicago streets, \"marching through the streets 12 abreast for about four hours,\" to cap ten days of protest and police raids — more than 150 arrests in the first week of March, and the Party's headquarters raided and wrecked twice. The march ended with a meeting at the stockyards addressed by Clarence Hathaway and Nels Kjar. In Detroit the same day a two-hour battle between 25,000 demonstrators and 3,000 police, some mounted, put 26 in hospital. The odd detail: Only four months after the Crash, the city's first mass Depression march ended at the stockyards gate — the place most jobless men had once stood for work.",
