      "summary": "The colony's first opera — really a two-act zarzuela, Marina — was staged 8 March 1930 in full costume, directed by the tenor Silvano R. Ramos, temporarily in Chicago from Mexico City; the pianist-composer Francisco Bustamante had written the \"President Ortiz Rubio March\" for Mexican officials. The cast rehearsed at the home of William L. Ortiz, an artist tied to Hull House. México called it \"an unprecedented and outstanding success,\" with a full house. But on 11 March the same paper ran \"Mexicans Resent Yankee Slur,\" protesting a sarcastic Chicago Daily News review of the performance. Other stage work: a \"Pro Arte Hispano Mexicano\" company debut (8 Dec 1928, El Arcediano de Sangil as rendered); a \"Cuadro Artístico Olimpia\" (Apr–May 1931); Paco Perafán's zarzuelas (the 1931 Chin Chun Chan). The odd detail: the colony's premiere came with a newspaper insult, and México printed its reply within three days.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the premiere; the review's text was not read.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a zarzuela plot about a Spanish patriot — a stage for an aria that goes wrong.",
