      "summary": "The Municipal Tuberculosis Sanitarium began a Spanish-language campaign aimed at Chicago's roughly 10,000 Mexicans (Chicago Daily News, Oct 25–28, year not stated in the FLPS record) with 642 already under supervision; the opening meeting drew 400 at Hull House, with Dr. Benjamin Goldberg, sanitarium board member LeRoy Miller, Consul Rafael Aveleyra and Dr. E. García Treviño. On 30 March 1930 the Chicago Tuberculosis Institute was to inaugurate (announced 29 March) a Mexican health center at 840 S. Halsted that México called \"the first of its kind\" in the United States (unverified — V_M18: the \"first\" is the paper's claim, and the record is an announcement of the 30 March 4 p.m. ceremony), funded by Christmas Seals, with remarks by Aveleyra and Drs. William Evans, Goldberg and Arnold H. Kegel. A Cook County Hospital TB roster of Mexican patients ran in El Nacional on 2 Sept 1933. Earlier (20 Oct 1928) México condemned \"a doctor's insolent declarations\" — Dr. Goldberg's earlier statements about Mexicans — before he became the campaign's champion. The odd detail: the man the colony's paper attacked in 1928 opened its clinic in 1930.",
