      "summary": "Martin Quigley (b. Cleveland 1890; Chicago police reporter from 1910; bought Exhibitors Herald 1915; by 1930 owned Motion Picture Herald and Motion Picture Daily) lobbied for a Catholic moral code for films. Jesuit Fr. FitzGeorge Dinneen of Chicago took the idea to Cardinal Mundelein; Fr. Daniel A. Lord, S.J., went home to St. Louis and wrote the Code alone. Dinneen delivered it to Mundelein and Quigley to Will Hays. Mundelein leaned on Halsey, Stuart & Co. — a Chicago bond house and a major Hollywood financier — and the bankers pressed the studios. The producers' association adopted it on 31 March 1930. The odd detail: Hollywood's morals for a generation were set by a Chicago cardinal leaning on a Chicago bond house.",
