      "summary": "Frank J. Loesch (b. 9 April 1852; one of the Commission's organisers in 1919; its president from 1928 for five terms; the man who in 1928 warned Capone against more \"pineapple primary\" violence) picked 28 men from a list of about a hundred \"outstanding hoodlums,\" put Al Capone at the head and Ralph Capone second, and sent the names to the chief of police, the sheriff and every law officer in the county, calling them \"public enemies.\" His stated purpose was to keep the \"publicity light\" on the city's most prominent gangsters so that police and citizens would keep them under constant observation. The phrase was taken up by Hoover's Bureau for fugitives already under federal charge; between June 1934 and May 1936 it named four men in succession \"Public Enemy No. 1\" — Dillinger, Floyd, Nelson, Karpis — and the practice became the Ten Most Wanted list. Loesch was the one who, when Dillinger walked out of Crown Point, said: \"That's what might be expected of having a woman for sheriff.\" He died in 1944, aged 92. The odd detail: The Bureau's most famous phrase was a Chicago lawyer's press stunt, and the lawyer was seventy-eight when he coined it.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A civic list of twenty-eight names with a \"publicity light\" on it is a hunter's list. In the setting the Commission may have been told which names to leave off.",
