      "summary": "A candle set against oily rags on the West Block roof — by inmates James Raymond, Hugh Gibbons and Clinton Grate, officials said, to cover an escape — burned through a prison holding 4,300 men in space for 1,500 (unverified — V2_R06). Guards refused to unlock cells as the smoke came in; inmates overpowered guards to free others; 320–322 died and 230 were hospitalised. A riot followed and 500 soldiers from Fort Hayes came in with machine guns, bayonets and shoot-to-kill orders. Raymond and Grate were \"said to have died by suicide\" in the months after; historians doubt the arson story and read it as cover for the management of the fire. The odd detail: The three named arsonists, two of whom then died by their own hands in the same prison — a story that reads, in the owner's three layers, as a sacrifice to close a scandal.",
