      "summary": "A candle lit oily rags on the roof of the six-story West Block; 322 inmates burned or suffocated and 230 were hospitalised — one of the deadliest fires in North American prison history. Officials said three inmates (James Raymond, Hugh Gibbons, Clinton Grate) set it to escape; historians doubt it. Guards refused to unlock cells as the smoke came in; some prisoners overpowered a guard for his keys and freed others. Five hundred soldiers from Fort Hayes arrived with machine guns and \"shoot to kill\" orders; a riot followed and arriving firemen were stoned. The prison held far over capacity (1,990 against 700 as late as 1983). The odd detail: the men who freed the most prisoners that night were prisoners who had taken the keys by force.",
