      "id": "R43-005",
      "title": "The Ohio Penitentiary fire, the deeper cut",
      "date_start": "1930-04-21",
      "date_end": "1930-04-21",
      "date_label": "Easter Monday, 21 April 1930 (extends R06 and R13)",
      "category": "Ohio beyond Cleveland and Toledo, 1920–1940: the cities and their communities",
      "tags": [
        "beyond",
        "Cleveland",
        "Ohio Penitentiary",
        "deeper"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Preston E. Thomas",
        "Myers Cooper",
        "Hal Griswold",
        "Chester Himes",
        "James Raymond",
        "Hugh Gibbons",
        "Clinton Grate",
        "William R. Noel"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "The West Block of the Ohio Penitentiary",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "downtown Columbus, east of Neil Avenue — the ground is now Nationwide Arena in the Arena District",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=The%20West%20Block%20of%20the%20Ohio%20Penitentiary%2C%20downtown%20Columbus%2C%20east%20of%20Neil%20Avenue%20%E2%80%94%20the%20ground%20is%20now%20Nationwide%20Arena%20in%20the%20Arena%20District"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "A candle set against oily rags on the West Block roof — the official story — started the fire that killed 322 prisoners (early reports said 317) and hospitalised about 230 (other accounts say about 130 injured). The prison held roughly 4,000–4,300 men in space for 1,500; the cell block beside the fire held about 800, most already locked in for the night (unverified — V_R43). Guards refused to open cells. The cell keys were kept about 900 feet from where the fire started, and were missing when guards went to get them (Marietta Times, quoting the 1930 press). Warden Preston E. Thomas told investigators he was more worried about escapes than the fire and relied on guard \"common sense\" rather than an evacuation plan. Some inmates overpowered a guard for his keys and freed others; the convict-writer Chester Himes — an East High School graduate and former Ohio State student doing time for armed robbery — watched men working \"through the smoke with reckless haste\" to save each other and wrote it up as \"To What Red Hell\" for Esquire. Five hundred soldiers from Fort Hayes with machine guns and orders to shoot to kill, and about 1,000 National Guard, ringed the walls; inmates stoned the arriving firemen. State Welfare Director Hal Griswold demanded, \"Where were the keys to those cells?\" and Gov. Myers Cooper faced calls to suspend Thomas; the prisoners refused to testify about how it started. The prisoners, segregated by race, refused hard labour for two weeks. Five days after the fire the unclaimed dead were buried — whites at Eastlawn Cemetery, Black inmates at Evergreen Cemetery a mile away. The odd detail: The keys were 900 feet from where the fire started. And the deaths were sorted by race even in the burying — two cemeteries, one mile apart, one fire.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the event, the 322 dead, the keys, the burial split and the troops; Medium on the injured count and the exact population. • the cell block beside the fire held about 800, most already locked in for the night (unverified — V_R43).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The same prison put 315 men in its electric chair between 1897 and 1963, and Pierpont and Makley (R02) and Anna Marie Hahn (R06) ended there. Put the Death House and the West Block under one ground: something that feeds on the moment before a door is opened. A GM gets a prison-shaped haunting, a race-segregated burial trail for the Kabbalah/IHS to walk, and Company A of the 166th Infantry from Marietta (45 men, called out by the city hall fire bell on 29 April) as the ordinary boys who guarded the wreck.",
