      "summary": "Frank J. Loesch, president of the Chicago Crime Commission (a civic body he had helped organise in 1919), picked 28 names from a longer list of roughly 100 and published them in April 1930 as Chicago's \"public enemies\", with Al Capone at the head and his brother Ralph next. The New York Times of April 24, 1930 ran it as \"LISTS 28 GANGSTERS AS PUBLIC ENEMIES\", naming \"Capone, Moran, Diamond and Saltis among others\", with police plans to \"HARASS THEM\". In September 1930 Municipal Judge John H. Lyle turned the list into a legal weapon, issuing 26 vagrancy warrants against the named men (Chicago Daily Tribune, Sept 17, 1930) — the theory being that a man with no visible lawful job was a vagrant. By October papers were calling Ralph \"public enemy number 2\" and \"Scarface Al\" \"public enemy number one\" (Albuquerque Journal, Oct 10, 1930), the earliest found use of the exact phrase. Loesch said in 1933 that the point was to keep \"the publicity light shining\" on the gangsters. By 1934 every man on the original list had been removed one way or another (unverified — V_R01). The odd detail: The weapon against the most powerful criminal in America was a vagrancy warrant — the charge used on hoboes — because a millionaire gangster could not show a lawful occupation. (Sources disagree whether Ralph was number 2 or number 3.)",
