      "summary": "Frank J. Wilson of the Treasury's Intelligence Unit was ordered to Chicago on May 19, 1930 (his report says the fieldwork ran May 23, 1930 to October 30, 1931) at age 42. The key ledger — the daily-profit book of the Hawthorne Smoke Shop in Cicero, seized from the club's safe by police in the raid after the murder of Assistant State's Attorney William McSwiggin on April 26, 1926 — had sat unrecognised for four years; Wilson \"accidentally discovered\" it in a miscellaneous lot of papers left behind by Special Agent Pat Roche when Roche resigned. It carried no identifying marks. It showed profits split between \"A\", \"R\" and \"J\" (Wikipedia quotes the entry \"Frank paid $17,500 for Al\") and net profit of $587,721.95 over 24 months. Handwriting comparison identified the cashier, Leslie A. Shumway, who had vanished in April 1926; after a four-month search he was found in Miami on February 18, 1931, and his secret grand-jury appearance on February 24, 1931 was hidden from the defence until he walked into the courtroom. A second cashier, Fred Ries, was caught in St. Louis on August 30, 1930 just as a special-delivery letter from Jack Guzik's brother-in-law Louis Lipschultz ordered him to California; held in the county jail at Danville, Illinois on a $15,000 witness bond, he identified $224,704 in cashier's cheques bought under the name \"J. C. Dunbar\" at the Pinkert State Bank in Cicero — and the Chicago Association of Commerce paid for him to be hidden in South America until trial. Wilson also records a tip that Capone had brought five New York gunmen, \"cruising around in a blue sedan bearing a New York license tag\", to kill U.S. Attorney George E. Q. Johnson, SAC A. P. Madden, Pat Roche and Wilson himself (Wikipedia notes this was never substantiated). Wilson went on to become Chief of the U.S. Secret Service (1936/37–1946/47; sources disagree). The odd detail: The evidence that convicted Capone sat unrecognised among a departed agent's leftover papers for four years; and a star witness was shipped to South America on the money of the Chicago Association of Commerce — the body whose president founded the Secret Six.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High — Wilson's own report. The New York gunmen plot is Wilson's contemporaneous belief, not a proven fact.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Secret Service hook. This is the \"knew what Capone was and took him anyway\" seam: Wilson's handwriting hunt found Shumway, but the ledger had a fourth column the trial never used. Wilson's reward for keeping it out of court was his next job.",
