      "summary": "Marshall Field & Co. announced a $15 million wholesale building on 13 March 1927, doubled it to $30 million on 26 May 1928, and broke ground on 16 August 1928. Graham, Anderson, Probst & White (chief designer Alfred P. Shaw) put 458 concrete caissons 80–100 feet down to bedrock; John W. Griffiths & Sons employed up to 5,700 men over eighteen months. It opened on 5 May 1930, six months after the Crash: about 4,000,000 square feet, roughly 200,000 to a floor, eighteen floors plus a central tower (25 storeys, 340 feet in all), 577 feet along the river and 724 feet on Kinzie Street, corridors 650 feet long lined with showrooms, a private railway freight station with conveyors at ground level, restaurants, banks, a post office, a telegraph office and the Merchants' Club in the tower — \"the world's largest business building,\" until the Pentagon in 1943. Field's president James Simpson, who also chaired the Chicago Plan Commission, built it to fold thirteen scattered warehouses into one wholesale market; Field's took floors three to six and leased the rest to hundreds of mercantile firms. The Depression gutted the idea: Field's wholesale lost $5 million in 1931 and $8 million in 1932 and shrank from four floors to a floor and a half; before the decade ended the company had quit wholesaling and found the building \"burdensome\" to manage. The federal government filled empty floors during the war, and Joseph P. Kennedy bought the whole thing in 1945 (reports say $12.5–13 million — less than half what it cost) — note only, per the owner. Kennedy's 1953 \"Hall of Fame\" busts came later still. The odd detail: a building bigger than any in the world, opened into the worst year to open it, whose owner had abandoned its purpose within five years — floor after floor of showrooms for goods nobody was ordering.",
