      "summary": "Marshall Field & Co. broke ground 16 Aug 1928 and opened the world's largest building on 5 May 1930 — 4 million square feet, 29 million bricks, 40 miles of plumbing, 380 miles of wiring, 4,000 windows, about $26 million, built by 2,500 men at a time (5,700 in all) a year and a half into the Depression; Graham, Anderson, Probst & White designed it. NBC's Chicago studios opened on the 19th floor on 20 Oct 1930 (65,000 square feet at first) — home to Amos 'n' Andy until 1935, Fibber McGee until 1939, and the WENR/NBC origin of Lights Out (Medium — inferred from WENR being NBC's Chicago outlet). The archive's W05 covers the building and its emergency battery room; the tenants above are the addition here. It later had its own ZIP code (60654). The odd detail: the largest building on earth opened six months after the crash, and the country's most-listened-to fifteen minutes came from its 19th floor.",
