      "summary": "Sears executive Max Adler's planetarium opened May 12, 1930 (his birthday), the first in the United States and the Western Hemisphere. The sky came from a Zeiss projector designed by Walther Bauersfeld at Carl Zeiss, Jena, Germany (concept 1923); the original instrument ran until 1967. In 1930 Adler bought the A. W. Mensing collection of antique astronomical instruments at an auction in Amsterdam — the seed of what is now some 8,000 objects, the largest historic scientific-instrument collection in the Western Hemisphere. Ernest Grunsfeld Jr.'s Art Deco building won the Chicago AIA gold medal; the fair grounds of 1933 opened at its feet. The odd detail: In 1930 the only way to see the heavens indoors in America was through a German machine; the antique astrolabes and armillaries around it were bought from a Dutch dealer in a single lot.",
