      "summary": "Max Adler, a retired Sears, Roebuck executive and brother-in-law of Julius Rosenwald, paid for the first planetarium in the Western Hemisphere, opened on his birthday, 12 May 1930. The star projector was a Zeiss (the mechanism invented by Walther Bauersfeld at Carl Zeiss, Jena, first shown in 1923) — a German instrument installed on the Chicago lakefront within a decade of the war. In 1930 Adler bought the A. W. Mensing collection of antique astronomical instruments at auction in Amsterdam, giving the building its \"Astronomical Museum\" half. Ernest Grunsfeld Jr. designed the twelve-sided rainbow-granite building and won the Chicago AIA gold medal for it in 1931. Philip Fox, professor of astronomy at Northwestern, was the first director (to 1937). Beyond the 09-22 archive entry: the original Zeiss projector survives — its essential parts were preserved and it can be reassembled for display. The odd detail: the sky over the 1933 fair was a German machine; the museum's astrolabes and armillaries came from a Dutch dealer's shelves in one lot.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
