      "places": [
        {
          "name": "An apartment over a grocery at 5200 S. Wentworth Ave",
          "address": "5200 S. Wentworth Ave",
          "geography": "South Side, Chicago",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=An%20apartment%20over%20a%20grocery%20at%205200%20S.%20Wentworth%20Ave%2C%20South%20Side%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "A well-spoken woman calling herself \"Julia Otis\" began visiting the poor Moroney family with food and gifts, spoke of taking two-year-old Mary Agnes (b. 10 May 1928) to California, then on 15 May 1930 asked to take the child shopping for clothes. Katherine Moroney agreed; neither came back. Letters followed — one from \"Otis\" saying the child was in California and would be returned in a month, another from a \"cousin,\" \"Alice Henderson,\" saying Otis had suffered losses and a nervous breakdown but would care for the girl. A warrant was sworn for Otis; she was never found. In 1952 the Chicago Daily News announced it had found Mary Agnes as 24-year-old Mary McClelland, adopted in California; Katherine rejected it — the woman lacked the strawberry birthmark on the cheek and the navel scar. DNA testing in 2023 showed Mary Agnes had lived as Jeanette Burchard, a nurse with three children, and died in Florida around 2003. The odd detail: The letters — the kidnapper wrote to the mother, twice, in two voices — and the fact that the girl lived a whole life under another name; her stepfather once told her that the woman who raised her \"was not her natural birth mother,\" and never explained it.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A visiting benefactress who takes a child and writes in two hands is, in this setting, a changeling story told by the period's own social workers. The 1936 Section 13 file is still open; the mother still lives at Wentworth.",
