      "summary": "On 31 May 1930 Gaspar Milazzo, 43, a Castellammarese boss sent by Angelo Meli to mediate with the West Side's Chester La Mare, sat down to eat at the fish market with his driver Sam Parrino, and \"two gunmen leaped out and unleashed a barrage of shotgun blasts\"; Milazzo died at once, Parrino soon after. At least 14 men died between 31 May and 23 July 1930 in the Eastside–Westside war — the \"Bloody July\" of ten gangland murders in two weeks that Jerry Buckley denounced on air. La Mare was killed on 7 February 1931 by his own men (Joe Amico and Elmer Macklin). The survivors — Joseph Zerilli, William \"Black Bill\" Tocco, Angelo Meli, John Priziola and Peter Licavoli — formed the Detroit Partnership, which later \"filled the void\" the Purples left. Some historians see Milazzo's killing as the opening shot of New York's Castellammarese War. The odd detail: A peace meeting over a plate of fish — the same \"peace conference\" template the Purples used at Collingwood fifteen months later.",
