      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Manning's Hotel",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "on Pistakee Lake near W. Grand Avenue, west of Route 12, Fox Lake, Lake County, Illinois. The Lake County history blog says the building still stands as a priva",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Manning%27s%20Hotel%2C%20on%20Pistakee%20Lake%20near%20W.%20Grand%20Avenue%2C%20west%20of%20Route%2012%2C%20Fox%20Lake%2C%20Lake%20County%2C%20Illinois.%20The%20Lake%20County%20history%20blog%20says%20the%20building%20still%20"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "At 1:40 a.m. on 1 June 1930 gunmen machine-gunned \"without warning\" the enclosed porch at Manning's. Three men died: Michael Quirk (Klondike O'Donnell gang), Sam Pellar (Capone gang) and Joseph Bertsche (Druggan-Lake gang). Two survived: George Druggan, brother of Terry \"Machine Gun\" Druggan, severely wounded, and Vivian Ponic McGinnis (1903–1993), his girlfriend and the wife of a Chicago attorney, Arthur J. McGinnis. The Tribune's theory, as quoted by the blog, was beer: \"Alderman Manning had changed beer dealers, putting in Druggan beer in preference to 'local beer' in which the Bugs Moran gang had taken a partnerly interest.\" State's Attorney A. V. Smith said within days that the killers were professionals from New York and long gone. Nobody was convicted on the page I read. Bugs Moran was arrested in October 1930 on an unrelated matter and later left Chicago. Correction to R29: R29 called this \"revenge for the Lingle killing.\" That cannot be right: Jake Lingle was shot on 9 June 1930 (R01 in this folder), eight days after Fox Lake. The odd detail: The three dead men belonged to three different rival outfits, yet they were together on one porch at an alderman's resort, with a Chicago lawyer's wife (Druggan's girlfriend) among them. Somebody arranged that table.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium. One local-history page for names, time and place; the motive is contested (beer versus Miller's revenge) and only the beer theory is on a page I read. • UNVERIFIED.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A resort porch where men from three mobs sit down at 1:40 a.m. and someone with a machine gun knew the seating plan. A \"who called the meeting\" investigation for the Secret Service (which \"knew what Capone was\"), with the occult layer being a host who needed that particular blood spilled under his roof.",
