      "summary": "The white marble sarcophagus that had covered Lincoln's coffin from 1874 to 1876 (when the attempt to steal the body was foiled) lay on the lawn outside the tomb while the monument was taken apart; a newspaper photographer photographed it intact on 5 July 1930. On 15 September an Illinois State Journal reporter found it wrecked; the paper reported it \"wrecked\" (16 Sept) and Hammond blamed \"vandals or souvenir hunters\". State supervising architect C. Herrick Hammond said (19 September) it must have been \"accomplished at night\" (page read) [the rest of the quotation, \"or those who did it would have been observed,\" is UNVERIFIED — V_R38]. The paper's editorial of 20 September blamed the state — \"It looks like a case of clear negligence\". A minister from Virginia later returned a fragment; one carved piece was displayed in the rotunda in 2013. The tomb itself was rededicated by Hoover on 17 June 1931. The odd detail: Between 5 July and 15 September nobody knows on what night the marble that had covered Lincoln's coffin was smashed, and the fragments went home in souvenir hunters' pockets.",
