      "id": "R05-019",
      "title": "W. D. Fard and the Nation of Islam",
      "date_start": "1930-07-04",
      "date_end": "1934",
      "date_label": "Detroit, 4 July 1930 → vanished 1934",
      "category": "Religion, churchmen, relics, revivals, new religions, cults, spiritualists and occultists (Chicago & the Great Lakes, 1925–1939)",
      "tags": [
        "religion",
        "occult",
        "W. D. Fard",
        "Nation of Islam"
      ],
      "people": [
        "W. D. Fard",
        "Robert Harris",
        "James J. Smith",
        "Ugan Ali",
        "Elijah Muhammad"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Detroit",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(Paradise Valley / Black Bottom)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Detroit"
        },
        {
          "name": "Chicago",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(Temple No. 2)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "A man calling himself W. D. Fard appeared in Detroit on 4 July 1930 peddling silks door to door, saying they matched those of the ancestors in Mecca. Within three years he had some 25,000 followers across Detroit, Chicago and other cities in the Allah Temple of Islam (later the Nation of Islam), with the Fruit of Islam for men and Muslim Girls' Training for women. On 20 Nov 1932 follower Robert Harris (\"Robert Karriem\") killed James J. Smith on a home altar in what Smith thought was an initiation — the press called it a human sacrifice. Fard and Ugan Ali were arrested, put in straitjackets and padded cells; Harris was found insane. Police put Fard on a Chicago-bound train on 7 Dec 1932; he returned, was re-arrested 25 May 1933 and released on condition he leave for good. His last known letter came from Mexico in March 1934. His fate is unknown. Elijah Poole (Elijah Muhammad) led the movement from Chicago. The odd detail: The founder's last known trace is a letter from Mexico — in the setting, American-occupied Mexico.",
