      "summary": "Buckley's nightly 6 p.m. commentary came from WMBC's studios at the top of the LaSalle, where he also kept an emergency room. After \"Bloody July\" he attacked the police and Mayor Bowles night after night (sources disagree on whether he championed or opposed the recall; one account says he reversed himself in his last broadcast). Told to take a bodyguard, he said: \"I get twenty threats a day. There is always someone who says I'll be killed.\" Reporter Riley Murray urged him to write down a list of the men who might kill him; he refused. On 23 July, after the recall had passed, three men shot him eleven times in the lobby. About 150,000 people came to the funeral after the Mass at St. Gregory's. A 23-member grand jury heard taxi-stand owner Gus Reno, porter Robert Jackson and police informant Fred Tara; witnesses contradicted each other; no one was convicted. Police Commissioner Thomas Wilcox produced an affidavit that Buckley had taken $4,000 in protection money from a bootlegger — dismissed as coerced. W. S. Martin, a WMBC engineer, disappeared after the murder and resurfaced a week before the trial to say he would know the killers — and that they were not the men in the dock. The odd detail: The station engineer who was the likeliest eyewitness vanished, came back just before trial, and cleared the defendants.",
