      "summary": "Gerald E. \"Jerry\" Buckley, WMBC's nightly commentator, had spent months attacking city hall over open gambling and crime, and had campaigned for the recall of Mayor Charles Bowles. The recall passed, 120,863 to 89,907 — the first mayor of a major American city removed by recall (unverified — V2_R06) (the vote came before 23 July; Wikipedia dates it 21 July 1930). At about 1:50 a.m. on 23 July, just after Buckley signed off and came down to the lobby, three gunmen shot him eleven times and walked out. Ted Pizzino, Angelo Livecchi and Joe Bommarito of the Licavoli mob were tried; witnesses contradicted each other (the porter and an informant identified them, an engineer said \"they were not Pizzino, Bommarito, and Livecchi\"); all were acquitted. Police Commissioner Wilcox produced an affidavit calling Buckley \"a known extortionist and racketeer,\" later deemed coerced. 150,000 people attended the funeral. Unsolved. The odd detail: Eleven bullets in a hotel lobby two days after the city voted the mayor out (vote 21 July; shot about 1:50 a.m., 23 July) — and the police commissioner's answer was to call the dead man a racketeer.",
