      "summary": "On 1 Aug 1930 the German daily Abendpost printed its reading of the new census, counting people \"of foreign descent regardless of birthplace.\" Its figures: German descent ~350,000 (vs 112,228 foreign-born in 1920); Poles up from 137,611 (1920) to ~400,000; the \"Negro population\" almost doubled to ~200,000; ~175,000 Irish; 75,000 Swedes; 30,000 Danes; 150,000 Italians; 150,000 Czechs; 60,000 Greeks; 2,500 Chinese; 300 Japanese. The WBEZ summary of the real census gives 1930 as the peak of immigrant Chicago: ~859,000 foreign-born plus 1.3 million children of immigrants, ~2.2 million people in a city of ~3.4 million. The odd detail: the German paper was quietly conceding that the Poles had passed the Germans. The two biggest \"tribes\" of Chicago in 1930 are the two whose old countries the 1916 break changes most.",
