      "summary": "Eleanor \"Cissy\" Medill Patterson — sister of Joseph Medill Patterson of the New York Daily News, cousin of Col. McCormick — talked William Randolph Hearst into letting her edit his fifth-place Washington Herald in 1930. She did what her brother would have done: local features, local writers instead of \"canned stuff\" from the Hearst wires, society, women's pages and fashion, and doubled the circulation where Hearst had failed. On 28 January 1939 she bought both the Herald and the Times and merged them as the Times-Herald. Her feuds were public — with her childhood friend Alice Roosevelt Longworth (two satirical novels), her daughter Felicia, and her former son-in-law Drew Pearson. An alcoholic all her adult life, she died of a heart attack at Mount Airy, Maryland, on 24 July 1948. The odd detail: A Chicago woman ran the capital's morning paper through the whole Roosevelt decade and used it to settle scores.",
