      "summary": "Thomas Shipp and Abram Smith, young Black men accused of robbing and killing 24-year-old Claude Deeter and assaulting his fiancée Mary Ball, were dragged from the county jail by a crowd of about 5,000 — women and children among them — who broke in with sledgehammers, and hanged from a tree on the square; when Smith fought the noose they broke his arms. The third boy, 16-year-old James Cameron, was already at the rope when an unknown woman's voice in the crowd said he was innocent, and the mob put him back in the jail. Lawrence Beitler's photograph sold thousands of postcard copies within ten days; Flossie Bailey of the NAACP and Attorney General James Ogden got eight indictments, two acquittals, and the rest dropped. Abel Meeropol wrote \"Strange Fruit\" from the photograph in 1937. The odd detail: The voice in the crowd that nobody could identify, and the postcard trade.",
