      "summary": "A small, violent Category 4 hurricane (peak winds about 155 mph, 933 mbar, a radius of maximum winds of only 8 miles, a destruction path about 2 miles wide at landfall) struck the capital on 3 September 1930 — San Zenón's day — about two weeks after Trujillo's inauguration. Deaths are estimated at 2,000 to 8,000; the Red Cross counted 2,000 dead and 8,000 injured in the city. About half the city was levelled, three districts almost entirely. Trujillo put the whole army on the streets within 24 hours, took emergency powers, and built his reputation on the rebuilding. The Red Cross sent $15,000 the next day; the British HMS Danae and RFA Serbol brought help. The ship Coamo spent seven hours in the storm, passing through the eye. The odd detail: a hurricane named for the saint of its day, arriving in a new dictator's third week, gave him the ruins to build a personality cult on. (Mass burning of bodies is widely reported but not confirmed this session.)",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A city half-flattened in an afternoon: which old walls, crypts and colonial foundations the wind opened in the first city of the Americas, and what walked out before the Marines arrived.",
