      "summary": "Irna Phillips created, wrote and sometimes played Mother Moynihan in Painted Dreams, a fifteen-minute daily about an Irish-American widow and her unmarried daughter, from 20 Oct 1930 — widely regarded as the first daytime serial; the orphan Joe Giolotti joined the household in autumn 1933. When WGN refused in 1932 to sell the show to a network, Phillips sued for ownership (settled 1938 when CBS bought it) and simply re-made it for WMAQ as Today's Children. Chicago's soap factory followed (The Guiding Light, 1937 — from memory, UNVERIFIED this session). It ran to July 1943. The odd detail: the daytime serial was invented as an Irish widow's kitchen on the Tribune's station, and its author sued the Tribune for the widow.",
