      "id": "R38-011",
      "title": "\"Peoria clamps the lid on\": 20 October 1930 and the ambush of Clyde and Cora Garrison",
      "date_start": "1930-10-20",
      "date_end": "1930-10-20",
      "date_label": "20 Oct 1930",
      "category": "The Illinois ring cities, 1920–1940: Rockford, Joliet, Aurora, Elgin, Waukegan, Kenosha/Racine, Peoria, Springfield, Rock Island–Moline, Danville",
      "tags": [
        "Illinois",
        "cities",
        "Peoria",
        "October",
        "Clyde"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Clyde Garrison",
        "Cora Garrison",
        "Harry Pratt",
        "Mayor Ed",
        "the Shelton brothers"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Peoria — the Garrison home at 201 W. McClure Avenue",
          "address": "201 W. McClure Avenue",
          "geography": "(a rear-garage ambush)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Peoria%20%E2%80%94%20the%20Garrison%20home%20at%20201%20W.%20McClure%20Avenue%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "the whole gambling-house district",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20whole%20gambling-house%20district%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "the office of Illinois State's Attorney Harry Pratt",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(as the Tribune words it)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20office%20of%20Illinois%20State%27s%20Attorney%20Harry%20Pratt"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The Chicago Daily Tribune of 21 October 1930 (p. 2) reported that Illinois State's Attorney Harry Pratt had ordered Peoria's gambling houses closed on 20 October 1930, for the first time in the city's history, following a murder case involving a gambling-house proprietor's wife. per the blog's summary of the Tribune, Pratt \"was less concerned with solving the violent murder ... than with quelling rumors of corrupt financial interests in the gambling houses\" (the blog's characterisation, not a Pratt statement); Mayor Ed Woodruff and the city's leaders understood that Peoria's economy depended on organised vice, and both sides knew reform had always been unpopular and short-lived. The murder case is that of Cora Garrison: a local history blog says gunmen (two or three) hid in the bushes behind the couple's house when Clyde Garrison, a well-known casino owner, drove in; Clyde pushed his wife aside and fired at \"the talking bushes,\" and Cora died of a bullet to the head while Clyde took a serious wound to the right leg — an attempted kidnapping of a gambler that was never solved. (The blog contradicts itself — \"A Map to Mayhem\" says October 1930, \"Peoria's Unsolved Murders\" says November 1930 — but the Tribune of 21 Oct 1930 says the 20 October closure order followed the murder, so it happened in October 1930, before the 20th; the exact day was not found.) After the ambush Garrison hired the Shelton brothers for protection. The odd detail: The Tribune's own headline calls Peoria \"wide open\" and says the lid went on \"for the first time in its history\" — and the same story says the mayor and the city's leaders knew the town's economy leaned on the rackets.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the Tribune headline facts and the ambush; murder date: October 1930, before the 20th; exact day not found.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A city whose economy is the vice machine is the perfect parish for a cult that pays the police in the same coin. The Garrison ambush is a kidnap attempt — what did the kidnappers want Clyde Garrison to sign?",
