      "summary": "Joe Aiello had fought Capone since November 1925, when Antonio Lombardo was made head of the Unione Siciliana over him; he once offered the chef of \"Diamond Joe\" Esposito's Bella Napoli Café — Capone's favourite restaurant — between $10,000 and $35,000 to put prussic acid in Capone's and Lombardo's soup. By 1930 he had risen to lead the Unione, backed at a conference in Atlantic City. On October 23, 1930, as he left Prestigiacomo's apartment, gunmen in positions rented across the street opened fire. The coroner removed 59 bullets, weighing over a pound, from his body — more than any single victim of the St. Valentine's Day Massacre. Police found a third machine-gun position, never used, rented a week earlier by a man who called himself \"Lon Celespe\". The odd detail: A second, unused machine-gun nest — the killers had rented enough windows to cover every direction he might run.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"Lon Celespe\" rented a room a week early and never came back for his deposit. The landlady remembers he never ate, never lit the lamp, and paid in coins older than the city.",
