      "id": "R42-007",
      "title": "The Gorman–Horrigan farm killings",
      "date_start": "1930-11-11",
      "date_end": "1930-11-11",
      "date_label": "Crawford County, Tuesday 11 November 1930",
      "category": "Wisconsin beyond Milwaukee, 1920–1940: the cities and their communities",
      "tags": [
        "Wisconsin",
        "beyond",
        "Gorman",
        "Horrigan",
        "BENDS"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Patrick Gorman",
        "Paul Gorman",
        "Cornelius",
        "Stephen Horrigan"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "The Horrigan farm about 15–17 miles northeast of Boscobel",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Crawford County (Kickapoo hills, western Wisconsin), a hollow locals called \"Treasure Hollow.\"",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=The%20Horrigan%20farm%20about%2015%E2%80%9317%20miles%20northeast%20of%20Boscobel%2C%20Crawford%20County%20%2C%20a%20hollow%20locals%20called%20%22Treasure%20Hollow.%22%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "At dinner time three men and a woman posing as hunters asked leave to hunt. One asked for water; when the woman of the house brought it, \"the big man\" grabbed her and drew a gun and ordered everyone to face the wall. A shoot-out followed: Patrick Gorman and his son Paul, 22 (visiting from Canton, South Dakota) were killed at the table; Stephen Horrigan (died later, per one summary) and Cornelius Horrigan, about 60, were wounded; the household's return fire wounded at least one bandit. The bandits' 1928 Chevrolet sedan turned up abandoned at Mount Sterling; posses formed at Soldiers Grove and sheriffs of four counties (Crawford, Grant, Richland and Vernon) worked the case, with vigilantes guarding roads. Nobody was ever arrested. Locals had long said an Irish immigrant, John Horrigan, had buried gold on the place; people were seen digging afterward and nothing was found. The odd detail: The robbery may have been aimed at buried money that a family legend put under the very farm the family was born on.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium on the event; Low on the ages and on exactly who died beyond the two Gormans. • read twice; the summaries disagree on ages — Patrick Gorman is given as 36, 56 and 79 across readings, and find-a-grave-type pages give 1865–1930 — treat as UNVERIFIED",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A four-person crew with a woman as the door-opener, a hollow with a treasure name, a two-year silence. What was actually buried on the Horrigan farm — and did the family leave because they found it?",
