      "summary": "Thirteen months after the Milwaukee went down with all 52 hands (R07/R13), the Grand Trunk Milwaukee Car Ferry Company launched her replacement at Manitowoc on 25 November 1930, completed 15 January 1931: 354 ft, 56 ft beam, 28–30 railcars, twin triple-expansion engines of 2,700 hp, 14.5 knots, a crew of 52 — the same number of men the lake had taken. She sailed for Grand Trunk until 1978 and for the Ann Arbor road until 1982, and is today a National Historic Landmark and museum at Manistee, \"the last unmodified traditional railroad car ferry afloat.\" The odd detail: The replacement carried exactly the crew the lost ship drowned; her sailors of the 1930s were, in many cases, the friends and relatives of the men in the lake seven miles off Milwaukee.",
