      "summary": "Andrew \"Rube\" Foster built the Chicago American Giants in 1911 with white partner John M. Schorling (Schorling's Park, 39th and Wentworth) and in spring 1920 organised the Negro National League with seven other Midwestern owners. On 26 May 1925 a gas leak in an Indianapolis rooming house nearly asphyxiated him; his behaviour grew erratic, and midway through the 1926 season he was committed to the Kankakee asylum, suffering delusions — among them that he was about to be called to pitch in the World Series. He died there on 9 December 1930. Thousands came to the Bronzeville funeral, with an overflow of 3,000 standing in snow and rain; his coffin was closed \"at the usual hour a ballgame ends.\" The odd detail: The coffin closed at the hour the game ends; the founder of Black baseball waiting four years in an asylum for a call to the World Series.",
