      "summary": "Herman \"Baron\" Lamm, a former Prussian army soldier, invented the modern bank robbery: hours of casing, a floor plan, timed roles, and a getaway chart (\"git\") of back roads (\"cat roads\") pasted on the dashboard, measured block by block. On 16 December 1930 his gang took $15,567 from the Citizens State Bank at Clinton, Indiana — and the getaway fell apart in stages: the driver's Buick blew a tyre jumping a kerb; the car they took from an old man had a governor his family had fitted, holding it to 35 mph; the truck after that had no water in its radiator. Near Sidell, Illinois, about 200 officers closed in; Lamm and G. W. \"Dad\" Landy, 71, shot themselves rather than surrender. Walter Dietrich survived, went to Michigan City, and taught the Lamm technique to Harry Pierpont's circle — which is how it reached Dillinger and the 1933 raids. The odd detail: The father of the timed bank job was beaten by a governor on an old man's car.",
