      "summary": "Chicago's Orthodox rabbinate went to war with many of the city's shochtim (ritual slaughterers) over \"terrorism in the kosher butcher business\" and breaches of kashrut. The charges: shochtim skipping the required monthly examination on their knowledge of the law and the keenness of their blades; and terrorism, slugging and racketeering. A shochet named Noah Greenberg had been slugged; officials of the shochtim union, including business manager Joe Etkins, \"have been tried for murder and other crimes in the Criminal Court and have been acquitted.\" The rabbis announced from every Orthodox pulpit at Sabbath services that from Monday 29 December \"no fowl found in Orthodox markets shall be kosher after that date\"; any shochet who kept killing would be \"barred forever from the profession and branded as unfit to kill,\" because \"the Talmud demands clean, pious men for ritual killings.\" Synagogue presidents were told to stop unauthorised slaughter. The odd detail: A union business manager acquitted of murder, and the rabbis' answer was religious law: a racketeer's knife cannot make meat kosher.",
