      "summary": "A National Archives photograph dated February 1931 records unemployed men queuing at a Capone-backed soup kitchen beneath a sign offering food and coffee. The image makes a useful physical reference for clothing, queue spacing, storefront lettering and a relief encounter on an ordinary block. A documented 2011 photograph returns to the same South State Street site and shows its later transformation. The two images anchor a recognizable place while avoiding the assumption that every Depression queue was a job line.",
