      "summary": "Eisenstein's ¡Que viva México! (begun 1930 under a contract with the American socialist author Upton Sinclair and his backers) was to have four novellas; the Sandunga section, shot in the Isthmus, opens on swaying coconut palms and a wedding, with women in the markets, fields and at the comals — the men mostly absent from the work, though the wedding musicians are men. Eisenstein wrote of the region as an Eden and of a \"queen-bee\" mother who reigns in Tehuantepec. The Tehuana is described in the standard modern account as a trader of \"assertive personality\" with \"relatively equal relations with men,\" which some call \"matriarchal\"; the traje de tehuana became a national icon (the title of a The Americas article). La Sandunga, the Isthmus's waltz, is its unofficial anthem. The odd detail: the \"matriarchy\" is an outsider's category; the Zapotec women were markets-women and traders — the economy simply ran through them.",
