      "summary": "Born in Chicago March 25, 1878, daughter of International Harvester's John J. Glessner, she inherited a fortune and in 1931 endowed Harvard's Department of Legal Medicine — the first in the country — and its George Burgess Magrath Library. In the 1940s she built the \"Nutshell Studies of Unexplained Death\", twenty dollhouse crime scenes at $3,000–4,500 each, used to train detectives in 90-minute exercises (18 still in use in Maryland), became the first woman police captain in the U.S. (New Hampshire, 1943), and earned the title \"Mother of Forensic Science\". As a girl she had built a miniature Chicago Symphony for her mother's birthday. The odd detail: A Chicago heiress whose money founded American death-investigation, and whose hobby was building perfect tiny murders.",
