      "summary": "Lester Armour (b. 1895), grandson of Philip D. Armour, Yale, left the meat business for banking in the early 1920s and became president of the Chicago National Bank and chairman of Harris Bank. In 1931 — the year the Chicago banks were failing by the dozen — David Adler built him a Colonial-and-Federal brick house modelled on the James and Potomac River plantations of Virginia, \"one of his most pure works as well as one of his largest,\" with interiors by Adler's sister Frances Elkins (one of the few times they worked together); the Armours approved the drawings without a change. (In 1997 the singer Richard Marx paid $4.7 million for it.) The odd detail: A Chicago bank president building a Virginia plantation house on Lake Michigan in the year of the bank runs — and not changing a line of the plans.",
