      "summary": "By the county's own 1932 report, The Story of Unemployment Relief Work in Lake County, Indiana, repatriation removed 1,800 people from Indiana Harbor, 1,500 from Gary — 3,330 in all, including 250 children. The one-way rail fare to Mexico was $49; Judge Maurice E. Crites of the Lake Superior Court, a former East Chicago city attorney and Community Chest leader, negotiated a \"charity rate\" of $15 for adults and $7.50 for children, and the report claimed $21,000 a year in savings from repatriating the children. Walter J. Riley chaired the county relief committee. Newspapers presented it as a \"relief program\"; historians describe it as \"subtly coercive,\" since being ineligible for relief rolls and withholding help from the colony pushed families to go. Over 34 percent of the Mexican population of northwest Indiana had left by 1932. The odd detail: The county's accounting shows a price list: $49, $15, $7.50 — and the annual saving from sending children away.",
