      "summary": "The Svenska Tribunen-Nyheter editorial for Swedish Day at the fair (14 June 1933) claimed \"more than one third of all the buildings here were built by Swedes,\" and reflected that an older person \"who saw the Columbian Exposition … forty years ago\" had lived through airplanes, radio and automobiles. In Nov 1931 the paper celebrated the dedication of Swedish sculptor Carl Milles's Triton Fountain at the Art Institute. The Abendpost's 1930 estimate put Chicago's Swedes at ~75,000. The odd detail: a community claiming it physically built a third of Chicago — the carpenters and contractors of the post-fire city.",
