      "id": "R20-004",
      "title": "The first skyscraper is torn down for the last one — Home Insurance Building 1931, Field Building 1931–34",
      "date_start": "1931",
      "date_end": "1934",
      "date_label": "1931 – 1934",
      "category": "The city's guts: power, water, gas, sewers, tunnels, bridges, digging, building and demolition (Chicago and the Great Lakes, 1925–1940)",
      "tags": [
        "utilities",
        "building",
        "Home Insurance Building",
        "Field Building"
      ],
      "people": [
        "William Le Baron Jenney",
        "the Marshall Field estate"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "LaSalle and Adams streets",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(the Loop financial district)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=LaSalle%20and%20Adams%20streets%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "the Field Building filled the block to Clark St",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20Field%20Building%20filled%20the%20block%20to%20Clark%20St%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "William Le Baron Jenney's Home Insurance Building (1884–85; 138 ft, raised to 12 floors in 1891) is the building usually called the world's first skyscraper. In September 1929 Marshall Field's estate announced a large office block spanning Adams, Clark and LaSalle; at least six buildings were cleared and the Home Insurance Building came down in 1931. The Field Building (45 storeys, 535 ft, 1931–34; Graham, Anderson, Probst & White) was the last major office building erected in Chicago until the Prudential in 1955. It had high-speed elevators, air-conditioning, a multi-level arcade from LaSalle to Clark — and no 13th floor. In 1932 a plaque in its lobby credited the demolished building as the first to use skeleton construction. The odd detail: Chicago demolished the first skyscraper to build what turned out to be its last for twenty years, then hung a plaque to the dead building in the new one's lobby.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Field Building has no 13th floor on its elevator panel — but the stairwell count does not add up. A floor built in 1934 for tenants who did not want to be listed.",
