      "summary": "Theodore \"Handsome Jack\" Klutas (born John Edward Klutas, 1900, Lyndon Township, Whiteside County, Ill., to William, German-born, and Ida E. May Klutas) led a gang the papers called the College Kidnappers. Regular members: Edward Doll alias \"Eddie LaRue\", Russell Hughes, Frank Souder, Gale Swolley (also spelt Swoley), Ernest Rossi, Eddie Wagner, Earl McMahon, Julius \"Babe\" Jones and Walter Dietrich; a chauffeur, Adolphe Anzone; and a Paul Stroud named in the press. They chose their victims from the underworld — \"gamblers, bootleggers, and quite wealthy mobsters,\" most of them of the Capone organisation — precisely because such men \"were less likely to approach the police.\" Named victims: James Hackett, a Blue Island gambler, kidnapped twice — the first ransom, $75,000, paid by his wife (May 1931); for the second the gang demanded double (Sifakis, via Wikipedia, says it bankrupted him), but the period press reports the gang \"was paid $1500\" for it; William Urban of Peoria, the first victim; a James Ward; and one manufacturer held for $50,000 and released when he proved unable to pay. Total take: \"nearly $500,000\" / \"over half a million dollars\" in roughly two years. Klutas also robbed banks and was indicted for counterfeiting $5 bills (24 Nov 1933, with Rex Howard, George Milligan and others). The odd detail: by November 1933 the Cook County assistant state's attorney (Mal Coghlan) told the press that \"stories had come to him\" that Klutas had \"had his finger prints changed, and his visage … marred to make identification difficult\" — a rumour, not a finding (Wikipedia's \"one of the first to try this tactic\" rests on it), and at Bellwood \"fingerprints established the identities\" anyway; and the Evening Star's wire story of the raid (7 Jan 1934, p. 2) says he had \"posed to the owner\" of the Bellwood bungalow \"as a Government Secret Service agent.\" A kidnapper said to have changed his face and his fingerprints and who goes about as a Secret Service man is already a doppelganger story before the occult is added.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High that this is the gang and on the frame (leader, name, years, Bellwood death); Medium on the ransom figures and the full victim list (the encyclopaedias and 1933 wire stories differ in small ways — the Evening Star credits the \"collegiate band\" with \"nearly a dozen kidnappings and extortions which netted more than a half million dollars\"; the fingerprint surgery is a single Nov 1933 wire report — a period claim; the Secret Service pose is in the Star's raid story, a primary wire account). Primary: https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83045462/1934-01-07/ed-1/?sp=2 (Evening Star, 7 Jan 1934, p. 2; OCR read via the tile.loc.gov text service)",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the owner's instinct — implanting themselves into society — fits the record uncannily: the gang's whole method was to take people the law would never look for, and its leader was said to have remade his face and prints and wore the Secret Service as a mask. Play it as the owner wrote it: something that wears men. The two-year run ends the day the \"Klutas\" the police shoot is not the thing they were hunting — and Section 13 has a man on its rolls with that face.",
