      "summary": "The Detroit Historical Society names the four brothers Burnstein (the family's own spelling; most sources write Bernstein) — Raymond, Joseph, Isadore and Abraham — and traces them to Bishop School and to the older mobsters Charles Leiter and Henry Shorr at the Oakland Sugar House (\"the Sugar House Boys\"). Capone chose to buy Old Log Cabin whisky from them rather than fight them for Detroit. After Collingwood (16 Sept 1931; R06/R07 cover it), Raymond Bernstein, Harry Keywell and Irving Milberg were taken to Marquette by \"special Pullman train\". Harry Fleisher — \"sought throughout the United States since the beginning of the Lindbergh kidnapping affair\" (Reuters) — surrendered to Detroit police on 9 June 1932; the witness Sol Levine was gone by late July and Fleisher went free. Abe Axler (1901–1933), the Milaflores gunman, was dead by 1933 — killed with Eddie Fletcher in the gang's infighting — and lies in Beth David Cemetery on Long Island. By 1935 the gang \"had fractured entirely, with at least 18 members dead and others imprisoned\". Abe Bernstein had opened the Club Manitou at Harbor Springs in 1929, later took shares in syndicate casinos in Miami, and spent years trying to get Raymond out. The odd detail: The Purples were hunted in the Lindbergh kidnapping — the most famous crime of the decade touched Detroit through a man who then walked free because the witness vanished.",
