      "summary": "Compton (Nobel 1927 for the Compton effect, at Chicago from 1923) organised a world survey of cosmic-ray intensity: eight cooperating teams with identical ionisation chambers measured in Greenland, Denmark, India, Ceylon, Java, Tibet, South Africa, Eritrea, Spitsbergen and Switzerland; Compton himself covered 50,000 miles — a Hawaiian volcano, Mt Cook in New Zealand, Panama, Peru — and flew within 350 miles of the north magnetic pole. Two members of the 1932 Mt McKinley cosmic-ray party died on the mountain. The result (about 15–20% more intensity at the poles than the equator at sea level) showed cosmic rays were charged particles, contradicting Robert Millikan's \"birth cries of atoms\" photon theory; the two men had a public altercation at the December 1932 Atlantic City meeting, and by 1936 Millikan had \"steadily, almost imperceptibly, retreated.\" Compton was a Presbyterian minister's son who served as a Baptist deacon and lectured on science and God. The odd detail: the men who died on McKinley were carrying an ionisation chamber up a mountain to measure rays from beyond the solar system, in 1932, for a Chicago professor.",
