      "summary": "Colt introduced the Monitor (R80) in 1931: a Browning Automatic Rifle with an 18-inch barrel and Cutts compensator, 13.2 lb, for law enforcement and prisons. About 125 were made — 90 to the Bureau of Investigation, 11 to the Treasury (1934), 24 to \"state prisons, banks, security companies and accredited city, county and state police departments\". The list price was $300; Colt recorded no civilian sales. Outlaws got military BARs instead by robbing National Guard armories in the Midwest (Clyde Barrow most famously); by 1936 a black-market Monitor was said to fetch $5,000. The odd detail: only 125 Monitors existed; the Treasury had eleven of them — the Secret Service's department.",
