      "summary": "Opened August 15, 1904, as an amusement park built by the A. C. Frost Company to sell rides on the electric interurban (a baseball diamond, an electric fountain, a refectory). In the 1920s the Chicago businessman Louis Eckstein turned it into \"the summer opera capital of the world,\" staging full seasons with Chicago Opera and Metropolitan stars — Yvonne Gall, Edward Johnson, Giovanni Martinelli — as the North Shore estates' own summer season. The cost of the productions broke it: Ravinia closed in 1931 and stayed dark four summers, until North Shore residents raised the money and the Chicago Symphony made it its summer home in 1936. The odd detail: For four summers of the decade the rich had no summer opera — the one fixed evening of the Lake Forest calendar simply stopped, and the park sat empty on the interurban line.",
