      "summary": "Rodolfo Elías Calles (son of ex-president Plutarco; governor 1931–35) ran a \"de-fanaticising\" campaign whose stated targets, in the historian Ignacio Almada's summary, included \"closing the temples, expelling priests, burning saints, persecuting the bishop.\" He asked President Abelardo L. Rodríguez to apply the federal decree so as to close about thirty temples; the state budget gave the campaign 37,042 pesos of 1,420,000; a state law split Sonora into 16 districts with one priest each (each needing the governor's permission; fines of 50–100 pesos or up to eight days in jail); arrest warrants were issued for the priests and for Bishop Juan Navarrete y Guerrero, who stayed hidden in the Sonoran mountains until 1937. Catholic women ran the resistance: at Hermosillo a crowd of women gathered in the Plaza Zaragoza, a teacher named Antonia Figueroa told them \"the government could not do more than God,\" and speakers at the kiosk said they must \"recover the churches at all costs\" (city report, quoted by Almada). At Úres women led by ex-teachers broke up a party meeting. Prayer went on in private homes. Román Yocupicio took the governorship on 4 January 1937; that year the churches were reopened by women crying \"Viva la Virgen de Guadalupe, Viva Yocupicio, Viva Cristo Rey,\" and the bishop and all the priests returned to Hermosillo; in 1939 Yocupicio invited the bishop to lay the first stone of the University of Sonora. The odd detail: the campaign's budget line — 37,042 pesos of a 1,420,000-peso state budget, about 2.6 percent — closed thirty churches cheaply.",
