      "summary": "The Society of the Helpers of the Holy Souls (Auxiliatrices des âmes du purgatoire) was founded in Paris in 1856 by Eugénie Smet (Mary of Providence); seven Helpers sailed to New York in May 1892. The 1931 Directory lists the Chicago convent with its superior, Mother Mary of St. Catherine, \"Supr. and Provincial\" — the house was the seat of a province — and says the Helpers \"take care of the sick poor in their own homes.\" In 1938 there were 17 professed sisters, and the register adds that the work was \"gratuitous\" and included religious instruction for adults and public-school children, children's clubs and sewing guilds. Their chaplain in both years was Rev. C. A. Poissant — the priest who had brought the first relic of St. Anne to Chicago in 1900 (next entry). The odd detail: An order that offered all its work — nursing the poor, for nothing — on behalf of the souls in purgatory, and whose chaplain was the city's relic-bringer.",
