      "summary": "Beside the \"Retired\" list (priests living in Hollywood, Oceanside and Villa Park), the 1931 register prints a list of Chicago priests who were simply \"Absent\" — about twenty names, most with no address. The addresses that are given: Batavia, Ill.; Milwaukee; \"A. A. Riss and J. J. Farrell, Alexian Brothers Hospital, St. Louis, Mo.; Joseph B. Crowe, Alexian Brothers Hospital, Chicago\"; and \"G. J. Blatter, care of French Legation, Kabul, Afghanistan.\" By 1938 the heading was \"Absent with Leave\" — thirty-odd names, again including Crowe and a Rev. James C. Barry \"Alexian Brothers Hospital,\" one monsignor lent to the Diocese of Santa Fe, and one priest at an address in Los Angeles. The 1938 register's necrology lists \"Blatter, George J. (Chic), absent with leave … Died Dec. 19\" (the year is cut off in the OCR; the necrology covers roughly the preceding year). The odd detail: In 1931 one priest of the Archdiocese of Chicago could be reached only through the French Legation in Kabul.",
