      "summary": "Congress authorised the national park on 3 March 1931; Roosevelt established it on 3 April 1940. Between them the island was a working place — Scandinavian fishing families (Johnson, Edisen, Holte, Mattson, Anderson, Bangsund, Sivertson) at their fish camps, Booth Fisheries' steamer America (wrecked 1928, above) their link, and a few resorts — and from 1935 to 1941 a CCC place: Roosevelt approved camps on the island in 1935 with $600,000 (Michigan's CCC employed 102,814 young men in 42 camps, 1933–41, at $30 a month of which $25 went home). The NPS's own history says \"the CCC years happened to correspond with the massive fire of 1936\" — the fire's dates and acreage are on other pages not reached (Leads). The last caribou was seen in 1925. After 1940 the fishermen's places were bought by the government; about a dozen families kept life leases. The odd detail: An island being turned into wilderness by law while it burned, with 18-to-35-year-olds who had to have at least three teeth to be hired living in camps on it.",
