      "summary": "Jorge Ubico was president from 14 February 1931 to 1 July 1944. On 10 May 1934 Decree 1996 created the Vagrancy Law: indigenous workers who could not prove 100–150 days of estate labour were \"vagrants\" and could be put to unpaid road-building — a Road Service Law partner. On 18 September 1934 five men (Efraín Aguilar Fuentes, Juventino Sánchez, Humberto Molina Santiago, Rafael Estrada Guilles and Col. Luis Ortiz Guzmán) were tortured and executed in the National Penitentiary for allegedly plotting his overthrow. United Fruit controlled the country's only railroad, its electricity and the port of Puerto Barrios — a \"state within a state.\" In early January 1936 the German cruiser Emden put in at Puerto San José (Escuintla, on the Pacific coast); her crew went by train to Guatemala City and paraded before Ubico's army staff and the public, then were received at the German Club and taken to German-owned coffee fincas (Prensa Libre; Emden's 1935–36 cruise). He thought himself \"another Napoleon,\" earning the nickname \"the Little Napoleon of the Tropics,\" and surrounded himself with Bonaparte statues and paintings. He resigned on 1 July 1944 and died in New Orleans on 14 June 1946. The odd detail: a German cruiser's crew marching before a dictator who thought he was Napoleon — the real 1936 visit was Nazi-era Olympics promotion.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for dates; the Napoleon details are typical of biographies.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a dictator in a Napoleon fixation, a cruiser in port and an American fruit company that owns his railway: a three-way court. Germany as patron — the Golden Circle's rival is one bay away.",
