      "summary": "From 1921 to 1945 Brown issued 47 short pamphlets (444 pages in all) of Wisconsin folklore, most only four to eight pages, mostly without citing sources. Titles include Ghost Tales (1931 — \"ghosts, spooks, phantoms, spirits, skulls, skeletons, ghouls, hexes, undertakers and burying grounds; short stories for use at the fireside and camp fire\"), The Ridgeway Ghost (1943), Sea Serpents: Wisconsin Occurrences of These Weird Water Monsters (1942), Lost Treasure Tales (1945; \"Lake Mendota chests, Wisconsin River coin, jug of gold dust, Potawatomi cache, Clarno gold hoard\"), Hermits (1945). Separately, in 1936–38 the WPA Federal Writers' Project (headed in Wisconsin at first by Brown, then run by Dorothy Miller from December 1935) gathered 600+ interviews — about 1,000 stories, 3,000 songs, 9,000 sayings — including Ho-Chunk and Ojibwe accounts. Recorded lake-monster sightings: 12 June 1897 on Lake Monona (Eugene Heath, Mr. Schott and his two sons saw a creature \"roughly 20 feet long\" shaped like the bottom of a boat; Heath fired two shots and it came toward them — Wisconsin State Journal, \"Sea Serpent Appears Early This Year\"); 1917 on Lake Mendota (a fish-scale-like object at Picnic Point; a fisherman saw a snake-like head with blazing eyes; a napping girl felt something tickle her foot); and on Geneva Lake the Janesville Gazette of 29 September 1902 reported a snake-like creature 65 feet long and 8–10 inches thick. (unverified — V_R42) The odd detail: The state's own museum director spent the Depression issuing four-page booklets on fireside ghosts and lake serpents — and the Federal Writers' Project paid people to knock on doors to ask what kind.",
