      "summary": "From 12 January to 2 February 1931 the federal government tried the Rockford liquor ring for conspiracy to violate the Prohibition Act: 80 men and one woman were accused, 59 went to trial, 36 were found guilty and 28 sentenced. Sentenced names include Tony Musso, Tony and Paul Giovingo, John Alto, Joe Baraconi, Joe Bendetto, Frank Rumore, Louis Verace, Alfred Falzone, Lorenzo Buttice and others. The federal task force was led by C. Edson Smith, deputy prohibition administrator in Chicago, with Secret Service and federal agents working the case in 1930 — including a June 1930 machine-gun volley from a curtained car at agents dismantling a still on Sanford Street (nobody hit; police suspected Dominick Rossi, 710 Sanford). Jack DeMarco, tried there, got 12 months of an 18-month sentence at Leavenworth and was murdered on the day after he came home. The odd detail: Sentences went out to 28 of the men, but the ones who were shot afterwards — DeMarco and Paul Giovingo — were both returning convicts: the town killed its Leavenworth men when they came home.",
