      "summary": "A magnitude 6.1 shock struck at 10:10 am on 31 March 1931, lasting five to six seconds; an epicentre off the Chinandega coast, 15 km deep. Between 1,000 and 2,450 people died; the markets were crowded for Holy Week (65 women and 17 men were found dead at the markets), and the highest mortality occurred in the city penitentiary. A major fire burned for five days, destroying 33 blocks of the richest district; 45,000 were left homeless; losses were $15–35 million. Martial law was declared. Marines and the Guardia Nacional mobilised, the U.S. Navy set up a hospital, army personnel fought fires, and Col. Daniel I. Sultan — leading the Nicaragua Canal Survey — organised a 34-soldier relief team and chaired food relief (24,000 rations a day by 24 April). The Red Cross gave $10,000 and then $100,000. Rebuilding proceeded in Spanish Colonial style; by March 1932 rents had dropped 40 percent. The odd detail: the survey team for a second American canal happened to be standing in Managua on Holy Tuesday — and the city's prison is where most people died.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Holy Tuesday, a crowded market, a prison, and five days of fire in the rich district: an occult event hiding under a disaster; a dead man in the penitentiary who was not supposed to be there. Sultan's engineers as witnesses.",
