      "id": "R20-017",
      "title": "Poison smoke in the sanitary tunnel — eleven dead 35 feet under the Southwest Side",
      "date_start": "1931-04-13",
      "date_end": "1931-04-13",
      "date_label": "night of 13–14 Apr 1931",
      "category": "The city's guts: power, water, gas, sewers, tunnels, bridges, digging, building and demolition (Chicago and the Great Lakes, 1925–1940)",
      "tags": [
        "utilities",
        "building",
        "Poison",
        "Southwest Side"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Capt. James O'Neil",
        "the eleven dead"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "A Sanitary District of Chicago sewer tunnel under construction",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "35 ft underground, \"on the Southwest Side\" (exact street not in the snippets read)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=A%20Sanitary%20District%20of%20Chicago%20sewer%20tunnel%20under%20construction%2C%2035%20ft%20underground%2C%20%22on%20the%20Southwest%20Side%22"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "During the night of 13–14 April 1931 a fire the wire services called \"mysterious\" broke out in a Sanitary District tunnel bore and filled it with smoke and poison fumes. Eleven men died — tunnel workers and firemen who went down after them (the Indianapolis Times: eleven \"killed during the night by clouds of poisonous smoke\"). Next morning fifteen men were found alive — eight firemen and seven tunnel workers — because a bulkhead in the tunnel had held back the smoke while fire squads pumped fresh air down the shaft. Fire Captain James O'Neil stayed below after the others were brought up. The Washington Daily News counted a twelfth death connected to the rescue (a policeman struck by an ambulance, per the search summary). (unverified — V_R20) The odd detail: The survivors lived because of a wall: a bulkhead in a sewer, 35 ft down, kept the air on their side breathable through the night — and nobody could say what had burned.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium — four same-day wire reports agree on the fire, depth, bulkhead and 10–11 dead; the street, the cause and the victims' names were not readable (page OCR blocked). • The Washington Daily News counted a twelfth death connected to the rescue (a policeman struck by an ambulance, per the search summary). (unverified — V_R20)",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Owner's wish list — \"unanswered things\": the fire had no source anyone could name, the tunnel belonged to Kelly's Sanitary District, and the Nash firm built the sewers. The bulkhead that saved fifteen men was not on the contractor's drawings.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Library of Congress: chronicling america",
          "url": "https://www.loc.gov/collections/chronicling-america/?q=chicago+sewer+workers+overcome+gas&dates=1925/1940&fo=json",
          "publisher": "Library of Congress",
          "kind": "web page cited in the 2026-09-26 sweep",
          "supports": "Washington Daily News 14 Apr 1931 p. 5; Indianapolis Times 14 Apr 1931 p. 1; Milwaukee Leader 14 Apr 1931 p. 1; Washington Times 14 Apr 1931 p. 1 — \"sixteen men trapped … snatched from death\", ten dead at that edition"
        }
      ],
