      "places": [
        {
          "name": "a workshop at 1706 W. 51st Street",
          "address": "1706 W. 51st Street",
          "geography": "Chicago (reported in the Mexican press)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=a%20workshop%20at%201706%20W.%2051st%20Street%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "El Nacional (29 Apr 1931) reported that federal agents, per Captain Thomas J. Calloghan [as rendered], arrested three men — Cormine Pirofalo, Leo Cuda and Louis C. Olivo — who washed one-dollar bills with acid and reprinted them as twenties. Pirofalo did the printing, having left honest work; Cuda was his associate; Olivo owned the workshop. Bond was $10,000 each, and the notes were called among the best counterfeits ever found in the city. The odd detail: the trick started from genuine one-dollar paper, so each \"counterfeit\" was made on real government stock (inference from the report).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (the paper's report).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the Secret Service's old-guard case: a bleached dollar bill reported in a Mexican colony's newspaper, and the workshop it came from doing other printing for other customers.",
