      "summary": "Harm \"Herman\" Drenth (b. Beerta, Netherlands, 17 Nov 1892) ran a grocery with his wife Luella near Clarksburg and, as \"Cornelius O. Pierson,\" worked the lonely-hearts columns of the American Friendship Society; postal records showed 10–20 replies a day. He wrote to Asta Buick Eicher, a Park Ridge widow, called on her on 23 June 1931, took her away, then came back for her children Greta (14), Harry (12) and Annabel (9) (unverified — V2_R06). He also took Dorothy Pressler Lemke of Northborough, Massachusetts. In August 1931 Park Ridge police, tracing the love letters, reached Quiet Dell (unverified — V_R06) and found a basement garage fitted with four small rooms, and a freshly filled ditch containing all five bodies. A mob tried to storm the Clarksburg jail. The trial was moved to the Moore Opera House to hold the crowd; convicted 12 Dec 1931 for Lemke's murder; hanged at Moundsville 18 Mar 1932. Davis Grubb built The Night of the Hunter on the case. The odd detail: The \"garage\" with four cells and a drain (unverified — V2_R06) — and Powers' own account that he kept the children alive for days in it so their mother could hear them (unverified — V2_R06).",
