      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on names, dates and the garage; the children-kept-alive detail is UNVERIFIED this session (widely repeated from his confession, not read on a primary page). • He wrote to Asta Buick Eicher, a Park Ridge widow, called on her on 23 June 1931, took her away, then came back for her children Greta (14), Harry (12) and Annabel (9) (unverified — V2_R06). • In August 1931 Park Ridge police, tracing the love letters, reached Quiet Dell (unverified — V_R06) and found a basement garage fitted with four small rooms, and a freshly filled ditch containing all five bodies. • The \"garage\" with four cells and a drain (unverified — V2_R06) — and Powers' own account that he kept the children alive for days in it so their mother could hear them (unverified — V2_R06).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Park Ridge end — a Chicago-suburb widow answering a mail-order suitor — is where a Section 13 investigator would start. The four-room cellar is the physical shape of a cult's holding pen; the lonely-hearts trade is how a vampire feeds in high society without leaving a mark.",
