      "id": "R01-019",
      "title": "Capone's tax trial",
      "date_start": "1931-06-05",
      "date_end": "1931-10",
      "date_label": "indicted June 5, 1931; convicted October 17, 1931; sentenced October 1931",
      "category": "The Outfit and gangland Chicago, 1929–1939",
      "tags": [
        "gangland",
        "the Outfit",
        "Capone's"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Al Capone",
        "James H. Wilkerson",
        "George E. Q. Johnson",
        "Phil D'Andrea"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "U.S. District Court",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Chicago (Federal Building)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=U.S.%20District%20Court%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "Cook County Jail",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Cook%20County%20Jail%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Capone was indicted on June 5, 1931 on 22 counts of evading income tax for 1925–29 (a second Wilson-era source says 23 counts across March and June grand juries) and released on $50,000 bail. He pleaded guilty on June 16 expecting a light plea bargain, but Judge James Herbert Wilkerson refused to be bound by it on July 30, 1931, and Capone went to trial. Wilson's report records the jury fight: the defence bought an advance copy of the special venire of 100 prospective jurors, which was delivered to Capone at the Lexington Hotel and handed out to gang members, union agents and friends to work on; Judge Wilkerson swapped the entire panel for another on the first day of trial, then locked the jury up under deputy marshals when Capone's people began reaching jurors' families. Capone's bodyguard Phil D'Andrea sat behind him glaring at witnesses — and was caught in court with a revolver and ammunition in his pocket; Wilkerson's contempt ruling called the Lexington Hotel the \"outlaw camp\" of \"an organized body of men\" whose coercion was \"nothing less than insurrection against the laws of the United States.\" On October 17, 1931 Capone was convicted of tax evasion for 1925–27 and failure to file for 1928–29. On October 24 Wilkerson gave him five years on each of the three felony counts (two concurrent) and a year in Cook County Jail on each misdemeanour — 11 years in all — a $50,000 fine plus $7,692 costs, and liability for $215,000 in back taxes plus interest. The odd detail: Capone tried to plead guilty — the trial only happened because the judge would not honour the deal; and the judge beat the jury-fixers by throwing away the whole panel on the morning of trial.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Secret Service hook. Why refuse a guilty plea from a man who had already confessed? Because the plea deal came with a sentence short enough that he would be back on the street before the Secret Service finished with what they had learned from him.",
