      "summary": "Security-Home Trust failed to open on 16 June 1931; Ohio Savings froze deposits for 60 days on 18 June (the EH.Net review dates the moratorium 17 June — sources differ); on 17 August three more banks stayed shut. Five banks with 34 branches were closed and more than $100 million in deposits frozen — about 70 per cent of the city's banking deposits, and Toledo depositors lost more per head than any other US city. Police recalled officers from vacation and the Guard was alerted. Insiders had moved first: Security-Home officers took out about $1.5 million in the last six weeks (confirmed: EH.Net says officers, relatives and their businesses) against $679,971 from the public on the final day, and a vice-president withdrew $1,000 (more than his balance) (unverified — V_R40: the $679,971 and $1,000 figures are on none of the pages read). Three bankers were indicted in 1931; no one was convicted. Toledo ended with one in three workers on relief (a figure the search summaries repeat; check before quoting). The odd detail: The county prosecutor who later broke the Licavoli gang (see next entry) — Frazier Reams — appears in one account as a director of Commercial Savings, one of the failed banks. (Sources spell his first name Frazier, Frazer and Frank; the director link rests on one review — the EH.Net review does state it — \"Frank Reams\", a Commercial Savings director who later led the prosecution — so it is single-sourced rather than unconfirmed.)",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the collapse (two sources agree on 16 June/17 Aug); Medium on totals; UNVERIFIED on Reams's exact role. • … six weeks (confirmed: EH.Net says officers, relatives and their businesses) against $679,971 from the public on the final day, and a vice-president withdrew $1,000 (more than his balance) (unverified — V_R40: the $679,971 and $1,000 figures are on none of the pages read).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The banker who cleans out his own account while the doors are still open is the low-level version of every \"no one is clean\" villain. Put the vault of a failed Toledo bank in the ninth month of its receivership — and a Toledo prosecutor who owes someone.",
