      "summary": "On 17 July 1931 UP reported that \"Lake Erie navigators\" were watching for a 30-foot sea serpent seen by at least ten people: fisherman H. E. Welsh took it for a row of kegs until it raised \"a huge head resembling that of an alligator\"; campers at the Portage River mouth confirmed it; Sandusky fisherman Jacob Teasel saw \"something like a huge limb\" in the Sandusky River — \"We didn't take time to haul anchor, but cut the rope and rowed ashore.\" On 22 July: \"SEA MONSTER IS CAPTURED; IT'S AN EIGHTEEN-FOOT PYTHON\" — two Cincinnati cement salesmen brought an 18-foot snake ashore, saying they had caught it from a rented rowboat in Sandusky Bay; Harold L. Madison, curator of the Cleveland Museum of Natural History, identified an Indian python. On 5 Sept 1934 a Cleveland UP item said that \"after two years of calmness following the Sandusky sea serpent 'scare',\" a 20-foot \"sea serpent\" with a head as large as a dog's had been reported off Edgewater bathing beach. On 21 March 1935 UP reported the \"Sandusky sea serpent\" drawing crowds at the Florida state fair in Tampa — \"a big snake from a Cleveland tent show, allegedly 'captured' in Sandusky Bay … by two zealous carnivalists.\" The odd detail: The museum curator's identification made the hoax respectable — and the hoax does not explain the ten witnesses and the alligator head before the python was \"caught.\"",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the July 1931 core event (an AP bulletin confirms fishermen hauling ashore a serpent-like creature that was exposed as a python exhibited by carnival hucksters); CORRECTED — the commonly reprinted size for the 22 July 1931 creature is twenty feet, not thirty (this session could not re-open R22's own cited Indianapolis Times page images to check which figure that specific paper used, so the two figures may both appear across the wire coverage; flagged, not silently changed).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The period's own three layers, in order: witnesses, a convenient \"capture\" by outsiders, a carnival exposure. A GM's fourth: who paid the salesmen to produce a python the week the fishermen started talking?",
