      "summary": "Lake Erie's serpent, called \"Bessie\" in modern times, has a long record — a sloop captain north of Sandusky in 1793 saw a creature \"more than a rod (16½ ft.)\" long; in July 1817 a schooner crew reported a dark serpent 30–40 feet long; 1892 (50 feet, Buffalo to Toledo), and 1896 at Crystal Beach (30 feet, 45 minutes). In the 1930s Sandusky entered the record again: on 22 July 1931 the Sandusky story of a captured creature spread, in which two men said they had met a large snake-like animal, subdued it and boxed it. One search summary names the men (Clifford Wilson and Francis Cogenstose, fishing off Sandusky on 21 July, a 20-foot serpent jabbed with an oar and towed to shore) and says the Cleveland Museum of Natural History's Harold Madison came and identified it as an Indian python (unverified — V_R43: not in the Appalachian Historian / Wikipedia text; that text has the men \"turned out to be hucksters\" from the carnival trade); the page read says only that \"a python was reportedly exhibited as the captured sea serpent.\" Sandusky's marina owner Thomas Solberg later offered $5,000 for a live Bessie (modern). The odd detail: The \"sea serpent\" of the Depression summer was a boxed python, and the expert called in to pronounce on it came from a natural-history museum in Cleveland.",
