      "summary": "Two municipal-court bailiffs and a real-estate agent (the building belonged to the M. J. Kolliner real-estate company) put 72-year-old Diana Gross's furniture on the street; neighbours and Unemployed Council members carried it back in. Police fought a crowd estimated from 450 to 4,000 (the Associated wire figure was 2,000). Three Black men were killed: Communist organiser Abe Gray (or Grey), John O'Neil, and — depending on the source — Thomas Page (the Daily Worker) or Frank Armstrong (found later in Washington Park; Communists believed he had been \"taken for a ride by the police\"). Three officers were injured and 30 people arrested. For days afterwards 5,000–10,000 people met every evening in Washington Park. The bodies lay in state for three days (Wikipedia: \"Old Fellows Hall,\" i.e. an Odd Fellows hall); the funeral of 8 August drew about 60,000 marchers, roughly 40% of them white. The odd detail: Relief agencies declared a rent moratorium almost at once — the killings bought the neighbourhood two years' reprieve. Chicago evictions ran to 63,152 in 1932 and fell to 8,876 in 1934 once relief paid rent.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
