      "summary": "Two municipal-court bailiffs and a real-estate agent put 72-year-old Diana Gross's furniture on the sidewalk. Neighbours and Unemployed Council activists carried it back in; a march came over from Washington Park, the crowd grew to somewhere between 450 and 4,000, and police fired. Three died: Abe Grey, a Communist Party organiser, shot by police; John O'Neil, a Black marcher killed after trying to fire a policeman's confiscated gun; and Frank Armstrong, found shot in Washington Park that night — party members said he had been \"taken for a ride.\" (The Daily Worker claimed a fourth on 10 August.) Council leader Joseph Gardner was arrested. For a week, nightly meetings in Washington Park drew 7,000–10,000; on 8 August a funeral procession of some 60,000 — about 40 percent of them white — marched behind the coffins, with 50,000 party leaflets and 75,000 funeral leaflets handed out on the South Side. The Tribune praised the police for acting \"with firmness and as much moderation as the circumstances permitted\" and blamed \"red propaganda\"; the Defender conceded the unemployment but warned that \"Communism had no place in America.\" Context: 750,000 Chicagoans were out of work in 1931 and rent-relief cases were up 311 percent. The odd detail: The third dead man was found in the park after dark, not at the eviction — the killing nobody could pin on the riot. (unverified — V_R14)",
