      "summary": "Construction began on 31 October 1929. First Lady Lou Henry Hoover christened the Akron on 8 August 1931; first flight 23 September 1931. It carried F9C Sparrowhawk biplane fighters, launched and caught in flight on a trapeze. On 11 May 1932, mooring at Camp Kearny near San Diego, the ship rose uncontrollably; the mooring cable was cut and four sailors held on — one dropped at about 15 ft with a broken arm, Aviation Carpenter's Mate Robert H. Edsall and Apprentice Seaman Nigel M. Henton fell to their deaths, and Apprentice Seaman C. M. \"Bud\" Cowart lashed himself to the line and was hauled in an hour later; the scene was filmed and shown in newsreels nationwide. In a violent thunderstorm around 00:23 on 4 April 1933 the Akron struck the sea: 73 of 76 died, including Rear Admiral William A. Moffett, chief of the Navy's Bureau of Aeronautics. The Navy blimp J-3, sent to search, also crashed, killing two more. The odd detail: The Akron carried an experimental \"spy basket\" — a subcloud observation car lowered on a cable so the hidden ship could see below the overcast; in trials it swung so violently it was called frighteningly unstable.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A ship designed by German engineers, christened by the President's wife, that swallows an admiral and 72 men in a thunderstorm — and the rescue blimp falls too. The spy basket, dangling a lone observer below the clouds, is a perfect set-piece for something seen that no one believes.",
