      "summary": "Goodyear-Zeppelin built USS Akron in the Airdock under chief designer Karl Arnstein and \"a team of experienced German airship engineers\" from Friedrichshafen — 785 feet, 6.5 million cubic feet of helium, eight Maybach engines, a hangar for five Sparrowhawk fighters. First Lady Lou Hoover christened her on 8 Aug 1931. At Camp Kearny, San Diego, in May 1932, a mooring went wrong and two sailors (Edsall and Henton) fell to their deaths from the lines while a third, Cowart, tied himself on and was hauled up. On 4 Apr 1933 she flew into \"one of the most violent stormfronts to sweep the North Atlantic states in 10 years,\" struck the sea tail-first and sank; 73 of 76 died — the deadliest airship disaster in history. The odd detail: the Great Lakes' rubber city built the largest flying machines on earth with German engineers, and both (her sister Macon too) died in the sea.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A hangar in Akron large enough to have weather inside. What the German engineers kept in the north end, and why the Hunter Guild's Ohio man will not go in after dark.",
