      "summary": "A Category 2 hurricane (about 100 mph) that had crossed the Caribbean made its final landfall just east of Veracruz at 06:00 UTC on 16 September 1931 — Mexican Independence Day. Winds in the city reached about 95 mph; it unroofed many homes, toppled trees, demolished warehouses and docks, and sank numerous ships in the harbour; damage exceeded $100,000. Three days earlier, the great British Honduras hurricane (c. 2,500 dead in Belize) had come ashore, much weakened, north of Tampico on 13 September. The odd detail: the storm took the docks on the national holiday — in the same summer as the cathedral shooting (25 July) and the Catemaco church sacking.",
