      "summary": "Juror Arthur O. Prochno told his story in the Chicago Tribune on May 10, 1936. Twelve men were kept in three rooms of the Great Northern Hotel, some sleeping two to a bed; \"Pockets were turned inside out. Cuffs of trousers were rolled down\"; newspapers were cut with scissors before they saw them; no phone calls home and no church on Sunday. Capone was \"a big, fat man\" with \"a ready smile\". Evidence included the \"walled-in estate on Palm Island\" and \"diamond belt buckles that Capone gave to his friends\". Deliberation took eight hours from Saturday evening into the night; the first ballot was 6–6. They had been given no food; one juror threatened to smash the face of the first man who held them up \"over an hour\"; another told Prochno \"To hell with the evidence!\"; another urged guilt because \"After all, he's only a dago.\" The foreman wrote the verdict wrong and had to rewrite it. The odd detail: The verdict that ended Capone came from twelve hungry men who wanted to get home before the judge left for the weekend — and at least one voted on ethnic contempt.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High that the account was published as described; the account itself is one juror's recollection five years later.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Twelve men sequestered in a Loop hotel with every newspaper censored — the perfect place for someone to reach a juror without any gangster being involved. A GM can have one of the six \"not guilty\" votes change after a dream.",
