      "summary": "Chester Gould drew \"Plainclothes Tracy\"; Joseph Medill Patterson of the Tribune syndicate renamed him Dick Tracy and supplied the origin (his sweetheart's father murdered by robbers); the strip debuted October 4, 1931 in the Detroit Mirror and spread nationwide. Tracy was modelled on Eliot Ness; his first great villain, \"Big Boy\", was Capone. By April 1937 it was America's third-favourite strip (Fortune poll), behind Little Orphan Annie and Popeye, and already attacked for its violence. The odd detail: The nation's comic-strip detective was invented in the Tribune's building from the city's own gangsters, the same year Capone went down.",
