      "summary": "Frank McErlane, Joe Saltis's partner, introduced the Thompson submachine gun to Chicago gang war on September 25, 1925, firing on Spike O'Donnell from a passing car after someone called \"Hello, Spike!\"; the Illinois Crime Survey called him \"the most brutal gunman who ever pulled a trigger in Chicago\". A drinker who slid into alcoholic psychosis, he was shot in the leg on January 28, 1930 (police suspected his common-law wife, Elfrieda Rigus), then fought off gunmen from his hospital bed on February 24, 1930 — one dropped a .45 traced to rival John \"Dingbat\" Oberta's people. On October 8, 1931 he shot Rigus four times and killed her German shepherds; associates then paid for his \"retirement\" to a houseboat. He died of pneumonia on October 8, 1932 — exactly one year later — convinced in his delirium \"that rival gangsters were coming to his hospital room to kill him; it took four attendants to hold him down.\" The odd detail: He died on the anniversary of the day he killed her, fighting enemies no one else in the room could see.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the dates as given in the article (it is internally inconsistent in one heading, which says \"1931\" for the 1930 leg shooting); the delirium quote is the article's.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A ready-made ghost story with real dates. Three layers: the papers saw a drunk dying of pneumonia; the gangland whisper said the woman came back on her anniversary; underneath, she did — and the dogs came with her.",
