      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Tlapacoyan",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Veracruz — the parish church of the Asunción de María Santísima and the municipal palace",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Tlapacoyan%2C%20Veracruz%20%E2%80%94%20the%20parish%20church%20of%20the%20Asunci%C3%B3n%20de%20Mar%C3%ADa%20Sant%C3%ADsima%20and%20the%20municipal%20palace%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "M01 has the reprisal on the town hall. Detail from the SciELO study: on 17 October 1931 the parish church was \"profaned by unknowns, who burned the images of the temple,\" including more than ten paintings from the San Joaquín chapel of the El Jobo hacienda that had been left there for safekeeping. Local testimony says police from Xalapa entered the church, some on horseback, to remove and burn the saints; the government blamed Catholic activists. The crowd (200–300 by the official count) then surrounded the municipal palace, used firearms, machetes and burning chile smoke, and killed municipal officials, mostly agrarian leaders Tejeda's land programme had promoted; one survivor escaped. The parish stayed closed: in 1932 the town's civic board asked federal authorities to reopen it, citing the destruction of images and furniture, and in 1933 the request was denied on reports of illegal religious services. Two men, Miguel Mota Benavides and Baldomero Mota, were charged (case 132, Jalacingo court). The same study lists other Veracruz towns where \"religious fetishes\" (images) were destroyed in 1931: Otatitlán, Jáltipan, Tierra Blanca, Medellín, Huatusco. The odd detail: the images burned were partly paintings on deposit from a hacienda chapel — a church used as a bank vault for another chapel's saints.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the burning, the closure and the reopening refusal; Medium on who set the fire (the study itself says the record is ambiguous).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a deposited image: a saint that was sent to the parish for safekeeping and burned in someone else's church. The hacienda owner (or the estate's heirs) wants the accounting — who else deposited what? The parish stays closed to 1933+, i.e., a sealed church, not a demolished one: the perfect module set-piece.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "scielo.org.mx: scielo",
          "url": "https://www.scielo.org.mx/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S1665-44202018000200232",
          "publisher": "scielo.org.mx",
          "kind": "web page cited in the Mexico and Caribbean research dossier",
          "supports": "read"
        }
      ],
