      "summary": "The independent Polish paper Przebudzenie reported that Senator William Borah, chairman of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, had backed revising the Versailles Treaty so that the \"Polish Corridor\" and Pomerania \"should belong to Germany.\" Believing Hoover agreed, the PNA and PRCU wired protests. Hoover's secretary Theodore G. Joslin answered: \"The President neither wishes nor can intervene in that kind of European affairs.\" The paper boasted Poland's borders were guarded by \"our brave Marshal Pilsudski and his three million army\" and called Germany's propaganda money \"waste.\" The odd detail: the thing Chicago's Poles feared most in 1931 — German revision of Poland's borders — is simply the setting's world.",
