      "summary": "Dr. Cornelius P. Rhoads of the Rockefeller Institute, working under the hematologist William B. Castle, studied pernicious and iron-deficiency anemia, tropical sprue and hookworm in 200-odd clinical subjects on controlled diets. On 10 November 1931, after finding his car vandalised in Cidra, he wrote an unmailed letter to a colleague, Fred W. Stewart, saying of Puerto Ricans: \"the dirtiest, laziest, most degenerate and thievish race of men ever inhabiting this sphere,\" and \"I have done my best to further the process of extermination by killing off 8 and transplanting cancer into several more.\" A staff member found and circulated the letter; it reached Albizu Campos in January 1932, who sent copies to the League of Nations, the Pan American Union, the ACLU, newspapers, embassies and the Vatican. Puerto Rico's Attorney General Ramón Quiñones and two Puerto Rican physicians (P. Morales Otero, E. Garrido Morales) plus the Rockefeller Institute itself reviewed 257 patient files and 13 deaths and found no evidence of killing; Rhoads was cleared. Ivy Lee's PR firm persuaded Time to remove \"and transplanting cancer into several more\" from its printing. Rhoads went on to direct Memorial Hospital (1940), the Sloan-Kettering Institute (1945; Legion of Merit for chemical-warfare work) and appear on the cover of Time in 1949. In 2003 the AACR renamed the Rhoads Award after a review by bioethicist Jay Katz found \"no evidence\" of killings but called the letter \"reprehensible.\" The odd detail: the sentence about \"transplanting cancer\" was cut by the editor of Time at the Rockefeller family's PR man's request — so the American reader in 1932 read the milder half.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the letter and clearance; the intent is disputed (Rhoads called it a joke).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — cult versus population, exactly: the letter was almost certainly a bitter joke in a racist mind, but it is what a population believes about a laboratory in its midst, and a GM can hang a real one behind it. The Secret Service or FBI Section 13 as the office that reads the letter and decides which of the two it is. Keep the real doctor's story exact; invent a different laboratory.",
