      "summary": "The tramp-poet who had walked the country trading self-printed rhymes for bread (1906–12) and made his name with The Congo (1912/14) wrote \"Abraham Lincoln Walks at Midnight\" (1914), in which Lincoln's mourning ghost walks the streets of Springfield at night and will not rest. Poor, ill and worn by reading tours, Lindsay killed himself on 5 December 1931 by drinking lye; his last words were reported as \"They tried to get me; I got them first!\" The odd detail: The man who put Lincoln's ghost on Springfield's midnight streets died in the house facing the governor's mansion, saying \"they\" had tried to get him.",
