      "summary": "Fr. Justin (Justyn) Figas, a Conventual Franciscan born in McClure, Pa., in 1886, had begun a Polish-language rosary broadcast in 1927 over WKEN and formed a six-station network from Buffalo's WEBR in 1931. On Sunday 6 December 1931 his Rosary Hour went out from Corpus Christi to Polish communities across America; it grew to about 79 stations and an estimated 5 million listeners, the oldest Polish-language Catholic radio programme in the world. He hosted it until his death in 1959. The parish built the Kolbe Center in 1928; the programme went out from its second floor. The odd detail: A Polish-American weekly says he was \"one of only 50 trained exorcists in the Catholic Church at his time\" and that the author's aunt was his secretary — a claim only on that page; treat as UNVERIFIED family lore. (The same piece links the 1949 St. Louis exorcism that inspired The Exorcist to a different Polish priest, Fr. Gregory Holewinski, not Figas.)",
