      "summary": "On 29 December 1931 the biologist Cyrus L. Lundell of the Mexican Exploitation Chicle Company (one source: the Tropical Plant Research Foundation, Washington, which had sent him to study how to tap Achras zapota for the gum industry) rediscovered the Maya city from the air. He named it \"Calakmul\" — ca (two), lak (adjacent), mul (artificial mound), \"City of the Two Adjacent Pyramids.\" He reported it to Sylvanus Morley at Chichén Itzá in March 1932 and Morley visited that year; Carnegie surveys in the 1930s mapped the core and recorded 103 stelae. Calakmul was the first of some sixteen sites Lundell found (unverified — V_M15). (Lundell: born 5 November 1907, died 28 March 1994; his early work was for the sapodilla-gum trade, of which Wrigley was \"a prominent user.\") The odd detail: a botanist working for the gum trade, looking down from the air at the forest the trade was tapping, found a Maya city with 103 recorded stelae — the trade that was bleeding the forest found what it hid.",
