      "summary": "Paul S. Taylor, a Berkeley social scientist, published Mexican Labor in the United States: Chicago and the Calumet Region in 1932 (reviewed in the American Journal of Sociology, vol. 39, no. 1, 1933; vol. II = Migration Statistics). He hired Robert J. Jones as research assistant and traded field notes with him; he followed his Chicago interviewees to Arandas, Jalisco, for his 1933 village study. Anita Edgar Jones wrote a 1928 University of Chicago study of Mexicans in Chicago (the boxcar maps; the view that every Mexican planned to return). Robert C. Jones (the FLPS names him \"C.\"; Arias & Durand give \"J.\" — one man or two is not settled here) ran the 1928–31 Church Federation survey (entry 06) and lectured to University of Chicago students in May 1931. Jackson Baur's WPA-supported master's thesis on \"Juvenile Delinquency in the Mexican Colony of South Chicago\" surveyed about 2,000 cases. Burgess's 1933 \"Mexicans on relief\" survey is entry 21. I did not read Taylor's own chapters. The odd detail: three Joneses — Anita Edgar, Robert C. and Robert J. — turn up across these sources, and the editors themselves say the name is uncertain.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on titles and dates; Taylor's contents unread.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the Chicago School's maps and case files are the setting's own institutional memory of the colonias; an investigator who needs \"the file on that family\" reaches for Burgess's 939 households first.",
