      "summary": "The Society's new museum opened in 1932 on the south edge of Lincoln Park — the former City Cemetery — after 36 years at Dearborn & Ontario. Its treasures came largely from Charles F. Gunther's estate (bought 1920 for $150,000): Lincoln's deathbed and the furniture of the room he died in, clothing said to be worn by the Lincolns at Ford's Theatre, the table on which Lee surrendered; the Society's first collection, including Lincoln's final draft of the Emancipation Proclamation, burned in 1871. The 1896 Dearborn building's ghost stories (legend says Eastland bodies were laid out there — folklore, not on the page) belong to the old house. The odd detail: In 1932 the city put Lincoln's deathbed in a new building on top of its first cemetery, a few yards from the Couch tomb.",
