      "summary": "The University of Chicago's great refractor — the 40-inch Alvan Clark doublet, still the largest refracting telescope ever used for research — sat in a Beaux-Arts pile by Henry Ives Cobb, financed in the 1890s by the streetcar swindler Charles T. Yerkes and carved with lion gargoyles and faces of the men who built it. Edwin B. Frost directed it 1903–1932; in 1932 Otto Struve, a Ukrainian-born astronomer of the great Struve dynasty who had fled the Russian civil war, took over (1932–1947) and made it a working centre for interstellar-medium research. Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar and Gerard Kuiper were on the 1930s staff. The odd detail: the observatory's carvings include a face that legend says is Yerkes's, and the building was paid for by a man later jailed for fraud; a great telescope in a Wisconsin resort village, reachable from Chicago by the North Western's Lake Geneva train.",
