      "summary": "Nearly a mile long (4,490 ft; 13 cantilever truss spans), 93 ft above the river, completed in 1932 after a 1920s bond issue. On its two great pylons stand eight Art Deco \"Guardians of Traffic\" designed by Henry Hering with architect Frank Walker, each holding a vehicle — a hay wagon, a covered wagon, a stagecoach, a 1930s automobile, and four kinds of motor truck. They are Berea sandstone, carved in Little Italy. A lower deck for four lanes of traffic and two streetcar tracks was built but never completed. In June 1937, boys found a skeleton in a burlap sack under this bridge — the body identified, tentatively, as the Torso victim Rose Wallace (R06). The odd detail: Eight giant stone watchmen, cut by the cemetery carvers of Little Italy, stood over the valley where the Butcher left a body — and underneath them ran an empty, never-used deck.",
