      "summary": "The phrase the world uses for Milwaukee's socialism was an insult. When Hoan challenged Morris Hillquit for the national chairmanship in 1932 (and lost 105–86), Hillquit derided the Milwaukeeans as \"practical\" Socialists: \"I do not belong to the Daniel Hoan group to whom Socialism consists of merely providing clean sewers of Milwaukee.\" Milwaukee took the name and kept it. Emil Seidel, the first Socialist mayor (1910–12), had raised the municipal minimum wage from $1.75 to $2 a day and given city workers the eight-hour day; the party had won 9 of 46 council seats as early as 1904 and never had more than about 5,000 members before the war. The odd detail: The Milwaukee sewer system Hillquit sneered at is the one that sells its sludge as lawn fertiliser (R20, Milorganite).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"Clean sewers\" as the period's own reading; underneath, a city that knows exactly what goes into its drains and dries it into pellets.",
