      "summary": "In 1932 University of Chicago scholar Edgar Goodspeed found a complete thirteenth-century Byzantine New Testament in a Paris antique shop. His colleague Harold Willoughby acquired it for Edith Rockefeller McCormick, who lent it for study to the University’s Department of New Testament. The University Library’s donor history documents this acquisition and distinguishes it from the manuscript’s later purchase and donation in 1942. This offers an actual bridge between a wealthy Chicago household, the antiquities trade, religious scholarship and a medieval object newly present in the city during the campaign window.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Do not conflate the 1932 loan with the 1942 donation or the separate Elizabeth Day McCormick Apocalypse donated in 1937. No supernatural property is asserted.",
      "hook": "Fictional campaign idea: scholars find a marginal note naming a Chicago street that did not exist when the manuscript was copied.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Building for a Long Future: The University of Chicago and Its Donors, 1889–1930",
          "url": "https://www.lib.uchicago.edu/media/documents/exblf-UChicago-Donors-T.pdf",
          "publisher": "University of Chicago Library",
          "kind": "Institutional exhibition catalogue",
          "supports": "PDF page 15: 1932 discovery, acquisition for McCormick, scholarly loan and later ownership"
        }
      ],
