      "summary": "DIA director Wilhelm Valentiner — German-born — commissioned the Mexican muralist Diego Rivera, and Edsel Ford put up $20,000. Rivera painted 27 fresco panels of the Rouge and Detroit industry in 1932–33. One panel shows a haloed, Christ-like child being vaccinated by a doctor and nurse, framed like a Nativity, with scientists behind; Catholic and Episcopal clergy called the murals blasphemous, the Detroit News called them vulgar and un-American, and some demanded they be destroyed. Valentiner and Ford refused; 10,000 people came on one Sunday after the outcry. The odd detail: A Nativity where the gifts are serum and the Magi are laboratory men — painted on a museum wall paid for by Ford's son.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
