      "id": "R20-057",
      "title": "Poison under the ice — the phenol winter of 1932–33, and Indiana's law that exempted the lake",
      "date_start": "1932",
      "date_end": "1933",
      "date_label": "1932 – 1933",
      "category": "The city's guts: power, water, gas, sewers, tunnels, bridges, digging, building and demolition (Chicago and the Great Lakes, 1925–1940)",
      "tags": [
        "utilities",
        "building",
        "Poison",
        "Indiana's"
      ],
      "people": [
        "—"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Lake Michigan off the mouth of the Indiana Harbor Ship Canal",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(East Chicago, Indiana) — drifting to Chicago's intake cribs, which stood an average of 14,432 ft offshore",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Lake%20Michigan%20off%20the%20mouth%20of%20the%20Indiana%20Harbor%20Ship%20Canal%20%20%E2%80%94%20drifting%20to%20Chicago%27s%20intake%20cribs%2C%20which%20stood%20an%20average%20of%2014%2C432%20ft%20offshore"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Since the winter of 1928–29 Chicago's water had periodically carried phenol, which combined with the chlorine to give a \"very disagreeable chlorophenol taste\" and — worse — \"absorbing the chlorine, reducing the amount available for the purpose of disinfection.\" The city traced it to six by-product coke plants, three in Illinois and three in Indiana; after agreements the tastes eased in 1931–32. Then in December 1932, in extremely cold weather, phenol wastes piled up under and in the ice off the Indiana Harbor Ship Canal; \"when the ice broke they floated in large quantities to Chicago's intakes.\" A new oil refinery on the Grand Calumet was adding phenol, and since 1930 Indiana refinery wastes had given the water a \"musty-oily\" taste. Indiana's own 1927 anti-pollution law ended with a proviso: \"none of the provisions of this act shall apply to Lake Michigan\" or to any stream or channel draining into it along the Indiana or Illinois shore. The odd detail: A neighbouring state wrote the lake out of its pollution law in so many words — and one hard freeze turned Indiana's waste into a slick that drifted, under the ice, to Chicago's drinking-water intakes.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Owner's wish list — \"loss of water\": a winter when the city's water went bad and the chlorine stopped working, exactly when something wanted the city's water unguarded. The cause is chemical — mostly.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Internet Archive: watersupplyorgan00whit",
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/watersupplyorgan00whit",
          "publisher": "Internet Archive",
          "kind": "book or scanned text",
          "supports": "pp. 51, 63–65; citing Acts of Indiana 1927, ch. 45, and U.S. Public Health Bulletin No. 170, 1924–25"
        }
      ],
