      "summary": "Wisconsin passed the nation's first unemployment-compensation law, three years before the federal Social Security Act. The idea was conceived by John R. Commons and his students at the university; the bill was the Groves Act, named for Harold Groves, a Commons student and UW professor. Gov. Philip La Follette signed it in 1932 (the Wisconsin Department of Workforce Development's own timeline gives 28 January 1932). It was modelled on workmen's compensation: each employer had a separate reserve fund, to give employers a financial incentive to steady employment; employers won a one-year delay and the act took effect in 1934. The odd detail: The world's first unemployment insurance was built on the idea of making employers pay for the boredom of layoffs, not the state.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (dates of signing conflict).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A \"reserve fund\" in each employer's name — an actuary's audit turns up a fund that an employer keeps paying into for workers who do not exist.",
