      "summary": "Paul Rader's Tabernacle gathered and canned surplus farm produce for hungry Chicago households. Cabbage odors and disagreements over the proper scale of relief work became part of its story. At the same time, financial support collapsed as wealthy backer Albert Johnson suffered Depression losses. Rader took on debt, the radio ministry went off the air in early 1933, and he resigned that spring; broadcasting later resumed under his successor. Wheaton's archive preserves institutional records and oral histories. This offers intersecting locations and interests: a mass congregation, volunteer cannery, creditors, microphones, missionaries and families waiting for food.",
