      "summary": "When John L. Lewis imposed a cut from $6.10 to $5.00 a day, Illinois miners broke from the UMWA and founded the Progressive Miners of America at Gillespie on 1 September 1932 (about 18,000 members). At Mulkeytown on 24 August 1932 a caravan of striking miners was attacked — \"vehicles were upended and workers were shot at and beaten\". The rival-union war ran for years; in 1937, 39 Progressive members were federally indicted for racketeering (despite evidence of UMWA–Peabody collusion) and 34 went to prison, many to Leavenworth. The PMA's Women's Auxiliary was one of the few movements where non-wage-earning women led the organising of an all-male industry. The odd detail: A union war fought against another union — with miners' wives as its officers.",
