      "summary": "In August 1932 a dispute over a proposed pay cut split the miners between the United Mine Workers and the new Progressive Miners of America. On 25 January 1933 a giant rally brought 8,000 women to Springfield in support of the Progressives. On Easter Sunday, 21 April 1935, PMA activist Edris Mabie (1894–1935) was shot dead at Sixth and Washington Streets in a street confrontation, not a drive-by: a car of UMWA partisans collided with other cars, PMA miners surrounded the cars, and both sides began shooting; Mabie was killed and Art Gramlich badly wounded. UMWA Illinois president Ray Edmundson and Fred Thomasson, \"a cohort of\" Charlie Birger, were in the UMWA party, but no one was ever charged. (The Sangamon County timeline dates the killing 25 April; the fuller page says Easter Sunday, 21 April — take 21 April.) On 18 December 1937 a federal jury in Springfield convicted all 36 Progressive Miners of racketeering over 44 mine and railroad bombings; a summary says the union fight \"began and effectively ended in Springfield,\" with strikes, bombings, street riots and at least six killings in Sangamon County. The odd detail: A Birger cohort was in the UMWA party at a fatal Easter-morning street shootout with the Progressive Miners — the southern-Illinois gang war of R29 reaches the state capital, though nobody was charged.",
