      "summary": "Insull's utility empire — $500 million controlled with only $27 million in equity — collapsed in 1932 and wiped out 600,000 shareholders; from January to March 1932 Insull stock fell 76%, and by early June Insull debt had lost 98% of its value. On 1 October 1932 the Cook County state's attorney asked Samuel and his brother Martin to \"return voluntarily\"; on 4 October they were indicted for embezzlement and larceny; on 5 October Samuel vanished from his Paris hotel; on 8 October he flew from Italy to Greece, which had no extradition treaty with the U.S. On 15 March 1934 he vanished from his apartment in Greece; on 16 March he was found aboard a Greek freighter bound for Egypt and the ship was turned back; on 18 March he was allowed to sail on condition he enter no Greek port; on 29 March he reached Istanbul, where on 1 April Turkish authorities took him off the ship for a 15-minute hearing, jailed him on 2 April, and on 13 April handed him to American agents aboard a ship at Smyrna. He landed in American custody on 7 May 1934. On 24 November 1934, after a seven-week mail-fraud trial, he and 16 associates were acquitted after two hours' deliberation. He died of a heart attack at the Place de la Concorde Métro station in Paris on 16 July 1938 with 30 francs in his pocket, identified by a hotel laundry bill. The odd detail: The Civic Opera House he built opened 4 November 1929 — six days after Black Tuesday — with Aida. (The 09-22 archive has this as W08.)",
