      "summary": "The state's Depression relief ran through the Illinois Emergency Relief Commission (created 1932 — UNVERIFIED date), whose chairmen included the Democratic publisher John C. Martin (1935–38) and which drew protests as well as thanks: in Bond County five hundred marchers went to its office over the delivery of supplies. Its nutrition services were directed by Adelaide Spohn, also of the Elizabeth McCormick Memorial Fund. The private side was the Council of Social Agencies under the steel man Edward L. Ryerson Jr. (section 4) and the settlement-house money of Bowen and her circle; the Junior League of Chicago (the first League to stage children's theatre, 1921) supplied the debutantes who served. The 1931–32 Joint Emergency Relief Fund drive (remembered as raising over $10 million from the rich) could not be confirmed from a page this session — UNVERIFIED. The odd detail: Relief in Illinois was administered by the same names that appear on the opera boxes — the men who lost the money and the women who married it.",
