      "summary": "A working writers' club of \"about 20 or so\" met every other week in Milwaukee through the decade. Robert Bloch (b. Chicago 5 Apr 1917; in Milwaukee from 1929 after his father lost his bank job) wrote to H. P. Lovecraft in 1933 as a Lincoln High schoolboy, was answered, coached and lent manuscripts, sold \"The Feast in the Abbey\" and \"The Secret in the Tomb\" to Weird Tales in July 1934 at seventeen (printed Jan and May 1935), joined the Fictioneers in 1935 (where Gus Marx was a fellow member) and later, c. 1942–53, worked as a copywriter at Marx's advertising agency; Lovecraft's death in March 1937 left him feeling \"a part of me had died with him.\" Stanley G. Weinbaum (b. Louisville 4 Apr 1902; Riverside High; left Madison in 1923 after sitting an exam for a friend on a bet) published \"A Martian Odyssey\" in July 1934, changed the field in two years, and died of lung cancer in Milwaukee on 14 Dec 1935, aged 33. Raymond A. Palmer (b. Milwaukee 1 Aug 1910; a truck's wheel-spokes at seven, Pott's disease, five years prone in a Bradford frame, a sanitarium from Sept 1930, four feet tall as a man) joined about 1932 and on 14 Feb 1938 became editor of Amazing Stories in Chicago at $75 a week — the man who would print the Shaver Mystery and found Fate. Ralph Milne Farley was Roger Sherman Hoar (b. 1887; Harvard Law 1911; a Massachusetts state senator in 1911; great-great-grandson of the Declaration's Roger Sherman), corporate lawyer for Bucyrus-Erie in South Milwaukee and Marquette engineering lecturer (unverified — V_R27), whose Radio Man ran in Argosy from June 1924; he declined the Amazing editorship in 1938. In 1939–40 Bloch and his schoolfriend Harold Gauer wrote the speeches, advertising and stunts for the campaign (managed by James Doolittle) that put Carl Zeidler in City Hall (below) — and were never paid. The odd detail: In the decade the setting is about, the man who would write Psycho was a teenage Lovecraft disciple in Milwaukee, in a club with the man who would invent the flying-saucer press and a Bucyrus lawyer descended from a signer of the Declaration — and one of them died the week of the winter solstice, 1935.",
