      "summary": "Widowed in 1921, Jenckes (born Terre Haute 6 November 1877) ran the farm and grain business herself, and in 1932 beat Republican Fred Purnell with 54 per cent to become Indiana's first woman in Congress. She voted for the Cullen–Harrison beer bill in March 1933, won an $18 million flood-control appropriation for the Wabash basin, backed the New Deal on her own terms, and grew loud against communism, \"which subjected Jenckes to public ridicule.\" She lost in 1938 to Noble Johnson and said the ballots had been tampered with. She died in Terre Haute 9 January 1975, aged 97, after twenty years with the Red Cross. The odd detail: A widow grain-dealer from the Wabash who sat in Congress through the whole first New Deal and left claiming the count was fixed.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
