      "summary": "Purvis was Special Agent in Charge at Chicago from 1932. Little Bohemia (April 1934) — \"the FBI's worst failure for the next 59 years\" — cost him command of the hunt: Hoover put Inspector Samuel Cowley over all agents on the case wherever they were. At the Biograph Purvis gave the signal by lighting his cigar and waving his hand; in October he led the agents at the Conkle farm. He became the Bureau's public face, resigned in July (unverified — V2_R02) 1935 under Hoover's displeasure (a 2005 study frames Hoover as jealous of the attention Purvis got after Dillinger), and was erased from the Bureau's own tellings. On 29 February 1960 he died at his home in Florence of a gunshot to the head from the pistol fellow agents had given him when he left the Bureau; a later inquiry allowed that it may have been accidental. The odd detail: The man who lit the cigar died by the gun his colleagues gave him as a going-away present.",
