      "summary": "In 1932 the United States and Canada signed a treaty to build a deep waterway and power works on the St. Lawrence. It went to the U.S. Senate in November 1932; hearings ran until 14 March 1934, when the vote won a majority but not the two-thirds needed to ratify. Opposition came from Atlantic and Gulf ports, inland-waterway interests and the railroads — and from the Ontario (Mitchell Hepburn) and Quebec governments. A 1941 Roosevelt–King agreement also failed in Congress; the Seaway came only in 1954–59. The odd detail: Chicago's hope of being a seaport died in a Senate vote that the treaty actually won — just not by enough.",
