      "summary": "Sears president Julius Rosenwald died at home in Highland Park on January 6, 1932, aged 69. After a 1911 visit to Munich's Deutsches Museum he pledged $3 million (eventually over $5 million) to convert the 1893 fair's Palace of Fine Arts into a museum of industry; it opened in stages from 1933, during the Century of Progress, with a working coal mine built from Old Ben No. 17 at Johnston City, Illinois (closed 1923). His Rosenwald Fund (1917) was designed to spend itself to nothing (gone by 1948). In 1929 he built the 421-unit Michigan Boulevard Garden Apartments for Black tenants, taking 2.4% on $2.7 million. His grandson Armand Deutsch believed he had been Leopold and Loeb's intended 1924 victim. The odd detail: The museum's underground coal mine is a real mine's machinery re-buried under a fair palace — a mine shaft in a museum, open through the whole decade.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A coal mine under a museum under a fair; a fund built to die; a grandson marked by the century's most famous murderers. The IHS would want to know what else Rosenwald moved from Munich.",
