      "summary": "The Sears, Roebuck president behind nearly 5,000 Southern Black schools, 25 Black YMCAs in 24 cities (1911–33, over $600,000, on a $25,000-to-$75,000 challenge), the Rosenwald Fund (1917; fellowships to Black artists and scholars; designed to spend itself out, which it did by 1948), Provident Hospital's move, and the $2.7 million Michigan Boulevard Garden Apartments (1929, accepting a 2.4 percent return) died aged 69 on 6 January 1932, in the Depression's worst winter. Almost every institution in this file — the Wabash Y, the Rosenwald Apartments, Provident, Dunham's fieldwork, Davis's fellowship — ran on his money. The odd detail: The Black Belt's largest single patron was a German-Jewish mail-order man from the North Shore whose fund was ordered to die — and did, in 1948.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A fund designed to spend itself to nothing by a fixed date is the shape of a bargain. Who wrote the terms, and what happens to the institutions when it runs dry?",
