      "summary": "On the evening of 26 January 1932 Howard Woolverton (b. 1880), secretary-treasurer of the Malleable Steel Range Manufacturing Company, was driving home from a movie with his wife Florence and a family friend, Bessie Studebaker, when a man jumped onto the running board of their Pierce-Arrow. Ordered to stop a few miles west of South Bend, he was told he was being held for $50,000; his wife was sent home with the ransom instructions and he was taken to a farmhouse. He talked the price down to $8,000, was dropped in Chicago about 24 hours later, and came home by train unharmed. Hoover later laid the job to George \"Machine Gun\" Kelly, Kathryn Kelly and Edward Doll, but no one was ever charged. The case \"revived discussion\" of the stalled federal kidnapping bill in Congress five weeks before the Lindbergh baby was taken, and \"played a leading role\" in the Act's passage that June. The odd detail: A Studebaker in the car, a Pierce-Arrow under them, a steel-range maker as the prize — South Bend's industry in one kidnapping. And the victim haggled his own ransom down by 84 per cent.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A kidnapping that ends with the victim negotiating in a farmhouse and walking away — the \"period's own reading\" is a soft-hearted gang; what is actually there could be a man who talked his way out of something else.",
