      "summary": "Churchill was on a paid North American lecture tour, resumed after he had been knocked down by a car in New York in December 1931. In Chicago McCormick sent Capt. Maxwell Corpening to collect him at Dearborn Station and put him up at his Astor Street house, where Churchill \"would park himself in front of a certain Cézanne.\" He spoke on \"The World Economic Crisis\" to more than 1,200 at the Union League Club and gave a second lecture at Orchestra Hall. McCormick gave at least one luncheon for him with University of Chicago president Robert Maynard Hutchins and Illinois Supreme Court Justice Frederic DeYoung; Mayor Cermak was sick and did not attend. Threats from \"an Indian society\" meant the Scotland Yard bodyguard Walter Thompson worked the visit; the secretary Phyllis Moir travelled with him. The Tribune praised his \"candor\" and called him \"a forthright Briton… seasoned statesman.\" The odd detail: An Indian nationalist society's threats turned a Loop lecture into a guarded event — and the guard was the same detective who shadowed Churchill through the next war.",
