      "summary": "Two weeks after the Lindbergh baby was taken (1 March 1932), Arthur Brisbane's syndicated column (Syracuse Journal, 17 March 1932) reported his talk with Al Capone. Capone believed that if he were let out of jail he could \"connect with his various associates, or 'angles,' and seek information throughout the country.\" His terms: he would \"put up a bond for his return, leaving his young brother in prison as a hostage\", and he would make the search \"accompanied everywhere by Thomas Callaghan, a skilled man of the United States secret service.\" The offer was not taken up. Capone went to federal prison in May 1932 (per R01). The odd detail: the escort Capone proposed was not a policeman or a G-man but a named Secret Service captain, and he offered his own brother as a hostage.",
