      "summary": "After the shooting at Gate 3 on 7 March 1932 (R07), wounded marchers were chained to their hospital beds — Mayor Frank Murphy condemned \"the chaining of patient prisoners to beds\" as \"a brutal practice\", called Harry Bennett an \"inhuman brute\" and Henry Ford \"a terrible man\". The funeral on 12 March drew 25,000–60,000 under the slogan \"Smash the Ford-Murphy Terror\" — the marchers' organisers blamed the liberal mayor too, since Detroit police had joined the pursuit. The four dead were buried side by side at Woodmere. Prosecutor Harry S. Toy convened a grand jury, which reported at the end of June 1932 that it found \"no basis for indictments\", condemned the marchers and cleared the police and Ford's men; one juror, Mrs Jerry Houghton Bacon, a Murphy ally, called it \"the most biased, prejudiced, and ignorant proceeding imaginable\". Curtis Williams died of his wounds on 7 August 1932; refused burial at Woodmere because he was Black, he was cremated and his ashes scattered from a chartered aeroplane over the Rouge. The Workers Film and Photo League filmed the march and showed the footage at meetings. The odd detail: Wounded men chained to hospital beds as prisoners, for being shot.",
