      "summary": "Between 3,000 and 5,000 unemployed auto workers marched on the Rouge with 14 demands (rehiring, health care, the right to organise). At the Dearborn line police and Ford's Service men met them with tear gas, fire hoses and then gunfire; Ford security chief Harry Bennett, hit by rocks, fired into the crowd and kept firing at the retreating marchers. Four were killed — Joseph York (20), Kalman Leny (26), Joseph DeBlasio (31), Joseph Bussell (16) — and Curtis Williams (36) died of his wounds months later; at least 22 marchers and 25 police were hurt. Between 25,000 and 60,000 joined the funeral march on 12 March; the four were buried together at Woodmere Cemetery. The odd detail: Woodmere refused to bury Curtis Williams because he was Black, so his ashes were scattered from an airplane over the River Rouge plant.",
