      "summary": "Harold Hart Crane (born Garrettsville, Ohio, 21 July 1899) grew up in Cleveland; his father Clarence A. Crane invented Life Savers and sold the patent for $2,900 before it made money. Hart dropped out of East High in December 1916 and worked in his father's chocolate factory between stints in New York (1917–24). In 1931–32 he went to Mexico on a Guggenheim Fellowship. Returning on the Orizaba on 27 April 1932, about 300 miles from Cuba, drunk, he said \"Goodbye, everybody!\" and jumped overboard at about noon. No note; the body was never found; the family stone at Garrettsville reads \"lost at sea.\" The odd detail: The son of the man who invented the Life Saver drowned without a trace.",
