      "summary": "Capone went to Atlanta in May 1932, aged 33; the intake examination diagnosed syphilis and gonorrhea. He was moved to the new Alcatraz in August 1934. In February 1938 he was formally diagnosed with neurosyphilis, then transferred to Terminal Island, and paroled on November 16, 1939. He was treated at Union Memorial Hospital in Baltimore in 1939–40, took penicillin in 1942, and lived out his life at Palm Island in Florida. He had a stroke on January 21, 1947, cardiac arrest the next day, and died January 25, 1947, aged 48. He was buried at Mount Olivet Cemetery, Chicago, and reburied at Mount Carmel Cemetery, Hillside, in 1950. The odd detail: The disease that ended him was found at the Atlanta intake examination in 1932 — the most powerful gangster in America went into prison already carrying the thing that would take his mind.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on dates and places; the Alcatraz prisoner-number story is omitted (not in the source read).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Three layers: the period saw a gangster who lost his mind; the medicine says syphilis; the thing underneath is that someone had been in his head since before Atlanta. Section 13 visits him in the Alcatraz hospital ward to ask questions only a man going mad would answer.",
