      "summary": "In El Nacional, 14 May 1932, Consul Rafael Aveleyra announced (in a letter dated 10 May) that, \"through the efforts of the Secretary of Foreign Relations,\" \"all Mexican families who find themselves in economical straits\" could return to Mexico for $15 by the Missouri Pacific, with Mexican National Railways charging a cent a mile beyond Laredo, and that the consulate wanted to organise the first train for emigrants at that rate. (The Indiana figure — $15 per adult, $7.50 per child, Lake County, six trainloads June–Oct 1932 — is in R37 and M06; this is the same price from the Chicago side.) A Nov 1929 editorial in México had already said Mexican workers \"are not exiles or outcasts\" and that \"repatriation is for the outcasts\" (R15). The odd detail: the same consul who asked for the man who defrauded 200 workers in 1928 organised the discounted trains that removed them. (unverified — V_M18: the FLPS never names the 1928 consul; Aveleyra first appears in it in January 1930, and no train is recorded as having run.)",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. • the same consul who asked for the man who defrauded 200 workers in 1928 organised the discounted trains that removed them. (unverified — V_M18: the FLPS never names the 1928 consul; Aveleyra first appears in it in January 1930, and no train is recorded as having run.)",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the consul's first train is a manifest with names, and the Golden Circle needs a way to move people quietly to the border. The train's passenger list is the mission.",
