      "summary": "On his first American journey Meher Baba travelled west from the retreat at Harmon, N.Y., by way of Erie, Pa., and \"reached Chicago, Illinois (La Salle Station) the next evening at 26th May, 1932 at 7:20 P.M.\" He had to change to the Golden State Limited for Kansas City and Los Angeles, \"necessitating an hour's wait, during which time reporters surrounded Baba. Baba gave them a short message and was photographed.\" Then he and his companions \"quickly drove around Chicago in taxis, along Lake Shore Drive and through the city, before reboarding the train,\" leaving at 9:15 p.m. With him: Jean and Malcolm Schloss, Meredith and Margaret Starr, Kaka, Chanji, Adi Jr., Beheram and Quentin Tod. Confirmed: he was in Chicago — for under two hours. The odd detail: A master who (by the general record) had kept silence since 1925 \"gave a short message\" to the Chicago press in a railway station — the page does not give its words, and Chicago's papers are where they'd be.",
