      "summary": "Charles G. Dawes resigned as president of the new Reconstruction Finance Corporation on 6 June 1932 to go back to his Chicago bank, the Central Republic Bank & Trust Co. On Sunday 26 June its directors met on an emergency that \"threatened all Loop banks.\" Dawes's management was preparing to close the bank voluntarily; the other Chicago bankers, fearing collapse of the whole Loop, persuaded him to stay open. At about 3 a.m., with RFC officials in Washington on an open telephone wire, a $90 million \"Dawes loan\" was arranged (Calomiris & Mason: the plan was $80 million RFC plus $10 million from banks, with the RFC accepting the city's tax warrants — $30 million of which the Loop banks held — as collateral). It was the largest loan in the RFC's history (unverified — V_R03: not stated by Time, Calomiris & Mason, or the RFC pages read), and cries of favouritism followed. Central Republic still withdrew from banking in October 1932 and was officially closed two years later; Dawes started a new bank, City National Bank & Trust Co., and chaired it until his death. The RFC recovered about $45 million, then in November 1934 sued some 5,000 stockholders for $14 million under double liability — and won. The odd detail: The city's unpaid tax warrants — the same paper the teachers were being handed instead of salaries — were accepted as collateral to save a banker's bank.",
