      "summary": "The Democrats opened on 27 June 1932 — the very day the RFC's Dawes loan was agreed and the Loop panic broke. On 29 June they voted 934–213 to advocate repeal of the Eighteenth Amendment. Roosevelt had 666¼ votes on the first ballot, short of the 769½ needed; after Garner's Texas delegates were brought over he won on the fourth ballot with 945 votes to Al Smith's 190½ (1 July). Melvin Traylor of the First National Bank of Chicago drew 42¼ votes on the first ballot. On 2 July Roosevelt, who had made an airplane trip to Chicago, accepted in person — breaking precedent — and pledged \"a New Deal for the American people.\" When the band had repeated his favourite \"Anchors Aweigh\" without enthusiasm, someone reportedly shouted \"For God's sake, have them play something else,\" and it struck up \"Happy Days Are Here Again\" (Ager/Yellen, 1929, from the film Chasing Rainbows), which became the party's anthem. The odd detail: A Loop banker (Traylor) was drawing presidential votes inside the Stadium in the same week his own depositors were queuing outside his bank.",
