      "summary": "The Secret Service took \"full-time responsibility for presidential protection\" in 1902, and the White House Police (founded 1922) \"was fully integrated into the Secret Service in 1930.\" But Congress \"authorized protection of major presidential and vice presidential candidates and nominees\" only in 1968, after Robert Kennedy's assassination. So when Governor Roosevelt flew to Chicago in July 1932 to accept the nomination, he was a candidate, and the Service had no statutory duty to guard him. That is an inference from the 1968 date; the pages read do not describe who did guard him in Chicago. The odd detail: the Service met Roosevelt as its charge only after the November election. Its first big night with him was Bayfront Park.",
