      "summary": "Directed by Victor Halperin, produced by Edward Halperin, released by United Artists on 28 July 1932 — the seventeenth anniversary of the Marines' landing, to the day. Bela Lugosi plays the zombie-master \"Murder\" Legendre, whose dead labourers turn a sugar mill in Haiti. Shot in 11 days for about $50,000, largely at night, on sets reused from Dracula, Frankenstein, The Hunchback of Notre Dame and The Cat and the Canary; make-up by Jack Pierce. Based on Seabrook; Kenneth Webb, author of the 1932 Broadway play Zombie, sued and lost. The first feature-length zombie film; loose sequel Revolt of the Zombies (1936). The odd detail: the anniversary coincidence (release 28 July 1932; landing 28 July 1915) is real and apparently unremarked at the time.",
