      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Port Weller",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(Lake Ontario) to Port Colborne (Lake Erie), across the Niagara Escarpment at Thorold",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Port%20Weller%20%20to%20Port%20Colborne%20%2C%20across%20the%20Niagara%20Escarpment%20at%20Thorold"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Begun in 1913, halted 1916–19 for lack of workers (the Wikipedia page attributes the stoppage to the Great War — see Divergence), and completed and officially opened on 6 August 1932, the fourth canal had eight locks — seven on the escarpment, including the twinned flight locks 4–6 at Thorold, and a guard lock at Port Colborne — each 766 ft by 80 ft, with a 25-ft depth. It let the big upper-lakes freighters into Lake Ontario for the first time. The Welland Canal Fallen Workers Memorial at Lock 3 (unveiled 12 November 2017) names 137 men who died building it. The odd detail: A hundred and thirty-seven dead for eight locks — and the canal that let ocean-going ships from Europe (the Viator, above) climb 326 feet into the upper lakes and reach Chicago.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — 137 dead in the locks — a Kabbalah of David reckoning would call the canal a built thing with a price paid in blood; Golden Circle engineers might have paid it on purpose. The flight locks at Thorold are three stacked chambers a ship cannot leave.",
