      "summary": "The Series was poisoned before it started: the Cubs had voted mid-season pickup Mark Koenig (an ex-Yankee) only a half share of Series money, and the Yankees rode them for it; Chicago fans reportedly cursed and spat at Ruth and his wife Claire arriving at the park, and pelted him with lemons in left field and at the plate. In the fifth inning, score 4–4, with two strikes on him from Charlie Root, Ruth made some gesture — toward the centre-field flagpole, the scoreboard, or the Cubs' dugout — and hit the next curveball an estimated 440–490 feet to deep centre. Root swore to his death that Ruth never pointed (\"he'd have ended up on his ass\"); Lou Gehrig (on a 2020-surfaced audio clip) said he pointed \"toward the flagpole in center field.\" In the stands: Governor Franklin D. Roosevelt, the Democratic candidate, five weeks from the election and five months from the presidency, and twelve-year-old John Paul Stevens, who as a Supreme Court justice still insisted Ruth pointed at the scoreboard. Two amateur 16-mm films (Kandle's, surfaced 1970s; Warp's, 1999) show a gesture and settle nothing. The odd detail: The future President and a future Justice were both in the seats: Roosevelt reportedly laughed as Ruth ran the bases; Stevens insisted for the rest of his life that Ruth pointed.",
