      "summary": "Peoria had been \"the Whiskey Capital of the World\" for about 50 years until 1917 (federal liquor tax averaging nearly $30 million a year, 1912–June 1917; liquor was the fourth-largest US industry). Prohibition began for the town on 17 January 1920; by 1921 it was described as the \"wettest\" spot in Illinois, and distilleries went over to yeast, malt syrup, acetylene gas, corn products, paint and varnish. William Edgar Hull — a former government gauger, postmaster (1898–1906), general manager of the Clarke Brothers distillery and a Congressman (1923–33: five terms, beaten in 1932 by Everett Dirksen) — sold the Canadian company Hiram Walker & Sons on the old Great Western site. Ground was broken on a $7-million plant on 21 October 1932, before repeal; it opened for public tours on 4 July 1934 with Hull as general manager. It employed over 1,000 at its peak and closed in 1981, after which Archer Daniels Midland moved in (unverified — V_R38: not on the Peoria Magazine page read). (Repeal itself: 5 December 1933.) The odd detail: The man who represented Peoria in Congress from 1923 to 1933 — the Prohibition years — was the man who got a Canadian whisky house to break ground on a $7-million Peoria plant a full year before repeal, and ran it.",
