      "summary": "On the night of Roosevelt's 1932 landslide, Probate Judge Henry Horner beat former governor Len Small by a plurality of 550,000 and carried Illinois by a larger margin than Roosevelt; JTA reported him alongside Herbert Lehman of New York as the first Jewish governors of both states, elected the same night. The campaign had been \"contentious,\" with opponents introducing anti-Semitic rhetoric as an issue. Inaugurated on 10 January 1933 in \"a simple ceremony,\" aged 54 and unmarried, he took the oath from Chief Justice Oscar Heard, was presented by retiring Governor Emmerson, and asked \"the cooperation of all elements during the crisis.\" He had served five terms on the Probate Court, described as \"the largest in the world directed by one man.\" (R03 covers his sales tax, the 1936 machine primary against Bundesen, his 1938 stroke and death in office in 1940.) The odd detail: A bachelor probate judge — the man who had spent five terms deciding the city's inheritances and guardianships — becoming governor.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. The specific anti-Semitic slogans or pamphlets of 1932 were not found — UNVERIFIED.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Five terms of probate means Horner has read the wills, guardianships and sealed estates of every old Chicago family — including the ones that never seem to die. A governor who knows which heirs are suspiciously identical to their grandfathers is the Kabbalahs' natural patron, and a vampire salon's natural enemy.",
