      "summary": "The Escanaba (WPG-77), a 165-ft, 1,005-ton cutter with two 3-inch guns, was built at Bay City in 1932 and commissioned at Grand Haven on 23 November 1932 — the answer to the Andaste jury's demand for rescue ships (see A). She spent the 1930s icebreaking and on search-and-rescue on Lake Michigan: in the winter of 1934 she took off the crew of the Henry Cort at Muskegon. Her sister Tahoma (WPG-80) was launched at Bay City on 5 September 1934, commissioned 22 October 1934 and stationed at Cleveland for the 9th District until 1941; the class of six (Algonquin, Comanche, Escanaba, Mohawk, Onondaga, Tahoma) was built 1932–34. Beyond the window: Escanaba was sunk on convoy duty on 13 June 1943 at 5:13 a.m. with 103 of 105 men, probably by a drifting mine; Grand Haven raised over $1 million for a replacement and renamed its park Escanaba Park in 1949. The odd detail: A Coast Guard cutter on Lake Michigan armed with two 3-inch guns in 1932, ten months before repeal — the \"rum war\" at its end, and a ship the town later mourned like a son.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The one federal warship on Lake Michigan in the 1930s, with a home port sixty miles from Chicago's water: whoever controls the Escanaba's sailing orders controls what gets searched for on the lake and what does not. Her captain is a character.",
