      "summary": "Mike Fritzel (\"Dean of Chicago nightclub proprietors\") and Joe Jacobson opened the Chez Paree in December 1932 — in the pit of the Depression, before repeal — as a Parisian-style supper club. Its chorus line were the \"Chez Paree Adorables\" (long-legged showgirls in skimpy costumes and exaggerated headdresses); cigarette girls doubled as photographers; there were \"dice girls\" for a game at the tables. Behind the bandstand was the Key Club, a back room that took an actual key to enter — the club's private gambling room (unverified — V_R16). On repeal night, December 5, 1933, the Chez Paree charged a $2-a-quart corkage on outside liquor brought to the tables (see the repeal entry below). It ran until 1960 and booked the biggest acts of the era (Sophie Tucker and Joe E. Lewis among the 1930s regulars (unverified — V2_R16) — Lewis's Chez Paree run is widely stated but UNVERIFIED from a page read this session). The odd detail: The second floor of the same building housed László Moholy-Nagy's School of Design (the \"New Bauhaus\" successor) — the Bauhaus below, the mob's showgirls above.",
