      "summary": "Leonard Percival Howell (born 16 June 1898, Clarendon) had sailed to Panama and New York, joined Garvey's UNIA in Harlem, was jailed, and was deported to Jamaica, arriving in December 1932. From January 1933 he preached that the crowning of Ras Tafari as Emperor Haile Selassie I (November 1930) was the sign for the African diaspora — the Messiah returned. On 13 March 1934 he was tried for sedition and sentenced to two years. In prison he wrote The Promised Key under the pen-name \"G. G. Maragh\" (Gangun Guru), calling the Pope \"Satan the Devil\". In 1940 he founded Pinnacle, a self-sustaining commune in St Catherine. The odd detail: the Crown's charge against a man preaching a living emperor's divinity was \"hatred toward the government\" — the emperor himself was never asked.",
