      "summary": "At 2:45 p.m. on a day of 11 degrees below zero, Officer William D. Lundy, 57 or 58, 27 years on the force, father of three, was warming himself in the delicatessen's kitchen when two robbers shouted \"This is a stickup\" and shot him seven times. Joseph Majczek, 24, and Theodore Marcinkiewicz, 26, were convicted in November 1933 before Judge Charles P. Molthrop on the sole identification of Mrs. Walush after 2 hours 40 minutes of deliberation; each got 99 years. Eleven years later Majczek's mother Tillie placed a classified advertisement in the Chicago Times offering a $5,000 reward for information on the true killers; reporters James P. McGuire and Jack McPhaul took up the case, and Majczek's innocence was proven by finding the original arrest report (unverified — V_R01). It became the 1948 film Call Northside 777. The odd detail: A mother's newspaper ad, eleven years late, undid a police conviction built on one frightened shopkeeper's word. (The popular story that she scrubbed floors for years to raise the reward is UNVERIFIED this session.)",
