      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Room 554",
          "address": "221 N. LaSalle Street",
          "geography": "221 N. LaSalle Street (the LaSalle-Wacker Building), the Loop",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Room%20554%2C%20221%20N.%20LaSalle%20Street%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Frank Nitti, released from Leavenworth on March 25, 1932 after an 18-month tax sentence, had become the working boss of the Capone organisation. On December 19, 1932 a police squad led by Detective Sergeants Harry Lang and Harry Miller, with Officer Chris Callahan, raided his office. Lang fired five times and hit Nitti three times — neck, chest and back near the spine — and claimed Nitti had shot him in the hand. Lang was paid $300 for \"meritorious service\". At the 1933 trial Callahan testified that Nitti had been searched twice and was unarmed: \"There was only one gun fired up there. Lang must have shot himself.\" Nitti said Lang shot him while Callahan held his wrists. Nitti was acquitted of attempted murder in February 1933; Lang and Miller lost their jobs, and courtroom testimony suggested the hit had been ordered by the new mayor, Anton Cermak. The odd detail: The officer shot himself in the hand to make the story work — and got a $300 commendation for it before it came apart.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the event and the testimony; Low on Cermak having ordered it (testimony and later authors; unproven).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A mayor using his own police as hit men against the Outfit is the city's open secret. The hidden layer: Lang was not aiming at the man Nitti was, but at the thing that had moved into him in Leavenworth.",
