      "summary": "A published 19th-century sonnet by the Mexican poet Manuel Carpio (1791–1860) makes La Llorona the ghost of a murdered woman named Rosalía — not a child-killer; the infanticide version (the woman who drowned her children for a lover) is the one that hardened later. The story reached film in 1933 with La Llorona, shot in Mexico — among the first Mexican sound-era horror films (director not confirmed this session: see Leads). The folk song \"La Llorona\" (Isthmus of Tehuantepec) was popularised by Andrés Henestrosa in 1941 — after the period. Versions are recorded across Guatemala, El Salvador, Nicaragua, Costa Rica and the American Southwest. The odd detail: the oldest literary Llorona in this search is a murder victim, not a murderer — the monster was a victim first.",
