      "summary": "Chicago's Ukrainian community, under committee chair Dr. Myroslav Simenovich, built the fair's only ethnic pavilion funded by no government, to a design by the Ukrainian Technical School in Lviv (then in Poland). Its heart was the \"Archipenko House\": 44 works by Alexander Archipenko, valued at $25,000, including the life-size terracotta \"Ma\" series (Ma-Contemplation, Ma-Apparition, Ma-Distributive Force); artists of the Ukrainian diaspora from France, Brazil, Argentina and Czechoslovakia hung beside him; Vasile Avramenko's dancers performed. Maxim Litvinov, the Soviet foreign commissar (the source calls him \"ambassador\", an anachronism: Washington did not recognise the USSR until 16 Nov 1933, and he was ambassador only in 1941–43), objected to the very existence of a separate Ukrainian pavilion — this in the year of the Holodomor. Archipenko later gave \"Ma\" to the National Museum in Lviv. The Ukrainian National Museum of Chicago holds 1,300 artefacts from it. The odd detail: A stateless people's pavilion drew a formal protest from a superpower's foreign commissar.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the pavilion and Archipenko; Medium on the Litvinov objection (single source read). • plot UNVERIFIED",
