      "summary": "Sweden sent an official pavilion and, as its representative, Count Folke Bernadotte (1895–1948), nephew of the king — the future Red Cross rescuer of the \"white buses\" and UN mediator murdered in Jerusalem in 1948 — who \"represented Sweden in 1933 at the Chicago Century of Progress Exposition, and later served as Swedish commissioner general at the New York World's Fair in 1939–40.\" Sweden's presence at the fair was larger than its pavilion: Sven Hedin's temple and Vincent Bendix's money (Bendix was a Swedish-American, knighted in the Order of the Polar Star in 1929) made the Golden Pavilion a Swedish project in all but name. (Contents of the 1933 pavilion were not found in pages read; the famous Orrefors crystal fountain photograph is from the 1939 New York fair, not Chicago.) The odd detail: The man who would negotiate with Himmler for the lives of 15,000 prisoners spent 1933 handing out Swedish glassware on a Chicago beach.",
