      "summary": "The fair's own guide lists, country by country: Italy; the British Train (the Royal Scot — Britain's only \"participation\"); the Irish Free State; Palestine (tourist displays in the Travel and Transport Building); Canada; Mexico; Denmark; Norway; Luxemburg; China; Japan; Czechoslovakia; the Dominican Republic; Sweden; Morocco; Egypt; Spain; Costa Rica. Germany has no heading; the section's closing \"Foreign Scientific Displays\" paragraph names \"the Pasteur Institute of France, the Robert Koch Institute of Berlin, the Deutches Museum of Dresden, and the Wellcome Research Institute of London\". The \"Old Heidelberg Inn\" appears in the guide's dining listings, operated by Eitel, Inc.. The 1934 guide adds \"the winter Black Forest Village from Germany\" to a villages list of Spanish, English, American Colonial, Irish, Swiss, Italian, Tunisian, a Saharan \"Oasis,\" Belgian, Streets of Paris, Midget, Streets of Shanghai, Dutch and Mexican villages, and notes that \"scientific medical institutions of England, France, Germany and Italy have cooperated with the American associations\" in the medical section. The odd detail: Britain's national showing was a locomotive (plus the Wellcome medical exhibit); Germany's was a glass man and a Koch memorial. The printing press came at a Chicago printer's expense.",
