      "summary": "The Cuyahoga (then Cleveland) Metropolitan Housing Authority, founded 1933, was \"the nation's first such organization,\" pushed by Ernest J. Bohn with Msgr. Robert B. Navin's survey of a slum neighbourhood as evidence. With federal money it built Cedar Apartments and Outhwaite Homes among the first public housing in the country (the ECH article ties CMHA's building powers to the 1937 Housing Act). The odd detail: A Catholic monsignor's door-to-door slum survey helped make Cleveland the birthplace of American public housing.",
