      "summary": "Walter M. Singler's Wisconsin Cooperative Milk Pool struck three times in 1933 to force up the price of milk, which had fallen from $4.79 per hundredweight (1927–29) to $3.48 (1930–33): 15–22 Feb (first violence near Appleton — convoy guards throwing horseshoes at strikers), 13–19 May, and 21 Oct–18 Nov. In May the National Guard fought a pitched battle at a Shawano County dairy (30 hurt), cleared pickets from Durham Hill in Waukesha County with fixed bayonets and tear gas (16 May), and a guardsman shot two teenagers in Racine County, killing one (16 May); on 18 May a farmer in his fifties died falling — or pushed — from a milk truck between Saukville and Grafton. In the autumn strike seven creameries were bombed; on 28 Oct a 60-year-old farmer bringing food to the pickets was shot dead at Burke (the killer got 2–4 years for manslaughter). The farmers lost $10 million. The odd detail: Dynamited cheese factories and bayonet charges within thirty miles of Milwaukee, in the year beer came back — and the dead man on the Grafton road, four years before the Bund bought its camp there.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The bombed creameries of November 1933 are the template for the 1935 boys' bombs — and the farmer killed on the Ozaukee road is a death nobody investigated on the same river where the German camp later stood.",
