      "summary": "Albert E. Goodrich's line (1868) had run the Chicago–Milwaukee overnight and excursion boats for sixty years — the Christopher Columbus (above) from 1899, the Alpena lost in Oct 1880, the Iowa crushed by ice two miles off Chicago on 4 Feb 1915 — and went bankrupt in 1933, \"its operations ceased permanently.\" From the summer of 1933 the only way between the two cities by water was the car ferries and the odd excursion. The odd detail: The Depression killed the lake steamers in the summer of the World's Fair, the one year Chicago had the most visitors to carry.",
