      "summary": "In late 1933 and early 1934 workers of the U.S.-owned sugar companies struck for better pay; Pedro Albizu Campos, the Nationalist president, addressed the strikers and represented them in court. The strike is credited with doubling cane wages from 75 cents to $1.50 a day (unverified — V_M05: the EBSCO and Against the Current pages give no wage figures; a search result says the cutters were offered 90 cents, wanted $1.50 and about 50,000 struck — the outcome is not confirmed) (secondary accounts; one Latino-press and one EBSCO summary — I did not fetch the primary). Mother Jones dates the island-wide strike to January 1934 and says four Nationalists died at Río Piedras. The federal government's answer, as some accounts put it, was to send in Governor Blanton Winship (Feb 1934) and Police Chief Col. E. Francis Riggs (1933), who militarised the Insular Police with machine guns, grenades and tear gas, and to send FBI agents to watch the Nationalists. The odd detail: the wage the strikers won ($1.50) is the figure a Chicago breadline man in 1934 would have laughed at — and in Puerto Rico it doubled a man's life. (unverified — V_M05: $1.50 was the strikers' demand; the doubling is unconfirmed)",
