      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (the doubling and the \"sent in as a reaction\" causation are partisan-summary claims). • The strike is credited with doubling cane wages from 75 cents to $1.50 a day (unverified — V_M05: the EBSCO and Against the Current pages give no wage figures; a search result says the cutters were offered 90 cents, wanted $1.50 and about 50,000 struck — the outcome is not confirmed) (secondary accounts; • the wage the strikers won ($1.50) is the figure a Chicago breadline man in 1934 would have laughed at — and in Puerto Rico it doubled a man's life. (unverified — V_M05: $1.50 was the strikers' demand; the doubling is unconfirmed)",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — sugar companies as the \"Imperial Boom\" (§29.3) in miniature — an American company that owns the land, the company store and the police. A cane strike as the cover for something else moving through the fields.",
