      "summary": "Edward Hungerford (New York Central) did much of the writing of the libretto of a one-hour pageant of American transportation from the travois to Kitty Hawk; Helen Tieken (Geraghty), \"a slender young woman\" who also ran the Enchanted Island children's theatre (unverified — V2_R04), staged it — 150 players, 60 horses, eleven locomotives running under their own steam on a quarter-mile of double track, canoes from the Rosenwald museum, a real Wells Fargo money coach from the Council Bluffs–Sacramento run, and a Spanish royal dress worn by the Infanta Eulalia. Five months to build, a month of rehearsal, cost \"over six figures\"; children five cents on Friday afternoons. On 26 May 1934, opening day of the second season, the Burlington's stainless-steel Pioneer Zephyr finished its dawn-to-dusk run — 1,015.4 miles from Denver in 13 h 5 min, averaging nearly 78 mph, peaking at 112.5 — and rolled from Halsted Street straight onto the pageant stage. (Britain's LMS Royal Scot also came in 1933, shipped on the Beaverdale, and toured 11,194 miles of North America, \"inspected by 3,021,601 people.\") The Zephyr was given to the Museum of Science and Industry on 26 May 1960. The odd detail: A train that had left Denver at dawn drove onto a theatre stage in Chicago at dusk, in front of an audience, as the last act.",
