      "summary": "Advertised as \"sixty Lilliputians\" and later counting 115 inhabitants, the village reproduced \"the ancient Bavarian city of Dinkelsbühl, one of the few remaining walled towns in Europe\" at small scale: 45 buildings — municipal building, police and fire departments, church, school, shops of \"midget handicrafts,\" a miniature taxicab, a filling station and a newspaper — with a restaurant serving full-size meals and an outdoor stage of 1,500 free seats. Its mayor was \"Major Doyle,\" 33 inches; its celebrity resident Werner Krueger, billed the smallest man in the world at 24 inches and 18 pounds; three professional troupes (Ritter, Rose, Singer) performed. A 1933 documentary was made of it. The odd detail: The fair's \"German village\" of 1933 was, in fact, this one — a Bavarian town scaled to its inhabitants.",
