      "summary": "A full reproduction of the 1803 fort that stood where Michigan Avenue crosses the river: quadrangle parade ground, 70-foot flagpole with a 15-star flag, double palisades (10 ft and 5 ft), two blockhouses with gun slits, officers' and soldiers' quarters, magazine; inside, photostats of the original plans, the 1832 Sac and Fox treaty in facsimile, pewter, a four-poster, a wooden meat grinder, two 1804 brass cannon and two Paris-made ones lent by West Point, assembled with the DAR, the American Legion, the Chicago Historical Society, the Smithsonian and the Army and Navy. The display told of \"a day in August, 1812\" when the garrison \"marched from the fort and were massacred by the Indians\" (86 of 148 killed). Beside the fort an \"Indian Village\" employed performers from several tribes — one of the few places on the grounds where Native people spoke for themselves. Its fate after 1934 was not found in pages read (UNVERIFIED — see leads). The odd detail: The fair's history lesson was a massacre site rebuilt as a picnic ground. (unverified — V2_R04)",
