      "summary": "Kamil Roškot designed \"a simple building with a large, tall, glass entrance decorated by a mosaic of the Czechoslovak emblem,\" its lower wing curving away under a strip of glass. In the hall: a statue of President Masaryk between panoramas of Prague and the Tatras; about 70 firms showing industry, tourism (the spas), agriculture and transport; textiles, glass, porcelain, embroidery, jewellery; Slovakia represented by the Detva company; and a \"Czechoslovak America\" exhibit of statistics, newspapers, coins and — for some reason — a ship's propeller. Chicago's Czechs (48,814 foreign-born and 73,275 native-born in 1930 — the largest Czech community of any American city: Čapek's 1930 table gives Chicago 122,089, Cleveland 91,683, New York 72,282) lobbied Prague for it, and Mayor Anton Cermak, born in Kladno, went to Czechoslovakia himself to promote the fair; the consulate framed the pavilion as a way to keep \"love and pride in their Czechoslovak roots\" alive in the young. Cermak was shot beside Roosevelt in Miami on 15 Feb 1933 and died 6 March, eleven weeks before the gates opened; some 50,000 mourners attended the services at Chicago Stadium, and he was buried at Bohemian National Cemetery. The community's souvenir book, the World's Fair Memorial of the Czechoslovak Group (1933), carries his portrait and a biographical essay on him by R. A. Ginsburg. The odd detail: The pavilion's patron died before it opened, and the book that accompanied it carries his portrait and life story.",
