      "summary": "Discrimination on the grounds was technically illegal under Illinois civil-rights law, yet Black visitors \"often still found specific vendors and businesses to be unwelcoming,\" and the Midway sold \"degrading entertainments\" — an \"Old Plantation Show\" and the \"African Dip,\" a dunk-tank with a Black man as the target, eventually closed after protests. National Negro Day on 12 Aug 1933 brought a parade, a national African-American athletic meet, and the pageant \"Epic of a Race\" — 1,500 performers and 3,000 singers — to Soldier Field (per Wikipedia's list of Soldier Field events); in 1934 the pageant \"O, Sing a New Song\" followed (UNVERIFIED detail). Against this, Annie E. Oliver — educator, beauty culturist, clubwoman — and the women of the National De Saible Memorial Society spent five years (unverified — V2_R04) raising money to build an 8 × 12-foot replica (one-third scale) of Jean Baptiste Point du Sable's cabin, sold a booklet on the city's Black founder, and \"overcame blatant discrimination by fair officials\" (unverified — V2_R04) to place it. The exclusion led directly to James Washington's American Negro Exposition of 4 July–2 Sept 1940 at the Chicago Coliseum (1513 S. Wabash), funded $75,000 each by Congress and Illinois, opened by Roosevelt by remote button — 250,000 came where two million were hoped for. The odd detail: The fair that put a Bavarian midget town and a Tibetan temple on its grounds gave Chicago's founder an eight-by-twelve cabin, paid for by Black clubwomen over five years (unverified — V2_R04).",
