      "summary": "Piquett was a Chicago bartender who read law at night, chief clerk to the city prosecutor by 1915, and Mayor Thompson's city prosecutor in the early 1920s (indicted for corruption 1923, dismissed). At Crown Point he won Dillinger the right to appear in court unshackled and without armed guards; after the escape an Indiana inquiry implicated him, and the PBS timeline says the escape was made with \"a wooden gun he claims to have carved from a washboard — and cash payments made by his lawyer.\" He brokered the plastic surgery and took the largest share of the fee (unverified — V2_R02) (the page gives only the $4,400 split among Piquett, Loeser and O'Leary). Acquitted in January 1935 of harboring Dillinger (jury out under four hours), he was convicted in May 1935 of harboring Van Meter — $10,000 fine and Leavenworth — pardoned by Truman in 1951 and dead in Chicago that December. Pearl Elliott (1887–1935), madam of Kokomo, was the gang's \"treasurer\" and advance fixer, harbouring its members at her boarding house; on 28 December 1933 the Chicago Police Department put her on its public-enemies list with orders to \"shoot to kill.\" She was at Dillinger's funeral (\"He never threw me down and I wouldn't do it on him\") and died at her mother's house in Clinton County, Indiana, on 10 August 1935. The odd detail: A Chicago police \"shoot to kill\" order on a Kokomo brothel-keeper; a lawyer whose \"cash payments\" opened an escape-proof jail.",
