      "summary": "Studebaker entered receivership on 18 March 1933 with $6 million in overdue loans. President Albert Erskine (who had led it since 1915) resigned and later that year took his own life (R07 has that). His last gamble had been a small cheap car named \"Rockne\" for the Notre Dame coach who died in 1931; when the company went into receivership production of the Rockne \"10\" moved to the South Bend plant, where it was built from April to July 1933. Studebaker emerged from receivership in 1935 with a city-wide celebration. In June 1935 a threatened general strike in South Bend was \"narrowly averted\" (per the Bailey study of the Terre Haute strike). The odd detail: A car named for Notre Dame's dead football coach went into production in the very months the company that made it was in receivership, and its president was dead by his own hand before the year was out.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium on Studebaker (search summaries only); High on the strike-threat line.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Studebaker's Rockne is the \"cursed car\" of the setting — a company town, a dead hero, a dead president. Notre Dame's own IHS neighbours would notice.",
