      "summary": "The pages read this session record four manufactured stories in three years. (1) \"Don't shoot, G-Men!\" — attributed to Machine Gun Kelly at Memphis, 26 Sept 1933; the earliest Bureau account has him standing in a corner with his hands up, and the cry is \"a media myth created months after the arrest.\" (2) \"Public Enemy No. 1\" — Loesch's Chicago phrase, applied by Washington to Dillinger (22 June 1934), then Floyd, Nelson and Karpis in succession to May 1936. (3) Ma Barker as \"the most vicious, dangerous, and resourceful criminal brain of the last decade\" (Hoover), against Karpis's \"old-fashioned homebody … generally law abiding\" and historian Maccabee's finding that Fred Goetz directed the kidnappings. (4) The Director's \"personal\" arrest of Karpis, 1 May 1936, provoked by a Senate committee — no handcuffs, a necktie, and two irreconcilable accounts of where Hoover stood. Add the erasure of Purvis and the 1979 Chester Smith claim about Floyd, and the Bureau's founding decade is as much a press operation as a manhunt. The odd detail: Every one of the Bureau's four signature stories has a documented counter-version from someone who was there.",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (each item is labelled as legend vs. record above).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Bureau's press office as an antagonist: it needs monsters with faces, and it will make them. The real monsters, which have no faces, prefer it that way.",
