      "summary": "Dr. Francis Townsend's September 1933 letter to the Long Beach Press-Telegram became Old Age Revolving Pensions, Ltd. (January 1934, with his brother Walter and realtor Robert E. Clements): $200 a month to every retiree over 60, to be spent within 30 days. By early 1936 there were about 2 million members in some 8,000 clubs; a congressional probe found Clements had personally taken over $70,000, and the McGroarty bill failed 56–206. Huey Long's Share Our Wealth (February 1934) claimed 7.5 million members in 27,000 clubs by 1935, promising a $5,000 \"Household Estate\" and capping fortunes; Long's office answered about 60,000 letters a week with 48 stenographers. After Long was shot in Baton Rouge on September 8, 1935 (d. two days later), Gerald L. K. Smith took over and folded it into the 1936 Union Party. The odd detail: a pension movement was also a business — its co-founder pocketed $70,000 from the dues of the elderly.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on national figures; Midwest club numbers not found.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — a Townsend club in a Chicago church basement is the most ordinary gathering in America — the perfect \"population\" around a cult that has learned to use its mailing list.",
