      "summary": "Morris argued that most men arrested or named held public jobs, and that the Legion placed members through officials such as M. Wesson Dickinson, Michigan's superintendent of private employment bureaus (who also endorsed Lupp's pistol permit), and Ecorse Village's employment director, who fed Legionnaires into Great Lakes Steel. Isaac \"Peg-Leg\" White, a retired one-legged Detroit policeman and former Klan official, told the Detroit News (2 June 1936) that \"a few years ago\" he had carried typed lists of alleged Communists to Hudson, Ford, Budd Wheel \"and all the plants that had strikes or threats of strikes\" — \"the personnel departments… thanked us. It was merely a courtesy proposition.\" Hudson's A.F. of L. local (about 7,000 members) and the Pontiac local (4,000) broke away and collapsed; Pontiac union president Tice Woody told Morris he had joined the Legion thinking it would help labour, only to find Hudson local president Arthur Greer and Richard L. Byrd — Roosevelt's appointee as labour's representative on the Automobile Labor Board — already members. The Detroit Federation of Labor's Frank X. Martel set up a committee of five to root Legionnaires out of the unions. The odd detail: A one-legged ex-cop walking typed blacklists into the personnel offices of Detroit's car plants as \"a courtesy\".",
