      "summary": "From the 1910s tens of thousands of Haitian and Jamaican braceros cut Oriente's cane for the American-owned centrals (the Marines were guarding those mills 1917–22). In the Depression Cuba halted Caribbean immigration (1931), passed labour-nationalisation laws after 1933 (the \"50 per cent law\" — UNVERIFIED detail), and began mass removals: the first ship, the Cuban steamer Julián Alonso, left Santiago on 22 November (1933) with 995 Haitian deportees (unverified — V_M03: the ship, date and 995 were not found on any page read), many singled out for their part in the 1933 sugar strikes; deportees were often not allowed to tell their families or collect belongings. Almost 40,000 Haitians were forcibly deported in the 1930s (Casey, read: \"around 38,000\"), more than 25,000 in 1937 alone (unverified — V_M03). (In October 1937 the Dominican dictator Trujillo's Parsley Massacre killed thousands of Haitians on the Dominican border — another lane's subject.) The odd detail: a sugar economy built on the migrants' backs, emptied of them in cattle-boat loads when sugar fell.",
