      "summary": "The Senate's La Follette Civil Liberties Committee (1936–41) investigated the five largest agencies — Pinkerton's National Detective Agency, William J. Burns International Detective Agency, National Corporation Service, Railway Audit and Inspection Company and Corporations Auxiliary Company — and counted 3,871 labor spies at work in 1933–37. Pinkerton alone had operatives in almost every union: 55 in the United Auto Workers, 17 in the textile workers, 9 in the rubber workers, 5 in the mine workers. General Motors spent $839,764.41 on detective services in 1934–36. Federal Laboratories' president John W. Young testified in March 1937 about machine guns and tear gas sold to employers. Pinkerton dropped labor spying after the 1937 hearings. The odd detail: a union local in 1935 could have a Pinkerton on its executive board — the committee found spies who had risen to union office.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on numbers. The \"spies in union office\" line is a well-known committee finding but was not in the text I read — treat as Medium. Pinkerton's 1930s Chicago office address not found — see Leads. Illinois private-detective licensing law and fees NOT confirmed this session.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The easiest cover for a player investigator in a Chicago plant is the true one: a detective-agency operative. The Hunter Guild, founded by T. R., would run its own agency — and sell the same spies to the steel companies.",
