      "summary": "Set up in the winter of 1933–34 by the new federal Soil Erosion Service under Hugh Hammond Bennett, Coon Valley was the first of twenty national watershed demonstration projects (unverified — V_R42: the pages read call it only \"the first\" demonstration project; a search result gives 10 sites by Jan 1934 and ~24 in all) (a search summary gives the basin as 92,589 acres (unverified — V_R42; a basin that size would make 43,000 acres less than \"more than half\")). Aldo Leopold lobbied the Roosevelt administration to send the CCC and wrote the essay describing the valley as one of a thousand communities that had abused rich soil and helped make the Mississippi flood problem. Farmers had to enrol in five-year cooperative farm plans; most joined, and many who did not adopted the practices anyway. When the CCC left in 1937, over 43,000 acres — described as \"more than half\" (of the farmland, presumably: the basin figure above is larger) — was under management; farm income of participants rose about 25% (1934–42); by 1974 erosion was down 75% and sedimentation 98%. The CCC built terraces and a 1936 dam. The odd detail: The first national soil-conservation project in America was one Wisconsin valley of steep hillsides, run by federal men who signed the farmers to five-year plans and had the CCC dig the terraces.",
