      "summary": "Chicago Tribune reports of January 22 and 29, 1939 described how Lt. James B. Kerr's detectives broke the truck-hijacking gangs of the mid-1930s: there were 84 hijackings in Chicago in 1933 and not one in 1938. The \"Diamini gang\" — five men sent to Leavenworth for \"interfering with interstate shipments\" — confessed to eighteen jobs; a Melrose Park gang specialising in butter-and-egg trucks (about 300 tubs, $2,500 to $5,000 a load) cleared fifteen more; a La Grange case in March 1935 ended in \"100 per cent convictions\". Loads included $10,000 of tyres and radios. The odd detail: After repeal, the gangs went after butter and eggs.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (period reports); no explicit Outfit link in the articles.",
