      "summary": "Born in Füssen, Bavaria (7 July 1906), sent to Cincinnati in 1929, married Philip Hahn in 1930. From 1933 she attached herself to elderly German men as a live-in \"nurse\" and poisoned them for their money to feed a gambling habit: Ernest Koch (73, d. 6 May 1933, left her a house), Albert Parker (72, a $1,000 IOU that vanished), Jacob Wagner (78, d. 3 June 1937, $17,000 to his \"beloved niece\"), George Gsellman (67, d. 6 July 1937, $17,000), and Georg Obendoerfer (67), whom she took by train to Colorado Springs and who died there 1 Aug 1937; the Colorado autopsy found arsenic and unravelled the rest. A jury of eleven women and one man (unverified — V_R06) convicted her on 6 Nov 1937 after four weeks; she went to the Ohio Penitentiary chair on 7 Dec 1938, the first woman Ohio electrocuted. Her confession ran in the Cincinnati Enquirer after her death (unverified — V_R06). The odd detail: A variety of poisons (arsenic, croton oil) and a jury of eleven women to convict a woman (unverified — V_R06) — the press called her \"Angel of Mercy\" before it called her the \"Blonde Borgia\" (unverified — V2_R06).",
