      "summary": "The date is contested. Popular accounts put a Lansky–Batista handshake in 1933 (right after the Sergeants' Revolt) — I found no source for that. Wikipedia's Oriental Park article says Lansky gained control of the track and casino in 1937; other summaries say that by 1936 Lansky had added Cuban gambling to his New Orleans and Miami operations, and that a January 1937 decree moved Cuba's gambling from civil to military control under Batista. Cigar Aficionado (following Robert Lacey's biography Little Man) says that in 1939 Batista recruited the New England track owner Lou Smith to revive Oriental Park and Lansky \"to clean up the casinos\" — throwing out the cardsharps so the high rollers would come back. The Hotel Nacional's great mob years (the Havana Conference, December 1946; Lansky's casino, 1956) are after the window. The odd detail: the dictator's gambling revenue was under army control from 1937 — the casinos were a military asset.",
