      "summary": "In 1933 Lake Shore Drive was extended on landfill — \"mostly made of dirt\" — from Belmont north to Foster. The southern lakefront road was then named Leif Ericson Drive (1927; renamed Lake Shore Drive in 1946). The Outer Drive Bridge opened in 1937 (see Part 6), its approach crossing the Illinois Central yards and turning sharply onto the bridge. The Works Progress Administration, created 6 May 1935, peaked nationally in November 1938 with 3,334,594 workers at about $52.50 a month; among its signature airport jobs was Chicago's Municipal Airport (Midway), 1935–39. The odd detail: The new lakefront drive was built on land that did not exist a few years before — the city's Depression work relief literally made new ground in the lake.",
