      "summary": "Henry Horner, a Chicago probate judge, was elected governor in 1932 — the first Jewish governor of Illinois. To pay for relief he signed Illinois's first permanent sales tax in 1933 at 2%, raised to 3% in 1935. His insistence on cleaning up corruption set him against the Kelly–Nash machine, which ran Health Commissioner Herman Bundesen against him in the 1936 primary; Horner won with a big downstate vote. He had a stroke four days before the November 1938 election, never fully recovered, and died in office on 6 October 1940, aged 61. A lifelong bachelor, he collected Lincoln memorabilia, now held by the state. The odd detail: The machine's candidate against him, Bundesen, was the same health commissioner who closed the schools for polio in 1937 and whose name was on every baby-care pamphlet in the city.",
