      "summary": "The city had 11,000 men on short-shift make-work in 1930–31 and 20,500 in 1932; in the winter of 1933–34 the Civil Works Administration employed 26,000 on 138 city projects — among them \"almost 2,000 men straightening an S-curve on the Milwaukee River\" and building the lagoon and islands at Lincoln Park (98% men; 2.4% Black). The county's outdoor-relief department paid 50 cents an hour. The Milwaukee Handicraft Project employed more than 5,000, mostly women, making educational toys, dolls, wall hangings, rugs, furniture and textiles for schools, hospitals and orphanages; a sewing centre ran shifts of three to four hundred women and turned out nearly a million garments; in Aug 1935 \"several thousand women\" were classed as their family's breadwinner. By spring 1940 the WPA had built 84 public buildings, 884 miles of streets, 31 bridges, 206 miles of sidewalk and 215 miles of street lighting in the county and rebuilt 478 buildings; in 1937 there were 44,200 relief cases and 2,035 families on Aid to Dependent Children. The odd detail: The city rerouted its own river with pick and shovel in the coldest winter of the decade, and paid women to make dolls by the thousand.",
