      "summary": "William Dudley Pelley founded the Silver Legion of America in Asheville, an antisemitic, avowedly \"Christian and Aryan\" fascist militia; Jews and African Americans could not join. One of Pelley's principal organizers worked in the Twin Cities, and a period count credits the Minneapolis chapter with about 800 members out of ~6,000 statewide (1936) (figures confirmed by Wikipedia's Silver Legion of America — about 6,000 statewide, about 800 in Minneapolis — and the UMN page for the statewide figure; neither states the year 1936), with prominent businessmen and state officials among active participants. A Twin Cities Jewish response was organized by Samuel Scheiner and the Jewish community's council; in 1936 a recent Minnesota graduate, Arnold Eric Sevareid, published a six-part exposé (another source says five) in the Minneapolis Journal on the chapter. The group opposed the Farmer-Labor Party in the 1938 governor's race, was investigated by the Dies Committee, and dissolved in 1940 (the UMN page; Wikipedia says 1941, after Pearl Harbor). (Pelley himself was a spiritualist who claimed a death-and-return revelation — Seven Minutes in Eternity, 1928 — general knowledge, UNVERIFIED this session; see R10 for the Silver Shirts' broader reach.) The odd detail: The man who blew the chapter open was a recent university graduate — Eric Sevareid, later the famous CBS correspondent.",
