      "id": "R39-015",
      "title": "The Wabasha Street Caves and the Castle Royal nightclub",
      "date_start": "1933",
      "date_end": "1940",
      "date_label": "1933–1940",
      "category": "The Twin Cities, Duluth, the Iron Range and the Northwoods, 1920–1940, and their communities",
      "tags": [
        "Cities",
        "Duluth",
        "Wabasha Street Caves",
        "Castle Royal"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Josie",
        "William Lehmann",
        "John Dillinger",
        "Ma Barker",
        "Alvin Karpis"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Wabasha Street South",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "on the bluff of the Mississippi, St. Paul's West Side",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Wabasha%20Street%20South%2C%20on%20the%20bluff%20of%20the%20Mississippi%2C%20St.%20Paul%27s%20West%20Side"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The caves are man-made sandstone mines dating to the 1840s, cut into the soft St. Peter sandstone; they were used for storage, mushroom cultivation, music and dancing (Yoerg's Brewery dug about a mile of caves nearby to store beer, per one summary (unverified — V_R39)). On 26 October 1933 Josie and William Lehmann opened the Castle Royal, built into the caves — a dining room with chandeliers, oriental carpets, a bandstand and a gambling room in the back (unverified — V_R39: not on the Wikipedia page cited); it closed in the late 1930s, after which the space went back to mushrooms. Legend says John Dillinger, Ma Barker and Alvin Karpis dined there; Wikipedia states plainly that there is no evidence of these visits. The caves have killed people since — carbon monoxide poisoning, drowning, fires, collapse — and St. Paul has blocked entrances. The odd detail: The Castle Royal opened four months after the Hamm kidnapping (June 1933), in the city the Barker–Karpis gang used as a base — so the legend of the gangsters' table needs no evidence to feel right.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the Lehmann opening and the dates; the gangster visits are LEGEND (flagged by the source). • Yoerg's Brewery dug about a mile of caves nearby to store beer, per one summary (unverified — V_R39) • … 26 October 1933 Josie and William Lehmann opened the Castle Royal, built into the caves — a dining room with chandeliers, oriental carpets, a bandstand and a gambling room in the back (unverified — V_R39: not on the Wikipedia page cited);",
