      "summary": "Four DeLisa brothers — Louis, John, Jimmy and Mike — opened the club around 1933–34 with an 800-seat room, nightly revues of singers, comedians and dancers, a house band of 7–12 pieces, and, in the 1930s–40s heyday, doors open 24 hours a day; gambling ran in the basement. Red Saunders's band held the stand from 1937 to 1945 and again from 1947 to the end. The original building burned in 1941 and was replaced by the larger New Club DeLisa. It closed 16 February 1958 after two of the brothers died (\"I haven't had a vacation since 1952,\" said Saunders). Joe Williams, Count Basie, Fletcher Henderson and Sun Ra all worked there. (From memory, UNVERIFIED: the Monday-morning \"breakfast dance\" for musicians and waiters coming off the night shift.) The odd detail: A club that never closed, with a casino under the floor, run by four Italian brothers at the south end of the Stroll.",
