      "summary": "On 4 Jan 1933, in a Chicago-datelined story, Capt. Thomas J. Callahan [sic] of the Secret Service revealed \"a large international counterfeiting plot\" after the arrest at Newark of Henry Dechow, \"once known here as 'Count Enrique Dechow von Buelow.'\" Posing under the title, Dechow had married a wealthy widow, Mrs. Lottie Brenner, in 1930; after the deception came out she filed divorce suits. About 25,000 counterfeit notes had been passed in Chicago and about $2,000,000 in fake currency brought into the country. The counterfeiting \"was done in Berlin.\" Eight Chicago racketeers had bought $100,000 in counterfeit notes for $30,000. The agents recovered most of it, and the racketeers threatened retaliation. A year later (1 Feb 1934) a syndicated story on the Service's war on the counterfeiting wave said von Buelow \"is said to have had $90,000 in bogus $100 bills when he was taken into custody,\" and that the ring was among \"the biggest international rings ever to operate in this country.\" The odd detail: the Chicago mob were the customers: they paid 30 cents on the dollar for Berlin-made money, the Secret Service took the stock, and the gangsters threatened the government for their losses.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on what the papers printed (OCR read). The trial outcome and sentence are UNVERIFIED; a Getty photo caption (search snippet only) mentions \"$30,000 in spurious US currency\" delivered to Montreal — see Leads.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"Germany as the patron of other people's revolutions\", in banknotes. The Secret Service's file holds that the Berlin plates were cut in a government print shop. The false count who married a Chicago widow was the courier, and the widow's house on the North Side is where the unrecovered notes went.",
