      "summary": "Station owner George W. Trendle and writer Fran Striker (credit disputed between them) launched The Lone Ranger on WXYZ on 31 Jan 1933 after tests on WEBR Buffalo; it went to Mutual and by 1939 had about 20 million regular listeners. George Seaton voiced the Ranger Jan–May 1933, then Earle Graser for about 1,300 episodes until his death in a car crash on 8 Apr 1941, then Brace Beemer; John Todd was Tonto throughout; the theme was Rossini's William Tell with Smetana's Moldau for the quiet passages. Exactly three years later, 31 Jan 1936, the same team (with director James Jewell) launched The Green Hornet: Britt Reid, newspaper publisher, the Lone Ranger's grand-nephew, with his valet Kato and the car Black Beauty; Mutual carried it from 12 Apr 1938. Challenge of the Yukon was the third WXYZ serial. The odd detail: two masked vigilantes born on the same date, three years apart, in a Detroit studio — and a publisher-hero whose paper hunts crime the police cannot, exactly the Chicago press's self-image.",
