      "summary": "Michigan declared a banking holiday on 14 February 1933 after \"troubled Detroit banks were unable to secure loans from the RFC\" (Federal Reserve History); Governor William Comstock's eight-day holiday was the domino that led to Roosevelt's national bank holiday of 6 March. Reuters, from Detroit on 15 February: \"A serious run on the Union Guardian Trust bank, of which Henry Ford and his son Edsel Ford are directors, precipitated the Michigan State bank crisis\"; a clearing-house scheme would let depositors withdraw no more than five per cent of their balances; shopkeepers were discussing \"a system of food coupons\" to replace cash. The governor said Henry Ford's refusal to subordinate his $7,500,000 deposit in the Union Guardian to the claims of small depositors was partly responsible, and that of the three largest depositors — GM, Chrysler and Ford — only the first two had agreed to subordinate; later the same day the governor \"retracted his statement\" blaming Ford. The president of Briggs Manufacturing — Ford's body supplier, whose strike had nearly shut Ford's Detroit works that winter — also sat on the Union Trust board. Forty million dollars was rushed from the Federal Reserve banks of New York and Chicago by midnight. The odd detail: The governor blamed Henry Ford by name in the morning and took it back by evening.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the date, the Fords' directorships and the $7.5 million figure (Reuters); the wire's total of immobilised deposits is illegible in the OCR; Senator James Couzens's role (he is widely said to have opposed an RFC loan to the Guardian group) is UNVERIFIED this session. • Walter O. Briggs, from memory — UNVERIFIED",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Food coupons instead of money — a city where for eight days nobody's cash worked. What else changes hands in Detroit when money stops? A good week to set a Great Lakes module.",
