      "summary": "Anton Cermak — born May 9, 1873 in Kladno, Bohemia, a former Pilsen horse-tender and haulage man — had beaten \"Big Bill\" Thompson on April 6, 1931 with 58% of the vote after Thompson mocked him as a \"Bohunk\" (\"Tony, Tony, where's your pushcart at?\"), to which Cermak replied that he hadn't come over on the Mayflower \"but I came over as soon as I could.\" On February 15, 1933 he was in Miami with President-elect Franklin Roosevelt when Giuseppe Zangara opened fire; Cermak was hit in the lung. Four others were wounded: Margaret Kruis (hand), Russell Caldwell (forehead), Mabel Gill (abdomen) and William Sinnott (scalp). Cermak died March 6, 1933; his physician put the main cause as ulcerative colitis rather than the wound, which is disputed. Zangara was executed in Florida's electric chair on March 20, 1933. A theory that the Outfit hired Zangara to kill Cermak (in revenge for the Nitti shooting) has long circulated; Roosevelt biographer Jean Edward Smith found no proof. The odd detail: Zangara went from shooting to electric chair in 33 days — and the man he hit may have died of his bowels rather than the bullet.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the event, casualties and dates; Low on the Outfit-target theory (no proof).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Three layers: the papers said an anarchist tried to kill Roosevelt; the gangland rumour said the Outfit paid him to kill Cermak; underneath, Zangara was never aiming at either — the bullet was meant for something standing next to them. Handle carefully; the presidency is a load-bearing wall in the setting.",
