      "summary": "State-chartered banks heavy in real estate — \"particularly in the Van Sweringen brothers' new Terminal Tower complex\" — were under pressure by late 1932. On Saturday 25 February 1933 Union Trust and Guardian Trust \"lost half their deposits in one day\"; neither ever reopened. Guardian's president J. Arthur House was convicted and imprisoned \"for illegal dealings\"; Union Trust's J. R. Nutt was cleared. After Roosevelt's bank holiday only four big Cleveland banks reopened — National City, Central National, Society for Savings and Cleveland Trust — Cleveland Trust helped by President Harris Creech's \"dramatic appearance in the lobby,\" standing on the counter to promise every withdrawal would be paid; former mayor Newton D. Baker was said to have got federal help for it. The odd detail: A bank president climbing onto his own counter to face the crowd — and politics, not solvency, reportedly deciding which banks lived.",
