      "places": [
        {
          "name": "From the Lorain Street Savings and Trust bank along Lorain Ave to W. 38th St by St. Mary's Catholic School",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Cleveland's West Side",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=From%20the%20Lorain%20Street%20Savings%20and%20Trust%20bank%20along%20Lorain%20Ave%20to%20W.%2038th%20St%20by%20St.%20Mary%27s%20Catholic%20School%2C%20Cleveland%27s%20West%20Side"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Klink, 40, a woodworker fired eight months earlier because his employer thought him dangerous, had been diagnosed with \"persecutory delusions and dementia praecox\" and believed the government was spying on him. On the morning of 6 Mar 1933 — the day the national bank holiday began — he argued about money at a bank where he had no account, then walked the avenue with a .22 rifle loaded with expanding bullets, killing Louis Kallay (52), Roy Kneale (43), Albert Marquis (67), Patrolman Herman Pahler (45) and Joseph Sapko (22), and wounding six more aged 11 to 45, before Detective Jay Cook shot him dead on Lorain Ave. The papers called him a \"maniac\" (unverified — V2_R06). The odd detail: The date: he shot up a bank street on the first morning America's banks were closed by decree.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. • The papers called him a \"maniac\" (unverified — V2_R06).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"The government was spying on him\" — in a city where the FBI's Section 13 (not the Secret Service, its rival) really does keep files, the delusion of the mad is the plain truth. Two years later Ness arrives; the West Side remembers Klink.",
