      "summary": "Jeanette Whitton Moffett, the admiral's wife, christened the Macon on 11 March 1933 — three weeks before her husband died in the Akron. First flight 21 April 1933 over northern Ohio with 105 aboard. Its home field in California was renamed for the dead admiral. On 12 February 1935 wind shear broke the upper fin's attachment (an unstrengthened ring, frame 17.5) and debris punctured the rear gas cells; the ship came down at sea. 81 of 83 survived: Radioman 1st Class Ernest Edwin Dailey jumped from too high, and Mess Attendant 1st Class Florentino Edquiba drowned going back for belongings. The wreck, with four Sparrowhawks, was found by the Monterey Bay Aquarium Research Institute in February 1991 at about 1,500 ft. The odd detail: The widow christened the ship; the ship's home field took the husband's name; the second giant was lost within two years of the first.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Two sister ships from one German-built shed, both lost to \"weather.\" A GM could make the Airdock's indoor rain and the ships' storms the same thing.",
