      "id": "R32-014",
      "title": "Einstein's fifty-fourth birthday at the Standard Club",
      "date_start": "1933-03-14",
      "date_end": "1933-03-14",
      "date_label": "Tuesday 14 March 1933",
      "category": "Who came to Chicago, 1925–1940: royalty, heads of state, statesmen, scientists, churchmen, celebrities and notorious visitors",
      "tags": [
        "Chicago",
        "royalty",
        "Einstein's",
        "Standard Club",
        "BENDS"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Albert Einstein",
        "Elsa Einstein",
        "Clarence Darrow",
        "the governor"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "The Standard Club of Chicago",
          "address": "320 S. Plymouth Court",
          "geography": "(320 S. Plymouth Court — address general knowledge, UNVERIFIED)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=The%20Standard%20Club%20of%20Chicago%2C%20320%20S.%20Plymouth%20Court"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Einstein \"made a typical entrance to Chicago Tuesday, his wife at his side and violin case under his arm. This was his fifty-fourth birthday anniversary\" (wire story in the Daily Oklahoman, 15 Mar 1933). The couple, arriving that morning from Pasadena, were guests at \"a birthday luncheon given by Chicago pacifist groups\" at the Standard Club, before an audience of professors, university presidents, doctors, the governor and Clarence Darrow. He said: \"We see international difficulties, always the danger of a new war. How to organize international affairs so as not to bring war is the problem\"; he pleaded for the protection of Jewish educational institutions; and he told the room that scientific problems had never been so \"terribly interesting.\" Asked about home: \"The political situation there is as shaky as California was last week\" — so he and Elsa would go to Antwerp rather than back to Germany. (\"Last week\" was the Long Beach earthquake of 10 March 1933, felt in Pasadena while he was there — general knowledge, UNVERIFIED here.) The odd detail: He compared his homeland to the earthquake he had just ridden out — with a violin case under his arm.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on date, venue, hosts-as-a-class, Darrow and the quotations; the sponsoring organisations are not named in pages read (UNVERIFIED). • 320 S. Plymouth Court — address general knowledge, UNVERIFIED • \"Last week\" was the Long Beach earthquake of 10 March 1933, felt in Pasadena while he was there — general knowledge, UNVERIFIED here. • Henry Horner by date — his name is not in the pages read, UNVERIFIED",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Three layers: the period's reading (a pacifist professor nervous about German politics); the folklore (the Reich's court occultists — Hitler among them — making noise); what is actually there (Einstein has seen something the Kaiser's men are building and will not go back to it). He said it in front of Darrow and a governor, which is why both the Secret Service and Section 13 want the guest list.",
