      "summary": "After Cermak's death the City Council elected alderman Frank J. Corr acting mayor on 15 March 1933; Patrick Nash talked him into it with a promise that it would be brief, while the Illinois General Assembly passed a law letting the Council pick a permanent successor. As a temporary appointee Corr lacked authority to sign tax warrants, which throttled salary payments to city employees — during the very weeks the teachers were storming City Hall. He went to Hot Springs, Arkansas to fetch five vacationing aldermen home so Nash's man Kelly could be elected quickly. On 6 April 1933 his bodyguards shot dead a 19-year-old burglar at a hotel near his home. He died 3 June 1934, running unopposed for a circuit-court judgeship. The odd detail: For three weeks in spring 1933 Chicago literally had no one legally able to sign the paper the teachers were being paid in.",
