      "summary": "Czech and Slovak organizations held their own memorial for those who missed the official funeral. At 3 p.m. the curtain opened on a bust of Cermak draped in the flag and in mourning, and a large book, History of Anton J. Cermak, 1873–1933, with dignitaries seated in a circle round it. The audience was asked not to applaud. Speeches: \"Cermak A Martyr\" (Prof. Zmrhal), \"Cermak as a Patriot\" (Dr. Peter Hletko), \"Cermak and the Homeland\" (the Czecho-Slovak Consul General, Dr. Smetanka), \"Cermak as a Character\" (Rev. J. Pelikán), \"as a Humanitarian\" (John Straka), \"a friend of the working people\" (Dr. J. Vojan); music by Hruška's quartet, Dvořák, the Lyra choir. The Sokols and Legionnaires closed with a drama of his life from birth to death, ending with his \"last words\" to Roosevelt — \"I am glad that it was me and not you\" — and \"many shed tears.\" The odd detail: the dying words scholars doubt he ever said were, within two weeks, being acted on stage by Legion veterans as scripture.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Zangara's bullets were meant for Roosevelt — or for Cermak? A memorial play that rewrites a man's last words is a ritual; someone in the Legion wants Cermak remembered as a martyr for a reason.",
