      "summary": "Edward J. Kelly, chief engineer of the Chicago Sanitary District through the 1920s, was hand-picked by his friend Patrick Nash and took office as mayor on 17 April 1933 (Wikipedia; the Corr article gives Corr's last day as 8 April). Nash and his brother Richard ran Nash Brothers, a sewer-building firm that earned more than $14 million from the Sanitary District — the district Kelly had engineered; in 1925 its city contracts gave Nash one of the ten highest incomes in Chicago, and he kept racehorses at Shannon Farm and on a Kentucky farm. Nash had turned down the mayoralty himself. Together they built the \"Kelly–Nash Machine,\" one of the most powerful and most corrupt city organisations in the country — more inclusive than its predecessors, spreading jobs across European ethnic groups and drawing Black Chicagoans into the Democratic fold. Kelly was mayor through the Century of Progress, the teacher cuts, the Memorial Day Massacre, and on to 1947. Nash died 6 October 1943. The odd detail: The two men who ran Depression Chicago made their fortunes underground — in the sewers.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for roles and dates; Kelly's inauguration date (17 April) vs Corr's end date (8 April) leaves a gap not explained in the sources read.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A machine built on sewer contracts owns the maps of what runs under the city. Whoever wants a tunnel, a sealed chamber, or a body to vanish goes to Nash Brothers — \"no one is clean.\"",
