      "summary": "John \"Jake the Barber\" Factor, half-brother of the cosmetics maker Max Factor, had run a 1926 stock swindle in England worth some $8 million, reportedly hitting members of the British royal family, then rigged tables at Monte Carlo and came home. Tried in absentia and sentenced to 24 years in England, he fought extradition to the U.S. Supreme Court, which heard argument in Factor v. Laubenheimer on April 18, 1933 and ruled on December 4, 1933 (290 U.S. 276) — after both \"kidnappings\" — that he should be handed over. On the day of the hearing his son Jerome was kidnapped; eight days later Factor and Outfit men \"found\" and freed him. On June 30, 1933 Factor himself disappeared; he reappeared on July 12 claiming a $50,000 ransom had been paid. The kidnappings kept him in America as a witness. He later ran the Stardust in Las Vegas (from 1955) and received a presidential pardon in 1962. The odd detail: A man ordered extradited by the Supreme Court escaped the order by being kidnapped — twice, counting his son — at exactly the moments the law closed in.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the dates, the Supreme Court case and the reappearance; the staging is the conclusion of later courts and historians rather than a fact proved at the time. • the Dells roadhouse area — UNVERIFIED this session",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Factor's twelve missing days are the lead. Where does a man go for twelve days when he is not really kidnapped? A GM can put him in a house where men with British accents asked him what he had taken from Monte Carlo besides money.",
