      "summary": "The Cullen–Harrison Act made 3.2% beer legal from 7 April 1933 in states that passed enabling laws; \"throngs gathered outside breweries and taverns to celebrate.\" What the Milwaukee brewers had lived on for thirteen years: Pabst made more than 8 million pounds of \"Pabst-ett\" cheese (sold to Kraft when beer returned) and in 1932 merged with Premier Malt Products of Peoria, whose president Harris Perlstein became Pabst's president; Blatz — which sold part of its brewing interests to an English syndicate in 1891, the city's third brewer by the 1900s — made malt soap, sodas and near beer, and resumed brewing in 1933 under federal U-Permit No. WIS-U-712; Schlitz made Eline chocolate; Miller, founded when Frederick Miller of Hohenzollern bought the Plank Road Brewery for $2,300 in 1855, stayed in the family until 1966. Pabst under Perlstein worked with American Can on the first beer cans (1935, date from general knowledge). Milwaukee had had 1,980 saloons in 1918 — one for every 230 residents — and Carrie Nation had said of it: \"If there is any place that is hell on earth, it is Milwaukee.\" The odd detail: The federal permit number — WIS-U-712 — that let Blatz brew again; and that the man who ran Pabst on Beer Night was a Peoria malt-syrup magnate, i.e. the man who had supplied the home brewers.",
