      "summary": "After Cermak died on March 6, 1933, county Democratic chairman Patrick A. Nash — whose firm Nash Brothers \"specialized in sewer building\" and earned more than $14 million from the Sanitary District over 40 years — first promoted Frank J. Corr as acting mayor, then declined the job himself and had the City Council appoint Edward J. Kelly, the Sanitary District's chief engineer in the 1920s, on April 17, 1933. Kelly and Nash built what Wikipedia calls \"one of the most powerful, and most corrupt, big-city political organizations\", handing out jobs across every European ethnic group and beginning to bring Black voters into the Democratic machine. Kelly presided over the 1933–34 Century of Progress, banned Nelson Algren's Never Come Morning from the public library in 1942, and stepped aside for Martin Kennelly on April 15, 1947. Nash died October 6, 1943. The odd detail: The city's new mayor was chosen by the man who built its sewers — and had been chief engineer of the very Sanitary District that paid Nash's firm.",
