      "summary": "After Cermak's death, Cook County Democratic chairman Patrick Nash — owner of a sewer-contracting firm doing \"millions of dollars of business with the city\" — hand-picked his friend Edward J. Kelly, chief engineer of the Sanitary District, and the City Council elected him mayor on 17 Apr 1933. Kelly, who left school before finishing grammar school and went to work at ten, was the first of five Chicago mayors from Bridgeport. The \"Kelly-Nash Machine\" became \"one of the most powerful, and most corrupt, big-city political organizations\"; Kelly served to 1947, ran the fair's second year and broke ground for the subway on 17 Dec 1938. The odd detail: the man who ran Chicago's sewers handed the city to the man who dug them — the Irish machine's return after Cermak's brief multi-ethnic interlude.",
