      "summary": "John \"Jake the Barber\" Factor, a British-born swindler who took about $8 million in a 1926 London stock fraud (victims reportedly including members of the royal family) and rigged tables at Monte Carlo, was fighting extradition when his son Jerome was kidnapped on April 18, 1933 — the day the Supreme Court heard the extradition argument; eight days later Factor and Chicago Outfit men found the boy in a car, gave chase and freed him. On June 30 Factor himself vanished and reappeared July 12, his family saying they had paid $50,000. Roger Touhy, the Outfit's northwest-suburban rival, was convicted of the Factor kidnapping and served decades insisting it was staged to keep Factor in the country and to break the Touhys; the FBI believed the son's kidnapping genuine. Factor later fronted the Stardust in Las Vegas (1955; sold for $7 million c. 1962–63), was pardoned in late 1962 on the eve of deportation and became a citizen in 1963. The odd detail: A con man whose \"kidnapping\" kept him out of a British prison, and whose real friends were the Outfit — society's idea of a kidnapping victim, engineered.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on dates; the staging is disputed on the page itself.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Golden Circle's Chicago money-man is a Factor: a foreigner with a fraud behind him, protected by the Outfit and by a court that would not send him home. His son's real kidnapping is the leverage someone had on him.",
