      "summary": "The Nation reported that on 27 April 1933 Paul Schneider, a 44-year-old Chicago school teacher who had \"not been paid for eight months,\" killed himself, leaving a widow with three children, unpaid grocery bills and a house losing value. The same account gave the toll among Chicago teachers: 400 in \"asylums and sanitariums as a result of the strain\"; and, from a national NEA questionnaire (William G. Carr, NEA Research Division — not Chicago-specific), 2,278 teachers in 263 schools who had lapsed on $7,800,567 of life insurance and 759 who could not meet payments on their homes; more than 500 on charity (unverified — V_R03: not in the article; its nearest figure is 954 unemployed eligible-list teachers supported by the Unemployment Relief Committee); many in debt to loan sharks. The Daily Kos summary (not ISR) names Schneider as a Washburne manual-arts teacher. The odd detail: The date — the day after three thousand of his colleagues fought mounted police in the Title & Trust lobby (Wednesday 26 April).",
