      "summary": "The Jewish Telegraphic Agency (26 May 1933) reports Hans Weidemann, 29, described as the right-hand man of Dr. Joseph Goebbels, arriving with an assistant, Gotthold Schneider. On the dock the German vice-consul in New York, Otto Schlitter, and police officials told him that more than 500 hostile demonstrators were waiting at the pier. Weidemann said he was going to the Chicago fair \"only as a technical art adviser to the German department of religious art,\" and in a fifteen-minute press interrogation \"asserted his complete sympathy with the anti-Jewish measures in Germany.\" Wikipedia has a short page for a Hans Weidemann, born 1904, German artist, photographer and film director, in the Nazi party from 1927, who directed the 1937 propaganda film Festliches Nürnberg and produced the 1942 thriller Attack on Baku; it does not mention this trip, so the identification is likely but UNVERIFIED. A search summary of a scholarly article and a Wiley abstract says 50,000 people rallied two weeks before the fair to demand that Goebbels not be welcomed as Germany's representative — not read; UNVERIFIED. The odd detail: A propaganda minister's right-hand man arrives to advise on religious art, and the dock is already full of protesters.",
