      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Hall of Science courtyard",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Burnham Park",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Hall%20of%20Science%20courtyard%2C%20Burnham%20Park%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Edwin B. Frost, the blind retired director of Yerkes Observatory (Williams Bay, Wis.), proposed opening the fair with light from Arcturus, then reckoned about 40 light-years away, so that light which left the star during the 1893 fair would arrive to open the 1933 one. At 9:15 p.m. photocells at four observatories — Yerkes, Harvard, Allegheny (Pittsburgh) and the University of Illinois (Urbana) — converted the starlight to current, relayed by Western Union to the grounds; operators called \"Harvard, are you ready?\" — \"Yes\" — down the line, threw the switch, and a searchlight atop the Hall of Science and then the coloured floods of the whole fair came on before about 30,000 people. Frost asked the crowd: \"Is this not a fitting demonstration of this century of progress?\" It worked so well that Elgin Observatory repeated the trick nightly all season. Two caveats: modern distance for Arcturus is 36.7 light-years, so the light actually left in 1896; and one fair worker, Ralph Mansfield, later claimed he personally worked a telescope on the Hall of Science balcony and that the four-observatory relay was window-dressing — curators \"acknowledge uncertainty about which account is accurate.\" The odd detail: A blind astronomer opened the fair with light he could not see, and nobody is sure whose telescope actually did it.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the ceremony; Medium on the Mansfield dispute.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The owner already uses a real sky to time a plot. Light that left a star in 1896 — twenty years before the timeline's 1916 break — arriving to switch on a city is a natural \"message from the unbroken world\" device; and the Mansfield claim means the official record is already a cover story.",
