      "summary": "The 270-foot steamer George M. Cox (built 1901 as the Puritan, bought and renamed by promoter George M. Cox) was on her first cruise for her new owner, with special guests aboard, when she struck the reef in heavy fog although the crew heard the foghorn. Keeper John Soldenski of the Rock of Ages Light towed the life-rafts to his lighthouse; 127 passengers and crew spent the night crowded into the tower and on the surrounding rocks. No one was killed. The hull stayed on the rocks until an October storm broke her up; she sank into 10–100 ft beside two older wrecks, the Cumberland (1877) and the Henry Chisholm (1898). The odd detail: A brand-new venture's first night out ends with a hundred and twenty-seven people sleeping on a bare rock in Lake Superior under a lighthouse, in the same week Chicago's fair opened (the fair opened the same day, 27 May 1933).",
