      "summary": "On 27 May 1933 Mary McElroy, 25, daughter of Kansas City's city manager Henry McElroy (Pendergast's man), was taken from her bath in her father's house. The gang — brothers Walter and George McGee, Clarence Click, Clarence Stevens — asked $60,000 and took $30,000, paid on 29 May; she was released after 29 hours near a golf course, having been chained to a basement wall at Click's farmhouse in Shawnee and assaulted by Walter McGee. About $16,000 was recovered; McGee was arrested in Amarillo on 2 June trying to buy a car with ransom money. At trial she had trouble identifying her captors and showed sympathy for them; she broke down and fled to Illinois. Walter McGee was sentenced to death — he would have been the first person executed for kidnapping in the United States — and she wrote to Governor Guy Park, \"Walter McGee's sentence has hung as heavily over me as over him\"; it was commuted to life on 7 May 1935. On 21 January 1940 she shot herself, leaving the note: \"My four kidnappers are probably the four people on earth who don't consider me an utter fool.\" The odd detail: The note. And the first death sentence for kidnapping in America undone by the victim's own letter.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Not Great Lakes — Kansas City — but a case the whole Midwest read. The bond between the taken woman and the men who took her is the kind of thing the owner's \"what is actually there\" layer is for.",
