      "summary": "On Memorial Day, 30 May 1933, eleven convicts walked out of the Kansas penitentiary at Lansing with pistols smuggled in by Frank \"Jelly\" Nash — among them Wilbur Underhill, \"the Tri-State Terror\" of Oklahoma, Kansas and Missouri, and Harvey Bailey, the most successful bank robber of the 1920s (over a million dollars, by his page), who took a bullet in the leg on the way out; the warden was carried off as a human shield. Bailey's wound put him at the Shannon ranch when the Urschel kidnappers arrived and got him life (see §4); his last robbery was Kingfisher, Oklahoma, 9 Sept 1933, and he went from Leavenworth to Alcatraz (1 Sept 1934), out in 1964, dead in Joplin in 1979 at 91. Underhill was surrounded at Shawnee, Oklahoma on 30 December 1933 by a 24-man force of agents, troopers and police, shot several times, ran sixteen blocks, broke into a furniture store and collapsed; he died 6 January 1934. The odd detail: Shot to pieces, Underhill ran sixteen blocks and lay down on a bed in a furniture store.",
