      "summary": "Kansas examiners began questioning bonds in June 1933. More than $1 million in forged municipal bonds had been used as collateral for deposits and loans, including $600,000 found in the state's own vaults. More were found at a Chicago brokerage. Ronald Finney of Emporia was arrested in August 1933, and three Finney-controlled banks (Fidelity State and Savings Bank of Emporia, Eureka State Bank, Farmers State Bank of Neosho Falls) failed that month. Governor Alf Landon called out the National Guard. The State Treasurer, Tom Boyd, resigned and was later convicted of mail fraud; the Attorney General and the State Auditor were impeached and acquitted. In December 1933 Finney pleaded guilty to 31 counts of forgery after an insanity defence failed and was sentenced to 31 to 635 years. His father Warren Finney, who pleaded guilty to embezzlement, killed himself in June 1935 after his appeal failed. The odd detail: 31 to 635 years, and a state whose own treasury vault was full of forged paper. It was government bonds, not banknotes, and the trail ran to Chicago.",
