      "summary": "A federal investigation traced a major dysentery outbreak to two neighboring downtown hotels sharing part of a water system. Investigators identified sewer cross-connections and a decaying wooden sewer plug above a drinking-water tank as likely contamination routes. The 1936 report counted 1,409 reported cases and cautioned that reporting was incomplete. Contemporary TIME coverage named the Congress and Auditorium hotels and described canceled banquets and belated public alarm. This is a documented plumbing and disease episode, with an unusually strong trail of engineering evidence beneath glamorous hotel life.",
