      "id": "R02-045",
      "title": "The Kansas City Massacre",
      "date_start": "1933-06-17",
      "date_end": "1933-06-17",
      "date_label": "17 June 1933",
      "category": "The outlaw era in the Midwest: bank robbers, kidnappers, jailbreaks and manhunts, 1930–1936",
      "tags": [
        "outlaws",
        "kidnapping",
        "Kansas City Massacre",
        "BENDS"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Frank Nash",
        "Verne Miller",
        "Raymond Caffrey",
        "Otto Reed",
        "W. J. Grooms",
        "Frank Hermanson",
        "John Lazia",
        "Pretty Boy Floyd",
        "Adam Richetti"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Union Station",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Kansas City, Missouri (the plaza outside)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Union%20Station%2C%20Kansas%20City%2C%20Missouri"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Frank \"Jelly\" Nash, escaped from Leavenworth in 1930, was picked up in a store in Hot Springs, Arkansas on 16 June 1933 and brought by train to Kansas City. As officers walked him to a car outside Union Station on the morning of the 17th, gunmen opened fire: Bureau agent Raymond J. Caffrey, Kansas City detectives W. J. Grooms and Frank Hermanson, McAlester (Oklahoma) police chief Otto Reed, and Nash himself were killed; two others were wounded. Vernon \"Verne\" Miller led it; the Bureau named Pretty Boy Floyd and Adam Richetti as the other guns, with Kansas City boss John Lazia providing them, and Floyd denied it to his death. Later writers argue much of the killing was friendly fire from a shotgun in an agent's hands. Within a year, in May and June 1934, Congress gave Bureau agents statutory authority to carry guns and make arrests — the massacre is the standard explanation for it. The odd detail: The federal agents who walked Nash out were not, in law, allowed to carry guns. Their dead colleagues became the argument for arming them.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the event; the gunmen's identities are disputed in the record.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Not Great Lakes, but the seed of the whole armed-Bureau decade. The \"who fired\" dispute is a ready-made three-layers case.",
