      "summary": "Approved by Theodore Roosevelt in 1904 and dedicated by Taft in 1911 (first recruit, Joseph Gregg, 3 July 1911), it had 39 brick buildings and ~1,500 sailors before 1917; by the Armistice 776 buildings, 1,200 acres and ~45,000 sailors in training, 125,000 trained in the war, and John Philip Sousa led its band. Training slowed after the war and stopped entirely: on 1 July 1933 the station was closed and put in \"maintenance status\"; local business and the Illinois delegation lobbied it back open on 1 July 1935. By 1936 aviation training had moved to Glenview. The odd detail: For exactly two years the Navy's great training city on Lake Michigan stood empty, in \"maintenance status\" — hundreds of wartime buildings with no one in them.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
