      "summary": "Mayor Edward J. Kelly asked for a big sporting event to go with the Century of Progress; Tribune sports editor Arch Ward answered with a one-off \"Game of the Century\" — fans picked the starters by ballots printed in 55 newspapers, the managers (Connie Mack, John McGraw) picked the rest. 47,595 paid (the Tribune said 49,000); net gate of $45,000 went to a fund for disabled and needy ex-players; the American League won 4–2 in 2 hours 5 minutes, on Babe Ruth's two-run homer in the third. It was meant to happen once \"to boost morale during the Depression\"; it never stopped. The odd detail: The same park hosted, from that same summer, the Negro leagues' East-West All-Star Game (1933–1960), which some years outdrew the white one (cross-ref R14).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Arch Ward is the man who can put 50,000 people in one place on a Thursday afternoon; whoever owns Ward owns the crowd — a Golden Circle asset, or a Section 13 one.",
