      "summary": "On 12 July 1933 the Board, acting on the advice of the business-led Citizens Committee on Public Expenditures, approved a $4,000,000 cut of what it called \"fads and frills\": junior high schools, kindergartens, physical-education teachers and more; Crane Junior College was closed (it reopened in less than a year as Herzl Junior College). A board member, Helen Hefferan, was reportedly shut out of the meeting, and Superintendent Bogan said he had been blindsided. Time (31 July 1933) reported 20,000 parents and teachers at a mass meeting organised by a \"Save Our Schools\" committee claiming 40 civic organisations; Bogan appeared and said, \"I am living in terror of the effect of the economies on the public schools.\" University of Chicago dean Charles Hubbard Judd argued the real 1933 deficit was $1,162,940, not the Board's $10,000,000. The US Commissioner of Education called the cuts \"a return to the dark ages.\" The Board rescinded the worst cuts that October; by September 1935 all dismissed teachers had been reassigned. The odd detail: The business committee that pushed the cuts admitted it had \"taken charge\" of city finances — the schools were being run, in effect, by an unelected committee of the Loop's richest men.",
