      "summary": "Steponas Darius and Stasys Girėnas bought a Bellanca Pacemaker (serial 137, NC-688E) for $3,200 on 18 June 1932 and had it rebuilt at Laird's in Chicago with longer wings and extra tanks. They left Floyd Bennett Field at 6:24 a.m. EDT on 15 July 1933 — the same day Balbo's armada landed on Lake Michigan — and crashed at 0:36 a.m. CET on 17 July near Soldin, Germany, 636 km short of Kaunas. Lithuania's inquiry found the engine running and fuel aboard; rumour said the Germans shot them down as a spy plane; autopsies found no bullet wounds, but not all wreckage was recovered. Chicago Lithuanians raised an Art Deco monument in Marquette Park in 1935; a successor flight, Lituanica II, was \"flying again\" by 1937. The odd detail: Chicago's Lithuanians lost their heroes in a German forest at night, and never stopped believing Germany did it.",
