      "summary": "The Wolf Lake Speedway opened on 16 July 1933, in the middle of the Chicago World's Fair summer: a one-mile sand oval built on man-made land in the lake, completely surrounded by water, with part of the lake drained to build it; its slogan was \"Clean, Cool, Comfortable! No Dust! No Dirt!\" (Search summaries say it had no fences or guardrails.) The interior water held speedboat and hydro-glider racing; a one-fifth-mile dirt-and-oil midget track opened on 9 June 1935 with weekly Amateur Athletic Union boxing on the site in 1935. Drivers who raced there include Emil Andres, Frank Brisko, Duke Nalon, Harold Shaw, Jimmy Snyder and Tony Willman. It closed in 1936; Wikipedia lists it among demolished sports venues. The Whiting–Robertsdale Historical Society calls both tracks \"very short-lived.\" The odd detail: An island race track in the middle of the lake, open only through the Depression's worst years, sold as \"clean and cool\" — and the scorching summer of 1934 (09-22 archive H11) fell inside its run.",
