      "summary": "A bus drivers' strike that began on 27 July 1933 grew into a general strike; Sumner Welles pressed Machado to go and the army turned. On 12 August Machado flew out at 3:32 p.m. from the airport named after him, in a chartered amphibian with four aides, landed overnight on Andros and went on to Nassau (reportedly with a £500,000 deposit at the Bank of England); his wife Elvira escaped by gunboat to Key West. Crowds sacked the $2 million palace (\"I've got Machado's sheets!\"), and the city hunted the Porra: the Porra's chief Col. Antonio Jiménez was cornered on the Prado and shot by a Lieutenant Rogelio Pérez Villalón while the crowd shouted his name, and his corpse was kicked; TIME counted fifty porristas killed; one, Carlos Souto, was handed a pistol and allowed to shoot himself. Houses were marked \"Vacant\" and \"For Rent\". Three US destroyers arrived at 1:35 a.m. Carlos Manuel de Céspedes was sworn in in a garden (unverified — V_M03 as worded: TIME says he gave \"an inaugural garden party\" at 9:30 a.m. on the terrace of his house). Machado died in Miami Beach in 1939. The odd detail: \"mercy\" in August 1933 was a borrowed pistol.",
