      "summary": "JTA reported on 13 Aug 1933 that Dr. Hugo Ferdinand Simon, consul general in Chicago since November 1926, was being recalled to Germany, and that Chicagoans traced it to his \"refusal to take an active part in the controversy in which local Nazis demanded the swastika flag be flown from the German American building at A Century of Progress.\" Simon said \"he did not feel the matter was one which required official action by him.\" He was not Jewish (French Huguenot descent) but had been adjutant to the Jewish foreign minister Walther Rathenau, which was thought to make him suspect in Berlin. He was reported \"distressed\" at growing Nazi influence in Chicago's German community, and was said to be \"considering seriously the offer of a home in Chicago made by a friend here\" rather than going home. The odd detail: The same day the JTA story ran, the German Day crowd of 25,000 at Soldier Field noted that the Consul General \"was not represented\" — the consulate had simply gone silent.",
