      "summary": "The fair sponsored a stratosphere balloon, A Century of Progress — gas bag by Goodyear-Zeppelin of Akron, gondola from Dow Chemical, hydrogen from Union Carbide, instruments from Arthur Compton and Robert Millikan (cosmic rays). Its inflation at Soldier Field on 4 Aug 1933 was watched by 40,000 as \"the world's largest hydrogen balloon\"; on 5 Aug Navy Lt. Cdr. Thomas \"Tex\" Settle lifted off alone before thousands and \"ended in a flop\" — an open gas-release valve dropped the balloon in a nearby railroad yard within minutes. On 20 Nov 1933, from Akron before \"only a few hundred spectators,\" Settle and Marine Maj. Chester L. Fordney rose to a world record 61,237 ft (18,665 m), broadcast by radio, and came down in the marshes at Bridgeton, N.J., where they spent the night in the gondola and Fordney waded five miles for help. In October 1934 Jeannette Piccard flew the same balloon (with Jean Piccard) to become \"the first woman to reach the stratosphere\" (launch site — Ford Airport, Dearborn, Mich. — UNVERIFIED in pages read). The odd detail: The fair's balloon fell into a rail yard in front of the whole city, then set a world record in front of only a few hundred spectators at Akron (though radio carried it).",
