      "summary": "The fair's Century of Progress stratosphere balloon — gondola from Dow, gasbag from Goodyear-Zeppelin, hydrogen from Union Carbide, instruments from Arthur Compton and Robert Millikan — rose from Soldier Field before thousands on 5 Aug 1933 with Navy Lt. Thomas Settle aboard, and an open gas valve dropped it minutes later into a nearby railroad yard. On 20 Nov 1933 Settle and Marine Maj. Chester Fordney flew it from Akron to 61,237 feet (a world record until 1935; a Soviet flight was not recognised), came down in the Bridgeton marshes at the mouth of the Cohansey, spent the night in the gondola, and Fordney waded five miles for help. On 23 Oct 1934 Jeannette Piccard, with her husband Jean as scientist and their turtle Fleur de Lys, lifted the reconditioned balloon from Ford Airport, Dearborn, at 6:51 a.m. before 45,000 people (Henry Ford lent the hangar; the National Geographic had refused to sponsor \"a woman — a mother\"), crossed Lake Erie for eight hours to 57,579 feet — the first woman in the stratosphere — and landed near Cadiz, Ohio, having thrown out every sandbag because a newspaper alliance would pay $1,000 for the record. The odd detail: the first woman in space carried a turtle; the fair's balloon fell into a Chicago rail yard in front of the crowd.",
