      "id": "R04-003",
      "title": "German Day at Soldier Field",
      "date_start": "1933-08-13",
      "date_end": "1933-08-13",
      "date_label": "Sunday 13 August 1933",
      "category": "A Century of Progress International Exposition (Chicago, 1933–34) and the fairs around it",
      "tags": [
        "World's Fair",
        "exposition",
        "German Day",
        "Soldier Field",
        "BENDS"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Bernard De Vry",
        "George Seibel",
        "Hans Marlow",
        "Mayor Edward J. Kelly"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Soldier Field",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "on the fairgrounds' northern edge",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Soldier%20Field%2C%20on%20the%20fairgrounds%27%20northern%20edge%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The German Day Association filled Soldier Field with an estimated 25,000 people. About 1,200 male singers and 800 female singers from German singing societies of Chicago, Milwaukee, Hammond, Appleton, St. Paul, Peoria, Kansas City and Muscatine sang German compositions, with delegations (not choirs) from German-American associations of Omaha, St. Louis, New York, Newark, Detroit, Baltimore and Providence, the Milwaukee choruses under Hans Marlow (former conductor of the Chicago Singverein). Bernard De Vry (association president) welcomed the crowd; George Seibel, First Speaker of the Amerikanischer Turnerbund, gave the main address on German contributions to America (gymnastics, the kindergarten); Mayor Edward Kelly called German-Americans \"the best taxpayers in this great community.\" The Rosegger Steirer Klub danced a \"star dance.\" Resolutions passed opposing the League of Nations and the World Court, demanding repeal of the national-origins immigration clause, calling for an investigation of the Versailles Treaty's war-guilt article, endorsing Roosevelt's depression measures and urging repeal of Prohibition. The consul general was not represented. No swastika flew; per Herman (2020) \"the Nazi flag was never flown at German American days or events during the Century of Progress.\" (unverified — V_R04) (German Days were held at Soldier Field every year 1925–37.) The odd detail: The day's politics came in its resolutions — 25,000 Chicagoans, at a World's Fair, voting to reopen the Versailles war-guilt question and to scrap the immigration quotas.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (translated contemporaneous German-language press). • per Herman (2020) \"the Nazi flag was never flown at German American days or events during the Century of Progress.\" (unverified — V_R04) (German Days were held at Soldier Field every year 1925–37.)",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — 2,000 singers rehearsing in a stadium the night before is cover for anything; a Turner hall's gymnasium is where a werewolf pack would hide in plain sight. \"The best taxpayers in this great community\" is the mayor telling the Secret Service to leave the Germans alone.",
