      "summary": "A junior baseball game between Harbord Playground (mostly Jewish and Italian players) and the St. Peter's Church team ended with St. Peter's supporters unfurling a blanket with a large swastika painted on it. Jewish youths rushed to tear it down, and a six-hour brawl followed involving, per the Toronto Daily Star, \"more than 10,000 citizens\" — \"one of the worst free-for-alls ever seen in the city.\" Earlier that summer a \"Swastika Club\" had formed in the Beaches neighbourhood to resent Jewish families using the public beaches. Police, warned in writing, were criticised for being unprepared; Mayor William Stewart warned against the swastika, and there was no repeat. Heritage Toronto put up a plaque in 2008. The odd detail: A church team's fans, a painted blanket, and a symbol whose meaning was still being fought over.",
