      "summary": "NCOs meeting at Columbia from late August over pay and promotions turned, on the night of 4 September 1933, into a coup led by a 32-year-old stenographer-sergeant, Fulgencio Batista: \"From this moment forward, do not obey anyone's orders but mine.\" Céspedes fell; a five-man Pentarchy lasted five days; then Ramón Grau San Martín led the \"Hundred Days\" government, which Washington never recognised. Roosevelt sent some thirty warships into Cuban waters (another count: 23 vessels including eight destroyers); American — and Japanese — warships stood by but did not intervene. In January 1934 Grau fell under pressure from Batista and Welles's successor, Jefferson Caffery; Batista ruled through presidents (Mendieta and others) until he was elected himself in 1940. The odd detail: the coup that made Batista was plotted on the ground where, thirty-three years earlier, American volunteers let mosquitoes bite them for $100.",
