      "summary": "Gus Greenlee, Pittsburgh numbers banker and owner of the Crawfords, copied the major leagues' new All-Star game (also Chicago, July 1933) for the Negro leagues; the Defender and the Pittsburgh Courier ran the fan ballots. The first game drew 19,568 on 10 September 1933; then about 30,000 (1934), 25,000 (1935), 26,400 (1936), 25,000 (1937), 30,000 (1938) and about 40,000 in 1939 — the biggest annual gathering of Black America anywhere, in Chicago, every summer. Josh Gibson, Buck Leonard and Alex Radcliffe were the perennials. The odd detail: The two founding Negro-league all-star promoters — Greenlee in Pittsburgh, and (through the Giants' owner Robert A. Cole, see leads) Chicago — were numbers bankers; the game was policy money's public face.",
