      "summary": "On Greek Day, 10 Sept 1933, more than 10,000 Greeks filled the space before the Science Building expecting the Greek minister. Bishop George Kallistos and Fr. Ereneos Tsourounakis chanted a prayer; then the Byzantine hymn \"Hypermacho Stratego\" began \"real musical bedlam,\" and Proodos called the day \"a day of humiliation for the Greeks of Chicago\" — saved only by \"the impressive Cretans … with their music.\" It shared the day with the fair's Commodore Perry memorials (unverified — V_R15). In Sept 1935, as Greece moved to restore its king, Greek News asked \"Are There Greek Royalists in America?\" — \"worshipers of King Constantine here and those who hate Venizelos\" — and argued no one who believes in American democracy \"can believe in a Monarchy for Greece.\" The odd detail: a Byzantine hymn to the Virgin — \"To thee, the Champion Leader\" — sung so badly before 10,000 that the Greek press called it a national humiliation.",
