      "summary": "Mary Longnecker, 23, the sister of one of Dillinger's Indiana prison friends, lived in Dayton, and Dillinger visited her at the boarding house through the summer. Pinkerton agents learned of the romance; with the landlady, Lucille Stricker, and Dayton police a trap was set. Stricker saw his car arrive and alerted police; at about 1 a.m. she knocked on Mary's door, Mary opened it, and Detective S. E. Yendes's squad came in. Officer Russell Pfauhl told the man in bed to raise his hands — \"I'll kill you if you don't put them up\" — and Dillinger, reportedly thinking they were rival gangsters rather than police, complied without a fight. The room held four pistols (one a Colt .38 Super semiautomatic), $2,600 in cash, rifle and shotgun shells, written notes on the fastest ways out of various cities, and sacks of carpet tacks meant to puncture pursuers' tyres. Judge Robert C. Patterson transferred him to armed guards for the trip to the Lima jail on the Bluffton bank charge — and the Lima jailbreak of 12 October (R02) followed. The odd detail: The carpet tacks. The most famous fugitive in the country was arrested in a boarding-house bed with sacks of tacks and a notebook of getaway routes — and an escape plan already in his head for the Lima cell.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on date, place, seizure and the quoted line; Medium on Mary Longnecker's exact relationship to Dillinger's cellmate (sources word it two ways).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A Pinkerton-arranged trap in a boarding house — private detectives, a landlady informant, and a fugitive who thought the men at the door were rivals. The Secret Service (which took Capone) and Section 13 both want the \"mob\" cases to look like nothing but mob cases; a Dayton landlady is the perfect quiet witness.",
