      "summary": "Held at Lima on the Bluffton bank charge, Dillinger waited nineteen days. On the evening of 12 October 1933 Pierpont, Makley and Russell Clark walked into the jail office claiming to be Indiana State Police officers come to extradite him; Harry Copeland, Hamilton and Shouse stood guard outside. When Sheriff Jess Sarber asked for their credentials, Pierpont shot him. Sarber was pistol-whipped as he lay wounded and died; his wife Lucy and deputy Wilbur Sharp were locked in a cell (the wife/deputy detail comes from the general record and is UNVERIFIED on the pages read). Dillinger was released from his cell and the gang drove out of Ohio. This is the killing that eventually put Pierpont and Makley on Ohio's death row. The odd detail: The gang's impersonation of lawmen was a habit — the same trick (badges, credentials, \"we've come to move the prisoner\") recurs in the outlaw era, and it worked because a small-county sheriff's office in 1933 had no way to check (unverified — V2_R02) (at Lima it did not: Sarber asked for credentials and was shot).",
