      "summary": "A major hurricane (peak ~160 mph at sea) struck Tampico on 24 September 1933; the Pánuco and Tamesí overflowed. About 184 died (the storm's total; Tampico's flooding drowned hundreds by some accounts) and damage ran to $5 million (1933 USD). The New York Times reported the port \"in ruins.\" (unverified — V_M01: the Wikipedia article cites AP, not the Times; early reports put the dead as high as 5,000; the NHC later listed 184–200; landfall was just after 00:00 UTC on 25 Sept, i.e. the evening of 24 Sept local time) The odd detail: the oil capital drowned by its own two rivers — the Pánuco and the Tamesí — and the Times called the port \"in ruins.\" (unverified — V_M01)",
