      "summary": "At 1:30 p.m. on Tuesday 26 September 1933 ten convicts walked out of the Indiana State Prison armed with pistols that had been smuggled in for them: Harry Pierpont, Charles Makley, Russell Clark, Edward Shouse, Walter Dietrich, John Burns, James Jenkins, Joseph Fox, John Hamilton and James Clark. Dillinger himself — paroled from this same prison in May 1933 after nine years — had been financing the plan with a summer of bank jobs (Massachusetts Avenue State Bank, Indianapolis, 6 Sept 1933, $24,000) and is credited with arranging the guns; the exact mechanism (the long-told story is a box of thread delivered to the prison shirt factory) is not stated on the pages read this session and is marked UNVERIFIED. The irony every account notes: four days before the break, on 22 Sept, Dillinger had been arrested in Dayton, Ohio, at the boarding house of his girlfriend Mary Longnaker, and on 23 Sept was transferred to the Allen County jail at Lima, Ohio. James Clark was recaptured 29 Sept; James Jenkins was shot dead near Beanblossom, Indiana, 30 Sept. The odd detail: The man who paid for the escape was already in a cell when it happened — and the escapees' first job, the St. Marys, Ohio bank on 3 October, was robbed to pay for coming to get him; the Lima raid followed on the 12th.",
