      "summary": "The LZ-127 Graf Zeppelin, 776 ft long, left Friedrichshafen 14 Oct on its \"Century of Progress\" flight via South America (Recife, Rio) and Miami, then Akron, reaching Chicago on 26 Oct. Under new German Air Ministry rules the port tail-fin carried the swastika, the starboard fin the old black-white-red tricolour; Commander Hugo Eckener, no friend of Hitler, \"was careful to fly a clockwise pattern so that Chicagoans would see only the tricolor German flag on the starboard fin, and not the swastika.\" After some two hours over the lake and the fair (unverified — V_R04) it set down at Glenview for only about twenty minutes because of weather, leaving Eckener behind for a 24-hour round of fair events, then flew under Capt. Lehmann over the fairgrounds again and on to Akron, docking there at 3 p.m.; Eckener rejoined it at Akron by United Air Lines plane, and it went back to Germany via Seville. Aboard: Eckener, Capt. Ernst Lehmann, F. W. von Meister, and Dr. Hans Luther (unverified — V_R04) — former Chancellor (1925–26), now Hitler's ambassador in Washington. The Union League Club gave a banquet for 600 (bankers, industrialists, German-Americans) at a table \"draped with the swastika banner and the Stars and Stripes\" where Luther \"set forth the creed of Adolf Hitler\" — the Washington Times of 27 Oct 1933 (p. 10) reported that Luther \"outlined the ideals and aims of the Hitler government,\" \"told of the Nazi position on disarmament,\" \"vigorously denied charges of German imperialism\" and \"criticized the Jewish American boycott of German goods\"; Rufus Dawes received the party and Mayor Kelly received Eckener at City Hall; North German Lloyd gave a luncheon at the Midday Club. A planned Milwaukee flyover was cancelled. The U.S. issued a 50-cent Zeppelin airmail stamp (on sale 7 Oct) and mail was carried at 50c per half-ounce to Akron, Seville and Friedrichshafen. The odd detail: The pilot flew in circles so a city would see only one side of his ship.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on date, route, the fin trick, the banquet; Medium on the exact landing duration (\"twenty minutes\" from the Tribune reprint). • … careful to fly a clockwise pattern so that Chicagoans would see only the tricolor German flag on the starboard fin, and not the swastika.\" After some two hours over the lake and the fair (unverified — V_R04) it set down at Glenview for only about twenty minutes because of weather, leaving Eckener behind for a 24-hour round of fair … • Hans Luther (unverified — V_R04) — former Chancellor (1925–26), now Hitler's ambassador in Washington.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The airship is the perfect sealed room: something (or someone) that crossed the Atlantic in the Graf's hold was on Chicago soil for twenty minutes at Glenview and then gone. Eckener's clockwise circuit becomes a ritual circuit — a widdershins/deosil choice made over a city for reasons his passengers understood.",
