      "summary": "Fr. Lourdes F. Costa, Smeltertown's priest since 1912, looked out of his back window on 28 October 1933 (the cited Historic El Paso page; other accounts say 25 October) and conceived a monument on the peak. A wooden cross went up on 13 February 1934, an iron cross was blessed on 25 March 1934; the Catalan-American sculptor Urbici Soler, commissioned in 1937, carved a 29-foot limestone Christ (stone from Austin quarries), working \"from 4:00 AM to sunset every day\"; begun spring 1939, completed 29 October 1939, dedicated 1940. It became — and remains — a pilgrimage mountain for people seeking a miracle. Its name answers the Christ the King of the Cubilete, dynamited by Calles's troops on 30 January 1928 (M01) — the connection is by title and date; a documented statement of Costa's motive was not found this session. The odd detail: a smelter-town priest put \"Viva Cristo Rey,\" the Cristeros' battle-cry, in limestone on the international line — above the ASARCO works that processed Chihuahua's ore.",
