      "summary": "Oscar M. Hartzell (born Monmouth, Illinois, 6 Jan 1876) sold Midwestern farmers shares in the \"estate of Sir Francis Drake\", said to be worth $22.5 billion, in return for \"donations\", with promises of up to $7,000 per dollar. At Adrian, Minnesota, over 1,500 locals pooled money. He ran it from London. On 4 Feb 1933 an Indiana wire story reported a British deportation order \"against Oscar M. Hartzell, of Monmouth, Illinois, on charges of swindling hundreds\". He was arrested by federal agents in New York on 16 Feb 1933. His investors raised $68,000 for his defence. Trial opened at Sioux City in October 1933. On 15 Nov 1933 Judge George C. Scott found him guilty \"on twelve counts of mail fraud\" and \"immediately sentenced him to a total of ten years' imprisonment and $12,000 fine\", remanding him to U.S. Marshal Don Preussner. (Wikipedia gives a $2,000 fine and a November 1934 conviction; the newspaper of the day says Nov 1933.) The postal inspector who built the case was John Sparks, with British police. Hartzell entered Leavenworth on 16 Jan 1935, was sent to the Springfield, Mo., prison hospital in May 1936, was diagnosed with paranoid schizophrenia, and died in custody on 27 Aug 1943. His line to a lieutenant, May 1929: \"What does newspapers or anybody in States know about what happened 330 years ago.\" The odd detail: the victims paid for the swindler's defence, $68,000 in the depth of the Depression, because they still believed.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the trial and sentence (newspaper of the day). The victim totals conflict wildly (10,000 to 80,000 victims; $80,000 to $2 million) — treat any single number as a claim.",
