      "summary": "By 1920 Nash, Simmons, American Brass, Black Cat Textiles and the Allen tannery accounted for 85 percent of Kenosha's jobs (unverified — V_R38: not on the cited Wikipedia or workers.org pages); Nash had been founded in 1916 when Charles W. Nash (ex-president of GM) bought the Jeffery company. In early November 1933 Nash imposed a piecework pay system; 100 workers staged a sit-down, Nash locked out all 3,000 in the plant, and after a week on the picket line the workers won recognition as an AFL local. A second strike in 1934 (seven weeks (unverified — V_R38: duration not on the page read)) won higher wages and a seniority system, and Nash workers joined the union almost to a man; the local was chartered in 1935 as one of the founding UAW locals (Local 72). Nash's Lafayette became \"the country's first union-made car.\" In May 1934 Local 72 voted a donation to the sit-downers at Auto-Lite in Toledo (unverified — V_R38: the page read says the Nash union became Local 72 only after the UAW's 1935 founding). The city's ethnic mix (Italians, Irish, Poles, Germans, Danes, all with large clubs) is described in city sources. The odd detail: The lock-out of 3,000 men began with 100 workers sitting down over how to be paid.",
