      "summary": "A National Weather Service historical review identifies a November 12, 1933 dust wall approaching Chicago from the west, reducing visibility and temporarily stopping World's Fair rides. Another substantial episode on May 10, 1934 followed light rain and gusty winds and persisted in observations for roughly a day. The study distinguishes instrument records from newspaper descriptions, making this a stronger lead than generic Dust Bowl imagery. Dust-covered clothing, interrupted amusements and an obscured skyline are grounded atmospheric details for the city itself.",
