      "id": "R38-017",
      "title": "Dillinger takes the American Bank & Trust",
      "date_start": "1933-11-20",
      "date_end": "1933-11-20",
      "date_label": "Racine, Monday 20 November 1933 (resolves a lead in R02)",
      "category": "The Illinois ring cities, 1920–1940: Rockford, Joliet, Aurora, Elgin, Waukegan, Kenosha/Racine, Peoria, Springfield, Rock Island–Moline, Danville",
      "tags": [
        "Illinois",
        "cities",
        "Dillinger",
        "American Bank",
        "Trust"
      ],
      "people": [
        "John Dillinger",
        "Harry Pierpont",
        "Charles Makley",
        "John Hamilton",
        "Harold Graham",
        "Grover Weyland",
        "L. C. Rowan",
        "Sgt. Hansen"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "American Bank & Trust Co.",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "the corner of Main and 5th Streets, Racine, Wisconsin (the building is now the Racine Art Museum)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=American%20Bank%20%26%20Trust%20Co.%2C%20the%20corner%20of%20Main%20and%205th%20Streets%2C%20Racine%2C%20Wisconsin"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Harry Pierpont walked in first and pasted a Red Cross poster on the window so the tellers' cages could not be seen from the street; then Makley, Hamilton and John Dillinger came in with machine guns. Teller Harold Graham was too slow for Makley and was shot in the arm, falling on a silent alarm; bank president Grover Weyland and assistant cashier L. C. Rowan were marched to the vault, but Weyland could not open it — bank policy gave him only half of the combination — and Rowan set off a second alarm when he tried to escape to the basement, a gong that told the gang police were coming. The gang emptied the tills and the vault (Wikipedia: $28,000), then found the bank had no rear exit. Summaries of other accounts have Sergeant Hansen of the police hit twice in the street shooting (wounded, not killed), and two hostages — the president and a Mrs. Patzke — taken in the getaway car and released unharmed (search summary of Wikipedia/Crime Museum pages; not confirmed on the two pages I read) (unverified — V_R38: also absent from the Wikipedia Dillinger Gang page; \"Ursula Patzke\" appears on the Racine Heritage page only as a photo credit, not as a hostage). Racine's own museum calls this \"a chapter of the city's history that is seldom remembered.\" The odd detail: The bank president could not open his own vault: the two-halves-of-a-combination rule that made him useless to the robbers is the reason there was a silent alarm in the story at all.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the core (place, date, robbers, Graham, the no-rear-exit detail, $28,000 per Wikipedia); Medium-Low for the hostage and Hansen details (search summaries only). • not confirmed on the two pages I read) (unverified — V_R38: also absent from the Wikipedia Dillinger Gang page; \"Ursula Patzke\" appears on the Racine Heritage page only as a photo credit, not as a hostage). • Hansen — police officer said to have been wounded in the street shooting [first name UNVERIFIED].",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A vault whose combination is split between two men is a two-key ritual object; the gang left with the tills and not the vault — what did the two halves protect? The FBI (bank robbery is the Bureau's field) and Section 13 will both want the bank's own paperwork.",
