      "summary": "Agnes Tufverson married Captain Ivan Poderjay, who was already married to Suzanne Marguerite Ferrand. The couple were booked on the liner Hamburg for a honeymoon on 20 December 1933; Poderjay instead sailed alone on the Olympic on 22 December. Agnes had withdrawn $25,000 in cash from her bank on the day they were to sail. A telegram in her name was sent from London to her sisters on 2 January 1934; nothing after. Austrian police arrested Poderjay in Vienna in June 1934 and found her belongings in his and Ferrand's apartment. New York police inspected his Olympic stateroom on 26 June 1934 and found the porthole \"easily large enough for an average sized woman's body to pass through.\" He was extradited by January 1935, convicted of bigamy (2½ to 5 years) though the judge said murder charges were warranted; released 1 February 1940 and returned to Belgrade with Ferrand. One blog page (read) and a book blurb seen only in search results call her a 43-year-old New York lawyer (the blog: \"New York's Most Intelligent Woman\" the year before); The History Press article calls her \"a 34-year-old woman from Chicago\" — so R28's \"Chicago/Michigan-born\" is UNVERIFIED, and this looks like a New York case with a possible Midwest family tie. The odd detail: The one piece of evidence is a porthole and a telegram that arrived from London after she vanished.",
