      "summary": "A United Press story datelined Chicago, 7 Dec 1933: \"Captain Thomas Callaghan of the secret service\" led police and agents in a raid that seized \"$500,000 in spurious bills\" in $5, $10, $20, $50 and $100 denominations, with \"printing, engraving and marking equipment,\" dies, money plates, ink and specially treated paper. Arrested or named: William E. Davis, 53, described as a former Capone gang member and \"passer\"; Harry Davis, a \"police character\" and passer; an unnamed woman (Davis's wife); and Milton C. Werling, believed to head the ring. The agents had learned of the \"nation-wide\" ring by questioning two men arrested weeks earlier. The raid came two days after repeal (5 Dec 1933). The odd detail: half a million dollars in five denominations, printed under a family cottage by a Capone alumnus: the Outfit's men moving from beer to banknotes in the week the beer became legal.",
