      "summary": "On Sunday 17 December 1933 more than 5,000 Ukrainian Americans marched through the West Side in a \"peaceful demonstration\" against the famine then killing millions in Soviet Ukraine — one of a series of such parades that autumn (New York, 19 November 1933: \"5 Hurt as 500 Reds Fight Parade Here\"; Syracuse, 1,000 marchers), timed around Washington's recognition of the Soviet Union that November. Several hundred communists attacked the Chicago march with bricks, rocks and clubs; the Ukrainian press said the \"Bolshevik bandits\" threw stones and rotten eggs and \"began sprinkling the marchers' eyes with pepper and paprika\"; one account has bricks \"hurled from an elevated station directly above the scene\" and twenty people taken to hospitals. The Associated Press and the New York Times put the injured at over a hundred: the Times ran it on page one on 18 December (\"100 Hurt in Communist-Ukrainian Riot as Reds Attack Paraders in Chicago\"); the Tribune's headline was \"100 HURT IN W. SIDE RED RIOT.\" Svoboda, the Ukrainian daily in Jersey City, had announced the march on 16 December, reported it on the 19th under \"Bolshevik bandits attack the solemn march,\" reprinted the Tribune's account on the 26th and the Times's on the 28th. An ACME Newspictures photograph of the attack survives (Wikimedia Commons, public domain in the U.S.). No deaths are recorded in any source read. The odd detail: the bricks from the \"L\" platform directly over the marchers — an attack from above, on a Sunday procession, in a neighbourhood of onion-domed churches.",
