      "summary": "Cowley, b. Franklin, Idaho, 23 July 1899, was the son of Matthias F. Cowley, an apostle of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints; he studied at Utah State Agricultural College and George Washington law. In early 1934 Hoover sent him to Chicago as head of the \"Flying Squad\" to run the Dillinger hunt, over Purvis; he negotiated with Anna Sage, ran the Biograph, and on 27 November 1934 went out himself after Nelson. Shot in the stomach at Barrington, he lived long enough to identify Nelson and his companions to Purvis and died at 2:00 a.m. on the 28th, about ten hours after the fight. The Wikipedia page notes the two histories: \"The media always played up Purvis … The official FBI accounts play up Cowley,\" under Hoover's hand. The odd detail: The Bureau's chosen hero of the Dillinger year was a Mormon apostle's son who died in a Fox Valley hospital; the public's hero resigned under the Director's displeasure.",
