      "summary": "By 1934 the Lindbergh, Urschel, Hamm, Bremer and Boettcher cases had made kidnapping the rich's daily fear. The Hearst Sunday feature described J. P. Morgan at a flower show \"with detectives at every entrance,\" Mrs. Louis A. Hager Jr., August Busch's daughter, keeping her son \"under careful guard,\" and a Greenwich heiress's son guarded in \"her magnificent home\"; in Los Angeles the May 1934 Gettle case turned on a $60,000 demand and a bodyguard, Kerrigan, who \"was to have received $18,000 for acting as Gettle's guard\" — the guard as accomplice. In Chicago the same fear produced the Secret Six's private detectives (see R01/R10) and, at the end of the decade, the Ross case above. UNVERIFIED this session: the specific Chicago families named in the feature (Wrigley, Swift, Armour, Field) — the page's OCR could not be fetched. The odd detail: The bodyguard who sold the client — in the Gettle case the man hired to prevent the kidnapping was paid by the kidnappers.",
