      "summary": "By the Park Consolidation Act of the Illinois Legislature, in 1934 the new Chicago Park District took over 22 separate park districts — each of which had had its own board, its own taxes, its own police and its own patronage. (The Depression-era insolvency that drove it is widely described but not on the page read.) The odd detail: Until 1934 a Chicagoan could walk through two or three separately governed park systems, with different police, in an afternoon.",
