      "summary": "\"Nancy Brown\" was the pen name of Mrs J. E. Leslie (born Annie Louise Brown), who wrote the Detroit News advice column \"Experience\" from 1919 to January 1942. In 1934 she held her first religious Sunrise Service on Belle Isle and drew about 50,000 people; it became an annual event. In 1936 she began raising money from her readers for a carillon; the 98-foot neo-Gothic Nancy Brown Peace Carillon (Harley, Ellington & Day) was dedicated on 16 June 1940 at the seventh Sunrise Service, again before about 50,000, and cost the city nothing. The last penny of its roughly $60,000 came in on 7 December 1941. The odd detail: An advice columnist, not a church, gathered fifty thousand Detroiters on an island at dawn, year after year.",
