      "summary": "An 8-minute silent surrealist short co-directed by Welles and his college friend William Vance. Welles plays Death; his future first wife Virginia Nicolson plays an old woman who rides a rocking bell pulled by a bell-ringer (Edgerton Paul, in blackface), is courted on a stair, and is found dead inside a grand piano; the film ends on tombstone-shaped cards bearing epitaphs. Vance gave the only print to the Greenwich (Connecticut) Public Library; film historian Joseph McBride announced its rediscovery in Film Quarterly, spring 1970. Welles is quoted (via cinematographer Gary Graver): \"Why did Joe have to discover that film?\" The odd detail: A teenage Welles made himself up as Death and filmed it in the same fortnight he was staging Trilby — and later wished the film had stayed lost.",
