      "summary": "The American-born prospector Harry Oakes (b. Sangerville, Maine) staked claims at Kirkland Lake in 1910–12; the Lake Shore mine ran from 1918 to 1965, produced almost 8.5 million ounces of gold — over a third of the camp's — and was called \"the jewel box of North America.\" Oakes built a copper-roofed \"Chateau\" in Kirkland Lake in 1929 (four fireplaces, a drive-through basement with a six-car garage, a nursery with fairy-tale plaster reliefs); his permanent home was at Niagara Falls. In 1934 he moved to the Bahamas (Wikipedia says 1935 — sources differ) partly in protest at Canadian taxes, was made a baronet by George VI in 1939 (\"Sir Harry Oakes, 1st Baronet\"), and was murdered at his Bahamas home in 1943 in a case that is still unsolved. The odd detail: A gold camp's richest man built a chateau with a six-car garage and a fairy-tale nursery in a frontier mining town — and left the country five years later on a point of tax.",
