      "summary": "John Timothy McNicholas (born Ireland 15 December 1877; died 22 April 1950) was Bishop of Duluth 1918–25 and Archbishop of Cincinnati 1925–50. In 1934 he founded the National Legion of Decency to identify objectionable motion pictures; a search summary says nearly half a million Catholics signed the pledge in 1934, and later pledges ran into the millions. Ratings ran A-I, A-II (acceptable), B (objectionable in part) and C (condemned); a \"C\" could damage a film's returns among about 20 million Catholics. Joseph Breen's Production Code Administration was created alongside it, and the studios often cut films to avoid a \"C.\" The odd detail: The moral censor of Hollywood was, that year, a bishop in a diocese without a film industry.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the founder, year and ratings; Medium on the first-year pledge count.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Church reviews what the people see. A film \"condemned\" for showing a real rite is the IHS's cheapest way to bury the footage — and a Chicago cardinal (Mundelein, R05) is one of the boycott's loudest voices.",
