      "summary": "In 1930 Variety reported \"Chicago is going pansy. And liking it\" (a figure of 35 \"pansy parlors\" in Towertown is widely quoted from the same report; UNVERIFIED here). University of Chicago sociology students wrote it up: Nels Anderson on the hobo world, Myles Vollmer (a divinity student) on hustlers and balls in the 1930s; one field note has a researcher entering the Ballyhoo Café at 11:30 p.m. on September 24, 1933 and counting \"seventy-five queer fellows and 25 queer girls\" (from a search-result excerpt of a Chicago magazine article this session could not open — Medium) (unverified — V_R16). After repeal the machine cleaned house: the Twelve-Thirty Club (women \"in male attire were nightly patrons\"; owner Becky Blumfield) and the Roselle Inn (owner Eleanor Shelly; couples married by a Black minister on the South Side) were ordered closed on the same day, December 27, 1934; early in 1935 police padlocked the K-9 Club and the Ballyhoo; in October 1935 police raiding the Cabin Inn and the De Luxe Café on the South Side told the drag performers \"put on pants or go to jail\"; in late November 1935 the Seven Arts Club was raided at a \"boy house\" at 3549 South Park Way (unverified — V_R16). By 1935 most of the scene was gone. The odd detail: Two lesbian cafés a few blocks apart padlocked on the same December day — an order, not a raid.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High (addresses and closure dates from secondary pages citing the Chicago History Museum and Jim Elledge's The Boys of Fairy Town). • Halsted (unverified — V2_R16); • one field note has a researcher entering the Ballyhoo Café at 11:30 p.m. on September 24, 1933 and counting \"seventy-five queer fellows and 25 queer girls\" (from a search-result excerpt of a Chicago magazine article this session could not open — Medium) (unverified — V_R16). • in late November 1935 the Seven Arts Club was raided at a \"boy house\" at 3549 South Park Way (unverified — V_R16). • a figure of 35 \"pansy parlors\" in Towertown is widely quoted from the same report; UNVERIFIED here",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A scene that vanishes by order in one winter is either the machine's morality or somebody clearing ground; what was in the K-9 Club that needed padlocking?",
