      "summary": "Game 7, October 9, 1934: with St. Louis ahead 9–0, Joe Medwick slid hard into Marv Owen at third; when he went back to left field the Detroit crowd (40,902) buried him in fruit, bottles and seat cushions until Commissioner Landis, in the stands, ordered him out of the game — the only time a commissioner has ejected a player; final 11–0. Detroit won its first championship the next October over the Cubs. The Olympia (James Norris bought the hockey club in 1932 and renamed it the Red Wings) was the city's boxing and wrestling house. The odd detail: The commissioner of baseball, a Chicago judge, ended a riot by throwing out the man it was aimed at.",
