      "summary": "Elijah Poole heard W. D. Fard speak in Detroit in August 1931, went up afterwards and asked if he was the Mahdi; Fard said he was, but \"his time had not yet come.\" Poole became Karriem, then Muhammad. In 1934 the movement founded its first newspaper and the Muhammad University of Islam, a school for its children; the Detroit and Chicago school boards treated the pupils as truants, and the fight led to the jailing of several of the school's board members and of Elijah himself, and \"violent confrontations with police\" — he got probation and the school stayed open. Fard, who had been in contact with Elijah, disappeared in 1934. In 1935, facing death threats from rival claimants — among them his own brother — Elijah moved his family to Chicago, where the Nation's Temple No. 2 became its centre. The odd detail: The movement's founder vanished the same year its school went to war with the city — and the successor fled to Chicago under threat from his own brother.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
