      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Simmons Island",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Kenosha, Wisconsin, on Lake Michigan (National Register of Historic Places, 2003)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Simmons%20Island%2C%20Kenosha%2C%20Wisconsin%2C%20on%20Lake%20Michigan"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "With Snap-on, Simmons Bedding and American Brass slashing shifts, Parks Director Floyd Carlson planned to turn 40 acres of island parkland into a public beach. The New Deal paid 70 percent of labour costs. Carlson and Chris Borggren, a 37-year-old Danish draftsman, had relief crews tear down bankrupt industrial sites and abandoned houses to salvage bricks, slate and timber, and Borggren designed a Tudor Revival bathhouse entirely from reclaimed materials — with roofless changing enclosures on the north and south wings, in keeping with 1930s beliefs about sunlight as a sanitiser. It opened in June 1935, was restored and reopened in 2024. The odd detail: The beach house is built from bricks of the dead factories — the town's unemployed were paid to demolish the jobs they had lost, and the stones came back as a lake-side shrine.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (one long-form blog; NRHP date and reopening year are on that page).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A building made from the bricks of forty condemned buildings is a bone-house: whatever was in those houses went into the walls. Roofless changing rooms aimed at the sun make it a solar-cult stage.",
