      "summary": "Official records place Chicago's 105°F maximum on July 24, 1934 at the University of Chicago, while the airport later known as Midway registered 109°F the previous day. In July 1936 the latter station endured eight consecutive days reaching at least 100°F. The National Weather Service warns that lakefront records make these summers look milder than inland experience. This supports neighborhood differences in heat exposure and a setting in which crowded housing and work routines matter, without inventing an unverified blackout or death toll.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Midway's modern name is retrospective; use Chicago Municipal Airport in period dialogue. This entry does not establish a specific hospital, fatality count or utility interruption.",
      "hook": "Fictional campaign possibility: an invented shaded courtyard remains cold while the surrounding blocks overheat.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "A Comparison of Chicago and Rockford Heat Waves and Hot Summers",
          "url": "https://www.weather.gov/lot/historic_heat_waves",
          "publisher": "National Weather Service, Chicago",
          "kind": "official historical weather synthesis",
          "supports": "1934 temperatures and station distinction; eight consecutive 100°F days at Midway in 1936."
        }
      ],
      "follow_up": "Find July 1934/1936 park, hospital and newspaper reports for sleeping outdoors, work stoppages and named relief facilities.",
