      "summary": "Father Charles Coughlin founded the National Union for Social Justice on November 11, 1934 (Armistice Day), claiming 7.5 million members at its height; its paper Social Justice launched in 1936. In 1936 Coughlin, Townsend and Gerald L. K. Smith combined behind the Union Party, nominating North Dakota Congressman William Lemke with Boston labour lawyer Thomas C. O'Brien; they got 892,378 votes (1.95 percent). Jacob Coxey — leader of 1894's \"Coxey's Army\" — ran for Congress in Ohio on the Union ticket and got 2,384 votes. The NUSJ dissolved in 1937 and was largely replaced by the anti-Semitic Christian Front. The odd detail: the NUSJ was founded on Armistice Day — a holiday that in Umbra marks a German victory.",
