      "summary": "Joseph Médard Carrière (1902–1970), a Franco-Ontarian scholar teaching at Northwestern University (naturalised American 1936; moved to Virginia 1942), made field trips to Old Mines from 1934 to 1936, gathering songs and 73 tales from local conteurs among some 600 French-speaking families — much of it from Joseph Ben Coleman, a French-speaking barite miner — and published Tales from the French Folk-Lore of Missouri (Northwestern University Press, Evanston, 1937). (Whether it contains loup-garou or feu-follet tales I could not confirm this session.) The odd detail: The last living French folk tradition of the Mississippi valley was being written down from Evanston in the setting's exact years — by a professor a streetcar ride from Chicago.",
