      "summary": "In May 1934 President Roosevelt pushed a ceremonial button in the White House and WLW's 500,000-watt transmitter came on — ten times the power of any other American station — under a five-year experimental authority. The station billed itself \"The Nation's Station.\" It fed programs to NBC. Its 831-foot tower's rows of five-foot glass tubes were water-cooled at more than 600 gallons a minute; a 3,600-square-foot cooling pond took the heat; the plant cost about $500,000 (built by RCA, GE and Westinghouse). Reported effects: lights in nearby houses would not switch off until wiring was changed; gutters rattled loose; a neon hotel sign near the transmitter \"never went dark\"; farmers heard WLW in their barbed-wire fences. The signal interfered with CFRB in Toronto (a directional antenna followed) and with WOR in Newark; one account has the station cut back to 50 kilowatts at night in December 1934. In 1939 the FCC declined to renew the 500-kilowatt authority and it went back to 50 kW; an appeal failed. The odd detail: The power was so great that people got the programme without a radio — in fences, gutters and lamps.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the years, the 831-foot tower and the effects (NEH); Medium on the December 1934 night cutback (one search summary).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A broadcast so strong it speaks through fences and light bulbs is a delivery mechanism: a voice that reaches everyone at once, at night. The obvious question — what else a 500,000-watt carrier could carry.",
