      "summary": "In 1920 the state highway commissioner proposed a \"floating tunnel\" across the straits. The state began car-ferry service in 1923; by 1928 it was so popular and expensive that Governor Fred W. Green ordered studies (estimate: $30 million). In 1934 Michigan created the Mackinac Straits Bridge Authority; from 1936 to 1940 it ran route studies and geological borings. Roosevelt and the Army Engineers endorsed it; Congress never appropriated the money. The bridge came only in 1957. The odd detail: For the whole decade the Upper Peninsula was reached by ferry, with a state authority drilling test holes in the straits for a bridge no one would pay for.",
