      "summary": "Ness came to Cleveland in 1934 to run the Treasury's regional liquor-tax office \"with 30 men under his command,\" after alcohol-tax work in southern Ohio, Kentucky and Tennessee. As Safety Director (police and fire) under Mayor Harold Burton he ran a Vollmer-style professionalisation: nine high-ranking policemen indicted for soliciting bribes from underworld figures, six convicted; four union officials convicted of racketeering; the Harvard Club closed (R07); the Cleveland Boystowns founded for delinquent boys; traffic reform that won \"Safest Large City in America\" in 1938. His named targets included \"Big\" Angelo Lonardo, \"Little\" Angelo Scirrca, Moe Dalitz, John and George Angersola and Charles Polizzi. Marriages: Edna Staley (1929, divorced 1938), Evaline McAndrew (1939, divorced 1945). Note only: the 1942 crash — driving home from a party, a hit-and-run with no victims (unverified — V_R25), which he tried to cover up — started his fall. He died in 1957 \"in impoverished obscurity,\" over $8,000 in debt, his family unable to pay for a burial (unverified — V_R25). The odd detail: The Safety Director of the IHS city died too poor to be buried. (unverified — V_R25)",
