      "summary": "Born 22 December 1883 in Lafayette, Alabama, a Tuskegee man who had worked as Booker T. Washington's office assistant, Mitchell came to Chicago as a Republican, switched parties in 1932 (\"ambitious and impatient with the entrenched black Republican leadership\"), and beat De Priest in 1934 with 53% — De Priest himself congratulated \"the first Negro Democratic congressman.\" He served 1935–43 as the only Black member of Congress, introduced anti-lynching bills, and in 1937, forced from a first-class Pullman into a Jim Crow car on entering Arkansas, sued; Mitchell v. United States (1941) held the railroad had violated the Interstate Commerce Act. He retired in 1942 to a 12-acre Virginia farm and died 9 May 1968. The odd detail: The Black Belt turned Democratic two years after the country did — the vote that had been Republican since Lincoln flipped in the 1st District in 1934.",
