      "summary": "Thomas Baker McQuesten (1882–1948) was Ontario's Minister of Public Works and Highways from 1934 to 1943 under Premiers Hepburn and Conant. His work: the Queen Elizabeth Way from Toronto to Niagara and Fort Erie (officially opened 23 August 1940), the Rainbow Bridge, Blue Water Bridge at Sarnia, Highway 20 along the escarpment, Highway 2A past Oshawa (the later 401); as chairman of the Niagara Parks Commission for about a decade he rebuilt Fort George. He joined Hamilton's Board of Park Management in 1922 and championed the Rock Garden and the Burlington Heights landscape that became part of the Royal Botanical Gardens in 1932; he helped bring McMaster University to Hamilton in 1930. The family house, Whitehern — built shortly before 1850, National Historic Site in 1962 — was left to the city on the death of the last of his five unmarried siblings in 1968 and opened as a museum in 1971. The odd detail: The Minister who laid out Ontario's road network had five siblings who never married; the house passed to the city only when the last of them died in 1968.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
