      "summary": "Arch Ward's Golden Gloves (idea 1923, Tribune-sponsored from 1927; the Chicago–New York Intercity from 1928, at the Coliseum among other halls) made amateur boxing the city's winter sport. A Detroit light-heavyweight named Joe Louis won the 1934 Chicago Golden Gloves and the Tournament of Champions, then turned pro at the Bacon Casino on the South Side on July 4, 1934, knocking out Jack Kracken in one round for $59; his backers were Detroit numbers man John Roxborough, Chicago promoter Julian Black and Chicago trainer Jack \"Chappy\" Blackburn. He went 12–0 that year (10 KOs). On June 22, 1937 at Comiskey Park he was put down by a Braddock uppercut in the first round, got up, cut the champion's face to a bloody swelling by the seventh and knocked him out with a left-right in the eighth; the fight came to Chicago because Madison Square Garden's contractual claim on Braddock was ruled unenforceable, and because promoter Mike Jacobs gave Braddock and manager Joe Gould a slice of Louis's future promotion profits for ten years (\"more than $150,000\"). Braddock had a $250,000 Chicago payday against a reported $25,000 to fight Max Schmeling in New York, and worries about Schmeling and the Nazi government weighed — fear that it would shut American fighters out of the title, and commentators' objections to his association with Hitler. The fight launched Louis's 11-year reign. (Attendance/gate not on pages read — UNVERIFIED; commonly given as ~45,000.) Cross-ref R14 for Bronzeville's celebrations. The odd detail: $59 for the first fight; a ten-year cut of the champion for the last man he beat for the title.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on Louis's Chicago start and the round; Medium on the money (the Braddock page's $25,000 figure looks low — treat as UNVERIFIED). • Attendance/gate not on pages read — UNVERIFIED; commonly given as ~45,000.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Bacon Casino, a numbers banker and a $59 purse: the Hunter Guild recruits at the same fights, looking for men who can take a hit.",
