      "summary": "In January 1934 Pure Milk Association members and allied farmers set up roadblocks, confiscated milk from non-striking neighbours and poured it into fields, creeks and roadside ditches; one recorded instance was about $275 of milk dumped in a ditch between Oswego and Naperville. There were \"few repercussions for violence\" and few trials for intimidation; processors agreed to pay a higher price and the state and federal governments moved toward milk price regulation, culminating, per Wikipedia, in the Supreme Court case Nebbia v. New York (Wikipedia draws no direct causal link to the Kendall strike). The odd detail: The Fox Valley's roadside milk war is one of the early-1930s milk strikes that preceded state and federal price regulation and, later, Nebbia v. New York (a New York case) — Wikipedia does not say Kendall County caused it.",
