      "places": [
        {
          "name": "NBC's Chicago studios",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(Merchandise Mart — UNVERIFIED that early episodes originated there), broadcast on WENR, Chicago, at midnight Wednesdays",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=NBC%27s%20Chicago%20studios%20%2C%20broadcast%20on%20WENR%2C%20Chicago%2C%20at%20midnight%20Wednesdays"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "NBC staff writer Wyllis Cooper conceived \"a midnight mystery serial to catch the attention of the listeners at the witching hour\" and put it on WENR, Chicago, from 3 Jan 1934 as a fifteen-minute show; by April 1934 it was thirty minutes, and on 17 Apr 1935 it went coast-to-coast on NBC. Cooper wrote about 120 scripts through mid-1936, when Arch Oboler took over (first script \"Burial Services,\" Jun 1936 — over 50,000 complaint letters, after which NBC banned the show before 11 p.m.). The opening: a deep voice, \"Lights out, everybody!\", then thirteen chimes. Sound men built a real gallows for one episode; Oboler dictated scripts into a Dictaphone in bed, some in thirty minutes. \"Chicken Heart\" (1937, a heart that grows to engulf the world, thump-thump) and \"Catwife\" became the famous ones. By mid-1936 there were about 600 fan clubs. It ran, on and off, to 1947. The odd detail: the first true American horror programme aired only at midnight, from Chicago, and its author begged listeners with weak nerves to switch off before it started.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. • Merchandise Mart — UNVERIFIED that early episodes originated there",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A Wednesday-midnight broadcast that the whole city listens to in the dark is the perfect cover for a working: 600 fan clubs are 600 cells. Section 13 gets a letter from a listener who heard a voice under the chimes.",
