      "id": "R17-002",
      "title": "The end at Bellwood",
      "date_start": "1934-01-06",
      "date_end": "1934-01-06",
      "date_label": "619 South 24th Avenue, Saturday 6 January 1934",
      "category": "Deep dossiers on the owner's own seven Chicago mission seeds",
      "tags": [
        "owner seeds",
        "Bellwood"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Lt. Frank Johnson",
        "Sgt. Joe Healy",
        "Capt. Daniel Gilbert",
        "Walter Dietrich"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "a rented apartment/bungalow at 619 South 24th Avenue",
          "address": "619 South 24th Avenue",
          "geography": "Bellwood, Ill",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=a%20rented%20apartment/bungalow%20at%20619%20South%2024th%20Avenue%2C%20Bellwood%2C%20Ill%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "State's attorney's police had watched the Bellwood flat for ten days on information from the turned member Babe Jones. Two squads under Lt. Frank Johnson and Sgt. Joe Healy, with Capt. Daniel \"Tubbo\" Gilbert of the state's attorney's office over the operation, went in on the afternoon of 6 January. They found Walter Dietrich shaving in the bathroom and took him without a shot; Klutas came out of the bungalow (both wire stories: \"mowed down as he came out of the bungalow\"; \"Finally Klutas and two others came out\" — the came-home-and-rang-the-bell version is the hobby blog's), was ordered to surrender, reached for his pistol (\"Klutas' pistol barked,\" said the wire copy), and Healy's Thompson put .45 slugs through his chest — \"bullets splattered through his body.\" He died on the sidewalk, aged 34. Tear-gas bombs and weapons were found inside; five people were arrested. The Evening Star's wire account of the next morning has it fuller: seven men taken, \"several machine guns,\" tear gas and ammunition seized; the bungalow's owner, Ray Nischwitz, released once police were satisfied he was not involved — Klutas had \"posed to the owner as a Government Secret Service agent\"; and \"fingerprints established the identities of Klutas and Detrick, officers said\" — so whatever had been done to his fingers had not worked. The Washington Times (8 Jan 1934, p. 12) adds that Gale Swolley and Frank Souder went on trial that Monday for the second Hackett kidnapping with the state demanding the death penalty, and that the informer — \"Augustus Jones\" in the wire copy — \"turned State's evidence because Klutas assertedly cheated him of $6,500 ransom money\" and sat under guard in the county jail as the star witness. The New York Times ran it on p. 24 the next morning. He was buried in Lyndon Township Cemetery, Whiteside County, beside his mother. Police \"captured the rest of these gangsters in February 1934.\" The odd detail: the man who ran the operation, Capt. Daniel Gilbert, is the same state's attorney's investigator who ran the O'Hare murder inquiry five years later (§3) — one police career threads two of the owner's seven seeds.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on date/place/officers; Medium on the shot-by-shot (the blog supplies Healy and the machine gun; the wire story names only Johnson's squad)",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the ten days of surveillance are the module: the watchers write down who goes in and out of 619 South 24th, and the list does not stay the same size as the number of people who come out.",
