      "summary": "A strike begun 12 April 1934 was kept alive by the Lucas County Unemployed League (Ted Selander, Sam Pollock), an arm of A. J. Muste's American Workers Party, whose Louis Budenz told them to defy Judge Roy Stuart's injunction limiting pickets to 25 a gate. On 23 May nearly 10,000 people fought sheriff's deputies with stones, bricks and bottles; on 24 May the Ohio National Guard (eight rifle companies, three machine-gun companies) arrived, fought 6,000 people, and fired into the crowd, killing Frank Hubay (27) and Steve Cyigon (20) — neither an Auto-Lite worker. The union won recognition, a 5% raise and a 35-cent minimum on 2–3 June. The odd detail: The strike was won by the unemployed — men with no jobs at the plant picketing for those who had them.",
