      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Electric Auto-Lite Company plant",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "North End of Toledo — the fighting centred on Elm and Champlain Streets, the Elm Street Bridge and \"Chestnut Hill\" (Toledo Public Library's exhibit gives the st",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Electric%20Auto-Lite%20Company%20plant%2C%20North%20End%20of%20Toledo%20%E2%80%94%20the%20fighting%20centred%20on%20Elm%20and%20Champlain%20Streets%2C%20the%20Elm%20Street%20Bridge%20and%20%22Chestnut%20Hill%22"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Federal Labor Union 18384 (AFL) won a five-day recognition strike on 23 Feb, then struck again on 12 April when management would not negotiate. Judge Roy Stuart's injunction capped pickets at 25 a gate; on 5 May the Lucas County Unemployed League leaders Ted Selander and Sam Pollock wrote him that they would \"deliberately and specifically violate\" it — and did (picket lines of 4 on 7 May, 40 on 8 May, 1,000 on 21 May, 4,000 on 22 May, 6,000 on the morning of 23 May). On 23 May deputies arrested American Workers Party organiser Louis Budenz; a crowd put at nearly 10,000 besieged the plant for seven hours with stones, bricks and bottles. About 900 Ohio Guardsmen arrived at 5:30 a.m. on 24 May (eight rifle companies, three machine-gun companies); around 10 p.m. they fired into the crowd and killed Frank Hubay (27, shot four times) and Steve Cyigon (20) — neither an Auto-Lite worker. At least 15 more were shot; the Guard ran out of tear-gas bombs on the evening of 24 May and threw the bricks back. The Central Labor Council counted 85 of 103 unions pledged to a general strike by 31 May; a settlement on 2–3 June gave recognition, a 5 per cent raise and a 35-cent minimum, and 20,000 marched in a torchlight parade before it. The final Guard troops left 5 June. The odd detail: The company had bought about $11,000 of tear- and vomit-gas munitions and hired roughly 1,500 strikebreakers — and when the gas ran short, a plane from Cleveland was hired to fly low over the plant and drop a shipment of tear gas by air (Wikipedia's account). The Guard included high-school-aged boys, one of whom was supposed to be receiving his diploma that evening; and the city had laid off 150 police the year before, so its own cops were half-sympathetic.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on dates and deaths; troop totals differ (900 arriving on 24 May; about 1,300–1,350 in all). The Toledo Public Library exhibit dates the strike start to 25 Feb and settlement to 1 June; Wikipedia gives 23 Feb and 2–3 June — use Wikipedia's.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Ohio's largest peacetime call-up, machine guns on the bridges, teenage soldiers, a plane over the plant: a real event with the mise-en-scène of a haunting. Ask who ordered the second volley at 10 p.m. Neither dead man worked there — why were two strangers in the crowd?",
