      "summary": "The morning after Little Bohemia, Dillinger, Van Meter and Hamilton were fired on by deputies at Hastings, Minnesota; a rifle bullet through the car's rear took Hamilton in the back. They drove him south to a Barker–Karpis safe house in Aurora rented by Volney Davis and his companion Edna \"Rabbits\" Murray. Dr. Joseph Moran, the gangs' surgeon, refused to treat him. Hamilton died there on Thursday 26 April 1934. Dillinger, Davis and others buried him in a gravel pit near Oswego and poured cans of lye over his face and hands to defeat identification. On 28 August 1935 (unverified — V_R02) federal agents, working from Davis's confession, dug up a body there whose dental amalgams matched Hamilton's Indiana prison records; he was reburied in an unmarked grave in Oswego Township Cemetery (unverified — V_R02). Because the confession's times and places did not agree (unverified — V_R02), and because tips kept coming that Hamilton was alive, \"Red Hamilton survived\" became a durable legend. The odd detail: The lye had done its work — no face, no hands — so the only proof of who lay in the Oswego gravel was the fillings in his teeth.",
