      "summary": "A two-day storm beginning 9 May 1934 lifted Great Plains topsoil eastward; it dropped an estimated 12 million pounds (5,400 tonnes) of dust on Chicago and, two days later, reached the East Coast, coating food, skin and furniture inside closed houses. That winter red snow fell on New England. Black Sunday (14 Apr 1935 — the storm that gave the AP's Edward Stanley the phrase \"Dust Bowl\") stayed on the Plains. The archive's H10 covers the Chicago sky and the fair rides; the tonnage and the eastward track are the additions here. The odd detail: twelve million pounds of Kansas fell on Chicago in a day.",
