      "summary": "The fire started at 4:14 p.m. on Saturday 19 May 1934 — Fire Marshal Corrigan thought a tossed match or cigarette, possibly spontaneous combustion. It burned about eight city blocks (some 80 acres); 2,200 firemen with five-sixths of the city's equipment fought it for more than four hours on a 5-11 plus 15 special alarms, and at times had to drop their hoses and run; six pumpers were lost. It destroyed the International Amphitheatre, the Stock Yard Inn, the new Exchange Building (home of 100+ livestock commission firms), the Livestock National Bank, the Drovers National Bank, the Saddle and Sirloin Club, and the Engine 59 firehouse itself. More than 50 were injured, mostly firemen; one yards worker was found under a fallen wall. Perhaps 150 families were made homeless. All telephone lines, electric lines and gas mains in the vicinity were knocked out; an \"L\" car abandoned when the power was cut burned. The Tribune put damage at $8 million. The odd detail: Burning timbers \"as large as coal scuttles\" rained on roofs, and the smoke was said to be seen in South Bend, 95 miles away. The Amphitheatre was rebuilt and reopened 1 December 1934.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. Livestock toll conflicts: the Tribune (day after) said \"several hundred cattle\"; Wikipedia says 8,000 head of cattle and one employee died; Wikipedia's damage figure is ~$6 million. • a meat-barons' club famous for its gallery of livestock portraits — gallery detail UNVERIFIED",
