      "summary": "On 26 May 1934 the streamlined diesel-electric Zephyr made a nonstop Denver-to-Chicago run in just over thirteen hours. The Museum of Science and Industry preserves the train and identifies the run as a milestone in speed and style. Contemporary publicity linked modern engineering to recovery during the Depression; a sleek train could symbolize a future that ordinary passengers had not yet experienced. A surviving photograph taken at Aurora on the approach to Chicago records crowds waiting alongside the railway. It is a dated visual source for spectators, automobiles, street lighting and the train itself.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "The name Pioneer Zephyr became standard later; for 1934 dialogue prefer Zephyr or Burlington Zephyr. The downloaded photograph is Aurora, not downtown Chicago.",
      "hook": "Fictional campaign idea: a sealed parcel arrives on the celebrated train before the same parcel has been dispatched, amid crowds that make a discreet handoff impossible.",
