      "summary": "Annette, Cécile, Émilie, Marie and Yvonne Dionne (first names from general knowledge — UNVERIFIED in my read) were born prematurely on 28 May 1934, delivered by Dr. Allan Roy Dafoe with local midwives — the first known quintuplets to survive infancy, 13 lb 6 oz between them. After a fight over exhibition contracts, the Ontario legislature passed the Dionne Quintuplets' Guardianship Act, 1935, making them wards of the Crown until 18. The province built a playground where visitors watched them through one-way screens: about 3,000 a day, nearly three million between 1936 and 1943 — more than Niagara Falls. They endorsed corn syrup, oatmeal and soap and appeared in films. In 1998 the three survivors received a $4 million settlement from Ontario. The odd detail: Souvenir stands sold small stones from the area as having magical fertility powers.",
