      "summary": "In the 1934 drought the federal Drought Relief Service bought nearly 8.3 million head of livestock from June 1934 to late January 1935, paying $14–20 a head for cattle ($4–8 for calves, $10–15 for yearlings). More than half were judged \"unfit for human consumption\" and killed on the spot; the rest went to the Federal Surplus Relief Corporation (chartered 4 October 1933) for distribution to relief families. A New York Times headline cited on the page links the surplus hides to Chicago's tanners and packers. The previous summer the Agricultural Adjustment Administration had had six million pigs killed and discarded. The odd detail: Chicago families on relief were eating canned beef from cattle the government had bought dying in Kansas, while the government shot the rest in the field.",
