      "id": "R02-021",
      "title": "Tommy Carroll, the wheelman, dies in Waterloo",
      "date_start": "1934-06-07",
      "date_end": "1934-06-07",
      "date_label": "7 June 1934",
      "category": "The outlaw era in the Midwest: bank robbers, kidnappers, jailbreaks and manhunts, 1930–1936",
      "tags": [
        "outlaws",
        "kidnapping",
        "Tommy Carroll",
        "Waterloo"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Tommy Carroll",
        "Jean Delaney Crompton",
        "Emil Steffen",
        "P. E. Walker"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Waterloo",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Iowa — a beer parlour, an alley, St. Francis Hospital",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Waterloo%2C%20Iowa%20%E2%80%94%20a%20beer%20parlour%2C%20an%20alley%2C%20St.%20Francis%20Hospital"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Carroll — b. Red Lodge, Montana 1900, an ex-boxer with a once-broken jaw, a Great War veteran, wheelman at Mason City — had walked out of Little Bohemia through the woods, stolen a car at a crossroads, and spent a month with Dillinger and Van Meter, part of it in a cabin outside East Chicago, Indiana. On 7 June 1934 detectives Emil Steffen and P. E. Walker noticed his bronze Hudson sedan (with mismatched licence plates, by the standard account) near a Waterloo beer parlour. Confronted, he reached for his gun and ran; five shots, four hits — three in the chest, one in the spine. He died about 6 p.m. at St. Francis Hospital. His wife Jean Delaney Crompton, taken at Little Bohemia, got a year and a day for violating parole. The odd detail: A bronze-coloured car in an Iowa town was enough; the getaway man had parked it across the street from the Waterloo police garage.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Minor, but a cabin \"outside East Chicago\" where three of the most wanted men in America sat for weeks in May 1934 is a place with a landlord.",
