      "summary": "A 1934 concession recreating a Black Forest hamlet in winter: \"snow banked on cottages and chalet roofs,\" icicles on the eaves, a frozen mill pond used for ice-skating exhibitions, all kept cold by an air-conditioning plant in a Chicago summer. Craftsmen demonstrated cuckoo-clock making, cane carving, blacksmithing and kirsch distilling; German orchestras and strolling musicians played; attendants wore \"quaint German mountaineer costumes.\" A German restaurant with a grill and lunch counter, orchestral floor shows and dancing \"afternoon and evening\" (Popular Mechanics, June 1934). A Phoenix Art Museum print records it as \"The Black Forest Village, A Century of Progress, Chicago, 1934.\" The odd detail: A refrigerated German winter, with real ice, on the lakefront in August.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High that it existed as described; Low on who built it and what it cost. • plot UNVERIFIED • operator UNVERIFIED",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A building kept below freezing all summer is where you store a body, or keep something dormant. Fae of the Schwarzwald, brought over in the cuckoo clocks. Three layers of truth: the period reads it as kitsch; the folklore is the Black Forest's own (the Wild Hunt, the Nachtkrabb); what is actually there is the reason the ice never melts.",
