      "summary": "In August 1933 Kohler workers took an AFL charter as Federal Labor Union No. 18545. On 22 June 1934 the union presented a 14-point demand for recognition; four days later management announced an indefinite shutdown, and a strike followed. On 27 July 1934 company-hired deputies, said to be answering vandalism, attacked with guns and tear gas: Lee Wakefield and Harry Englemann were killed and \"47 men, women and boys were wounded, gassed, and injured.\" By 29 July, 250 members of the National Guard arrived. Walter Kohler blamed \"outsiders and people with 'communistic affiliations.'\" The dispute's sequel, a 1954 strike, lasted until December 1965 and ended with $3 million in back wages. The odd detail: The killing was done by the owner's own deputies inside a village the family built around the factory.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High (one page, but names and dates specific and consistent with a search summary of the Wisconsin Historical Society's holdings, which I did not read). • named only in a search summary; UNVERIFIED",
