      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Brookfield",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Cook County (Chicago Zoological Park), 14 miles west of the Loop",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Brookfield%2C%20Cook%20County%20%2C%2014%20miles%20west%20of%20the%20Loop%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Built from 1926 on Edith Rockefeller McCormick's 1919 gift plus 98 acres from the Forest Preserve District, the zoo opened July 1, 1934, the first in America to show animals behind moats and ditches instead of bars (the Hagenbeck idea); over a million people came by the end of September 1934. In 1937 it became the first American zoo to exhibit a giant panda, Su Lin, brought out of China by the socialite-explorer Ruth Harkness; Su Lin died young and is now a Field Museum mount. Ziggy, a six-ton Asian elephant, arrived in July 1936 from Singer's Midget Circus (Florenz Ziegfeld had owned him briefly in the 1920s), attacked keeper George \"Slim\" Lewis on April 26, 1941 and was chained in the Pachyderm House for three decades. The odd detail: The first panda the West ever saw lived in a Chicago suburb in 1937 — brought by a widow who finished her dead husband's expedition.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for opening/barless/panda; Medium on Su Lin's dates (Wikipedia's Su Lin page not reachable this session — remembered: arrived US Dec 1936, sold to Brookfield 1937 for about $8,750, died April 1938).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A barless zoo is a zoo that trusts the moat. Something in the collection that is not an animal; the panda as the decade's most photographed creature is a perfect cover for what else came in the crate.",
