      "summary": "The Chicago Zoological Society (1921) began building in 1926 on McCormick's gift; the zoo opened 1 July 1934 with moats and ditches instead of cages — over a million visitors by September 1934, four million by 1936. The explorer Bill Harkness died of cancer in Shanghai early in 1936 mid-expedition; his widow Ruth Harkness, a dress designer, went on and on 9 November 1936 captured a nine-week-old panda cub, Su Lin (\"a little bit of something very cute\"). Brookfield became the first US zoo to show a giant panda; Su Lin died in 1938 and was stuffed and put on display in the Field Museum. Harkness brought a second panda, Mei-Mei, in 1937; she died of drink in a Pittsburgh hotel on 20 July 1947. The odd detail: The most famous animal of the decade is still in Chicago — stuffed, in a museum case, since 1938.",
