      "summary": "The Austrian sick-benefit society Stock im Eisen held its summer fest at Elm Tree Grove: a dance hall filled to capacity, Franz Holz's orchestra and a Hungarian gypsy band in the beer garden, a 7 p.m. children's procession in which every child got three gifts, one a small American flag. The president of the German Day organization, Gustave A. Brand, attended. The society took its name from Vienna's Stock im Eisen, a medieval spruce trunk (grew c. 1400, felled c. 1440) into which hundreds of nails were hammered for luck — journeyman locksmiths did it from 1715 — and about which legend says the Devil himself bound the trunk in iron and an apprentice made an unopenable lock. The odd detail: an Austrian mutual-aid club in Chicago named after a nail-studded devil's tree.",
