      "summary": "On 20 July police, with about a dozen squad cars carrying shotgun-armed officers, cordoned the area while trucks loaded under escort. A pickets' truck was cut off and police \"opened fire with shotguns on the pickets' truck.\" Sixty-seven strikers were wounded, most shot in the back; two died — John Belor and Henry Ness. The governor's investigating board concluded that \"police took direct aim at the pickets and fired to kill\" and that no picket carried a weapon. Ness's funeral procession on 24 July drew a crowd counted at 100,000 (Wikipedia) or about 40,000 mourners (MNopedia) — sources disagree. On 25 July federal mediators issued the Haas-Dunnigan proposal; Olson declared martial law on 26 July (banning picketing, issuing truck permits — about 4,000 permits), and the Guard raided both the union and the Citizens Alliance headquarters, arresting union leaders (held at the State Fairgrounds). About 4,000 Guardsmen were mobilized on 1 August (unverified — V_R39). Employers accepted the settlement, with the Alliance's A. W. Strong (unverified — V_R39: name not found on any page read) agreeing on 22 August (R07 has 21 Aug; the sources read say 22 Aug). The odd detail: A Farmer-Labor governor who called himself \"a radical\" was the man who put the Guard on the pickets — and also on the employers' club.",
