      "summary": "At about 10:40 p.m. Dillinger came out of Manhattan Melodrama with Polly Hamilton (her orange skirt looked red under the marquee — hence Ana Cumpănaș's \"Lady in Red\") and turned toward the alley. Three agents fired — Clarence Hurt twice, Charles Winstead three times, Herman Hollis once (the Tribune's later retelling gives the killing shot to East Chicago police Sgt. Martin Zarkovich's .38; the Washington Times of 24 July named Inspector Samuel Cowley as the slayer — the Bureau's own story moved in the first days). One round entered the back of the neck, cut the cord and came out under the right eye. Two women bystanders, Theresa Paulas and Etta Natalsky, were hit. Before the body was cold the crowd was on its knees: \"handkerchiefs were whipped out and used to mop up the blood,\" newspapers were dipped in it, scraps of blood-stained paper sold for a quarter, someone offered $100 for a stained brick and $1,000 for the shirt (unverified — V_R17). The body went to Alexian Brothers, then to the county morgue, where the Tribune found \"lively\" scenes — thousands \"pushing and shoving\" down the basement stairs to file past the slab and, later, a glass window; a 19-year-old girl peering through a ground-level pane; a woman \"disappointed\" that he \"looks just like any other dead man\" but planning to come back. Some 15,000 viewed him in a day and a half. The FBI's file says \"every politician and his friend in Chicago crowded into the morgue\" and that the deputy coroner took bribes for viewings and mask-making (unverified — V_R17). His sister Audrey Hancock identified him by a thigh scar from childhood barbed wire. When the body moved to McCready's on Sheridan Road, 5,000 people filled the 4500 block; six men made a \"flying wedge\" to get the casket to the hearse. The odd detail: the blood on the pavement was gone within the hour — into handkerchiefs, newspapers and a quarter-a-scrap souvenir trade.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (the count of who fired what is contested between sources, as noted) • Before the body was cold the crowd was on its knees: \"handkerchiefs were whipped out and used to mop up the blood,\" newspapers were dipped in it, scraps of blood-stained paper sold for a quarter, someone offered $100 for a stained brick and $1,000 for the shirt (unverified — V_R17). • The FBI's file says \"every politician and his friend in Chicago crowded into the morgue\" and that the deputy coroner took bribes for viewings and mask-making (unverified — V_R17).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — in a world with vampires, a crowd mopping up a dead man's blood on a summer night is not colour, it is a plot: who collected the handkerchiefs, and what were they for?",
