      "summary": "The body left Chicago in a wicker temporary casket; 3,500 people viewed it on a mortician's slab on the way, before L. W. Howe of Indianapolis embalmed it; nearly all of Mooresville's 2,300 came to look (unverified — V_R17). The funeral was at the home of his sister Audrey and her husband Emmett Hancock in Maywood: about 2,500 filed through on Tuesday 24 July, and the Rev. Charles M. Fillmore read a short service on Wednesday the 25th. Some 5,000 gathered at Crown Hill (Benjamin Harrison's cemetery). An oilman, H. G. Cross, \"confirmed he telegraphed Dillinger an offer of $10,000 for his son's body\" — Cross was the bidder, not a go-between; the father refused. Hearing of plans to rob the grave, the family had 2½ tons of concrete (some accounts add scrap iron) poured over the $165 pine coffin, and a police detachment under Sgt. Otis Baker was set to guard the grave after the service. The headstone has been replaced several times as visitors chip it; people still leave coins and take earth. In 2019 a nephew obtained an exhumation permit to settle the identity question; the History Channel withdrew, Crown Hill objected, and the reburial date came and went. The odd detail: the $10,000 offer to tour the corpse — and the concrete poured, in the family's words, to stop exactly that.",
