      "summary": "The first National Air Meet for Women was held at Dayton in August 1934; during a 50-mile race Frances Harrell Marsalis, an endurance-record pilot, lost control of her aircraft and was killed on 5 August. Helen Richey won the meet's premier race — and that December became the first woman hired as a pilot by a U.S. airline (Central Airlines, on the Washington–Detroit run; from general knowledge, to be verified — see Leads). The odd detail: The first all-women's air meet in the country produced both a death and the first woman airline pilot within four months.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (snippets only).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Dayton is Wright country and Army air country; a woman pilot's unexplained loss of control over Ohio in 1934 belongs to the same file as the 1929 acid-eaten wires.",
