      "places": [
        {
          "name": "University Avenue and Marion Street",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "St. Paul, Minnesota",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=University%20Avenue%20and%20Marion%20Street%2C%20St.%20Paul%2C%20Minnesota"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Van Meter — Fort Wayne-born (1905), Dillinger's friend from the Indiana Reformatory, the \"prison clown\" transferred to Michigan City in 1925 for his jokes, wounder of Fostoria's police chief Frank Culp in the 3 May 1934 robbery there, and Dillinger's fellow patient at Probasco's (he had tried to shoot Dr Loeser on the spot when he saw the results) — was cornered at University and Marion by four St. Paul officers: Chief Frank Cullen, former chief Tom Brown and two detectives. They said he ran into an alley and fired; the coroner counted 26 buckshot slugs and one machine-gun bullet, and the family said he had been used for target practice. The officers reported $1,323 in cash, $2,000 in jewels and a $500 Liberty bond on him; his friends said he was carrying at least $10,000. In 1939 the Bureau concluded that St. Paul fixer Harry Sawyer had set him up to get at the money and split it with the four officers who did the shooting. He is buried at Lindenwood Cemetery, Fort Wayne (unverified — V_R02). The odd detail: The city that had guaranteed gangsters' safety for thirty years ended the arrangement by shooting one of them 27 times (the coroner's count per Wikipedia — 26 buckshot slugs and one machine-gun bullet; another account of the autopsy gives 17 gunshot wounds) for his bankroll — with the former chief of police holding a gun.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. • He is buried at Lindenwood Cemetery, Fort Wayne (unverified — V_R02).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The money that vanished from the alley is the hook; so is a former chief of police who shoots for a fixer. \"No one is clean\" is St. Paul's motto in 1934.",
