      "summary": "Arch Ward's second invention: the NFL champions against a team of graduated college stars, for Tribune Charities. The first game, August 31, 1934, drew 79,432 for a 0–0 tie with the Bears; 1935 Bears 5–0 (77,450; a Michigan lineman named Gerald Ford played for the collegians); 1936 7–7 with Detroit (76,000); 1937 the All-Stars beat Green Bay 6–0 (84,560; Sammy Baugh threw a 47-yard touchdown); 1938 All-Stars 28–16 over Washington (74,250). It ran until 1976 (42 games in all) and raised over $4 million. The odd detail: A future president in a helmet in front of 77,000 people, unnoticed.",
