      "id": "R06-002",
      "title": "The \"Lady of the Lake\"",
      "date_start": "1934-09-05",
      "date_end": "1934-09-05",
      "date_label": "5 Sept 1934",
      "category": "Murder, madness and disappearances: Chicago and the Great Lakes, 1925–1940",
      "tags": [
        "murder",
        "disappearance",
        "Lady",
        "Lake"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Jane Doe",
        "Coroner A. J. Pearse"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Lake Erie shore at Euclid Beach / east of Bratenahl",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Cleveland (the Wikipedia table puts it east of Bratenahl; the Police Museum page puts it at Euclid Beach)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Lake%20Erie%20shore%20at%20Euclid%20Beach%20/%20east%20of%20Bratenahl%2C%20Cleveland"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "A year before the official series began, the lower torso of a woman, thighs amputated at the knees, washed ashore. The head was never found. The skin had been treated with a chemical the coroner took for lime chloride (a preservative), which had reddened and toughened it. She was never identified and is usually listed as \"Victim Zero.\" (unverified — V2_R06) When the Euclid Beach torso of Feb 1937 came ashore at almost the same spot, the papers immediately tied the two (unverified — V2_R06). The odd detail: The chemical on the skin — the cited page's reading is that the killer meant to use a quicklime to speed decomposition and used a preserving lime by mistake.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium — the two sources differ on the exact shore location; the \"lime chloride\" detail is from Wikipedia's victim table. UNVERIFIED: whether Pearse personally handled it. • She was never identified and is usually listed as \"Victim Zero.\" (unverified — V2_R06) When the Euclid Beach torso of Feb 1937 came ashore at almost the same spot, the papers immediately tied the two (unverified — V2_R06).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A preserved body dumped in the lake reads, in the setting, as an offering — or a failed attempt at a rite. Something in Lake Erie wanted her whole.",
