      "id": "R16-019",
      "title": "Londos v. Lewis at Wrigley Field",
      "date_start": "1934-09-20",
      "date_end": "1934-09-20",
      "date_label": "September 20, 1934 — and wrestling's decade",
      "category": "Nightlife, vice, sport, spectacle and entertainment in 1930s Chicago (and the Great Lakes)",
      "tags": [
        "nightlife",
        "spectacle",
        "Londos",
        "Lewis",
        "Wrigley Field"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Jim Londos",
        "Ed \"Strangler\" Lewis"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Wrigley Field",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Wrigley%20Field%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "regular cards at Chicago Stadium and the Coliseum",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=regular%20cards%20at%20Chicago%20Stadium%20and%20the%20Coliseum"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Jim Londos, \"the Golden Greek,\" the biggest draw the business had ever had (he had turned Madison Square Garden from sub-3,200 crowds to 20,000 sell-outs), finally pinned Ed \"Strangler\" Lewis at Wrigley Field before 35,625 paying more than $96,000 — both North American records for wrestling at the time. Chicago's Greek community (see the Karzas brothers, the Chamales brothers) treated him as a national hero (unverified — V2_R16). The odd detail: The Greek grocer's son (unverified — V_R16) and the Strangler drew more people to Wrigley than a Cubs game (unverified — V2_R16).",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the figures; wrestling's outcomes were, of course, arranged. • Chicago's Greek community (see the Karzas brothers, the Chamales brothers) treated him as a national hero (unverified — V2_R16). • The Greek grocer's son (unverified — V_R16) and the Strangler drew more people to Wrigley than a Cubs game (unverified — V2_R16).",
