      "summary": "Convicted at Lima in March 1934 of Sheriff Sarber's murder (Makley found guilty 17 March, after a trial that began on the 13th; Pierpont's page puts the convictions in early March, so Pierpont was not convicted after Makley; Russell Clark got life on 24 March after his lawyer pleaded that the triggerman was already condemned), Pierpont and Makley waited on death row. On 22 September 1934 — two months after Dillinger's death — they tried his trick: pistols carved from cakes of soap and blackened with shoe polish. The guards opened fire. Makley, 44, of St. Marys, Ohio, was shot in the head and shoulder and died; Pierpont was \"riddled\" and shot in the spine. On 17 October 1934, still crippled, Harry Pierpont, 32, was carried to the electric chair and pronounced dead at 12:14 a.m. Makley lies in Sugar Ridge Cemetery, Leipsic, Ohio; Pierpont in Holy Cross and St. Joseph Cemetery, Indianapolis. The odd detail: A wooden gun got Dillinger out of Crown Point; a soap gun got Makley killed and Pierpont carried to the chair. Same trick, opposite result, six months apart.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
