      "summary": "Retired Marine Maj.-Gen. Smedley Butler testified that bond salesman Gerald C. MacGuire (of Grayson M.-P. Murphy & Co., $100 a week, a Connecticut American Legion man) had promised him command of an army of 500,000 veterans to march on Washington, with Butler as a \"Secretary of General Affairs\" holding real power and Roosevelt as figurehead. MacGuire had studied veterans' organisations of \"Fascist character\" in Europe and wrote on March 6, 1934 about France's Croix-de-Feu. The New York Times called it \"a gigantic hoax\" (Nov 22, 1934); the committee's February 15, 1935 report said the attempts \"were discussed, were planned, and might have been placed in execution when and if the financial backers deemed it expedient.\" MacGuire died in a New Haven hospital on March 25, 1935, aged 36. The odd detail: the plot's go-between died at 36 within four months of being named.",
