      "summary": "Three agents (Metcalfe, Winstead, McRae) had sat for nearly three weeks in the house of Hobart Hermanson at Lake Como — Hermanson, an ex-bootlegger (unverified — V2_R02) with a lakeside inn, had himself tipped the Bureau that Nelson would winter there. Early on the afternoon of 27 November, about two hours before the shootout, Nelson, his wife Helen and John Paul Chase drove up in a Ford V-8; Agent Metcalfe on the porch mistook the car for Mrs Hermanson's; Nelson asked for \"Hobe,\" sensed the trap and drove off — and there was no Bureau car to follow because McRae had taken it to Lake Geneva for groceries. The Bureau's Chicago office was alerted. On US-12 near Fox River Grove agents Ryan and McDade met the Ford coming the other way; Chase fired an automatic rifle into their car; a return shot pierced Nelson's radiator. Cowley and Hollis, following, caught up at the entrance to North Park in Barrington at about 3:15 p.m., where Nelson stopped and walked into their fire with a machine gun. Hollis hit him in the legs with a shotgun; Nelson shot Hollis in the head and Cowley in the stomach, then took the agents' car. He had nine wounds. Helen and Chase drove him to a safe house on Walnut Street in Wilmette, where he died in bed at 7:35 p.m. with Helen beside him. On an anonymous call the next day agents found the body in front of St. Paul's Lutheran Cemetery in Niles Center, wrapped in an Indian-pattern blanket; Helen said she had wrapped him because \"he always hated being cold.\" Hollis died on the way to hospital; Cowley died after surgery in Elgin, about ten hours later, having lived long enough to identify Nelson. Nelson and Helen are buried at St. Joseph Cemetery, River Grove. Chase, caught at a fish hatchery at Mount Shasta, California on 27 December, became the first man tried under the new federal statute for killing a federal agent (convicted 24 March 1935; life; Alcatraz; paroled 1966; died 1973). The odd detail: The gangster's host tipped the Bureau; the Bureau's only car was out buying groceries; the dead man was left at a cemetery gate in a blanket because his wife could not stand him being cold.",
