      "summary": "The Karageorge society's memorial book (9 Dec 1934) wrote of the guslar with his one-string gusle, who \"can still move the hearts of the Serbian people to tears\" with the tales of Kosovo Polje, and of the Serbian soldiers led \"from victory to victory\" under \"the late King Peter Karageorgevich and his equally famous and illustrious son, the late King Alexander\" — Alexander had been assassinated in Marseille two months earlier (9 Oct 1934). The United Serbian Society (grown from an \"Obilich Pleasure Club,\" admitting women from 1917) ran all its junior meetings in Serbian under a girl president, Desanka I. Tarailo. The odd detail: a Chicago choir's book mourning a murdered king by name, weeks after the killing — the Croatian sculptor's \"Jugoslav regime\" seen from the other side.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
