      "places": [
        {
          "name": "the parish church of San Juan Bautista",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Plaza Hidalgo, Coyoacán, Mexico City",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20parish%20church%20of%20San%20Juan%20Bautista%2C%20Plaza%20Hidalgo%2C%20Coyoac%C3%A1n%2C%20Mexico%20City"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "M01 has the outline. Additions from Crónica and CNDH-linked sources: on 29 December Catholic Action members learned that the Red Shirts would rally outside the church on Sunday 30 December 1934; the priest warned people in advance (per a search summary). After an anticlerical speech the Catholics who had come to Mass came out and confronted the \"garridistas,\" who retreated toward the Municipal Palace and shot. Five Catholics were killed, among them María de la Luz Cirenia Camacho González, 27, Catholic Action catechist (b. 1907), shot in the chest at the door of the parish and given last rites by Fray Alejandro Torres before she died. The Red Shirt Ernesto Malda, a young student who had arrived late and tried to escape by streetcar, was lynched. 65 Red Shirts were arrested and 23 prosecuted, none convicted of the deaths (Crónica); the English Wikipedia says 62 Red Shirts and three Catholics. Garrido, then Secretary of Agriculture, is said to have sent champagne to the jailed gunmen (UNVERIFIED — see M01/V_M01). Cárdenas restricted political activity to the National Revolutionary Party; Garrido resigned in June 1935 and went to Costa Rica. The odd detail: in the same parish, the priest warned the congregation the day before.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the date, the church (San Juan Bautista) and the two named dead; Medium on the numbers arrested. • UNVERIFIED — see M01/V_M01",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — M01's \"clean one-session mystery\" — plus a second seam: the priest's advance warning meant the Catholics came armed with knowledge — someone inside Catholic Action already knew the schedule. The IHS asks who told the priest.",
