      "summary": "Buffalo's Black musicians, barred from the white union, had their own Local 533 (about 500 members); in early 1934 they rented the upstairs at 145 Broadway, bought the building in early 1935, licensed the club on 24 February 1935 and incorporated it separately on 14 May 1935 — a union hall by day and an after-hours room by night where every touring band (Armstrong, Basie, Ellington, Fitzgerald, Gillespie, Hines) came to jam. It outlived the 1969 merger of the locals and is a museum today. The odd detail: A union hall that became the only room in Buffalo where the two halves of the music business met after midnight.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Great Lakes ring's after-hours rooms — Buffalo, Cleveland, Detroit, Chicago — are a circuit; a band that plays all four in a week is a courier line from Niagara (the Canadian border) to the Stroll.",
