      "summary": "AEG engineers, using Fritz Pfleumer's magnetic-tape invention, showed \"the world's first practical tape recorder, the K1\" at the 1935 Berlin Radio Show. In 1936 Sir Thomas Beecham's London Philharmonic, on tour in Germany, was recorded (Mozart's 39th) on an AEG K2 — the players disliked the noisy playback. AC bias (1941) made tape sound live; Americans only got hold of the machine in 1945, when Jack Mullin took two from a German radio station at Bad Nauheim. The odd detail: in the real record, America did not see a working tape recorder until it captured one.",
