      "summary": "In 1935 Eleanor Roosevelt broke ground for a 701-unit development — \"the nation's first federally funded public housing development for African Americans\". The first low-rise blocks were finished in 1938; an expansion to 941 units followed in 1941. Tenancy required an employed parent in each family and capped residents' earnings. The Brewster recreation centre beside it is where Joe Louis learned to box (R07/R14). The odd detail: The first federal housing for Black Americans anywhere was a Detroit project — with a job required to get in, in a city where the jobs had vanished.",
