      "summary": "All three railroads put a streamliner on the Chicago–Twin Cities run in the same year, 1935: the Milwaukee Road's Twin Cities Hiawatha (first service 29 May 1935), the Burlington's Twin Cities Zephyr, and the Chicago & North Western's Twin Cities 400 (which used refurbished steam locomotives). The Hiawatha ran a daily 6½-hour schedule over 410 miles, its four new Class A 4-4-2 locomotives styled by Otto Kuhler and \"capable of sustained speeds of more than 100 mph,\" with a maximum operating speed quoted at 112.5 to 125 mph and five-car trains. (By June 1941 the afternoon train was scheduled at six hours fifteen minutes; the \"beaver tail\" observation car came in 1938.) The service lasted until Amtrak, 30 April 1971. The odd detail: Three railroads launched a streamliner race on the same 400-mile route in a single year — and the winner's schedule (six hours and a quarter by 1941) meant a Chicago morning departure landed you in St. Paul by mid-afternoon.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (times and speeds are the railroad's own claims; the \"gangsters and agents\" line is a proposal, not a source).",
