      "summary": "James Cleveland Owens, born 12 September 1913 in Oakville, Alabama, came to Cleveland at nine in the Migration (a teacher heard \"J.C.\" as \"Jesse\"). At East Tech he tied the world 100-yard record (9.4) and long-jumped 24 ft 9½ in at the 1933 national high-school meet in Chicago. On 25 May 1935, between 3:15 and 4:00 p.m. at the Big Ten meet in Ann Arbor, he set or tied six world records in four events — the long jump mark (8.13 m) stood 25 years. In Berlin in August 1936 he won four golds (100, 200, long jump, relay); the Hitler \"snub\" is a myth (accounts differ on whether they shook hands), the Luz Long \"advice\" story Owens later admitted was untrue, and Jewish sprinters Marty Glickman and Sam Stoller were pulled from the relay for Owens and Ralph Metcalfe (a Chicago man, Marquette — from memory). Avery Brundage suspended him when he refused more exhibition races in Europe; he got a ticker-tape parade, $10,000 in cash from an unknown source, the freight elevator at the Waldorf, and then work as a gas-station attendant, playground janitor and dry cleaner, racing horses and motorcycles for money. The odd detail: Six world records in 45 minutes, then a job as a playground janitor.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Owens in Berlin as an unwitting courier — someone in the German occult world (Hitler the occult figure exists) wants a word with the fastest man alive. Cleveland is IHS territory (John Carroll University); what did he bring home?",
