      "summary": "Kodachrome, perfected by two professional musicians, Leopold Godowsky Jr. and Leopold Mannes, went on sale as 16mm movie film in 1935 (speed ASA 10) and as 8mm movie film and 35mm (135/828) slide film in 1936. It was sold with processing included; the customer mailed the exposed film to Kodak and got back finished slides — no local lab could develop it (a rule that lasted until a 1954 antitrust ruling). The odd detail: every colour photograph taken in 1936 went through Kodak's own lab before its owner saw it.",
