      "summary": "Arthur Lupp, the Legion's Michigan commander, was a Detroit Board of Health milk inspector; Charles T. McCutcheon was the department's chemist and bacteriologist. Guthrie, a Legion \"intelligence man\", said the two came to his house to see whether typhoid could be bred in his basement, for injection into milk and cheese sold in Jewish markets (McCutcheon was asked whether germs could be preserved in cottage cheese). McCutcheon told the prosecutor that Lupp also discussed poisoned \"death needles\" to be jabbed into people in a crowd, a \"cyanide gas\" to be pumped into a room through a keyhole, and a bomb \"the size of a cigarette\" to blow up a car. The Health Department fired McCutcheon for keeping the plot to himself for over a year — and disclosed that he had been doing bacteriological experiments at the city laboratory that were no part of his job. He said the Legion shadowed him and would kill him if it came out. The odd detail: The city's own laboratory, used after hours for experiments nobody assigned.",
