      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Saint-Laurent-du-Maroni → by open boat to Trinidad",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(17 days) → Colombia → Central America → Los Angeles (March 1937)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Saint-Laurent-du-Maroni%20%E2%86%92%20by%20open%20boat%20to%20Trinidad%20%20%E2%86%92%20Colombia%20%E2%86%92%20Central%20America%20%E2%86%92%20Los%20Angeles%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Belbenoît, born in Paris on 4 April 1899 and raised partly behind his uncle's café, the Rat Mort, was convicted of thefts and swindles (arrested 21 August 1921) and sentenced to eight years' hard labour with doublage; he landed at Saint-Laurent on 21 June 1923. Escapes in 1923, 1924 and 1927 failed and lengthened his time. On 2 May 1935 he and five others — \"Bébère\", \"Dadar\", \"Cap\", \"Panama\", \"Chiflot\" — escaped by sea, reaching Trinidad in 17 days, then Colombia. He reached Los Angeles in March 1937 with his manuscript; Dry Guillotine (Blue Ribbon Books, 1938) ran through 14 printings in under a year; Hell on Trial followed in 1940. He later advised on Passage to Marseille (1944), became an American citizen in 1956 and died in Lucerne Valley, California, on 26 February 1959. The odd detail: a Paris thief carried the manuscript that shamed the bagne through a dozen jungles and borders — and it became an American bestseller the year France ended transportation.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the classic noir memoir of the decade, and an American bestseller a player can have read. A seventh man who left in the boat and is not in the book.",
