      "summary": "The New Deal's Public Works Administration built Lockefield Gardens between 1935 and 1938 as Indianapolis's first public housing complex, for low-income African American residents: 748 housing units in 24 buildings designed by architects Russ and Harrison, on 22 acres with a central mall, four playgrounds and a school, at a cost of about $3 million and roughly 9,000 construction jobs. It became the nucleus of northwest Indianapolis's Black community; from 1948 to 1974 it hosted the Dust Bowl basketball tournament (Oscar Robertson played). The apartments closed in 1976 after federal judge S. Hugh Dillin blocked a redevelopment plan; by 1983 only six original buildings remained after demolitions for IUPUI and the Pan Am Games. The odd detail: A federal housing project meant to replace slums sat within a block or two of the school and theatre the segregated city had built for the same people (Crispus Attucks, the Walker Theatre).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium — one page.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A brand-new federal-funded Black neighbourhood in 1938: agents who move in as tenants can watch the whole Avenue; a landlord's ledger is a list of people the Klan's descendants would want.",
