      "summary": "Jane Addams died on 21 May 1935 (funeral in the 09-22 archive, R03). Adena Miller Rich, of the Immigrants' Protective League, headed the settlement 1935–37; then Charlotte E. Carr (born Dayton, Ohio, 3 May 1890; Vassar 1915), who had been Pennsylvania's Secretary of Labor and Industry under Pinchot (1933–34) and run New York City's Emergency Relief Bureau under La Guardia (1935), took over in 1937. Her tenure \"was notable for welcoming African American residents\" — the settlement's first — and for clashes with the trustees \"over her political activities and the direction of the program\"; she was dismissed in 1942 and went on to the War Manpower Commission and the CIO. Through the decade the house kept its dispensary, day nursery, public baths and craft classes. She died in New York 12 July 1956; Eleanor Roosevelt attended her funeral. The odd detail: Addams's successor was a relief bureaucrat from La Guardia's New York, and the board that hired her spent five years trying to be rid of her.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Hull House's residents' rooms are the cheapest respectable lodging for a woman investigator on the Near West Side, and its 1930s \"political activities\" are cover for anything; the trustees' fight with Carr can hide a faction fight.",
