      "summary": "Born 15 March 1900 at Siculiana, Sicily; emigrated 1909; quit school at 14 to sell the Cleveland News on the street. Joined the Mayfield Road Mob in his teens; Volstead Act conviction 1926; suspected (with Charles Colletti) in the 1928 bombing of Nathan Weisenberg's home; sold bootleg into Detroit with Moe Dalitz; took part in the 1930 killings of Joseph Porrello and Sam Tilocco at Milano's restaurant. He succeeded Milano in 1935; Senate investigators later called him \"one of the most influential members of the American mafia.\" His businesses: Lubeck Brewing (1939), Sunrise Brewing renamed Tip Top (1940), Tornello Importing (olive oil, pasta, tomato paste) with Milano, and — with Dalitz — the Molaska Corporation, making \"dehydrated molasses for use in alcohol manufacturing nationwide.\" A 1943 liquor-fraud case (28 federal counts) ended in a 1944 plea and two years; he retired to Coral Gables with $300,000–500,000. The odd detail: Powdered molasses — the Cleveland mob's answer to the end of Prohibition was a powdered raw material for other people's stills.",
