      "summary": "On the night of 8 January 1935 Bureau agents in Chicago arrested Arthur \"Doc\" Barker on the street, unarmed; asked where his gun was, he said, \"Home — and ain't that a hell of a place for it?\" In a second apartment raid the same night gang member Russell Gibson chose to fight and was killed, and Byron Bolton — who would later confess to being a lookout at the St. Valentine's Day Massacre — was taken. In Doc's room agents found a map of Florida with the Ocala area marked, and letters from Ma mentioning an alligator called \"Gator Joe\" on Lake Weir; eight days later they had the house. Doc went to Alcatraz and was shot dead by guards on 13 January 1939 trying to reach the water. The odd detail: The gang that had erased its fingerprints was undone by a road map with a circle on it, and a letter about an alligator.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
