      "summary": "Thomas E. Maloy ran Local 110 of the Motion Picture Machine Operators' union from about 1920, shaking down theatre owners for \"labor peace\". He was the main suspect in the unsolved murder of projectionist Jacob (Jack) Kaufman. In 1935 he was indicted for failing to report $350,000 of income over three years (unverified — V_R01). He was shot dead from a passing car in early February 1935 (secondary accounts give February 4; the Chicago Daily Tribune called him the \"slain czar of the Motion Picture Machine Operators' union\"). His murder, officially unsolved, cleared the way for the Outfit's hold on the projectionists. His funeral drew more than 1,000 people and 310 cars including ten flower cars. The odd detail: Ten flower cars for a union boss — the projectionists who ran the city's picture palaces buried the man who had been robbing them.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium — exact date (Feb 4 vs the Tribune's Feb 6 report) and the Outer Drive location not settled this session; the funeral and burial details are High (from the Tribune via the blog). • the Outer Drive is the usual account — UNVERIFIED • In 1935 he was indicted for failing to report $350,000 of income over three years (unverified — V_R01).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Projectionists are the men alone in the booth with the light. A union that controls every projection booth in the city controls what flickers on every screen — and some films shown at 2 a.m. in closed theatres were never made by any studio.",
