      "summary": "In February 1935 Senator William E. Borah of Idaho — no Catholic — introduced S. Res. 70 for the Foreign Relations Committee to hold hearings on \"religious persecution and antireligious compulsion in Mexico.\" The Knights of Columbus backed it; the State Department warned that, as drafted, it would have the Senate render judgment before hearing evidence. On 8 July 1935 Supreme Knight Martin H. Carmody and other officers met President Franklin Roosevelt about the persecution. The same year Francis C. Kelley, now Bishop of Oklahoma, published Blood-Drenched Altars, a polemical history of the Church in Mexico (Encyclopedia.com: \"a controversial account\"). The resolution never passed; FDR held to the Good Neighbor line. The odd detail: the Senate's great isolationist — the man who kept America out of the League — wanted hearings on Mexico's religion.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High (resolution number and Carmody date from search summaries of FRUS and a K of C council history; not fetched in full).",
