      "id": "R26-011",
      "title": "The Maurice Sugar plot and the forged \"Communist\" leaflet",
      "date_start": "1935-03-30",
      "date_end": "1935-03-30",
      "date_label": "30 March 1935",
      "category": "Detroit 1925–1940, in depth",
      "tags": [
        "Detroit",
        "depth",
        "Maurice Sugar",
        "Communist",
        "BENDS"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Maurice Sugar",
        "Dayton Dean",
        "Leslie"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Northern High School",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Detroit",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Northern%20High%20School%2C%20Detroit"
        },
        {
          "name": "the Workers' Camp in Oakland County",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20Workers%27%20Camp%20in%20Oakland%20County%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Labour lawyer Maurice Sugar ran for Recorder's Court judge in spring 1935 with the Detroit Federation of Labor, independent unions and the Communists behind him, and polled about 63,000 votes. Dean confessed, after the Poole arrest, that on the night of Sugar's final rally (30 March) one Legion squad burned the Workers' Camp while another went to the school: he and League president Leslie Black were to cut the lights, six Legionnaires would set off stench bombs, and forged leaflets signed \"Communist Party of America\" would be thrown in the dark (\"Negroes, rise against your white oppressors…\"). Dean cut only the emergency wires; the janitor found them and a bundle of leaflets the next day, but the Board of Education and the Hearst press still called the leaflet genuine. When deputies searched Dean after the Poole arrest they found a clipping with Sugar's photograph: Dean said Lupp had ordered him to bomb Sugar, and records showed he had rented an apartment in Sugar's building for five months; he \"got cold feet because too many people would have been killed\". The odd detail: The assassin had lived in the target's building for five months, and still carried his photograph.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium — Morris only (Dean's confession as reported). Sugar's later UAW role is from memory — UNVERIFIED.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A forged leaflet that the city believed. The owner's \"three layers\" in one sheet of paper: the Legion wrote it, the Board of Education believed it, and the janitor kept the proof.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Internet Archive: TheBlackLegionRides",
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/TheBlackLegionRides",
          "publisher": "Internet Archive",
          "kind": "book or scanned text",
          "supports": ""
        }
      ],
