      "summary": "Maria Rasputin, daughter of Grigori Rasputin, landed in New York by steamer on 8 March 1935, speaking little English, to join the Hagenbeck-Wallace Circus — then second only to Ringling — as a lion tamer, billed as \"the daughter of the famous mad monk whose feats in Russia astonished the world.\" At the Peru winter quarters, rehearsing with three bears, two lions, two tigers, two leopards and two pumas, she lost her footing and was mauled by a Himalayan black bear named Himmy (10 April per one account, 13 April per Billboard of 20 April 1935): twelve wounds, four of them two inches deep. She was taken to Dukes Memorial Hospital and released six days later in a wheelchair; the circus doctor S. D. Malouf gave her \"a week to 10 days.\" The show was due in Chicago by 20 April. She followed it by ambulance, and when it was announced that she would appear in the Grand Entry, \"the [Chicago] Stadium was packed to the roof with people who gave her an ovation as she waved to them from her bed upon a beautifully decorated wagon.\" She stayed with the circus one season. The odd detail: Rasputin's daughter, like her father, would not stay down — mauled, stitched, and in the Stadium's Grand Entry in a bed on a float within about two weeks.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the mauling and the Stadium appearance (two accounts, one citing Billboard); the exact date of the attack and of the Chicago opening conflict slightly.",
