      "summary": "Coleman, a Black WPA labourer, was lured out of Detroit with the promise of back wages, driven to a Legion member's cottage (unverified — V2_R06), made to run, and hunted down and shot for sport — five men later got first-degree murder (Harvey Davis, James Roy Lorance, John Bannerman, Ervin D. Lee, Charles Rouse). A year later, on the night of 12 May 1936, Legionnaires abducted Charles A. Poole, a 32-year-old (unverified — V2_R06) WPA organiser and French-Canadian (unverified — V2_R06) Catholic married to a Protestant, on the false story that he beat his pregnant (unverified — V2_R06) wife; Dayton Dean shot him on a country road. Dean turned state's evidence, eleven were convicted of Poole's murder, and Prosecutor Duncan McRae used his testimony to bring nearly fifty Legionnaires to conviction for murder, conspiracy, kidnapping, arson and perjury — including the shootings of union men George Marchuk (found 22 Dec 1933, a bullet in his head) and John Bielak (found 15 Mar 1934 near Monroe, \"riddled with bullets\"), an arson squad, a 1933 plot against Highland Park publisher-candidate Arthur Kingsley, and a plan by milk inspector Arthur Lupp Sr. to put typhoid germs into milk at Jewish markets. The AP (31 May 1936) called the Legion \"night-riding bands... enforc[ing] by lash and pistol.\" R10 covers the Legion as an organisation. The odd detail: The typhoid-in-the-milk plot by a city milk inspector, and the Coleman \"hunt\" — men who shot a man for the pleasure of the chase.",
