      "summary": "Early on Wednesday 31 July 1935 Dr. Walter J. Bauer, 38, a chemistry professor at the Kirksville (Missouri) College of Osteopathy and Surgery, was found mutilated with a pocketknife and dying at a South Side filling station, having been driven out from Ann Arbor, where he was at summer school. He died at Jackson Park Hospital after giving a deathbed statement that \"Jones\" had done it. Police arrested Mandeville W. Zenge, 26, a dairy farmer's son from Canton, Missouri, at a taxicab garage at 4642 West Madison Street at 3:30 a.m. on Thursday; he gave the names \"Orville Smith\" and \"Al Johnson\" until his father, J. Andy Zenge, arrived. The motive was a seven-year courtship: Zenge had bought rings for Louise Schaffer, a 23-year-old Kirksville nurse, who on 14 July told him she had married Dr. Bauer only hours before; friends quoted him saying he would \"fix that doctor so he won't be able to treat another girl that way.\" The Tribune reports that Bauer had checked into the Masterbilt Hotel on 25 July as \"E. L. Jones\" — which of the two men used the alias \"Jones\" is UNVERIFIED on the OCR text I read. At trial (October 1935) Assistant State's Attorney Charles S. Dougherty's key witness was a taxi driver, William Leinnert, whom Zenge befriended in taverns and who told police; Leinnert said Zenge told him \"he had cut Dr. Bauer... but had not intended to kill him.\" A 17-year-old newsboy, Herman Eichner (Pichner in an earlier issue) of 6133 Champlain, pointed Zenge out — but on cross-examination admitted police had shown him Zenge first. The defense, led by Joseph Roach, asked the jury to find insanity (\"one of the strangest defenses ever heard in a murder trial,\" per the Tribune), and floated a jurisdiction argument that the fatal wound (a \"brain clot due to the penknife operation\") may have happened over the line in Indiana. Defense alienists were Dr. Harold S. Hulbert (of the Loeb–Leopold case) and Dr. Franz Alexander, a psychoanalyst formerly of Berlin; Dr. Harry Hoffman of the Criminal Court behavior clinic testified for the state. The Tribune of 19 October 1935 reports the verdict: guilty, life in the penitentiary, after 17 ballots and about 4½ hours (the first ballot had one vote for death and eight for life; foreman Edward Kieson); at least 20 years before parole; Zenge could be moved to a state security hospital if insanity developed. (A search summary says the Michigan Daily covered it from Ann Arbor and calls the injury \"emasculation\" — not read.) The odd detail: The dying man named a \"Jones\" and the hotel register holds another \"E. L. Jones\"; and one of the defense's alienists was a psychoanalyst \"formerly of Berlin.\"",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High for the trial facts (four Tribune issues); the OCR text is rough, so names and street numbers may carry small errors. • Jones\" — which of the two men used the alias \"Jones\" is UNVERIFIED on the OCR text I read. • Dr. Franz Alexander's Berlin past is real record; in the setting Berlin is the winning capital, so a refugee from it needs a different reason to be in Chicago — UNVERIFIED.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Two \"Jones\"es, a chemistry professor from a Kirksville osteopathy college registered under a false name at a Lake Park Avenue hotel, and a jury told to weigh insanity by the alienist from the Loeb-Leopold case. A chemist with an alias is a natural target or contractor for the Kabbalahs' or the Church's forbidden work.",
