      "summary": "Time magazine (12 Aug 1935) groups it with the Dunkel case as the summer's \"Midwest Murders\": Dr. Walter Bauer, a professor of chemistry at the Kirksville (Missouri) College of Osteopathy, newly married, was forced to drive to a lonely Chicago alley and castrated by Mandeville Zenge, a carpenter who had been an old suitor of the bride; Bauer died; Zenge disappeared and was then caught. (Time names the bride as Louise Schaffer, night superintendent of nurses at the college hospital; Bauer left her three hours after the wedding for postgraduate work at the University of Michigan — there was no honeymoon — and Zenge was caught the next night in a West Side garage telephone booth. The Chicago trial and life sentence are not in Time — UNVERIFIED.) The odd detail: A bridegroom mutilated by the man his wife had left, on a Chicago street, in the same week Chicago's headlines read \"TORSO MURDER; SWAMP MURDER.\"",
