      "summary": "Haiti's government issued a decree-law on \"superstitious practices\" on 5 September 1935, repealed by Article 297 of the 1987 Constitution, which lists \"the decree law of September 5, 1935 on superstitious beliefs\" (a June 2012 amendment is reported to have removed Article 297 itself, not to have repealed the decree-law). Reviews of Kate Ramsey's The Spirits and the Law say Haitian governments, the Catholic Church and the occupying US forces all used anti-\"superstition\" laws against Vodou, and that during the occupation trials and \"the confiscation and destruction of ritual objects\" were part of the pattern. No page I read gives a named temple, date or place for a specific raid. The 1941–42 Church-led \"rejeté\" campaign (destroying temples and sacred objects) falls just outside the window and I could not confirm details. The odd detail: the law's own term — \"superstitious\" — is the period's category, kept in the statute for 52 years (1935–1987).",
