      "summary": "Louis's trainer Jack Blackburn (born 20 May 1883, Versailles, Kentucky; pro from 29 April 1900; three fights with Joe Gans and six with Sam Langford; 117–24–22 by the newspaper count) had served four years and eight months for manslaughter after a 1909 Philadelphia \"shooting spree\" that left several dead. In Chicago, where he trained champions Sammy Mandell, Bud Taylor and John Henry Lewis before taking Louis in June 1934, he was indicted for manslaughter in October 1935 after two shootings, jailed briefly on 21 October, and saw the charges dropped. He drank hard in the Louis years and died of a heart attack during a routine physical in Chicago on 24 April 1942, aged 59. The odd detail: A month after his fighter beat Max Baer (24 September 1935), the trainer was in a Chicago cell (21 October 1935) for shooting people — and walked.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the indictment and dates; the victims of the 1935 shootings are not named in the source.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Charges dropped against the champion's trainer in the autumn of 1935 — who made the call, and what did the policy men pay for it?",
