      "summary": "R27 already tells the campaign. This adds what the historic-preservation blog \"Lost Milwaukee #2: Eight Days of Terror in 1935\" gives: the two — Hugh \"Idzy\" Rutkowski (leader) and Paul \"Shrimp\" Chovanec (16, spelled Chevanek and Chovonec elsewhere) — stole about 150 lb of explosive from a CCC project (a search summary of another page says \"150 sticks of dynamite\" from the Estabrook Park CCC camp, and that the garage was rented for them by Chovanec). Bombs: Shorewood Village Hall; both bank branches on 28 October, 30 minutes apart; the Fifth and Third District police stations on Halloween at 6:45 and 7:00 p.m. On Sunday 3 November the \"super bomb\" they were building went off: both boys were killed instantly, Patricia Mylnarek (9) next door was killed, 11 were injured and 40 buildings damaged. Rutkowski had mailed the police barely literate demand letters (\"Polis gard sity ha ha ha — poooey on g mans\") and police believed the target was a downtown movie theatre. He was identified four days later from fingerprints on a charred, severed hand found on a garage roof. The remains were displayed at the county morgue \"visited by thousands,\" then buried together in one casket at Forest Home Cemetery; 25,000 filed past Patricia's casket in a two-day wake and 2,400 attended her funeral. The odd detail: Thousands lined up to look at the bombers' remains — and 25,000 to see a nine-year-old's coffin.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High for the sequence; the ages, the first date and the \"lb vs sticks\" figure are inconsistent across sources.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Two teenagers with New Deal dynamite and a \"super bomb\" for a downtown theatre; the 16-year-old \"dominated.\" Who was teaching them? A Milwaukee Polish street cult, a Golden Circle recruiter, or nobody — the \"cult versus population\" principle says the city itself came to gawk.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "historicmilwaukee.blogspot.com: lost milwaukee 2 eight days of terror",
          "url": "http://historicmilwaukee.blogspot.com/2011/02/lost-milwaukee-2-eight-days-of-terror.html",
          "publisher": "historicmilwaukee.blogspot.com",
          "kind": "web page cited in the 2026-09-26 sweep",
          "supports": "read"
        },
        {
          "title": "a search summary of it gave the 26 October Saturday first blast, which fits the calendar (3 November 1935 was a Sunday, so 26 October was a Saturday) — the blog's \"October 27\" for the first blast is therefore probably a slip",
          "url": "",
          "publisher": "",
          "kind": "citation without a link",
          "supports": ""
        }
      ],
