      "summary": "The former White Stockings outfielder turned evangelist — \"Get on the Water Wagon\" — had lost his crowds to radio and movies after the war. After repeal (Dec 1933) he called for Prohibition's return. His last years were dark: his three sons lived what he preached against and the family paid blackmail to several women to keep the scandals quiet; his daughter died in 1932 (apparently multiple sclerosis) and his eldest son George killed himself in 1933. After a mild heart attack early in 1935 he kept preaching against advice and died on 6 Nov 1935 in Chicago, a week after his last sermon, on the text \"What must I do to be saved?\" The odd detail: America's loudest preacher against sin spent his last years paying blackmail to hide his own sons' sins.",
