      "summary": "A Chicago White Stockings outfielder from 1883 (Cap Anson signed him; he beat the sprinter Arlie Latham in a 100-yard dash in 1885), Billy Sunday was converted at the Pacific Garden Mission around 1886–87 and became America's most famous evangelist of the 1910s, preaching to perhaps 100 million people face to face without amplification. His wife Nell ran the campaigns from 1908. The 1930s were ruin: his housekeeper died in 1930, his daughter in 1932, and his eldest son killed himself in 1933. He died in Chicago on 6 November 1935, one week after his last sermon. The odd detail: The man who preached Prohibition into law died two years after Repeal, in the city where a rescue mission on skid row had saved him fifty years earlier.",
