      "id": "R02-035",
      "title": "Garrettsville: the last great train robbery, and a Stinson to Hot Springs",
      "date_start": "1935-11-07",
      "date_end": "1935-11-07",
      "date_label": "7 November 1935",
      "category": "The outlaw era in the Midwest: bank robbers, kidnappers, jailbreaks and manhunts, 1930–1936",
      "tags": [
        "outlaws",
        "kidnapping",
        "Garrettsville",
        "Stinson",
        "Hot Springs"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Alvin \"Creepy\" Karpis",
        "Harry Campbell",
        "Fred Hunter",
        "Ben Grayson",
        "John Zetzer"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "The Erie Railroad depot",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Garrettsville, Portage County, Ohio",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=The%20Erie%20Railroad%20depot%2C%20Garrettsville%2C%20Portage%20County%2C%20Ohio"
        },
        {
          "name": "Port Clinton",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Ohio (the airfield)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Port%20Clinton%2C%20Ohio"
        },
        {
          "name": "Hot Springs",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Arkansas",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Hot%20Springs%2C%20Arkansas%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Karpis, Harry Campbell, Fred Hunter (watching the car park), Ben Grayson (in a comic false moustache) and others held up Erie train #626 (unverified — V_R02), Cleveland to Pittsburgh, at the Garrettsville depot. Karpis, with a machine gun, threw an unlit stick of dynamite into the mail car and promised the next would be lit. They expected about six times what they got — the Republic Steel payroll and, they hoped, Youngstown Sheet & Tube's — and got $34,000 in cash and $12,450 in bonds ($72,000 was the take of the gang's April 1935 Warren mail-truck robbery, not the Garrettsville expectation). They drove back roads via Ravenna to Port Clinton on Lake Erie, where Karpis and Hunter had chartered a Stinson monoplane and were flown next morning to Hot Springs, Arkansas. The getaway car, by the standard account, was cut up with torches and sunk in Lake Erie by the pilot, John Zetzer (search-summary detail; Medium). Ohio calls it America's last great train heist; a historical marker stands at the depot (unverified — V_R02). The odd detail: Bandits leaving by aeroplane from a Lake Erie resort town, with the car cut to pieces and dropped in the lake.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the robbery and the flight; Medium on the sunk car. • Karpis, Harry Campbell, Fred Hunter (watching the car park), Ben Grayson (in a comic false moustache) and others held up Erie train #626 (unverified — V_R02), Cleveland to Pittsburgh, at the Garrettsville depot. • a historical marker stands at the depot (unverified — V_R02).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A mail car is full of other people's letters; the gang took the payroll and left the mail — or did they? A Section 13 pouch on Erie #626 is an easy plant.",
