      "summary": "António Egas Moniz's first leucotomies (performed by Pedro Almeida Lima, 12 Nov 1935; twenty patients by Feb 1936, ages 27–62, claimed 35% much improved, 35% somewhat, 30% unchanged) began as ethanol injections into the frontal white matter, then a wire leucotome. Walter Freeman and James Watts did the first American lobotomy on 14 Sep 1936 in Washington and adapted their \"precision method\" in 1937. Adoption through the 1930s stayed low (Britain: six operations before 1942; Italy's Racconigi hospital an exception with ~200 by 1939); the ice-pick transorbital method is 1945–46, not the 1930s. For a 1930s Chicago game: the operation exists, is news, and is rare — a state hospital superintendent who has done one is a novelty. The odd detail: the first cure for madness was alcohol injected into the front of the brain.",
