      "summary": "The Revolutionary Mexicanist Action (ARM), the \"Gold Shirts\" (Camisas Doradas), founded 10 March 1934 and led by Nicolás Rodríguez Carrasco, a former Villista, was a fascist, secular, antisemitic, anti-communist militia that broke strikes and preached the expulsion of Chinese and Jews; it was supported by the German embassy. On Revolution Day 1935 Gold Shirts (many mounted) clashed with communists and taxi drivers, who used their cabs against the horsemen (as period prints show): 3 dead, 40+ injured; Rodríguez was stabbed twice in the abdomen. The Senate moved to ban the group two days later (22 Nov); Cárdenas decreed it dissolved on 27 Feb 1936 and Rodríguez was arrested and exiled to Texas in Aug 1936. The odd detail: the fascist cavalry charge was broken by taxis — a scene the printmaker Alfredo Zalce made famous (Choferes contra \"Camisas Doradas\", held at the Art Institute of Chicago, LACMA and the Met).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. • the period evidence would be 1935 press photographs) [date UNVERIFIED — V_M01: the museum page returned 403; other museums' impressions are from the 1947 and 1960 portfolios, one search summary dates the Chicago print 1940].",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"Gold\" — the Golden Circle's colour on a Mexican militia's shirts, funded by Berlin, led by Villa's old horseman. Either a Circle front or the Circle's enemy wearing its colour; a GM can play it both ways. Zalce's print of the battle is in the Art Institute of Chicago's collection — a clue in the players' own city (made later, 1947–60, so a modern-set clue only; the period evidence would be 1935 press photographs) [date UNVERIFIED — V_M01: the museum page returned 403; other museums' impressions are from the 1947 and 1960 portfolios, one search summary dates the Chicago print 1940].",
