      "summary": "Liggett, publisher of the Midwest American, had since Sept 1934 accused Governor Floyd B. Olson and the Minneapolis rackets of a corrupt bargain; Isadore \"Kid Cann\" Blumenfeld had offered him a bribe, then had him beaten. On the evening of 9 Dec 1935, as Liggett got out of his car with groceries, a Thompson gun cut him down from a passing car. His wife Edith and daughter Marda saw it; Edith picked Blumenfeld out of a police line-up, and she and three other witnesses named him at trial. He was acquitted; no one else was charged. Officially unsolved. The odd detail: A murder witnessed by the victim's wife and child — the wife and three other witnesses named the killer under oath — and still no conviction.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
