      "summary": "Pedro Linares López (29 June 1906 – 25 January 1992) was a cartonero who made piñatas, carnival masks and the papier-mâché Judas figures burned (often with firecrackers) on Holy Saturday, and figures for Diego Rivera and Frida Kahlo. In 1936, aged 30, he fell into high fever and unconsciousness from peritonitis and dreamed of a strange forest full of polychrome creatures — a winged donkey, a lion with a dog's head, a rooster with bull's horns — that shouted one word: \"¡Alebrijes!\" On recovering he built them in paper. The form became famous only with Judith Bronowski's 1975 film. The odd detail: the monsters named themselves — the word alebrije exists because the dream-creatures shouted it.",
