      "summary": "Victim 4, a man estimated at 20 to 23, was found 5 June 1936, decapitated, the head found separately wrapped in his trousers (unverified — V2_R06). He carried six tattoos — including the names \"Helen and Paul,\" a Cupid, a butterfly, and the initials \"W.C.G.\" — so the police reasoned that somebody, somewhere, had to know him. Detective Lloyd Trunk made a plaster mould of the head (unverified — V2_R06); the death mask, with a chart of the tattoos, went on display in the police exhibit at the Great Lakes Exposition. More than 100,000 fairgoers filed past the face. Nobody claimed him. The mask, with three others from the case, is still displayed at the Cleveland Police Museum. The odd detail: A hundred thousand people (unverified — V_R06) paid to look (unverified — V2_R06) at a murdered man's face at a world's-fair-style exposition, in a summer when the killer was still working a mile away — and the tattoos (\"Helen and Paul\") name someone who never came forward.",
