      "summary": "After the Supreme Court's diversion decrees (1929–30) limited how much Lake Michigan water Chicago could send down its canal, the Sanitary District of Chicago built a lock and controlling works at the river's mouth, 1936–38, to limit the diversion and keep the reversed river from flowing back into the lake. The chamber is 600 ft long, 80 ft wide and 22 ft deep, with two pairs of 30-ft sector gates; the controlling works have eight 10 × 10 ft sluice gates. A cycle takes 12–15 minutes for a two-to-five-foot difference in level. The odd detail: From 1938 the whole river sits in a box: the lake is literally higher than the city's river, held back by gates the Sanitary District opens in storms.",
