      "summary": "To hold Chicago's diversion of lake water within the limits the Supreme Court had imposed (R03 has the lake-and-sewers fight), the Sanitary District of Chicago built a lock at the river mouth between 1936 and 1938: 600 ft long, 80 ft wide, 22 ft deep, with two pairs of 30-ft sector gates and eight 10-by-10-ft sluice gates; a cycle takes 12–15 minutes for the usual 2–5-ft difference; it can hold up to 100 small boats at once. It remains one of the busiest locks in the country. The odd detail: From 1938 the reversed river is not merely reversed but locked — nothing floats from the lake into the city, or the city into the lake, without a gate opening. The Loop has a moat with a door.",
