      "summary": "In 1936 Florence Thompson Castle, 24, a nightclub hostess, was beaten to death in a Chicago hotel room with her seven-year-old son present; the killer wrote \"Black Legion Game\" on the mirror in lipstick. In Los Angeles in 1937 a series of brick attacks (H. W. Koll, 25 January; Elizabeth Reis, 3 February; Zoe Damrell, 28 March) turned to murder — Rose Valdez, 20, killed 2 March 1937 beside her sleeping baby; Edna Worden, 48, and her daughter Marguerite, 12, murdered and violated with a brick in their Bunker Hill apartment on 4 April 1937. Back in Chicago, Florence Johnson, a fireman's wife, was beaten to death with a brick in a burglary on 27 May 1938; Robert Nixon, 18, was arrested the next day with blood on his clothes and scratched hands, with accomplice Earl Hicks (alias Howard Jones Green), and confessed to the Johnson, Castle and nursing-student Anna Kuchta (1937) killings and three assaults — then recanted, saying police had beaten it out of him. A fingerprint on a milk bottle in the Wordens' flat tied him to Los Angeles. The Tribune's Charles Leavelle wrote him up on 5 June 1938 as \"BRICK SLAYER IS LIKENED TO JUNGLE BEAST\" — hunched shoulders, \"missing link.\" Hicks drew 14 years on 27 January 1939; Nixon, born 19 July 1919, went to the electric chair at Cook County Jail on 16 June 1939, aged 19, under the felony-murder rule. Richard Wright, halfway through Native Son, said the case \"flared forth in the newspapers of Chicago\" and used it. The odd detail: \"Black Legion Game\" on a hotel mirror in 1936 — the year the Black Legion's Detroit murders broke in the press — written at a Chicago killing that no Legion had anything to do with. The Black Legion is already in the setting.",
