      "id": "R07-010",
      "title": "The Harvard Club raid",
      "date_start": "1936-01-10",
      "date_end": "1936-01-10",
      "date_label": "10 January 1936",
      "category": "The Great Lakes ring beyond Chicago, 1929–1939",
      "tags": [
        "Great Lakes ring",
        "Harvard Club"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Eliot Ness",
        "James \"Shimmy\" Patton",
        "Frank J. Lausche"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Harvard Club",
          "address": "4209 Harvard Ave.",
          "geography": "Harvard Avenue, Newburgh Heights (addresses 3111 and 4209 Harvard Ave.), just outside the Cleveland city line",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Harvard%20Club%2C%204209%20Harvard%20Ave%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The Harvard Club took 500–1,000 gamblers a night from across the country — craps, slots, roulette, all-night poker — under operators including James \"Shimmy\" Patton, Arthur Hebebrand and Daniel T. Gallagher. Safety Director Eliot Ness led the celebrated raid that closed it on 10 January 1936. It reopened the next month with more gambling and a fleet of limousines giving free rides from downtown, survived scandals and grand juries, and only closed in April 1941 after Judge Frank J. Lausche deputised a police captain as an officer of the court to enforce his order. The odd detail: Raided shut, it came back within a month offering free limousine pickup.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A casino just over a city line, fed by limousines, that cannot be kept closed — a GM's favourite kind of door.",
