      "summary": "The Federal Theatre Project's Negro Unit opened Orson Welles's Macbeth (Welles 20, producer John Houseman) on 14 April 1936, transposed to a 19th-century Caribbean court modelled on Henri Christophe's Haiti, with Vodou in place of the Scottish witches. Hecate became a male Vodou priest with a bullwhip (Eric Burroughs); African drummers and dancers were led by the Sierra Leonean choreographer Asadata Dafora; the dancer Abdul Assen played the \"Witch Doctor\". Cast of 150; ten sold-out weeks; a free performance on 20 April drew 3,000 more people than the house held. The Herald Tribune's critic Percy Hammond — formerly the Chicago Tribune's drama critic — panned it. According to Welles's own story, the drummers made a doll and stuck it with pins, and Hammond died shortly after; it became the production's legend. The odd detail: Welles told the curse story for decades, delighted — the period's \"voodoo\" as publicity, from the man the Chicago book already uses (Woodstock).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the production; the curse is Welles's anecdote. Hammond died 25 April 1936, aged 63, eleven days after the opening.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The tour came to Chicago in 1936 — the core book's city. The drummers were West African, not Haitian; the \"curse\" was a joke; a GM can decide one member of the company was not joking.",
