      "summary": "On 29 May 1936 United Press reported five separate investigations under way and a Detroit probe into a women's auxiliary: \"forty-five women members have been found\", and \"the chief function of the auxiliary seems to be tea parties, at which the extermination of Roman Catholics is discussed.\" McCrea asked whether mailing pamphlets \"designed to inflame religious and racial hatred\" broke postal law, to get federal jurisdiction. In early July Reuters reported that a \"message written in human blood\" had been received by the Detroit police — as far as the OCR can be read, signed \"…of the Black Legion — The White Legion\" — while in California a mob of about 100 men and women claiming to be a \"White Legion\" beat the film actor William Haines and a companion at Manhattan Beach and drove them out of town. The odd detail: Tea parties about extermination; a letter to the police written in human blood.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium — wire copy; the blood message's exact wording is garbled in the OCR; the Haines connection is only the wire's speculation.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A successor naming itself in blood within weeks of the arrests — the first sign of who moves into the vacuum. A women's auxiliary nobody prosecuted is the part of the Legion that survived intact.",
