      "summary": "After a Senate committee needled Hoover for never having made an arrest, the Director flew to New Orleans and on 1 May 1936 (his Wikipedia biography gives 2 May) \"personally\" arrested Karpis outside his apartment. Karpis's version: Hoover appeared only after other agents had him covered; the Bureau's: Hoover kept him from reaching a rifle. Nobody had brought handcuffs, and Karpis was tied with an agent's necktie. Harry Campbell was taken in Toledo on 7 May (search-summary date; Medium) — the gang had used Toledo and Cleveland as its base after St. Paul closed. Karpis pled guilty to the Hamm kidnapping in St. Paul, got life, and served 26 years at Alcatraz (1936–62), longer than any other prisoner; paroled 1969, deported to Canada, died at Torremolinos, Spain, 26 August 1979. His erased fingerprints made even his Canadian citizenship a problem. The odd detail: The most famous arrest in the Bureau's history was made without handcuffs; the prisoner's hands had no prints to take.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High; the 1 vs 2 May date differs between pages.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Director flies to New Orleans to make an arrest with his own hands, for the newspapers. What he actually wanted from Karpis — the fixer's ledger, the surgeon's list — is the setting's question.",
