      "summary": "Coughlin presided over a rally at Cleveland Municipal Stadium on 11 May 1936 and predicted his National Union for Social Justice would \"take half of Ohio\" in the primary. On 16 August 1936 the Union Party's convention met in the same stadium and Coughlin endorsed William Lemke; he \"fainted near the end of his speech.\" He had promised his radio audience he would quit broadcasting if he failed to deliver nine million votes for Lemke; the ticket got about 850,000 (Wikipedia's figure), and he did briefly quit. Chicago rallies in the 1936 campaign were not found in pages read this session (lead). The odd detail: The radio priest collapsed on the platform of a lakefront stadium in front of the convention he had made — three months before the December the setting's alpha is built around.",
