      "summary": "Norman \"Red\" Ryan (1895–1936), the \"Jesse James of Canada,\" a Toronto armed robber and safecracker who killed six people in his career, was arrested in Minnesota in 1923 and served life in Kingston Penitentiary, where he became a model prisoner and the face of a parole-reform movement. With Prime Minister R. B. Bennett's backing and press support he was released in 1935 and hosted a radio programme on CFRB denouncing crime. Secretly he had rejoined Edward McMullen and Harry Checkley in a ten-month robbery spree across Ontario. On 23 May 1936 police shot Ryan and Checkley dead during a liquor-store holdup in Sarnia; a constable died too. The Archdiocese of Toronto refused him a Catholic burial. The odd detail: He was on the radio preaching reform while robbing banks.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
