      "summary": "The Steel Workers Organizing Committee was formed on 7 June 1936 in Pittsburgh under Philip Murray. On 2 March 1937 it won a contract with U.S. Steel without a strike — partly by capturing the company unions from inside. It then went after \"Little Steel\" — 186,000 workers at six companies including Republic and Bethlehem — and lost the 1937 strike (see above). The odd detail: The biggest steel company in the world signed quietly; the smaller ones fought with guns — the violence in Chicago was over the holdouts.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. Chicago-district leaders not confirmed this session.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A company union captured from the inside is exactly how a cult would take over a lodge or a parish society — a model the GM can reuse.",
