      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Joseph W. Bolton Jr.'s office in a Loop office building",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(unverified — V2_R06), Chicago (exact building UNVERIFIED this session)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Joseph%20W.%20Bolton%20Jr.%27s%20office%20in%20a%20Loop%20office%20building%20%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Bolton, a broker, had filed for divorce in Jan 1936 on grounds of cruelty (unverified — V2_R06); Mildred (b. 1886) had stalked him for years, police had been called repeatedly, and days before she made a scene at a hotel accusing a woman of impropriety. On 15 June 1936 she walked into his office and shot him several times; he died within hours. She joked with reporters, said she had meant to shoot herself, and told the press \"they just don't convict women for killing in Cook County.\" They did: she was sentenced to death — the first woman sentenced to the electric chair in Cook County — and by one account her head had been partly shaved before Governor Horner commuted the sentence to 199 years. She cut her wrists at the Dwight women's prison and died 29 Aug 1943. The odd detail: Her line — \"they just don't convict women for killing in Cook County\" — was true for a generation (though at least a few, Klimek among them, had drawn life terms); Bolton was the first to find out it had stopped being true.",
