      "theme": "6 (continued). Weather and disasters, and the utilities",
      "entry_type": "event",
      "why_go": "",
      "notes": [
        {
          "label": "Chicago's own numbers",
          "text": "the city's official all-time high is 105°F on 24 Jul 1934 at the University of Chicago station — with an unofficial 109°F at Municipal Airport the same day; in July 1936 the airport logged eight consecutive days of 100°F or more, peaking at 107°F on 11 Jul, with suburbs at 110°F and above. The official thermometer sat by the lake at the University and read low; Midway did not become the official station until 1 Jul 1942 (this is the \"different Chicagos\" of the archive's H11). For the other end of the scale: the largest pre-solstice snowstorm on record dropped 14.8 inches at Midway in December 1929."
        }
      ],
