      "summary": "In the great North American heat wave of July 1936 — as many as 5,000 heat deaths nationwide, concentrated in Chicago, Detroit, St. Louis, Milwaukee, Cleveland and Toronto — Detroit hit 102°F on 9, 10 and 13 July and 104°F on 14 July; Flint reached a record 108°F on 8 July; Grand Rapids 108°F on 13 and 14 July. The Black Legion defendants were in the Wayne County jail awaiting examination through it. The odd detail: Detroit's hottest week fell between the Legion's arrests and its trials — every cell in the county jail at 104 degrees.",
