      "summary": "The Material Service was a purpose-built, steel, diesel, self-unloading \"workbarge\" (239.6 ft, 1,077 GRT, hull #253, built 1929 by Leathem D. Smith at Sturgeon Bay), owned by the Leathem Smith-Putnam Navigation Company and under ten-year lease to the Material Service Corporation of Chicago — Henry Crown's sand-and-gravel company. She was designed low with a lowerable A-frame to pass under the Chicago River bridges, carrying sand from the lake to the river docks. On 29 July 1936, in the record heat summer, she sank off Calumet with the loss of fifteen lives. (The page read gives no survivor count, captain's name, cause or inquiry finding — those are leads.) The odd detail: A vessel built to duck under Chicago's bridges dying on the open lake within sight of the steel mills; fifteen dead in Chicago's own harbour approach in mid-summer, in the same month the heat killed hundreds on shore (see R13).",
