      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Our Lady of Victory Institutions",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Lackawanna, N.Y. (just south of Buffalo, in the Bethlehem Steel town)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Our%20Lady%20of%20Victory%20Institutions%2C%20Lackawanna%2C%20N.Y%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Baker was born in Buffalo in 1842, served briefly with the 74th New York Militia at Gettysburg in July 1863, and was ordained in 1876. He built a \"City of Charity\" of about fifteen buildings — orphanage, protectory, maternity hospital, nurses' school, a 275-bed hospital, a working farm; he is said to have sheltered some 100,000 children, who arrived with \"Father Baker's\" on a tag round the neck. He funded it with the Association of Our Lady of Victory (25 cents a year), built by writing to postmasters across the country for the names of local Catholic women — described as the first direct-mail campaign in the US. The basilica (ground broken 1921, consecrated 25 May 1926 by Bishop William Turner and Cardinal Hayes, made a minor basilica two months later) cost $3.2 million with no parish debt; a 165-foot dome, four 18-foot copper angels, white Georgia marble outside, 40-plus kinds of Italian marble inside, a grotto of black lava rock from Vesuvius. He died on 29 July 1936 at 94; about half a million people came to view the body; Pius XI and President Roosevelt sent condolences and the BBC broadcast the requiem. He was declared Venerable on 14 January 2011; his casket was reinterred in the grotto in 1999 with about 6,000 present. The odd detail: In the Depression, some men who had already been given help would run to the back of the line to be given more; Baker is quoted: \"do you think I will be asked if I gave?\" The Association mailed a nation's worth of small donations to a steel town's back road.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the outline; the half-million figure is from America magazine (other sources give lower numbers for the funeral Mass itself — a search summary says about 50,000 on 3 Aug 1936; UNVERIFIED which figure is which).",
