      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Skeleton found under the Lorain–Carnegie Bridge",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Cleveland, in a burlap bag with a newspaper from June 1936",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Skeleton%20found%20under%20the%20Lorain%E2%80%93Carnegie%20Bridge%2C%20Cleveland%2C%20in%20a%20burlap%20bag%20with%20a%20newspaper%20from%20June%201936"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Wallace, about 40, a Black woman of the Scovill Ave neighbourhood (unverified — V2_R06), disappeared on 21 Aug 1936. Ten months later boys found a skeletonised, decapitated body under the bridge, missing a rib, in a burlap sack with a year-old newspaper. Dental work led to a tentative identification as Wallace, which her son accepted and Coroner Gerber never fully endorsed (unverified — V2_R06). Charles Sadoti later told police she had partnered with a \"one-armed Willie\" — Willie Johnson — two weeks before she vanished; Johnson was executed in 1944 for a separate 1942 torso murder (Margaret Frances Wilson, 19, torso in a trunk) and was Gerber's favoured Torso suspect. The odd detail: The June 1936 newspaper in the sack — either the body sat somewhere for ten months before the dump, or the killer wrapped it in old paper on purpose.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium — the identification was never formally confirmed; the Johnson link is circumstantial. • Wallace, about 40, a Black woman of the Scovill Ave neighbourhood (unverified — V2_R06), disappeared on 21 Aug 1936. • Dental work led to a tentative identification as Wallace, which her son accepted and Coroner Gerber never fully endorsed (unverified — V2_R06).",
