      "summary": "Chicago-born (23 December 1860), sister-in-law of the architect John Wellborn Root (she wrote his biography, 1896), Monroe founded Poetry on 23 September 1912 by persuading one hundred Chicago businessmen to pledge fifty dollars a year for five years, edited it unpaid while reviewing art for the Tribune, and printed Pound, Eliot, Stevens and Williams though she personally preferred nineteenth-century forms. On the way to Machu Picchu in September 1936 the altitude brought on a cerebral haemorrhage and she died at Arequipa on 26 September — three months before the setting's December 1936 eclipse. The odd detail: One hundred Chicago businessmen at fifty dollars each financed modernist poetry; the magazine's guarantors' list is a roll of the city's rich.",
