      "summary": "Weather: no rows at the Midway station for these dates (record gap). The 5th opened at 76°/55°, so the first days of the month were most likely mild (est.). Sky: Moon waning from full (30 Sep) — a bright gibbous moon rising mid-evening. In the papers: the World Series (Yankees v. Giants, 30 Sep – 6 Oct): on 2 Oct \"New York Yankees set World Series single-game runs record with 18\" (Wikipedia, October 1936). 1 Oct: \"Al Smith endorsed Alf Landon for President.\" 3 Oct: John Heisman died — the Downtown Athletic Club's trophy was then renamed for him; its first winner (1935) was Jay Berwanger of the University of Chicago (Wikipedia, Jay Berwanger, search snippet). Spain: Franco invested as Chief of State at Burgos (1 Oct); the \"fifth column\" phrase first used publicly (3 Oct); Battle of Cable Street, London (4 Oct). In the city: Sat 3 Oct — Northwestern 18, Iowa 7 at Dyche Stadium, Evanston (season opener); Notre Dame 21, Carnegie Tech 7 at South Bend. Sun 4 Oct — the Bears won 27–9 at Pittsburgh (Forbes Field, 29,000) to go 3–0 ( ; ; ). Context all month: Chicago's public schools had reopened late in September after the summer's polio epidemic, with lessons read over the radio (\"In response to a polio outbreak, Chicago Public Schools launches a distance education program\", 13 Sep — Wikipedia 1936, search snippet).",
