      "summary": "Cardinal Secretary of State (and Camerlengo) Eugenio Pacelli — the future Pius XII — landed in New York on 8 Oct 1936 off the Italian liner Conte di Savoia, the highest-ranking Catholic official ever to visit the US. Fr. Francis Spellman organised most of the trip, including a chartered United Airlines plane from Roosevelt Field that took him coast to coast; the press called him \"the Flying Cardinal.\" At Notre Dame he took an honorary doctorate; in Chicago he met Cardinal Mundelein; Cleveland was also on the air route. On 5 Nov, the day after FDR's re-election, he lunched with Roosevelt at Hyde Park for two hours. He sailed on 6 Nov and was back in Rome on 14 Nov. The odd detail: The Vatican's number-two man spent the 1936 election campaign flying across the Midwest in a chartered airliner — he was in the air over the Great Lakes six weeks before the setting's alpha adventure (Dec 1936).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the tour; the exact Chicago date and programme UNVERIFIED (article lists the city, not the day).",
