      "summary": "Twelve men were tried for Poole's murder; one was acquitted and eleven convicted. Life for first-degree murder: Dayton Dean, \"Colonel\" Harvey Davis, Ervin D. Lee, Urban Lipps, Paul R. Edwards, Edgar Baldwin, John Bannerman and Lowell Rushing. Second-degree: Thomas R. Craig (10–20 years), Virgil Morrow (5–15), Albert Stevens (7½–15), John S. Vincent (3–10). Reuters reported from Detroit on 8 October that Dean \"was sentenced to life imprisonment to-day\", the seventh Legionnaire found guilty of first-degree murder, and that at trial he still alleged Poole had ill-treated his wife before the birth — which the widow denied; the Legion's \"Colonel\" Davis and six others drew life the same week. On 21 December five Legionnaires were convicted of Silas Coleman's murder — the wire called Coleman \"a negro war veteran\" and said his body \"had 18 bullet wounds\". Dean died in prison on 18 January 1960; Urban Lipps's sentence was commuted in June 1960 by Governor G. Mennen Williams; Ervin Lee's in 1966 by Governor George Romney. The odd detail: The killer kept repeating the wife-beating lie in open court, months after the widow had buried it.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. The acquitted twelfth man is not named in the sources read — UNVERIFIED. Coleman as a \"war veteran\" is the Reuters wording only.",
