      "summary": "Weather: 21st 68°/44°, 0.36 in.; 22nd 47°/35°; 23rd 47°/34°. Sky: first quarter Fri 23 Oct, 6:54 am. In the papers: Pacelli \"spent the night of October 21 in the capital\" and toured Washington on the 22nd, with an hour's stop in Baltimore ( ). 21 Oct — Pan American opened weekly passenger service San Francisco–Manila via Honolulu. 22 Oct — James J. Couzens died (Detroit: Ford's first business manager, then Detroit's mayor and Michigan's Republican senator who backed the New Deal). 23 Oct — \"Hitler ordered Condor Legion to Spain\"; the USSR renounced Spanish neutrality. In the city: Fri 23 Oct — the first issue of Coronet (cover-dated November 1936) went on sale — a pocket \"general interest digest\" from Esquire, Inc., David A. Smart's Chicago publishing house ( ). Chicago was the home of Esquire itself in 1936.",
