      "summary": "The Vatican Secretary of State sailed from Naples on 1 October 1936 on the Conte di Savoia with the Italian ambassador Fulvio Suvich, Prince Marcello Gentili del Drago and Bishop Hugh Lamb, landed in New York on 8 October (met by Cardinal Hayes and the Apostolic Delegate Cicognani) and was the guest of Genevieve Garvan Brady at Inisfada, Manhasset. Bishop Francis Spellman \"had boarded the ocean liner even before Pacelli disembarked\" and ran the trip. Boston 12–15 October (Coughlin was in Boston at the same time; they did not meet); Knights of Columbus headquarters, New Haven, 18 October; Washington 21–22 October (Catholic University, the Library of Congress, Mount Vernon, Georgetown, a broadcast speech at the National Press Club, a brief stop at Baltimore's cathedral). On 23 October he left Roosevelt Field, Long Island, in a chartered United Airlines plane for a coast-to-coast tour — \"the Flying Cardinal\" — that took in South Bend (honorary doctorate at Notre Dame; Bishop John Noll and president John F. O'Hara), Chicago (where he met Cardinal Mundelein), St. Paul, San Francisco, Los Angeles, then Cleveland, Cincinnati, Syracuse and St. Louis, ending in New York on 1 November with Mass at St. Ignatius Loyola and a Fordham doctorate. On 5 November, the day after the election, he lunched two hours with Roosevelt at Hyde Park; Spellman told the press they were forbidden to ask about Coughlin. He sailed on 6 November from Pier 59 after seeing Mayor La Guardia and Al Smith, and was in Rome on 14 November. The odd detail: The one question the press was forbidden to ask the Vatican's foreign minister was about a Detroit parish priest.",
