      "id": "R24-019",
      "title": "Tue 3 November — ELECTION DAY",
      "date_start": "",
      "date_end": "",
      "date_label": "Tue 3 November",
      "category": "November 1936",
      "tags": [
        "R24",
        "calendar"
      ],
      "people": [],
      "places": [],
      "summary": "Weather: 60°/31° — a mild, dry polling day that turned cold overnight (the 4th was 38°/28°). Sky: sunrise 6:25 am, sunset 4:42 pm CST; waning gibbous 74%, rising 8:58 pm CST — on Chicago's EST clocks 7:25 am, 5:42 pm and 9:58 pm — the moon came up as the returns came in. The result: Roosevelt 27,752,648 (60.8%) and 523 electoral votes; Landon 16,681,862 (36.54%) and 8 — \"Maine and Vermont only\"; Lemke's Union Party 892,378 (1.95%). Illinois: Roosevelt 2,282,999 (57.70%), Landon 1,570,393 (39.69%). . Governor: Henry Horner (D) 2,067,861 (53.13%) over C. Wayland Brooks (R) 1,682,685 (43.24%) — Horner had beaten the Kelly–Nash machine's man, Dr. Herman Bundesen of the Board of Health, in the April primary (820,313 to 659,221; Bundesen \"carried Cook County\", Horner won downstate) after vetoing the machine's bookmaking bill; re-elected, he \"was now determined to defeat the Kelly-Nash machine.\" ; . Bridgeport sent Richard J. Daley to Springfield on the dead Speaker's line (see 18 Oct). Frank Knox of the Daily News lost the vice-presidency. In the papers, next morning: The Daily Banner, 4 Nov, p. 1: \"DEMOCRATIC TICKET SWEPT INTO OFFICE WITH ROOSEVELT LANDSLIDE\"; \"Franklin Delano Roosevelt Re-Elected President By Voters Of United States\"; \"Landon Smiles In His Defeat\"; \"President Is Highly Elated\"; Indiana: \"Clifford Townsend Elected Governor Indiana Over Springer\". . Also 3 Nov: \"Edward VIII opened Parliament\"; Miyagi earthquake; Swiss cantons banned the Communist party.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Imported original calendar research; retain estimates, missing observations and time-zone caveats in the source.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — election night is the one night the whole city's police are on the precincts and the whole Secret Service detail is on the President-elect's wire; a good night for something else to happen.",
