      "id": "R20-028",
      "title": "The Granville crash — a North Shore express telescopes an old wooden \"L\" car",
      "date_start": "1936-11-24",
      "date_end": "1936-11-24",
      "date_label": "24 Nov 1936",
      "category": "The city's guts: power, water, gas, sewers, tunnels, bridges, digging, building and demolition (Chicago and the Great Lakes, 1925–1940)",
      "tags": [
        "utilities",
        "building",
        "Granville",
        "North Shore"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Van Grooms",
        "the Granville towerman"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "The North Side \"L\" embankment near Granville station",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(Edgewater), on the evening run",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=The%20North%20Side%20%22L%22%20embankment%20near%20Granville%20station%20%2C%20on%20the%20evening%20run%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The Granville towerman set the signal against the Loyola Express — \"contrary to custom\" — and let a local onto track 3; a three-car North Shore Line Mundelein Express (lead car #721) ran into the rear. \"The 50-ton North Shore car, no match for the thirty-plus year old wooden car, telescoped right through it.\" Nine \"L\" passengers died at once, a tenth the next day; 36 were seriously injured; riders were thrown into the alley on the east side of the embankment and onto the tracks to the west. The inquiry blamed North Shore motorman Van Grooms for following too closely, and recommended automatic block signals, the end of wooden cars, and no more mixed wooden-and-steel trains. The odd detail: In 1936 Chicago's \"L\" still ran wooden cars from before 1906 — and the dead fell from the embankment into a residential alley.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Why did the towerman set the signal against custom? A towerman under a compulsion — or paid. One of the ten dead was carrying something an FBI Section 13 agent was sent to the alley to find before the police did.",
