      "summary": "Reuther was fired by Ford in 1932 (Ford's record said he quit) after organising a rally for the Socialist Norman Thomas. He and his brother Victor then worked for nearly two years at the auto plant in Gorky, in the Soviet Union, teaching Soviet workers the machines and the assembly line — writing letters to the Moscow Daily News about the plant's inefficiencies — and came home the long way, through Turkey, Iran, British India and China, bicycling across Japan, and sailing on the SS President Harding. Back in Detroit he became president of the West Side Local 174 and in December 1936 led its first successful strike, a ten-day sit-down at Kelsey-Hayes; the local grew from 200 members to 35,000 within a year. The odd detail: The man Ford's guards beat on the overpass had learned mass production by teaching it in a factory on the Volga.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
