      "people": [],
      "places": [],
      "summary": "Weather: 40°/21°. In the papers: \"Edward VIII signed the Instrument of Abdication\" at Fort Belvedere, witnessed by his three brothers; Nobel prizes given in Stockholm; Luigi Pirandello died. Indiana's evening papers had it the same day: Decatur Daily Democrat, 10 Dec, p. 1: \"KING EDWARD VIII ABDICATES\" ( ); The Daily Banner, p. 3: \"KING ABDICATES\"; the Walkerton Independent, in its \"Did You Know That\" filler column (a quip, not a headline): \"King Edward Abdicates; just another good man gone wrong!\" ( ). ; In the city: Black Hawks lost 1–2 at the Maroons. The evening papers (Daily News, American, Times) had the abdication in the Loop by afternoon. - Divergence note: BENDS. The abdication happens in a Britain that lost the Great War and pays reparations to Berlin. The crown of a defeated, indebted empire — its colonies \"bought cheap\" by Washington — abdicating over an American divorcée is a bigger, stranger story: Chicago's Irish would enjoy it more, its Anglophile Gold Coast less, and the German press (Berlin's) would gloat. The Walkerton line, \"just another good man gone wrong,\" is the Midwestern reading either way.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Imported original calendar research; retain estimates, missing observations and time-zone caveats in the source.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the setting's vampires \"conspire in high society\" — a King who gives up a throne for a woman from Baltimore is the kind of story that makes the Gold Coast's older residents smile.",
