      "people": [],
      "places": [],
      "summary": "Weather: 36°/22°, dry. Sky: first quarter moon 5:30 am. The December solstice fell at 6:27 pm CST on Monday 21 December 1936 (USNO seasons table for 1936 at UTC−6: \"Solstice – December 21, 1936 at 18:27\" — ). That is 00:27 UT on the 22nd — so by Greenwich, Rome and every printed almanac using Universal Time the solstice is \"22 December 1936,\" which is what the owner's source (takemeback.to, quoted in Docs/sources/period-research-notes.md) gave. Both are right; the alpha's \"Tonight's December 22nd\" can stand as the solstice night if the GM reads it as the night after the instant, or the line can be spoken on the evening of the 21st. The 21st and 22nd are within a second of each other in day-length (9 h 08 m). In the papers: the Supreme Court's Curtiss-Wright decision (presidential power over foreign arms sales); the Junkers Ju 88's first flight; Cuba's president vetoed the army's sugar tax.",
