      "places": [],
      "summary": "Weather: 55°/33°, dry — a spring-like afternoon; coats open on State Street. Sky: sunrise 7:16 am, sunset 4:24 pm, civil twilight ends 4:56 pm; moon 84%, risen 1:17 pm, on the meridian 8:51 pm, setting 4:32 am on Christmas morning (USNO; the 3:18 am set listed for the 24th is the previous night's moon) — a bright, nearly full moon over the Midnight Masses. In the papers: Pope Pius XI's Christmas message; Cuba's Miguel Mariano Gómez impeached by the army's congress; the Villarreal offensive failed; the Spanish coast guard seized the German steamer Palos (the \"Palos incident\" that opens January's news); The Jungle Princess (Dorothy Lamour's sarong) released. In the city: Midnight Mass at Holy Name Cathedral (State and Superior) — the real annual event under the alpha's Christmas Eve; who celebrated it in 1936 (Mundelein or an auxiliary) is UNVERIFIED. The Christmas trees of the Loop hotels; the Tribune's and Daily News's charity funds; the last-minute crowds at Field's, Carson's and the Fair. General, not year-specific.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Imported original calendar research; retain estimates, missing observations and time-zone caveats in the source.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the alpha's Mass is on a warm night with a bright moon — a GM can lose the snow. The alpha's cardinal is the one thing to fix: the real host was Mundelein (Stritch came in 1940), and Pacelli had sailed on 6 November.",
