      "summary": "On 28 December 1936 workers at Cleveland's Fisher Body plant struck \"due to two brothers being fired from the assembly line.\" The United Auto Workers then declared it would not settle Cleveland until GM signed a national agreement for all plants — which pushed forward the plan to shut Fisher Body No. 1 in Flint. The Flint sit-down began two days later (30 December; see R07). The Cleveland sit-down ended in February 1937. ECH credits Communist organisers — Wyndham Mortimer's Cleveland District Automobile Council (nine locals) — with building the union at Fisher Body and White Motor. The odd detail: The great Flint sit-down that made the UAW began because two brothers were fired in Cleveland.",
