      "summary": "UAW men occupied the Fisher Body plants that made the dies for GM's lines, and later seized Chevrolet No. 4, which built every Chevrolet engine; the 44-day strike idled more than 136,000 GM workers. On 11 January 1937 police stormed Fisher Body 2 with guns and tear gas and were driven back with hinges, bottles and bolts — the strikers called it \"the Battle of the Running Bulls\" (14 workers wounded). Genora Johnson (later Dollinger) organised the women's Emergency Brigade. Governor Frank Murphy sent in the National Guard to keep police and company guards off the strikers rather than evict them. The one-page settlement recognised the UAW; its membership rose from 30,000 to 500,000 within a year. The odd detail: \"Running Bulls\" — the strikers' own mocking name for the police in retreat.",
