      "summary": "Weather: 38°/28°, dry — cold enough for coats, no snow on the ground. Sky: sunrise 7:18 am, sunset 4:29 pm; waning gibbous 85%, rising 8:49 pm — the moon comes up as the parties start and is high at midnight. In the papers: \"Aceituna Campaign ended in Nationalist victory\"; Lebanon founded its Order of the Cedar; Miguel de Unamuno died at Salamanca; \"Doc\" Casey died. Sonja Henie's first picture, One in a Million, opened (general knowledge; not on the list fetched). In the city (venues real; 1936 bookings UNVERIFIED unless noted): the Palmer House Empire Room (17 E. Monroe; \"Shep Fields\" was among its 1930s bands — ); the Stevens on Michigan Avenue, 3,000 rooms, \"the largest hotel in the world\" and in receivership — \"like four out of five American hotels during the Great Depression, the Stevens Hotel went bankrupt\" ( ); the Edgewater Beach Hotel's Marine Dining Room, 5300 N. Sheridan, with its own broadcasts ( ); the Aragon on Lawrence and the Trianon on Cottage Grove (WGN's nightly hour — ); the Blackhawk restaurant on Wabash, where Kay Kyser's band was \"popular … where Kyser came up with an act combining a quiz with music\" ( ; whether he was there this particular night UNVERIFIED); the College Inn at the Sherman; the Drake; the Congress's Joseph Urban Room (general knowledge). The alpha's own detail — \"the Drake Hotel… the New Year's Eve party last year… 2,000 movers and shakers\" (R12 §3a) — is the owner's fiction built on a real hotel.",
