      "summary": "In the 30 May 1937 Memorial Day Massacre, ten protesters were killed, 40 shot and more than 90 wounded by police; the Chicago History Museum records two Mexicans: Max Guzmán, 26, a steelworker struck on the head, detained at the South Chicago station for up to three days without medical care, fingerprinted, questioned about communist ties and threatened with deportation; and Guadalupe \"Lupe\" Gallardo Marshall, 31, an immigrant and Hull-House volunteer social worker, who was among about 200 women in the march and was beaten while helping unconscious protesters. (R37 has Earl Handley; Rosales & Simon report Mexicans were up to 75 percent of the Inland/Youngstown picket in East Chicago and 6 of 24 East Chicagoans injured were Mexican.) The odd detail: a Hull-House social worker was beaten at the plant gate, in the district her clients lived in.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Guzmán was fingerprinted, questioned and threatened for three days — and released; whoever kept the sheet on him has a file on a Mexican steelworker that later matters.",
