      "summary": "In 1937 Milwaukee had 54 major strikes involving more than 10,000 workers: 34 by AFL unions (including a 65-day Checker Cab walkout) and 20 by the new CIO (including a 50-day strike at Bucyrus-Erie in South Milwaukee). Twenty-nine of the fifty strikes of 1937–38 were sit-downs, until the Supreme Court declared the tactic illegal. Union membership went from about 20,000 (1930) to more than 100,000 (1939), \"with virtually every major plant unionized.\" The same year juvenile delinquency rose 34% and the Common Council banned pinball machines and barred anyone under 21 from taverns; the WPA had 24,000 county residents on its rolls by 1938; the federal Parklawn project (518 units) opened in 1937 and the greenbelt town of Greendale (572 houses, eight miles south-west) in 1938; 21 \"toy libraries\" opened from 1937. The odd detail: In the year every factory sat down, the city's answer to its teenagers was to outlaw pinball.",
