      "summary": "Columbus's Near East Side was a destination for thousands of Black migrants from the Civil War through the Great Migration, but segregation and overcrowding produced deteriorating conditions. After the 1937 Federal Housing Act, the Columbus Metropolitan Housing Authority razed a slum called \"the Blackberry Patch\" and built Poindexter Village, the city's first public housing and among the first in the country, complete in 1940: 400 units for African American families, named for Rev. James M. Poindexter, minister of Second Baptist Church and the first Black member of Columbus City Council. A search summary says President Roosevelt dedicated it on 12 October 1940 (unverified — the summary is the only source). A business district of Black-owned firms grew beside it. The odd detail: The slum that was razed had a name from a bramble that grows in ruins.",
