      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Cincinnati",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Louisville, Evansville, Paducah, Cairo, Shawneetown and Harrisburg, Illinois",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Cincinnati%2C%20Louisville%2C%20Evansville%2C%20Paducah%2C%20Cairo%2C%20Shawneetown%20and%20Harrisburg%2C%20Illinois"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The Ohio crested at 79.9 feet at Cincinnati on 26 Jan 1937 (the city's all-time record; 12 square miles flooded), 57 feet at Louisville on 27 Jan (70% of the city under water; 175,000 evacuated), 54 feet at Evansville (23–24 Jan; martial law), 60.8 feet at Paducah (2 Feb), and 65.4 feet at Shawneetown, Illinois, which was afterward moved three miles inland. Harrisburg, Illinois, was 80% under water with 4,000 homeless; at Frankfort, Kentucky, over 3,000 prisoners were evacuated during riots. Deaths: 385; a million homeless; $500 million damage. Radio stations WLW (Cincinnati) and WHAS (Louisville) went commercial-free as emergency relays, and WSAZ (Huntington) got FCC leave to broadcast round the clock from 22 Jan. Carousel pieces from Cincinnati's Coney Island fetched up at Paducah. The WPA put thousands of men into rescue work. The odd detail: a whole Illinois town abandoned to the river and rebuilt on higher ground; horses from a carousel washing up 400 miles downstream.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Old Shawneetown, drowned and abandoned in 1937, still stands on the bank — a real ghost town at the state's southern tip, one winter old when the game is set.",
