      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Flint",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Michigan — Fisher Body No. 1 and No. 2, Chevrolet No. 4",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Flint%2C%20Michigan%20%E2%80%94%20Fisher%20Body%20No.%201%20and%20No.%202%2C%20Chevrolet%20No.%204"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Genora Albro Johnson (born Kalamazoo 20 April 1913), a 23-year-old strike wife, organised the Women's Auxiliary (food, childcare, a first-aid post) and on the night of 11 January 1937 — the \"Battle of the Running Bulls,\" when Flint police tried to retake Fisher No. 2 with tear gas and buckshot — took the sound-car microphone and called the women of Flint to break through the police line. She then founded the Women's Emergency Brigade: red berets and armbands, and under their winter coats bats, cudgels, chains and whips; she refused the word \"women\" in the name \"lest someone thought they were there to feed and clothe, not fight in the streets.\" On 1 February the Brigade fronted the diversion at Chevrolet No. 9 that let the men seize Chevrolet No. 4. GM signed on 11 February. Johnson was blacklisted, became chief steward of an all-women UAW local at Briggs in Detroit, and in 1945 was beaten with a lead pipe in her home — Senator Kefauver's later inquiry found the attack on her and other UAW officers had been bought from organised crime. Press called her \"the Joan of Arc of labor.\" The odd detail: The Brigade carried chains and whips under respectable winter coats, and wore red berets so the men inside could count them from the windows.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Fifty women in red berets who will break windows for a cause are the best militia a Hunter Guild recruiter ever saw; the 1945 lead-pipe attack shows who else noticed.",
