      "summary": "Dr. Bernard Fantus (b. Budapest 1 Sep 1874, MD Chicago 1899), director of the hospital's Department of Pharmacology and Therapeutics, opened the first hospital blood bank in the United States on 15 Mar 1937. It cost $1,500 to set up (unverified — V_R13) and handled 1,354 transfusions in its first year; the hospital had been doing about 40 transfusions a month, and within three months was doing 400 (unverified — V_R13). Fantus published the working plan in the Journal of the American Medical Association on 10 Jul 1937 so any hospital could copy it. He had read Russian and Spanish reports on storing blood. Earlier (1910–15) he had invented \"candy medication\" — sweet tablets for children — and his 1915 book of formulas included aspirin, heroin and cocaine. He died 14 Apr 1940. The odd detail: the phrase \"blood bank\" was his daughter Ruth's — \"everyone in society knows how a bank works.\" Blood was literally deposited and withdrawn: a donor's family earned credit against a later need.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High. • It cost $1,500 to set up (unverified — V_R13) and handled 1,354 transfusions in its first year; • the hospital had been doing about 40 transfusions a month, and within three months was doing 400 (unverified — V_R13).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A refrigerator on Harrison Street holding ten days of stored blood, credited by name, opened in March 1937 — the single most vampire-relevant address in the city. Who made the first large \"deposit,\" and why did the ledger not balance?",
