      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Ponce",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "at the corner of Marina and Aurora streets, 3:15 pm",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Ponce%2C%20at%20the%20corner%20of%20Marina%20and%20Aurora%20streets%2C%203%3A15%20pm"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The Nationalist Party planned a Palm Sunday march to commemorate abolition of slavery in Puerto Rico (1873) and protest Albizu Campos's imprisonment. Governor Blanton Winship had Police Chief Enrique de Orbeta pressure Mayor José Tormos Diego into cancelling the legally granted permit shortly before the march, and had police from around the island concentrated in Ponce — some 200 heavily armed, including squads with Thompson submachine guns, machine guns, rifles and tear gas. When the cadets marched anyway, police fired for more than 15 minutes into the marchers and bystanders. 19 died (17 civilians, including a 12-year-old girl, Georgina Maldonado; and two members of the forces, hit by their own side) and over 200 were wounded; most were shot in the back and none was found armed. Bolívar Márquez Telechea, a cadet, wrote with his own blood on the wall: \"¡Viva la República, Abajo los asesinos!\" A newspaper photographer, Carlos Torres Morales (El Imparcial), took photographs that Luis Muñoz Marín studied and described as showing police shooting at \"a terrified crowd in full flight.\" Winship radioed Washington that the Nationalists fired first; the New York Times and Washington Post first reported a \"Nationalist political revolt.\" An independent ACLU commission led by Arthur Garfield Hays arrived 13 May and read its report in San Juan's Plaza de Armas on 22 May, calling it \"a massacre and mob action by the police.\" No officer was convicted, demoted or suspended. Rep. Vito Marcantonio filed charges against Winship; he was removed 12 May 1939. The odd detail: a dying cadet's message written in his own blood on a wall — the sentence became the Nationalist rallying cry.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (death count: 17 civilians plus 2 police is the standard figure; the search summary said \"17 civilians, one policeman and one Guardsman\").",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — three layers of truth in one afternoon. Layer 1: a Nationalist revolt. Layer 2: 19 dead, 200 wounded, a photograph. Layer 3: who wanted the march to happen at all. The march for slavery's abolition on Palm Sunday, in a city with a Cathedral, is a ready-made investigation set-piece for a Catholic-order character (IHS).",
