      "summary": "From 8 to 23 April 1937 more than 4,000 workers struck GM at Oshawa for an eight-hour day, better pay, seniority and recognition of the United Automobile Workers (a CIO affiliate). Ontario's Premier Mitchell Hepburn backed the company and created his own force of about 300 special constables — jeered as \"Sons of Mitches\" and \"Hepburn's Hussars\" (one search summary says they were given riot training and told to shoot for the knees; university students were among the recruits). Two of his own ministers — Attorney General Arthur Roebuck and Labour Minister David Croll — resigned in protest. Oshawa's Mayor Alex Hall refused to let the force or the RCMP into the city and became an honorary member of Local 222. On 23 April GM accepted many demands without formally recognising the union; the strikers afterwards publicly renounced the CIO to secure their terms. The odd detail: A Premier raised a private police force partly from university students (per a search summary) and lost two cabinet ministers over it.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on dates and names; Medium on the knee-shots and 300 figure.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A private police force hired for a month from among students is a made-to-order occult hunting party; the mayor who bars the gate is a fine ally for players.",
