      "summary": "The Steel Workers' Organizing Committee struck the \"Little Steel\" independents — Republic, Youngstown Sheet & Tube, Inland — on 26 May 1937. Mayor Burton revoked Republic's permit for its St. Clair Ave. airfield, which the company was using to fly supplies to non-strikers. Governor Martin Davey sent the National Guard to protect the reopening of the Republic mills on 6 July; Cuyahoga County Sheriff Martin O'Donnell declared military rule beforehand. Ness met the union; the Guard left 16 July; violence between workers broke out at Corrigan-McKinney around 26 July; the strike faded by the end of August. Contracts came only in the early 1940s after NLRB rulings. (Chicago's Memorial Day Massacre at Republic's South Chicago plant, 30 May 1937, belongs to the same strike.) The odd detail: A steel company ran an airlift into its own besieged mill — and the mayor shut its airfield.",
