      "summary": "The single national oil workers' union — the Sindicato de Trabajadores Petroleros de la República Mexicana (STPRM) — was founded in 1935 (Spanish Wikipedia: 15 August 1935; the union's own history, per search summary: 27 December 1935 — conflict, both recorded). Demanding a collective contract, a 40-hour week, full pay during illness and 65 million pesos in wages and benefits, it struck on 28 May 1937; for about twelve to thirteen days gasoline vanished from the pumps and industry nearly stopped, until Cárdenas asked the union to lift the strike on 9 June 1937. The labour board awarded 26 million pesos on 18 December 1937; the Supreme Court confirmed it on 1 March 1938 with a deadline of 7 March; the companies refused; the expropriation followed on 18 March (M01). The odd detail: for twelve days the country that pumped the Golden Lane had no gasoline.",
