      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Sam's Place",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "a former tavern and dance hall at 113th Street and Green Bay Avenue (SWOC headquarters), then across the open prairie toward the Republic Steel mill, South Chic",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Sam%27s%20Place%2C%20a%20former%20tavern%20and%20dance%20hall%20at%20113th%20Street%20and%20Green%20Bay%20Avenue%20%2C%20then%20across%20the%20open%20prairie%20toward%20the%20Republic%20Steel%20mill%2C%20South%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Four days into the \"Little Steel\" strike (called 26 May 1937 against Republic, Youngstown Sheet & Tube and Inland Steel; 67,000 out within days), a holiday rally of 1,500–2,500 strikers, families, sympathisers and passers-by at Sam's Place marched across the prairie toward the Republic plant to set up a picket line. About 300 Chicago police blocked them and opened fire. Ten demonstrators died — four on the spot, six of their wounds: Hilding Anderson, Alfred Causey, Leo Francisco, Earl Handley, Otis Jones, Sam Popovich, Kenneth Reed, Joseph Rothmund, Anthony Tagliori and Lee Tisdale. At least 30 were wounded by gunfire and 67+ injured in all; nine were permanently disabled and 28 had serious head wounds from clubs; no police died. A coroner's jury ruled the killings \"justifiable homicide,\" and no officer was prosecuted. The Little Steel strike killed 18 in all (Chicago, Youngstown, Massillon) and collapsed after five months; Little Steel was unionised by 1942 and Republic had to pay $20 million back pay to men blacklisted in 1937. The odd detail: A Paramount News cameraman, Orlando Lippert, filmed it. Paramount's editor judged the footage \"not fit to be seen,\" fearing it could \"incite local riot\"; the Chicago police illegally banned it from Chicago theatres and Paramount agreed not to show it elsewhere — while releasing edited clips that made the strikers look like a mob. The La Follette Civil Liberties Committee screened the full reel in Washington on 2 July 1937 with Lippert testifying. It is now on the National Film Registry (1997).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the event and names; wounded counts vary by source.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A reel of film the police banned and a Senate committee screened behind closed doors — what else is on the reel, in the frames nobody printed? Section 13 has a copy.",
