      "id": "R17-016",
      "title": "The field — Sam's Place to the Republic gate, Sunday afternoon 30 May 1937",
      "date_start": "1937-05-30",
      "date_end": "1937-05-30",
      "date_label": "30 May 1937",
      "category": "Deep dossiers on the owner's own seven Chicago mission seeds",
      "tags": [
        "owner seeds",
        "Sam's Place",
        "Republic",
        "Sunday"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Capt. James Mooney",
        "Capt. Thomas Kilroy",
        "Lupe Marshall",
        "Max Guzmán"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Sam's Place",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "a former dance hall (some say tavern) at 113th St and Green Bay Ave, the union's temporary headquarters",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Sam%27s%20Place%2C%20a%20former%20dance%20hall%20%20at%20113th%20St%20and%20Green%20Bay%20Ave%2C%20the%20union%27s%20temporary%20headquarters%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "the prairie between it and the Republic Steel gate at 117th and Burley",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20prairie%20between%20it%20and%20the%20Republic%20Steel%20gate%20at%20117th%20and%20Burley%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "After a rally at Sam's Place some 1,500–2,500 people — strikers, wives (about 200 women), children, sympathisers and the curious; white, Black and Mexican — set off across the open ground toward the mill to picket. A line of police (the Tribune said 150; the Chicago History Museum counts 264; Wikipedia ~300) under Capt. James Mooney and Capt. Thomas Kilroy stopped them. Kilroy: \"You can't get through here … We must do our duty.\" Tear gas, then gunfire, then clubs; the police said the crowd shot first. Ten died: Hilding Anderson, 27 (d. 3 June); Alfred Causey, 43; Leo Francisco, 17 (d. 15 June); Earl Handley, 37 (the Tribune's \"Earl Hanley,\" the only one identified by nightfall); Otis Jones, 33 (d. 8 June); Sam Popovich, 45; Kenneth Reed, 23; Joseph Rothmund, 48 (d. 31 May); Anthony Tagliori, 26 (d. 1 June); Lee Tisdale, 50 (d. 19 June). Guadalupe \"Lupe\" Marshall, 31, a Hull-House volunteer social worker, was clubbed to the ground; Max Guzmán, 26, a Mexican steelworker, was struck on the head; at the South Chicago station police held injured marchers for up to three days (the Chicago History Museum does not say Guzmán himself was held). Injured marchers were thrown into patrol wagons and driven to hospitals without first aid. The odd detail: a seventeen-year-old and a fifty-year-old among the dead; and a Hull-House social worker who came to the Senate a month later still wearing the suit bloodied on the field.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Hull-House (Jane Addams dead two years) sends a social worker into a labour field; IHS could as easily have sent a priest. Who else was on the prairie that afternoon, and why did the police fire when the film shows nobody had?",
