      "summary": "The Chicago Tribune reported that Earl Hanley (the spelling R03 gives is Handley), 37, \"3307 Michigan avenue, Indiana Harbor, East Chicago,\" a carpenter for Inland Steel at Indiana Harbor, died of a fractured skull in the Memorial Day clash — the only one of the ten dead with an identified Indiana Harbor address in the account read. Three other victims were at first unidentified, \"which lent support to reports that outside agitators played a leading part in the raid on the mill.\" Inland Steel was one of the three \"Little Steel\" firms struck by the SWOC on 26 May 1937, so the dead carpenter's own company was on strike; 22,000 steelworkers were out in the Chicago area. The odd detail: The victim came from the other side of the state line — an Inland Steel man at a Republic Steel picket, a mile across the Calumet, killed by Chicago police — and the press's first reaction was that the unidentified dead must be outsiders.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (single newspaper quotation; the page itself gives only four identified dead).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Calumet steel towns' link across the state line is a fact of union life; a carpenter from Michigan Avenue killed at the wrong mill is a character a Gary investigator could be hired to trace — a widow, a union card, a name that isn't quite right.",
