      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Lansing",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Ingham County, Michigan — City Hall, the State Capitol, the Capital City Wrecking Company",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Lansing%2C%20Ingham%20County%2C%20Michigan%20%E2%80%94%20City%20Hall%2C%20the%20State%20Capitol%2C%20the%20Capital%20City%20Wrecking%20Company"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "A strike at the Capital City Wrecking Company began 21 May 1937. On 1 June an Ingham County Circuit Court judge issued an injunction against the strikers, which they largely ignored, and Sheriff Allan MacDonald responded with middle-of-the-night arrests at about 2 a.m. on 7 June, jailing UAW Local 182 president Lester Washburn, his wife Nevah and other organisers (eight or nine people, sources differ) (unverified — V_R41: a local-history summary says the sheriff found only Nevah Washburn at home that night; libcom confirms eight prisoners). Union members — between 12,000 and 15,000 — stopped work (unverified — V_R41: sources give Local 182 as roughly 15,000 members but only 2,000–5,000 in the streets; no source read gives a stoppage total): cars and trucks were parked to block streets, stores closed, \"flying squadrons\" arrived from out of town, and thousands occupied City Hall and the State Capitol demanding the prisoners' release. Governor Frank Murphy came to mediate and addressed the crowd at 2:30 p.m.; three prisoners were freed on $200 bonds and the other five on bail after 5 p.m. Capital City Wrecking signed a contract recognising the UAW the next day. Washburn paid a $250 fine for violating the injunction (unverified — V_R41: not on libcom or in any source I could reach). The odd detail: A capital city of 12,000 to 15,000 strikers was shut down over a strike at a wrecking yard — and the governor was the one who had to come and talk them out of the Capitol.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on dates and outline (two sources agree); numbers vary. • … with middle-of-the-night arrests at about 2 a.m. on 7 June, jailing UAW Local 182 president Lester Washburn, his wife Nevah and other organisers (eight or nine people, sources differ) (unverified — V_R41: a local-history summary says the sheriff found only Nevah Washburn at home that night; libcom confirms eight prisoners). • Union members — between 12,000 and 15,000 — stopped work (unverified — V_R41: sources give Local 182 as roughly 15,000 members but only 2,000–5,000 in the streets; no source read gives a stoppage total): cars and trucks were parked to block streets, stores closed, \"flying squadrons\" arrived from out of town, and thousands … • Washburn paid a $250 fine for violating the injunction (unverified — V_R41: not on libcom or in any source I could reach).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A whole capital city stopped by its own factories with a 2 a.m. sheriff's raid to start it — an easy cover for a burglary at the Capitol or the state's asylum files.",
