      "summary": "Tubal Uriah \"Buzz\" Butler (born St George's, Grenada, 21 January 1897; British West Indies Regiment 1914–18 in Egypt; a pipe-fitter at the Roodal oilfields from 1921, and a Spiritual Baptist preacher) led a strike that began 19 June 1937. Police tried to arrest him at a meeting in Fyzabad; Corporal Charlie King chased into a shop, was thrown from its window, broke his leg, and was doused with kerosene and set alight by the crowd. Sub-Inspector Bradburn was also killed; nine civilians died and fifty were wounded as the strike spread island-wide. Butler went into hiding; promised safe passage to testify before the inquiry, he was arrested when he came out and held from 9 September 1937 to May 1939 — then detained again for the whole of the Second World War. The odd detail: the strike leader was a preacher of a faith that was illegal in Trinidad at the time (next entry).",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for Butler and the date; the manner of King's death and the casualty figures from search summaries of the National Trust and press pages — Medium-High.",
