      "summary": "A late-summer polio outbreak (by September, 228 children ill and 18 dead — search-result figures) led the Board of Health on 31 Aug 1937 to postpone the school year; on 2 Sep the schools were declared \"indefinitely closed.\" Superintendent William H. Johnson and assistant superintendent Minnie Fallon organised lessons by radio: from 13 Sep, six stations donated fifteen-minute slots for grades 3–8 (grades 1–2 excluded as needing individual attention), six daily newspapers printed the schedules, directions, questions and assignments, and sixteen teachers staffed a telephone help line for parents. Johnson estimated 315,000 children listened, while admitting it was \"impossible to give an accurate check.\" High schools reopened 16 Sep; elementary schools 27 Sep. Three weeks in all. The odd detail: for two weeks in September 1937 every child in Chicago was at home listening to the same voice at the same time — the whole city's schoolchildren on one wavelength.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
