      "summary": "A polio outbreak led Chicago Board of Health president Herman Bundesen to postpone indefinitely the school opening set for 7 September 1937. For two weeks from 13 September, grades 3–8 were taught by radio in 15-minute slots from 7:15 a.m. on six donated stations (science and social studies Mon/Wed/Fri, English and arithmetic Tue/Thu/Sat), with lessons, questions and assignments printed in six newspapers and a help line of sixteen teachers for parents. Superintendent William H. Johnson estimated 315,000 pupils listened. High schools reopened 16 September, elementary schools 27 September. Rochester, NY and Cleveland copied the scheme; the Board later formed the Chicago Radio Council and founded WBEZ in 1943. The odd detail: The idea came from George H. Biggar of WLS — the farm-and-barn-dance station — and every morning the whole city's children sat by the wireless listening to the same teacher's voice at the same minute.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (Wikipedia article resting on Sept 1937 Chicago Tribune and NYT reports). Case/death counts for Chicago not given there.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — 315,000 children hearing one broadcast voice each morning, at a fixed minute, for two weeks — a summoner's dream of a mass audience. What if one lesson was not written by Miss Tobin?",
