      "summary": "A United Press dispatch datelined Chicago, 1 Sept 1937, reported that Richard Stefinak, 30, was held in jail after confessing to killing his sixty-year-old mother, Mrs. Mary Stefinak, with an axe; she had been found \"hacked to death\" a few hours before his arrest. A policeman quoted him: \"I killed her with an axe because she was a werewolf.\" Police Sergeant William Shea said the occupant of the apartment above told him mother and son had quarrelled. (The item's broken OCR also appears to say the son was the father of five children — UNVERIFIED.) The odd detail: A real 1937 Chicago homicide in which the killer's stated reason is werewolf — the one word the setting needs, in a wire story the rest of the country read between the Sino-Japanese war and the cinema listings.",
