      "summary": "Longmans sent the novelist to report on the persecution; he travelled through Tabasco and Chiapas, where churches had been destroyed or closed and priests driven out or shot. He lamented Villahermosa's lack of any church; a month after he left, peasants tried to put up an altar in a ruined church (shots fired — LEAD). The trip produced The Lawless Roads (1939) and The Power and the Glory (1940), whose \"whisky priest\" and atheist lieutenant (modelled on Garrido) fixed the Anglo-American image of the era. The odd detail: the period's best-known English picture of anticlerical Mexico is a novel about a drunken priest who cannot stop being a priest.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
