      "summary": "Sweeney (b. 5 May 1894), an army surgeon in a First World War amputation unit who came home gassed, alcoholic and, by 1934, separated from his wife, was Ness's private suspect. Ness had him held for days and had Leonarde Keeler of Chicago's Northwestern crime lab run two polygraph tests; Keeler told Ness Sweeney was his man. Sweeney was the first cousin of Congressman Martin L. Sweeney, a Democrat and Ness's loudest political enemy, which made prosecution on a lie-detector result impossible. Days after the questioning, on 25 Aug 1938, Sweeney committed himself voluntarily to a veterans' hospital (unverified — V_R06); the murders stopped. From institutions in Sandusky and Dayton (unverified — V_R06) he mailed Ness taunting postcards into the 1950s. He died 9 July 1964 at the Dayton veterans' hospital. The odd detail: The postcards — a suspect sending threatening cards to the detective and his family into the 1950s, stopping only after Ness's death in 1957, from inside an asylum he could leave whenever he liked (unverified — V2_R06).",
