      "summary": "Samuel Insull's showpiece interurban went into receivership in 1932 as his empire crumbled, with ridership and revenue falling through the Depression. In 1938 a strike \"precipitated by a 15% reduction in wages\" halted the whole line for seven weeks — a Montana labour paper of 20 Oct 1938 noted \"a dandy electric line between Chicago and Milwaukee that has been collecting rust for more than two months.\" Even so, the road spent $4.3 million of Public Works Administration money on grade separation through Winnetka and Glencoe, built under 200-plus daily trains and finished in late 1941. The odd detail: for seven weeks in the late summer and autumn of 1938 there was no electric train between Chicago and Milwaukee — the North Shore's towns cut off from the Loop except by the steam road and the highway.",
