      "summary": "The strike wars of 1938 turned to black powder. 6 May: a bomb at the Cadillac Gingerale Company plant downtown. 22 June: bombs thrown from cars at five west-side Kroger stores within fifteen minutes after midnight, during a grocery workers' strike. 28 July: a black-powder bomb thrown through the boiler-room window of the Imperial Hotel, whose president John Anhut was a leader in a labour dispute. 20 August: after his expulsion from the UAW, Richard Frankensteen's garage was blown in by a black-powder bomb. Elsewhere that spring: on 1 March 150 Detroit police ejected 38 sit-downers from a Consolidated Brass plant; on 30 March a riot at the Federal Screw Works injured 40, 13 of them police; on 2 April strikers seized six utility plants in Jackson, Bay City, Flint and Milwaukee. The odd detail: Five grocery stores bombed from moving cars in a quarter of an hour — organised, timed, and never, in what I read, solved.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High that the events were reported (the page cites contemporary papers); the bombers are not named.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The Black Legion's \"arson squad\" was broken up in 1936 — and two years later someone in Detroit was still very good at timed black-powder bombs. The vacuum was filled by professionals.",
