      "summary": "By the 1938 Directory the city had 245 parochial grammar schools with 127,085 pupils and the country 119 schools with 27,328 — 154,413 in all, about 34,500 fewer than in 1931, even though the number of schools rose (from 337 to 364). Other 1938 lines: 297 diocesan seminarians; 12 seminaries for religious orders (491 students); Quigley's 650 students; 39 girls' academies (11,480 students); 19 boys' high schools and colleges (9,000 students); 151 country churches with resident priests; orphanages with 2,401 children; 4,500 adult converts in the year. (The priest and population lines of the 1938 recapitulation are illegible in the OCR.) The odd detail: The Depression shows up as empty desks — more schools, fewer children — the birth rate falling faster than Mundelein could build.",
