      "summary": "William Rhodes Davis (1889–1941), an Alabama-born Oklahoma wildcatter, built an oil refinery in Hamburg in 1933. After Cárdenas expropriated the foreign oil companies on 18 March 1938 (M01) and the Anglo-American majors boycotted Mexican oil, Davis — introduced to the labour leader Vicente Lombardo Toledano through John L. Lewis of the CIO — arranged for Germany and Italy to stockpile Mexican oil; the British blockade of 1939 ended it. In 1939–40 he carried a supposed peace proposal reflecting Göring's views; he died of a heart attack in Houston on 1 August 1941 — British intelligence files later hinted at foul play; his biographer thought natural causes likeliest. The odd detail: an American wildcatter with a Hamburg refinery became the man who sold a revolution's confiscated oil to Berlin.",
