      "summary": "Bronzeville's artists began raising money in 1938 (first sponsoring-committee meeting 25 October 1938); Margaret Burroughs ran the \"Mile of Dimes\" on South Parkway, and the Artists' and Models' Ball at the Savoy on 23 October 1939 became the annual fundraiser; the mansion was bought for about $8,000 of community money, classes began unofficially on 15 December 1940 under the WPA Federal Art Project, and Eleanor Roosevelt dedicated it on 7 May 1941. Of more than a hundred WPA community art centres it is the only one still open; Charles White, Gordon Parks, Archibald Motley, Eldzier Cortor and Richard Hunt passed through it. The odd detail: A mile of dimes laid on South Parkway to buy a grain merchant's mansion.",
