      "summary": "Chicago had asked the PWA for a State Street subway; Harold Ickes, the PWA administrator, pushed for a second line, and the \"Dearborn plan\" was adopted in 1938 as the price of the money. Work on the Milwaukee–Dearborn tube began in March 1939. When war materials were rationed the federal government let State Street continue but stopped the Dearborn subway in 1942 at about 80–82 per cent complete; the bored tunnels sat finished, unlit and empty until work resumed in December 1945, and the line opened only at midnight on 25 February 1951. The odd detail: For most of a decade Chicago had a nearly finished subway under Dearborn Street that no train ran through — mile after mile of empty tube under the Loop.",
