      "id": "R28-060",
      "title": "Marie Porter — the first woman Illinois put in the electric chair",
      "date_start": "1938-01-28",
      "date_end": "1938-01-28",
      "date_label": "28 January 1938",
      "category": "Women of 1930s Chicago and the Great Lakes",
      "tags": [
        "Women",
        "Chicago",
        "Marie Porter",
        "Illinois"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Marie Porter",
        "her co-defendant"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "The Illinois State Penitentiary at Joliet",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(Stateville) — the chair's location is from general knowledge, UNVERIFIED this session",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=The%20Illinois%20State%20Penitentiary%20at%20Joliet%20%20%E2%80%94%20the%20chair%27s%20location%20is%20from%20general%20knowledge%2C%20UNVERIFIED%20this%20session"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Marie Porter, 38, was electrocuted on 28 January 1938, the first woman executed in the electric chair in Illinois, together with the man convicted with her, for what the wire-service headline called an \"insurance slaying\" — the victim was her brother, killed for his life insurance (the standard account names the brother William Kappen, the sum about $3,300 and the shooter Angelo Giancola; those details were not confirmed from a page read this session — UNVERIFIED). She was 37 at the time of the crime. Only two women were executed in the United States that year — Porter in January and Anna Marie Hahn, Cincinnati's arsenic poisoner, in Ohio's chair on 7 December 1938 (R06). The odd detail: Both American women executed in 1938 died in Great Lakes states, eleven months apart, one at each end of the region.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (date, age, state, method and \"insurance slaying\" are from the list; the names and sum are not). • The Illinois State Penitentiary at Joliet (Stateville) — the chair's location is from general knowledge, UNVERIFIED this session. • the standard account names the brother William Kappen, the sum about $3,300 and the shooter Angelo Giancola; those details were not confirmed from a page read this session — UNVERIFIED • Giancola, UNVERIFIED",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A GM needs to know what the state does to a woman who kills: in 1938 Illinois, it electrocutes her. The brother's insurance policy — who wrote it, and who else was insured through the same agent — is the ledger under the crime.",
