      "summary": "Archibald Stansfeld Belaney (1888–1938), born in Hastings, England, came to Canada in 1906, worked as a trapper in northern Ontario, and remade himself as \"Grey Owl,\" claiming a Scottish father and an Apache mother. His books (The Men of the Last Frontier, 1931; Pilgrims of the Wild, 1935), films and lectures made him the most famous Native conservationist in the Empire; in December 1937 he gave a Royal Command Performance at Buckingham Palace before King George VI and the princesses. He died of pneumonia on 13 April 1938 and was exposed as an Englishman almost at once. The odd detail: The most famous \"Indian\" in the British Empire was an Englishman from a seaside town.",
