      "summary": "The Black Hawks finished 14–25–9 (.291, the lowest winning percentage of any champion in the four major leagues), coached by a rookie — Bill Stewart, a major-league baseball umpire. Goalie Mike Karakas broke a toe before the Final; for Game 1 in Toronto the Hawks used Alfie Moore, a minor-leaguer reportedly located in a tavern (the legend; the page says \"emergency replacement\"), and won; NHL president Frank Calder then ruled Moore ineligible but let the win stand. Karakas came back in a steel-toed skate for Games 3–4 and the Hawks won the Cup at the Stadium on April 12, 1938, 4–1 — with eight American-born players, a record — and the Cup itself was not in the building for the celebration. The team had also won in 1934 on home ice. The odd detail: The champions' trophy was not there when they won it.",
