      "summary": "Governor Philip La Follette, whose Progressives had governed Wisconsin (and shared Milwaukee's politics with the Socialists — Hoan ran on their federation ticket in 1936), launched a new national party with a 90-minute speech on 28 April 1938. Its emblem — a blue cross in a white field inside a red circle, \"typifying the voter's mark on the ballot\" — was instantly read as a \"mutilated swastika,\" in the spring of the Anschluss and the Munich crisis. Julius P. Heil, a Milwaukee industrialist, crushed La Follette in the November 1938 governor's race; the party was moribund by 1939 and formally dissolved in 1946. The odd detail: The cross-in-a-circle: in a world with no Nazis, nobody would have seen a swastika in it — they would have seen a circle.",
