      "summary": "On 30 July 1938 — proclaimed \"Henry Ford Day\", with banquets — the German consul in Cleveland presented Ford, on his 75th birthday, with the Grand Cross of the German Eagle, the highest honour Germany then gave a foreigner (R10 has the Mooney and Lindbergh awards). In September 1938 (not 12 October, as one chronology gives it — two independent sources, including Liebold's own page, put it two months after Ford's award) Ford executive Ernest G. Liebold, \"a fervent anti-Semite\", received the German Eagle order of merit. On 20 November, two weeks after the November pogrom, 5,000 people including Governor Murphy and labour leaders protested German persecution of Jews at the Detroit Naval Armory; that same day Coughlin told his radio audience that \"Jewish persecution only followed after Christians first were persecuted\", and New York and Chicago stations began censoring him. On 30 November, after meeting Rabbi Leo M. Franklin, Ford issued a statement criticising the persecution and favouring admission of European Jews under a quota — and Coughlin claimed the rabbi had written it. The odd detail: The medal came from the consul in Cleveland — the setting's IHS city — not from Detroit.",
