      "summary": "On 29 September 1937 Chebatoris — described in the Michigan press as a Detroit gangster — and Jack Gracey tried to rob the Midland bank of the $75,000 Dow Chemical payroll; Chebatoris shot bank president Clarence Macomber, cashier Paul Bywater and a bystander, Henry Porter, a Bay City truck driver whose uniform he mistook for a policeman's; Porter died on 11 October. Tried under the 1934 federal bank-robbery law — the first murder trial under it — he was convicted on 28 October 1937 and sentenced to death, though Michigan had abolished capital punishment for murder in 1846–47, the first English-speaking government to do so. Federal law required the execution to follow the method of the state where the crime happened; U.S. Attorney John Lehr found that Michigan still kept a death penalty for treason. Chebatoris \"plunged through the trap door at 5:08 a.m.\" at Milan on 8 July 1938 and was pronounced dead thirteen minutes later. The odd detail: To hang him, the government borrowed Michigan's treason law — the only execution in the state since statehood.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A gallows built for one man in a state that has no gallows. In the setting, the Milan farm's scaffold could be kept — and used again, quietly.",
