      "summary": "Pittsburgh led the Cubs by 1½ games coming into the series. The game began at 3:00 p.m.; sunset was 5:37 (Tribune). Bottom of the ninth, 5–5, two out, 0–2 count, near-dark — the umpires had ruled this was the last inning, and the rules of the day did not allow a suspended game: it would have been replayed entirely next day. Player-manager Gabby Hartnett hit Mace Brown's pitch \"into the darkness\" for the winning homer; players and fans stormed the field and escorted him round the bases. The Cubs took first place, won 10–1 next day and clinched three days later — then were swept by the Yankees. The odd detail: Nobody in the park saw the ball land; they saw Hartnett running and believed him (unverified — V2_R16).",
