      "id": "R20-041",
      "title": "Mining the State Street tubes: clay, \"bench\" and \"shield\", streetcars riding on planks",
      "date_start": "1938-11",
      "date_end": "1943",
      "date_label": "Nov 1938 to 1943",
      "category": "The city's guts: power, water, gas, sewers, tunnels, bridges, digging, building and demolition (Chicago and the Great Lakes, 1925–1940)",
      "tags": [
        "utilities",
        "building",
        "Mining",
        "State Street"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Philip Harrington",
        "Edward J. Kelly",
        "James R. Quinn",
        "Dennis J. Normoyle"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Route No. 1 — from Armitage and Sheffield",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "southeast under Clybourn Avenue, east under Division Street, south under State Street to 16th Street",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Route%20No.%201%20%E2%80%94%20from%20Armitage%20and%20Sheffield%2C%20southeast%20under%20Clybourn%20Avenue%2C%20east%20under%20Division%20Street%2C%20south%20under%20State%20Street%20to%2016th%20Street%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "the Loop stretch under State",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20Loop%20stretch%20under%20State%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The City Council authorised the subway on 3 November 1938; \"in six weeks — a record for speed on a project of such size — work was begun\". The city bought 351 parcels of right-of-way (for both routes) for $2,325,000; every purchase went through condemnation, and Circuit Judge Dennis J. Normoyle stayed on the bench through his vacations to hear the sixteen cases. The tubes were mined 44 to 48 ft below the street. Outside the Loop, in clay \"comparatively free from water\", miners used the \"bench\" method; under the Loop, where the soil was as much as 58 per cent water and street and building loads were heavy, they used the shield method \"because of its greater safety in such fluid soil\". The mezzanine stations were dug as open cuts covered with heavy decking that carried all street traffic — trucks and streetcars — during construction. More than 2,000,000 cubic yards of clay came out; the two routes used 135,000 tons of steel, 1,000,000 barrels of cement and 230 miles of conduit. The finished line had the world's first fluorescent lighting in a subway, a 3,500-ft, 22-ft-wide continuous Loop platform, and stations colour-coded in rotation — blue, red, green, brown — so riders knew their stop. Route 1 cost about $34 million; both routes about $57.4 million, of which Washington gave $23.13 million. The odd detail: Under the Loop the \"ground\" was more than half water — the tunnellers were mining through a buried swamp, while State Street's streetcars rolled over their heads on planks.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (primary source, the city's own figures).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — \"58 per cent water\": the Loop stands on a buried marsh, and the shields were pushed through it blind. The colour code — blue, red, green, brown — is the kind of thing an occultist reads as a working laid under the city.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Internet Archive: chicago subways",
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/chicago-subways",
          "publisher": "Internet Archive",
          "kind": "book or scanned text",
          "supports": "City of Chicago, Department of Subways and Superhighways, The Chicago Subways, October 1943 — full OCR read"
        }
      ],
