      "summary": "John J. \"Bathhouse John\" Coughlin, alderman of the First Ward for 46 years (with \"Hinky Dink\" Kenna the boss of the Levee and the First Ward Balls that raised $50,000 a year until 1909), dressed in vaudeville-costumer suits of green and lavender, published verse so bad that Chicagoans wrote worse and signed his name to it, and in 1906 opened Zoo Park in Colorado Springs with an elephant, Princess Alice, he had talked Lincoln Park Zoo into giving up. He died in office on November 11, 1938, aged 78, destitute after racetrack losses. The odd detail: The Levee's poet-alderman took Lincoln Park's elephant to Colorado; his ward's dead brothel-palace (the Everleigh) came down in 1933 while he still held the seat.",
