      "summary": "On 13 October 1938 President Roosevelt approved a PWA grant of $18,000,000 toward a total cost of $40,000,000 (unverified — V_R20) — the Washington Evening Star (14 Oct 1938) wrote that Chicago's subway hopes, \"nurtured for 30 years\", were \"on the threshold of realization\". (R09 and Wikipedia say \"WPA\"; the contemporary report says P.W.A. — Ickes's Public Works Administration, which is right.) Ground was broken on 17 December 1938 at State and Chicago. Among the guests was Robert Ridgway, 76, the New York engineer who had \"a part in the construction of every foot of the rapid transit systems of New York\" (IRT, the Joralemon Street tunnel under the East River, the northern 60 miles of the Catskill Aqueduct) and who sat on the 1938 board of engineers that studied the Chicago plans. On the train home he suffered a heart attack, was taken off at Fort Wayne, Indiana, and died in Methodist Hospital there on 19 December 1938 — two days after the ceremony. The tube was bored deep enough for tunnelling machines most of the way; only short stretches were cut-and-cover. The odd detail: The man who dug every foot of New York's subways lived to see Chicago's first shovel go in and died on the way home — the dig's first casualty was its most famous engineer.",
