      "summary": "Saul Alinsky — a criminologist at the Institute for Juvenile Research who claimed to have spent two years as a \"nonparticipant observer\" with the Capone mob — set up the Back of the Yards Neighborhood Council in 1939 with Joseph Meegan, a park supervisor. Working with the archdiocese, it rallied \"otherwise mutually hostile Catholic ethnics (Irish, Poles, Lithuanians, Mexicans, Croats…)\" and Black residents to win concessions from the meatpackers, alongside the packinghouse union. In 1940, with Auxiliary Bishop Bernard J. Sheil (founder of the Catholic Youth Organization, auxiliary bishop of Chicago 1928–59, known for a pro-labour stance) and Marshall Field, Alinsky founded the Industrial Areas Foundation. The council later set itself against housing integration. The odd detail: A man who learned organising by studying the Outfit, working hand-in-glove with a Catholic bishop — \"no one is clean,\" and the Church is at the table.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High (the exact founding date of 14 July 1939 is from memory — UNVERIFIED this session).",
