      "summary": "Manteno was built to take Chicago's overflow from Dunning (unverified — V2_R06). In 1939 contaminated water got into the hospital's supply and a typhoid epidemic ran for six months: 37 patients died, hundreds were sick, and 45 patients ran away during it. (The outbreak became a state scandal and a Chicago Tribune crusade against the Horner administration's Department of Public Welfare — that political layer is UNVERIFIED this session; see Leads.) Elgin in the same decade ran 100–150 dairy cattle, up to 1,500 hogs and 5,000 chickens with patient labour; Dr. Bronner of the soap company escaped from it in 1945. The odd detail: Forty-five of the insane walked out of a quarantined asylum in a typhoid summer (unverified — V2_R06) and into the Kankakee County cornfields.",
