      "summary": "Authorised in 1927, the cottage-plan asylum took its first patients in December 1930 and by 1936 covered 1,200 acres with its own farms, livestock, fire department, police and power plant (steam from the plant heated the laundries and showers). In 1939 a typhoid epidemic ran for six months: hundreds sick, 45 patients ran away during it, and the dead were counted at 37 patients by the Wikipedia account but at \"52 inmates and employes\" in the wire story of 22 Nov 1939, which reported a grand jury indicting the state director of public welfare, A. L. Bowen, the hospital's director Dr. Ralph T. Hinton, his assistant Dr. D. L. Steinberg and the dietician Mrs. Lillian Williams for malfeasance in office over \"the typhoid epidemic and outbreak of dysentery\"; the hospital then held about 5,200 patients with some 700 attendants. Contaminated water was suspected to have leaked into the hospital's own supply. The hospital peaked at 8,195 patients in 1954 and closed 31 Dec 1985. The odd detail: a self-contained town of the insane, with its own well, its own power and its own police, poisoned from inside its own pipes — 45 escapes during the sickness, and the state's welfare director indicted for it.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High — two sources disagree on the toll (37 vs 52); the indictments are from a single wire snippet.",
