      "summary": "The WPA's 1939 Illinois guide gives Chicago's fares: surface lines (streetcars) 8 cents; buses 10 cents; elevated lines 12 cents. \"Transfers issued from street cars and buses to elevated, and vice versa; no transfers between surface lines and buses.\" Taxis (the guide's Chicago entry): 20 cents the first mile, 10 cents each additional mile, 5 cents each additional passenger. The \"L\" itself was bankrupt: Samuel Insull gave up his traction holdings and the Chicago Rapid Transit Co. went into receivership on 6 June 1932 (receivers named 28 June 1932). The \"L\"'s own funeral trains — which carried coffins and mourners to the cemeteries — ended in 1932; the last funeral train over the \"L\" (Chicago Aurora & Elgin equipment, on the Metropolitan Division's tracks) ran on 13 July 1934. The odd detail: until July 1934 you could ride the \"L\" to a funeral with the coffin in the car ahead.",
