      "summary": "Burnham and Root's 21-storey, 302-ft Masonic Temple (1892), also called the Capitol Building, was the tallest building in Chicago from 1895 to 1899. Its central court was ringed by nine floors of shops, with offices above and, at the very top, meeting rooms for the Masons that also served as theatres. Both John Wellborn Root and the Masons' representative Norman Gassette died while it was being built. Its elevators could not cope with its crowds and it fell out of favour; in 1939 it was demolished — poor services, and the new State Street subway would have required expensive foundation retrofitting. A two-storey \"taxpayer\" with a Walgreens went up in its place. The odd detail: The building's architect and the Masons' own man both died during its construction, and the subway killed it — lodge rooms at the top of Chicago's tallest tower, gone in the year the tunnel came.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
