      "summary": "The Ligonier Banner of 20 April 1939 quoted George B. Loy, \"secret service head in Indianapolis\": \"Merchants in Indiana cities are losing approximately $600 weekly through acceptance of forged WPA checks.\" More than 200 stolen and forged relief cheques had been cashed in the southern Indiana district in recent months, losses over $5,500, about 75 in Indianapolis alone. In one Saturday's mail his office received seven from Evansville, five from Muncie, one from Ligonier and one from Richmond. The thieves stole the cheques from the mail and forged the endorsement. Loy wanted the WPA to hand cheques to workers on their projects instead of mailing them, and said that change eight months earlier would have prevented \"at least 55\" imprisonments. He told merchants to \"identify definitely the bearer of a WPA check before cashing it.\" The odd detail: the head of the Secret Service in Indianapolis counting the prison sentences his own agency could have avoided: an unusual kind of arithmetic for a cop.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (OCR read). Chicago's equivalent figures were not found — UNVERIFIED.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — relief-cheque forgery is the Depression's small, sad federal crime, and it gives Secret Service player characters a daily beat: mailboxes, tenement hallways, grocers. The occult layer hides there. The same forged endorsement turns up on cheques from four towns in one week, in the handwriting of a man dead since 1932.",
