      "summary": "The civilian United States Lighthouse Service, under Commissioner George R. Putnam (1910–35) and then H. D. King (1935–39), had raised the number of aids to navigation from 11,713 to about 24,000 in Putnam's 25 years while cutting more than 800 employees through automation. On 1 July 1939, under Roosevelt's reorganisation, the Service was merged into the Coast Guard, which took over every lighthouse and lightship; keepers were given the choice of staying civilian or enlisting (the choice is standard history but not on the page read — UNVERIFIED there). The Grays Reef and Grosse Point pages both record the 1939 transfer. The odd detail: In the setting's last summer of the decade, every keeper on the lakes woke up working for a different, armed service — and the men who had kept logs as civilians now kept them as military records.",
