      "summary": "Dolezal, a 52-year-old Slavic bricklayer who had lived with Florence Polillo, was an acquaintance of Andrassy and Wallace, and frequented a bar where one of the victims hung out, was picked up by Sheriff Martin L. O'Donnell's men — not Ness's police — in a sheriff's-office attempt to steal the case. After days of questioning he confessed to killing Polillo \"in self-defence,\" then retracted, saying the confession had been beaten out of him. On 24 Aug 1939 he was found hanged in his cell by a strip of cloth from a bunk (unverified — V2_R06), from a hook lower than his own height (unverified — V_R06). The death was ruled suicide; the autopsy found six broken ribs, injuries friends said he did not have when arrested (unverified — V_R06). Modern researchers treat him as an innocent man killed in custody; he was declared exonerated in Aug 2010 after investigators found no case against him in his confessions (Find a Grave; Wikipedia says only \"later posthumously exonerated\"; no judge named). The odd detail: He hanged himself from a hook lower than his own height (unverified — V_R06) — and the sheriff who held him was running a rival investigation against the Safety Director.",
