      "id": "R31-001",
      "title": "Manteno's typhoid, from the state's own report: Well No. 4",
      "date_start": "1939-07-08",
      "date_end": "1939-10",
      "date_label": "8 July to October 1939 (deepens R06/R08/R13)",
      "category": "Places of confinement in Chicago and Illinois, 1925–1940",
      "tags": [
        "Places",
        "confinement",
        "Manteno's",
        "Well No"
      ],
      "people": [
        "Dwight H. Green",
        "the report's compilers' names are OCR-garbled in the scan"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Manteno State Hospital",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Kankakee County, about 40 miles south of Chicago — \"100 buildings virtually comprise a small municipality.\"",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Manteno%20State%20Hospital%2C%20Kankakee%20County%2C%20about%2040%20miles%20south%20of%20Chicago%20%E2%80%94%20%22100%20buildings%20virtually%20comprise%20a%20small%20municipality.%22"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The first case fell ill on 8 July 1939; cases peaked on 15 August among employees and 23 August among inmates, and the outbreak ran into October: 453 cases and 60 deaths in a hospital of about 6,200 people (the state's 1945 report; R13 has the 37 and 52 figures from other sources — this is the fullest count). The cause was Well No. 4, one of four drilled wells a few hundred feet apart, drawing from creviced limestone and cased with 14-inch pipe to a depth of only 19 feet, barely a foot into the rock; a sanitary sewer ran about 35 feet away, and sewage had backed up 30 inches deep in a manhole 80 feet from the well. Typhoid organisms were cultured from the well's water on 22–31 August. The activated-sludge sewage plant, built in 1930, was designed for 3,000 people and was taking the waste of 6,200. The Department of Public Health had warned in written water-supply reports in 1931, 1935 and 1937 that the limestone was dangerous and that officials were not exercising \"reasonable care\". By 5 September practically everyone in the hospital had had three doses of vaccine; chlorinators went in on 19 and 26 August; films, dances, church services and all gatherings of patients were stopped, visitors banned, admissions halted. Two cottages, Clouston and McDowell, became typhoid wards; nine state nurses were sent in on 20 September. An amoebic dysentery case followed on 13 October. The state put the cost at $120,000, and the case went up to the Supreme Court of Illinois (the report sniffs that the court gave the health findings \"little probative value\"). The odd detail: The typhoid wards' excreta were boiled down in 90-gallon cauldrons until only a paste remained, and the paste was thrown on the fire and burned.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High — the state's own epidemiological report (numbers are its own; they differ from press figures). • Milton Esswright\") — UNVERIFIED spellings.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The state knew for eight years that the well drank from the sewer. The occult layer: in the 1939 records the first case is on a ward where a patient had been drawing the same well, in chalk, for weeks. The cauldrons in the yard look like witchcraft to the patients watching from the windows — and to one of them, it is.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Internet Archive: 34730270R djvu.txt",
          "url": "https://archive.org/stream/34730270R.nlm.nih.gov/34730270R_djvu.txt",
          "publisher": "Internet Archive",
          "kind": "book or scanned text",
          "supports": "Illinois Department of Public Health, A report on a typhoid fever epidemic at Manteno State Hospital in 1939, 1945, NLM copy"
        }
      ],
