          "text": "the shotgun charges hit him in the head and neck; his coupe \"bounded over the curb, side wiped a trolley pole and careened down the street car tracks\" into a light pole. On the seat lay an unfired Spanish-made .32 automatic. On him: $53, a crucifix, a rosary and a religious medallion, memoranda listing women's names, notes about roller-derby contracts with Leo Seltzer — and notes showing concern about \"religious trends\". He had managed Capone's Hawthorne dog track in Stickney (1928–30) and became president of Sportsman's Park when it opened in 1932. A letter found in a 1937 hideout warned that Capone was \"going to have Ohare\"."
