      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the roles, Pascua's naming and Spicer 1940; mask-burning UNVERIFIED this session. • Pascua village, built of adobe and tin cans c. 1920 northwest of downtown Tucson, took its name — \"Pascua\", Easter — in 1921 (unverified — V_M02: no page read dates the naming; 1921 is when Old Pascua was formally established on land donated by A. M. Franklin, per a search summary). • Heading: (unverified — V_M02: the \"1921\" naming date; 1921 is the year Old Pascua was formally established)",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Respect first — a living religion; do not monster it, do not make the Chapayekas literal demons. A proposal that stays outside the ceremony: a Chicago anthropology student (Spicer's University) writes home in 1936 that someone not of the village has been buying Chapayeka masks after Easter — masks meant to be destroyed — and shipping them east. The villain is the buyer.",
