      "id": "M10-009",
      "title": "Jamaica's Obeah Law — 1898, and the de Laurence books banned",
      "date_start": "1940",
      "date_end": "1940",
      "date_label": "1940",
      "category": "The European Caribbean colonies the setting's America may have bought, 1900–1940",
      "tags": [
        "European",
        "Caribbean",
        "Jamaica's Obeah Law",
        "Laurence"
      ],
      "people": [
        "L. W. de Laurence"
      ],
      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Jamaica",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(all parishes; the resident magistrates' courts)",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Jamaica%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The Obeah Law of 1898 made it a crime to practise obeah, to consult a practitioner, and to publish obeah material; it defined a practitioner as anyone who \"uses, or pretends to use any occult means, or pretends to possess any supernatural power or knowledge\" for gain or to frighten; possession of \"instruments of obeah\" was presumed practice. Penalties: up to 12 months' hard labour and flogging (up to 18 lashes; women exempt), six months and whipping for the client, £20 or (in default) up to six months for publishing. It also folded Myalism into obeah by definition. In 1940, under wartime emergency controls, Jamaica banned a list of books — mostly political — that included the Chicago mail-order occult house of L. W. de Laurence, whose name had become island shorthand for obeah; the de Laurence ban outlived the others and still stands. The odd detail: the grimoire the Crown feared most in Jamaica was printed on Chicago's Michigan Avenue (see R05) — a 1930s Chicago book in a Kingston courtroom.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the 1898 law; Medium for the 1940 list's details (search summary). (Also in R05: de Laurence.)",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the Chicago–Kingston book route — de Laurence's mail-order catalogue as the thread from a Michigan Avenue warehouse to an obeah trial in St Elizabeth. Three layers: the law says fraud; the people say power; the files say some of the books work.",
