      "summary": "Admiral Georges Robert, Vichy's high commissioner for the French Antilles, held 286 tons of Bank of France gold, landed from the cruiser Émile Bertin on 24 June 1940, together with the aircraft carrier Béarn (carrying 104 aircraft that had been bound for Canada), the cruiser Jeanne d'Arc and auxiliaries. He bargained with Washington — supplies for neutrality — while the US Navy watched and planned for seizure. In April 1943 the US cut off supplies; on 24 June 1943 pro-Free French crowds rose in Fort-de-France, the garrison at Balata mutinied on 29 June, and Robert left for the United States on 30 June. The odd detail: a French empire's gold sat for three years on a volcanic island forty miles from the Pelée ruins, guarded by a carrier whose planes rotted on deck.",
