      "summary": "Nitti had been convicted in 1930 of failing to pay tax on $743,000 of income for 1925–27, pleaded guilty and served 18 months at Leavenworth (released March 25, 1932). After the 1932 police shooting he ran the organisation, with Paul Ricca widely believed to hold the real power. In 1943 he and six others were indicted in New York for extorting more than $2 million from movie executives and their union employees. On March 19, 1943, the day before his grand-jury appearance, he walked to the railroad yard in North Riverside and shot himself in front of three freight-crew men — L. M. Barnett, William F. Seebauer and E. H. Moran. He wore a gray checked suit, brown plaid overcoat, blue-and-maroon scarf and brown fedora. The final bullet entered behind his right ear. He is buried at Mount Carmel. The odd detail: The Outfit boss whom Mayor Cermak allegedly tried to have murdered died by his own hand at the corner of the road renamed for Cermak.",
