      "summary": "Every night at 9 p.m. a cannon is fired from La Cabaña — the cañonazo, an eighteenth-century custom that once signalled the closing of the city gates and (by tradition) the chain across the harbour mouth. No invader ever stormed La Cabaña; in the nineteenth century Cuban patriots faced firing squads in its moat, and Machado and Batista used it as a military prison. (Che Guevara's 1959 executions there are outside the window.) The odd detail: a city that still sets its evening by gunfire; in the 1930s the 9 o'clock gun sounded over a fortress holding the regime's prisoners.",
