      "places": [
        {
          "name": "the Code Pénal's articles against sorcery",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "(usually cited as Arts. 405–407; Seabrook cites \"Article 409\")",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20Code%20P%C3%A9nal%27s%20articles%20against%20sorcery%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Seabrook's appendix (1929) quotes the French text: all \"faiseurs de ouangas, caprelatas, vaudoux, dompèdres, macandals et autres sortilèges\" are punished with three to six months in prison by the police court, and on a repeat offence six months to two years; and \"all dances and other practices\" that keep up \"fetishism and superstition\" in the population are to be treated as sorcery and punished the same. Under the occupation Vodou was suppressed as an obstacle to \"modernisation\" (Wikipedia notes the specifics are thinly documented). After the Marines, Sténio Vincent's 1935 law again banned \"superstitious practices\", and the Catholic Church's campagne anti-superstitieuse (peaking c. 1939–42, under President Élie Lescot from 1941) destroyed ritual objects and sacred spaces and made practitioners swear the rejeté — a public renunciation oath. The odd detail: the list names \"macandals\" — the law still carries, as a word for sorcery, the name of François Makandal, the maroon leader burned at Cap-Français in 1758.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the quoted text (as Seabrook printed it); Medium for the 1935 law's exact date and terms. Makandal's 1758 execution is well known but not re-checked this session.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The IHS and the Catholic hierarchy in Haiti are not the same thing, and the 1939–42 campaign is the place to show it — a burning of drums and altars that a Umbra IHS priest knows is destroying the only people who could hold something back.",
