      "summary": "In Dominican folklore a galipote is a person who, by a pact with the devil, can become an animal or even a tree or a stone, has terrible strength and is immune to firearms, and was said to drink children's blood at night. It can only be killed with a stake of palo de cruz cut on Good Friday. Variants: the lugarú (from French loup-garou), which becomes only a dog; the zancú, which becomes invisible. The legend probably dates to the 17th–18th centuries; travellers in remote areas still carry amulets or say a prayer. The odd detail: a Caribbean monster that is proof against the Marines' rifles by definition.",
