      "summary": "In the legend a beautiful girl became pregnant, drowned her child in a river and was punished by being transformed into a hideous creature. Most versions describe a short heavy woman in a drooping straw hat (tule), with swollen milk-leaking breasts that attract ants, sometimes bird or bat wings and inverted hawk-legs that leave reversed footprints; she frequents extinguished campfires and eats charcoal and ashes; reciting \"Alabado sea el Santísimo\" makes her flee toward the sun; blessed tule vines can bind her. It is rooted in Bribri, Huetar and Cabécar traditions (Bribri names Itsa' or Itsö; the Ngäbe-Buglé call her Tepesa), and in some regions merges with La Llorona. The written sources the Wikipedia article cites are modern (Elías Zeledón Cartín 1998, 2000; a Bribri mythology dictionary 2003). The odd detail: the reversed footprints — any tracker who follows them goes the wrong way.",
