      "summary": "Manuel Estrada Cabrera, a lawyer with no military background, was Guatemala's president from 8 February 1898 to 15 April 1920 (22 years). From 1899 he held the Fiestas Minervalias — annual festivals celebrating students and teachers, inspired by Rafael Spínola — and ordered Hellenic-style Temples of Minerva built in the capital and other cities, paid for by local taxpayers; a temple inscription read \"Manuel Estrada Cabrera, President of the Republic, to the Studious Youth.\" He signed a 1904 contract with Minor Cooper Keith of United Fruit granting tax exemptions, land grants and control of all railroads on the Atlantic side (the Puerto Barrios–Guatemala City railway was completed in 1908), and answered UFC strikes with violence, once ordering armed forces to fire into sleeping workers' quarters. He survived a bomb (29 April 1907: it killed only his driver and one horse) and a shooting at a reception by a cadet (20 April 1908), after which he executed the cadet's unit, closed the Military Academy, had it demolished and had salt spread on the ground. His wife died in 1910; a son died of tuberculosis; another son shot himself on 8 November 1912 over a debt. He built a \"well-organized web of spies,\" and by his fourth term only the president's name was on the ballot. The odd detail: a president who ordered Greek temples to the goddess of wisdom built for his youth festivals — and salted the ground where the cadets' academy had been.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium-High (Wikipedia; a number of dramatic details are single-source).",
