      "summary": "Maximón (also Ri Laj Mam, Don Ximon, San Simón, El Gran Abuelo) is described as a Maya deity and folk saint; his worship is believed to have begun at the Spanish conquest, and the figure \"appears to be a blend of several historical, biblical and Maya figures\" including Pedro de Alvarado, Judas Iscariot, Saint Peter and Mam. He is venerated as an effigy that \"resides in a different household every year,\" displayed at Holy Week, with special attendants who keep the altar year-round, drinking and smoking alongside it; offerings include money, tobacco and moonshine. In Santiago Atitlán his effigy is cared for by the Cofradía Santa Cruz, one of 13 cofradías; the Church tried suppression and then absorption by identifying him with Judas Iscariot. The odd detail: the saint's altar has attendants whose job is to drink and smoke with him.",
