      "summary": "The devotion began in Spain: legend says that when the Moors held Christians prisoner at Atocha and only children under twelve could bring them food, an unknown child in pilgrim's dress fed the prisoners who had no children — the Christ Child. An image came to Zacatecas about 1554; after a silver strike at Plateros and a mine explosion at Fresnillo, trapped miners said a child came to help them, and each time \"the image of the child … was found to be dirty and his clothes had little holes in them.\" Pilgrims still bring the Niño new shoes, because he wears his out walking at night. The devotion crossed north early: a Santo Niño chapel stands at Chimayó, New Mexico (see next entry). The odd detail: a holy image that leaves its shrine at night and comes back with mud on its shoes — the miners' saint, the prisoners' saint.",
