      "summary": "Every year pilgrims walk or ride to Magdalena for the feast of San Francisco (4 October; Xavier's own feast is 3 December) to visit a reclining statue of St. Francis Xavier, said to have been carried by Fr. Kino and to have become immovable en route to San Xavier del Bac in 1711 — so it stayed. Anthropologist James Griffith's 1967 observation lists three groups: Mexicans and Mexican-Americans, Tohono O'odham from Arizona, and Yaqui and Mayo from southern Sonora. Pilgrims cite a manda — a promise made to the saint to come on foot or horseback in return for a petition. The tradition \"predates modern borders.\" The odd detail: the statue is a corpse-like reclining figure in vestments, and the legend is about a statue too heavy to carry north — the saint chose the town.",
