      "summary": "Jones's survey found three Mexican colonies at the Stock Yards; the largest near Ashland and 46th. Redfield's informants told him Brighton Park had been Jewish in the 1890s, Polish in the next decade and Mexican from the Great War on — an area still \"Mexicanizing.\" Mexican arrival lowered property values but raised rents, they said, and Mexican shops caused friction with Jewish, Polish and (less) Irish shopkeepers. Redfield's classmate Samuel Cohen, who lived in Brighton Park, was one of his two channels for information there (the other was Manuel Bueno). The neighbourhood's older Central and Eastern European residents \"reluctantly allowed\" the Mexican enclave as long as it stayed within a few blocks (a general-history summary). Mexican packers appear in the union story only faintly: the 1921–22 Amalgamated Meat Cutters strike (broken with Black strikebreakers) is not documented for Mexicans in the sources I read. The odd detail: three successive tenants of the same Brighton Park blocks in thirty years — Jewish, Polish, Mexican — with the rents rising each time.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the layout; Medium on the \"reluctantly allowed\" line (secondary).",
