      "summary": "An FLPS clipping from Robert C. Jones's \"1927\" scrapbook advertises a Mexican Fiesta at the Cloister Club on a Monday, 5:30–6:45 p.m., 50-cent dinner (enchiladas — \"you will have to guess the contents\" — tortillas, rice, frijoles, guava paste, or roast beef and ham with eggs), a string trio, a cornet solo by Manuel Contreras, \"Señorita Concka Martizen [as rendered] from the film Flying Down to Rio,\" films of Mexico, and drawings for Mexican pottery given by Guillermo Hawley of the Mexican National Railways and his wife in native dress; nearly 100 reservations were in. The scrapbook also holds a Women's Chicago Beautiful Association gala titled \"A Night in Old Mexico,\" a Goodman Theatre recital by Clarita Martin and Milla Domínguez, and a Field Museum tour for the colony. Dating problem: Flying Down to Rio was released in late 1933, so the \"1927\" label cannot be right for this clipping. The odd detail: guessing the contents of an enchilada was the entertainment; and a railway man giving away pottery in \"native dress.\"",
