      "summary": "Guzik was the organisation's financial officer and chief bagman in payoffs to police and politicians — the \"greasy thumb\" came from counting out bribe money. He was convicted of tax fraud in 1930 and sentenced to five years, serving three years and eight months. The Treasury file shows how his family formed the money's skin: brother-in-law Louis Lipschultz bought the Harlem Inn brothel and funnelled cash to keep witnesses like Fred Ries away from Chicago; sister-in-law Jeannette Keithly held the deed; his chauffeur Bobby Barton collected the cashier's cheques. The cash from the Hawthorne Smoke Shop safe after the McSwiggin murder — $84,000 — went to Guzik, Louis La Cava and Frank Nitti at the Atlantic Hotel. The odd detail: The Outfit's money was held in the names of in-laws — a sister-in-law owned a brothel on paper.",
