          "label": "Plausible contents, by precedent",
          "text": "(1) A Krupp hall — the 1893 firm returned with the \"Thunderer\" coastal gun; a 1933 Krupp would show the Paris Gun's successor and armour plate — and be a live diplomatic problem in a city whose president-brother runs the fair and whose government is in a tariff war with Berlin. (2) Zeiss — already present in fact: the Adler Planetarium (opened 1930, the first in the Western Hemisphere) ran a Zeiss projector, and Zeiss instruments would anchor an Imperial science hall. (3) The Dresden Hygiene Museum's Transparent Man — actually sent in 1933 (see the Hall of Science entry) — as the state's own exhibit. (4) The Gutenberg press from Mainz — actually lent in 1933. (5) Lufthansa / Zeppelin — the Graf Zeppelin's call would be an official state visit with the Kaiser's ambassador, not a stunt. (6) A German Village on the Midway model — the Black Forest Village and Old Heidelberg exist as private concessions anyway. (7) Reparations-funded largesse: an imperial Germany collecting from France, Britain and Russia could afford the kind of gift Italy actually gave — a monument on the lakefront (Italy gave the Balbo column; Germany's 1893 precedent was the Germania fountain)."
