      "summary": "Twelve model houses (1.5 million visitors): George Fred Keck's twelve-sided glass House of Tomorrow (with an aeroplane hangar, a Pierce-Arrow Silver Arrow in the garage, one of the first home dishwashers, an \"iceless refrigerator\" and early central air — and a notorious overheating problem); Robert Law Weed's flamingo-pink Florida Tropical House; the Armco-Ferro porcelain-enamel steel house; the Wieboldt-Rostone house; the Cypress Log Cabin; plus the Masonite, Stran-Steel/Good Housekeeping, Brick Manufacturers (Rebori), Lumber Industries (Grunsfeld; later trucked to Manitowish Waters, Wis.), General Houses, Design for Living and Universal houses. After 1934 the developer Robert Bartlett bought five and in January 1935 barged four of them across Lake Michigan to a specially built pier at his Beverly Shores subdivision (the log cabin went by truck) as a Depression sales stunt. All five survive under the National Park Service and Indiana Landmarks; the House of Tomorrow was still under a $2.5 million restoration from 2018. The odd detail: A glass house with an aeroplane hangar crossed the lake on a barge in January.",
