      "summary": "At the opening the dedicatory prayer was given by the Episcopal bishop of Chicago, George Craig Stewart — no Catholic prelate is named in the accounts read. Cardinal George Mundelein (archbishop 1915–39) had already staged Chicago's great Catholic mass spectacle — the 28th International Eucharistic Congress of June 1926 at Soldier Field and at his new seminary of St. Mary of the Lake — and he was Roosevelt's friend and a New Deal man; on 18 May 1937 at Quigley Seminary he called Hitler \"an Austrian paperhanger, and a poor one at that,\" bringing a formal German protest. He died 2 Oct 1939 and is buried behind the altar of the Mundelein Seminary chapel. Whether he blessed anything at the fair, or visited, was not established from pages read; the other lane on churchmen should have it (lead). The odd detail: The fair's blessing was Protestant; the city's cardinal built his own fair-sized spectacle seven years earlier and then took on Hitler from a seminary lectern.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
