      "summary": "In Irish folklore the bean sí (\"woman of the fairy mound\") keens — the caoineadh of the keening woman — to announce a death in certain families, said to be those of old Gaelic (\"Milesian\") stock with Ó or Mac names, not Norman or Saxon lines (with exceptions such as the Geraldines). She may be young or old, one to four feet tall, grey-cloaked over green, red-eyed from weeping, combing long hair; her Scottish cousin is the bean nighe, the washer at the ford, rinsing the bloody clothes of those about to die. Hearing her means certain death to a family member; several together mean someone \"great or holy\" is dying. The source notes no American tradition of banshees following emigrants. The odd detail: she follows surnames, not places — so the logic of the legend lets her cross the ocean with the family.",
