      "places": [
        {
          "name": "the Near West Side and beyond — Italian Taylor St.",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Greek \"Delta\" at Halsted & Harrison, Jewish Maxwell St./Lawndale, Mexican Hull-House area and Back of the Yards",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20Near%20West%20Side%20and%20beyond%20%E2%80%94%20Italian%20Taylor%20St.%2C%20Greek%20%22Delta%22%20at%20Halsted%20%26%20Harrison%2C%20Jewish%20Maxwell%20St./Lawndale%2C%20Mexican%20Hull-House%20area%20and%20Back%20of%20the%20Ya"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Four neighbouring communities shared one fear with different cures. Italian malocchio, cast (knowingly or not) by a jettatore with \"high arching brows\" and \"a stark stare,\" warded by the red-coral or gold cornicello, the mano cornuta and the fig sign. Greek mati/vaskania: a healer silently recites a secret prayer learned from an older relative \"of the opposite sex\"; healer and victim yawn; olive oil dropped into holy water floats (no eye) or sinks (eye); blue beads; \"ftou.\" Jewish ayin hara: never boast of children or luck; say kinehore; spit (or pretend) three times; a red string on infants. Mexican mal de ojo: the curandero sweeps a raw egg over the body, breaks it into water under the bed; if it looks \"cooked,\" the envy is drawn out; let admirers touch the baby so no envy is left. In Chicago in 1901 Rabbi Emil G. Hirsch lectured at Central Music Hall on \"the superstition of an 'evil eye', still dreaded by mothers of small children, to an extent as if Satan, himself, were at work.\" The odd detail: a Reform rabbi's public lecture on the evil eye, which \"wisely omitted the advice to throw off the yoke of superstition\" — the Staats-Zeitung reporter's own verdict: \"To relinquish one's religion is far less difficult than to throw off superstition.\"",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Folklore (labelled); Hirsch lecture High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — three layers: settlement workers call it superstition; the folklore offers four cures; underneath, one jettatore on Taylor Street is real — and each community's cure works only on its own people, so a multi-ethnic block is defenceless.",
