      "summary": "The Rialto (1917, Marshall & Fox, Italian Renaissance, 1,548 seats, operated by Jones, Linick & Schaefer) was Minsky's Rialto by 1930 — the city's premier burlesque house through the 1930s–50s (briefly renamed the Loop End on November 7, 1931; closed December 31, 1953; demolished January 1954). The Star and Garter (opened February 9, 1908, 1,955 seats) and the Haymarket (1887, a 2,475-seat legitimate house gone to burlesque) held the West Madison trade. Hinda Wausau claimed to have invented the striptease by accident about 1928 at the Haymarket or the State-Congress when her costume came apart in a shimmy. The odd detail: The city's burlesque capital stood two blocks from the Palmer House (unverified — V2_R16), with a Marshall & Fox façade — the same architects as the Drake and the Blackstone.",
