      "summary": "Samuel Insull's 45-storey office building with an opera house in its lap — shaped like a chair, \"Insull's Throne\" — opened November 4, 1929, six days after Black Tuesday (the earlier archive has the opening-night scene). The auditorium (3,276 seats today) is the only house that has ever staged Hamilton Forrest's Camille (mixed reviews; the page's date for it is garbled — UNVERIFIED). The Civic Opera company collapsed with Insull's empire in 1932 and the house limped through the decade with successor companies (widely stated; not confirmed from a page read this session). Meanwhile Louis Eckstein's all-opera Ravinia seasons in Highland Park went dark after 1931 and the park stayed closed four years until North Shore money brought the Chicago Symphony there in 1936. The odd detail: The two opera stages of the North Shore and the Loop both went silent within a year, and the one that came back came back as an orchestra.",
