      "summary": "The Chicago Club (1869; Pullman, Potter Palmer and Robert Todd Lincoln among the founders, Marshall Field later) lost its Burnham & Root house when it collapsed during remodelling in the 1920s and moved into Granger & Bollenbacher's granite fortress in 1929 — 1,200 hand-picked members (unverified — V2_R16). The Racquet Club (founded 1922; Rebori's Classical Revival building; 28 bedrooms, 500 members; a court-tennis court converted for lawn tennis in 1936) was the Gold Coast men's house. The Fortnightly (1873, the oldest women's club in Chicago; Bertha Palmer and Jane Addams among its early members) met in the Lathrop House. The Arts Club under Rue Winterbotham Carpenter (president 1918–31) and then Elizabeth \"Bobsy\" Goodspeed (1932–40) put on Picasso's first American solo show (1923) and Brancusi curated by Duchamp. The Tavern Club (arts and business men over the river) and the Casino Club (society's women's club) are known this session only from their repeal-night parties (see above). The odd detail: The city's oldest men's club literally fell down in the 1920s.",
