      "summary": "Schaller's (1881) ran as a speakeasy with a peephole in the south-wall door — kept as a relic under beer signs for 80 years — and took its name from the Ambrosia Brewery next door, whose beer was \"supposedly pumped in straight from the brewer's giant casks\"; George \"Harvey\" Schaller bought it after repeal. The 11th Ward Democratic headquarters stood across the street, and five Bridgeport mayors — Kelly, Kennelly, the two Daleys, Bilandic — used it as a second office. Twin Anchors' 1881 building had a saloon from 1890; during Prohibition Don and Ethel Humphreys ran it as \"Tante Lee Soft Drinks\" with the front windows boarded to look empty; Bob Walters and Herb Eldean, Chicago Yacht Club men, reopened it as Twin Anchors (1932 is the usual date — UNVERIFIED on the page read) with no kitchen — Walters' wife cooked Sunday dinners until the ribs forced them to build one. The odd detail: The Kelly machine's second office was a speakeasy with — so the story goes — a beer pipe from next door.",
