      "summary": "Edward Joseph O'Hare, born 5 Sept 1893 in St. Louis to Patrick and Cecelia (Malloy) O'Hare; married Selma Anna Lauth 4 June 1912; three children — Edward \"Butch\" (1914), Patricia (1919), Marilyn (1924); Missouri bar 1923. His fortune came from representing Owen P. Smith, \"high commissioner of the International Greyhound Racing Association,\" who held the patent on the mechanical rabbit — every dog track in America needed O'Hare's client — and from running dog tracks in Chicago, Boston and Miami from 1925. Divorced 1927 and moved to Chicago, into Capone's orbit at the Hawthorne Kennel Club in Cicero, which became the Sportsman's Park horse track. He was engaged for seven years to Ursula Sue Granata, a secretary and sister of a mob-friendly Illinois state representative; as Catholics they were waiting on a Vatican dispensation he hoped for \"by 1940.\" The 1939 Tribune found \"a secret hideout O'Hare maintained in an apartment building he owned at 1220 Sherwin avenue,\" \"expensively furnished and double locked\"; the \"basement,\" the \"$100,000\" building, the period furniture, oil paintings and bronze vases and the \"felt the need to disappear\" come only from a search snippet of the Rogers Park historical-society page (unverified — V_R17). He flew (a ride in Lindbergh's mail plane; lead pilot for Robertson Aircraft at Lambert Field) and taught Butch to shoot on the Meramec. His safety-deposit box held deeds, $96,000 of life insurance and stock; his will gave a $600 annuity to his sister and the rest equally to the three children. Butch O'Hare won the Medal of Honor as a Navy fighter pilot (action of 20 Feb 1942), was lost on 26 Nov 1943, and Chicago's Orchard Field was renamed for him in 1949. The odd detail: a man with a Glencoe house, a Massachusetts penthouse and a Miami villa also kept a secret furnished basement in Rogers Park to vanish into.",
      "evidence_status": "research lead",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on family/business; Medium on the Sherwin Avenue hideaway (snippet only) and Low on the Nitti marriage • the \"basement,\" the \"$100,000\" building, the period furniture, oil paintings and bronze vases and the \"felt the need to disappear\" come only from a search snippet of the Rogers Park historical-society page (unverified — V_R17). • a Rogers Park historical-society page reached only through a search snippet says Frank Nitti married her after O'Hare's death; the same Tribune story names O'Hare's secretary as Antoinette \"Toni\" Cavaretta, and Nitti's later wife is usually given as Annette Caravetta — UNVERIFIED which woman the Nitti marriage story belongs to; see Leads • the Sherwin Avenue detail comes from its search-result snippet, UNVERIFIED by a read",
