      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Union Stock Yards",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Halsted St between 41st and 43rd",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Union%20Stock%20Yards%2C%20Halsted%20St%20between%2041st%20and%2043rd%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "the fire crossed Halsted eastward and burned the blocks between Halsted",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "Emerald, 41st and 42nd Streets",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20fire%20crossed%20Halsted%20eastward%20and%20burned%20the%20blocks%20between%20Halsted%2C%20Emerald%2C%2041st%20and%2042nd%20Streets%2C%20Chicago"
        },
        {
          "name": "the surviving gate stands at Exchange Ave and Peoria St",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=the%20surviving%20gate%20stands%20at%20Exchange%20Ave%20and%20Peoria%20St%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "At 4:14 p.m. on Saturday 19 May 1934 fire broke out in the straw-bedded wooden cattle pens below the 43rd Street viaduct — a still-lit cigarette dropped from the viaduct is the accepted cause; the fire marshal also allowed a match or spontaneous combustion. Chicago had had less than four inches of rain since 1 January; it was 92°F; the wind gusted to 60 mph and drove the fire \"faster than a man could run.\" Alarms went from 5-11 to fifteen special calls; some 2,200 firemen and five-sixths of the city's engines and trucks came, and at least 31 suburban departments (Blue Island, Chicago Heights, Oak Lawn, Harvey…) sat in the emptied city firehouses. Six pumpers and a hook-and-ladder burned where they stood at the hydrants, with Engine Company 59's own firehouse; the Halsted Street \"L\" station burned and a streetcar with it ($500,000 to the Rapid Transit). Destroyed: the International Amphitheatre (the old Dexter Park Pavilion), the Stock Yard Inn at 42nd and Halsted (the stockmen's hotel), the Saddle and Sirloin Club on Dexter Park Avenue, the Drovers National Bank (4201 S. Halsted), the Live Stock National Bank (Exchange and Halsted), the new Exchange Building with a hundred-odd commission firms in it, radio station WAAF and the Daily Drovers' Journal. Telephones, electricity and gas in the district went out. One yards employee, about 33, was found under a fallen wall; more than 50 were hurt, most of them firemen overcome by smoke; several hundred cattle died (the sheep were mostly got out). Losses: $8,000,000 (≈$193M today). The column of smoke was seen by airmen over South Bend, 95 miles away; twenty police captains were sent to hold back the crowds; about 150 families were burned out. Mayor Kelly called it \"the worst fire Chicago has known since the great one of 1871\" — Fire Marshal Michael Corrigan said the same — and turned down Governor Horner's radio offer of the National Guard. The fire was held by 8:30 p.m.; the packing plants themselves were saved. Rebuilding began under engineer A. Epstein and the new International Amphitheatre opened on 1 December 1934, in time for the stock show. The first morning's wire figures were wilder — the Evening Star's front page of 20 May read \"BLAZE SWEEPS CHICAGO RAZING 42 BLOCKS … FIRE MOST DISASTROUS SINCE 1871 HOLOCAUST,\" with \"500 injured, 1,200 left homeless,\" three missing, $10,000,000 and 2,000–3,000 firemen; its inside page put the origin in a hay barn of the Union Stock Yards & Transit Co., added the Manhattan Brewery, P. Brennan Packing Co. and Illinois Bell's yards exchange to the losses, said Mayor Kelly had ordered 300 patrolmen in, quoted Corrigan — \"in all my years with the department, this is the worst fire I have ever seen\" — and reported that at 6:15 p.m. the \"L\" structure had partly collapsed and a car \"plunged down into the station.\" Its best detail: the hundred-odd telephone operators at the yards exchange, \"lungs clogged with smoke and eyes smarting,\" who kept the boards going \"at one time with only two trunk lines working\" and left only when the fire cut the cables. The odd detail: the stone gate (Burnham & Root, c.1875–79) with the bull's head \"Sherman\" over the arch — John B. Sherman's favourite bull — and its gatehouse were \"the only substantial buildings to survive\"; the yards' own 1910 dead (21 firemen including Chief James J. Horan, plus three civilians, killed 22 Dec 1910 in the Nelson Morris warehouse collapse at 4300 S. Loomis, when the hydrants had been shut off against frost) are memorialised on that same gate.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High (figures vary between sources: \"six square blocks\" vs \"80 acres / eight blocks\"; 1,600 vs 2,200 firemen; the death toll is one employee in all accounts)",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the design notes already say clean ground is scarce in the slaughterhouse district — here is the day the district burned to the ground and the records of a hundred commission firms with it. What was in the Exchange Building's vaults that somebody needed gone before the Saturday-night stock count?",
