      "summary": "(1) \"May 26 1937\" — correct: SWOC called the strike against Republic, Inland and Youngstown Sheet & Tube on 26 May. (2) \"85,000 steel workers\" — that number is Wikipedia's (Chicago in the 1930s, citing Ron Grossman, Chicago Tribune, 27 May 2012). The Tribune of 31 May 1937 said 70,000 \"in five states\"; Wikipedia's Little Steel strike article says 67,000 out within days and 80,000 across thirty mills of the three companies in eight states; some accounts reach 92,000 by counting Bethlehem later (unverified — V_R17). Best figure: 70,000–80,000. (3) \"five mills\" — correct as the owner used it: his source (Chicago in the 1930s) reads \"Five Chicago-area mills were shut with 22,000 workers going on strike\"; the strike as a whole touched about thirty mills. (4) \"22,000 of them from Chicago\" — correct, the Tribune's own figure for the Chicago area (Republic South Chicago, Inland at Indiana Harbor, Youngstown Sheet & Tube at East Chicago). (5) \"four days later\" — correct: Wednesday 26 May to Sunday 30 May. (6) \"Ten demonstrators were killed\" — correct: four died on the field, six more of gunshot wounds by 19 June; most were shot in the back or side. (7) \"60 injured\" — low side: about 30 were shot and about 28 more clubbed with serious head wounds (Wikipedia: 67+ wounded, nine permanently disabled); the Tribune's first-day count was 90 sent to hospitals, 30 of them by gunfire; the Chicago History Museum says 40 shot, 90+ wounded. (8) \"40 police officers were hurt\" — the police/press figure. The Tribune counted 26 police among the 90 hospitalised; the La Follette committee found the police injuries minor and the encounter \"clearly avoidable by the police.\" Wikipedia's summary is that no policeman was seriously injured and none prosecuted. So: keep 10 dead, ~30 shot, ~90 hurt, 22,000 Chicago strikers in five Chicago-area mills (keep \"five mills\"); treat \"40 police hurt\" as the police's own claim. The odd detail: Republic Steel, not the police, \"acquired more than $50,000 worth of tear gas … merely ten days prior,\" and \"procured distinct white clubs fashioned from hatchet handles\" for the police (Chicago History Museum) — the equipment, the Senate found, came through Republic Steel.",
