      "summary": "Orlando Lippert's camera caught the police charge, the shooting and the clubbing of people on the ground. Paramount's editor judged it \"not fit to be seen\" and likely to \"incite local riot\"; the Chicago police banned it in the city (Wikipedia calls the ban illegal), and Paramount then withheld it everywhere, releasing only edited clips whose captions had the police using \"nightsticks and tear gas to subdue the attackers.\" The press, \"right up to the New York Times,\" carried the police story. La Follette's committee obtained the reels and screened them on 2 July 1937; the film is now in the National Film Registry (1997). The odd detail: the country saw the footage only because a Senate committee subpoenaed a newsreel company.",
