      "summary": "Across the Little Steel strike eighteen people died and about three hundred were hurt. On 19 June at Youngstown police fired gas at women picketing with their husbands (\"Women's Day Massacre\") — at least two killed. Two Republic mills, at Youngstown and South Chicago, kept running \"using around two hundred to three hundred workers who disapproved of the strike\" — non-strikers, not imported replacements, and not a per-mill figure. After five months the strike collapsed; the companies took men back selectively and blacklisted the rest. The NLRB case ran for years; Republic eventually paid some $20 million in back wages, and the mills were unionised under wartime pressure in 1942. The odd detail: the mill the marchers never reached kept rolling steel through the whole strike with the men who had refused to strike.",
