      "summary": "As many as four moulds were taken from the face within hours. Prof. D. E. Ashworth of the Worsham College of Embalming cast one — confiscated by Chicago police officer Alfred Mulvaney. Harold N. May, president of Reliance Dental, made two about twelve hours after death (unverified — V_R17), ostensibly to show off a new impression material (\"Reprolastic\"); he sent one to J. Edgar Hoover, who let FBI National Academy trainees copy it as a forensic-modelling exercise, and D. J. Parsons of the Bureau exhibited a cast at an American Association for the Advancement of Science meeting in St. Louis. The funeral director complained that the moulds had stripped hair and skin from the face. The plaster shows the exit wound under the right eye. A cast (McCrone does not say whose, or which of the four it descends from) was examined by McCrone Associates: plaster over an interior black-rubber mask carrying wax particles and no hairs or skin cells — a copy of a copy, not a face-cast; \"the black rubber… was not used for direct replication of facial features.\" Masks have sold at auction (CBS Chicago); the FBI keeps one on display with his straw hat, glasses and cigar (unverified — V_R17). The odd detail: the dentist's advertisement: a death mask of Public Enemy No. 1 taken to demonstrate a new brand of dental plaster.",
