      "summary": "A wire photograph from the morgue showed the body under a sheet with a tent-like rise at the midsection — almost certainly the arm, stiff in rigor mortis. Fuzzy newsprint turned it into a legend of Dillinger's endowment; editors retouched and reprinted the picture with the sheet flat. Indiana oral tradition embroidered it (he fainted when aroused; that was why the Woman in Red gave him up). About thirty years after his death the tale settled into its final form — that the organ is preserved at the Smithsonian — which the Institution denies by form letter to this day. The autopsy \"reported nothing unusual about the man.\" Legend says — none of it is history, but a 1930s Chicagoan would have seen the photograph. The odd detail: a newspaper retouched a corpse to make it decent, and the retouching proved the rumour to everyone who had seen the first print.",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High that this is the legend's shape; folklore by definition",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — the three layers, exactly: what the paper printed, what Indiana whispered, and what was under the sheet.",
