      "summary": "In Polish belief the upiór drank human blood and used superhuman strength to tear victims apart; like the strzyga it was often said to have two hearts and two souls. It could arise from a person cursed before death, a sudden death, a desecrated corpse, a suicide, a witch, or an unbaptised child. Precautions at burial: garlic heads, bricks or iron in the mouth; wild rose, hawthorn or blackthorn in the coffin; poppy seed sprinkled in the coffin \"so the upiórs would have a chore\" (counting it); burial face down with the hands tied with blessed herbs (Lublin region). Near Kraków in 1847 one was to nail the upiór's head and put paper with a teacher's writing under its tongue. The odd detail: Poppy seed in the coffin — the dead must count every seed before they can rise.",
