      "summary": "The lake's water averaged under 36 °F (2 °C) year-round (a c. 1970 figure); in such cold the bacteria that bloat a drowned body do not work, so the dead sink and do not rise — the physical basis of the saying (popularised by Lightfoot's 1976 ballad but older among lakemen; its 1930s currency is UNVERIFIED). The lake's largest single loss of life was 78 dead in the 1918 sinking of two French minesweepers; the Edmund Fitzgerald's 29 (10 November 1975) lie in 530 ft, and a 1994 expedition photographed a crewman's body in a life-jacket by the pilothouse door. Folklore with a physical cause. The odd detail: A lake in which the drowned stay where they fell, dressed, for decades — Kamloops' \"Old Whitey\" (below) is the proof.",
