      "summary": "\"November gale\" is the formal name; \"Witch of November\" is old mariners' and weathermen's slang (a 1998 textbook calls it as real to upper-Midwest sailors as the older peoples' spirits), popularised by Lightfoot. The mechanism: deep lows over the lakes pulling Arctic air from the north-west against Gulf air from the south, producing hurricane-force winds and huge short seas. The great ones: the Mataafa Storm of 27–28 November 1905 (about 29 vessels wrecked or damaged, 36 seamen dead, $3.6 million on Superior); the 7–10 November 1913 White Hurricane (R13); and, just outside the window, the Armistice Day storm of 11 November 1940 (below). The 1929 season's wrecks fell in September–November for the same reason. The odd detail: The period's lakemen already personified the November storm as a witch — the \"three layers\" already in place: a weather pattern, a witch, and what is actually there.",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the storms; the phrase's 1930s currency Medium.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A cult that keeps the lake's calendar — the equinox week of Our Son (1930), the November of 1929 — would call the Witch by name and feed her. The IHS knows the name too.",
