      "summary": "Kapparot is \"a customary atonement ritual\" performed on the afternoon before Yom Kippur: money, or a live rooster (a hen for a woman), is swung over the head three times while a prayer is recited three times, expressing the hope that any harsh decree for the new year pass to the bird: \"This rooster (hen) will go to its death, while I will enter and proceed to a good long life and to peace.\" The bird is then slaughtered. Great authorities opposed it — Nachmanides, Shlomo ben Aderet and Joseph Karo, who warned it resembled non-Jewish rites — but Moses Isserles encouraged it, and his view prevailed among Ashkenazim. The odd detail: A folk rite that the greatest code of Jewish law tried to forbid, and that survived anyway because the Ashkenazi gloss allowed it.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High for the ritual; Chicago-specific detail UNVERIFIED. Folk practice — labelled. • UNVERIFIED for Chicago; an FLPS search for \"Kaporos\" found nothing",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — Three layers: the settlement worker sees a superstition; the tradition itself argues about it for centuries; underneath, a Kabbalah of David practitioner treats the transfer idea with professional seriousness. The rite stays what it is for the population; the case is the one family on the West Side that paid a stranger, not a rabbi, to swing the bird for them that year — and the Church, which has no rite of its own for this, wants to know who the stranger was.",
