      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Holy Family Church — the two carved acolytes over the entrances to the sanctuary",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Holy%20Family%20Church%20%E2%80%94%20the%20two%20carved%20acolytes%20over%20the%20entrances%20to%20the%20sanctuary%2C%20Chicago"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Legend says (in the version printed in 1923): one stormy night two young boys rang the rectory bell and asked for a priest for an urgent sick call; the porter wanted them to wait until morning, but Damen, overhearing, went with them. They led him to a tumble-down house in a remote part of the parish and up to a garret, where a dying woman lay. She was astonished to see him — she had no one to send. Asked if she had sons, she answered: \"None living … I had two boys who were acolytes of the Holy Family Church, but they are dead.\" She died before morning, and \"the two statues, the story runs, were erected over the entrance to the sanctuary in memory of the incident.\" The odd detail: The earliest written version I found is the parish's own 1923 centenary-era history, which calls it \"one quite well authenticated tradition\" and \"one of many versions of this particular sick call story.\" It prints the testimony of a living parishioner, Mrs. John Griffin: \"I heard that story some time in the seventies. I either heard Father Damen himself mention it, or heard that he mentioned it in his sermons from the pulpit.\" So the story was in oral circulation by the 1870s, possibly from Damen's own pulpit, and in print by 1923 — thirteen years before the setting's alpha. (I could not check whether an earlier printed version exists, e.g. in the New World or a Damen biography — UNVERIFIED.)",
      "evidence_status": "folklore",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High that the tradition is attested in 1923 and claimed for the 1870s; the event itself is folklore and is labelled so by its own source. • I could not check whether an earlier printed version exists, e.g. in the New World or a Damen biography — UNVERIFIED.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — The owner's three layers are already in the source: the Church's reading (Damen believed in the power of prayer), the folklore (the dead boys came for him), and room for what is actually there — dead acolytes who still serve at the altar of a church that kept a ward burning. An IHS priest in 1936 who hears the bell ring on a stormy night at 1080 W. Roosevelt answers it.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Mulkerins, Holy Family Parish, Chicago: Priests and People (1923), pp. 135–138",
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/holyfamilyparish00mulk",
          "publisher": "Internet Archive",
          "kind": "book or scanned text",
          "supports": "read in full-text this session"
        }
      ],
