      "summary": "The Alexians began in 14th-century Flanders as laymen who nursed the victims of the Black Death \"and buried the deceased\"; they were also called Cellites, from the cells they lived in. Their Chicago hospital (1866) was destroyed in the fire of 9 Oct 1871, rebuilt the next year, and moved in 1897 to Belden and Lakewood when the new elevated displaced it from Old Town. It was a hospital for men, nursed by brothers. The 1931 register: provincial Brother Frumentius Horn; 54 brothers, 18 novices, 22 postulants; a \"Training School for Male Nurses\" (40 pupils); 3,765 patients that year; a Precious Blood chaplain. By 1938: 60 professed brothers, 27 novices, 11 postulants; the school of nursing \"exclusively for members of the Novitiate\" (45 pupils); 3,116 patients. The 1931 and 1938 registers also list diocesan priests \"absent\" and living at the Alexian hospitals in Chicago and St. Louis (next entry). The sister hospital in St. Louis (founded 1869), which trained male nurses 1928–52 and took only men until 1962, is where, the hospital later claimed, part of the 1949 exorcism behind The Exorcist took place — outside the window, but it is the same order. The odd detail: The body of John Dillinger was taken to this hospital from the Biograph alley on 22 July 1934 (R02, R17) — to brothers whose order was founded to carry out the dead.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High.",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A men-only hospital run by an order of religious brothers, where priests the chancery would rather not see are quietly kept — the natural IHS infirmary, and the place an agent who has been through a door (§20) goes to be looked after without a report being written.",
