      "summary": "R29 already gives Bradbury's story. This adds what the search for the man turned up. Bradbury said a sideshow performer sat in an electric chair, was hit with somewhere between 50,000 and \"10 billion\" volts depending on the telling, and touched children with the charged sword and told them to \"live forever.\" Bradbury named the show as the Dill Brothers Combined Shows, and insisted it was not Sam B. Dill's Circus; no record of a Dill Brothers Combined Shows has been found. Two Bradbury scholars (Sam Weller, who wrote The Bradbury Chronicles, and the Ray Bradbury Center's director, Jason Aukerman) believe it happened but cannot corroborate it. The dating is shaky: Bradbury tied it to Labor Day, but his uncle Lester Moberg, whose funeral came soon after, was shot on 17 October 1932 and died on 24 October 1932. Labor Day 1932 records show the Hagenbeck-Wallace and Downie Brothers circuses reaching Waukegan and an American Legion festival that weekend; there are no advertisements for \"Mr. Electrico.\" Candidates raised: the Scottish \"electric chair\" magician Walford Bodie (died 1939; no evidence he was in Waukegan) and Ralph Redden, who worked the Downie Brothers sideshow in 1932. A search-result summary of a 2012 Chicago magazine piece describes him as a \"Cairo native, defrocked minister, and veteran of the Ardennes\" — UNVERIFIED, page not read (it redirected to the Tribune). The odd detail: A man who tapped a child on both shoulders and the nose with an electrified sword and said \"Live, forever!\" — and has left no trace in any record.",
