      "summary": "A Military History of the Upper Great Lakes page describes the German-American Bund's Camp Hindenburg: in its first year it \"served a total of 103 boys from Milwaukee and Chicago,\" with athletics, flag-raising, inspections and limited marching; the children wore Nazi uniforms; swastikas and American flags flew together at meetings; George Froboese was the Bund's Midwest leader. The Federation of German-American Societies, an established German-American body, \"bought out the land\" and forced the camp to move about a mile away, then opened its own Camp Carl Schurz on the original site. A radio segment on the Bund in Grafton recalls (from an 11-year-old in 1941, now 86) German flags marched through a village of about 1,100, a police force of one officer helped by about ten, and the marchers told to take down the German flags and carry an American one — which they dropped as soon as they reached their camp. The Bund's meetings were held at the Grafton Hotel. Search summaries give 1937 as the opening year and a 1939 speech by Fritz Kuhn — neither is on the pages I read; UNVERIFIED. No acreage found. The odd detail: The people who ended it were Milwaukee's own German-American clubs, who bought the land and renamed the camp for Carl Schurz.",
