      "summary": "R22 tells the crime and V_R22 confirms the verdicts (Nancy Bowen pleaded guilty to manslaughter; Lila Jimerson was acquitted at her second trial). This adds the politics, from Time (7 April 1930) and Wikipedia's page on Alice Lee Jemison. Time: Nancy Bowen, 66 (R22 says 60; Time calls her Cayuga, others Seneca), killed Mrs. Marchand with a hammer and chloroform-soaked paper after Mrs. Marchand, asked \"Are you a witch?\", jestingly answered yes; Lila \"Red Lilac\" Jimerson (39 in Time; R22 says 35) had told Bowen that Mrs. Marchand caused the death of Bowen's husband Charley. When the trial began, the U.S. District Attorney Richard Harkness Templeton tried to take over Jimerson's defence on the orders of Attorney General William DeWitt Mitchell, and the state prosecutor protested: \"I deeply resent this eleventh-hour and unwarranted interference by Washington bureaucracy!\" Wikipedia's Jemison page: District Attorney Guy Moore treated it as an \"Indian\" crime and ran warrantless searches of Seneca and Cayuga homes; the Seneca journalist and advocate Alice Lee Jemison, with Chief Clinton Rickard and Seneca President Ray Jimerson, appealed to Vice President Charles Curtis, and the Bureau of Indian Affairs arranged for a U.S. Attorney to represent the two women. Jemison's page says nothing about witchcraft; Time does not mention the ouija board that R22's press sources do. The odd detail: The witch accusation ended in a jurisdiction fight — a Buffalo prosecutor calling the Department of Justice a \"Washington bureaucracy\" for defending a Seneca woman.",
