      "summary": "Canada's oldest museum was opened in 1827 by the Englishman Thomas Barnett in a former brewery; it held over 5,000 curiosities — taxidermy, minerals, \"Native American curiosities,\" whale skeletons, a mastodon (1857) — and Egyptian mummies bought by Barnett's son Sydney on three trips to Egypt in 1854. After Barnett's bankruptcy in 1878 Saul Davis bought it; Jacob Sherman took it in 1942; it closed in 1998 after 171 years. In 1999 the collector William Jamieson sold the Egyptian material to Emory University's Michael C. Carlos Museum in Atlanta, whose scholars and doctors identified one unlabelled male mummy as Pharaoh Ramesses I — a search-summary version says it came out of a Theban tomb cache in the 1860s and reached Niagara by the early twentieth century. It was returned to Egypt in 2003. The odd detail: For most of the 1930s the founder of Egypt's Nineteenth Dynasty stood, unknown, in a tourist museum between a whale skeleton and taxidermy, with nobody on the honeymoon circuit knowing what they were looking at.",
