      "summary": "Oshkosh had German newspapers from 1852; the Wisconsin Telegraph (October 1866–16 April 1920), founded by the Kohlmann brothers at the Kohlmann Building (1875, 21 Wagoo Avenue — Oshkosh's first steam press), ran a Sunday supplement Der Feier-Abend and a Tuesday Das Dienstags-Blatt (1905–1917); it was $2 a year in 1889. Its publisher W. F. Weber's farewell editorial blamed the war years: advertisers withdrew, \"fanatical mobs\" acted, editors who spoke freely were jailed, and the paper shrank; it survived to 1920 but not the peace. Elsewhere: in 1889 the Bennett Law required English for the main school subjects and was repealed within a few years after German voters revolted (search-result summary); in 1917 many Wisconsin Germans hid their ethnicity and quit German clubs (search-result summary); the weeklies of the Milwaukee Herold and Germania merged in 1918 as Milwaukee America. Watertown was the seat of the first U.S. kindergarten (Margarethe Schurz, 1856, now at the Octagon House Museum) and of the Wisconsin Synod's Luther Preparatory School; its population went 8,437 (1900) → 11,301 (1940). Wausau grew from 12,354 (1900) to 27,268 (1940); in 1867 August Kickbusch returned from Germany with 702 people who are believed to have settled the Wausau area, and became its first mayor in 1872. Sheboygan's Germans arrived in the wave after the revolutions of 1848. The odd detail: The Oshkosh German-language paper that outlived the war by a year and a half died in April 1920 — its own publisher said the war's hatred killed it.",
