      "summary": "In December 1921, working for Charles Kettering at General Motors' Dayton Research Laboratories, Thomas Midgley Jr. found that tetraethyllead stopped engines knocking; it was sold as \"Ethyl,\" with lead deliberately unmentioned. In the late 1920s, with Albert Leon Henne, he developed dichlorodifluoromethane — the first CFC, sold as Freon — as a safe refrigerant. At a 1924 press conference he showed the lead was safe by pouring it on his hands and inhaling the vapour, although the Bayway plant had multiple deaths and cases of hallucination and insanity within its first two months of operation. In 1940 he contracted polio and devised a rope-and-pulley to lift himself; he was found strangled in it on 2 November 1944, ruled a suicide. The odd detail: The man who put lead in the world's fuel and CFCs in its air was killed by his own labour-saving invention.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: High on the discoveries and the 1924 demonstration; the Bayway death toll is not on the page read (do not quote a number).",
