      "summary": "At its peak around 1900 Over-the-Rhine held about 45,000 people, roughly 75 per cent of them German-Americans, with German-language schools, churches, newspapers, clubs, beer gardens and most of the area's big breweries. Seventeen breweries operated there and in the West End between 1875 and 1900, and in 1890 Cincinnati was \"dubbed\" the beer capital of the world (Moerlein, Hauck, Windisch-Mulhauser, Kauffman among them). In the real record the 1917 war with Germany brought a purge — schools dropped German, the library removed German books, council renamed 13 streets, and police required German songs to be sung in English — and Prohibition then erased thousands of jobs. In the setting there is no American war on Germany. The odd detail: The \"German-ness\" of the neighbourhood was no longer celebrated after Prohibition — not because of a lost war in the setting's version, but because of a law.",
