      "places": [
        {
          "name": "Cedar Point",
          "address": "",
          "geography": "the peninsula off Sandusky, Erie County, Ohio",
          "precision": "search locator, not surveyed",
          "map_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Cedar%20Point%2C%20the%20peninsula%20off%20Sandusky%2C%20Erie%20County%2C%20Ohio"
        }
      ],
      "summary": "The Hotel Breakers opened in 1905 with 600 rooms — among the biggest hotels in the Midwest — and was modernised in 1935 with a Tavern Terrace. The Coliseum (1906) hosted big bands through the Depression and the war; in 1939 its dance floor became a ballroom with refreshment service, the one popular and profitable innovation of the decade. Few rides were added in the 1930s; Boeckling — the man who had made the resort — died in 1931, and in the late 1930s the resort looked as though it would be sold to the State of Ohio for $3,000,000. The odd detail: The largest lakeside resort hotel in the Midwest was on the edge of becoming a state park in 1938–39, and only the ballroom made money.",
      "evidence_status": "documented",
      "uncertainties": "Confidence: Medium (search summaries).",
      "hook": "Proposal, not canon — A 600-room hotel and a ballroom on a peninsula: a rich people's party venue for a society-vampire weekend, and a place where a state's purchase offer arrives at exactly the moment its owner has died.",
